window.CPP_EN=window.CPP_EN||{};window.CPP_EN["physical"]={"physical-1":{"html":"<p><strong>Origin and purpose</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>ESRM was born in <strong>2010</strong>. It was created by members of the CSO Roundtable of ASIS International, today the <strong>CSO Center</strong> (CSO = Chief Security Officer).</li>\n<li>Aim: manage security with risk principles, coordinating with the senior management whose operations generate that risk.</li>\n<li>Intended results: integrate the security professional into business leadership. Move from managing programs to managing risks.</li>\n<li>Error it combats: the \"quick fix.\" Buying a fashionable system or service without asking which assets exist or which threats they face.</li>\n<li>More detail: Chapter 1 of the volume <em>Security Management</em> of Protection of Assets.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-1-e0\">1.1 Taking an Enterprise Security Risk Management Approach</h3><p><strong>Definition:</strong> ESRM is a strategic approach to security management. It links security practice to the overall strategy of the organization. It uses globally accepted risk management principles.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a <strong>management process/system</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is <strong>not</strong> a program or an element of an existing program. It <strong>replaces</strong> the security program methodology.</li>\n<li>It connects all the key elements of security risk to the assets. It informs the decisions of their owners.</li>\n<li>It is <strong>scalable and dynamic</strong>: it serves public or private organizations of any size or scope.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Traditional program-based management</th>\n<th>ESRM</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Structure</td>\n<td>Security program with many subprograms</td>\n<td>No programs; addresses all security risks to assets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Objective</td>\n<td>A program for each threat or topic</td>\n<td>Effective mitigation of prioritized risks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk assignment</td>\n<td>Risk is assigned to a program</td>\n<td>Does not assign risks to specific programs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Role of security</td>\n<td>Does what it is asked or ordered; implements processes and programs</td>\n<td>Advises; with the owner identifies, prioritizes, and sets mitigation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who decides</td>\n<td>Security carries out what is requested</td>\n<td>The <strong>asset owner</strong> owns the risk decisions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>As partners, security and the owner identify risks, prioritize them, and set mitigation steps or methods.</li>\n<li>The asset owner is <strong>ultimately responsible</strong> for security risk decisions. Just as with any other risk to their assets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two misconceptions about ESRM</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Error</th>\n<th>What the book says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. ESRM = convergence</td>\n<td>False. Convergence = bringing traditional, corporate, or physical security together with information security/cybersecurity under a single leader. ESRM manages risk under any structure. There is effective ESRM with different leaders for physical and information security.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. ESRM = ERM (Enterprise Risk Management)</td>\n<td>False. ERM covers <strong>all</strong> key risks: <strong>financial, operational, and strategic</strong>. ESRM deals with security risks.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Convergence: not indispensable. But, since cyber is a key risk for every organization, it can streamline management and improve resource allocation.</li>\n<li>Key to success: that everyone who manages security risks adopt and use ESRM.</li>\n<li>ESRM–ERM relationship:<ul>\n<li>Not all organizations have a formal ERM function, although they manage risks in various ways.</li>\n<li>ESRM can be a <strong>subcomponent</strong> of ERM.</li>\n<li>High-impact security risks can be tracked formally in ERM. Not all reach that level.</li>\n<li>That is why ESRM is an <strong>independent</strong> system: it works with or without ERM.</li>\n<li>Where there is a well-defined ERM, security is usually on the <strong>ERM operations committee</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.1.1 The Benefits of an ESRM Program</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the title says \"ESRM Program,\" but the chapter insists that ESRM is <strong>not</strong> a program, but a management process.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Benefits for the security professional and team (10)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>By passing the decision to the asset owner, more resources and perspectives are added to risk management.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Security moves from tactical program owner to strategic partner and trusted advisor. It can oversee mitigation processes, but supported or directed by the owners. E.g.: a centralized access control, operated by security, that mitigates risks for several owners.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Asset owners do not manage by programs. With a risk approach, security works like them and collaborates better.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Security better understands the organization, its strategy, and its goals. It thus helps better to identify and prioritize risks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Communication with internal and external stakeholders improves. Security learns what they consider critical and refines priorities and plans.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The focus on risk allows more innovation in solving problems and reducing risks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>The holistic approach provides more value and reduces security and related risks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Security moves from reactive to proactive: fewer incidents and lower impact.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Better resource allocation. Budgets are more manageable and better supported when aligned with owners' goals and identified risks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>As a partner who understands the business, security enters other strategic areas. It gains visibility and value.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Benefits for the organization (9)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Critical risk decisions at the enterprise level, aligned with mission and objectives. Valid for multinationals and for single-facility organizations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Owners and stakeholders have a broader, more coherent understanding of the security function and risk.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Senior management sees key risks better, because they are raised by managers who are asset owners.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Security resources are aligned with strategy to mitigate prioritized risks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Early identification and proactive monitoring of a wide range of risks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Better understanding and commitment by stakeholders regarding risk and the security function.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Better support for legal, regulatory, and contractual functions and issues.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Platform for integrating security into the organization's culture.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>More resilience, better response to events, and better crisis management.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"physical-1-e1\">1.2 Adopting an ESRM Approach</h3><p><strong>The three components of ESRM</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Context of ESRM</strong></td>\n<td>Aspects of the organization that security must understand to adopt ESRM successfully.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Foundation of ESRM</strong></td>\n<td>Organizational concepts that sustain the approach and maximize its impact.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ESRM Cycle</strong></td>\n<td>The actual security risk management process; emphasizes understanding the assets.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book lists Context → Foundation → Cycle, but it develops the Cycle (1.3) before the Foundation (1.4).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>1.2.1 The Context of ESRM</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The organization is the environment where assets and risks live. Risks and incidents vary greatly from one organization to another.</li>\n<li>Understanding the organization is an <strong>initial step</strong> of ESRM. It is at least as important as understanding threats.</li>\n<li>Aspects that security must understand before implementing ESRM:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mission and vision</td>\n<td>They are the top of the strategy. Aligning the security strategy with the organization's is one of the most critical requirements. Reinforcing this knowledge is usually the first step when adopting ESRM.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Services and products</td>\n<td>Basis for engaging with owners and stakeholders, and for identifying and prioritizing risks. The larger the organization, the more complex.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key personnel, leadership, and operating structure</td>\n<td>Makes it possible to move around the organization and build key relationships.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal requirements and regulations</td>\n<td>Those that affect security and the organization in general. They help to see compliance risks and to understand stakeholders' positions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic plans and long-term goals</td>\n<td>They are the road map. Security reviews goals and strategies looking for risks and raises them to leadership.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Resulting message to stakeholders: \"security exists to help you achieve your goals\" and \"we want the same thing you do.\"</li>\n<li>Effect: more engagement and less perception of security as an <strong>obstacle</strong> to the business.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.2.2 Core Values</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They usually go beyond profit and shareholder value. Examples from the book: environmental care, community, employee safety, product quality, brand and image protection, new product development.</li>\n<li>ESRM emphasizes <strong>partnership, collaboration, and transparency</strong>. If the values fit with this, the organization will adopt ESRM more easily.</li>\n<li>Linking ESRM to the values ensures alignment with senior management. It is advisable to cite them in the security strategy and messages.</li>\n<li>Values usually define the culture. A culture that does not support security makes it hard to implement ESRM.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Questions to assess values and culture (6):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>How does the organization handle change?</li>\n<li>What is its risk tolerance/risk appetite?</li>\n<li>Are some business units more open than others to working with security?</li>\n<li>What do employee surveys say about values, culture, and change?</li>\n<li>Is there effective communication with all stakeholders?</li>\n<li>What are the organization's key motivators?</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>1.2.3 Operating Environment</h4>\n<p>Three types of environment:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Environment</th>\n<th>Includes</th>\n<th>What it is for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Physical</strong></td>\n<td>Type of location, building, and surroundings; pedestrian and vehicle traffic; access by nonemployees; industrial control systems; criticality and sensitivity of processes and assets; products on site or stored.</td>\n<td>Understanding processes and functions makes it possible to partner with owners and prioritize risks. Knowing the operations makes security a trusted advisor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nonphysical</strong></td>\n<td>Geopolitical environment; external pressures on the industry; legal, regulatory, and compliance requirements; intensity of competition; growth phase; speed of decision-making; impact of technology; complexity of continuous change, including leadership change.</td>\n<td>They move owners and partners, and are sources of risk. They serve as a reference for designing mitigation options.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Logical</strong></td>\n<td>Types of information; digital assets and the network that connects them with each other and with stakeholders. E.g.: servers, workstations, network infrastructure, connectivity, other devices and end points.</td>\n<td>Essential for advising on risks, because every business increasingly depends on digital assets and data.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>1.2.4 Stakeholders</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> anyone who interacts directly with the organization. They can affect, or be affected by, the organization, its assets, or its personnel.</li>\n<li>Security must know what matters to each one. That way it advises better and helps design mitigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stakeholders cited by the book (7):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stakeholder</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Executive team, Board of Directors, and those who set the strategy</td>\n<td>With more mature ESRM, there is more contact with them and more need to know their concerns.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Asset owners</strong>, tangible and intangible</td>\n<td><strong>Key partners</strong> of ESRM; the ones worked with most. Some assets have shared or multiple owners.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People who work for or on behalf of the organization</td>\n<td>They can be key allies. Some are owners of intangible assets: purchasing, human resources, legal, marketing. Others are partners in mitigating an owner's risks. Includes employees and contractor personnel.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Those who provide knowledge or support</td>\n<td>Suppliers, distributors, consultants, audit firms.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Industry organizations</td>\n<td>Their actions affect the organization. They can generate risk or be a mitigation resource.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customers</td>\n<td>Distributors, resellers, retailers, and direct customers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Surrounding community</td>\n<td><strong>Often forgotten.</strong> The organization affects its surroundings, traffic, noise, and economy. There are joint risks; partnering with it helps reduce them.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>How to build the relationship with asset owners:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First, identify them.</li>\n<li>If there is already a relationship, the partnership arises more easily.</li>\n<li>If there is not: directly ask for a brief meeting, or lean on a <strong>champion</strong>.</li>\n<li>The champion is an asset owner with whom you have already worked, at the same level as the others. Opens doors and defends ESRM and the professional.</li>\n<li>Initial meetings: short; get to know the owner and what matters to him or her.</li>\n<li>Follow-up meetings: focused on risk <strong>as the owner sees it</strong>. Look for an opportunity to propose a partnership.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keys:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stakeholder support is critical: identify them, involve them, understand what matters to them, and align with their priorities.</li>\n<li>Understanding them does <strong>not</strong> mean harmonizing their interests. It means understanding their needs and their view of risk.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-1-e2\">1.3 The ESRM Cycle</h3><ul>\n<li>It describes <strong>how</strong> security risks are mitigated. It resembles other processes in the sector.</li>\n<li>Distinctive feature: emphasis on understanding the assets and involving their owners.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Process</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>Identify and Prioritize Assets</strong></td>\n<td>Prioritization is by each asset's criticality to the mission and strategy. With guidance from senior management and input from owners and stakeholders. The prioritized list is the basis for the rest of the cycle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong>Identify and Prioritize Risks</strong></td>\n<td>Prioritization is by each risk's potential to prevent fulfilling the mission and strategy. With guidance from senior management and input from stakeholders.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>Mitigate Prioritized Risks</strong></td>\n<td>In order of priority, with controls recommended by security and <strong>approved by the owner</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>Continuous Improvement</strong></td>\n<td>Spans the <strong>entire</strong> cycle.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>1.3.1 Identify and Prioritize Assets</h4>\n<p>Key roles at this stage and throughout the cycle:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Asset Owner</strong></td>\n<td>Person most directly responsible for the asset operating successfully. ESRM assigns the risk to the owner because he or she best understands the asset. <strong>The asset owner is the risk owner.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Top Management</strong></td>\n<td>Highest executive level: the first executive level (c-suite) or an executive committee. Always <strong>above</strong> the security leader (CSO, Chief Security Officer) and <strong>does not include</strong> him or her.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security Professional</strong></td>\n<td>Subject matter expert (SME) in security risk. Trusted advisor to owners, senior management, and stakeholders. Guides the owner in the risk decision.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>ESRM considers <strong>all</strong> types of assets: physical, information, cyber, personnel, etc. It does not segment disciplines or work in silos.</li>\n<li>Everything within the scope of the security professional is within the scope of ESRM.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.3.2 Identify and Prioritize Risks</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Criterion: each risk's potential to undermine the mission and strategy.</li>\n<li>The prioritized lists of risks and assets form <strong>strategic priorities</strong>. They \"cascade\" down through the entire cycle and guide the owners' decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.3.3 Mitigate Prioritized Risks</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The owner decides, with security's guidance.</li>\n<li>Security documents assets, risks, and owners' decisions, perhaps in a <strong>security risk management plan</strong>.</li>\n<li>The plan is ultimately <strong>executed</strong> by the security professional.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> distinguish roles. Decides and approves = asset owner. Recommends, documents, and executes the plan = security professional.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>1.3.4 Continuous Improvement</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the final process, but it spans the entire cycle.</li>\n<li>It arises from ESRM results: better communication, more visibility of risks, effective prioritization and mitigation.</li>\n<li>Mechanisms: lessons learned and feedback loops.</li>\n<li>Functions that contribute most: <strong>incident response, investigations and analysis, and information sharing</strong>.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-1-e3\">1.4 The Foundation of ESRM</h3><p>To ensure sustained longevity and success, <strong>four critical concepts</strong> are incorporated:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Key idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Holistic Risk Management</strong></td>\n<td>All stakeholders take part; covers all types of risk without silos.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Partnership with Stakeholders</strong></td>\n<td>Security as advisor and advocate for stakeholders.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Transparency</strong></td>\n<td>Clear, open, and honest approach to risks and process.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Governance</strong></td>\n<td>Rules and processes by which a function or organization is governed.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>1.4.1 Holistic Risk Management</h4>\n<p>\"Holistic\" = inclusive, comprehensive, complete. Two meanings in ESRM:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>All stakeholders take part.</strong> All have a role, an interest, and a benefit. Especially asset owners and senior management, who must participate actively.</li>\n<li><strong>It covers all types of security risk</strong>, without qualifiers or silos (physical, cyber, information, personnel). Whatever is within the scope of the security function falls under ESRM: one, several, or all disciplines.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Scope does not make ESRM more or less effective. But the more it covers, the more positive impact it can have.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.4.2 Partner with Stakeholders</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>In the Context (1.2.4)</th>\n<th>In the Foundation (1.4.2)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stakeholders are a component: identify them, involve them, and align with them.</td>\n<td>The component is <strong>forging partnerships</strong>: collaborative role of advisor and advocate.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>It is especially important with asset owners and senior management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Expected conduct of the professional:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Do</th>\n<th>Instead of</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Give <strong>guidance</strong></td>\n<td>Giving instructions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Support</strong> decisions about assets</td>\n<td>Making them yourself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rely on <strong>measured risk</strong></td>\n<td>Fear, uncertainty, doubt</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Proactively define and \"socialize\" your own role with senior management and owners. If not, others will define it.</li>\n<li>This makes relationships more positive, valuable, and partnership-based.</li>\n<li>Other typical results (4):<ul>\n<li>Greater stakeholder engagement.</li>\n<li>Greater sense of ownership and buy-in.</li>\n<li>Better understanding of security risk on their part.</li>\n<li>Organizational culture more favorable to security priorities.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the list in 1.4 says \"Partnership with Stakeholders\" and the title of 1.4.2 says \"Partner with Stakeholders.\" It is the same component.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>1.4.3 Transparency</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> a clear, open, and honest approach. It usually improves culture and morale; stakeholders feel like partners.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it requires</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Risk Transparency</strong></td>\n<td>Presenting risks as they are: neither exaggerated nor minimized. Use objective statements, quantitative analysis, and quantifiable metrics. Supports the owner as risk owner and enables informed decisions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Process Transparency</strong></td>\n<td>Having owners and stakeholders understand the risk management process and each step. It almost never has value to keep it confidential. Sharing it increases engagement and acceptance. Analogy: the doctor who explains the procedure and calms the patient's defensive or anxious attitude.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>What to share with owners and stakeholders (5):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a. Current and planned security measures.</li>\n<li>b. The reasons for those measures.</li>\n<li>c. Other relevant decision makers.</li>\n<li>d. Other mitigation options that may be considered.</li>\n<li>e. Final decisions on mitigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.4.4 Governance</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> rules and processes by which a function or organization is governed.</li>\n<li>It manages expectations, provides clarity and consistency, and ensures that efforts cover the organization's needs.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Definition and notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Organizational Governance</strong></td>\n<td>System by which the organization is directed and controlled. It addresses: role of senior executives and the board; audit and oversight; rights and responsibilities of stakeholders; decision procedures. ESRM governance must align with it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ESRM Governance</strong></td>\n<td>Process of setting security risk policy and direction, allocating resources, and ensuring compliance. It is a <strong>subset</strong> of corporate governance and is modeled on organizational governance. It is exercised by a security governance body (committee, council, or other group). Its members must be <strong>mostly risk stakeholders, not security professionals</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Typical documentary framework that governance produces:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Document</th>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Feature</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Policies</strong></td>\n<td>High level</td>\n<td>Corporate strategy and direction. They are the \"laws\" of the organization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standards</strong></td>\n<td>Tactical</td>\n<td>How to comply with policy. More detailed than policies. They set what \"shall\"/\"must\" be done.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Guidelines</strong></td>\n<td>Tactical</td>\n<td>Like standards, but less rigid. They recommend what \"should\"/\"may\" be done.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Procedures</strong></td>\n<td>The most operational</td>\n<td>Step-by-step instructions to comply with the preceding documents.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>ESRM weighs heavily on physical security: from how products or solutions are adopted to their implementation and use across the enterprise.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-1-e4\">Key Terms</h3><p>enterprise security risk management (ESRM) · CSO Roundtable / CSO Center · quick fix · program-based security management · asset owner · risk owner · top management · c-suite · security professional · trusted advisor · subject matter expert · convergence · enterprise risk management (ERM) · ERM operations committee · Context of ESRM · Foundation of ESRM · ESRM Cycle · mission and vision · core values · partnership, collaboration, transparency · risk tolerance / risk appetite · operating environment (physical, nonphysical, logical) · stakeholders · champion · Identify and Prioritize Assets · Identify and Prioritize Risks · Mitigate Prioritized Risks · Continuous Improvement · security risk management plan · lessons learned · feedback loops · holistic risk management · partnership with stakeholders · fear, uncertainty, doubt · ownership and buy-in · risk transparency · process transparency · organizational governance · ESRM governance · security governance body · policies · standards · guidelines · procedures</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"1.1 Taking an Enterprise Security Risk Management Approach"},{"id":"e1","t":"1.2 Adopting an ESRM Approach"},{"id":"e2","t":"1.3 The ESRM Cycle"},{"id":"e3","t":"1.4 The Foundation of ESRM"},{"id":"e4","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[]},"physical-2":{"html":"<ul>\n<li>Starting problem: security lacks universal definitions, physical security included.</li>\n<li>Knowing what physical security is and is not is key to an integrated protection and risk management strategy.</li>\n<li>It applies to assessment, planning, systems design, implementation, and program management.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-2-e0\">2.1 Definition and Purpose of Physical Security</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Purpose:</strong> protect tangible, intangible, or mixed assets, whether people, property, or information.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Defines physical security as…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Physical Asset Protection Standard</strong> (PAP; ASIS International, 2021)</td>\n<td>Part of security that uses physical measures to: protect people; prevent unauthorized access to equipment, facilities, material, and <strong>documents</strong>; and protect them against a security incident.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>U.S. Department of Homeland Security</strong> (DHS)</td>\n<td>Part of <strong>homeland security</strong> that uses physical measures to: protect personnel; prevent unauthorized access to equipment, facilities, material, and documents; and defend them against <strong>espionage, sabotage, damage, and theft</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>This volume</strong> (Protection of Assets, <em>Physical Security</em>)</td>\n<td>Same as the PAP, but with <strong>information</strong> instead of documents.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Security, according to this volume:</strong> the condition of being protected against hazards, threats, risks, tampering, or loss.</li>\n<li>Physical security combines physical measures against the full range of <strong>realistic and credible</strong> threats.</li>\n<li>Usefulness of the definition in any project or program:<ul>\n<li><strong>Limit</strong> the scope: do not stray from the objective or mission.</li>\n<li><strong>Broaden</strong> the scope: include all relevant measures and techniques.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It keeps focus while allowing broad thinking, without being limited to a single tool such as video surveillance (<strong>Peterson, 2014</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the volume's definition says \"information\"; the PAP's and the DHS's say \"documents.\" Only the DHS's names espionage, sabotage, damage, and theft.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>The four Ds</strong> — they must be the basis of <strong>every</strong> physical security project. Each measure is valued for its ability to:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>D</th>\n<th>What the measure does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deter</strong></td>\n<td>Deter the adversary.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Detect</strong></td>\n<td>Detect an attack.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Delay</strong></td>\n<td>Delay an attack.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deny</strong></td>\n<td>Deny the adversary access to the target.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"physical-2-e1\">2.2 Functions of Physical Security</h3><p>An effective strategy uses <strong>seven basic functions</strong>, in an orchestrated and tailored way:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Definition and notes from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>Access control</strong></td>\n<td>Any technique to limit or manage access to an area, facility, enclosure, system, person, or other asset. Access is controlled for a person, a vehicle, or an object (package, weapon, explosive device, or monitoring device).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong>Deterrence</strong></td>\n<td>Barriers, technology, and procedures to convince the attacker that he or she will fail, and so avoid the site. <strong>It is the hardest measure to measure.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>Detection</strong></td>\n<td>Ability of the physical protection system to perceive that there is a possible intruder. On its own it is worth little: false positives depend directly on the system's sensitivity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>Assessment</strong></td>\n<td>Ability of the system to determine what was detected and decide whether to initiate a response.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong>Delay</strong></td>\n<td>Measures that slow the intruder's advance. Examples from the book: no cut/no climb fences, high-security locks, secure door installation and hardware, window bars.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><strong>Response</strong></td>\n<td>Actions to neutralize, contain, or mitigate an event. Often, the police respond and arrest the intruders.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><strong>Evidence gathering</strong></td>\n<td>Essential for prosecuting intruders. Every prosecution is good \"publicity\" for the site's security. It is usually CCTV (closed-circuit television) video. CCTV also supports other purposes, such as safety and operations.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In each project, consider <strong>all</strong> applicable measures. Value them for their contribution to the complete physical protection system.</li>\n<li>The measures are a <strong>menu of options</strong>. They are applied as <strong>building blocks</strong> in several <strong>layers of security</strong>.</li>\n<li>The selection of measures for a site must cover <strong>all</strong> the functions.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> do not confuse the 4 Ds (conceptual basis) with the 7 functions. Also, the book is not consistent: the text speaks of 7 functions; Figure 2.2 shows 4; and the paragraph before 2.3 mentions \"the four security functions.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Figure 2.1 — Measures to Employ in a Physical Security Strategy</strong> (measure × function matrix; \"x\" = the measure fulfills that function)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Access Control</th>\n<th>Deter</th>\n<th>Delay</th>\n<th>Assess</th>\n<th>Respond</th>\n<th>Evidence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Guards</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mobile Patrols</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CCTV</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FIDS (Fence Line Intrusion Detection System)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IDS (Intrusion Detection System)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Locks</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lights</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alarms</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LIDAR (Light Detection and Ranging)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>x</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fence/Wall</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td>x</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Only <strong>Guards</strong> mark all 6 columns. Mobile Patrols and CCTV follow, with 5 each.</li>\n<li>Reading note: the matrix was reconstructed from the position of each \"x\" in the PDF.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Figure 2.1 has no \"Detect\" column, even though detection is one of the 7 functions. It also clashes with the text: it puts FIDS and IDS under \"Delay\" and does not mark \"Delay\" for Locks or Fence/Wall. The text, by contrast, cites fences and high-security locks as delay measures. If that column were \"Detect,\" the marks would fit the text. Possible erratum; do not memorize the figure as a rule.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Figure 2.2 — Relationship of Physical Security Concepts, Functions, and Measures</strong> (four levels, from the conceptual to the peripheral)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Conceptual Basis</td>\n<td>Deter · Detect · Delay · Deny</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Functions of physical security (4)</td>\n<td>Control Access · Observe · Detect Events · Respond</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measures/Tools (21)</td>\n<td>Alarms · Access control · Barriers · Communications · Concierge · Doors/locks · Glass treatments · Incident management systems · Ingress/egress · Layout · Lighting · Natural surveillance · Patrol · Robotic systems · Screening · Security officer force · Sensors · Vaults · Video surveillance · Weapons · Working dogs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Peripheral Systems/Interfaces (5)</td>\n<td>Life safety systems · Building controls · IT infrastructure · Liaison relationships · Outsourced services</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Reading note: part of the text of this figure came with the font encoded in the PDF and was deciphered letter by letter. The initial capitals of Alarms, Access control, Barriers, Communications, and Building controls were inferred from context.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.2.1 Physical Security Measures</h4>\n<p>General features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are the <strong>hard framework</strong> of the physical protection system.</li>\n<li>They are the <strong>most visible</strong> part of the program and help greatly with deterrence.</li>\n<li>They usually operate in environmental extremes: cold, heat, and precipitation.</li>\n<li><strong>All</strong> are vulnerable to some type of attack.</li>\n<li>Choose them to meet the operational requirements. <strong>Test them regularly</strong> after installing them, especially if they have moving parts.</li>\n<li>The technology of attackers and defenders changes fast. Strategy for staying current: good relationships with reliable suppliers.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Barriers</strong></td>\n<td>Mostly structural, but they can be of any of the \"three formats.\" E.g., of a human barrier: a riot line or a human barricade. Broad category.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fencing</strong></td>\n<td>Consider alternatives to chain link according to purpose, application, environment, and objectives. Decorative fencing is increasingly used in low to moderate risk. It often meets the objectives; there are products that combine security and aesthetics.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bollards</strong></td>\n<td>Many styles and types. <strong>Fixed or active</strong> (e.g., retractable \"pop-up\"). Some are purely functional; others combine security and architectural appeal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Terrain</strong></td>\n<td>Natural barriers, highly effective in certain cases. They can be preexisting (entirely natural) or built on purpose.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Locks</strong></td>\n<td>Mechanical or electronic. They must fit doors, hardware, environment, and objectives. The <strong>door–lock mismatch</strong> is common: it lowers security, raises costs, and complicates maintenance. E.g.: if there are windows beside the door, a <strong>double-cylinder keyed deadbolt</strong> is advisable.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Design, architecture, and engineering</strong></td>\n<td>Structural measures built in by taking advantage of the design. E.g.: the layout or traffic pattern helps minimize the number of openings in the building. It is critical to get involved <strong>from the start</strong> of the project to include security in design and budget.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CPTED</strong> (Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design)</td>\n<td>Consider it in almost every project. It is often neglected or arrives late. It is a <strong>force multiplier</strong> and can reduce other physical measures, often more expensive.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Landscaping</strong></td>\n<td>Element of CPTED. It serves to deter, detect, and delay intruders. It guides pedestrian traffic and marks the boundary between <strong>public, semi-private, and private</strong> space.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lighting</strong></td>\n<td>Value it for its effect on deterrence, detection, and delay, and for its support of observation and response.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Glass treatments</strong></td>\n<td>According to the objective (e.g., mitigating explosions): films, glazing, tinting, and the type of glass. Also window treatments: blast curtains, blinds, and meshes.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says barriers can be of \"three formats,\" but does not list them there. From the rest of the chapter they appear to be structural, electronic, and human.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> a double-cylinder deadbolt requires a key to exit as well. It can conflict with life safety and egress codes; it is advisable to check local regulations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>2.2.2 Electronic Security Measures</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Surveillance</strong></td>\n<td>Although listed as electronic, it can also be <strong>natural</strong> (Chapter 10, CPTED). E.g.: employees who watch the area around their post, or a space designed for human observation. Electronic surveillance is more than cameras: power, field of view, getting the signal to where it is needed, camera control, analytics, storage, maintenance, and scalability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Access control</strong></td>\n<td>It is thought of as electronic, but can be natural, structural, human, or mixed/hybrid. Common electronic elements: card readers, keypads, biometric systems, and locks. Key: an access database and communication between reader, database (local or remote), and lock. Consider maintenance, database updating, configuration, and scalability. It evolves fast: facial recognition and smart cards.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Intrusion detection systems</strong> (IDS)</td>\n<td>They usually include <strong>sensors; alarms or annunciators; and a communication or transmission mechanism</strong>. Match the IDS to its purpose, to the type of facility or space, and to the environment. <strong>False positives and false negatives</strong> are a major problem. A poor initial design or installation can leave it totally ineffective.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Communications</strong></td>\n<td>Two fronts: communicating with employees and occupants in routine and emergency; and protocols so that electronic systems talk to each other. Systems cited: cameras, monitors, recorders, sensors, alarms, card readers, databases, electronic locks, control centers, and receiving stations. Plan for power outages, telecommunications failures, IT (information technology) network failures, users competing for the channel, and other factors.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>2.2.3 Personnel and Guard Force Security</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security officer force</strong></td>\n<td>Design it carefully so that it is effective and efficient. Planning factors (9): staffing, selection, training, equipment, post orders, specific duties, quality control, quality assurance, and supervision.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Other human support functions</strong></td>\n<td>Other departments take part: facilities, maintenance, reception, legal, risk management, and IT. Employees throughout the organization may have security duties in addition to their own. Planning must include this personnel; it can influence the structural and electronic systems in the plan.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Visitor management/control</strong></td>\n<td>Written logs or automated systems; usually the task of officers or reception. Automated ones are <strong>much more effective</strong>. They integrate with incident management, access control, and investigations. Their cost has dropped greatly. Do not leave it for last.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Badging</strong></td>\n<td>Identification strategy for: employees and staff; contractors or other regular visitors; other users; and occasional or one-time visitors. In many organizations, especially government, a badge gives physical and also <strong>logical</strong> access to IT systems. This dual use requires close, ongoing coordination between physical security and IT security.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security operations centers</strong></td>\n<td>Nerve centers of security information. Also called <strong>control centers</strong> and <strong>fusion centers</strong>. Take care with location, design, staffing, and use. They affect and are affected by the whole plan: structural measures, electronic systems, and the human element.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Weapons</strong></td>\n<td>Irrelevant in some environments; very important and even controversial in others. Topics: whether to arm officers and which tactics and tools the response force will use. Guiding questions: What would the response to a major incident look like? What will the response force do? What role will weapons have in the plan?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Incident management systems</strong></td>\n<td>Some see them as peripheral, but they are a <strong>key input</strong> to any risk assessment architecture. Uses below.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Uses of an effective incident management system (5):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Guide the response to an incident in real time.</li>\n<li>Support post-incident analysis and reduce recurrence.</li>\n<li>Inform the planning of physical security improvements or program changes.</li>\n<li>Evaluate the effect of the facility's design and layout on prevention and response.</li>\n<li>Compare characteristics of incidents among similar facilities, according to their physical security features.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Not everything depends on the security department</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Possible owner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Landscaping and natural or terrain barriers</td>\n<td>Facilities or building maintenance department</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction materials and methods</td>\n<td>General contractor or building owner/manager</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loading dock operations</td>\n<td>Logistics or transportation function</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Even so, physical measures must be coordinated with each security function and with all physical security planning.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-2-e2\">2.3 Peripheral Systems and Interfaces</h3><ul>\n<li>In modern facilities it is critical to address the relationship between security components and peripherals.</li>\n<li>Peripherals according to the text (6): <strong>life safety systems</strong>, <strong>building controls</strong>, <strong>IT infrastructure</strong>, <strong>liaison relationships</strong>, <strong>outsourced services</strong>, and <strong>policies and procedures</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text lists 6 peripherals; Figure 2.2 only 5 (it does not include policies and procedures).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Peripheral</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Life safety systems</td>\n<td>A major challenge in new construction, remodeling, or system upgrades: the <strong>conflict between security and life safety systems</strong>. National and international standards and local codes usually ease it. Sometimes compensating measures are needed; they must be recommended within the planning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Building controls</td>\n<td>Automatic elevator control; automatic or motion-activated lighting; climate control; automatic zone lockdowns; environmental monitoring (temperature, humidity, smoke, chemical, and other sensors). Like life safety, they usually interoperate with security management systems. Plan to avoid conflicts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IT infrastructure</td>\n<td>Shared or dedicated, it usually supports the electronic security systems. Assess its reliability and its security: \"the security of the security system.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liaison relationships, outsourced services, policies and procedures</td>\n<td>They can greatly influence the operation and effectiveness of physical security components, separately or as a system.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>All peripherals deserve close attention in any project.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-2-e3\">Key Terms</h3><p>physical security · security · Physical Asset Protection Standard (ASIS PAP-2021) · Department of Homeland Security (DHS) · internal security · espionage, sabotage, damage, theft · realistic and credible threats · four Ds (deter, detect, delay, deny) · access control · deterrence · detection · assessment · delay · response · evidence gathering · physical protection system · false positives / false negatives · menu of options · building-block · layers of security · FIDS · IDS · LiDAR · CCTV · hard framework · barriers · human barrier · fencing · chain link · decorative fencing · no cut/no climb fencing · bollards (fixed / active / pop-up) · terrain · double-cylinder keyed deadbolt · CPTED · force multiplier · landscaping · public / semi-private / private space · glass treatments · glazing · blast curtains · natural surveillance · card readers · biometrics · access database · annunciators · security officer force · post orders · quality control / quality assurance · visitor management · badging · logical access · security operations center · control center · fusion center · response force · incident management system · peripheral systems and interfaces · life safety systems · building controls · zone lockdown · IT infrastructure · liaison relationships · outsourced services</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"2.1 Definition and Purpose of Physical Security"},{"id":"e1","t":"2.2 Functions of Physical Security"},{"id":"e2","t":"2.3 Peripheral Systems and Interfaces"},{"id":"e3","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[]},"physical-3":{"html":"<h3 id=\"physical-3-e0\">Chapter Introduction (unnumbered in the book)</h3><ul>\n<li>It applies to any project: from the complete design of a system to the upgrade of a single component.</li>\n<li>Fit within ESRM (Enterprise Security Risk Management):<ol>\n<li>Understand the context.</li>\n<li>Identify the assets.</li>\n<li>Identify and prioritize security risks.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Basic tool: <strong>security risk assessment</strong> or <strong>security survey</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Patterson (2013):</strong> no security program without first identifying the assets, their threats, and how vulnerable they are to each threat.</li>\n<li>Problem: there are a great many models. They vary by sector, region or country, specialty, and even by consultant.</li>\n<li>National and international standard models exist, but here they are of little use because they are too general.</li>\n<li>The chapter offers a <strong>general framework</strong> (not a concrete model) for the risk assessment and a <strong>specific</strong> framework for the security survey.</li>\n<li>In the book, <strong>security survey = physical security assessment</strong> (synonyms).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Expected level of mastery</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Comprehensive security risk assessment</td>\n<td>Somewhat familiar</td>\n<td>General concept</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security survey</td>\n<td>Intimately familiar</td>\n<td>It is the basis of every physical security project. It is most of the fieldwork. It collects data and evidence that justify countermeasures</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"physical-3-e1\">3.1 General Risk Assessment Models and Considerations</h3><p><strong>Steps of a comprehensive risk assessment:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Step</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Identify the assets and assign them a value (absolute or relative)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Assess the relevant threats against those assets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Assess the vulnerabilities (vulnerability assessment)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Consider the impact of a loss event: attack, theft, or property damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Summarize and prioritize the risks found</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Recommend mitigation measures and merge them into a comprehensive protection strategy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Likelihood (or probability) factor:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many models include it. It is the probability that a loss event will occur in the future.</li>\n<li>It is based on:<ul>\n<li>historical data from the site;</li>\n<li>history of similar events at similar companies;</li>\n<li>makeup of the neighborhood and the immediate surroundings;</li>\n<li>general geographic location;</li>\n<li>political and social conditions;</li>\n<li>changes in the economy;</li>\n<li>other factors (the book leaves the list open).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Examples from the book:<ul>\n<li>A company in a flood-prone or coastal area: more likelihood of flooding than one inland. It applies even if it has never flooded, because the location favors it.</li>\n<li>A business with a crime history in and around the property: high likelihood of more crimes if it does not improve its security posture. It assumes the other factors (economic, social, political) remain constant.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conditions that increase assets' exposure to the risk of loss (ASIS ESRM Guideline 2019):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Factors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Physical</strong></td>\n<td>Types and location of facilities or campus; surroundings; volume of pedestrian or vehicle traffic; access by nonemployees needed to operate; operational technology or industrial control systems needed; sensitivity and criticality of processes and assets on site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nonphysical</strong></td>\n<td>Geopolitical landscape; culture; sector pressures; legal, regulatory, and compliance requirements; intensity of competition; the organization's mode of growth; speed of decision-making; willingness to adopt technology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Logical</strong></td>\n<td>Information and digital assets, plus the network or digital space that connects them with each other and with users and stakeholders. Examples: network infrastructure, connectivity, servers, workstations, other network devices, and endpoints</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Cyclical and continuous process:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The elements change over time. Best example: the rapid evolution of technology, especially electronic security systems (see Ch. 11, “Electronic Security Systems”).</li>\n<li>Also changing, sometimes without notice: asset value, threat environment, state of vulnerabilities, likelihood calculations, and impact of loss events. They must be monitored.</li>\n<li>Best method for monitoring and preparing: an <strong>ongoing risk assessment program</strong> (Peterson, 2014).</li>\n<li>Adopting ESRM pushes the security program toward constant improvement.</li>\n<li><strong>ESRM cycle</strong> (ASIS ESRM Guideline, 2019): identify and prioritize assets → identify and prioritize risks → mitigate the prioritized risks → continuous improvement.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in 3.1 the book words the cycle as if it were three steps (“assets and risks” together). In 3.2.5 it says mitigation is “one of four processes.” The four are those in the line above.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-3-e2\">3.2 Qualitative and Quantitative Methods</h3><ul>\n<li>Each component of the assessment is valued qualitatively or quantitatively.</li>\n<li>The value of assets is usually expressed in money, but it cannot always be. Typical case: intangibles such as intellectual property or workforce stability.</li>\n<li>Even when preferring monetary figures, it is hard to arrive at a defensible number.</li>\n<li>What is usually most suitable is to <strong>combine both</strong>. It depends on the strategic level, on what the executive who decides wants, and on the style of the security professional.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Qualitative analysis</th>\n<th>Quantitative analysis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition</td>\n<td>Any approach that does <strong>not</strong> use numbers to describe the components of risk</td>\n<td>Any approach that uses <strong>numerical measures</strong> for the value of assets or the level (severity or likelihood) of threats, vulnerabilities, impact, or loss events</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical scale</td>\n<td>Comparative terms: critical, high, medium, low, negligible</td>\n<td>From simple scales (e.g., 1 to 5) to statistical methods and mathematical formulas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When it applies</td>\n<td>Basic security applications</td>\n<td>Measuring the effectiveness of a physical protection system whose main functions are to detect, delay, and respond</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Advantages</td>\n<td>Simpler and faster. Its results are usually as meaningful as numerical ones</td>\n<td>Executives prefer graphs and tables that summarize a lot of data. The data are processed automatically with programs and algorithms</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blended approach</strong>: combine elements of both when appropriate.</li>\n<li>In any case, the most important things are:<ol>\n<li>clearly define each level or descriptor used;</li>\n<li>ensure the best possible quality of input data;</li>\n<li>display the information clearly: trends, curves, and ranges.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book puts “and” after the second point, as if there were two. There are three.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2.1 Assets</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Identifying assets is key to estimating the risk of a site.</li>\n<li>Difficulty: there is no agreement on what exactly an asset is.</li>\n<li>The security professional and the asset owner must agree <strong>at the start</strong> on what counts and what does not count as an asset.</li>\n<li>It is advisable to walk through the operating processes to detect assets. Many owners only know the ones that appear on the balance sheet.</li>\n<li><strong>Asset:</strong> anything that has tangible or intangible value to the company.<ul>\n<li>It is valued and prioritized in relation to the company's mission and objectives.</li>\n<li>The maturity of the valuation process depends on the available resources. Over time it matures and becomes an integral part of the organization.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Factors for setting an asset's value</strong> (nonexhaustive list):<ol>\n<li>Purchase or replacement cost.</li>\n<li>Operational impact of its being unavailable (usually identified with a business impact analysis).</li>\n<li>Effects of damage to the asset.</li>\n<li>Time needed to replace it.</li>\n<li>Reputational impact of its being unavailable.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Valuation:</strong> quantitative (numerical measurements), qualitative (interviews, surveys, industry publications), or mixed.</li>\n<li>Direct monetary value ≠ total value. An asset can be worth little in money and a lot in accumulated value to the organization (ASIS ESRM Guideline, 2019).</li>\n<li><strong>Criticality:</strong> the impact of loss measured in currency.<ul>\n<li>It depends on: the mission; the resources used to fulfill it; how those resources interact; and how the organization would cope with the loss or maintain business continuity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Ways to assign value: economic cost, reputation, value of the information, and regulatory compliance.</li>\n<li><strong>Costs to consider:</strong> immediate response and recovery; investigation; replacement; and <strong>indirect</strong> costs (often forgotten).</li>\n<li><strong>Indirect costs (the book's 19):</strong><ol>\n<li>Temporary leased facilities.</li>\n<li>Equipment rental or purchase.</li>\n<li>Alternate suppliers.</li>\n<li>Alternate carriers and logistics.</li>\n<li>Temporary warehouses.</li>\n<li>Special employee benefits.</li>\n<li>Psychological counseling and employee assistance.</li>\n<li>Loss of market share (temporary or permanent).</li>\n<li>Lower employee productivity.</li>\n<li>Higher insurance premiums.</li>\n<li>Temporary staff.</li>\n<li>Recruiting costs for the permanent workforce.</li>\n<li>More security spending (temporary or permanent).</li>\n<li>More communication capabilities.</li>\n<li>Data recovery and restart or reconfiguration of computer systems.</li>\n<li>Administrative support.</li>\n<li>More travel.</li>\n<li>Marketing and public relations.</li>\n<li>Redoing emergency and continuity plans.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Intangible and mixed assets:</strong> hard to value, but they suffer loss events and can greatly damage vitality and the mission. Executives must be educated about them.</li>\n<li><strong>Two ways to quantify value:</strong><ul>\n<li>Relative value: number from 1 (low) to 5 (high) according to priority.</li>\n<li>Cost-of-loss formula (Patterson 2013):</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">K</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9694em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2806em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">t</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">I</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>K</td>\n<td>Total cost of loss</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cp</td>\n<td>Permanent replacement cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ct</td>\n<td>Temporary substitute cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cr</td>\n<td>Total related costs: removing the old asset, installing the new one (open list in the book)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ci</td>\n<td>Lost income cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Available insurance or indemnity. It is <strong>subtracted</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Own example (outside the book): Cp = 10,000; Ct = 2,000; Cr = 1,000; Ci = 5,000; I = 8,000 → K = 10,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Four criteria for ranking assets by criticality:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>What it measures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Workforce</td>\n<td>Number and type of personnel located on site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Service delivery</td>\n<td>Percentage of total service delivery that depends on the asset</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Dependencies</td>\n<td>Importance of the asset to other assets of the organization or of the network</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Mission/objectives</td>\n<td>Overall importance of the asset to the business mission or objective, combining all factors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in criterion 3 the book says “within the organization of the network.” From context it should be read as “organization or network.”</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2.2 Evaluating Threats</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Threat, according to ASIS International 2020:</strong> any verbal or physical conduct that conveys intent to cause physical harm or to instill fear of suffering it. It also counts if it is reasonably perceived that way.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> that definition comes from the 2020 ASIS standard on workplace violence (Workplace Violence and Active Assailant Standard) and centers on people. Even so, the book includes natural threats. Later it speaks of inadvertent threats, which do not appear in its list. In practice there are three categories: manmade, natural, and inadvertent.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it is assessed</th>\n<th>Keys</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Manmade threats</strong> (intentional)</td>\n<td>By identifying and studying potential adversaries</td>\n<td>Think beyond the obvious: employees, terrorist organizations, organized crime, aggressive competitors, and activist groups (e.g., environmentalists). Growing challenge because of the global economy, worldwide demographic changes, and the <strong>asymmetric</strong> nature of today's threats (less conventional and harder to define). Adversaries are judged by their <strong>capabilities</strong> and their <strong>intentions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Natural threats</strong></td>\n<td>With historical trends and statistics</td>\n<td>Long-term data on climate and natural hazards by geographic area, terrain, and environment. Sometimes there are data by sector or type of facility. The unexpected tends to happen, so comprehensive contingency planning and some all-hazard preparedness are recommended</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Sources of information on adversary capabilities and intentions (11):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Prior history.</li>\n<li>Adversary sequence diagrams: they reflect the penetration paths into a closed building.</li>\n<li>Rhetoric of the organization.</li>\n<li>Public statements.</li>\n<li>Intelligence and other open sources.</li>\n<li>Internal communications (newsletters, websites, blog posts).</li>\n<li>Police reports.</li>\n<li>Crime analysis.</li>\n<li>Professional organizations.</li>\n<li>Automated databases.</li>\n<li>Threat assessment professionals.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>3.2.3 Vulnerabilities</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vulnerability, according to ASIS International 2017:</strong> state of being susceptible to harm or injury.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the citation “ASIS International 2017” does not appear in the chapter's references.</li>\n<li>Common approach: measure them by <strong>observability</strong> and <strong>exploitability</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of threat</th>\n<th>Observability</th>\n<th>Exploitability</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Adversary (intentional)</td>\n<td>The adversary's ability to see and identify the vulnerability. E.g.: a hole in a chain link fence is highly observable; a video camera that does not work is not</td>\n<td>The adversary's ability to take advantage of the vulnerability once he or she knows it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural</td>\n<td><strong>It is inverted:</strong> the ability of security personnel to observe, detect, or track an approaching threat (e.g., a storm). Requires early warning and notification mechanisms</td>\n<td>The natural threat's ability to damage the facility, the mission, or the organization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inadvertent</td>\n<td>The approach changes to two questions: Do security personnel know the vulnerabilities? Does the opportunity for a loss event depend on the nature of the mission, the operation, or the facility?</td>\n<td>The same two questions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Inadvertent threats and their vulnerabilities are the <strong>hardest</strong> to identify and measure. Even so, they must not be neglected.</li>\n<li>With this approach, mitigation is planned for the long term (strategic) and the immediate (tactical).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2.4 Risk Analysis</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Assets, threats, and vulnerabilities are combined. Then the potential impact is considered and prioritization is done according to the consequences of a loss event.</li>\n<li>The assessment levels (threat, vulnerability, impact) are best set <strong>by committee</strong>, especially in quantitative analyses.</li>\n<li>That committee is a multidisciplinary team of subject matter experts. That way the numbers are credible and justifiable.</li>\n<li><strong>Justifying the numbers</strong> is where clients, executives, and decision makers question the assessor most.</li>\n<li>One possible formula:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">e</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0197em;\">l</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">er</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">abi</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0197em;\">l</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.188em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">I</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.938em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1/3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Why it works:<ul>\n<li>It <strong>multiplies</strong> instead of adding. If any factor is 0, the risk is 0 (at that time and place).</li>\n<li>Each factor is rated from <strong>0 to 100</strong>. It is easy to understand because it resembles a percentage.</li>\n<li>The <strong>cube root</strong> returns the result to the 0–100 scale. That way it displays well in graphs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says “cubed root”; the correct term is “cube root,” exponent 1/3. In the PDF the exponent appears loose (“1/3”) after the parenthesis.</li>\n<li>Own example (outside the book): T = 80, V = 50, I = 40 → 80 × 50 × 40 = 160,000 → cube root ≈ 54.3.</li>\n<li>Presentation: it must help the decision maker understand and decide. Risks are ordered by priority or into categories, to show what to address first according to the assessor.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2.5 Risk Mitigation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is one of the <strong>four processes of the ESRM cycle</strong>.</li>\n<li>The approach is chosen according to the organization's goals and mission.</li>\n<li>It is not practical to attack all risks. Priority: those that can cause significant impact or damage to the mission.</li>\n<li><strong>Good practice:</strong> appropriate technologies + structural and architectural elements + the human element + strong policy and procedure controls.</li>\n<li><strong>Security-related options:</strong><ol>\n<li>Measures to reduce the risk of the event.</li>\n<li>Architectural improvements.</li>\n<li>Security systems.</li>\n<li>Policies and procedures.</li>\n<li>Management practices.</li>\n<li>Security staffing.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Other options:</strong><ul>\n<li><strong>Transfer</strong> the financial risk: insurance or contract clauses (e.g., indemnification clauses in security service contracts).</li>\n<li><strong>Accept</strong> the risk as a cost of doing business.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Some options will not be available because they are infeasible or too costly (in money or otherwise).</li>\n<li>Every option is evaluated for <strong>availability, affordability, and feasibility</strong> for the operation.</li>\n<li>Frequent consideration besides cost: does it substantially interfere with the operation?</li>\n<li>Cases from the book:<ul>\n<li>Store with shoplifting: closing it keeps out the thieves, but also the customers; it is infeasible. Alternative: electronic article surveillance with VHF (very high frequency) or microwave technology.</li>\n<li>Business open to the public that tightens access control so much that it scares off customers: it loses business.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Challenge: balancing a sound strategy with operational needs and with the <strong>psychological impact</strong> on the people affected.</li>\n<li><strong>When choosing countermeasures (Patterson, 2013):</strong><ol>\n<li>The effectiveness of each countermeasure and of the complete system depends on the adversary and the threat.</li>\n<li>Different threats require different levels of countermeasure effectiveness and system performance.</li>\n<li>If the threat becomes more sophisticated, the effectiveness of countermeasures must rise; if not, the extra risk is managed by other means.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2.6 Leveraging Outside Expertise</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is usually advisable to add a well-chosen outside provider or consultant to the assessment.</li>\n<li>Advantages:<ul>\n<li>Fresh eyes, without prior opinions, prejudices, preferences, or internal politics.</li>\n<li>Experience and up-to-date knowledge of best practices, products, and industry standards.</li>\n<li>With more regulation and government oversight, an outside assessment can <strong>strengthen the company's position in an audit</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Selection criteria: specialty, experience, professionalism, and <strong>degree of independence</strong>.</li>\n<li>There must be clear agreement among all parties on purpose, scope, and expected product.</li>\n<li>The consultant must design the system <strong>from the start</strong> of the project. If not, <strong>scope creep</strong> appears: small undocumented changes with a large impact on the project.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-3-e3\">3.3 Physical Security Assessments</h3><ul>\n<li>According to the ESRM guideline, risks are identified and assessed with a risk assessment process.</li>\n<li>The chosen methodology must <strong>align with the organization's other risk frameworks and tools</strong>.</li>\n<li>It must examine:<ol>\n<li>general and specific threats;</li>\n<li>vulnerabilities;</li>\n<li>criticality of the assets;</li>\n<li>potential consequences;</li>\n<li>likelihood;</li>\n<li>severity;</li>\n<li>velocity.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>It must cover the risks to all relevant assets, whether <strong>inherent or residual</strong>.</li>\n<li>(Outside the book) Inherent risk: before applying controls. Residual risk: what remains after the controls. Velocity: how quickly a risk produces its impact.</li>\n<li>The physical security assessment or security survey is the tool for the risk to <strong>physical (tangible)</strong> assets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.3.1 What Is a Security Survey?</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The concept of <strong>adequacy</strong> is weighing more and more. Reason: negligent security litigation is growing.</li>\n<li>Around the world, courts and regulators increasingly decide what is and is not adequate security.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two useful definitions:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Definition (paraphrase)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS International, Physical Asset Protection Standard (2021): “Security assessment (survey)”</td>\n<td>Thorough physical examination of a facility, its systems, and procedures. It serves to evaluate the current level of security, locate deficiencies, and gauge the degree of protection needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Momboisse (1977), classic definition cited by Broder (2012)</td>\n<td>Critical <strong>on-site</strong> examination to learn the current state of security, identify <strong>deficiencies or excesses</strong>, determine the protection needed, and recommend improvements for the overall security of the operation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Purposes of the security survey</strong> (thorough on-site examination):<ol>\n<li>Determine and document the current security posture.</li>\n<li>Identify deficiencies <strong>and excesses</strong> in existing measures.</li>\n<li>Compare the current posture with the appropriate level of security or protection.</li>\n<li>Recommend improvements to the overall situation.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Done well, it benefits the professional (often together with a design team) and the client. Result: more security (less risk) for occupants and users.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key difference:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Comprehensive risk assessment</th>\n<th>Security survey</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Focuses <strong>equally</strong> on assets, threats, vulnerabilities, and consequences</td>\n<td>Puts <strong>more emphasis on vulnerabilities</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Its appraisal component consists of developing and communicating improvement recommendations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Factors to review when considering vulnerabilities (Patterson 2013):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lack of redundancy or backup in critical functions or systems.</li>\n<li>Single points of failure.</li>\n<li>Collocation of critical systems, organizations, or components.</li>\n<li>Insufficient response capability to recover from an attack.</li>\n<li>Ease of the aggressor's access to the facility.</li>\n<li>Insufficient security measures.</li>\n<li>Presence of hazardous materials.</li>\n<li>Possible collateral damage from other businesses in the area.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Cost-benefit analysis:</strong> usually part of the assessment.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Estimate the real cost of implementing the program.</li>\n<li>Compare it with the projected impact: loss reduction, financial savings, acquisition, life cycle, replacement, or other measures.</li>\n<li>Example (Patterson 2013): it makes no sense to spend <strong>$100,000</strong> on equipment to prevent the theft of a <strong>$1,000</strong> item. Better to insure it or move it to a safer place.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.3.2 Choosing a Physical Security Assessment Methodology</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>There are almost infinite methodologies. Certain general protocols give <strong>consistency and repeatability</strong>.</li>\n<li>Methodologies can be used alone or combined.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two perspectives on topics to review in a survey:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Perspective 1</th>\n<th>Perspective 2</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>CPTED (Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design)</td>\n<td>Site topography</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access control</td>\n<td>Monitoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intrusion detection systems</td>\n<td>Access points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security officer force</td>\n<td>Building layout and construction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communications</td>\n<td>Blast protection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emergency plans</td>\n<td>Lighting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identification approach</td>\n<td>Surveillance systems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Checklists:</strong> they help avoid forgetting key elements and give the survey a sequence. But they must <strong>not</strong> be the center or the entirety of the assessment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Methodologies:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Outside-Inward</th>\n<th>Inside-Outward</th>\n<th>Functional / Security Discipline</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Team's role</td>\n<td><strong>Adversary</strong> (perpetrator). It is a penetration test</td>\n<td><strong>Defender</strong> (security professional)</td>\n<td>Analyst of each discipline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Path</td>\n<td>From outside (where the public circulates freely) → outer perimeter (fence, natural barrier, building wall, or other barrier) → outer, middle, and inner layers → asset</td>\n<td>From the asset or target → toward the outer perimeter</td>\n<td>Each function or discipline separately; then the findings are cross-checked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Question at each layer</td>\n<td>“How do I defeat or evade this layer's measures and advance toward the asset?”</td>\n<td>How do the measures operate, how effective are they, how do they contribute to deter-detect-delay-deny, and how can the layer be improved?</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommendations</td>\n<td>“What measure would stop me or slow me so much that I would abandon the attack?”</td>\n<td>Several solutions per layer: architectural elements, structural security, CPTED, electronic security systems, officer support, policies and procedures (open list in the book)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extras</td>\n<td>Considers the criminal behavior of the surroundings, including actual crimes outside the facility. Thinks like the adversary against the 4 Ds: deter, detect, delay, deny</td>\n<td>Analyzes <strong>interdependencies</strong> in each layer. E.g.: “How would a power outage affect it?” or “If a master key is stolen, how many layers does the intruder cross before being stopped or detected?”</td>\n<td>Integrate with environmental factors, peripheral risks (neighbors), and policies and procedures</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Layers of security (examples valid for outside-inward and inside-outward), from the inside out:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Typical measures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Asset/target</td>\n<td>Sensors, alarms, surveillance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Container, safe, or vault</td>\n<td>Walls or sides, locks, sensors, alarms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Controlled or restricted area</td>\n<td>Walls, doors, locks, surveillance, sensors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Security desk or entry control point</td>\n<td>Security officer, turnstile, access control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Building perimeter</td>\n<td>Walls, doors, locks, surveillance, sensors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Secure compound or campus</td>\n<td>Surveillance, distance, lighting, patrolling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Property perimeter or fence line</td>\n<td>Sensors, entry control, patrols, barriers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Neighborhood or surroundings</td>\n<td>Police patrols, neighborhood watch, observation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>They are only examples. The actual layers depend on the type of facility and the level of security. A shopping mall differs greatly from a government facility or a university campus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Disciplines of the functional method (in the book's order):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Security Architecture and Engineering (Ch. 7).</li>\n<li>Structural Security Measures (Ch. 9): barriers; locking mechanisms; lighting.</li>\n<li>Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design (Ch. 10).</li>\n<li>Electronic Security Systems (Ch. 11): access control systems; physical intrusion detection systems; surveillance systems; communication and annunciation systems; security of the electronic security systems on an IT backbone.</li>\n<li>Security Officers and the Human Element (Ch. 12).</li>\n<li>Almost all approaches are conceptually consistent. They differ in details of method or style.</li>\n<li>Conclusion: protocols must be <strong>systematic, consistent, and repeatable</strong>, even if the same model is not used in every case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Security Analysis Approaches (analysis tools):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fishbone diagrams, Five Whys, MoSCoW method, and more (open list in the book).</li>\n<li><strong>SWOT</strong> (strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, threats): complements the preceding approaches.<ul>\n<li>It originated in business management and adapts easily to security.</li>\n<li>Walker (2021): business situational analysis; strategic assessment of key internal and external factors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Positive attributes</th>\n<th>Negative attributes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Internal</strong> (building, campus, facility, or security program)</td>\n<td>Strengths</td>\n<td>Weaknesses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>External</strong> (may or may not be under the control of the professional, the organization, or the owner or manager)</td>\n<td>Opportunities</td>\n<td>Threats</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Figure 3.2 (SWOT Analysis Diagram):</strong> 2×2 grid. Top row: Strengths and Weaknesses. Bottom row: Opportunities and Threats. Labels “Positive Attributes” and “Negative Attributes.”</li>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> the figure's labels come out with a font encoded in the PDF; “Positive/Negative Attributes” was deciphered from the illegible text. In the figure's text there are no internal/external labels; that split comes from the body of the chapter.</li>\n<li><strong>“Threat” in SWOT</strong> is not necessarily a security threat (terrorist or criminal). It can be the adverse effect of a power or network outage on security systems, or the risk of many false alarms in an intrusion detection system. It can also be a security threat: both senses must be distinguished.</li>\n<li><strong>“Opportunity”:</strong> e.g., a new security technology becoming available, or the possibility of the company entering a new market.</li>\n<li>Usefulness: thinking creatively about how to do the assessment or how to structure a security program. More detail in POA (Protection of Assets): Business Principles.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text refers to “Figure 3-2” and the caption says “Figure 3.2.” The chapter has no Figure 3.1.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-3-e4\">3.4 General Guidelines – Areas to Assess</h3><ul>\n<li>These are general guidelines; they do not apply to every facility.</li>\n<li><strong>General team sequence:</strong><ol>\n<li>Assess the surroundings, the neighbors, and possible peripheral risks.</li>\n<li>Walk the perimeter: fence lines, barriers, and gates (vehicle, rail, and pedestrian).</li>\n<li>Inspect exterior enclosures, power sources, storage tanks, boiler rooms, and storage of flammable liquids or hazardous materials (HAZMAT).</li>\n<li>Also inspect utility connections, similar spaces, building exteriors, rooftops, tunnels, and underground accesses.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rings of protection, typical case:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ring</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1st (outer)</td>\n<td>Property perimeter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2nd (middle)</td>\n<td>Building perimeter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3rd (inner)</td>\n<td>Internal controls</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>3.4.1 Typical Areas and Items to Assess</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It is not a checklist:</strong> it is a guide to possible areas, items, and topics.</li>\n<li>Each item is evaluated for: suitability to the situation, age, operability, maintenance, interoperability, aesthetics, and consistency with the current use of the space.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Items to review</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Barriers</strong></td>\n<td>Gates; fences and fence top guards; bollards; other access barriers; natural barriers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Doors</strong></td>\n<td>Construction (wood, metal, glass, solid core; open list in the book) and direction of swing; frames; proper use of <strong>astragals</strong> (molding on the edge of the door that covers the gap between two leaves); hinges and hinge pins; locks and bolt travel (lock-throws)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Windows</strong></td>\n<td>Accessibility from ground level; materials and glazing; locks; frames; protection (bars, meshes, grilles; open list)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Other Openings</strong> (accessibility and protection)</td>\n<td>Manholes; skylights; roof hatches; ventilation or air-conditioning grilles and ducts; penthouses and access to penthouse, roof, or terrace; sidewalk grates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Locks</strong></td>\n<td>Types suited to the application (quality and level of security; master-keyed; interchangeable cores); sufficiency; maintenance; key and card control protocol (responsibility and policy; records and inventory; key recovery procedures; change when appropriate, e.g., due to turnover of key personnel or after a theft or break-in)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Safes and Containers</strong></td>\n<td>Type and rating suited to the use; control of combinations and keys; alarms (if applicable); location and visibility; weight and anchoring (how easy they are to lift and carry out)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lighting</strong></td>\n<td>Sufficiency (type, beam direction, controls); backup and emergency lighting; wiring (integrity and protection); inspections and maintenance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alarm (Physical Intrusion Detection) Systems</strong></td>\n<td>Contracts (competitive price; up to date; in the facility's best interest; liability or hold harmless clauses if applicable; cancellation clauses); monitoring (central station; direct to police or fire department; local security personnel); response; sensors (type, range, coverage, maintenance, updates); signal integrity; certification (e.g., UL approval, Underwriters Laboratories); access authorizations; inspection, maintenance, and testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Electronic Security Systems (Other)</strong></td>\n<td>Access control (hardware and software; database management; application and location; maintenance and inspection; scalability); video surveillance; correct equipment and application or planning (transmission; monitoring and storage; system management; maintenance and testing); mass communication and notification systems; watch tour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security Officer Services</strong></td>\n<td>Staffing, schedules, and shifts; duties and tasks (patrol, fixed post, inspection, access control; open list); requirements, screening, training, and licenses; initial and recurrent training, and exercises; incident management system and automated visitor management; equipment, uniforms, and personal gear (including communications); supervision and quality assurance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vehicle, Traffic, and Parking Controls</strong></td>\n<td>Entry and exit traffic patterns; parking areas; vehicle registration and control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Protection of Utilities</strong> (electricity, oil, gas, water, and the like)</td>\n<td>Location and physical protection; access control; backup or emergency sources; protection of telecommunications and data lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Visitor Management</strong></td>\n<td>Entry and exit points for visitors, delivery people, and contractors; identification systems (cards, passes; open list); entry and exit log, automated or manual; monitoring of entries and exits; escort procedures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Package/Mail Handling</strong></td>\n<td>Control and protection (incoming and outgoing); location of the mailroom and its access control; equipment and instructions for protection and inspection; receiving and distribution procedures; spot checks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Signage:</strong> standardized, easy to read and understand, visible (unobstructed), and at eye level. It must also:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>use images and letters large enough to be read from a distance;</li>\n<li>use colors that stand out against the surroundings;</li>\n<li>be well lit;</li>\n<li>be kept clean and clear;</li>\n<li>be in the right place according to its relevance (entrances, areas).</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>3.4.2 Tests</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The survey may include tests of certain systems and procedures.</li>\n<li>They must be fully coordinated with the building owner or manager and, if applicable, with outside agencies (first responders, alarm monitoring services).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>What is done</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Shipping and receiving</td>\n<td>Physically observe selected shipments (incoming and outgoing) and check them against bills of lading or inventory records</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alarms</td>\n<td>Activate an intrusion alarm; evaluate the response and the reaction of occupants and officers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Computer/server room security</td>\n<td>Attempt to gain access during working and nonworking hours to test security and access controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>General access controls</td>\n<td>Attempt to enter the facility and internal areas during normal hours and outside them. See whether it succeeds and whether employees later challenge the “intruders”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the shipping test the book says “bills of lading of inventory records.” From context it is “or.”</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-3-e5\">3.5 Applying Assessment Results</h3><ul>\n<li>The report must give the <strong>risk owner</strong> enough understanding of the risk and of how it can affect the mission.</li>\n<li>The deepest study is useless if the results are not reported or applied well.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Five criteria of a good report (Broder, 2012):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Accuracy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Clarity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Conciseness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Timeliness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Slant or pitch</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The first four are straightforward. <strong>Slant/pitch</strong> is the tone of the report:<ul>\n<li>Courteous; without being picky or focusing on minor details.</li>\n<li>Without a “gotcha” tone that highlights how bad the posture or strategy is.</li>\n<li>Centered on <strong>how to improve</strong> and <strong>why it matters</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tailored to its audience, but useful both to senior management and to those who will implement.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Roles:</strong> the risk owner is responsible for mitigation and owner of the plan. The security team advises and supports.</li>\n<li>Highlight the <strong>positive</strong>: that way what works is not changed or eliminated, and best practices and effective measures are pointed out.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book repeats that paragraph twice in a row (editing erratum).</li>\n<li>Assessments are not paper exercises or box-checking formalities (square fillers).</li>\n<li>ASIS International Risk Assessment Standard (2015): the risk assessment is the <strong>cornerstone</strong> for deciding with information how to address uncertainty in achieving objectives.</li>\n<li>Solutions must be orchestrated thoughtfully. They must not be a reaction to a loss event or to attractive electronic systems promoted by vendors.</li>\n<li>Uses beyond specific projects (renovations, designs, system upgrades):<ul>\n<li>Large organizations: a coordinated assessment program benefits the entire company.</li>\n<li>Small organizations: review and compare reports periodically to inform management and generate improvement ideas.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Automated incident management system:</strong> a tool for organizing and sharing reports.<ul>\n<li>McIlravey (2014): every risk assessment architecture has components that depend on incident management data (historical and empirical).</li>\n<li>Those data include: history of loss events; threat frequency analysis; single and annual loss expectancy; impact assessment.</li>\n<li>Storing survey reports in the same database improves filing, searching, and correlation with daily incidents and investigations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Annual loss expectancy</strong> according to the book:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Cost of incident impact</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Frequency of occurrence</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>(Outside the book) The usual form is ALE = SLE × ARO: single loss expectancy times annualized rate of occurrence. Own example: a $5,000 incident that occurs 4 times a year → ALE = $20,000.</li>\n<li>Summary: the value of an assessment grows with reports, dissemination, good use of results, and updating. It applies to internal and external (consultant) assessments.</li>\n<li>Objective: turn the results into <strong>actionable intelligence</strong>, immediate and long-term, and not leave them gathering dust on a shelf.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-3-e6\">3.6 Automated Assessment Tools</h3><ul>\n<li>There are many on the market. The key word is <strong>assist</strong>: the software must not be the only element.</li>\n<li>Main problem: many do not handle intangible factors or hard-to-quantify information well. Example: the <strong>character or nature</strong> of a risk.</li>\n<li>Peter Drucker (1970), on long-term planning: it is not enough to assess the <strong>magnitude</strong> of the risk; above all its <strong>character</strong> matters. It applies equally to physical security risks.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages (arguments against)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>They store, process, and manipulate large volumes of data</td>\n<td>People without asset protection knowledge may believe that buying and installing software is enough to produce a valid assessment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They compare related data and project the benefit of different protection options</td>\n<td>Commercial software may be too expensive for some organizations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Useful when there are many similar assessments or several complex ones</td>\n<td>It may be unnecessarily complex</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Systematic, repeatable process: similar results across assessments</td>\n<td>It may not consider nonquantifiable characteristics with great weight in risk, such as the personality or culture of the organization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assessments are kept and compared with earlier studies</td>\n<td>They do not always cover all security risks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Examples from the book (US):</strong><ul>\n<li>Federal Protective Service: uses software to assess hundreds of federal government facilities.</li>\n<li>Interagency Security Committee: federal body created by <strong>Presidential Executive Order 12977</strong> to set security standards for federal facilities. It uses different software.</li>\n<li>(Outside the book) Executive Order 12977 dates from 1995, after the Oklahoma City bombing.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>When they are useful:</strong> <strong>low-risk</strong> scenarios, or when the assessor knows little about security risk and wants to set a <strong>baseline</strong> according to best practices.</li>\n<li>Conclusion: the user must know how to calculate risk as a function of <strong>threat, vulnerability, and consequence</strong>.</li>\n<li>That requires a professional who understands the site's threats, the vulnerabilities to them, and the consequences of not mitigating them.</li>\n<li>Key to effective use: trained professional + credible, reproducible, and defensible assessment process + the tool's technology.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-3-e7\">Key Terms</h3><p>security risk assessment · security survey · physical security assessment · ESRM cycle · asset · intangible asset · mixed asset · asset owner · business impact analysis · criticality · indirect costs · cost-of-loss formula · loss event · likelihood · threat · manmade threats · natural threats · inadvertent threats · asymmetric threats · adversary sequence diagram · all-hazard preparedness · vulnerability · observability · exploitability · qualitative analysis · quantitative analysis · blended approach · risk analysis · subject matter experts · cube root · risk mitigation · risk transfer · risk acceptance · indemnification clause · electronic article surveillance · countermeasures · outside expertise · scope creep · inherent risk · residual risk · velocity · adequacy · negligent security litigation · deficiencies and excesses · appraisal · single point of failure · collocation · cost-benefit analysis · checklist · outside-inward methodology · inside-outward methodology · functional (security discipline) methodology · penetration test · deter-detect-delay-deny · layers of security · rings of protection · interdependencies · SWOT analysis · fishbone diagram · Five Whys · MoSCoW method · astragal · lock-throw · master-keyed · interchangeable core · hold harmless clause · central station · watch tour · mass notification · spot checks · bills of lading · risk owner · accuracy, clarity, conciseness, timeliness, slant or pitch · square fillers · automated incident management system · single loss expectancy · annual loss expectancy · actionable intelligence · automated assessment tools · baseline security · Federal Protective Service · Interagency Security Committee · Executive Order 12977</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"Chapter Introduction"},{"id":"e1","t":"3.1 General Risk Assessment Models and Considerations"},{"id":"e2","t":"3.2 Qualitative and Quantitative Methods"},{"id":"e3","t":"3.3 Physical Security Assessments"},{"id":"e4","t":"3.4 General Guidelines – Areas to Assess"},{"id":"e5","t":"3.5 Applying Assessment Results"},{"id":"e6","t":"3.6 Automated Assessment Tools"},{"id":"e7","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"3.1 General Risk Assessment Models and Considerations","h":"in 3.1 the book words the cycle as if it were three steps (“assets and risks” together). In 3.2.5 it says mitigation is “one of four processes.” The four are those in the line above."},{"s":"3.2 Qualitative and Quantitative Methods","h":"the book puts “and” after the second point, as if there were two. There are three."},{"s":"3.2 Qualitative and Quantitative Methods","h":"in criterion 3 the book says “within the organization of the network.” From context it should be read as “organization or network.”"},{"s":"3.2 Qualitative and Quantitative Methods","h":"that definition comes from the 2020 ASIS standard on workplace violence (Workplace Violence and Active Assailant Standard) and centers on people. Even so, the book includes natural threats. Later it speaks of inadvertent threats, which do not appear in its list. In practice there are three categories: manmade, natural, and inadvertent."},{"s":"3.2 Qualitative and Quantitative Methods","h":"the citation “ASIS International 2017” does not appear in the chapter's references."},{"s":"3.2 Qualitative and Quantitative Methods","h":"the book says “cubed root”; the correct term is “cube root,” exponent 1/3. In the PDF the exponent appears loose (“1/3”) after the parenthesis."},{"s":"3.3 Physical Security Assessments","h":"the text refers to “Figure 3-2” and the caption says “Figure 3.2.” The chapter has no Figure 3.1."},{"s":"3.4 General Guidelines – Areas to Assess","h":"in the shipping test the book says “bills of lading of inventory records.” From context it is “or.”"},{"s":"3.5 Applying Assessment Results","h":"the book repeats that paragraph twice in a row (editing erratum)."}]},"physical-4":{"html":"<h3 id=\"physical-4-e0\">4.1 Understanding Metrics</h3><ul>\n<li>Starting motto: what is not measured cannot be managed.</li>\n<li>Context: tight budgets and more regulation. Management demands hard data and ROI to justify funds and resources.</li>\n<li>The budget limits the design of the physical security system.</li>\n<li>Performance metrics serve to:<ul>\n<li>show the status of the program;</li>\n<li>identify performance trends;</li>\n<li>demonstrate ROI or value-added, within the existing corporate culture.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>They are an important part of an ESRM (Enterprise Security Risk Management) program (Allen &amp; Loyear, 2018).<ul>\n<li>Senior management or the risk owner must always know two things: how security helps protect the business and its mission, and what the current level of risk is.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Definitions by Rathburn (2009)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Metric</td>\n<td>A measure based on a reference with at least two points (e.g., quantity over time). Its main goal is to give insight into performance and operations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security</td>\n<td>Protection from danger, or absence of danger. A security metric indicates the degree of security against a reference point. It also indicates what to do to avoid danger.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Metrics measure the effectiveness and efficiency of operations over time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Limits and challenges of metrics</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the organization has not suffered harm or been the target of attacks, success is hard to measure.</li>\n<li>Williams &amp; Schultz (2016): the emphasis on metrics is sometimes poorly focused.<ul>\n<li>Risk: executing a bad process well. The numbers look good, but they measure an immature or broken process.</li>\n<li>Example: IT boasts of having cleaned 17,000 viruses, but 5,000 got past it because of a deficient process or scope.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Lesson: know what is measured, the quality of those metrics, and what is NOT measured.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text cites William and Schultz (2016); in the references it appears as Williams, T. and Schultz, T. (2016), <em>Metrics and the Maturity Mindset</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.1.1 Benefits of a Security Metrics Program</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Metrics make it possible to:<ul>\n<li>compare the effectiveness of the different components of the program;</li>\n<li>measure the performance of a specific system, product, or process;</li>\n<li>evaluate the response capability of security personnel.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Properly applied, what is learned helps the security professional to (10 benefits):<ol>\n<li>Better understand performance.</li>\n<li>Identify potential risks within the program.</li>\n<li>Detect problems and broken internal processes.</li>\n<li>Measure internal compliance with the organization's policy.</li>\n<li>Make better use of the security system's current capabilities.</li>\n<li>Measure the program against established references (benchmarks).</li>\n<li>Improve accountability.</li>\n<li>Communicate program performance.</li>\n<li>Drive performance improvement.</li>\n<li>Justify the allocation of resources.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.1.2 Designing a Metrics Program</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Order: first management understands what metrics are and how they are applied. Then the elements of the program that will yield useful metrics are chosen.</li>\n<li>Planning at the start avoids a lot of frustration later.</li>\n<li>Main tool: the measurement framework. It guides what performance means and what leads to sustainable success (Frost, 2007).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Research sponsored by the ASIS Foundation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Work: <em>Persuading Senior Management with Effective, Evaluated Security Metrics</em> (Ohlhausen, Poore, McGarvey &amp; Anderson, 2014). Free download at www.asisonline.org.</li>\n<li>Three products:<ol>\n<li><strong>Security MET (Security Metrics Evaluation Tool):</strong> self-administered tool to develop, evaluate, and improve metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Library of metric descriptions,</strong> each evaluated with the Security MET criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Guidelines for effective use of metrics</strong> to inform and persuade senior management, with emphasis on organizational risk and ROI.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Purpose of the Security MET: identify the strengths and weaknesses of a metric so the weaknesses can be corrected.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Security MET criteria group</th>\n<th>Criteria (9 in total)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Technical Criteria</td>\n<td>reliability · validity · generalizability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational (Security) Criteria</td>\n<td>cost · timeliness · manipulation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic (Corporate) Criteria</td>\n<td>return on investment (ROI) · organizational relevance · communication</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>For each criterion, the MET offers: definition, illustration of the concept, behavioral summary scale, and sample applications.</li>\n<li>It includes a score sheet. A low score on a criterion indicates where to improve the metric.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Enterprise Performance Framework (Frost, 2007) — Figure 4.1</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Idea</th>\n<th>Question it answers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Effectiveness</td>\n<td>How well do systems and programs operate? E.g., at detecting, delaying, or annunciating.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Efficiency</td>\n<td>How fast do programs respond to alarms, incidents, and customer requests?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic improvement</td>\n<td>How well aligned are security objectives with the organization's objectives?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Labels of Figure 4.1 (text recovered from a poorly encoded font):<ul>\n<li>Top goal: Sustained Quality Performance.</li>\n<li>Operational Performance (managing today's business): Effectiveness → How do we accomplish the mission?; Efficiency → How are we operating?</li>\n<li>Strategic Performance (preparing for future business): Strategic Initiatives → How do we prepare for tomorrow?</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> exact arrangement of boxes and arrows in Figure 4.1.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text calls the third idea Strategic improvement; the figure labels it Strategic Initiatives.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Evaluating each element of the program</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>After choosing the framework, each element of the program is evaluated from its core functions. Examples from the book: physical security, personnel security, information protection, and emergency management.</li>\n<li>Two questions per element:<ol>\n<li>What primary and secondary functions does it perform? They usually appear in the unit's mission statement.</li>\n<li>With what procedures, processes, tools, and resources does it carry them out?</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Example with the physical security program:<ul>\n<li>Function: protect assets by coordinating and integrating everything needed. Goal: create, maintain, and improve the capability to prevent, prepare for, mitigate, and respond to unauthorized access.</li>\n<li>Tools and resources:</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Resource</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Systems</td>\n<td>intrusion detection systems, access control, CCTV (closed-circuit television), visitor management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personnel</td>\n<td>professional support staff (contractor and department), security officer force, other full-time employees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Regulations</td>\n<td>compliance requirements; federal, state, and local regulations; policies and standards</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Two principles for deciding which metrics to collect</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Principle</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Start small</td>\n<td>Security data are messy and there is no easy way to organize them. That is why few departments collect data, and even fewer act on them (McIlravey, 2009). Modern software can already collect and sort data. A few initial data points show the effort the full program will require.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Collect relevant data</td>\n<td>Is the information useful to anyone? Some metrics only interest the manager and his or her team, and must not be circulated. It must pass the <em>who cares?</em> test (Rathburn, 2009). Without a consumer with a defined need, the metric only adds noise.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A metrics program wastes resources if the measures are not defined, bounded, collected, analyzed, and applied well.</li>\n<li>That is why it should only be created after serious introspection and planning.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>SMART metrics (Payne, 2006)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Letter</th>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>What it requires</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>Specific</td>\n<td>It matters to someone and gives him or her clear, actionable intelligence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>Measurable</td>\n<td>The formula gives a quantifiable value; preferably a whole number or a percentage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>Attainable</td>\n<td>If gathering it takes too much time, it loses effectiveness. It may be better to use that time to fix known problems.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>Repeatable</td>\n<td>If it is not produced consistently and uniformly, it cannot be compared with anything, not even with itself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>Time-dependent</td>\n<td>It is always collected over the same periods (day, week, month); otherwise it gives inaccurate results.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in Payne's SMART, R = Repeatable and T = Time-dependent. In the classic management SMART they are Relevant and Time-bound (outside the book).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-4-e1\">4.2 Physical Security Metrics</h3><ul>\n<li>Three main components of the physical security program: systems, personnel and compliance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2.1 Physical Security Systems Metrics</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Applies, e.g., to the PACS (physical access control system) and video surveillance.</li>\n<li>Useful perspective: operational efficiency.</li>\n<li>The SOC (security operations center) monitors and responds to alarms, among other tasks.</li>\n<li>An inefficient system floods the SOC with alarms and notices. The SOC does not respond in time and, over time, the alarms become noise.</li>\n<li>Goal of the metrics: reduce the noise on the operator's screen so that only legitimate alarms and notices get through.</li>\n<li>Modern PACS and video systems generate many user-defined alarms and system notices, with reports by alarm type.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What can be measured? Common alarm types</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Alarm</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Causes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Forced door</td>\n<td>Entry through a controlled door without authorization or without an authorized device.</td>\n<td>1) User error: nighttime office cleaning staff who enter with a physical key instead of the tenant's card. 2) Malicious intent: someone forces their way in (e.g., kicking the door). 3) System device malfunction (hard to diagnose; frequent at the start of the program): faulty door position switch; faulty exit device; misaligned door; system programming error.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Door held open</td>\n<td>The predefined bypass time of the position switch expires. The PACS considers the door held or propped open.</td>\n<td>1) User error: opens with a valid card and props the door so others can pass, typical at events with visitors without cards. Also out of courtesy, by holding it for someone else and exceeding the bypass time. 2) System device malfunction (hard to diagnose; common at the start): misaligned position switch; misaligned door; error when programming the bypass duration.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unauthorized access attempts</td>\n<td>Due to user error, programming error or malfunction. Useful as a metric to diagnose the system. Also as an investigation tool for patterns by location or by person.</td>\n<td>1) Card misread: generated by the user; frequent when migrating from a 125 kHz proximity card to a smart card. The user does not hold the card long enough. At a turnstile it is usually followed by a forced door alarm: the user presents the card and keeps walking. 2) Card expired: the cardholder ignores the renewal notices. 3) Unauthorized access: tries to enter an unauthorized area. Either their privileges are incorrectly programmed, or someone is trying to enter improperly; in that case, when, where and why are investigated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User-defined actions/alarms</td>\n<td>Order the PACS to take another action after an alarm (e.g., activating entry X unlocks door Y). They are the focus of SOC operators; they carry detailed information and response instructions.</td>\n<td>Defined actions that are announced on screen are noise: the system only executes an action. A system report makes it possible to change how they are recorded and announced.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communications failure</td>\n<td>A component does not communicate with another within a set time. Occurs in PACS and CCTV. In PACS: reader ↔ control panel and panel ↔ PACS server. In video: network outage at a device or between it and the VMS (video management system) interface.</td>\n<td>1) Network availability: network outage; greatly affects the ability to monitor alarms. It usually cuts PACS server ↔ local panel; resolved by coordinating with IT. 2) Cabling/wiring issues: cable shorted against metal, cut, or with interference from nearby cables or power lines. Slow diagnosis and laborious repair; often requires new cable.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says 13.53 MHz for the smart card. The standard frequency is 13.56 MHz (outside the book). It also writes KHz; the correct form is kHz.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Examples of system metrics (reports from a real PACS, weeks of June–July 2021)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All the charts come from the same initial report. Each one adds actionable information.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Figure</th>\n<th>What it shows</th>\n<th>Lesson</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>4.2 <em>Total Forced Doors by Week</em></td>\n<td>Weekly total of forced doors, weeks 6/7/21 to 6/28/21 (axis up to 20,000).</td>\n<td>Is it good or bad? Without a baseline, the totals give no useful information; more detail is needed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4.3 <em>Total Forced Doors and Turnstiles by Week</em></td>\n<td>Separates card reader doors, turnstiles and total. Most alarms come from turnstiles and drop sharply in the last week.</td>\n<td>The turnstiles required a card on entry and exit. An executive decision removed card exit, but free egress mode was not programmed. Every exit generated an alarm. Once corrected, only the entry side alarms.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4.4 <em>Forced Door Details by Location and Week</em></td>\n<td>Removes the turnstiles and shows the doors with the most alarms per week (axis 0–4,000): NY 9th Flr S Lobby Entrance; Jersey City 16th Flr Main Entrance; NY 10th Flr SE Entrance; HQ Mail Room Door; Chicago North Entry Door; Houston Equipment Rm.</td>\n<td>Ideally the cause is found by reviewing video of the door with a reader. Sometimes a technician has to be sent.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4.5 <em>Total Held Open Doors by Week</em></td>\n<td>Sharp drop in the last week because of a change in the calculation (axis up to 5,000; dates 6/7/21 to 7/3/21).</td>\n<td>In the first 4 weeks, every state change of the position switch was counted. Video showed innocent errors. The alarm was reprogrammed to trigger only after 15 minutes. This located doors left open for long periods (training sessions, ventilation) and measures were taken per facility.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4.6 <em>Total Unauthorized Access Attempts by Type and Week</em></td>\n<td>Classes: All Card Rejects, Expired, Access Privilege and Card Misread (axis 0–2,000), weeks 6/7/21 to 6/28/21.</td>\n<td>Each class requires investigating the cause: user error, malfunction or malicious intent.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> exact values of the bars in Figures 4.2 to 4.6; only axes and labels can be read.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2.2 Physical Security Personnel Metrics</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They measure the human element of the program (e.g., the guard force).</li>\n<li>They indicate the appropriate spending and the number and type of personnel needed to operate effectively.</li>\n<li>They make it possible to see program growth, cost increases, efficiency gains and output costs.</li>\n<li>In sum, they give a view of the resources needed to meet goals and mission. More detail in Chapter 12 (Security Officers and the Human Element).</li>\n<li>First, the objectives of the human element within the program and how to measure them are defined.</li>\n<li>Two measurable categories: response and training.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>A. Response</strong></p>\n<p><em>1) Response to an alarm or incident.</em> It may already exist as a performance requirement in the guard contract or the integrator's contract. Two ways to apply it:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>How it is measured</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Guard force response</td>\n<td>A stopwatch is enough. It is done within a contract surveillance plan. The COR (contracting officer representative) triggers an alarm; e.g., a duress alarm in the director's office. It measures how long the appropriate response takes (in person, call attempt). It is compared with the contract requirements (baseline requirements). This measures operational efficiency and suggests improvements.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security systems integrator response</td>\n<td>The integrator's contract sets performance requirements or SLAs (service level agreements). E.g.: time limit to start an installation after the task order or work order is issued. The best method to identify and resolve problems is a single contractor responsible for the whole system. The data are reported every month to the COR as a contract deliverable: each service call, location, duration, response time and other data.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Figure 4.7 (<em>Contractor Response Time by Completion Status and Month</em>): hypothetical integrator response requirement for service requests. It shows % completed and % not completed before 5 p.m. the next business day, from 6/20 to 5/21. Most responses met the deadline.</li>\n<li>Figure 4.8 (<em>Actual Contractor Response Time by Completion Status and Month</em>): another case, with a required response time of four hours.</li>\n<li>Audiences for these data: department heads, senior management, third parties and regulatory authorities.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text cites Figure 4-8 twice; the first mention (results of the last year) corresponds to 4.7. Both figures carry the same title (<em>Service Calls Response Within 24 Hours</em>) and the same 5 p.m. next-business-day legend. However, 4.8 is described with 4 hours. It also says contact where it should say contract.</li>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> monthly percentages in Figures 4.7 and 4.8 (not legible in the text).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>2) Response to customer requests.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is one of the most important metrics any office can monitor.</li>\n<li>It directly affects its reputation (an intangible asset). Therefore, also its ability to obtain resources and fulfill the mission.</li>\n<li>It requires adequate technology. There are industry systems that measure and chart it well, but they usually require other areas to buy and implement them, with additional funds.</li>\n<li>Alternative: IT has long used this technology. By collaborating with IT, security can implement it with minimal cost and impact on the customer.</li>\n<li>How IT's customer service software works:<ul>\n<li>The request arrives online or by phone; it receives a tracking number and a time stamp.</li>\n<li>A representative resolves it on the spot or routes it to the appropriate support team.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Typical reports (the book clarifies there are more): number of requests; average response time; average resolution time; type of request; periods of high and low volume; most frequent requests.<ul>\n<li>Also: who resolved, how many each person processed and of what type. Useful for sizing resources and distributing the workload.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Physical security elements that can be integrated: problems with access cards; requests for access to restricted areas; lost or stolen cards; key requests.</li>\n<li>Advantages of integrating them into the existing customer service process (one-stop shop model):<ul>\n<li>the employee visits fewer places to resolve a matter;</li>\n<li>less time lost and lower local travel expenses;</li>\n<li>greater employee satisfaction;</li>\n<li>better understanding among IT, physical security and other areas;</li>\n<li>helps security management detect process inefficiencies.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>B. Training Metrics</strong> — developed from two perspectives:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Perspective</th>\n<th>Scope</th>\n<th>Metrics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Inward-facing</td>\n<td>Training required of the security staff themselves: annual performance plan, job requirements, contract or professional development.</td>\n<td>Baseline of 100%. The % achieved at the close of the period can count toward the employee's annual evaluation. Others: % of staff who meet all requirements; average score on end-of-course evaluations; professional development objectives achieved; proficiency demonstrated in exercises.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outward-facing</td>\n<td>Training that the security office designs and delivers to employees or other audiences. Topics: policies and procedures, IT security, handling of sensitive information, privacy rules, emergency procedures, business ethics, workplace violence prevention and travel safety.</td>\n<td>Training needs identified; courses developed; courses delivered; total % of students who attend. Baseline of 100% in each to detect areas for improvement. E.g.: a low value in courses delivered may indicate a lack of resources to deliver them.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Numerical example from the book (inward-facing):</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Completion rate</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.2519em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">15</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> courses required</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">9</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> courses completed</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">60%</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Customer satisfaction survey:</strong><ul>\n<li>Security collaborates with IT to host and manage the survey on an electronic site.</li>\n<li>It is distributed, e.g., with a link in selected emails from the security staff.</li>\n<li>Customers give anonymous feedback on the service received.</li>\n<li>It is tabulated weekly, monthly and quarterly to see areas for improvement and areas of success.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2.3 Physical Security Compliance Metrics in the Public Sector</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Government agencies' physical security programs must comply with various regulations and requirements of their own.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Compliance of Facilities</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Requirement for US federal facilities: conduct a security assessment (facility security assessment) at each site under the agency's charge.</li>\n<li>Two phases:<ol>\n<li>Initial assessment.</li>\n<li>Recurring assessment, with an interval according to the facility's security level. E.g.: low level, every 5 years; higher levels, every 3 years.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Key component: identify vulnerabilities and recommend countermeasures to mitigate their possible exploitation.</li>\n<li>Metrics by phase: assessments completed; vulnerabilities identified; time to deploy countermeasures; total cost of the assessment program.</li>\n<li>Formula 1 — % of facilities with a current assessment (goal: 100%):</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Facilities with a current assessment (%)</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0574em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total number of facilities</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total number of facilities with a current security assessment</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Formula 2 — MTTMV (mean time to mitigate vulnerabilities) (Center for Internet Security, 2010):<ul>\n<li>Indicates how the organization reacts to detected vulnerabilities. It is measured in units of time.</li>\n<li>Gives the average time to mitigate a known vulnerability and points to processes that need to be sped up.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.3074em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.427em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total number of mitigated vulnerabilities</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mop op-symbol small-op\" style=\"position:relative;top:0em;\">∑</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\"><span class=\"mopen delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\">(</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Date of mitigation</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Date vulnerability was detected</span></span><span class=\"mclose delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\">)</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the summation (Σ) is not visible in the extracted text. Since it is an average, the differences for all mitigated vulnerabilities are added (outside the book: this is how the CIS defines it). Also, the book cites CIS 2010 but does not include it in its references.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Compliance of Systems</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sometimes physical security must collect metrics to meet regulatory standards.</li>\n<li>In the US, PACS are critical and depend on IT infrastructure. Therefore they must comply with FISMA (Federal Information Security Management Act of 2014).<ul>\n<li>Federal agencies' FISMA information systems must operate within the controls and limits set by law.</li>\n<li>They go through a C&amp;A (certification and accreditation) process when deployed and then at predefined intervals.</li>\n<li>Auditors identify vulnerabilities or conditions outside acceptable limits. They deliver a final report to the executive committee.</li>\n<li>The findings touch the PACS infrastructure and operation: server configuration, network communications, disaster recovery operations and operating environment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Not all physical security managers are familiar with these topics. They matter more and more because the systems run on the IT network.</li>\n<li>Metrics (similar to those for facilities):<ol>\n<li>% of mitigations successfully deployed.</li>\n<li>Total mitigation time per finding.</li>\n<li>Total mitigation cost per finding.</li>\n<li>Average mitigation cost per finding.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the reference cites FISMA 2014 as 44 USC § 3541, which is the section of the 2002 FISMA. FISMA 2014 is at 44 USC § 3551 et seq. Today NIST calls C&amp;A assessment and authorization (A&amp;A) (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Biometrics</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>More and more US states have laws against the unlawful collection and storage of biometric information, which uniquely identifies the person. Many countries have similar laws.</li>\n<li>Compliance with applicable state, local and federal laws must be ensured.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2.4 Presenting Aggregate Status for Physical Security Metrics</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Purpose of collecting metrics: give the organization assurance about the program's effectiveness and, ultimately, facilitate its improvement.</li>\n<li>Steps:<ol>\n<li>Set performance requirements for each main component. They may come from external demands (regulatory compliance), existing baselines or established levels of risk acceptance.</li>\n<li>Measure each task or process of the component against its requirement → component rating.</li>\n<li>With the component ratings → program rating.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Figure 4.9 (<em>Metrics Summary Chart</em>): table by fiscal year, with quarters Q1–Q4 for Systems, Personnel, Compliance and Overall.<ul>\n<li>Gives management a quick snapshot of the program's effectiveness and efficiency.</li>\n<li>If management questions a rating, the security staff show the data from the individual metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> contents of the cells in Figure 4.9 (ratings or colors, not legible in the text).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-4-e2\">4.3 Additional Recommended Metrics</h3><ul>\n<li>Senior security staff can be creative in choosing which metrics to implement and how to use them.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>How it is measured and what for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of security requests received</td>\n<td>Against prior periods (week, month, quarter); measures efficiency and resource allocation. Types: new access credentials; changes to existing credentials; requests for system activity reports.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External dependency responsiveness</td>\n<td>Time in which external dependencies (e.g., contracting office, IT technical support) handle security requests.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change in the number of security-related incidents</td>\n<td>Against fixed periods (month, quarter, year). Shows trends and changes in incident type according to the countermeasures deployed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security cost per square foot</td>\n<td>Formula A.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security cost per employee</td>\n<td>Formula B.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Facility security assessments performed</td>\n<td>Formula C.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security system preventive maintenance inspections performed</td>\n<td>Formula D.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Formula A — security cost per square foot:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0929em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Security cost per ft</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8984em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.1473em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.2519em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total square footage</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total system cost</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total contractor labor cost</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total FTE</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Indirect cost</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Formula B — security cost per employee:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Security cost per employee</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.3074em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.427em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total number of employees</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total security department cost (Contract cost + FTE cost + Indirect cost)</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Formula C — facility security assessments performed:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Facility security assessments performed</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.2519em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total number of facility security assessments planned</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total number of facility security assessments performed</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Formula D — preventive maintenance inspections performed:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">PM inspections performed</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.2519em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total number of facilities with security systems</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total number of preventive maintenance inspections performed</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in Formula A the book adds Total FTE (full-time equivalent positions) to costs. It should be read as FTE cost, as in Formula B.</li>\n<li>Conversion to m² (outside the book): 1 m² = 10.764 ft².</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0818em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Cost per m</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8873em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.1362em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0929em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Cost per ft</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8984em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.1473em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10.764</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why metrics matter (chapter close)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Security competes with other areas for limited funds and resources.</li>\n<li>Metrics tell executives whether their investment improved security, reduced vulnerability and lowered risk to an acceptable level.</li>\n<li>They are used by program managers, system owners and security directors. They serve to isolate problems and justify funds and resources.</li>\n<li>They must drive improvement and focus on the organization's strategic objectives and goals.</li>\n<li>Avoid collecting data for the sake of collecting.</li>\n<li>Starting small makes it possible to detect errors in what is collected and in how it is collected and used.</li>\n<li>A program under way reveals new applications and process improvements. With metrics, every aspect of the program is measured and also better managed.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-4-e3\">4.4 Application of Metrics Throughout This Book</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n<th>Application of the metrics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>11 — Electronic Security Systems (ESS)</td>\n<td>They add value in selecting, scoping, upgrading and maintaining the ESS. When selecting one, the requirements document covers communication protocols, number of cardholders and readers per panel. The reporting function is often forgotten: does the system work well? how does it generate reports? Manufacturers neglected it: little operational experience and users who did not use report-based metrics. This is changing in new designs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12 — Security Officers and the Human Element</td>\n<td>Metrics fit into the guard force's QA (quality assurance) and QC (quality control) programs. Financial: contract fund burn rate, management of overtime cost and cost of turnover. Performance: guard deficiency reports, response times, training compliance and accuracy of what is entered in the automated incident management system. Trends guide changes in staffing, post locations, allocation of duties and program design.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13 — Principles of Project Management</td>\n<td>They are applied in the WBS (work breakdown structure), with activities, time and effort. Also in requirements, design, costing and contracting, and test protocols. Highlighted approach: earned value, the measurement of project progress to date. The book describes it as the cumulative budgeted cost compared with the actual cost of the work completed at a point in the schedule. Early warning of schedule and cost deviations, when correcting is most effective.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16 — Follow-On Support and Activities</td>\n<td>ESS metrics detect failures and components about to fail. They help define the maintenance contract, for one site or for dispersed facilities. Stable system with few failures → time-and-material contract. High failure rate in a complex integrated system → fixed-price maintenance contract. Region with more needs → the contract prioritizes it in price or in the location of the main provider. They also guide upgrades, deployment of new technology and transition of legacy systems.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exam note (earned value):</strong> the book's definition is confusing. In project management, EV (earned value) is the budgeted cost of the work performed. Compared with the actual cost (AC) it gives the cost variance; with the planned value (PV), the schedule variance (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span></span></span></span></span></div><h3 id=\"physical-4-e4\">Key Terms</h3><p>Metric · Security metric · Return on investment (ROI) · Value-added · Enterprise Security Risk Management (ESRM) · Risk owner · Effectiveness · Efficiency · Benchmark · Accountability · Measurement framework · Security Metrics Evaluation Tool (Security MET) · Reliability · Validity · Generalizability · Timeliness · Manipulation · Organizational relevance · Behavioral summary scale · Score sheet · Enterprise Performance Framework · Strategic improvement · Sustained Quality Performance · Start small · Collect relevant data · Who cares? test · SMART (Specific, Measurable, Attainable, Repeatable, Time-dependent) · Baseline · Security operations center (SOC) · Physical access control system (PACS) · Noise · Forced door · Door held open · Door position switch · Bypass duration · Propped open · Exit device · Unauthorized access attempt · Card misread · Card expired · Access privilege · User-defined actions/alarms · Communications failure · Network availability · Video management system (VMS) · Turnstile · Free egress · Guard force response · Contract surveillance plan · Contracting officer representative (COR) · Duress alarm · Baseline requirements · Service level agreement (SLA) · Task order / work order · Contract deliverable · Response to customer requests · Intangible asset · One-stop shop · Inward-facing training metrics · Outward-facing training metrics · Customer satisfaction survey · Facility security assessment · Countermeasure · Mean time to mitigate vulnerabilities (MTTMV) · Federal Information Security Management Act (FISMA) · Certification and accreditation (C&amp;A) · Biometric information · Aggregate status · Metrics Summary Chart · Risk acceptance · External dependency responsiveness · Security cost per square foot · Security cost per employee · Full-time equivalent (FTE) · Preventive maintenance inspection · Electronic security system (ESS) · Quality assurance (QA) · Quality control (QC) · Burn rate · Work breakdown structure (WBS) · Earned value · Time-and-material contract · Fixed-price contract · Legacy systems</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"4.1 Understanding Metrics"},{"id":"e1","t":"4.2 Physical Security Metrics"},{"id":"e2","t":"4.3 Additional Recommended Metrics"},{"id":"e3","t":"4.4 Application of Metrics Throughout This Book"},{"id":"e4","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"4.1 Understanding Metrics","h":"the text cites William and Schultz (2016); in the references it appears as Williams, T. and Schultz, T. (2016), <em>Metrics and the Maturity Mindset</em>."},{"s":"4.1 Understanding Metrics","h":"the text calls the third idea Strategic improvement; the figure labels it Strategic Initiatives."},{"s":"4.1 Understanding Metrics","h":"in Payne's SMART, R = Repeatable and T = Time-dependent. In the classic management SMART they are Relevant and Time-bound (outside the book)."},{"s":"4.2 Physical Security Metrics","h":"the book says 13.53 MHz for the smart card. The standard frequency is 13.56 MHz (outside the book). It also writes KHz; the correct form is kHz."},{"s":"4.2 Physical Security Metrics","h":"the text cites Figure 4-8 twice; the first mention (results of the last year) corresponds to 4.7. Both figures carry the same title (<em>Service Calls Response Within 24 Hours</em>) and the same 5 p.m. next-business-day legend. However, 4.8 is described with 4 hours. It also says contact where it should say contract."},{"s":"4.2 Physical Security Metrics","h":"the summation (Σ) is not visible in the extracted text. Since it is an average, the differences for all mitigated vulnerabilities are added (outside the book: this is how the CIS defines it). Also, the book cites CIS 2010 but does not include it in its references."},{"s":"4.2 Physical Security Metrics","h":"the reference cites FISMA 2014 as 44 USC § 3541, which is the section of the 2002 FISMA. FISMA 2014 is at 44 USC § 3551 et seq. Today NIST calls C&amp;A assessment and authorization (A&amp;A) (outside the book)."},{"s":"4.3 Additional Recommended Metrics","h":"in Formula A the book adds Total FTE (full-time equivalent positions) to costs. It should be read as FTE cost, as in Formula B."}]},"physical-5":{"html":"<h3 id=\"physical-5-e0\">Chapter Introduction (pp. 77–78)</h3><ul>\n<li>The chapter ties together the four parts of the book:<ol>\n<li>Risk Management: The Basis for Physical Security</li>\n<li>Design Principles and Practices</li>\n<li>Physical Security and Protection Strategies</li>\n<li>Managing Physical Security Projects and Programs</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Security risk management is applied to all decisions about:<ul>\n<li>facilities: design, layout and use;</li>\n<li>overall protection strategies;</li>\n<li>structural and electronic security systems;</li>\n<li>security officer operations and other human support;</li>\n<li>physical security program management.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The professional's mental model: plan, implement, evaluate and monitor protection programs.</li>\n<li>It is applied to every aspect of the integrated strategy, not just the building or the electronics.</li>\n<li>ASIS PAP standard (Physical Asset Protection Standard, 2021): to choose and apply measures properly, a risk assessment must first be done.<ul>\n<li>Its results must be interpreted, reported and used correctly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Reviewing good and bad examples makes it possible to benchmark protection strategies.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-5-e1\">5.1 Design Principles</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Foundational principle</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The four Ds</td>\n<td>Deter, Detect, Delay, Deny</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Layered security</td>\n<td>Also called defense-in-depth or security-in-depth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A layer can be:<ul>\n<li><strong>Physical:</strong> a wall or an entry control point.</li>\n<li><strong>Functional:</strong> e.g., an intrusion detection layer + a video surveillance layer + a response force layer, together (with or without a physical layer) to protect an area.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Layers must reinforce and back each other up, without overdesign or unnecessary duplication.</li>\n<li><strong>Critical detection point:</strong> the point where the remaining delay time exceeds the response force time.</li>\n<li>Defense in depth: the adversary must avoid or defeat several measures in sequence.<ul>\n<li>Example: defeating a sensor and penetrating two barriers to reach a room or a file cabinet in a restricted area.</li>\n<li>The times and effectiveness of each layer may vary, but each layer requires a separate act by the adversary.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Effects of layers on the adversary:<ul>\n<li>they create uncertainty in the adversary's mind;</li>\n<li>they increase the attack preparation time;</li>\n<li>they add steps to the intrusion;</li>\n<li>they give the security or police response time;</li>\n<li>they may convince the adversary to abort and look for a softer target.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says that at the critical detection point the remaining delay \"exceeds\" the response time. (outside the book) In the physical protection systems literature (Garcia) it is the last point along the path where detecting still allows interruption: remaining delay ≥ response time. Detecting after that is too late.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> this book uses deter, detect, delay, deny. (outside the book) Other sources replace \"deny\" with \"respond\" or use 5 Ds. Answer with this book's list.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>5.1.1 Point Versus Area Security</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Area security</th>\n<th>Point security</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Outer perimeter</td>\n<td>Strong perimeter protection</td>\n<td>Lax control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Entry control</td>\n<td>Very strict, at a single portal</td>\n<td>Focus on each building or on restricted areas within them</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Context from the book: sensitive research campus (intellectual property, high-end laboratory equipment, hazardous or controlled materials) or high-security government complex.</li>\n<li>Usual answer: a bit of both. It is not always practical because of budget, convenience or mission.</li>\n<li>The choice depends on the nature of the threat:<ul>\n<li>Under normal conditions, point security may be enough.</li>\n<li>If a threat from radical activists arises, it is advisable to move to area security.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>On a large campus (e.g., a dozen multistory buildings) the conclusion may change. Key questions:<ul>\n<li>How does it affect the budget, staffing, equipment, mitigation approach and overall strategy?</li>\n<li>What additional information is needed?</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Source: Tapestry Technologies (2010).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.1.2 Conflict Avoidance</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Video surveillance, sensors, doors, access control, lighting and security officers can clash with each other.</li>\n<li>More electronic measures → greater likelihood of false alarms caused by proactive employees.<ul>\n<li>Example: a security officer sets off the intrusion alarm when inspecting a cut or damaged fence.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Physical measures must be seen as parts of a whole: an integrated strategy or program.</li>\n<li>Conflict between security and safety:<ul>\n<li>Example: fail safe or fail secure electronic locks? This is usually decided by building or fire codes.</li>\n<li>Hardware, glazing, audible alarms, lighting and access control must meet security objectives, safety objectives and regulations at the same time.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Conflict with the mission or culture: measures must be consistent with the organization's operations, people and purpose.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>(outside the book) Fail safe: unlocks on loss of power (prioritizes evacuation). Fail secure: stays locked on loss of power.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>5.1.3 Balance</h4>\n<p>Several dimensions of balance make the architecture practical, efficient and effective:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension (book order)</th>\n<th>What it requires</th>\n<th>Example or key</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Among types of measures</td>\n<td>Electronic, structural (includes mechanical), human and procedural, orchestrated as a system</td>\n<td>Do not overvalue electronics over the building layout and structural measures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Among access points or methods</td>\n<td>Equivalent protection at all accesses</td>\n<td>Heavily controlled front door and open back door. Case: high school with magnetometers at the 2 main entrances and other entrances uncontrolled, open or with broken locks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2 (expansion). Through any attack path (balanced protection)</td>\n<td>Wherever the adversary comes from, they find effective measures</td>\n<td>All the surfaces of a room (list below)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Among objectives or assets</td>\n<td>Not just goods: also people, information, intangible assets, mission and other objectives</td>\n<td>Applies to any facility: shopping center, research center, warehouse, hospital, school, data center</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Related: security, safety and operations</td>\n<td>They can clash; good planning reduces the conflict and seeks mutual support</td>\n<td>Plant with large bay doors between two zones for moving machinery, one zone being restricted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Final: among threats</td>\n<td>Do not eliminate a measure because it overprotects against another threat</td>\n<td>Adequate protection against all threats by all paths, balanced with cost, safety and structural integrity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Surfaces surrounding a room (book example):<ul>\n<li>walls, floors and ceilings of various types;</li>\n<li>windows and doors of various types;</li>\n<li>equipment hatches in floors and ceilings;</li>\n<li>HVAC (heating, ventilating, and air conditioning) openings with different types of grilles;</li>\n<li>crawl spaces, fire escapes or ladders, skylights and ledges; the book leaves this list open.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Balanced system:</strong> the minimum time or difficulty to penetrate each barrier is equal, and so is the probability of detecting penetration of each barrier.<ul>\n<li>It is an ideal, probably unattainable.</li>\n<li>Conceptual goal: a balanced level of deterrence, delay, detection and denial on all sides of the enclosure (building, room, vault).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.1.4 Additional Design Elements</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The type or use of the facility greatly conditions the design and strategy.</li>\n<li>Having special needs: data centers, communications centers, hospitals, laboratories, manufacturing plants and universities.</li>\n<li>Data centers and biotechnology laboratories need specialized environmental control and air handling (Figure 5.1).<ul>\n<li>Decide early where that equipment goes and whether it needs protection against vandalism, tampering, intentional attack or sabotage.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Manufacturing plants: the location of hazardous materials areas is key.</li>\n<li>The location of certain elements can hinder the security or first responder response and evacuation.<ul>\n<li>Collaborate in advance with designers and stakeholders and rehearse scenarios.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Review for safety, security and operations the location of: air intakes, exhaust vents, water drainage and utility entry points.</li>\n<li>In addition to the physical layout, consider operating procedures.</li>\n<li><strong>Component failure:</strong> a much-overlooked aspect; components fail, perhaps at the worst moment.<ul>\n<li>Evaluate the timing and consequences of failure and include them in the plan.</li>\n<li>The plan covers: initial design, maintenance, testing and contingency operations of electronic and mechanical systems.</li>\n<li>It is unlikely that a complex system will operate its entire life without failures.</li>\n<li>Causes: from environmental factors (expected) to adversary actions that exceed the design threat.</li>\n<li>Knowing the cause helps restore the system. More important: contingency plans to keep operating or maintain the security level for a set period.</li>\n<li>Automatic contingency (redundant equipment that takes over the function): very desirable in some cases. Example: generators or batteries if the adversary cuts the main power.</li>\n<li>Some contingencies need outside help. Example: local police who reinforce the airport at an elevated alert. Here the \"failure\" is the temporary lack of response force against the new threat.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Figure 5.1 \"HVAC/Environmental Control Systems at High-Tech Facilities\" (photographs). <strong>Pending:</strong> the content of the photos cannot be read as text.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Design Criteria (unnumbered subsection)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every design needs criteria to evaluate its elements.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Performance criteria</th>\n<th>Feature criteria</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How it chooses</td>\n<td>By each element's contribution to overall performance</td>\n<td>To meet the requirement that certain elements be present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measure of effectiveness</td>\n<td>Overall system performance</td>\n<td>Presence of those features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What it allows or causes</td>\n<td>Compare current (baseline) and improved system, quantify the improvement and do cost-benefit analysis</td>\n<td>Leads to the checklist method</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommendation</td>\n<td>Preferred: overall performance is what matters</td>\n<td>Avoid it in standards or requirements, or use it with great care</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example (perimeter detection)</td>\n<td>Detect an intruder running by any attack method</td>\n<td>Include two specific sensors: motion and fence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> after the example, the book states that the fence-mounted sensor is \"the best\" for monitoring the perimeter. It is an absolute statement that clashes with the performance logic of the section itself. On the exam, prioritize the performance criterion.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Performance measures:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Measures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Detection</td>\n<td>Probability of detection; communication and assessment time; frequency of nuisance alarms (nuisance alarms = false positives); frequency of undetected intrusions (false negatives)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delay</td>\n<td>Time to defeat obstacles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Denial/response</td>\n<td>Probability of accurate communication to the response force; time to communicate or assess; probability of deployment to the adversary's location; deployment time; response force effectiveness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book equates nuisance alarms with false positives. (outside the book) In physical protection systems, a nuisance alarm is caused by a real non-intruder stimulus (animal, weather). A false alarm is caused by the equipment itself.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>Procedures (unnumbered subsection)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Effective strategy = people + procedures + equipment, integrated against the expected or reasonably expected threat.</li>\n<li>The emphasis is usually on the human element and technology, but the value of procedures is enormous.</li>\n<li>Changing procedures can be a cost-effective solution, always together with the other existing or recommended elements.</li>\n<li>Procedures include:<ul>\n<li>operating and maintenance functions;</li>\n<li>training and awareness of employees and users;</li>\n<li>training of security officers and the response force;</li>\n<li>business practices (e.g., whether visitors are escorted in certain areas).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Investigations</strong> as a procedural tool:<ul>\n<li>Types: incident and exploratory (e.g., due to a complaint or a detected discrepancy).</li>\n<li>They may respond to a loss or anticipate a threat (e.g., background investigations of candidates).</li>\n<li>Their results go into an incident management system, together with: routine incident reports, access control records, video surveillance information and visitor logs, among other sources.</li>\n<li>Review those data periodically to evaluate and readjust the program; they also feed design and improvement projects.</li>\n<li>Frequent error: not using investigative information to refine objectives or recommend measures.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Procedures complement good technical design, layout and systems (ASIS International, 2012).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> the text cites \"ASIS International, 2012\", but the chapter's bibliography only includes the 2021 ASIS PAP standard. Possible citation error.</p>\n</blockquote><h3 id=\"physical-5-e2\">5.2 Examples of Design Practices: Good and Not So Good</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Figure</th>\n<th>Title in the book</th>\n<th>Assessment</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>5.2</td>\n<td>Design Features of a Professional Office Campus</td>\n<td>Good overall layout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5.3</td>\n<td>Using a stylish wall to partially hide protected assets</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5.4</td>\n<td>A Unique Terrain-Based Approach to Standoff</td>\n<td>Good, with one weak point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5.5</td>\n<td>A Good Practice: Separating Shipping and Receiving</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5.6</td>\n<td>Inefficient Placement of Surveillance Cameras</td>\n<td>Bad</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5.7</td>\n<td>First-Floor Office Lobby Using Compartmentation</td>\n<td>Good, with one flaw</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5.8</td>\n<td>Bollards Protecting Half of a Driveway</td>\n<td>Bad</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Figure 5.2 — Office campus: overall layout (facility layout)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Basic practice, applicable to any multibuilding complex or enclosed site.</li>\n<li><strong>Surroundings:</strong><ul>\n<li>Behind: interstate highway. It gives advantages and risks; both must be considered.</li>\n<li>On the left: transportation facility (perhaps a bus terminal and commuter parking). It may be a crime generator, though not always.</li>\n<li>That facility has partial perimeter fencing → another layer of security for the complex.</li>\n<li>In front and on the right: not shown in the diagram. Probably ordinary streets, but this should not be taken for granted.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Perimeter:</strong> attractive berm instead of a fence.<ul>\n<li>There was enough land; it was judged at least as effective as a mechanical measure (fence).</li>\n<li>Landscaping chosen for security objectives and for the aesthetics of the office park and the neighborhood:</li>\n<li>certain trees or large boulders → effective structural barriers;</li>\n<li>thorny shrubs → visual privacy and a somewhat lesser physical barrier;</li>\n<li>low stone wall or decorative fence → deterrence, demarcation and some barrier.</li>\n<li>Key factor in a barrier's contribution to system effectiveness: the time to defeat it.</li>\n<li>Risk: landscaping can block cameras (poor maintenance or seasonal changes in foliage).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Changes over time:</strong> case of a new commuter rail station to the right of the campus.<ul>\n<li>It generated foot traffic between the terminal and the station; people cutting across the campus or parking on it.</li>\n<li>Options: perimeter fence + staffed checkpoint, or simply a low wall or decorative fence + signage.</li>\n<li>No solution fits everything; risk assessment and knowing the area help.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Internal layout:</strong><ul>\n<li>Buildings that receive visitors, at the front; the most secure or restricted, at the back.</li>\n<li>Visitor parking separate or well defined, well located.</li>\n<li>Data center and service area somewhat isolated (behind Building 1).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Lesson: a well-thought-out layout gives present and future benefits and makes the other measures (structural, electronic and human) cheaper.</li>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> the Figure 5.2 diagram cannot be read as text; this summary is based only on the book's explanation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Figure 5.3 — Decorative wall that hides assets</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Loading dock, trash containers and backup generator, behind a brick wall that matches the building.</li>\n<li>Small bollards (low security) protect the generator from accidental impacts. Sometimes sturdier bollards or barriers are recommended.</li>\n<li>The three areas and the vehicle access are easily covered with cameras → fewer cameras and less load on control center operators.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Figure 5.4 — Separation distance through terrain (standoff)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Standoff distance matters for blast protection.</li>\n<li>The terrain gives natural separation and grade difference: vehicles stay at least 40 feet (≈ 12 m) from the building exterior.</li>\n<li>The slope isolates the lower level; it provides privacy and keeps away or gives away intruders or unlawful observers.</li>\n<li>Weak point: egress is limited to the ramps (bridges). Solution: enough ramps on all sides.</li>\n<li>Berms, slopes, vegetation and natural terrain can be integrated into the strategy at many types of facility.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book gives the figure in feet: 40 ft. The conversion (40 × 0.3048 = 12.19 m) is mine; on the exam, think in feet.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>Figure 5.5 — Separating shipping and receiving</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Recommended in commerce, warehouses and freight transport: plants, warehouses, docks, transshipment points and stores.</li>\n<li>It segregates incoming goods from outgoing goods; it frustrates thefts that occur when they are mixed or close together.</li>\n<li>It also reduces paperwork and inventory errors or losses.</li>\n<li>High-value items: in a cage or closed room → physical barrier and psychological deterrence.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Figure 5.6 — Poorly placed cameras</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>There is one camera for each entrance door; a single one could cover both.</li>\n<li>There are no cameras at the loading docks.</li>\n<li>If the docks are covered, a parked trailer can block the view of the next doors.</li>\n<li>The docks deserve surveillance equal to or greater than the entrances: easy access for intruders, vandalism and improper movements of goods.</li>\n<li>Plan mounting location, field of view and number of doors per camera.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Figure 5.7 — Lobby with compartmentation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The conference room is outside the restricted space.<ul>\n<li>Attendees do not sign in at the desk or receive a badge.</li>\n<li>They are separated from the private areas; they usually have no direct link to the organization.</li>\n<li>Useful where there are sensitive activities; it protects information, assets, occupants and employees.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Security office in a central position; mail and loading dock somewhat segregated.</li>\n<li>Elevators behind the desk: only for those who signed in and were authorized by a security officer.</li>\n<li>Flaw: the emergency exit on the right is partly blocked by sofas. An inspector or the fire marshal would have it corrected on the spot.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Figure 5.8 — Bollards at half a vehicle entrance</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>After several truck bomb attacks, many buildings extended the standoff with bollards or large concrete planters.</li>\n<li>Here the owner, the manager and the security director did not coordinate with the neighboring building, which has no bollards.</li>\n<li>The attacker can go around the bollards and attack either building.</li>\n<li>It is more a security management failure than a physical security failure; both disciplines influence it.</li>\n<li>Lesson: avoid myopic vision (only one's own); consider the neighbor and peripheral threats. Collaborative planning (Peterson, 2014).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Case: the vehicle barrier and the attack on foot</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>An attacker wanted to enter by car with explosives, tailgating another vehicle.</li>\n<li>The retractable barrier at the checkpoint was too fast and he could not enter.</li>\n<li>Plan B: he got out with the explosives and ran on foot to the building perimeter. The controller, focused on the car, reacted late.</li>\n<li>Lesson: the barrier fulfilled its mission but did not stop the attack. A vehicle barrier may not stop a pedestrian, and vice versa.</li>\n<li>Think outside the conventional about threats and scenarios; avoid the piecemeal or silo approach.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Risk management approach</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Collaborate with architects, designers, systems consultants and other stakeholders.</li>\n<li>Review scenarios, benchmark, evaluate similar environments and think outside the conventional.</li>\n<li>Goal: an integrated, well-orchestrated strategy, not just buying and installing hardware.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Purchasing and vendors (Purpura, 2013, pp. 262–263)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Warns against relying too much on vendors' advice or pressure.</li>\n<li>Unethical sales practices it cites:<ul>\n<li>selling obsolete technology and unnecessary equipment;</li>\n<li>pushing intrusion detection systems with excess inventory, useless or unnecessary for the client;</li>\n<li>omitting extra staff, additional hardware, software problems and expensive maintenance.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The same can happen with computer security products (IT: information technology).</li>\n<li>Rules for buying well:<ol>\n<li>Apply the \"buyer beware\" principle.</li>\n<li>Decide as a team, with several specialists.</li>\n<li>Evaluate the organization's needs, not the vendor's.</li>\n<li>Stay up to date on the technological state of the art.</li>\n<li>Think critically; do not decide on emotion.</li>\n<li>Avoid panic purchases or instinctive (kneejerk) reaction.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Risk analysis helps the buyer to: identify areas of potential loss, point out weaknesses, develop and install countermeasures and evaluate needs.</li>\n<li>Vellani, <em>Strategic Security Management</em> (2007): \"match product to need and not need to product\".</li>\n<li>The designer must choose equipment only on determined requirements and needs.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-5-e3\">Key Terms</h3><p>four Ds · deter · detect · delay · deny · layered security · defense-in-depth · security-in-depth · critical detection point · response force time · softer target · area security · point security · single portal · conflict avoidance · fail safe · fail secure · balance · balanced protection · balanced system · protection objectives · component failure · contingency plans · redundant equipment · backup power · design criteria · performance criteria · feature criteria · checklist method · baseline system · cost-benefit analysis · performance measures · probability of detection · assessment · nuisance alarms · false positives · false negatives · time to defeat obstacles · response force effectiveness · procedures · training and awareness · incident investigation · exploratory investigation · background investigations · incident management system · facility layout · berm · crime generator · standoff distance · bollards · shipping and receiving separation · compartmentation · tailgating · vehicle barrier · silo approach · buyer beware · kneejerk reaction purchases · match product to need · Physical Asset Protection Standard (PAP)</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"Chapter Introduction"},{"id":"e1","t":"5.1 Design Principles"},{"id":"e2","t":"5.2 Examples of Design Practices: Good and Not So Good"},{"id":"e3","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[]},"physical-6":{"html":"<h3 id=\"physical-6-e0\">Chapter Introduction (p. 95)</h3><ul>\n<li>Applies to new construction, expansion, renovation, upgrade or protection of another type of asset (vehicle, person, intellectual property).</li>\n<li>Starting with the right mindset gives a greater chance of success and eases the process.</li>\n<li>Some factors apply only to certain environments or assets. Even so, the team must discuss them at the start and during the project.</li>\n<li>They influence, sometimes greatly, three things: relevance, operational effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of the strategies and measures.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-6-e1\">6.1 Characteristics of the Assets Under Protection</h3><ul>\n<li>Sometimes the project's focus is not a building. The program may protect:<ol>\n<li>a person (executive, official, celebrity);</li>\n<li>an item of intellectual property (trade secret or physical model of a device);</li>\n<li>an event or a series of events;</li>\n<li>a vehicle (automobile, train, ship, aircraft or other means of transport);</li>\n<li>data stored or processed by a computer, computer network (IT: information technology), device or telecommunications system;</li>\n<li>a high-value object (painting, sculpture, jewel, cash);</li>\n<li>animals, plants, bioorganisms or other living things;</li>\n<li>a capability (e.g., the bandwidth of a communications or transmission system).</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Each type of asset requires a different approach and different metrics to measure effectiveness.</li>\n<li>Guiding questions:<ul>\n<li>Must the measures be mobile or travel with the asset? Does that limit size, weight, connectivity, personnel or power?</li>\n<li>Must they adapt to conditions or are they essentially static?</li>\n<li>Is the threat constant or variable? If it varies: over time, in intensity, in nature, by location, by season or time of day.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>They are critical if the asset is outside the usual domain of the professional, the team or the vendors. An outside consultant can be hired.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-6-e2\">6.2 Characteristics of the Building or Facility</h3><p>If the asset is a building or facility, other factors apply:</p>\n<h4>6.2.1 Ownership and Occupancy</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Key question: who is the owner? It affects the ability to modify the structure, sign contracts and do other business.</li>\n<li>Dealing with the owner gives more leverage and flexibility than dealing with a tenant.</li>\n<li>Direct communication with the owner helps, though it is a distraction if the owner wants to get too involved.</li>\n<li>If the owner occupies the building, they are usually more interested in the problems and details.</li>\n<li>Special arrangements if the owner occupies it: executive protection, robust communications, high-level or sensitive meeting rooms, operations center.<ul>\n<li>Especially if the owner is the CEO (chief executive officer) and the building houses senior management (C-suite: CEO; CFO, chief financial officer; CIO, chief information officer).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Case: a large financial corporation built, occupied for a few years and sold.<ul>\n<li>Buildings tailored to it, but designed for the next occupant (perhaps several tenants).</li>\n<li>Lesson: architectural and security elements that are easy to remove or install.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>One or several tenants:</strong><ul>\n<li>Multi-tenant building: more adaptable design, maintained throughout the life of the facility. Each tenant has different and changing requirements.</li>\n<li>Also in government: a mix of government and commercial occupants, or of several agencies with their own policies (Gilmore, 2009).</li>\n<li>Even more so with international or multinational organizations: culture, social customs, language, business practices and risk profile have an influence.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Case: a US (United States) government agency vacated a leased building in an office park.<ul>\n<li>By policy it removed vault doors, special floors, cabling, security systems and other equipment.</li>\n<li>Another agency with a sensitive mission arrived and installed the same things, with the same specifications.</li>\n<li>Lesson: great cost and waste of resources, plus longer transition delays.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.2.2 Purpose of the Facility</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>How and by whom will it be used? It greatly influences the strategy, design and layout.</li>\n<li>Facilities with a specific purpose (the book's full list):<ul>\n<li>power plants;</li>\n<li>dams;</li>\n<li>other utilities;</li>\n<li>telecommunications facilities;</li>\n<li>large retail facilities;</li>\n<li>manufacturing plants;</li>\n<li>facilities that handle classified or sensitive information;</li>\n<li>transportation facilities;</li>\n<li>military installations;</li>\n<li>hospitals;</li>\n<li>laboratories;</li>\n<li>chemical plants;</li>\n<li>schools and educational facilities.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The purpose determines: access control, standoff distance, types of sensors, nature of the security force and dependence on utilities, among other factors.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.2.3 Access</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Intended access: private, public or mixed.<ul>\n<li>Public: e.g., shopping centers. Private: only authorized personnel (employees).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It affects: layout, parking, security systems and the role or use of security officers.</li>\n<li>Related variable: hours. Normal hours, extended (store open late) or 24/7 (24 hours, 7 days)?</li>\n<li>Are there dual-egress doors, required by local code, that prevent locking down the main building from an adjacent parking lot?</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-6-e3\">6.3 Characteristics of the Surroundings</h3><ul>\n<li>Consider the terrain and the neighbors.</li>\n<li>Neighbors influence the threats, the vulnerabilities and the potential impact of an event.<ul>\n<li>A school would not be placed next to a hazardous waste depot or a dangerous chemical plant.</li>\n<li>Neighbors are almost never chosen, but they must be part of the risk assessment and the strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Neighbors and ingress and egress: can a neighbor's incident cut off one's own access?<ul>\n<li>Incidents cited: chemical spill, vehicle accident, facility lockdown, labor dispute or other.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Museum case: a facility depended on deliveries and shipments day and night, next to a museum threatened by hate groups.<ul>\n<li>A shooter entered the museum; police cordoned off the whole neighborhood for several hours.</li>\n<li>The plant's only transportation route was inaccessible for a long time.</li>\n<li>Lesson: a neighbor's incident can paralyze one's own operation if there is only one access route.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-6-e4\">6.4 Characteristics of the Location</h3><ul>\n<li>The environment changes security: dense urban area, suburb, town, rural or remote area.</li>\n<li>The response time of first responders varies greatly. It may require more self-reliance in an incident.<ul>\n<li>The risk assessment helps determine those needs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other location factors:<ul>\n<li>proximity to a body of water (can be positive or negative);</li>\n<li>reliability of supporting utilities and infrastructure;</li>\n<li>proximity to transportation facilities;</li>\n<li>access by roads and bridges;</li>\n<li>telecommunications infrastructure.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>These elements can be a valuable asset in operations or in an emergency.</li>\n<li>Do trains or expressways pass next to the site or beneath it, posing a threat?</li>\n<li>Climate and natural disasters (key factor): tornadoes, lightning, wind, extreme temperatures, sandstorms, floods, hurricanes, earthquakes, typhoons, tsunamis, snow or ice and other weather conditions.<ul>\n<li>How do they affect security systems and elements?</li>\n<li>How to bring in more security officers in an emergency if transportation and access are cut off?</li>\n<li>How to compensate for electronic systems out of service for a time?</li>\n<li>The answers go into the security strategy and into the continuity-of-operations and disaster management plans.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-6-e5\">6.5 Additional Influencing Factors</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>Key</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Senior management support</td>\n<td>Level of management support for security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Budget and resources</td>\n<td>Budget process and availability of resources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Human capital and services</td>\n<td>Access to personnel, outsource providers and special expertise or services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relationship with vendors</td>\n<td>Systems integrators, installation and maintenance, door and hardware suppliers, central station services and consultants. They must be responsive, capable and open to discussing the client's needs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Effective integration</td>\n<td>All elements work as a comprehensive protective system (see below)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Elements that must be integrated: electronic systems, incident management system, visitor management system, structural elements and security staff.</li>\n<li>The physical infrastructure must act in concert (transparently) with:<ul>\n<li>personnel security;</li>\n<li>information assets protection;</li>\n<li>IT security;</li>\n<li>investigations;</li>\n<li>training and awareness (which give credibility and authority).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>And with related disciplines: safety, regulatory compliance, business continuity and facilities or real estate management.</li>\n<li>Business continuity (the book inserts it in this section):<ul>\n<li>The greatest predictor of the success or failure of business continuity planning is senior management commitment.</li>\n<li>Processes for developing the business continuity plan (BCP), according to p. 100: readiness, prevention, response and recovery.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> internal contradiction. On p. 100 the BCP processes are 4 (readiness, prevention, response, recovery). On p. 101 they are 3 (readiness, response, recovery). Memorize both versions; if an option includes \"prevention\", it is the one from p. 100.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>6.5.1 Selecting Mitigation Options Based on Influencing Factors</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>General goal: cohesion among the organization, mission, risk profile and protection strategy (measures and tools).</li>\n<li>When choosing mitigation, consider goals and mission to protect people and assets and reduce legal liability.</li>\n<li>It is not practical to address all identified risks.<ul>\n<li>Prioritize the asset–threat/vulnerability pairs (threat vectors) that can cause great mission impact or damage.</li>\n<li>Also consider their likelihood.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>There are more and more options, but some are unfeasible or too costly (in money or other terms).</li>\n<li>A sound strategy combines the measures in a well-orchestrated way.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of option</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>General security categories</td>\n<td>1. Architectural improvements. 2. Security systems. 3. Policies and procedures. 4. Management practices. 5. Security staffing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transfer the financial risk</td>\n<td>Insurance or contractual terms (e.g., indemnification clauses in security service contracts)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accept the risk</td>\n<td>As a cost of doing business; always an option</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Every option is evaluated by four criteria for the company's operation:<ol>\n<li>availability;</li>\n<li>affordability;</li>\n<li>feasibility;</li>\n<li>risk acceptance.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Besides cost: does the strategy substantially interfere with operations?<ul>\n<li>Obvious example: closing the store to stop shoplifting also locks out customers; the store would go under.</li>\n<li>Less obvious example: access control so severe that it discourages customers also loses business.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The professional's challenge: balance a sound strategy with operational needs and the psychological impact on people and culture (Patterson, 2013).</li>\n<li>Business continuity (again, p. 101):<ul>\n<li>Processes: readiness, response and recovery (see the Exam note above).</li>\n<li>Guiding principle: the plan must contain a risk assessment and a business impact analysis (BIA). It must be flexible and adapt to the situation.</li>\n<li>The BIA includes data analysis. It helps management and the planner decide which functions are critical to continue operating.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-6-e6\">Key Terms</h3><p>influencing factors · assets under protection · intellectual property · trade secret · high-value object · capability (bandwidth) · constant vs. variable threat · outside consultants · ownership and occupancy · owner-occupied facility · tenant · leverage · executive protection · C-suite · multi-tenant facility · purpose of the facility · standoff distance · private, public or mixed access · business hours · 24/7 · dual-egress doors · lockdown · surroundings · neighbors · ingress and egress · location · dense urban area · rural or remote area · first responders · response time · self-reliance · supporting utilities and infrastructure · natural disasters · continuity of operations · disaster management plans · senior management support · senior management commitment · systems integrators · central station services · integration · incident management system · visitor management system · personnel security · information assets protection · IT security · mitigation options · risk profile · threat vectors · liability · architectural improvements · security staffing · risk transfer · insurance · indemnification clauses · risk acceptance · availability · affordability · feasibility · shoplifting · business continuity plan (BCP) · readiness · prevention · response · recovery · business impact analysis (BIA)</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"Chapter Introduction"},{"id":"e1","t":"6.1 Characteristics of the Assets Under Protection"},{"id":"e2","t":"6.2 Characteristics of the Building or Facility"},{"id":"e3","t":"6.3 Characteristics of the Surroundings"},{"id":"e4","t":"6.4 Characteristics of the Location"},{"id":"e5","t":"6.5 Additional Influencing Factors"},{"id":"e6","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[]},"physical-7":{"html":"<p><strong>Security architecture and design</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Discipline that designs facilities and complexes with security built in from the origin.</li>\n<li>Applies to new construction, renovation and expansion.</li>\n<li>Ideal: the security professional collaborates with architects, engineers and the general contractor from the start and throughout the process.</li>\n<li>Using the results of a risk assessment saves money and improves protection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Castles: defensive design factors (6)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>What it consists of</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>location</td>\n<td>geography, terrain and position</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>structural design</td>\n<td>size, shape and construction materials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>clear zones</td>\n<td>cleared zones for surveillance, threat detection and standoff distance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>access control</td>\n<td>moats, drawbridges and few access points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>defensive points</td>\n<td>points for deploying weaponry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>multiple layers or zones of protection</td>\n<td>several layers or zones of protection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Lesson: these ancient techniques serve modern design, in physical and procedural security. If attacks evolve, defenses do too.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Figure</th>\n<th>Castle</th>\n<th>Dates</th>\n<th>Protection features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>7.1</td>\n<td>Castel dell’Ovo, Naples (Italy)</td>\n<td>built in 1128</td>\n<td>water on 3 sides; entrance with portcullis; thick, slightly sloped curtain walls; battlements; loopholes for arrows and cannons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7.2</td>\n<td>Castel Nuovo, Naples (Italy)</td>\n<td>completed in 1343; renovated in the 1500s</td>\n<td>star-shaped plan; several ramparts; crenellated towers; curtain walls; portcullises</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7.3</td>\n<td>Häme Castle, Hämeenlinna (Finland)</td>\n<td>13th or 14th century, gray stone, later reinforced with brick; bastions in the 18th century; prison since 1837</td>\n<td>excellent vantage points; berms; defined clear zone around the perimeter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"physical-7-e0\">7.1 Design Overview</h3><ul>\n<li>First priority of every project: <strong>determine the client's requirements</strong>.</li>\n<li>In engineering this includes site layout, structure size, number of occupants and building activities.</li>\n<li>Sources: the client and the codes and regulations of the jurisdiction.</li>\n<li><strong>Basic factors that the security design must know from the start (9):</strong><ol>\n<li>governing codes and regulations</li>\n<li>protection requirements</li>\n<li>company standards and guidelines</li>\n<li>type of construction</li>\n<li>site layout</li>\n<li>material selection</li>\n<li>utilities</li>\n<li>life safety</li>\n<li>infrastructure</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-7-e1\">7.2 Codes and Regulations</h3><ul>\n<li>Projects have federal, state and local requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>Ways they arrive (5):</strong><ul>\n<li>code regulations: mandatory by law</li>\n<li>trade or industry guidelines / best practices</li>\n<li>standards</li>\n<li>permitting requirements</li>\n<li>contractual requirements</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In addition: the client's own standards or guidelines. They are not code, but the designer must know them.</li>\n<li>Some codes and many guidelines address security design.</li>\n<li>Risk: a security requirement can violate another regulation, e.g., life safety codes. Solution: coordinate with the other design disciplines (holistic approach).</li>\n<li><strong>US federal guidelines:</strong><ul>\n<li>Department of Defense (DoD) → must comply with the Unified Facilities Criteria (UFC). The <strong>Series 4</strong> of the UFC deals with security.</li>\n<li>Whole Building Design Guide (www.wbdg.org): resource of federal guidelines by agency.</li>\n<li>Guidelines for commercial sites: Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) and American Society of Civil Engineers (ASCE).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Federal guidelines are useful even if the project is not federal. Full list in 7.9.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-7-e2\">7.3 Project Requirements</h3><ul>\n<li>Necessary part of the design: do a risk assessment.</li>\n<li>There are many risk models, but all must evaluate asset value, threat and vulnerability.</li>\n<li>Simple risk equation:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Risk</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Vulnerability</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Threat</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Asset Value</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>You cannot protect against everything. The client must:<ul>\n<li>set the necessary level of protection;</li>\n<li>define the acceptable levels of risk;</li>\n<li>apply reasonable mitigations according to cost-benefit.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The client may not share the detail of the assessment and deliver only the DBT.</li>\n<li><strong>Design basis threat (DBT):</strong> profile of the adversary on which the facility's engineering and security operations are based. It includes:<ul>\n<li>type, composition and capabilities;</li>\n<li>methods: tactics, techniques and procedures;</li>\n<li>goals, intent and motivation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>FEMA model: 2 classes of threats or hazards (FEMA 2003)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Natural hazards</th>\n<th>Human-caused hazards (<em>intentional threats</em>)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Examples from the book</td>\n<td>catastrophic weather events, earthquakes, wildfires (non-exhaustive list)</td>\n<td>technological hazards, terrorism, others</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distinguishing feature</td>\n<td>their probability is estimated with historical data</td>\n<td>they originate in human activity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Typical cases: disgruntled employees, students and even soldiers who attack what they see as the source of their problems, often public institutions.</li>\n<li><strong>Challenge:</strong> balance public access with public safety. People do not want to shop or conduct business in a fortress.<ul>\n<li>Some mitigations protect without losing the welcoming look.</li>\n<li>Others show the protection in plain view: they reassure the public and deter threats.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Trap when choosing technology:</strong> not being able to verify the product's advertising before installing it.<ul>\n<li>Research products and services diligently and verify that they meet the client's requirements.</li>\n<li>Some systems are tested and certified; others have no valid test method; some companies refuse to pay for tests.</li>\n<li>For some clients theoretical calculations are not enough: they demand real tests (certification).</li>\n<li>If the client wants a certified system, they must require it in the design specifications.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-7-e3\">7.4 Type of Construction</h3><ul>\n<li>Retrofit or renovation vs. new construction can change the design greatly. The requirements depend on use; their implementation, on the type of work.</li>\n<li>Consult the stakeholders about pros, cons and limitations of the project.</li>\n<li><strong>Change review board:</strong> representatives of each main project partner. Its most important duty: to be the central point (clearinghouse) for all changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure points:</strong> in any type of work, ask what will fail first in a catastrophic event. A system is only as good and strong as its weakest point. Taken up again in 7.6.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 7.4 — Considerations in New Construction versus Retrofit/Renovation</strong> (non-exhaustive list)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Pros</th>\n<th>Cons</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New construction</td>\n<td>integrated system and facility; based on own design criteria; systems designed and tested to be compatible; warrantied systems; incorporates future growth or expansion</td>\n<td>most costs up front (front-loaded); requires complete design of structure and systems; the land must be acquired; more design and construction time; permit requirements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retrofit construction or renovation</td>\n<td>takes advantage of the existing structure; only installation and minor work (system and components); schedule normally shorter</td>\n<td>interrupts current activities; existing utilities and systems may not provide capacity; the cost may exceed that of new construction; existing systems may be incompatible or hard to match</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text speaks of “figures” in the plural, but it is a single figure (7.4). It has typos (“Majrity”, “Constrction”) and two lines without bullets (“Warrantied systems”, “Permit requirements”), which read as separate items.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-7-e4\">7.5 Site Layout</h3><ul>\n<li>Premise of perimeter design: <strong>the 4 Ds — deter, detect, deny, delay</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> do not confuse with the functions of a physical protection system, <em>detect, delay, respond</em> (outside the book).</li>\n<li>The easiest: move the threat away from the target. It is not always realistic:<ul>\n<li>Urban area: little space; land is very expensive or nonexistent.</li>\n<li>Rural area: convenience is sacrificed; it is less desirable.</li>\n<li><em>Standoff</em> enlarges the site footprint and adds costs and environmental issues.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The facility must look like less of a target of opportunity than its neighbor.</li>\n<li>If the attacker is not deterred: the more time between detection and reaching the target, the more opportunity to respond.</li>\n<li>All systems (security and building) must be in good condition. Broken cameras or breached fences say that security does not matter → easy target.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.5.1 Perimeter</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>One of the first lines of defense. Depending on the level of protection, it combines: fence line, security officers, cameras, vehicle barriers, intrusion detection systems (IDS), signs, natural barriers.</li>\n<li><strong>Fence:</strong> the most common perimeter defense.<ul>\n<li>It marks a line that must not be crossed and gives some delay.</li>\n<li>In reality it only “keeps honest people honest”. It does not stop someone who wants to get in.</li>\n<li>A standard fence topped with barbed wire is climbed in seconds.</li>\n<li>Some industries require it: the cargo industry asks for 6 ft in height (≈1.8 m, conversion outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Fence + IDS</strong> (pressure plates, vibration sensors, laser/LiDAR, other electronic sensors): the fence deters; the IDS alerts and the response starts sooner.</li>\n<li><strong>Good practice:</strong> do not put the fence right at the property line, but a little inside.<ul>\n<li>The outside space allows maintenance and visibility of the boundary.</li>\n<li>It prevents neighbors from planting plants or placing objects that could be used to breach the fence.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Inside the fence, maintenance and landscaping remain key to detecting and monitoring.<ul>\n<li>Vegetation should be lower the closer it is to the building. That way explosives, espionage equipment or hidden people can be seen.</li>\n<li>If trees or other factors block the main camera or sensor of a critical area, add coverage from another angle.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Not all fences are equal: cheap battery-powered tools cut chain-link in seconds. For more delay there is <strong>no cut/no climb fencing</strong>, at greater cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.5.2 Lighting</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Each type of visual surveillance has minimum light requirements: normal vision or special cameras (infrared or digital).</li>\n<li>The type of lamp affects color perception:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lamp</th>\n<th>Light it emits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Metal halide</td>\n<td>white → the best for color CCTV (closed-circuit television)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-pressure sodium</td>\n<td>yellowish</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Design according to local code. Codes seek to reduce light pollution.</li>\n<li>The code may set maximum and minimum lumens according to site size or light spill.</li>\n<li><strong>Spillover:</strong> light that extends onto neighboring properties. It is a nuisance in residential neighborhoods at night and can dazzle drivers on nearby roads.</li>\n<li>The book expands on security lighting in a later chapter.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.5.3 Building Design Against Blast</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Site and building design can reduce or worsen the effect of an explosion. The building suffers more than the initial wave.</li>\n<li><strong>Blast pressure terms (5):</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Incendiary thermal effect</td>\n<td>flash or fireball on detonation; insignificant unless there are highly combustible materials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shock or blast wave</td>\n<td>leading edge of the energy wave; expands in a circular pattern from the source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reflective pressure</td>\n<td>wave energy redirected by objects in its path; can be much stronger than the initial wave at the same distance (Wilkinson &amp; Anderson 2003)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overpressure</td>\n<td>resulting pressure above ambient</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fragmentation</td>\n<td>materials that surround the explosive or are nearby, thrown by the pressure waves</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Urban environment:</strong> reflected pressures greatly increase damage, through multiple impacts from several angles and at different times.<ul>\n<li>The book's analogy: dropping a bucket of ping-pong balls onto a miniature town. They bounce until the energy dissipates; the waves do too.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Building shape:</strong> changes its vulnerability. The wave is reflected at an angle in keeping with the surface it hits.</li>\n<li>Figure 7.5 — Blast Pressure Effects on a Structure (FEMA 2003).</li>\n<li>Figure 7.6 — Effects of Building Shape on Air Blast (Hinman 2011): examples of building shapes and their effect against the wave.</li>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> the graphic content of figures 7.5 and 7.6 cannot be read (they are images).</li>\n<li>If explosion is a valid risk: design the structure (new construction) or the site to lower the risk to an acceptable level, with qualified personnel.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.5.4 Building Access</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Standoff:</strong> distance between the threat and the building. It must be enough to detect and respond.</li>\n<li>If there is land, standoff easily adds delay and improves detection and characterization of the threat.</li>\n<li>Figure 7.7 — Security Zones: several measures create standoff, establish security zones and lengthen the penetration time. <strong>Pending:</strong> graphic detail of the figure (image).</li>\n<li>Without detection there is no response. To gain response time you need:<ul>\n<li>secondary detection, e.g., IDS;</li>\n<li>delay tactics, e.g., serpentine drives and parking standoff.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Know the response time of internal security or local police. Design the site so that the delay comes as close as reasonable to that time.</li>\n<li>Physically effective and cost-effective approach: <strong>serpentine barriers in front of retractable bollards</strong>.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-7-e5\">7.6 Material Selection</h3><ul>\n<li>Architecture: the material gives the design its look. Engineering: it gives strength and protects assets.</li>\n<li>The structure includes glazing, wood, steel, walls, floors, ceilings and furniture. Everything can become a projectile if poorly designed.</li>\n<li>Materials also affect physical penetration and access to building utilities or controls.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.6.1 Building Structure</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every system is designed to the requirements set at the start; the structure protects against the design hazards.</li>\n<li>Design against natural hazards that also works against human hazards: <strong>seismic anchoring of suspended equipment</strong> reduces debris in an explosion.<ul>\n<li>The wave breaks windows and weak points in the facade and reaches the equipment; if the mounting is weak, it falls and damages assets.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive collapse:</strong> uncommon; it is considered in buildings of <strong>3 or more stories</strong>.<ul>\n<li>Definition (ASCE/SEI 59-11, <em>Blast Protection of Buildings</em>, ASCE 2011): chain failure of the structural system, disproportionate to the initial local damage, such as that from an explosion.</li>\n<li>Mechanism: a column or floor support falls, those above fail and the building collapses on itself.</li>\n<li>Examples from terrorist attacks: Khobar Towers (Saudi Arabia) and Murrah Federal Building (Oklahoma City). Figure 7.8: Khobar Towers (DoD photo).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.6.2 Facades</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It determines whether the building looks attractive or ugly. In engineering, the facade protects or causes harm.</li>\n<li>Some materials break under force or pressure; their fragments become hazards. Wind or explosion throws them against buildings, key assets or people.</li>\n<li>Example: ballast gravel on rooftops comes loose in a tornado and becomes a projectile.</li>\n<li><strong>Sound and sight lines:</strong> the material must be neutral or a countermeasure, never help the attacker.</li>\n<li>More glass = better view for the attacker. It may require treatment against viewing inside or against listening devices.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.6.3 Interior Layout</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Open, closed (compartmentalized) or mixed floor plans. The user decides according to their operations.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Open floor plan</th>\n<th>Closed / compartmentalized floor plan</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Advantages</td>\n<td>great visibility of activities and people; collaboration; overall situational awareness (reduces threats); easy to detect confrontations and leave quickly</td>\n<td>privacy to work; more material that blocks fragments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disadvantages</td>\n<td>little privacy; can be an “open attack zone” with few places to take cover</td>\n<td>much less awareness of what is happening; restricted escape routes; more material that can fragment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjustments</td>\n<td>mid-level screens for sensitive or offensive material; low ceilings shorten the columns (useful in an explosion) but reduce spaciousness</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Layered placement:</strong> high-security activities in the center; low-security ones in the outer layers. That way no one without authorization crosses secure zones.</li>\n<li><strong>Special risk areas</strong> (e.g., mail rooms with unknown threats): next to exterior walls, to limit damage if something goes off. Even better: screen the mail elsewhere.</li>\n<li><strong>Key or highly skilled personnel:</strong> away from parking lots, vehicle access points, structural overhangs and walls with a lot of glass.</li>\n<li>The client, with the designer's help, chooses; it is almost always a mix, and the percentage is decided objectively.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.6.4 Glazing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Benefit:</strong> natural light; open and welcoming atmosphere. It earns points toward LEED (Leadership in Energy and Environmental Design) certification from the U.S. Green Building Council.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk:</strong> fragmentation due to pressure imbalance, caused by an extreme storm (tornado) or explosion.<ul>\n<li>If the exterior pressure exceeds the interior pressure, the window fails and the fragments enter the occupied space.</li>\n<li>The design must anticipate fragmentation and choose mitigations according to the required level of protection.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 7.9 — Performance Conditions for Window System Response (GSA 2020)</strong> (GSA: General Services Administration)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Performance condition</th>\n<th>Protection level</th>\n<th>Hazard level</th>\n<th>Glass response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Safe</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>does not break; no visible damage to glass or frame</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Very High</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>cracks, but the frame retains it; dust or very small fragments near the sill or on the floor are accepted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3a</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Very low</td>\n<td>cracks; fragments enter and fall to the floor no more than 3.3 ft (1 m) from the window</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3b</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>cracks; fragments fall to the floor no more than 10 ft (3 m) from the window</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>cracks; fragments fall to the floor and strike a vertical witness panel no more than 10 ft (3 m) away, at a height no greater than 2 ft (0.6 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>cracks and the window system fails catastrophically; fragments strike the witness panel no more than 10 ft (3 m) away, above 2 ft (0.6 m) in height</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Rule to remember: the higher the condition number, the less protection and the more hazard.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the book, row 3b appears at the end of the table; here it is in its logical order. The table text came with a garbled font encoding and was read by deciphering it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How to reduce fragmentation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>First: ensure the minimum standoff from blast sources, such as improvised explosive devices (IED) in a vehicle or container.</li>\n<li>Designing without windows is neither desirable nor practical. <strong>Other options (7):</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Locate windows away from blast sources</td>\n<td>if the wall facing the parking lot or threats has a lot of glass, add a secondary interior wall that captures fragments; that curtain wall also reduces heating and cooling losses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Choose the type of glass carefully</td>\n<td>standard construction annealed glass fragments easily; look for reinforced options; the glass also affects heating and cooling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reduce the window size</td>\n<td>large unsupported windows fragment more: more surface receives the pressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reinforce the glass</td>\n<td>film on the surface or between panes, or wire mesh; in a retrofit, verify that the structure holding the window withstands the same; if it is weaker, it fails and the whole window enters as a large projectile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction and design that support the panels</td>\n<td>if the bite (overlap of the frame over the glass) is insufficient, the glass breaks and fragments more easily</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blast curtain</td>\n<td>fixed to the ceiling and floor; traps the fragments and collects them at its base; good retrofit option; lets light through; only works in its position: if occupants pull it aside or cut it at the bottom, it becomes useless</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Catch bar, in a retrofit</td>\n<td>together with a reinforced window; it goes at the center point of the window; if the glass fails, it folds over the bar instead of entering the room</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Double benefit:</strong> blast countermeasures also slow down intrusions.<ul>\n<li>Smaller, higher windows reduce fragmentation and access.</li>\n<li>Do not put windows next to doors: it avoids breaking the glass and opening the lock from inside.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-7-e6\">7.7 Site Utilities</h3><ul>\n<li>Determine what special requirements protect the facility against the DBT.</li>\n<li><strong>Aspects to consider (4):</strong> power redundancy; how utilities enter the site; maintenance needs; access to utilities.</li>\n<li>Protect the entry points of critical utilities.</li>\n<li>Critical utility → loop design or redundant feed: if lost, it is fed from another point (back-feed).</li>\n<li><strong>Utility provider access:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Implication</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Outside the fence</td>\n<td>technicians can provide maintenance and emergency support at any time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inside the fence</td>\n<td>requires coordination and monitoring</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Evaluate whether an intruder can enter through utility access points or attack the utilities themselves: natural gas, chemicals, telecommunications or power.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.7.1 HVAC</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>HVAC (heating, ventilation, and air conditioning): fresh air, temperature and humidity. It provides comfort and protects mission-essential equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>Temperature:</strong> server rooms require much lower temperatures than people. Heat damages equipment and cuts services → redundancy (secondary chiller, air handling units).</li>\n<li><strong>Humidity:</strong> high humidity damages equipment and over time causes mold. Keep it <strong>below 60% relative humidity</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Air quality:</strong> mix of conditioned recirculated air and fresh air.<ul>\n<li>Natural ventilation is the preferred and typical one, but fresh air intakes are targets for introducing chemical or biological weapons.</li>\n<li>Protect the intakes and keep them away from sources of contamination, such as the emergency generator exhaust.</li>\n<li>Elevation protects: on the roof or high on the wall, next to the roofline, it is hard to introduce contaminants.</li>\n<li>Intake near the ground: physical barrier with walls and an inclined mesh cover. The mesh lets air through, prevents throwing objects, and its slope makes them roll off.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Figure 7-10 — Protecting Intakes for HVAC Systems (AHU = air handling unit). <strong>Pending:</strong> graphic detail of the figure (image).</li>\n<li>High-risk facilities: sealed HVAC with filtration against chemical, biological or radiological attack.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.7.2 Emergency Power</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In the design, define which equipment or systems need power at all times and what interruption they tolerate.<ul>\n<li>That sets how much power the emergency generator must provide.</li>\n<li>It also indicates whether a UPS (uninterruptible power supply) is needed and with what capacity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Not everything has to go on emergency power; the client decides.</li>\n<li>Key staff's computers: individual UPS units with time to shut down properly and lose less data. It reduces the size and cost of the main backup.</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> generator load tests; UPS battery inspection. Without maintenance the risk of failure at the critical moment rises.</li>\n<li>Even generator fuel goes bad if stored too long → regular maintenance plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.7.3 Other Utilities</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Other site utilities (4):</strong> communications (telephone and internet); potable water; gas; sanitary sewer.</li>\n<li>They are usually protected at the source because the U.S. Department of Homeland Security (DHS) considers them critical infrastructure and key resources.</li>\n<li>Even so, they can be attacked along their supply lines.</li>\n<li><strong>SCADA</strong> (supervisory control and data acquisition): system with which owners or managers control settings and operation of building utilities. It is not a utility, but it must be protected: a breach can bring down critical components and damage operations.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-7-e7\">7.8 Life Safety Systems</h3><ul>\n<li>In the US the requirements come from two codes: the Life Safety Code of the National Fire Protection Association (NFPA 101) and the International Building Code (IBC).</li>\n<li>They make it possible to maintain security together with safe egress.</li>\n<li><strong>Golden rule: security cannot override the life safety code.</strong></li>\n<li>Some states, such as California and New York, have additional requirements.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book calls both “building codes”; NFPA 101 is strictly a life safety code, not a building code (outside the book).</li>\n<li><strong>Life safety and security considerations (10):</strong><ol>\n<li>evacuation and exit points, especially those affected by access control locks, elevator lobby closures or delayed locking</li>\n<li>door alarms</li>\n<li>smoke detection and trouble alarms</li>\n<li>fire detection systems</li>\n<li>sprinklers or other suppression system</li>\n<li>chemical and biological hazard detection</li>\n<li>mass notification systems</li>\n<li>emergency lighting and wayfinding</li>\n<li>annunciator panels</li>\n<li>emergency planning</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Locks:</strong> NFPA 101 regulates the types of locks on doors. None may prevent the occupant from reaching a safe place or an exit.</li>\n<li>Every system must <strong>fail open or be failsafe</strong> (automatic release of locks) in an emergency.</li>\n<li>Functions such as motion sensors that unlock the door on approach may be required. According to the book, that arrangement also prevents unauthorized access once the building is empty.</li>\n<li><strong>Doors to sensitive, hazardous or exterior areas:</strong> with alarm and monitoring. If it sounds, send a response to investigate and secure the area again.<ul>\n<li>They become a nuisance if staff leave them open to get back in, e.g., when going out to smoke. Train: that breach is not acceptable.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Trouble alarms in important rooms:</strong> a nuisance if there is a lot of dust. Even so, always monitor and attend to them: there may be a greater hazard, such as fire.</li>\n<li><strong>Emergency lighting:</strong> key for evacuation; test and maintain it regularly.</li>\n<li><strong>Wayfinding:</strong> not mandatory in every facility, but it guides staff in a distressing event. E.g.: photoluminescent signs low down, in case smoke forces people to crawl.</li>\n<li><strong>Response time:</strong> the panel must be next to on-site monitoring, or there must be a system that alerts outside emergency services. The sooner the threat is identified, the sooner it is stabilized and the less damage there is.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.8.1 Evacuation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Security systems cannot get in the way of life safety; they must work together.</li>\n<li><strong>Key steps for planning evacuations (7):</strong><ol>\n<li>Designate primary and secondary (or more) evacuation routes from all areas, including exterior ones such as rooftops.</li>\n<li>Identify muster points away from the facility and other hazards.</li>\n<li>Identify and train the personnel (security or employees) who will help with the evacuation.</li>\n<li>Identify those who need special assistance and locate the special equipment they require immediately.</li>\n<li>Locate the special equipment needed to evacuate (e.g., escape ladders).</li>\n<li>Locate fire extinguishers, smoke detectors and first aid kits.</li>\n<li>Locate the shutoff valves for gas, water and electricity.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Evacuation route:</strong> the easiest and closest, free of obstacles, to an exit. Corridors wide enough.</li>\n<li>Routes and areas of refuge: always clear, never used as storage.</li>\n<li>If possible, avoid exits that lead directly to parking lots or public areas with secondary hazards: car bombs or snipers.</li>\n<li>On exiting, go straight to the muster point for the count by the designated safety officers.</li>\n<li><strong>Muster points:</strong> easy for staff to recognize, not very obvious to an attacker. Identify them with colors or letters, not with department names that give away a target.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.8.2 Shelter-in-Place</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Sometimes it is better to shelter inside rather than go out. Examples: extreme weather (tornado) or an active shooter outside the facility.</li>\n<li>Every facility must have designated areas to gather occupants. They usually have isolated walls for more protection and are in the interior, e.g., stairwells.</li>\n<li><strong>Air quality when evacuation is not possible:</strong><ul>\n<li>HVAC mechanisms that shut off the air supply in a fire;</li>\n<li>duct filters that remove particles, including some biological hazards;</li>\n<li>ultraviolet (UV) light in ducts that kills some biological hazards;</li>\n<li>sensors for specific chemicals that shut off the airflow.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chapter close</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Architecture and engineering are part of the integrated asset protection strategy.</li>\n<li>A facility does not need to look like a fortress, but it can be protected like one.</li>\n<li>Correct design: several layers of protection; it starts with the risk assessment; then elements, systems and materials are chosen.</li>\n<li>Man-made hazards: explosion, intrusion, workplace violence, crime, cyberattacks. Natural: catastrophic weather, earthquakes, wildfires. All systems must work together.</li>\n<li>Total security is not guaranteed, but risks can be managed.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-7-e8\">7.9 Publications Relevant to Security Architecture and Engineering</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Issuer</th>\n<th>Publications</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>American Society of Civil Engineers (ASCE)</td>\n<td>Blast Protection of Buildings, ASCE/SEI 59-11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)</td>\n<td>Guidance for Protecting Building Environments from Airborne Chemical, Biological, or Radiological Attacks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FEMA</td>\n<td>E155, Building Design for Homeland Security · FEMA 426, Reference Manual to Mitigate Potential Terrorist Attacks Against Buildings · FEMA 427, Primer for Design of Commercial Buildings to Mitigate Terrorist Attacks · FEMA 428, Primer to Design Safe School Projects in Case of Terrorist Attacks · FEMA 429, Insurance, Finance, and Regulation Primer for Terrorism Risk Management in Buildings · FEMA 452, Risk Assessment: A How-To Guide to Mitigate Potential Terrorist Attacks Against Buildings · FEMA 453, Design Guidance for Shelters and Safe Rooms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>General Services Administration (GSA)</td>\n<td>Standard Test Method for Glazing and Window Systems Subject to Dynamic Overpressure Loadings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National Fire Protection Association (NFPA)</td>\n<td>NFPA 101, Life Safety Code · NFPA 3000, Planning for ASHER response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whole Building Design Guide</td>\n<td>E. Hinman, Blast Safety of the Building Envelope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other</td>\n<td>IT Security Expert Advisory Group of the Trusted Information Sharing Network for Critical Infrastructure Resilience: Generic SCADA Risk Management Framework for Australian Critical Infrastructure · U.S. Department of Homeland Security: several publications of the Industrial Control Systems Cyber Emergency Response Team</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Unified Facilities Criteria (UFC)</strong> — FOUO = For Official Use Only</p>\n<ul>\n<li>UFC 4-010-01 — DoD Minimum Antiterrorism Standards for Buildings, with Change</li>\n<li>UFC 4-010-02 — DoD Minimum Antiterrorism Standoff Distances for Buildings (FOUO)</li>\n<li>UFC 4-010-03 — Security Engineering: Physical Security Measures for High-Risk Personnel</li>\n<li>UFC 4-010-05 — Sensitive Compartmented Information Facilities Planning, Design, and Construction, with Change 1</li>\n<li>UFC 4-020-01 — DoD Security Engineering Facilities Planning Manual</li>\n<li>UFC 4-020-02FA — Security Engineering: Concept Design (FOUO)</li>\n<li>UFC 4-020-03FA — Security Engineering: Final Design (FOUO)</li>\n<li>UFC 4-021-01 — Design and O&amp;M: Mass Notification Systems, with Change 1</li>\n<li>UFC 4-021-02 — Electronic Security Systems</li>\n<li>UFC 4-022-01 — Security Engineering: Entry Control Facilities/Access Control Points</li>\n<li>UFC 4-022-02 — Selection and Application of Vehicle Barriers, with Change 1</li>\n<li>UFC 4-022-03 — Security Fences and Gates</li>\n<li>UFC 4-023-03 — Design of Buildings to Resist Progressive Collapse, with Change 2</li>\n<li>UFC 4-023-07 — Design to Resist Direct Fire Weapons Effects</li>\n<li>UFC 4-024-01 — Security Engineering: Procedures for Designing Airborne Chemical, Biological, and Radiological Protection for Buildings</li>\n<li>UFC 4-025-01 — Security Engineering: Waterfront Security</li>\n<li>Useful pattern: all the security UFCs start with 4 (Series 4).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> UFC 4-010-01 says “with Change” without a change number (book typo).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> ASHER = Active Shooter/Hostile Event Response; O&amp;M = operation and maintenance; SEI = Structural Engineering Institute (outside the book).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the guidance attributed here to the CDC appears in the references as NIOSH 2002; NIOSH is part of the CDC (outside the book).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-7-e9\">Key Terms</h3><p>security architecture and design · design basis threat (DBT) · risk assessment · natural hazards · human-caused hazards · Unified Facilities Criteria (UFC) · Whole Building Design Guide · design specifications · change review board · failure point · new construction · retrofit/renovation · four Ds (deter, detect, deny, delay) · standoff distance · security zones · perimeter · intrusion detection system (IDS) · no cut/no climb fencing · spillover · light pollution · metal halide · high-pressure sodium · incendiary thermal effect · shock/blast wave · reflective pressure · overpressure · fragmentation · progressive collapse · facade · sound and sight lines · open floor plan · closed (compartmentalized) floor plan · glazing · annealed glass · performance condition · witness panel · bite · blast curtain · catch bar · serpentine barriers · retractable bollards · site utilities · back-feed · HVAC · fresh air intake · relative humidity · emergency power · UPS · load test · SCADA · life safety · NFPA 101 · International Building Code (IBC) · fail open / failsafe · trouble alarm · annunciator panel · mass notification · wayfinding · evacuation route · muster point · shelter-in-place</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"7.1 Design Overview"},{"id":"e1","t":"7.2 Codes and Regulations"},{"id":"e2","t":"7.3 Project Requirements"},{"id":"e3","t":"7.4 Type of Construction"},{"id":"e4","t":"7.5 Site Layout"},{"id":"e5","t":"7.6 Material Selection"},{"id":"e6","t":"7.7 Site Utilities"},{"id":"e7","t":"7.8 Life Safety Systems"},{"id":"e8","t":"7.9 Publications Relevant to Security Architecture and Engineering"},{"id":"e9","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"7.4 Type of Construction","h":"the text speaks of “figures” in the plural, but it is a single figure (7.4). It has typos (“Majrity”, “Constrction”) and two lines without bullets (“Warrantied systems”, “Permit requirements”), which read as separate items."},{"s":"7.5 Site Layout","h":"do not confuse with the functions of a physical protection system, <em>detect, delay, respond</em> (outside the book)."},{"s":"7.6 Material Selection","h":"in the book, row 3b appears at the end of the table; here it is in its logical order. The table text came with a garbled font encoding and was read by deciphering it."},{"s":"7.8 Life Safety Systems","h":"the book calls both “building codes”; NFPA 101 is strictly a life safety code, not a building code (outside the book)."},{"s":"7.9 Publications Relevant to Security Architecture and Engineering","h":"UFC 4-010-01 says “with Change” without a change number (book typo)."},{"s":"7.9 Publications Relevant to Security Architecture and Engineering","h":"ASHER = Active Shooter/Hostile Event Response; O&amp;M = operation and maintenance; SEI = Structural Engineering Institute (outside the book)."},{"s":"7.9 Publications Relevant to Security Architecture and Engineering","h":"the guidance attributed here to the CDC appears in the references as NIOSH 2002; NIOSH is part of the CDC (outside the book)."}]},"physical-8":{"html":"<ul>\n<li>Two complementary perspectives:<ul>\n<li>integration <strong>within</strong> the physical security domain;</li>\n<li>integration <strong>among</strong> security disciplines, including physical.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The 4 levels of integration</strong> (from narrowest to broadest)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>English term</th>\n<th>Section</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>integrated electronic security systems</td>\n<td>8.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>integrated physical security elements</td>\n<td>8.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>integrated security programs</td>\n<td>8.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>integration in enterprise risk management</td>\n<td>8.4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>All four are highly relevant to the profession and to creating physical security strategies.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the list calls level 4 “integration in enterprise risk management”; the title of 8.4 is “Integration of ERM and ESRM”. It is the same level.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-8-e0\">8.1 Integrated Electronic Security Systems</h3><ul>\n<li>Video surveillance, access control, sensors, alarms and other elements interact and usually work together.</li>\n<li>That complexity helps, but it demands attention to detail, a lot of planning and testing.</li>\n<li><strong>Network backbone:</strong> almost all systems travel over a network.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Independent network</td>\n<td><strong>preferred approach</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Part of the enterprise network</td>\n<td>performance problems due to limited bandwidth or sporadic traffic spikes; difficulties with permissions to use the network</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Systems have become more complex and integrated because of network complexity.</li>\n<li><strong>Good option: a single source</strong> responsible for preventive and remedial maintenance. As a single point of contact, it diagnoses the cause and manages the solution.</li>\n<li><strong>System integrators:</strong> third parties that supply or maintain specific components. Other providers: network services, telecommunications and application software.</li>\n<li><strong>Example (renovation, expansion or new project):</strong> the security professional forms a team of stakeholders to:<ul>\n<li>choose the system (or systems) that meets everyone's needs;</li>\n<li>make the most of interoperability;</li>\n<li>correct the deficiencies of the current system;</li>\n<li>anticipate future uses.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Key to success: an open and cooperative relationship between the integrator and the IT (information technology) team that supports the network.</li>\n<li>Integration is an opportunity and also new challenges. It requires planning and collaboration throughout the project life cycle.</li>\n<li><strong>Other implications:</strong> costs, dependencies and the challenge of securing the security systems themselves.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-8-e1\">8.2 Integrated Physical Security Elements</h3><ul>\n<li>All elements must operate as part of a comprehensive physical security strategy.</li>\n<li><strong>3 general categories:</strong> electronic security systems; structural security measures; human capital.</li>\n<li><strong>Potential components of any strategy (22; non-exhaustive list):</strong><ol>\n<li>architecture and engineering</li>\n<li>facility layout and design landscaping</li>\n<li>natural access control</li>\n<li>electronic access control systems</li>\n<li>natural surveillance</li>\n<li>video surveillance systems</li>\n<li>doors and locking systems</li>\n<li>fencing, gates, and walls</li>\n<li>structural barriers (e.g., bollards and wedge barriers)</li>\n<li>visitor management systems</li>\n<li>threat intelligence sources</li>\n<li>incident and case management systems</li>\n<li>sensors</li>\n<li>alarms</li>\n<li>intrusion detection systems</li>\n<li>communications systems</li>\n<li>lighting (security and general)</li>\n<li>security officer force</li>\n<li>concierge or reception staff</li>\n<li>auxiliary personnel</li>\n<li>outsource providers</li>\n<li>canine services</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> “facility layout and design landscaping” appears this way on a single line; it could be two elements (“facility layout and design” and “landscaping”).</li>\n<li><strong>Objective:</strong> choose tools from the list and other measures for a tailored strategy, according to the facility's mission, nature, location and threat profile. Everything must be coordinated like an orchestra.</li>\n<li><strong>Human element:</strong> security officers are often a key component. What is critical is assigning the right person to the role and to the job requirements.<ul>\n<li>Benefits of a good fit: shift scheduling, client satisfaction, retention and greater effectiveness.</li>\n<li>It can make the difference between a lawsuit or none, keeping a client or even saving a life.</li>\n<li>Example: an officer who is good at a fixed post or patrol may not be ready for the security operations center (SOC). They may not know how to operate or interpret electronic or building control systems.</li>\n<li>A staffing mismatch can be disastrous, whether intentional, due to understaffing or due to a sudden increase in requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-8-e2\">8.3 Integrated Security Programs</h3><ul>\n<li>Physical security is only one element of the total protection strategy.</li>\n<li><strong>The protection target (asset) can be:</strong> facility, campus, community, project, person, physical object, other property (e.g., money) or an intangible.</li>\n<li><strong>Disciplines with which physical security is integrated (9):</strong><ol>\n<li>personnel security</li>\n<li>IT security</li>\n<li>information assets protection</li>\n<li>technical security</li>\n<li>investigations</li>\n<li>training and awareness</li>\n<li>executive protection</li>\n<li>product security</li>\n<li>crime prevention</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Successful security design:</strong> recognizes technological advances on all fronts, not just in electronic systems. It integrates <strong>3 primary elements:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Points from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Architectural aspects</td>\n<td>one of the most significant factors; limiting access to the building and routing corridors through control points makes it easier to control access</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security systems</td>\n<td>electronic measures complement procedures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational factors</td>\n<td>good operating procedures from the design phase; otherwise the human-machine interface will be inefficient</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Technology = force multiplier:</strong> it does not replace personnel. It increases their capabilities and provides checks and balances against individual misconduct.</li>\n<li><strong>Human element</strong> (decision-making, common sense, awareness): it is integrated in the design phase, not after the system is designed and implemented.</li>\n<li>Successful organizations know that their greatest security resources are their employees.<ul>\n<li>Understanding how they will use the technology and how they will respond makes it possible to prevent most incidents and respond well to the rest.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Operating procedures by threat level: before buying or installing any system.</strong><ul>\n<li>Installing equipment and software without an operating philosophy can leave systems that increase the risk of harm.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If everyone follows established, easy procedures, abnormal activity stands out (Patterson 2013).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-8-e3\">8.4 Integration of Enterprise Risk Management and Enterprise Security Risk Management</h3><ul>\n<li>It is the final and broadest aspect of integrated asset protection: the enterprise level.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition and features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Enterprise Security Risk Management (ESRM)</td>\n<td>strategic security management approach that links security practice to the organization's mission and goals, with globally accepted risk management principles. Foundations: holistic risk management, transparency, governance and partnership with stakeholders. Operates in the context of mission, vision, values, operating environment and stakeholders. Detail in the POA (Protection of Assets): Security Management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enterprise Risk Management (ERM)</td>\n<td>process or structure with which the organization identifies and understands its risks, plus the strategic plans to prepare for and manage them. Covers all key risks: financial, operational, market, strategic, reputational and security</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Key difference:</strong> ERM covers all of the organization's key risks; ESRM, the security ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Relationship between the two:</strong><ul>\n<li>Not all organizations have an operating ERM structure, even if they address risk in various ways.</li>\n<li>Since almost all have security risk, ESRM can be a subcomponent of ERM.</li>\n<li>High-impact security risks may fall among those that ERM manages. Not all ESRM risks reach that level.</li>\n<li>That is why ESRM is an independent risk management system, whether or not there is ERM.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Both combine many functions of the organization to focus on its key assets and on a very complete strategy to protect them.</li>\n<li><strong>Integration chain</strong> (from smallest to broadest): components → electronic security system package → physical security package → total security program → ERM domain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 8.1 — Enterprise Security Domain Functions</strong> (8 functions)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Zone of the figure</th>\n<th>Functions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ESRM side</td>\n<td>Emergency management · Business continuity · Occupational health and safety · Security/asset protection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Central zone</td>\n<td>Legal strategy and liability management · Insurance and indemnity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ERM side</td>\n<td>Public relations · Competitive intelligence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> the figure is a graphic; the split by zones comes from the position of the text. Confirm in the image whether the central zone is the intersection of ESRM and ERM.</li>\n<li>Part of the figure's text came with a garbled font encoding and was read by deciphering it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What it means for the security professional</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Physical security usually takes part in an ERM approach. It is worth knowing the concept and incorporating it into planning.</li>\n<li>Example: the security director of a financial institution must know the liability for thefts at or near automated teller machines (ATM). They must create a program to reduce that risk.</li>\n<li>Security takes part in almost any incident, whether or not it is within its direct scope. Examples: strategic risks such as legal or credit liability, or support for a civil investigation.</li>\n<li>Those incidents are risks for the organization and may require (or be expected to prompt) a security response.</li>\n<li>Holistic integration = seeing the big picture. It is crucial in an increasingly connected and fast-moving world.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-8-e4\">Key Terms</h3><p>integration · integrated electronic security systems · integrated physical security elements · integrated security programs · enterprise risk management (ERM) · enterprise security risk management (ESRM) · network backbone · single source · preventive and remedial maintenance · systems integrator · interoperability · securing the security systems · electronic security systems · structural security measures · human capital · natural access control · natural surveillance · threat intelligence sources · visitor management systems · security operations center (SOC) · staffing mismatch · force multiplier · checks and balances · architectural aspects · security systems · operational factors · operating procedures · human element · holistic risk management · governance · transparency · stakeholder partnership · Enterprise Security Domain Functions · big picture</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"8.1 Integrated Electronic Security Systems"},{"id":"e1","t":"8.2 Integrated Physical Security Elements"},{"id":"e2","t":"8.3 Integrated Security Programs"},{"id":"e3","t":"8.4 Integration of Enterprise Risk Management and Enterprise Security Risk Management"},{"id":"e4","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"Integrated Security and Protection Strategies","h":"the list calls level 4 “integration in enterprise risk management”; the title of 8.4 is “Integration of ERM and ESRM”. It is the same level."},{"s":"8.2 Integrated Physical Security Elements","h":"“facility layout and design landscaping” appears this way on a single line; it could be two elements (“facility layout and design” and “landscaping”)."}]},"physical-9":{"html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Barrier</strong> — ASIS definition (ASIS International 2021): natural or man-made obstacle to the movement or direction of people, animals, vehicles or materials.</li>\n<li>Structural security includes: overall structure of buildings and facilities, physical barriers, locking systems and lighting.</li>\n<li>Natural barriers are expanded on in Chapter 10.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-9-e0\">9.1 Barriers</h3><ul>\n<li>A building's structural components form a barrier, that is, a layer of security. They must be understood, leveraged and managed within the physical security strategy.</li>\n<li>Components: walls, floors, ceilings, roofs, doors, windows and other structures.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.1.1 Walls</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Walls resist penetration better than doors, windows, vents and other openings. Even so, almost all can be breached with the right tools.</li>\n<li>Sometimes the wall is the adversary's best route for a forced entry.</li>\n<li>Threats: cutting tools; vehicles that ram cinder block, wood frame and other common walls; explosives that open holes to get in.</li>\n<li>Thickening or improving the wall gives only a <strong>moderate</strong> delay against explosives, although the amount of explosive needed grows greatly with thickness.</li>\n<li>Improvements pay off more against hand, power or thermal tools.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Most common wall types (8):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Gloss</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>reinforced concrete</td>\n<td>reinforced concrete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>expanded metal/concrete</td>\n<td>expanded metal/concrete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>concrete block</td>\n<td>concrete block</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>clay tile</td>\n<td>clay block/tile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>precast concrete tee sections</td>\n<td>precast concrete T sections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>corrugated asbestos</td>\n<td>corrugated asbestos</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sheet metal</td>\n<td>sheet metal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>wood frame</td>\n<td>wood frame</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Reinforced concrete:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is used where there are sensitive materials and is believed to be formidable. Tests show that ordinary reinforced concrete is penetrated quickly.</li>\n<li>Reason: it is designed for structural loads, not to delay. In conventional construction, the strength, thickness, size and spacing of the reinforcement are set by the structure, not by security.</li>\n<li><strong>Two or more walls in series delay more than a single wall of the same total thickness.</strong> They require several efforts and carrying tools through each wall.</li>\n<li>With explosives, the contained pressure can bring down the roof and neighboring structures; the debris acts as an extra barrier.</li>\n<li>Rebar lengthens the delay: after an explosion it is usually intact and has to be removed. Removing the rebar usually takes longer than removing the concrete.</li>\n<li>More delay: more rebar, thicker rebar or smaller center-to-center spacing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.1.2 Doors</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The weakest part of a barrier determines its final value.</strong> That part is usually the door.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Door classification (7):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Gloss</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>standard industrial doors</td>\n<td>standard industrial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>personnel doors</td>\n<td>personnel (pedestrian)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>attack- and bullet-resistant doors</td>\n<td>attack- and bullet-resistant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vehicle access doors</td>\n<td>vehicle access</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vault doors</td>\n<td>vault</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>blast-resistant doors</td>\n<td>blast-resistant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>turnstile gates</td>\n<td>turnstiles</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Walls gain delay with thicker or composite materials. Doors are a weak link because of their functional requirements and their hardware.</li>\n<li>Typical example: heavy concrete walls with hollow steel doors, ordinary frames and hinges.</li>\n<li><strong>Balanced design principle:</strong> door, frame, hinges, bolts and lock must give the same delay as floors, walls and roofs.</li>\n<li>If the door assembly cannot be improved enough, it may not be cost-effective to improve the building structure.</li>\n<li>Large manufacturers offer attack- and bullet-resistant doors; properly installed, they increase resistance to penetration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Typical exterior door:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thickness 1¾ in (44 mm); 16 or 18 gauge steel sheets (1.5 or 1.2 mm).</li>\n<li>Hollow core or composite core; it may have glass or louvers.</li>\n<li>Composite core: noncombustible material that dampens sound, normally polyurethane foam or board.</li>\n<li>In hollow doors, light-gauge vertical channels are sometimes added for rigidity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Steel personnel doors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Single or double. Exterior ones open outward, with the door closer on the inside.</li>\n<li>Mortised hinges with removable or non-removable pins.</li>\n<li><strong>Panic bars:</strong> make the door a one-way barrier. They are easier to defeat when entering and make the attacker's escape easier.</li>\n<li><strong>Egress delay of 30 to 45 s:</strong> prevents opening during that time. It is overridden if the fire alarm or the automatic extinguishing system is activated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Door attack times:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attack</th>\n<th>Time or data</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Power tools on a light sheet-steel door</td>\n<td>crawl-through hole in 3 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explosives</td>\n<td>noisy and leave evidence, which helps detection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Picking a standard lock</td>\n<td>\\~1 min average for a skilled locksmith</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pipe or strap wrench on a key-in-knob lock</td>\n<td>well under 1 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exterior door hinges (normally 3)</td>\n<td>\\~1 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crawl-through hole in flat, tempered or wired glass (hand tools)</td>\n<td>15 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Forcing louvers or mesh</td>\n<td>30 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Industrial doors</td>\n<td>highly variable, from 10 s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Picking only works if there is a keyway; the pipe wrench, only with exposed hardware.</li>\n<li>Exit-only doors: flush mounting, no exterior hardware.</li>\n<li>If a keyway is needed: high-security locks with long picking times. Door sensors reduce the lock's vulnerability.</li>\n<li>Exposed exterior hinge pins = natural target. Even non-removable ones fall quickly to hand tools, thermal tools or explosives.</li>\n<li>Exterior doors are vulnerable to vehicle ramming and to rescue tools: breaching shotgun rounds (police) and hydraulic spreaders (firefighters).</li>\n<li>Industrial doors need improved designs to match the structure's delay.</li>\n<li>Fire and building codes may require interior panic bars.</li>\n<li>If it cannot be replaced, the old door is upgraded. In new construction or full replacement: new high-security attack-resistant doors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Upgrade of steel personnel doors in pressed steel frames:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Order or measure</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1st Eliminate unnecessary doors</td>\n<td>first step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2nd Eliminate unnecessary exterior windows, louvers, knobs and keyways</td>\n<td>next step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Steel plates on the surface</td>\n<td>more resistance to hand tools and light power tools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy-duty hinges</td>\n<td>support the added weight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grout the frames with concrete</td>\n<td>reinforces the supporting structure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wood core (especially redwood) between plates</td>\n<td>delay against thermal cutting 3 to 4 times greater than an air gap</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Prying attack on the lock/frame area:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The frame strike is separated from the latch. A separation of ½ in (13 mm) to ¾ in (19 mm) is enough.</li>\n<li><strong>Astragal:</strong> strip of sheet steel welded or bolted to the door. Same height as the door, ≥ 2 in (51 mm) wide and 1 in (25 mm) of overlap over the frame.</li>\n<li>Fill the frame with concrete at least 18 in (45 cm) above the frame strike, on both sides of the frame.</li>\n<li>Exterior personnel doors: high-security locks. A multiple-deadbolt system with key on both sides almost eliminates the prying attack.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hinges:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are defeated in \\~1 min by removing pins or cutting the knuckles.</li>\n<li>Welding the pin tip only works against hand tools. If the knuckles are cut with a power or thermal tool, it is still \\~1 min.</li>\n<li>Upgrades: stud-in-hole hinges and full-length hinges.</li>\n<li><strong>Z-strip:</strong> bolted or welded to the back face of the door. When prying, it butts against the frame or the stop, and forces more force and larger tools.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Panic bars (panic or crash bars):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are defeated in \\~1 min with small hand tools, with less noise than thermal cutting. If noise does not matter, with power tools.</li>\n<li>Upgrade 1: bent metal plate with drill-resistant steel section. It prevents chiseling and wire hooking of the bar.</li>\n<li>Upgrade 2: electronic control. The bar must be held pressed for a set time before an electronic bolt is released. It gives the officer time to assess by video and respond.</li>\n<li>Upgrade 3: remove exterior knobs and hardware on emergency exits. It makes prying harder without affecting evacuation.</li>\n<li>Louvers and glass in exterior doors: minimize them. If needed, reduce them so that no one fits and reinforce them from the inside with mesh or a bar grille.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.1.3 Windows and Other Openings</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Without upgrades, windows barely delay. They must follow balanced design so as not to be the weak link.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Types of commercial glass (ASIS International 2021):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Characteristics</th>\n<th>Use or limitation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Annealed or plate glass</td>\n<td>manufactured to control residual stresses and be workable. Examples: plate, float, sheet, rolled and some patterned</td>\n<td>breaks into large, dangerous fragments; codes may restrict it in door panels and fire exits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tempered</td>\n<td>treated to resist breakage (reheated and rapidly cooled); more mechanical and thermal strength</td>\n<td>required by codes because it breaks into small pieces. Even so, it falls in seconds to impact tools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wired</td>\n<td>some resistance to large objects, but may shatter. ¼ in (6 mm), diamond, square or hexagonal mesh</td>\n<td>main use: maintaining fire rating in fire doors and windows. It is penetrated in \\~20 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Laminated</td>\n<td>2 or more sheets of glass (annealed float, sheet or plate) bonded to plastic layers. The pieces stay stuck together</td>\n<td>must be in a frame fixed to the structure. <strong>Preferred type against explosions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bullet- or burglar-resistant</td>\n<td>laminate of several layers of glass, polycarbonate and other films</td>\n<td>various levels of ballistic resistance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Standard glass: very fragile; it is penetrated in a few seconds. Upgrades: thick security glass or grilles of expanded steel mesh or other metal grilles.</li>\n<li>¼ in (6 mm) laminated safety glass: 30 s. 9/16 in (14 mm) security glass: 15 min with hand tools to open a hole.</li>\n<li>Security glass is not transparent armor, but it resists forced penetration better than standard glass.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Transparent plastics (glass substitutes):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Some are combustible; fire codes may restrict them.</li>\n<li>Acrylics (Lucite, Plexiglas) under 1 in (25 mm): they break in under 10 s.</li>\n<li>Polycarbonates: they resist impact almost like bullet-resistant glass. ½ in (13 mm) Lexan: up to 2 min against hand tools.</li>\n<li>Thermal attack on polycarbonate: \\~1 min, but it burns and releases toxic gases.</li>\n<li><strong>Glass/polycarbonate composite:</strong> polycarbonate core between two layers of glass. Created for prisons, now used in all types of facilities. Good delay against hand tools.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The window as a system</strong> (glass + frame + mounting structure), for both delay and explosion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The strength and weight of frames vary greatly. Some manufacturers reinforce the sash (security sash) but not the frame.</li>\n<li>A window in a door is penetrated in seconds.</li>\n<li>Some special frames hide materials that resist cutting.</li>\n<li>On windows that open, the latch can be the weak link; it must not be reachable from outside. Options: fixed windows or sturdier latches.</li>\n<li>Fastening the frame to the structure: more or heavier hardware, or weld the frame's fin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other window materials:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>Limitation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Window bars</td>\n<td>steel bars that prevent using the opening as access</td>\n<td>only where building and fire codes allow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Window film or fragment retention film</td>\n<td>adheres on the inside; reinforces and holds broken glass. Uses: (1) protection against intrusion or \"smash and grab\"; (2) fewer injuries from shards in an explosion; (3) fewer injuries from projectiles in extreme weather (hurricane, tornado)</td>\n<td>defeated by repeated attacks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blast curtains</td>\n<td>fabric reinforced against material flying out in an explosion</td>\n<td>do not protect against intrusion; they are a people protection measure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security shutters</td>\n<td>roll-up (horizontal aluminum or PVC —polyvinyl chloride— slats that rise into a box) or accordion (vertical slats that slide to the sides)</td>\n<td>manual or electric operation with remote control, weather sensors or timers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Other openings:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Industrial facilities have many unmonitored openings: ventilation ducts, utility tunnels and service openings. They are possible intrusion routes.</li>\n<li>Few delay a determined adversary much, especially if they were designed for easy maintenance. Since they are hidden routes, they must have <strong>barricade and sensors</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Utility ports:</strong> any unmonitored framed opening that is neither a door nor a window.</li>\n<li>Their grilles serve for personnel safety, decoration or against pests, but give little security. They may have covers without a lock or interior barrier.</li>\n<li>Standard windows and utility ports are weak links. <strong>Most unimproved windows are penetrated with hand tools in under 30 s.</strong></li>\n<li>Upgrades: grilles, bars, expanded metal mesh or screens. Gratings of steel mesh, expanded metal, bars or tubes reduce the opening so that no one fits.</li>\n<li><strong>These covers are placed at the detection point or after it.</strong></li>\n<li>Tunnels between buildings: poorly protected, sometimes only with covers or manholes without a lock.</li>\n<li>Pipe chases: congested, but with room for maintenance. HVAC (heating, ventilation and air conditioning) ducts can be a route.</li>\n<li>Tunnels, manholes, roof and wall openings for equipment and ducts: reinforce with interior barriers.</li>\n<li>New buildings: windows smaller than a person and several small openings for ducts.</li>\n<li>Very narrow windows, 4 in (102 mm) or less: even without glass the opening has to be enlarged to get through.</li>\n<li>In existing buildings windows can be eliminated and the opening brought to the same delay as the wall.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.1.4 Roofs and Floors</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Functions: they protect from weather, provide work surface and are barriers.</li>\n<li>Evaluate threats from determined adversaries with hand, power and thermal tools, and explosives, combined.</li>\n<li>They are built the same way, but differ in thickness, type and amount of steel, and concrete strength according to load.</li>\n<li><strong>Floors resist more than roofs:</strong> the main structure protects them and they carry more load.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Current roof types (7):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Gloss</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>prestressed concrete tee beam</td>\n<td>prestressed concrete T beam</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>metal subdeck and reinforced concrete</td>\n<td>metal subdeck and reinforced concrete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>metal roof deck with lightweight concrete</td>\n<td>metal roof deck with lightweight concrete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>metal roof deck with insulation</td>\n<td>metal roof deck with insulation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>metal roof</td>\n<td>metal roof</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>reinforced concrete beam and slab</td>\n<td>reinforced concrete beam and slab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>wood sheathing with membrane</td>\n<td>wood sheathing with membrane</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Roof upgrade methods (7):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Membranes with embedded mesh.</li>\n<li>Several inches of rigid insulation.</li>\n<li>Concrete reinforced with deformed steel bars and expanded steel mesh.</li>\n<li>Thicker rebar arranged in several rows or layers.</li>\n<li>More fasteners and more structural members in corrugated roofs.</li>\n<li>In metal systems: mechanical fasteners or joints, continuous welding and thicker-gauge material.</li>\n<li>Thicker rebar to reinforce the flange of precast T beams.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Upgrades <strong>below the roof</strong> usually give the best value. Tests suggest that a barrier under the roof is more effective than reinforcing the roof itself.</li>\n<li>Sometimes it is added without major modifications. It forces a second penetration, in a reduced space and perhaps with other tools.</li>\n<li><strong>Optimal distance between roof and secondary barrier: 10 to 12 in (25.4 to 30.5 cm).</strong> The small space creates a \"hole effect\" or hinders the attack.</li>\n<li>Materials: quarry screen, bank-vault-type expanded steel mesh and floor gratings.</li>\n<li>Earth covering: increases delay through roof and walls. Buried and cut-and-cover structures use it against access and explosions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.1.5 Fencing and Perimeter Walls</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>One of the most common perimeter measures, in government and the private sector.</li>\n<li>Dual function: visual line of demarcation and barrier that gives delay and some resistance to intrusion or attack.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Chain Link Fencing</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Advantages:</strong> the most popular, durable and inexpensive for most uses. It installs quickly. Properly installed, it gives reasonable protection against vehicles, people and animals. It allows seeing from one side to the other, if desired.</li>\n<li>Fabric: wire coiled and interwoven into a continuous mesh, without knots or ties, except at the selvage.</li>\n<li><strong>Knuckling:</strong> pairs of neighboring ends are interlaced and bent into a closed loop.</li>\n<li><strong>Twisting and barbing:</strong> pairs of ends are twisted into a closed helix of one machine turn (3 full twists) and cut at an angle to leave a point.</li>\n<li>Coatings: zinc, aluminum or plastic/polymer.</li>\n<li><strong>Constant conclusion of tests:</strong> it is crossed easily, even without aids such as blankets, wire cutters or shears. It gives minimal protection against determined adversaries.</li>\n<li>It does deter some vehicles, animals, casual intruders and opportunists.</li>\n<li><strong>Its greatest use: marking the property boundary</strong>, so that the intruder cannot claim not to have known they were trespassing.</li>\n<li>There are many heights; it serves for almost any perimeter you want to define or where you want some delay.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Causes of poor-quality or ineffective fences (4):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Large-mesh fabric.</li>\n<li>Wire of insufficient gauge.</li>\n<li>Light, thin-walled posts and rails.</li>\n<li>Shallow post holes.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Manufacturing and installation standards: CLFMI (Chain Link Fence Manufacturers Institute), ASTM (formerly American Society for Testing and Materials) and BSI (British Standards Institution).</li>\n<li>Different ASTM standards apply depending on the fence material and the site.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Heights:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Height</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Marking boundaries or stopping small animals</td>\n<td>4 ft (1.2 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signs on boundary fence (property and danger warning)</td>\n<td>normally every 50 ft (15 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deterring people</td>\n<td>not less than 7 ft (2.1 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical security fence (Figure 9.1, CLFMI)</td>\n<td>8 ft (2.5 m) + top guard of 1 ft (0.3 m) with 3 strands of barbed wire</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book converts 8 ft as 2.5 m here and as 2.4 m for the electric fence. The correct value is 2.44 m.</li>\n<li>The installation points are indicative; the professional must consult ASTM and CLFMI for their application and area.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Chain Link Fence Installation</h5>\n<p><strong>Posts:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Terminal posts:</strong> ends, corners and gate supports.</li>\n<li><strong>Pull posts:</strong> terminals that brace a long run or a change in grade.</li>\n<li><strong>Line posts:</strong> all the others.</li>\n<li>Line posts: equal intervals, <strong>maximum 10 ft (3 m)</strong> on average center to center, measured parallel to the slope.</li>\n<li>Hole diameter: 4 times the largest section of the post; local conditions may change it.</li>\n<li>Depth: <strong>minimum 24 in (0.6 m) + 3 in (76 mm) for each foot (0.3 m) of height above 4 ft (1.2 m).</strong></li>\n<li>Own example: 8 ft fence → 24 + (4 × 3) = 36 in depth.</li>\n<li>Fill: 2,500 psi concrete (pounds per square inch; 17.2 MPa), projecting 2 in (50 mm) above ground. Crowned surface to shed water.</li>\n<li>Valid alternative if it equals or exceeds concrete: driven post with <strong>two steel blades</strong> driven diagonally through metal footplates bolted underground.</li>\n<li>The blades are at right angles to the fence and brace it front and back. It is compared to a tree root: the post bends or breaks before the anchor fails.</li>\n<li>Since the blades pass below the frost line, frost and thaw do not affect them.</li>\n<li><strong>In solid rock:</strong> depth = 3 times the largest section of the post; diameter = 0.5 in (13 mm) larger than that section.</li>\n<li>In rock: fill halfway with non-shrink hydraulic cement, sink the post plumb, fill without voids and crown the surface.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bracing:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Terminals are braced diagonally to the nearest line post when required. Otherwise: loose posts, loose fabric, poor appearance and a fence that invites penetration.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fabric of 6 ft (1.83 m) or less with top rail</td>\n<td>no braces required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fabric of more than 6 ft (1.83 m)</td>\n<td>braces mandatory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fabric of more than 12 ft (3.66 m)</td>\n<td>center rail mandatory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No top rail, any height</td>\n<td>braces at all terminals</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Angle between brace and ground: <strong>maximum 50°</strong>. The brace runs from each terminal to the adjacent line post or its footing.</li>\n<li>No top rail: tension wire from end to end of each run, fastened to the fabric within the top foot (0.3 m).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.1 (Typical Chain Link Security Fence Installation):</strong> front and side views. Labeled elements: concrete; fabric selvage; fabric tie; truss rod; brace rail; fabric; post spacing 10' max.; barb arm; line post; top rail; 3 strands of barbed wire; post cap; corner or end post; rail end; tension bands at 15\" centers; tension bar.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> the barb arm dimension reads \"4-5”\" in the extracted text; it is not clear whether it is 45° or a measurement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fabric:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gauge <strong>No. 9 AWG</strong> (American Wire Gauge) or heavier. Mesh openings <strong>of 2 in (50 mm) maximum</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> the diameter for gauge 9 that the book gives (0.148 in) corresponds to steel wire gauge, not AWG.</li>\n<li>Selvage on fences of 5 ft (1.5 m) or less: knuckled at both ends.</li>\n<li>Selvage on fences of more than 5 ft: knuckled at one end and twisted at the other.</li>\n<li>Mesh of less than 2 in (51 mm): all selvage knuckled.</li>\n<li>Fabric stretched to \\~2 in (51 mm) above hard ground or pavement and well secured to the posts.</li>\n<li>On soft ground, the fabric must go below the surface to compensate for ground movement.</li>\n<li>Against passing underneath: concrete apron at least 6 in thick; fabric extended below the bottom rail and embedded in concrete; pipe at the base of the fabric; or U-shaped stakes of \\~2 ft (0.6 m).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes 6 in (52 mm); the correct conversion is 152 mm.</li>\n<li>Culverts, channels, streams or other openings of 96 in² (619 cm²) or more: protect them with more fencing, grilles or other barriers without impeding drainage.</li>\n<li>Each run is fastened separately at all terminal posts after cutting the fabric.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wire breaking loads:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Gauge</th>\n<th>Diameter</th>\n<th>Breaking load</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 6</td>\n<td>0.192 in (5 mm)</td>\n<td>2,170 lb (984 kg)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 9</td>\n<td>0.148 in (3.8 mm)</td>\n<td>1,290 lb (585 kg)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 11</td>\n<td>0.120 in (3 mm)</td>\n<td>850 lb (386 kg)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 11 (sic)</td>\n<td>0.113 in (2.9 mm)</td>\n<td>750 lb (340 kg)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book repeats \"No. 11\" in two rows. According to its own Figure 9.2, 0.113 in is gauge 11½.</li>\n<li>Fastening: fabric to line posts every <strong>15 in (38 cm)</strong>; edges to top and bottom rails or tension wires every <strong>24 in (60.9 cm)</strong> at most.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fence top treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Top rail:</strong> horizontal member; fabric fastened with ties or clips every 2 ft (60.9 cm) at most. It improves appearance, but gives a handhold for climbing. Without a rail, use a top tension wire.</li>\n<li><strong>Top guard (barbed overhang):</strong> barbed wire overhang, normally at 45°. Its orientation depends on whether the threat is getting in or getting out.<ul>\n<li>Outward: makes it harder to enter from outside. It may encroach on the neighbor's land.</li>\n<li>Inward: makes it harder to climb out.</li>\n<li>Double overhang (inward and outward) or inverted-V arm.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>3 strands of barbed wire spaced 6 in (15.2 cm). The arms (outriggers) can be lengthened and strands added.</li>\n<li>The arms are fixed on top of the posts and must withstand <strong>250 lb (113 kg)</strong> applied on the outer strand.</li>\n<li><strong>Clear zone:</strong> remove boxes, ladders, containers, vehicles and equipment next to the fence, and prune overhanging branches (natural ladder).</li>\n<li><strong>Failing to maintain the clear zone is one of the most common mistakes and nullifies the fence's effectiveness.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fence gates:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The minimum compatible with efficient operation. All with locks.</li>\n<li>Common failure: the lateral stress when opening and closing weakens the corners. Tubular frames (round or square) welded at all corners.</li>\n<li>Same fabric as the fence, fastened to the frame every 15 in (38.1 cm).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Single-swing</td>\n<td>pedestrian walkways</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Double-swing</td>\n<td>vehicle entrances</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multifold</td>\n<td>any opening up to 60 ft (18.2 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overhead slide, single or double</td>\n<td>no room to swing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cantilever slide, single or double</td>\n<td>entrances where an overhead track would obstruct equipment taller than highway vehicles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertical-lift</td>\n<td>special uses, e.g., loading docks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hangar-type slide on narrow track</td>\n<td>very large openings</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Any of them can be motorized.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Turnstiles:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uses: stores, supermarkets, amusement parks, subways and corporate and industrial facilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Height</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Security</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Waist-high, \\~36 in (0.9 m)</td>\n<td>crowd control, charging admission, counting people</td>\n<td>little, unless always attended</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Full-height, \\~7 ft (2.1 m)</td>\n<td>completely surrounds the person passing</td>\n<td>can be automated with card, keypad or biometric</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Chain link variants:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Visual privacy: redwood or plastic slats inserted in the mesh, or fabric hung and well secured.</li>\n<li>Colors: hot-extruded PVC resin coating, up to 22 mil (0.022 in or 0.5 mm). Smooth and does not rust, ideal in marine areas.</li>\n<li>Standard PVC colors: green, olive green, brown and black.</li>\n<li>Interior use: e.g., in a warehouse, compartment for valuable material or information; it can also be closed on top.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.2 — Typical wire and mesh sizes</strong> (ASTM A392-11a, Standard Specification for Zinc-Coated Steel Chain-Link Fence Fabric):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Coated wire diameter (in)</th>\n<th>Gauge</th>\n<th>Mesh (in)</th>\n<th>Fabric heights (in)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0.192</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>36, 42, 48, 60, 72, 84, 96, 108, 120, 144</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.148</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>36, 42, 48, 60, 72, 84, 96, 108, 120, 144</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.148</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>36, 42, 48, 60, 72, 84, 96, 108, 120, 144</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.128</td>\n<td>10½</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>36, 42, 48, 60, 72, 84, 96, 108, 120, 144</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.120</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>36, 42, 48, 60, 72, 84 …</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.120</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>1¾</td>\n<td>… 96, 108, 120, 144</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.120</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>36, 42, 48, 60, 72, 84, 96, 108, 120, 144</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.120</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>6/8* (sic)</td>\n<td>36, 42, 48, 60, 72, 84, 96, 108, 120, 144</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.120</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>½*</td>\n<td>36, 42, 48, 60, 72, 84, 96, 108, 120, 144</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.120</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>3/8*</td>\n<td>36, 42, 48, 60, 72, 84, 96, 108, 120, 144</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.113</td>\n<td>11½</td>\n<td>2 1/5 (sic)</td>\n<td>36, 42, 48, 60, 72 …</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>* Sizes designed for security (small meshes).</li>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> incomplete cells (\"…\") in the 11/2, 11/1¾ and 11½ rows of the original. \"6/8\" and \"2 1/5\" appear that way in the text; probably 5/8 and 2⅛ (outside the book, verify).</li>\n<li>The professional uses this standard to choose wire, mesh and diamond count.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Applications for Decorative Fencing</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Factors when choosing a fence: security objectives, type, size, cost, location and appearance.</li>\n<li>Appearance depends on: level of security needed, purpose, environment, surrounding area, local codes or requirements and desired ambiance.</li>\n<li>Decorative fencing varies by material (wood, metal, composite), color, style or design.</li>\n<li><strong>Wall</strong> of brick, stone, concrete or others: it blends better with the architecture, but usually costs more and limits visibility inward and outward.</li>\n<li>Fences and walls serve as supports for lighting, sensors and other systems.</li>\n<li>They serve low, medium or high security objectives.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical Asset Protection Standard (ASIS International 2021):</strong> ornamental fences of carbon steel, galvanized steel or aluminum, assemblable in various forms.</li>\n<li>Depending on the application, the following is defined: type, style, height, picket and pale spacing, rail spacing, top treatment (including curved anti-climb system) and structural performance.</li>\n<li>High-security novelties: integrated barbs on top (Figure 9.4) and top inclined outward, like the chain link arms (bars bent at 45° with barbs).</li>\n<li>Combination used at some sites: decorative medium-high security vehicle gates with a chain link perimeter.</li>\n<li>With current offerings and costs, it is often just as sound or sounder to use decorative fencing with a uniform design.</li>\n<li>Figure 9.3: decorative fence next to a masonry wall, for elevation and landscaping.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Other Fencing Types</h5>\n<p><strong>Cable System Fencing (Figure 9.5):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>High-strength cables; used mainly for vehicle access control.</li>\n<li>They allow considerable deflection and partial penetration before resisting. The deflection depends on the distance between concrete anchors (dead men), typically <strong>\\~200 ft (61 m)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Therefore their installation differs somewhat from that of other fences and walls.</li>\n<li>Figure 9.5: cable fence combined with vegetation (Department of Defense, 1999).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Expanded Metal and Welded Wire Fabric:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cost: more expensive than chain link and cheaper than perforated metal or wrought ironwork. They look like a net.</li>\n<li>Expanded metal: does not unravel, is hard and very difficult to cut. It comes in uncoated, painted or galvanized steel, aluminum and stainless steel.</li>\n<li><strong>4 types of expanded metal:</strong> standard or regular; grating; flattened; architectural or decorative.</li>\n<li>Welded wire fabric: cheaper than expanded metal; for low-risk applications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wooden Fences:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>For low security. They must be hard to climb and strong enough for the desired level.</li>\n<li>Upgrades: barbed wire, razor wire or metal spikes.</li>\n<li>To delay: vertical boards of <strong>1¾ in (44 mm)</strong> wide at most; horizontal sections spaced <strong>50 in (1.27 m)</strong> apart and on the protected side.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Electric Security Fencing:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tight grid of wires, on posts with insulators.</li>\n<li>From 5-wire systems (wall top) to multizone with <strong>up to 50 wires</strong> (high security).</li>\n<li>Typical industrial: <strong>8 ft (2.4 m) high with 20 wires</strong>, mounted <strong>inside</strong> the perimeter chain link fence.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>All live-wire deterrent fence</td>\n<td>relies on human fear of shock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitored fence</td>\n<td>besides fear, detects cutting or climbing of wires and triggers an alarm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Monitored ones are integrated with intrusion alarm or access control and, increasingly, with cameras.</li>\n<li>Non-lethal, but unpleasant. They discharge energy in <strong>pulses \\~45 times per minute</strong>.</li>\n<li>There are international safety standards for the energizers and for fence construction.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says they \"do not use electric current\" but pulses. Understand it as meaning there is no continuous current (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Barbed Wire and Razor Wire:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Barbed wire varies in gauge, coating weight, number of barbs and spacing between them.</li>\n<li>Barbed-wire-only fence against people: <strong>at least 7 ft (2.1 m)</strong> not counting the top guard. Strands well tensioned, on posts <strong>less than 6 ft (1.8 m)</strong> apart.</li>\n<li><strong>Concertina:</strong> barbs or razor in coils. It serves as a top guard or as a fence by itself.</li>\n<li>Temporary or tactical concertina barriers are laid quickly. Local codes may regulate this use.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.1.6 Blocking Barriers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Physical barriers can control pedestrians or materials. <strong>Blocking barriers</strong> serve mainly to control or prevent <strong>vehicle access</strong>.</li>\n<li>A light vehicle goes through almost any ordinary fence by ramming it at high speed. A heavy one breaks sturdier barriers.</li>\n<li>If the first vehicle is disabled, the attacker removes it and drives a <strong>second vehicle</strong> through the gap.</li>\n<li><strong>Highway guard rail:</strong> effective only at impact angles <strong>under 30°</strong>. A light vehicle can get through, but is badly damaged.</li>\n<li>The book's conclusion: it is almost always best to use a <strong>security barrier designed for that purpose</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two categories of barriers</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Passive / fixed</th>\n<th>Active / operable</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What they are</td>\n<td>Fixed; they do not let vehicles in</td>\n<td>They open and close to let authorized vehicles through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where</td>\n<td>Perimeter, away from vehicle entrances</td>\n<td>Vehicle access control points on the perimeter, or the entrance to a specific building (parking, garage) to check or inspect vehicles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who operates them</td>\n<td>No one</td>\n<td>An operator (e.g., security officer) or an electronic access control system (card reader, keypad)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Types (all)</td>\n<td>1) fixed bollards; 2) engineered planters; 3) Jersey barriers; 4) heavy objects and trees; 5) water obstacles; 6) walls and fences (seen in 9.1.5)</td>\n<td>1) retractable bollards; 2) rising wedge systems; 3) rotating wedge systems; 4) drop arm crash beams; 5) crash gates; 6) surface-mounted wedges and plates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Source</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>U.S. Department of Homeland Security (DHS), 2011</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Bollards: design implications</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typical uses (full list): airports; corporate campuses; data centers; educational facilities; government facilities; military installations; nuclear facilities; ports; stadiums and arenas; water treatment plants.</li>\n<li>A bollard is an intrusion in the streetscape. The system must be well thought out, limited in extent and integrated into the perimeter and the urban landscape.</li>\n<li>Objective: little visual impact and not obstructing legitimate traffic, including <strong>emergency entry and exit</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Height:</strong> to reduce visual impact, normally <strong>no more than 30 in (0.76 m)</strong>. That height may not be enough for some vehicle threats.</li>\n<li>The slope of the roadway and the height of the curb can improve effectiveness and make the bollard less visible.</li>\n<li><strong>Sidewalk width:</strong> the bollard takes away the curb–bollard space, normally <strong>24 in (0.6 m)</strong>, plus its own width. A problem in downtown areas with heavy foot traffic and narrow sidewalks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other guidelines for bollard systems (all)</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Spacing between bollards: <strong>36–48 in (0.9–1.2 m)</strong>, depending on expected traffic and the needs of pedestrians and people with disabilities.</li>\n<li>In unavoidable long lines, interleave trees and large bollards that serve as seating. Over the years, the trees dominate the landscape and the barrier goes unnoticed.</li>\n<li>Keep bollards out of ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) ramps and street corners.</li>\n<li>Linear layout: the axis of the bollards parallel to the street axis.</li>\n<li>They can be custom designed to harmonize with the building, but must be <strong>tested at an independent laboratory</strong>.</li>\n<li>Bollards set very close together make it hard for wheelchairs to reach curb cuts.</li>\n<li><strong>In no case more than 38 in (1 m) tall</strong>, including the decorative sleeve.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> two different heights. 30 in (0.76 m) is the typical height for aesthetics; 38 in (≈1 m) is the absolute maximum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bollards at urban sites</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The typical foundation (Figure 9.6, cross-section of a bollard, U.S. Department of State) only works where the subsoil does not conflict with utilities, vaults or other urban infrastructure.</li>\n<li>Place them <strong>as close to the roadway as possible</strong>. If it is a public way (e.g., municipal sidewalk), a permit may be required.</li>\n<li>In cities like <strong>New York and Washington, D.C.</strong>, individual approvals must be obtained from several utility companies before building.</li>\n<li>Always check what is underneath before digging. The companies may demand proof that gravity and impact loads do not damage their networks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bollard types by operation</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Use / pros and cons</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed</td>\n<td>Permanently anchored</td>\n<td>Passive barrier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Removable</td>\n<td>Fits into a socket in the ground; secured with a padlock-type device against tampering</td>\n<td>Traffic control and low security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retractable (Figure 9.7, Delta Scientific Corporation)</td>\n<td>Manual, electric, hydraulic, pneumatic or gas-assisted. When retracted it is flush with the ground. New variant: <strong>twist-up</strong> mechanism, which rotates the bollard into position</td>\n<td>Cons: more maintenance than fixed; requires training officers and maintenance staff; risk of <strong>accidental activation</strong> with damage or injuries and lawsuits against the owner or the security provider</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Other functions and aesthetics</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are combined with fences or trees to provide <strong>multiple layers</strong> of protection (Figure 9.8: decorative fence resting on bollards at 1.5–3 ft, 0.5–0.9 m).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Figure 9.8 spaces the bollards 1.5–3 ft (0.5–0.9 m), less than the general guideline of 36–48 in (0.9–1.2 m). In the figure the bollards are combined with a fence.</li>\n<li>They can carry lighting, sensors or other electronics, and even exterior <strong>power outlets</strong>.</li>\n<li>Lighting offered: metal halide, CFL (compact fluorescent), induction fluorescent or LED, for low, medium or high security.</li>\n<li>There is a growing market (\"cottage industry\") of decorative bollards, many security-rated. Traditional manufacturers already offer catalog and custom models.</li>\n<li>Sleeves in brushed aluminum, stainless steel, bronze or custom colors; some with the logo engraved.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vehicle impact standards and ratings</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Credible standards cited: <strong>U.S. Department of State SD-STD-02.01</strong>; <strong>ASTM F2656-07</strong>; <strong>BSI (British Standards Institution) PAS 68</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes \"PAS68:2103\"; it is a typo for <strong>PAS 68:2013</strong>.</li>\n<li>They measure a barrier's ability to stop a test vehicle of a given <strong>weight</strong> at a given <strong>speed</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>ASTM F2656-07</strong> gives impact conditions, designations and penetration levels. It serves to choose passive perimeter barriers and active access barriers at facilities exposed to <strong>VBIED</strong> (vehicle-borne improvised explosive device).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.9: Barrier Protection Ratings (Calpipe 2013)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Vehicle weight</th>\n<th>Speed</th>\n<th>Department of State</th>\n<th>ASTM</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>15,000 lb (6,800 kg)</td>\n<td>30 mph (48 km/h)</td>\n<td>K4</td>\n<td>M30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15,000 lb (6,800 kg)</td>\n<td>40 mph (64 km/h)</td>\n<td>K8</td>\n<td>M40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15,000 lb (6,800 kg)</td>\n<td>50 mph (80 km/h)</td>\n<td>K12</td>\n<td>M50</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the figure prints \"48k/km/h\"; it is 48 km/h.</li>\n<li>These ratings are valid for judging effectiveness in general. For a detailed analysis you must add: angle of attack, bumper height, load height and other factors.</li>\n<li>(outside the book) In ASTM, the \"P\" indicates penetration; <strong>P1</strong> = the vehicle's load passes at most 1 m (3.3 ft) beyond the barrier.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mounting: shallow vs. deep</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Shallow mount</strong> or <strong>deep mount</strong> bollards, depending on terrain and environment.</li>\n<li>Deep is more effective. Shallow is reinforced with special footings and installation techniques.</li>\n<li>In both cases: local code restrictions, buried utilities and soil type.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other blocking barriers</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Barrier</th>\n<th>Key characteristics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wedge barriers, in general</td>\n<td>They can be fixed, but are almost always <strong>retractable</strong>. Retractable ones are sometimes called <strong>rotating plate barriers</strong>: metal plate in the roadway that an operator raises or lowers from a booth. They can have an impact rating (crash rated)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rising wedge (Figures 9.10 and 9.11)</td>\n<td>Among the <strong>first active systems</strong>. Utilitarian look compared with retractable bollards or rotating wedges. Provision must be made for the passage of bicycles, cars and emergency vehicles. Like every active barrier, it must be <strong>attended at all times</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mobile wedge (Figure 9.10)</td>\n<td>A medium pickup truck places it in <strong>15 minutes</strong>. Useful in a planned temporary system under elevated threat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wedge mounting</td>\n<td>On the surface or in an excavation about <strong>18 in</strong> deep. There are shallow foundation systems rated <strong>ASTM M50 P1</strong> or <strong>Department of State K12</strong>. Raised height: <strong>21–38 in (0.5–1 m)</strong>. Standard width: <strong>10 ft (3 m)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rotating wedge (Figure 9.12)</td>\n<td>Action similar to the rising wedge, but with a <strong>curved face</strong> (better appearance) and embedded at <strong>greater depth</strong>. Height <strong>24–28 in (0.6–0.7 m)</strong>; standard width <strong>10 ft (3 m)</strong>. Driven by heavy <strong>hydraulic cylinders</strong>. About <strong>3 seconds per movement</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book converts 18 in as 0.48 m; it is actually 0.46 m.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Figure 9.12 says \"Rotating Edge Barrier\"; it is a typo for rotating <strong>wedge</strong> barrier.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Maintenance and operation of active barriers</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Retractable bollards, and especially wedges, require careful maintenance and monitoring.</li>\n<li>Data sheets give <strong>cycles per unit of time</strong> or <strong>cycles between maintenance</strong>. Take them seriously and consider the expected use.</li>\n<li>Key question: is the barrier normally down and deployed only in the face of a threat, or is it normally deployed and lowered for each authorized vehicle?</li>\n<li>The difference can be <strong>1 cycle a month versus hundreds of cycles a day</strong>, with a large effect on power consumption and maintenance.</li>\n<li>Draft operating procedures (standard operating procedures or post orders) that set: when and how they are deployed; notification requirements; periodic test protocols; maintenance intervals.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Security planters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decorative planters designed as a barrier; many shapes, sizes, styles and designs.</li>\n<li>They are mounted above or below ground level. They are fixed with rebar dowels or larger steel tubes, or with special footings for more protection against impact or explosion.</li>\n<li>Common on the perimeter of commercial, municipal and federal buildings worldwide. They blend into the architecture and can be customized in style and level of protection.</li>\n<li>Figures: 9.13, custom planters in an urban office area (Wausau Tile); 9.14, planters with logos at a sports arena (Wausau Tile); 9.15, cross-section of a planter with a <strong>highway-type barrier core</strong> inside (Stonewear Force Protection).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Jersey barriers (Figure 9.16)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Standardized <strong>precast concrete</strong> element. Created in the <strong>1940s–1950s</strong> as a highway median to keep cars from crossing into the opposite lanes.</li>\n<li>Massive use in security <strong>after 9/11</strong>, as an anti-ram and traffic control barrier against terrorist attacks.</li>\n<li>They were believed to protect through their mass: a <strong>12 ft (3.6 m)</strong> barrier weighs about 5,700 lb (2,585 kg).</li>\n<li>But <strong>resting on the ground they are ineffective</strong> against a vehicle attack. To work they must be <strong>embedded</strong> and have <strong>vertical steel reinforcement anchoring</strong> through the barrier to the pavement.</li>\n<li>Cons: little adaptability; standard lengths of <strong>12.5 and 20 ft (3.8 and 6 m)</strong>, which makes them inflexible. Poorly installed, the overlaps leave unwanted gaps or narrow sidewalks and streets until they are impassable.</li>\n<li>They serve as a permanent or temporary barrier.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the weight example uses a 12 ft barrier, but the standard lengths cited are 12.5 and 20 ft.</li>\n<li>Every barrier system must be well designed, built and maintained, especially active barriers with moving parts (DHS 2011).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Effective improvised barriers</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Barrier</th>\n<th>Specification</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy machinery tires</td>\n<td><strong>7–8 ft (2.1–2.4 m)</strong> in diameter, <strong>half buried</strong>, with the soil around them well compacted</td>\n<td>A <strong>3/4-ton</strong> pickup does not get through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metal posts or railroad ties</td>\n<td>Partially buried in concrete; inclined <strong>outward, in the direction of the possible penetration, at 30–45°</strong>; spaced <strong>4 ft (1.2 m)</strong> apart</td>\n<td>A vehicle at high speed suffers serious damage and its occupants, severe injuries</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>9.1.7 Symbolic and Natural Barriers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A barrier does not have to be mechanical or manufactured.</li>\n<li>Symbolic and natural ones can be a very effective <strong>alternative or complement</strong> to typical security hardware.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Examples (all those in the book)</th>\n<th>How it acts</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Symbolic</td>\n<td>Landscaping; curbs; small decorative fences; changes in floor color or texture</td>\n<td>Normally <strong>not a solid physical barrier</strong>. It sends a <strong>psychological signal</strong> that helps control movement or makes anyone who wants to leave the designated routes or zones uncomfortable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural</td>\n<td>(the book gives no examples here)</td>\n<td>Usually very effective and <strong>blends into the setting</strong> of the place</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The topic is expanded in <strong>Chapter 10</strong>.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-9-e1\">9.2 Containers and Vaults</h3><ul>\n<li>Another layer of physical security, often <strong>the closest to the asset</strong>: containers, safes and vaults.</li>\n<li>The intrusion protection of a safe or vault can be measured by its <strong>lock resistance</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.2.1 Safes</h4>\n<p><strong>Basic ideas</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>That something is called a \"safe\" does not guarantee that it protects everything stored in it. You must know the characteristics and limits of each type.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fire safe</th>\n<th>Burglary safe</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How it protects</td>\n<td>Insulating material that dissipates heat</td>\n<td>Thick solid steel walls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weak point</td>\n<td>The insulation resists a hammer blow poorly: it is no good against forced entry</td>\n<td>Steel quickly transmits heat to the interior: paper is soon destroyed in a fire</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>UL</strong> (Underwriters Laboratories) has a well-established protocol for testing and rating safes according to the expected protection. Understanding its labels is essential for choosing well.</li>\n<li>The safes in this section are generally <strong>portable</strong>: unanchored, they can be moved with basic equipment.</li>\n<li>Even if burglary-resistant, they can be carried off whole and opened at leisure. <strong>A safe on wheels is not burglary-resistant.</strong></li>\n<li>In an isolated area with no other protection (alarms, surveillance) it is considered vulnerable.</li>\n<li><strong>UL requires anchoring any safe under 750 lb (340 kg).</strong></li>\n<li>Two general categories: 1) <strong>record safes</strong>, fire-resistant records safes; 2) safes for <strong>valuables</strong> against forced penetration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Record safes for fire protection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Records have changed: more electronic management, offsite and cloud storage. Storing them properly is still necessary and requires the right container.</li>\n<li>They protect, up to a point, with special insulation and construction against heat and flames.</li>\n<li>The insulation <strong>contains moisture</strong> that dissipates heat; this way the interior does not reach the temperature that destroys the records.</li>\n<li>That moisture is <strong>not replenished</strong>: a safe that has been through a fire may not protect afterward.</li>\n<li>The moisture also evaporates over time: a fireproof safe keeps its rating for about <strong>20–30 years</strong>, depending on the climate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Labels</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The level of protection is read from the product's <strong>UL label</strong>.</li>\n<li>Old products may carry a label from the defunct <strong>SMNA</strong> (Safe Manufacturers National Association). It is usually inside the door or on its upper interior part.</li>\n<li>Distrust \"ratings\" from \"manufacturer testing\" or \"built to UL class specifications\". There was probably no independent test and they perform worse than with a UL label or one from another recognized independent laboratory.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book gives as an example \"UL 687, burglary safe standard\" within the fire section. The fire standard for records equipment that the book itself cites (9.2.2) is <strong>UL 72</strong>.</li>\n<li>UL used <strong>letters until 1972</strong>. Since then the label indicates the type of container and the level of protection.</li>\n<li>Today it indicates whether it is a fire-resistant safe or an insulated records container, and the <strong>hours of protection</strong>.</li>\n<li>Example <strong>\"350–4 Hours\" (350-4 HR)</strong>: with the exterior at <strong>1,700 °F (927 °C)</strong>, the interior does not reach <strong>350 °F (177 °C)</strong> for <strong>at least 4 hours</strong>. At 350 °F almost all paper is damaged.</li>\n<li>The same classes are in <strong>NFPA 232</strong>, Standard for the Protection of Records (NFPA 2012). NFPA = National Fire Protection Association.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.17: pre-1972 and current equivalents (fire-resistant safes)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>SMNA</th>\n<th>Previous UL</th>\n<th>Current UL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>350–4 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>350–2 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>350–1 hour</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Three UL classes of records protection equipment</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>For which media</th>\n<th>Maximum interior</th>\n<th>Maximum relative humidity inside</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>350</td>\n<td>Paper and paper products</td>\n<td>350 °F (177 °C)</td>\n<td>65%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>150</td>\n<td>Magnetic media, tapes, discs and photographic materials</td>\n<td>150 °F (66 °C)</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>125</td>\n<td>Floppy discs and similar flexible computer media</td>\n<td>125 °F (52 °C)</td>\n<td><strong>Pending:</strong> the book does not give it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In class 150, <strong>temperature and humidity</strong> matter. More humidity (80%) is allowed than in class 350 (65%).</li>\n<li>More humidity = more protection against temperature rise: the interior stays cooler for the same time.</li>\n<li>In addition, paper is more sensitive to humidity than magnetic, photographic or disc media.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fire-resistant safes (fireproof safes for paper)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Three classes: <strong>350-4, 350-2 and 350-1</strong>, according to the hours they protect paper in a fire.</li>\n<li>All three must pass <strong>fire, explosion and impact</strong> tests:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire endurance</td>\n<td>The interior temperature is recorded: it may not exceed 350 °F (177 °C) at the limit of 4, 2 or 1 h. At the end, all papers <strong>legible</strong> and not crumbling when taken out and examined</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Impact (drop test)</td>\n<td>Maintain the same temperature conditions after <strong>dropping 30 ft (9.1 m)</strong>. Simulates falling <strong>three stories</strong> in a collapse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explosion</td>\n<td>Must not explode when suddenly exposed to <strong>2,000 °F (1,093 °C)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The same impact and explosion tests apply to records protection equipment (see electronic records, below).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Insulated filing devices</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Class 350-1 (formerly Class D)</strong> and <strong>Class 350-½ (formerly Class E)</strong>. They protect considerably less than the three fire-resistant safes.</li>\n<li>In the test, the thermocouples go in the <strong>center</strong> of the compartment. In fire-resistant safes they go <strong>1 in</strong> from the sides and the door.</li>\n<li>They are not designed for impact and do not pass the drop test (fire-resistant safes and insulated records containers do).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> it is very easy to confuse the <strong>350-1 insulated filing device</strong> with the <strong>350-1 fire-resistant safe</strong> and the <strong>350-1 insulated records container</strong>. The label says which it is: fire-resistant safe, insulated record container or insulated filing device (or file drawer).</li>\n<li>They only protect against total burnout in <strong>fire-resistant buildings</strong> with little combustible material around.</li>\n<li><strong>NFPA 220</strong> defines a fire-resistive building: all its structural elements (columns, beams, floors and roof) are of approved noncombustible or limited-combustible materials, with specified resistances.</li>\n<li>Do not place them on a floor that could collapse: on falling they may open and damage the contents.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes \"first-resistant safes\"; it is a typo for fire-resistant.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Electronic Data Processing Record Protection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Magnetic media deteriorate from <strong>150 °F (66 °C)</strong>, and sooner with humidity above 80%. A container for paper is <strong>not enough</strong>.</li>\n<li>You need one that withstands extreme heat and high humidity.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Safe within a safe\"</strong> option: insulated, sealed inner compartment for the media, inside an outer safe with a thick insulation wall.</li>\n<li>It protects against <strong>125 °F (52 °C) or 150 °F (66 °C) and 80% humidity</strong> for 4, 3, 2 or 1 hour. Other models achieve the same without that design.</li>\n<li>Classes 150 and 125: the label must say <strong>\"Record Protection Equipment\"</strong> and indicate the class and the time (1 to 4 h).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.18: UL designations and labels of fire-resistant containers</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Container type</th>\n<th>Designations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire-resistant safes, paper</td>\n<td>350-4 (4 h); 350-2 (2 h); 350-1 (1 h)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insulated records containers, electronic media</td>\n<td>150-4 (4 h); 150-3 (3 h); 150-2 (2 h); 150-1 (1 h); 125-3 (3 h); 125-2 (2 h); 125-1 (1 h)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insulated filing devices</td>\n<td>Class 350-1 or ½; Class 150-1 or ½; Class 125-1 or ½ (1 h or ½ h)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insulated file drawers</td>\n<td>Class 350-1; Class 150-1; Class 125-1 (1 h)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text gives 4, 3, 2 or 1 hour for 125 °F and 150 °F, but Figure 9.18 does not include 125-4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Safes designed to protect valuables</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are rated with test data and specifications from the <strong>ISO</strong> (Insurance Services Office) and UL.</li>\n<li><strong>They do not protect against fire.</strong> If both are needed: put the valuables container inside a larger fire-resistant safe or vault, or use a safe rated for fire and burglary.</li>\n<li>Their interior is smaller than that of records safes: money and valuables take up less space than papers.</li>\n<li>So that it is not carried off: install it in a <strong>steel-clad concrete block</strong> or anchor it well.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>UL Ratings and Performance Requirements</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>What it requires</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Net working time</td>\n<td>The safe resisted entry for <strong>15 net min</strong>. Entry = opening the door or making a 6-inch square opening through the door or front. Tools: common hand tools, drills, punches, hammers and pressure devices. Rule: <strong>\"when the tool leaves the safe, the clock stops\"</strong>. There are <strong>more than 50 types of attack</strong>; technicians usually test only 2 or 3, depending on what they know about the product</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safe construction requirements</td>\n<td>UL <strong>Group II, 1 or 1R</strong> combination lock; it may have a UL-rated <strong>\"Type 1\"</strong> high-security electronic lock. Minimum weight <strong>750 lb</strong>, or instructions for anchoring it in a larger safe, a concrete block or the premises. Walls equivalent to <strong>≥ 1 in of open-hearth steel</strong> with tensile strength <strong>≥ 50,000 PSI</strong> (pounds per square inch). Joints equivalent to <strong>¼ in</strong> continuous penetration weld of the same steel. One hole <strong>≤ ¼ in</strong> for electrical conductors, with no direct line of sight to the door or the mechanism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Performance requirements</td>\n<td>Body and door resist entry (opening the door or a 6-inch square opening through body, door or front) for <strong>30 net min</strong>. Tools: common hand tools, picking tools, portable mechanical or electric tools, grinding points, carbide drills, pressure devices, abrasive discs and power saws</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book does not label these rows. The 15 min row corresponds to TL-15 and the 30 min row to TL-30.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common Misunderstanding of UL Ratings</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is believed that <strong>5 min (RSC), 15 min (TL-15) and 30 min (TL-30)</strong> are how long it takes to open the safe. <strong>That is false.</strong></li>\n<li>A professional safe cracker, with knowledge, tools and time, opens it after several hours. It depends on their skill, what they know about the construction and luck.</li>\n<li><strong>Any safe can be opened.</strong> The higher the UL rating, the more time it costs.</li>\n<li><strong>TL-30:</strong> construction identical to TL-15. Same test except for the time (30 min) and two more tools: abrasive discs and power saws.</li>\n<li>The TL-30 label means they could not open the door or make the 6-inch square opening in 30 min.</li>\n<li>The test engineers have the <strong>manufacturing drawings</strong> and can take the safe apart before testing it.</li>\n<li><strong>TL-30X6:</strong> the TL-30 test done on <strong>all six sides</strong>. Combination safe with maximum body and door protection on 6 sides against common hand-held mechanical and electric tools, and their combinations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.19: how to read UL ratings</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Code</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>TL</td>\n<td>Tool Resistant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TR</td>\n<td>Torch Resistant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>X6</td>\n<td>Test done on all six sides of the safe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15, 30 or 60</td>\n<td>Minutes of the test</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Example <strong>TRTL-30-X6</strong>: torch- and tool-resistant on all six sides. It is among the highest burglary ratings on the market.</li>\n<li>According to the book, the highest of all is <strong>TRTL-60-X6</strong>: it withstands torch, explosives (<strong>8 oz of nitroglycerin</strong>) and tools (prying and other striking) on all 6 sides for <strong>1 hour</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in UL 687 resistance to explosives is marked with <strong>TX</strong>; the highest is <strong>TXTL-60X6</strong> (outside the book). The book attributes the explosives to TRTL-60-X6.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Burglary-Resistant Containers</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They resist an attack with tools, torch or explosives, given enough time to work.</li>\n<li>They are usually solid steel plate, or steel with a composite fill such as concrete.</li>\n<li>They are divided by level of protection and tests passed:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Construction or test</th>\n<th>Is there a test?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>B-Rate</td>\n<td>Industry catch-all for almost any safe with a lock. Unwritten standard: <strong>¼ in body, ½ in door</strong>. Today many use <strong>1/8 in</strong> in the body (or make both 1/8 and ¼) to cut costs because of the price of steel and shipping. When buying, check the lock work, hard plates and relockers</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RSC (Residential Security Container)</td>\n<td>UL rating: <strong>5 net min</strong>, on all sides, with several tools. UL tests and certifies the manufacturer. Combination or key unit against common mechanical tools; the whole unit is tested. Base standard: <strong>UL 1037</strong>, Anti-theft Alarms and Devices</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UL 1037 rule applied to the RSC</td>\n<td>The device resists <strong>≥ 5 min</strong> of attack; the disassembly needed to remove it counts within those 5 min. Tools: hammers, chisels, adjustable wrenches, pry bars, punches and screwdrivers. Hammers with head of <strong>≤ 3 lb</strong>; no tool <strong>over 18 in</strong>. Product mounted in its position; attacked by <strong>a single operator</strong></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RSC II</td>\n<td>Like the RSC, but <strong>10 min</strong>, <strong>2 technicians</strong> and more aggressive tools: picks, high-speed carbide drills and pressure devices. They try for a \"six-square-inch\" opening in the door or front</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B/C-Rate</td>\n<td>Catch-all: body <strong>≥ ¼ in</strong>, door <strong>½ in</strong>, plus layers of <strong>10 or 12 gauge</strong> metal with fireproof composite material. Check lock work, relockers and the rest</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C-Rate</td>\n<td><strong>½ in</strong> steel safe with <strong>1 in</strong> door and lock. Check lock work and relockers</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TL-15</td>\n<td>Passed the standard <strong>UL 687</strong> tests, with the same tools and almost always the same engineers</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> RSC II says \"six-square-inch opening\" (6 in²), while TL speaks of \"6-inch square\" (6 × 6 in). Possible typo.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text says \"see TL-15 and TL-30 below\", but those descriptions come earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Burglary/Fire-Resistive Containers</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Since <strong>1975</strong> there have been single containers that pass the UL fire and burglary tests.</li>\n<li>Burglary: <strong>30 min or 1 h</strong>. Fire: <strong>1 or 2 h</strong>.</li>\n<li>Thick insulation around the contents, with no gaps through which heat can enter.</li>\n<li>High tensile strength steel body, electrically welded. Doors of that steel and of armor plate, against electric carbide drills, abrasive discs and other tools.</li>\n<li>Insurers look at the rating when setting premiums: the right safe can <strong>lower the premium</strong>. Sometimes the annual savings soon equal the cost of the equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fire tests of class 350 safes (according to the book)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rating</th>\n<th>Heating</th>\n<th>Interior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>½ h</td>\n<td>½ h up to <strong>1,550 °F</strong> exterior</td>\n<td>&lt; 350 °F during heating and cooling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 h</td>\n<td>1 h up to 1,550 °F, then cooling</td>\n<td>&lt; 350 °F throughout the test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2 h</td>\n<td>2 h up to 1,550 °F, then cooling</td>\n<td>Does not exceed 350 °F in the whole process</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Paper begins to char at about <strong>410 °F</strong>; that is why the interior limit is 350 °F.</li>\n<li>After the test time: it is taken out of the furnace, hoisted <strong>30 ft (3 stories)</strong> and dropped onto concrete rubble.</li>\n<li>It is left to cool and put into a furnace preheated to <strong>2,000 °F</strong>. This is the <strong>explosion test</strong>: to see whether the door pops open. The contents must remain intact.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> here the book gives 1,550 °F exterior for ½, 1 and 2 h; earlier it gave 1,700 °F (927 °C) for 350-4. (outside the book) The standard curve rises with time: 1,550 °F at ½ h, 1,700 °F at 1 h, 1,850 °F at 2 h and 2,000 °F at 4 h.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> earlier the book treats drop and explosion as separate tests; here it chains them.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> this list includes a ½ h class 350 safe. According to Figure 9.18, ½ h exists only in insulated filing devices.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>GSA-Approved Safes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>GSA</strong> (U.S. General Services Administration) tested and approved <strong>six classes</strong> of safes.</li>\n<li>They protect against <strong>forced or surreptitious</strong> entry and serve to store <strong>classified government information</strong>. They are not intended for valuables.</li>\n<li>The standards are at www.gsa.gov.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.2.2 Vaults</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vault:</strong> room or area of special construction to limit access and protect the assets it contains. In general, it prevents <strong>forced entry and removal</strong> of the asset.</li>\n<li>The term also covers rooms made to protect the contents from <strong>fire</strong>, not necessarily from theft.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Burglary vault</th>\n<th>Fire vault</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n<td>Generally lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction standards</td>\n<td>Very different from each other</td>\n<td>Very different from each other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Burglary protection</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Much lower</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>To choose which standards to follow (fire or security), the owner considers: 1) the asset and its vulnerability; 2) the custodian's legal liability under requirements of a regulator, an insurer or a government agency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fire-Resistive Vaults</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Main standard in the US: <strong>NFPA 232</strong>. <strong>It does not consider forced entry.</strong></li>\n<li>Key factor: whether the vault will be in a fire-resistant building. According to <strong>NFPA 220</strong>, Standard on Types of Building Construction: walls, partitions, columns, floors and roofs of noncombustible or limited-combustible material.</li>\n<li>In non-fire-resistant structures <strong>more vault protection</strong> is required.</li>\n<li>Records of exceptional value or rarity: individualized measures, outside existing standards.</li>\n<li>Records essential for reconstructing other records: special protection.</li>\n<li>The vault demands excellent design and construction to withstand everything a fire imposes.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid below-grade vaults</strong>, for three reasons: 1) burning or smoldering debris accumulates in the basement and causes a prolonged \"cooking effect\" that defeats the protection; 2) humidity that lets mold damage the records; 3) flood risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Traditional vault construction</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Specification</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reinforced concrete</td>\n<td>Steel bars of <strong>≥ 0.5 in (13 mm)</strong> diameter, at <strong>6 in (15.2 cm)</strong> centers, at right angles in both directions. Wire-tied at crossings at no more than <strong>12 in (30.5 cm)</strong> in both directions, and centered in the wall or panel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Structural steel frame</td>\n<td>Protected with <strong>≥ 4 in (10.2 cm)</strong> of concrete, brick or equivalent. Tied with steel ties or mesh equivalent to <strong>No. 8 ASW gauge</strong> wire with <strong>8 in (20.3 cm)</strong> spacing. The brick is filled solid with concrete down to the steel</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Minimum wall thickness</strong> (fire resistance depends on thickness)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Vault</th>\n<th>Brick</th>\n<th>Reinforced concrete</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>4 hours</td>\n<td>12 in (30.5 cm)</td>\n<td>8 in (20.4 cm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6 hours</td>\n<td>12 in (30.5 cm)</td>\n<td>10 in (25.4 cm)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book gives 12 in of brick for both 4 and 6 hours; only the concrete changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Media Storage and Protection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Magnetic media are usually kept in the same vaults as paper. It is neither practical nor cost-effective to build the whole vault to their level: better an <strong>additional container inside the vault</strong>.</li>\n<li>Store them according to the manufacturer's specifications and/or industry standards.</li>\n<li>Long-term retention: update the media (software, versions) so the data can be recovered. Keep an inventory and a backup, preferably <strong>off-site</strong>.</li>\n<li>Digital media (memory sticks, USB, CDs and DVDs): safe that withstands <strong>238 °F (≈114 °C) or more</strong>.</li>\n<li>Backups, internal hard drives and tapes are <strong>the most vulnerable</strong>: safe that withstands <strong>150 °F (≈66 °C) or more</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hard drive: maximum non-operating temperature, about <strong>70 °C (≈158 °F)</strong>. It is about 10 degrees more than the operating one, because in use it heats up a lot.</li>\n<li>(outside the book) \"Withstand 238 °F or 150 °F\" is understood as keeping the interior below that temperature, like the UL 150 and 125 classes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Data safes</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rating</th>\n<th>Protects</th>\n<th>Interior during furnace and cooling</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1 h – UL class 150</td>\n<td>Computer data 1 h</td>\n<td>&lt; 150 °F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2 h – UL class 150</td>\n<td>Computer data 2 h</td>\n<td>&lt; 150 °F</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 h – UL class 125</td>\n<td>Computer data 1 h</td>\n<td>&lt; 125 °F</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Standards for media storage (full list)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Organization</th>\n<th>Standard</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ANSI (American National Standards Institute)</td>\n<td>ANSI IT9.11, Standard for Imaging Media — Processed Safety Photographic Film Storage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASTM (American Society for Testing and Materials)</td>\n<td>ASTM E 119, Test Method for Fire Test of Building Constructive Materials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FM (Factory Mutual Research Corporation)</td>\n<td>FM Approval Class 4200, Storage of Records and Valuables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NFPA (National Fire Protection Association)</td>\n<td>NFPA 232, Standard for the Protection of Records; NFPA 40, Standard for the Handling and Storage of Cellulose Nitrate Film Records; NFPA 232AM, Standard for Fire Risk Evaluation of Structures Containing Vital Records; NFPA 75, Standard for the Protection of Electronic Computer/Data Processing Equipment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UL (Underwriters Laboratories)</td>\n<td>UL 72, Test for Fire Resistance of Record Protection Equipment; UL 155, Tests for Fire Resistance of Vault and File Room Doors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Vaults for Protection Against Forced Entry</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Location: <strong>avoid an exterior wall</strong>. It would give the intruder a convenient point to attack from outside.</li>\n<li>All <strong>six surfaces</strong> must protect equally. Typical mistake: installing a heavy door and assuming the concrete walls around it are enough.</li>\n<li>Concrete looks strong, but it is drilled through quickly with modern tools. Those walls are designed for structural loads, not to delay.</li>\n<li>Concrete is brittle and resists tension and bending poorly: it is relatively easy to penetrate.</li>\n<li><strong>Never use unreinforced concrete</strong> against forced penetration.</li>\n<li>Reinforcement with deformed bar (<strong>rebar</strong>). It is numbered in multiples of <strong>1/8 in (3 mm)</strong> of diameter: <strong>No. 3 = 3/8 in (10 mm)</strong>.</li>\n<li>The current ASTM specification for building vaults <strong>requires rebar</strong>.</li>\n<li>All surfaces are vulnerable. <strong>A vault only delays, it does not stop</strong>, a determined intruder.</li>\n<li>It is impossible to build an impenetrable vault. It is designed to resist for a <strong>defined period</strong>. Even the best concrete yields to someone with skill, personnel and tools.</li>\n<li>Cast-in-place concrete vault: <strong>walls, roof and floor of at least 12 in</strong> to delay enough, provided there are other <strong>detection</strong> countermeasures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Penetration Techniques</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Explosives</strong>: very effective against concrete. Shock waves travel through all of it and cause internal fragmentation and splaying of both faces.</li>\n<li>Steel reinforcement <strong>increases the delay</strong>: the explosion breaks the concrete, but the rebar usually remains intact and has to be removed.</li>\n<li>The gauge of the rebar greatly influences protection:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rebar</th>\n<th>Tool needed to cut it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 4 or smaller</td>\n<td>Handheld bolt cutters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 5 to No. 8</td>\n<td>Motorized hydraulic shear, cutting torch or thermal lance (burning bar)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Larger than No. 8</td>\n<td>Only torch, thermal lance, power saw or explosives</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Wall Construction and Penetration</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Advantage of the thick reinforced concrete wall: it forces the use of sophisticated tools and explosives, and more supplies (e.g., acetylene tanks).</li>\n<li>Because of the tools, supplies, noise, smoke and heat, the attack must be made in a <strong>hidden place</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cases: buildings next to banks have been rented as a front to dig a tunnel and enter through the floor.</li>\n<li>A gang rented a building across from a bank and dug under the street. The vault had an alarm: it sounded when the floor was drilled and they were discovered. Lesson: alarm the floor as well.</li>\n<li>But if the vault has a <strong>time lock</strong>, like modern bank vaults, it does not open until the programmed time even if the alarm sounds. This makes it hard to isolate an attempt through the floor.</li>\n<li>Solution: <strong>CCTV</strong> (closed-circuit television) with audio and lighting <strong>inside the vault</strong>. It allows observation and, if recorded, leaves a record of the intrusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vulnerabilities of Vaults</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Poor <strong>opening and closing policies and procedures</strong> are often the undoing of the best-designed vaults.</li>\n<li>The <strong>two-man rule</strong> with a combination lock is vulnerable.</li>\n<li>Case: disc lock with a <strong>three-position changeable combination</strong>. They thought it had 4 numbers because they counted the <strong>opening index</strong> (the last one) as part of the combination.</li>\n<li>They gave the first two numbers to one person and \"third and fourth\" to another. Since there are only 3 numbers, the first could deduce the rest in <strong>fewer than 40 attempts</strong>.</li>\n<li>Common practice: limits on the first and last number of the combination (see the Exam note below). The gates of the lock wheels are <strong>two digits wide</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says not to put the first and last number \"above 10 or below 90\". It is inverted: the logical thing is to avoid numbers <strong>below 10 or above 90</strong> (outside the book).</li>\n<li>If a locksmith does not change the combination and the instructions are not followed exactly, the lock can end up <strong>set to a single number</strong>. It opens by dialing the last number and turning to the opening index. A simple and serious error from not following the dialing sequence.</li>\n<li><strong>Test:</strong> the custodian turns the dial <strong>three times to the left</strong>, stops at the <strong>third number</strong> and then turns <strong>right to the opening index</strong>. If the bolt opens, the combination is on a single number: <strong>change it immediately</strong>.</li>\n<li>Another error in the case: each person stood next to the door while the other dialed and could see their part.</li>\n<li>Another vulnerability: the <strong>escape mechanism</strong> (handle or release inside the vault).</li>\n<li>Resistance ratings are <strong>invalidated</strong> if any door, fan or other port is installed without following the manufacturer's instructions.</li>\n<li>A trusted locksmith must, periodically: 1) inspect the locking mechanism; 2) perform preventive maintenance on lock and door; 3) check that there are no unauthorized modifications.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-9-e2\">9.3 Locks and Locking Mechanisms</h3><p><strong>Central idea</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The lock secures the movable parts of a barrier (doors, windows, covers).</li>\n<li>It is the most widely used method of controlling physical access.</li>\n<li>It should not be the only protection for an important area: with skill and time, any lock can be defeated.</li>\n<li>Supplement it with periodic rounds by security officers or with electronic security systems.</li>\n<li>Uses cited: homes, vehicles, offices, hotels, safes, desks, cabinets, file cabinets, briefcases, display cases, jewelry boxes and computer systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two general classes</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Principle</th>\n<th>Associated with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mechanical</td>\n<td>Physical parts only</td>\n<td>Key or combination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electrified</td>\n<td>Electrical power + mechanical operation</td>\n<td>Automated access control systems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>9.3.1 Mechanical Locks</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> an arrangement of physical parts that acts as a barrier and prevents the bolt or latch from being opened.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Four functional sets</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bolt or latch</td>\n<td>Joins the movable part (door, window) with the fixed part (jamb, frame)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keeper or strike</td>\n<td>Housing the bolt enters. It is not an integral part of the mechanism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tumbler array</td>\n<td>Barrier or maze that must be overcome to move the bolt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key or unlocking device</td>\n<td>Overcomes the barrier and operates the bolt</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Normally: the bolt and the barrier are in the installed hardware (lockset); the key is separate.</li>\n<li>Exception: locks with physical logic devices, which form a single piece:<ul>\n<li>integrated digital keypad that mechanically releases the bolt with the correct sequence;</li>\n<li>dial combination lock.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Primary Types</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Uses</th>\n<th>Exam points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Warded lock</strong></td>\n<td>The first and oldest. Open keyhole that can be seen through; long barrel-type key</td>\n<td>Old houses, farms, old inns. Very simple</td>\n<td>Weak against spring manipulation with any key the wards do not stop, and against corrosion from weather and age. A modern program does not include it: phased replacement or reinforce with other locks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lever lock</strong></td>\n<td>18th century. Flat metal levers on a common pivot, held by a wire spring. Each lever has a cut, opposite the pivot, for a projection of the bolt called the <strong>fence</strong>. If all of them line up, the fence enters and the bolt retracts</td>\n<td>Desks, cabinets, lockers, bank safe-deposit boxes, U.S. mail boxes</td>\n<td>More secure than the warded lock. With two or more fence cuts per lever, several keys open it → allows master keying. Inherently vulnerable to picking, but can be designed to be resistant. Do not confuse with the lever handle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pin tumbler lock</strong></td>\n<td>19th century, Linus Yale (who also created the dial combination lock). Same elements: mechanism that moves the bolt, maze and keyway; the maze changes</td>\n<td>The most widely used in the U.S. on exterior and interior building doors</td>\n<td>The most important advance in the history of the mechanical lock. With recent improvements it reaches very high security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Wafer tumbler lock</strong></td>\n<td>Fairly late development. Flat wafers (metal or other material) lock the plug to the shell</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Allows master keying and double-bitted keys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dial combination lock</strong></td>\n<td>Keyless. Similar to the lever lock: aligns the gates of the wheels so that the bolt's fence enters. Wheels are fully circular and interdependent: moving one moves the others</td>\n<td>Safes and containers</td>\n<td>The order of movement matters → it is a true <strong>combination</strong> lock, not a <strong>permutation</strong> lock</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Pin tumbler: conventional cylinder vs. high security</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conventional: key pins evenly spaced in a single row; usually 5, 6 or 7 pins.</li>\n<li>When master keying, split pins are added and the number of possible key changes drops considerably.</li>\n<li>High security cylinder: interlocking pins and drivers. Picks or uncoded keys do not align them.<ul>\n<li>The keys are cut with precise depth and angle.</li>\n<li>The key raises each pin to the shear line and at the same time rotates it.</li>\n<li>In this way the interlock passes through a special slot at the base of the plug, and the plug turns enough to move the bolt.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Medeco</strong> (variant): instead of a slot at the base of the plug it uses a <strong>side bar</strong>.<ul>\n<li>The bar enters a recess in the shell or retracts into slots in the pins.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In both high security types, copying the key is very difficult except with special equipment from the manufacturer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Number of combinations (dial combination locks)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Theoretical maximum = positions per wheel raised to the number of wheels.</li>\n<li>A good quality padlock usually has 100 positions per wheel.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.109em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mop mtight\"><span class=\"mtight\">m</span><span class=\"mtight\">a</span><span class=\"mtight\">x</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.038em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8436em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">t</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇒</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.109em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mop mtight\"><span class=\"mtight\">m</span><span class=\"mtight\">a</span><span class=\"mtight\">x</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the PDF text the exponent appears as \"1004\"; it is 100 raised to the 4th power (10^8). The figure of 100,000,000 is correct.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Electronic combination locks</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They replace the mechanical dial on safes and secure document cabinets.</li>\n<li>They are powered by the turning of the dial by the user.</li>\n<li>The numbers appear on an LCD (liquid crystal display) or other display, not on the dial markings.</li>\n<li>The display can only be seen from a limited angle.</li>\n<li>The number dialed bears no relation to the physical position of the dial.</li>\n<li>Additional functions:<ul>\n<li>mandatory wait (time-out) of a certain number of seconds between each number;</li>\n<li>two-person rule: two numbers must be dialed to open;</li>\n<li>own number per user → audit trail of which combination opened it and when;</li>\n<li>it memorizes failed attempts.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Because of their electronic precision, no combinations are lost to unusable adjacent numbers.</li>\n<li>They are claimed to resist all the typical attacks on mechanical combinations and also electrical and magnetic ones.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Master Keying</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Principle:</strong> several different keys open the same lock. Each acts on different tumblers or on different aspects of the same ones.</li>\n<li>It creates an <strong>access hierarchy</strong>: one lock → increasingly larger groups → all the locks in the population.</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> with hundreds or thousands of locks, it would be impractical for someone with broad access to carry one key per lock.</li>\n<li>Master groups are formed: a single key opens its entire group.</li>\n<li>Three security problems, which must be weighed against the supposed convenience:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Problem</th>\n<th>Detail and mitigation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Control of master keys (accountability)</td>\n<td>Losing, compromising or using a master key without permission exposes its entire group. Restricted-access key cabinets and PC software help</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>More points for surreptitious opening</td>\n<td>Several shear lines or gate openings are created</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>More maintenance (in cylinders)</td>\n<td>The extra master pieces, often fragile, get displaced or break; the lock jams and has to be disassembled</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Security Vulnerabilities of Mechanical Locks</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Some attacks require a great deal of skill; others are within reach of amateurs and vandals.</li>\n<li>According to the book, <strong>rim-mounted, mortise and vertical rod</strong> locks offer optimal security.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attack route</th>\n<th>What it consists of</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Attack by force</td>\n<td>Separating the door from the jamb; pulling the lock out of its housing and manipulating the bolt; breaking the pins so the plug turns freely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Surreptitious attack</td>\n<td>Picking or manipulation with tools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Impression-making and try keys</td>\n<td>Objective: a facsimile key. (a) Impression: insert a blank key of the correct profile and force it to turn so that slight marks are left before cutting it. (b) Try keys or \"jingle keys\": blanks well milled for that keyway with random cuts; they are turned and \"raked\" until the lock opens</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Rearranging Mechanical Locks</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>When:</strong> change of authorized personnel; system compromise; loss or loss of control of keys; major changes in occupancy.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Relocation</td>\n<td>Rotate locks between doors, between cabinets, etc.</td>\n<td>Only works if the lock carries no markings that a former key holder would recognize</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Reordering the tumblers (most effective)</td>\n<td>Levers and wafers: disassemble and change the order. Pin tumbler: the same pins in other positions</td>\n<td>The same tumblers are reused many times</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Interchangeable / convertible core</td>\n<td>The core is changed on site for another one already combinated</td>\n<td>Very quick redistribution; offered by some pin tumbler manufacturers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>9.3.2 Electrified Locking Mechanisms</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They lock and unlock the door from a remote device: electric button, motion sensor, card reader or digital keypad.</li>\n<li><strong>Boolean logic:</strong> organizes conditions into sets. Example: \"if A and B are closed, C can be opened\".<ul>\n<li>Useful in mantraps and other high security arrangements.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>When analyzing failures and ways of defeating the lock, also include the remote-control devices.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Fail safe vs. fail secure</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>They are used mainly in relation to fire and life safety codes.</li>\n<li>They apply to doors that must be open throughout occupancy or only during a detected fire.</li>\n<li>The codes require exit with <strong>a single action, no special knowledge</strong>.<ul>\n<li>Exceptions: banks, jewelry stores and other high security environments.</li>\n<li>It is a single action: turning the handle; pushing the exit bar (panic bar).</li>\n<li>It is not a single action: pressing a button, turning a key, using a reader or entering a code before turning the handle.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Local fire codes vary by municipality: check them before specifying the lock.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fail safe</th>\n<th>Fail secure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>In case of failure</td>\n<td>It <strong>opens</strong></td>\n<td>It <strong>stays locked</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure considered</td>\n<td>The most common: power outage. Also failure of the mechanism or of a connected control</td>\n<td>Power outage or other failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When it is used</td>\n<td>Almost all (not all) locks on code-required exits, for free exit if a blackout and a fire coincide. Required by the IBC (International Building Code) and the NFPA LSC (National Fire Protection Association, Life Safety Code) when exiting requires special knowledge, e.g. electromagnets (Maglocks)</td>\n<td>On the egress route, only if free exit is guaranteed whatever the state of power or controls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>Electromagnet</td>\n<td>Electrified exit bar: when pushed it opens mechanically, with or without power; therefore it does not need to be fail safe</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes \"fail safe\" and \"failsafe\" interchangeably; it is the same concept.</li>\n<li>Exam note (outside the book): the NFPA LSC is the NFPA 101 standard.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Continuous Duty vs. Intermittent Duty</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Continuous duty</th>\n<th>Intermittent duty</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Designed to be energized for long periods</td>\n<td>Designed to be energized for short periods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sturdier construction; more resistant to physical attack, especially in electric strikes</td>\n<td>Less robust</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use when an attack on the lock is expected</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Door States</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Open</td>\n<td>Ajar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closed</td>\n<td>Together, but no hardware prevents it from being pushed. Depending on the case, it can meet the fire code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Latched</td>\n<td>Sealed: it is opened by turning the handle or releasing the latch, not by pushing. A change in air pressure does not open it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Locked</td>\n<td>It does not open without a key or credential; once unlocked, the handle must be turned to unlatch it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Fire Rating</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Each door assembly (door + frame) has a fire resistance rating; it is set by building codes.</li>\n<li>Installing electrified hardware, or drilling the door or frame for wiring, can affect that rating.</li>\n<li>The hardware that goes on fire-rated openings, frames and doors must also be fire-rated.</li>\n<li>Rated openings must be <strong>latched even when unlocked</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Locking Devices (lists from the book)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Electronic access control can lock and unlock by electrical means alone, with keypads and readers, together with electromagnets.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Magnetic locks</th>\n<th>Electronic locks</th>\n<th>Mechanical locking devices</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Continuous duty: shear locks and direct hold</td>\n<td>Electronic locks</td>\n<td>Grade 1: 800,000 cycles, heavy commercial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continuous duty with delayed egress, direct hold</td>\n<td>Electric strikes</td>\n<td>Grade 2: 400,000 cycles, light commercial and heavy residential</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Mortise-style</td>\n<td>Grade 3: 200,000 cycles, most residential</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Cylindrical-style</td>\n<td>Panic and latching hardware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Electric doorknobs and deadbolts</td>\n<td>Dead bolts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Mortise and cylindrical</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the book, \"Examples of Electronic Locks include\" appears as a bullet inside the list of electromagnets (a formatting error). The mechanical list mixes durability grades with hardware types.</li>\n<li>Exam note (outside the book): grades 1 to 3 correspond to the ANSI/BHMA (American National Standards Institute / Builders Hardware Manufacturers Association) classification; the book does not name the standard.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Primary Types of Electrified Locking Mechanisms</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Advantages and uses</th>\n<th>Limitations and precautions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Electric deadbolt</strong></td>\n<td>The oldest and simplest. A solenoid (electromagnet) moves a bolt, normally in the frame, in or out of a strike in the door</td>\n<td>Can be fail safe (opens when power is removed) or fail secure (stays locked)</td>\n<td>Not recommended on doors that must open on their own with the fire alarm: the bolt binds if people push the door when power is cut (panic). There are tapered bolts to prevent binding; consult local codes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Electric latch</strong></td>\n<td>Also with a solenoid, in the frame, with a strike in the door. Uses a beveled latch</td>\n<td>Advantage over the deadbolt: it does not have to be retracted to close; the spring yields as it passes over the strike</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Electric strike</strong></td>\n<td>Complement to any mechanical lock. A solenoid releases or blocks the keeper</td>\n<td>Widely used for years in apartment buildings, offices and stores. <strong>It is not a lock</strong>: it works with one</td>\n<td>The handle is fixed on the controlled side; access is remote (interior button, reader or keypad). If the interior handle turns, exit is free; if both are fixed, exit is made with the same devices. With a cylinder, it opens with a key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Electric lockset</strong></td>\n<td>Normal mortise lock, electrified to control whether the handle turns</td>\n<td>Increasingly popular in access control. Storeroom function: fixed exterior handle controlled by a reader; interior handle always free for exit</td>\n<td>The door must be drilled and the cable run through the hinge or around it. Option: a sensor that, when the interior handle is turned, sends a request-to-exit to shunt the magnetic contact or the local siren. Requires a 4-wire transfer hinge instead of 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Exit device</strong> (panic bar / crash bar)</td>\n<td>On egress routes of high-occupancy buildings. Rim: barely modifies the door. Mortise: mechanism in the edge. Vertical rod: double doors without a fixed frame or mullion; rods to the header and to the floor, exposed or concealed</td>\n<td>Can be electrified for remote re-entry (button or reader/keypad)</td>\n<td>See delayed egress below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Electromagnetic lock</strong> (magnetic lock, Maglock)</td>\n<td>Electromagnet + metal armature. When energized, it attracts the armature and holds the door. Usually mounted on the header; on the side it is called <strong>direct hold</strong>: it reduces the clear opening but is much more secure</td>\n<td>No moving parts; low maintenance if the surfaces are clean and aligned and power is guaranteed</td>\n<td>Inherently fail safe. In high security, backup power. Force of 500 to 2,000 lb (227 to 907 kg); several can be installed per door. The best ones monitor the magnet-armature bond and the door position, against the trick of a non-metallic sheet between the two</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Delayed egress</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A compromise between life safety and asset protection; for emergency-exit-only doors.</li>\n<li>When the bar is pushed, a delay of <strong>15 or 30 seconds</strong> begins; afterwards the door opens.</li>\n<li>Requirements:<ul>\n<li>special signage that warns of the delay;</li>\n<li>connection to the fire alarm system;</li>\n<li>no delay in a fire or other defined life safety emergency.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It does not usually give time to intercept the thief, but it activates a local alarm and reports it to a monitoring center.</li>\n<li>Video identifies the perpetrator and what he is carrying, and records the incident.</li>\n<li>On the electromagnet, a switch detects the push and starts the countdown; the same precautions apply.</li>\n<li>Electromagnets and electric bolts or strikes can be combined. Example: an area with entry and exit control that must allow free exit in an emergency.</li>\n<li>These locks are regulated according to the occupancy or occupancy group and its risk, in the IBC and the NFPA LSC.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Wireless Locks</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Useful where wiring is impractical or aesthetics matter; in large projects they save cable and labor.</li>\n<li>The lock is replaced with a <strong>battery-powered</strong> electrified one with an integrated reader (proximity or keypad).</li>\n<li>It almost always communicates by Wi-Fi with a hub.</li>\n<li>The hub connects to the LAN (local area network) or directly to the access controller, depending on the manufacturer.</li>\n<li>Battery life depends on the number of card reads and openings.</li>\n<li>Styles: cylindrical, mortise and electrified trim for exit bars.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says \"number of card readers\"; from context it refers to card reads.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.3.3 Designing Security Locking Systems</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Despite software-based access control, the mechanical key lock is still the most widely used in corporate and institutional facilities.<ul>\n<li>Reasons: low cost, simple operation and reliability.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If the threat or risk justifies it, use high security locks and restricted keys.<ul>\n<li>Objective: to resist system compromise or picking by a skilled adversary.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Everything is lost if the system is not planned, managed and installed professionally:<ul>\n<li>losing a master key can force the replacement of all the cylinders (thousands of dollars);</li>\n<li>failing to control the issue and return of keys can cause losses of millions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>ALOA (Associated Locksmiths of America) certifications: Registered Locksmith, Certified Locksmith and Certified Master Locksmith. They indicate the locksmith's knowledge and level.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What a locking system controls</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The opening of doors or portals: building perimeter, room, enclosure, confidential file, container, file cabinet or safe.</li>\n<li>The use of equipment (e.g., cash register operations).</li>\n<li>Door operators.</li>\n<li>Loose assets (e.g., security cables for portable computer equipment).</li>\n<li>Every application must be included in the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Evaluate the entire area</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Consider the strength of all the barriers that give access: door, frame, walls, windows, ceiling and floor.</li>\n<li>Examples from the book:<ul>\n<li>an expensive lock on a hollow wooden door: it deters the amateur, but does not stop the professional (it only leaves evidence);</li>\n<li>a strong metal door in a weak frame or in a sheet rock wall: it does not stop the determined intruder.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Professional's preliminary questions (8)</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>What assets must be protected (people, structure, equipment, negotiables, operations, information) and how much are they worth?</li>\n<li>What threats exist and how likely are they to occur?</li>\n<li>Who needs access and how often?</li>\n<li>How much flexibility must the system have?</li>\n<li>What impact will the controls have on normal operations?</li>\n<li>How much weight should be given to the organization's culture or image?</li>\n<li>Are there staff and skills to plan, design, implement, operate and maintain, or must it be outsourced?</li>\n<li>What budget is there to implement and, often more important, to operate and maintain?</li>\n</ol>\n<h5>The Design Plan</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>A locking system is a coordinated arrangement of elements that support and complement each other.</li>\n<li>The plan considers:<ol>\n<li>different, simultaneous levels of control for enclosed spaces;</li>\n<li>the likelihood that those levels will change over time;</li>\n<li>users who need common access in some spaces and exclusive access in others;</li>\n<li>the possible loss, compromise or damage of keys, cards or tokens;</li>\n<li>effective means of adapting the system to planned and unforeseen changes.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>It is designed, not just installed.</strong> Adding or removing locks \"as needed\" is not a locking concept.</li>\n<li>Without planning, security degrades to mere <strong>privacy</strong>. In addition:<ul>\n<li>unauthorized persons get in;</li>\n<li>control of the devices issued is lost;</li>\n<li>management by access levels is sacrificed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Quick test:<ul>\n<li>if it is not clear who accesses what → <strong>it is not secure</strong>;</li>\n<li>if there are no multiple levels of access → <strong>it is not convenient</strong>;</li>\n<li>in both cases → <strong>it is not economical</strong> (money is misapplied).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Security vs. convenience tension: more convenience usually subtracts security, and vice versa. The balance comes from defining needs well at the outset.</li>\n<li>Choose the mechanism suited to each application. Example: the <strong>latch bolt</strong> extends by spring and retracts by pressure on its end or with a lever. This distinguishes it from the <strong>deadbolt</strong> and the <strong>dead latch</strong>.</li>\n<li>They can be mechanical or electronic, with codes or with personal physiological characteristics (biometrics). The planning criteria are common to all.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Lock System Planning Considerations (7)</h5>\n<ol>\n<li>Total number of locks.</li>\n<li>Main categories or sectors of the system.</li>\n<li>Security objectives.</li>\n<li>Size and turnover of the population.</li>\n<li>Related or supporting security subsystems.</li>\n<li>Intelligence or information requirements.</li>\n<li>Criticality of asset exposure.</li>\n</ol>\n<h5>Locking Policy</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The program must be based on a <strong>written policy</strong>, especially with many employees or many sites.</li>\n<li>The policy must:<ol>\n<li>require a systematic approach to the use of locks for security purposes;</li>\n<li>assign specific responsibility for the program: to the security department if there is one; if not, to any responsible manager;</li>\n<li>hold accountable for compliance everyone who uses or has access to keys, locks, access devices or combinations.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>That compliance is enforced through the chain of command and evaluated in the salary review.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-9-e3\">9.4 Lighting and Security Applications</h3><ul>\n<li>Disciplines involved: lighting science and technology; electrical systems; power sources; design of luminaires and housings (aesthetics and function); initial and recurring costs; light trespass; environmental factors (e.g., the mercury in lamps).</li>\n<li>There is specific security lighting, but general lighting also affects security.</li>\n<li><strong>Benefits:</strong> deters crime; supports video surveillance; improves observation of the scene; supports response; distinguishes public from private space; possibly reduces legal liability.</li>\n<li><strong>Disadvantages:</strong><ul>\n<li>cost of equipment, installation and maintenance;</li>\n<li>light pollution and light trespass;</li>\n<li>luminaires that break the aesthetics or the architectural design;</li>\n<li>drawing attention to a site that would prefer a low profile.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.4.1 Types of Lighting Equipment and Lamps</h4>\n<p><strong>Security lighting equipment (5)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Equipment</th>\n<th>Beam</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Streetlight</td>\n<td>Downward, circular</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Searchlight</td>\n<td>Very narrow and high intensity, on a specific area</td>\n<td>Penitentiaries, construction sites, industry; supplement to other lights</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Floodlight</td>\n<td>Medium to wide, over a large area</td>\n<td>Commercial, industrial and residential perimeters; usually mounted on buildings near the perimeter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fresnel</td>\n<td>Narrow and horizontal</td>\n<td>Illuminates the potential intruder and leaves security personnel hidden; common on industrial perimeter fences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High mast lighting</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Parking lots and highways. Height of 70 to 150 ft (21 to 46 m). Main determinant of the light level outside the perimeter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Lamps or light sources</strong> (Purpura, Fennelly, Honey &amp; Broder, 2012; ASIS International, 2004)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lamp</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages and notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Incandescent</td>\n<td>Current through a tungsten filament that becomes white hot</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>The least efficient, the most expensive to operate and short-lived</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Halogen, quartz halogen</td>\n<td>Incandescent filled with halogen gas</td>\n<td>≈ 25% more efficiency and life than incandescent</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fluorescent</td>\n<td>Electricity through a gas in a glass tube; 40 to 80 lumens</td>\n<td>Twice the light and less than half the heat of an incandescent of equal wattage</td>\n<td>Does not give high light levels; hardly used outdoors except for signs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mercury vapor</td>\n<td>Current through a gas</td>\n<td>Long life</td>\n<td>Takes several minutes to give full light; poor color rendition for video (bluish light)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metal halide</td>\n<td>Gas lamp</td>\n<td>Imitates daylight and colors look natural (stadiums); goes well with video surveillance</td>\n<td>The most expensive to install and maintain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-pressure sodium</td>\n<td>Gas lamp</td>\n<td>Efficient and long-lived; streets and parking lots; in fog it lets more detail be seen at greater distance</td>\n<td>Poor color rendition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low-pressure sodium</td>\n<td>Gas lamp</td>\n<td>Even more efficient than high-pressure</td>\n<td>Expensive to maintain; poor color rendition for video</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LED (light-emitting diode)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Very cost-efficient alternative; lasts longer without losing illumination</td>\n<td>See below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Induction</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Long life</td>\n<td>Like fluorescent, mainly indoors; exceptions: covered parking lots, underpasses and tunnels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Exam note (outside the book): the fluorescent lamp's \"40 to 80 lumens\" should be read as lumens <strong>per watt</strong> (efficacy).</li>\n<li><strong>Infrared (IR):</strong> it may be invisible to the naked eye, but it is useful for illuminating video scenes.</li>\n<li>Each lamp differs in color rendition, service life, consumption and warm-up time.<ul>\n<li>Choose lamp and luminaire carefully, both in security lighting and in general lighting that affects security.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>LED and security</strong> (David Beausoleil, president and founder of CAST Lighting, LLC; Beausoleil, 2014)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Before: floodlights, wall packs and parking lot poles. \"More light was better\"; lumens were set by local code or contractor recommendation.</li>\n<li>Almost nothing changed since the 1950s; little attention to glare, placement and levels. Control by dusk-to-dawn photocell, timer, or both.</li>\n<li>LED brought:<ul>\n<li>better light quality, beam patterns and optical performance;</li>\n<li>lower consumption and longer lamp life;</li>\n<li>much smaller luminaires (from HID luminaires with ballast to printed circuit boards);</li>\n<li>more and more lumens per watt.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>With LED \"there can be no waste\": minimal internal reflector losses, spill and unnecessary light.</li>\n<li><strong>Key lesson:</strong> over-lighting and glare <strong>reduce</strong> security.<ul>\n<li>Extreme contrast creates black zones where people hide, cameras cannot see and personnel do not observe.</li>\n<li>The eye takes <strong>20 to 60 seconds</strong> to refocus after glare.</li>\n<li>The following must be resolved: luminaire placement, light distribution, glare control and lumen level.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other lessons: better placement and design; emergence of the wall pack; LED in floodlights, streetlights, high mast and warning signs; more efficiency and better optics.</li>\n<li>It is not a panacea: a poorly placed LED that causes glare saves energy but lights poorly. Design according to the objectives and the other measures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>24 V perimeter LED</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Operates at 24 V (instead of the typical 120 V), with lights mounted on a fence or wall.</li>\n<li>Spacing of 10, 20 or 30 ft (≈ 3, 6 or 9 m); gives reflected light without glare.</li>\n<li>The eye sees beyond the light into the darkness; it almost eliminates the contrasts of over-lighting.</li>\n<li>It can improve the image of cameras and Internet monitoring.</li>\n<li>Origin: derived from low-voltage decorative garden lighting.</li>\n<li>Simple installation: it is attached to existing poles and the cable is run with zip ties to the top rail.</li>\n<li>Estimated savings: up to <strong>80%</strong> compared with the traditional high-voltage system (installation, labor, materials and energy).</li>\n<li>Each LED consumes only <strong>7 W</strong>; the typical pole consumes much more.</li>\n<li>Objective: to put the light where it is needed without a prohibitive cost in the short or long term.</li>\n<li>Uses: storage yards, refineries, tank farms, oil fields, ports, train stations, airports, reservoirs and very large sites with an illuminated fence.</li>\n<li>Future: wireless, signal-over-power line and Internet control; custom products thanks to miniaturization; better color, optics, lumens per watt and life. <strong>Moore's law</strong> will raise performance and lower prices.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.4.2 Lighting Challenges</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Three security purposes of lighting (Purpura, 2013):</strong><ol>\n<li>psychological deterrence;</li>\n<li>enabling detection;</li>\n<li>enhancing video surveillance.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>To achieve them: the right quantity, type and intensity, over the right space and efficiently.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ineffective lighting</th>\n<th>Too much lighting</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Strong contrasts of light and shadow disorient human or electronic surveillance</td>\n<td>Glare blinds officers and response forces</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Poor light helps the intruder hide or evade detection</td>\n<td>The exterior/interior imbalance allows seeing in from outside</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inadequate light makes officers more visible and vulnerable than the intruder</td>\n<td>Over-lighting creates blind spots or \"white out\" in cameras</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>More contrast of light and shadow makes observation difficult</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Contrast:</strong> cases (Kurasz, 2007):<ul>\n<li>A short tunnel with very bright lights: on leaving at night, the contrast with the darkness blinded drivers and caused crashes. One of every two lamps was turned off, with costly rewiring. Lesson: more light is not better.</li>\n<li>A complex that would look well lit in a dark area looks poorly lit next to a very bright neighbor (car dealership, sports field). Lesson: in remote areas with no light around, a lower level is enough.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Glare:</strong> it can be an advantage or a problem.<ul>\n<li>Intentional glare: luminaires pointed outward from the premises to inhibit the intruder's vision. Take care that it does not affect cameras, personnel or legitimate activity.</li>\n<li>To reduce the unwanted kind: increase the mounting height and use steeper aiming angles. This also reduces light pollution and light trespass.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Light pollution</strong> includes:<ul>\n<li><strong>Light spillage:</strong> light that goes beyond the area or point where it is needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Light trespass:</strong> spillage that enters neighboring property or interferes (or could interfere) with others.</li>\n<li>Consequences: from wasted energy and annoyance to hostility or lawsuits.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Beam direction:</strong> depends on lamp, luminaire design, mounting, location and environmental conditions. The luminaire and the mounting weigh heavily.<ul>\n<li><strong>Figure 9.20</strong> (Lighting straight down, © 2013 Innovative Protection Solutions): luminaire pointing straight down; it lights the island but not the stalls or the aisles of the parking lot. Example of ineffective direction.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Beam overlap / dead spaces:</strong> applies to poles and wall packs.<ul>\n<li><strong>Figure 9.21</strong> (Appropriate Overlap Versus Coverage Gap for Pole-Mounted Outdoor Lighting, © 2014 Innovative Protection Solutions): on the left, good overlap in width at ground level and in height; on the right, ineffective design with a coverage gap at ground level and above.</li>\n<li>Rule of thumb: <strong>pole spacing = 2 × mast height</strong>. It varies greatly with the luminaire, the lamp and the angle.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9805em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2806em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">posts</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8444em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2806em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">mast</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>High mast outdoors: wider and more natural distribution, fewer poles (fewer obstacles) and more aesthetic (FEMA, Federal Emergency Management Agency, 2003). Drawback: it is struck by lightning (ASIS International, 2021).</li>\n<li>Directed LED evens out the lighting and makes visibility more efficient.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.4.3 Characteristics of Light and Lighting</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Four characteristics: quantity of light; level or intensity (illuminance); reflectance of materials; color temperature.</li>\n<li>Design depends on the site's objectives. A vehicle checkpoint where the officer reads IDs and looks for contraband needs much more light than a walkway.</li>\n<li>In addition: comply with local codes and standards and sustainability initiatives. The video system can also dictate the light level and the color temperature.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Quantity of light: lumens</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flow of light emitted by a lamp.</li>\n<li>Example: a 100 W household bulb ≈ 1,700 lumens.</li>\n<li>A spot and a floodlight can emit the same lumens: the spot concentrates them and the floodlight scatters them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Illuminance (light level, intensity or illuminance)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Concentration of light on an area.</li>\n<li>Guide in the U.S.: <em>Guideline for Security Lighting for People, Property, and Critical Infrastructure</em> (Illuminating Engineering Society, IES, 2017).</li>\n<li>Lux (metric) = lumens per m². Foot-candle (fc; English) = lumens per ft².</li>\n<li>For the camera or the eye, what is critical is the light that reaches the iris area of the lens: its illuminance.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0576em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">lux</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.224em;vertical-align:-0.8636em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3603em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3448em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5224em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">m</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.7463em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.786em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1776em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">Φ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">lm</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8636em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0576em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">fc</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.2629em;vertical-align:-0.9025em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3603em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3448em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4835em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">ft</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8019em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.8416em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2165em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">Φ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">lm</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.9025em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">fc</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10.76</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">lux</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\">:</span></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Figure 9.22: Natural and Visual Light Levels (levels in foot-candles)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>fc</th>\n<th>Natural source</th>\n<th>Visual experience</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>50,000</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Upper limit of visual tolerance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10,000</td>\n<td>Direct sun</td>\n<td>Fresh snow on a clear day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1,000</td>\n<td>Full daylight</td>\n<td>Average earth on a clear day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>Overcast day</td>\n<td>Average earth on an overcast day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Twilight</td>\n<td>White paper 1 ft from a standard candle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.1</td>\n<td>Deep twilight</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.05</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Snow with a full moon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.01</td>\n<td>Full moon</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.005</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Average earth with a full moon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.001</td>\n<td>Quarter moon</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.0001</td>\n<td>Starlight</td>\n<td>Snow with starlight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.00005</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Grass with starlight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.00001</td>\n<td>Overcast night</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0.000001</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Absolute limit of vision</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> in the figure, the word \"Candle\" appears on a separate line in the natural source column. It seems to complete \"standard candle\" from the 1 fc row.</li>\n<li>Exam note (outside the book): by historical definition, 1 fc is the light at 1 ft from a standard candle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reflectance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If an object does not reflect light it cannot be seen, except as a silhouette against the background.</li>\n<li>With light that is not white, colors look false.</li>\n<li>Light surfaces reflect more: a concrete parking lot reflects more than an asphalt one.</li>\n<li>Mirror or shiny surface: high; matte surface: low. In manufactured objects it varies with the material and age.</li>\n<li>Measure: ratio between incident light and reflected light (in lumens), expressed in %.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ρ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">%</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.1963em;vertical-align:-0.836em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3603em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">Φ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">incident</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">Φ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">reflected</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.836em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Exam note (outside the book): the book states the ratio the wrong way around (incident over reflected). Reflectance is reflected over incident; that is why it never exceeds 100%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.23: Reflectance Measurements</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Material</th>\n<th>Reflectance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Asphalt</td>\n<td>5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Old concrete</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New concrete</td>\n<td>25%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Red brick</td>\n<td>25%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grass</td>\n<td>40%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Snow</td>\n<td>95%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Exam note (outside the book): the book gives 40% to old concrete and 25% to new. The opposite would be expected (new is lighter in color); probable inversion. Know the table as it stands and the possible erratum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Color temperature (CCT)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The book calls it \"corrected color temperature\" (CCT): it measures how warm or cool a light is.</li>\n<li>It is measured in kelvin, an absolute scale; the book says that 0 K ≈ −272 °C.</li>\n<li>Exam note (outside the book): the standard term is \"correlated color temperature\". Also, 0 K = −273.15 °C, and in the international system one says \"kelvin\", not \"degrees Kelvin\".</li>\n<li>Analogy of metal in the furnace: bright red ≈ 2,700 K; white ≈ 4,100 K; blue ≈ 5,000 K (similar to daylight).</li>\n<li>Red is perceived as warm; white or blue, as cool. Color temperature strongly influences mood and atmosphere.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.24: Color Temperature</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Warm 3,000 K</th>\n<th>Neutral 3,500 K</th>\n<th>Cool 4,100 K</th>\n<th>Daylight 5,000 K</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Effects and mood</td>\n<td>Friendly, intimate, personal, exclusive</td>\n<td>Friendly, welcoming, nonthreatening</td>\n<td>Orderly, clean, efficient</td>\n<td>Bright, alert, exact coloring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Applications</td>\n<td>Restaurants, hotel lobbies, boutiques, libraries, offices, retail stores</td>\n<td>Public reception areas, showrooms, bookstores, offices</td>\n<td>Meeting rooms, classrooms, hospitals, offices, mass-market stores</td>\n<td>Galleries, museums, jewelry stores, medical offices, print shops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lamps</td>\n<td>Fluorescent, incandescent, halogen</td>\n<td>Fluorescent, mercury vapor</td>\n<td>Fluorescent, mercury vapor, metal halide</td>\n<td>Fluorescent, mercury vapor, metal halide</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Figure note: CCT of low-pressure sodium = 1,750 K; of high-pressure = 2,000 K.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Color rendition and CRI</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Security personnel must describe colors accurately: it helps to stop and process criminals captured on video.</li>\n<li><strong>Color rendition:</strong> the lamp's ability to distinguish, grade and faithfully reproduce the colors of an object.</li>\n<li>It is measured with the <strong>CRI</strong>, a scale from 0 to 100. \"Color rendition index\" and \"color rendering index\" are equivalent.<ul>\n<li>70 to 80: good. Over 80: excellent. 100: daylight.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>High- and low-pressure sodium and mercury vapor have a very low CRI: do not use them with color cameras or where identifying color is critical.</li>\n<li>Example from the book: under low-pressure sodium, a green shirt looks bluish.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says \"100 percent\"; the CRI is an index, not a percentage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.25: Color Rendition Index for Various Lamp Types</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lamp</th>\n<th>CRI</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Incandescent</td>\n<td>100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Halogen</td>\n<td>100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fluorescent</td>\n<td>75–100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metal halide</td>\n<td>70</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LED</td>\n<td>75–90</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mercury vapor</td>\n<td>50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-pressure sodium</td>\n<td>20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low-pressure sodium</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A high CRI also matters in retail, restaurants and precision manual work. It gives visual clarity and is associated with higher morale and productivity.</li>\n<li>At night outdoors, a high CRI gives pedestrians a greater sense of security: they see farther and perceive depth better.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Brightness and glare (more subjective terms)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Brightness:</strong> perception of the amount of light reaching the eyes.</li>\n<li><strong>Glare:</strong> excessive brightness. It hurts the eye, lowers its efficiency, creates excessive contrast, makes people look away and makes seeing difficult.<ul>\n<li>It can deter at the perimeter, but it harms patrols and response forces just the same.</li>\n<li>It can cause light trespass onto neighboring properties, sidewalks and streets. It must not dazzle or create excessive contrast for drivers and pedestrians.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Transitional lighting:</strong> standard practice to reduce risk when moving from a brightly lit place to the parking lot.</li>\n<li>Many communities limit the light level and its spillage or trespass through zoning.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.4.4 Overview of Lighting Systems</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lamp (light bulb)</td>\n<td>The manufactured light source: filament or arc tube, glass cover and connectors</td>\n<td>\"Incandescent\", \"mercury vapor\", etc., name the technology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Luminaire (fixture)</td>\n<td>Complete unit: lamp, socket, reflectors and diffusers that distribute and focus the light</td>\n<td>Some lamps (spots, floods) come with an integrated reflector. Includes the electrical connection and, depending on the technology, <strong>ballasts</strong> (correct voltage, current and waveform to start and operate) and <strong>photosensors</strong> (turn on or off according to ambient light). Chosen for performance and aesthetics. With industrial vibration: secondary retention, e.g. metal cable, so it does not fall on people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mounting hardware</td>\n<td>Wall or pole bracket</td>\n<td>Secures the luminaire at the right height and in the right place</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electrical power</td>\n<td>Feeds lamp, ballasts and photocells</td>\n<td>See HID below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HID (high-intensity discharge):</strong> metal halide, mercury vapor and high-pressure sodium.<ul>\n<li>Sensitive to voltage drops: they give light with a high-pressure arc; if the voltage drops enough, the arc goes out.</li>\n<li>Restarting usually takes a long time: up to <strong>20 minutes</strong>.</li>\n<li>In high security or life safety areas (vaults, cash registers, evacuation routes and assembly points): consider batteries, generators and UPS (uninterruptible power supply).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.4.5 Economic Considerations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Cost weighs heavily in deciding the lighting level.</li>\n<li>Lighting required by code or regulation: fire safety, retail banking, NRC (Nuclear Regulatory Commission) or OSHA (Occupational Safety and Health Administration).</li>\n<li>It is often an optional expense: it is justified by identifiable savings or a quantifiable reduction in risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Breakdown of operating cost</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Approx. %</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Capital goods (lamps, ballasts)</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maintenance</td>\n<td>4%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Energy</td>\n<td>88%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>That is why the most important thing is energy efficiency, called the lamp's <strong>efficacy</strong>.</li>\n<li>The next cost is replacement lamps; it varies with the technology and quality.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Effectiveness</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0574em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">watts consumed</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">lumens emitted</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:1em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">lm/W</span></span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Cleaning and replacing lamps</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maintenance includes the labor to change and clean lamps.</li>\n<li>Dirt on the luminaire lowers the light emitted:<ul>\n<li>clean environment (computer room, offices): drops ≈ 3 to 4% per year; cleaning every <strong>3 years</strong> is recommended;</li>\n<li>very dirty environment: after <strong>1 year</strong> it can give only <strong>80%</strong> of its design output (−20%).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Consumption is the same with or without dirt: it pays to clean regularly.</li>\n<li>Lamps perform less with age: at the end of their rated life they may give only <strong>80%</strong>, even when clean.</li>\n<li><strong>Planned replacement</strong> of all lamps or by groups: costs less labor than changing them one by one and avoids dark zones.</li>\n<li>It is advisable to replace at a multiple of the cleaning cycle. Example from the book: average life 6 years and cleaning every 2 → change all of them every 3 cleaning cycles.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">cleaning cycles between replacements</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.2519em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">cleaning interval</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">service life</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.2019em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">years</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">years</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Number of luminaires:</strong> depends on the area, the required light level, the height and design of the luminaires and the technology.</p>\n<p><strong>Uniformity</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A uniform distribution, especially outdoors, is expensive; some variation is accepted.</li>\n<li>It is measured as the ratio between the average level and the minimum level.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.1963em;vertical-align:-0.836em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3603em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3175em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0576em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">minimum</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0576em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">average</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.836em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Environment</th>\n<th>Typical ratio</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Work environments</td>\n<td>1:0.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pedestrian walkway</td>\n<td>4:1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Street or roadway</td>\n<td>10:1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>More uniformity → better depth perception and a greater sense of security.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book mixes notations. 4:1 and 10:1 are average:minimum. 1:0.7 appears to be written backwards (minimum = 0.7 × average, ≈ 1.4:1). More uniformity = ratio closer to 1:1 (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.4.6 Starting and Restrike</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Some lamps take time to come back on after being turned off (on purpose, by blackout or by voltage drop).</li>\n<li>It is typical of HID: they depend on an arc, and the tube must cool before it is restruck (restrike).</li>\n<li>HID (and, to a much lesser degree, fluorescent) lamps also take time, when cold, to reach their design light output.</li>\n<li>For the security professional: scheduled turn-on covers startup, but a total or partial outage, even a brief one, can leave the area without light for quite a while.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.26: Lamp Starting and Restrike Times (minutes)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lamp</th>\n<th>Starting</th>\n<th>Restrike</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Incandescent</td>\n<td>Instant</td>\n<td>Instant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Halogen</td>\n<td>Instant</td>\n<td>Instant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fluorescent</td>\n<td>Instant*</td>\n<td>Instant*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metal halide</td>\n<td>5–8</td>\n<td>10–20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LED</td>\n<td>Instant</td>\n<td>Instant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mercury vapor</td>\n<td>5–8</td>\n<td>10–20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-pressure sodium</td>\n<td>2–5</td>\n<td>1–20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low-pressure sodium</td>\n<td>5–8</td>\n<td>0–8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>(*) Fluorescent needs time, especially in cold weather, to reach full output.</li>\n<li>New technologies seek to reduce these times. Example: HID with <strong>two tubes</strong>; one is used and the other stays cold for quick restrike.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.4.7 Security Lighting for Selected Applications</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Four applications of security lighting:</strong> standby, continuous, movable and emergency. The book only lists them.</li>\n<li><strong>Rule of thumb</strong> for pedestrians or normal cameras (minimum reflected light):</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Task</th>\n<th>Minimum</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Detection</td>\n<td>0.5 fc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recognition</td>\n<td>1.0 fc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identification</td>\n<td>2.0 fc</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Recommendations and levels</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Perimeter fencing</td>\n<td>Light deters, just like the physical barrier. With perimeter intrusion detection, it supports alarm assessment by CCTV (closed-circuit television) and the response force in delaying and apprehending. NRC: <strong>0.2 fc</strong> at the perimeter and in the clear zone between the two fences. Consider light trespass onto neighbors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Site landscape and perimeter approaches</td>\n<td>Streets and walkways are lit for life safety and asset protection. Vertical light on the ground helps to see potholes or objects. For pedestrians to see each other, or for cameras, some of the light must be horizontal (lighting vertical surfaces). Trees and shrubs that offer hiding places: difficult and expensive. Continuous lighting can give glare projection and controlled results. Ground light directed at trees and shrubs is the most effective way to keep them from serving as hiding places, and it provides a high-contrast background for seeing movement. Levels: walkways <strong>1–4 fc</strong>; streets <strong>0.5–2 fc</strong>; entrances <strong>10 fc</strong>; open yards <strong>2 fc</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Building facade</td>\n<td>If exterior objects cannot be lit well, high contrast identifies shape and movement: light the facade with floodlights. If it reflects well, it contributes horizontal light for an observer (person or camera) between the facade and the object. <strong>0.5–2 fc</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parking structures</td>\n<td>Difficult: few vertical elements that reflect or give contrast. Some municipal codes require a horizontal white stripe at waist height. The low ceiling calls for luminaires that spread light horizontally (excellent for vertical surfaces). With cameras, choose luminaires that do not dazzle the lens. <strong>5 fc horizontal with 4:1 uniformity</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open parking</td>\n<td>Height is less limited, except for codes or light trespass; high sources give horizontal illumination. High-pressure sodium: high efficiency, many lm/W, cost-effective. From <strong>0.2 fc</strong> minimum (low activity: general parking and pedestrians) to <strong>2 fc</strong> (heavy vehicle activity). Cash collection and vehicle access control: <strong>5 fc minimum</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loading docks</td>\n<td>Depends on after-hours activity. Exterior with no trucks parked: <strong>1 fc</strong> on the facade (rolling doors, stairs, ramps) and <strong>0.2 fc</strong> in the open yard. Nighttime loading and unloading: <strong>5 fc</strong>. Interior: bays <strong>15 fc</strong>; unpacking and sorting <strong>20 fc</strong>; packing and shipping <strong>30 fc</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security control and monitoring rooms</td>\n<td>Computer work: <strong>30–50 fc</strong>; task areas such as the console: <strong>50–70 fc</strong>. Reflection on screens is a problem: watch the position of luminaires and the angle of screens; use flat or anti-glare screens. If monitoring (alarms, video) predominates, lower the ambient light considerably to reduce reflections and raise contrast. The security manager should discuss dimmers with the designer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guard and gate houses</td>\n<td>Well-lit exterior at night: <strong>2–5 fc</strong>. Interior by day: high task light, <strong>30 fc</strong>; at night, lower it below the exterior level in order to see the surroundings and approaching pedestrians and vehicles well</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> several figures do not match Figure 9.27 (see 9.4.9).<ul>\n<li>Walkways: 1–4 fc here vs. 0.20 fc in the table.</li>\n<li>Gate houses: 2–5 fc exterior here vs. 30 fc in the table (the 30 fc is the interior daytime light).</li>\n<li>Entrances: 10 fc here vs. 5 fc pedestrian and 10 fc vehicular in the table.</li>\n<li>Open yards: 2 fc in landscape vs. 0.2 fc in loading docks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.4.8 Lighting Considerations for Electronic Surveillance Systems</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Additional considerations with cameras:<ol>\n<li>CRI, to reproduce and identify colors accurately;</li>\n<li>reflectance of materials;</li>\n<li>directionality of reflected light.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Wavelength:</strong> the eye sees the visible spectrum, from <strong>400 nm (violet) to 700 nm (red)</strong>; nm = nanometers.<ul>\n<li>It is a small part of the electromagnetic spectrum. At its ends are ultraviolet and infrared.</li>\n<li>The sun and artificial sources emit energy outside human sensitivity.</li>\n<li>Cameras usually see what we see, but many capture the <strong>near infrared (700–1,100 nm)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>IR illumination:</strong> with a special-sensor camera it makes it possible to see without visible light.<ul>\n<li>Useful where zoning limits light trespass or covert surveillance is sought.</li>\n<li>It only works with <strong>monochrome</strong> cameras, not color.</li>\n<li>Place the illuminator next to the camera, with a beam aperture matching the lens setting.</li>\n<li>On PTZ (pan/tilt/zoom) cameras, mount the IR on the mechanism so that it follows the camera. Verify that it supports the weight of camera + luminaire.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Color temperature and cameras:</strong><ul>\n<li>Technical data sheets are usually based on incandescent tungsten filament (<strong>2,700 K</strong>).</li>\n<li>No usual color camera in video surveillance works well at the <strong>1,700 K</strong> of low-pressure sodium.</li>\n<li>New CCD (charge-coupled device) sensors greatly improve color with high-pressure sodium (<strong>2,200 K</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book refers to \"Figure 9-25\" for color temperatures; it is <strong>Figure 9.24</strong> (9.25 is the CRI one).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book gives different CCTs. Low-pressure sodium: 1,750 K (Fig. 9.24) vs. 1,700 K here. High-pressure sodium: 2,000 K vs. 2,200 K.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Color camera</th>\n<th>Monochrome (black and white) camera</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Light needed for equal quality</td>\n<td>Twice as much</td>\n<td>Half as much</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum video signal</td>\n<td>At least 50% of the full signal; otherwise the color record fades</td>\n<td>Only 20–30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Works with IR</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Other specifications on camera data sheets</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimum light levels quoted at <strong>75% or 89.9% reflectance</strong>.</li>\n<li>Minimum levels tied to a specific lens. Example: standard color camera with 0.25 fc at f/1.2; with f/2.0 it rises to 0.68 fc, that is <strong>2.7 times</strong> more light.</li>\n<li><strong>White balance:</strong> automatic adjustment of the camera to the color temperature of the source. Range of <strong>2,200 K to 7,000 K</strong>; it must be compatible with the existing or designed lighting.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0574em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0.25</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">fc</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0.68</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">fc</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.7</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<h4>9.4.9 Standards for Security Lighting Levels</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Standards are numerous and vary widely.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book's introductory sentence is truncated (\"The following guidance.\"); the guidance is Figure 9.27.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 9.27: Guidelines for Minimum Lighting Levels</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Application</th>\n<th>Minimum (IES standards)</th>\n<th>Comments (other standards)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Perimeter fence</td>\n<td>0.50 fc</td>\n<td>NRC 0.20 fc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outer perimeter</td>\n<td>0.50–2.00 fc</td>\n<td>NRC 0.20 fc; DoAFM 0.15 fc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open areas</td>\n<td>2.00 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open parking</td>\n<td>0.20–0.90 fc</td>\n<td>IES, high vehicle activity: 2.00 fc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parking structure</td>\n<td>5.00 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pedestrian walkways</td>\n<td>0.20 fc</td>\n<td>IES: 7.50 fc at automated teller machines (ATM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pedestrian entrances</td>\n<td>5.00 fc</td>\n<td>DoAFM 2.00 fc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vehicular entrances</td>\n<td>10.00 fc</td>\n<td>DoAFM 1.00 fc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Facade</td>\n<td>0.50–2.00 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gate houses</td>\n<td>30.00 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loading dock exterior</td>\n<td>0.20–5.00 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loading bays</td>\n<td>15.00 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offices: general</td>\n<td>30–50 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offices: task</td>\n<td>50–70 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interior public areas</td>\n<td>10–20 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retail stores</td>\n<td>50.00 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bank: lobby</td>\n<td>20.00 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bank: teller</td>\n<td>50.00 fc</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bank: automated teller machine (ATM)</td>\n<td>15.00 fc</td>\n<td>IES: 30 fc at the preparation table</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Acronyms in the table: IES = Illuminating Engineers Society of North America; NRC = Nuclear Regulatory Commission; DoAFM = Department of the Army Field Manual (U.S. Army field manual).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the table says \"Illuminating Engineers Society\"; elsewhere in the book it is Illuminating Engineering Society.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the PDF, the label \"(or other standards)\" falls under the \"Application\" column; by content it belongs to the comments column.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> for automated teller machines the table gives 7.50 fc (comment on walkways) and 15.00 fc (bank row).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Additional sources cited by the book</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>National Lighting Bureau (www.nlb.org).</li>\n<li>Illuminating Engineering Society (www.ies.org).</li>\n<li>National Council on Qualifications for the Lighting Professions (www.ncqlp.org).</li>\n<li>National Association of Lighting Designers (www.iald.org).</li>\n<li>Philips Lighting Company.</li>\n<li>GE Lighting.</li>\n<li>Sylvania Lighting.</li>\n<li>Exam note (outside the book): the acronym IALD corresponds to International Association of Lighting Designers, not \"National\".</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-9-e4\">Key terms</h3><p>barrier · balanced design · reinforced concrete · rebar · panic bar · astragal · Z-strip · stud-in-hole hinge · grout · annealed glass · tempered glass · wired glass · laminated glass · bullet-resistant glazing · polycarbonate · window film · blast curtains · security shutters · secondary barrier · chain link fence · selvage · top guard · outrigger · clear zone · passive barrier · active barrier · fixed bollard · removable bollard · retractable bollard · twist-up bollard · shallow mount · deep mount · rising wedge barrier · rotating wedge barrier · mobile wedge barrier · rotating plate barrier · drop arm crash beam · crash gate · engineered planter · Jersey barrier · K rating (K4/K8/K12) · M rating (M30/M40/M50) · P1 · SD-STD-02.01 · ASTM F2656-07 · PAS 68 · VBIED · symbolic barrier · natural barrier · cable system fencing · expanded metal · welded wire fabric · electric security fence · monitored fence · concertina · record safe · fire-resistant safe · insulated filing device · Class 350/150/125 · safe within a safe · Record Protection Equipment · SMNA · UL 687 · UL 72 · UL 155 · UL 1037 · NFPA 232 · NFPA 220 · NFPA 75 · net working time · TL-15 · TL-30 · TL-30X6 · TRTL-30X6 · TRTL-60X6 · TXTL-60X6 · B-Rate · C-Rate · RSC · relocker · hard plate · burglary/fire-resistive container · GSA-approved safe · fire-resistive vault · burning bar · time lock · two-man rule · opening index · escape mechanism · warded lock · lever lock · pin tumbler lock · shear line · high security cylinder · side bar · wafer tumbler lock · dial combination lock · electronic combination lock · master keying · interchangeable core · try keys / jingle keys · fail safe · fail secure · continuous duty · intermittent duty · electric strike · electric deadbolt · electric lockset · exit device · delayed egress · magnetic lock (Maglock) · direct hold · lock grades 1–3 · locking policy · Fresnel · high mast lighting · HID · lumen · foot-candle · lux · reflectance · color temperature (CCT) · color rendering index (CRI) · glare · light trespass · luminaire · efficacy · uniformity ratio · restrike time · standby / continuous / movable / emergency lighting · IES · NRC</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"9.1 Barriers"},{"id":"e1","t":"9.2 Containers and Vaults"},{"id":"e2","t":"9.3 Locks and Locking Mechanisms"},{"id":"e3","t":"9.4 Lighting and Security Applications"},{"id":"e4","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"the book converts 8 ft as 2.5 m here and as 2.4 m for the electric fence. The correct value is 2.44 m."},{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"the book writes 6 in (52 mm); the correct conversion is 152 mm."},{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"the book repeats \"No. 11\" in two rows. According to its own Figure 9.2, 0.113 in is gauge 11½."},{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"the book says they \"do not use electric current\" but pulses. Understand it as meaning there is no continuous current (outside the book)."},{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"two different heights. 30 in (0.76 m) is the typical height for aesthetics; 38 in (≈1 m) is the absolute maximum."},{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"Figure 9.8 spaces the bollards 1.5–3 ft (0.5–0.9 m), less than the general guideline of 36–48 in (0.9–1.2 m). In the figure the bollards are combined with a fence."},{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"the book writes \"PAS68:2103\"; it is a typo for <strong>PAS 68:2013</strong>."},{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"the figure prints \"48k/km/h\"; it is 48 km/h."},{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"the book converts 18 in as 0.48 m; it is actually 0.46 m."},{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"Figure 9.12 says \"Rotating Edge Barrier\"; it is a typo for rotating <strong>wedge</strong> barrier."},{"s":"9.1 Barriers","h":"the weight example uses a 12 ft barrier, but the standard lengths cited are 12.5 and 20 ft."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"the book gives as an example \"UL 687, burglary safe standard\" within the fire section. The fire standard for records equipment that the book itself cites (9.2.2) is <strong>UL 72</strong>."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"it is very easy to confuse the <strong>350-1 insulated filing device</strong> with the <strong>350-1 fire-resistant safe</strong> and the <strong>350-1 insulated records container</strong>. The label says which it is: fire-resistant safe, insulated record container or insulated filing device (or file drawer)."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"the book writes \"first-resistant safes\"; it is a typo for fire-resistant."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"the text gives 4, 3, 2 or 1 hour for 125 °F and 150 °F, but Figure 9.18 does not include 125-4."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"the book does not label these rows. The 15 min row corresponds to TL-15 and the 30 min row to TL-30."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"in UL 687 resistance to explosives is marked with <strong>TX</strong>; the highest is <strong>TXTL-60X6</strong> (outside the book). The book attributes the explosives to TRTL-60-X6."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"RSC II says \"six-square-inch opening\" (6 in²), while TL speaks of \"6-inch square\" (6 × 6 in). Possible typo."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"the text says \"see TL-15 and TL-30 below\", but those descriptions come earlier."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"here the book gives 1,550 °F exterior for ½, 1 and 2 h; earlier it gave 1,700 °F (927 °C) for 350-4. (outside the book) The standard curve rises with time: 1,550 °F at ½ h, 1,700 °F at 1 h, 1,850 °F at 2 h and 2,000 °F at 4 h."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"earlier the book treats drop and explosion as separate tests; here it chains them."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"this list includes a ½ h class 350 safe. According to Figure 9.18, ½ h exists only in insulated filing devices."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"the book gives 12 in of brick for both 4 and 6 hours; only the concrete changes."},{"s":"9.2 Containers and Vaults","h":"the book says not to put the first and last number \"above 10 or below 90\". It is inverted: the logical thing is to avoid numbers <strong>below 10 or above 90</strong> (outside the book)."},{"s":"9.3 Locks and Locking Mechanisms","h":"in the PDF text the exponent appears as \"1004\"; it is 100 raised to the 4th power (10^8). The figure of 100,000,000 is correct."},{"s":"9.3 Locks and Locking Mechanisms","h":"the book writes \"fail safe\" and \"failsafe\" interchangeably; it is the same concept."},{"s":"9.3 Locks and Locking Mechanisms","h":"in the book, \"Examples of Electronic Locks include\" appears as a bullet inside the list of electromagnets (a formatting error). The mechanical list mixes durability grades with hardware types."},{"s":"9.3 Locks and Locking Mechanisms","h":"the book says \"number of card readers\"; from context it refers to card reads."},{"s":"9.4 Lighting and Security Applications","h":"the book says \"100 percent\"; the CRI is an index, not a percentage."},{"s":"9.4 Lighting and Security Applications","h":"the book mixes notations. 4:1 and 10:1 are average:minimum. 1:0.7 appears to be written backwards (minimum = 0.7 × average, ≈ 1.4:1). More uniformity = ratio closer to 1:1 (outside the book)."},{"s":"9.4 Lighting and Security Applications","h":"several figures do not match Figure 9.27 (see 9.4.9)."},{"s":"9.4 Lighting and Security Applications","h":"the book refers to \"Figure 9-25\" for color temperatures; it is <strong>Figure 9.24</strong> (9.25 is the CRI one)."},{"s":"9.4 Lighting and Security Applications","h":"the book gives different CCTs. Low-pressure sodium: 1,750 K (Fig. 9.24) vs. 1,700 K here. High-pressure sodium: 2,000 K vs. 2,200 K."},{"s":"9.4 Lighting and Security Applications","h":"the book's introductory sentence is truncated (\"The following guidance.\"); the guidance is Figure 9.27."},{"s":"9.4 Lighting and Security Applications","h":"the table says \"Illuminating Engineers Society\"; elsewhere in the book it is Illuminating Engineering Society."},{"s":"9.4 Lighting and Security Applications","h":"in the PDF, the label \"(or other standards)\" falls under the \"Application\" column; by content it belongs to the comments column."},{"s":"9.4 Lighting and Security Applications","h":"for automated teller machines the table gives 7.50 fc (comment on walkways) and 15.00 fc (bank row)."}]},"physical-10":{"html":"<p><strong>Context (chapter introduction):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>CPTED is a cost-effective tool, but it is often forgotten when designing or renovating facilities.</li>\n<li>Causes of the oversight: lack of knowledge, excessive reliance on electronic systems, management concerns and the fact that the concept has detractors.</li>\n<li>Academic response: universities are creating CPTED certificates and courses in architecture, urban planning, criminal justice and security management.</li>\n<li><strong>1972 — Oscar Newman</strong> (architect at the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development, HUD): publishes <em>Defensible Space</em>.</li>\n<li>Newman showed how crime opportunities are \"designed\" into the built environment. From this CPTED was born (Kennedy 2006).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-10-e0\">10.1 Principles of CPTED</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Origin of the term:</strong> first used by Ray Jeffrey in his book <em>Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design</em> (1971).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes \"Jeffrey\"; the criminologist's name is C. Ray Jeffery (outside the book).</li>\n<li><strong>Central principle (Jeffery):</strong> proper design and effective use of the built environment reduce the incidence and fear of crime, and improve the quality of life.</li>\n<li>Basis: common sense and awareness of how people use space, for legitimate or criminal purposes.</li>\n<li>It is best applied with multidisciplinary teams: planners, designers, architects, landscape architects, law enforcement, security professionals and users (residents, employees).</li>\n<li>The security professional must master CPTED in order to work with local prevention officers, design authorities and architects, in new construction or renovation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CPTED as a set of management tools: its targets:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Target</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>Example from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Places</td>\n<td>Physical environments designed to produce behavioral effects that reduce opportunity and fear. Includes offices, parking lots, parks and public spaces, multifamily buildings, warehouses, schools, places of worship and shopping centers</td>\n<td>In a parking lot, emergency call posts and more light overcome the sense of distance and isolation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Behavior</td>\n<td>Some places predict, create, promote or allow crime or disorder; others induce law-abiding behavior</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design and use of space</td>\n<td>Redesigning or making better use of a space encourages desirable behavior and discourages crime</td>\n<td>Placing the parking lot in front of a convenience store</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Three underlying elements of CPTED:</strong> territoriality · surveillance · access control.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of CPTED measures (Crowe, 1991):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it involves</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mechanical</td>\n<td>Physical security hardware or electronic systems</td>\n<td>Locks, fencing, CCTV (closed-circuit television)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Organizational</td>\n<td>People or activities, not equipment</td>\n<td>Guards, police patrols, neighborhood watch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural</td>\n<td>Natural elements and nonmechanical objects</td>\n<td>Terrain, layout, landscaping, gates, walls</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Crowe places \"fencing\" among the mechanical measures, but \"gates\" and \"walls\" among the natural ones.</p>\n<p><strong>CPTED tools (examples from the book):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Idea</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural territorial reinforcement</td>\n<td>Create a sense of ownership, responsibility and accountability in owners, managers or occupants, so that they watch and detect intruders. People defend the space they feel psychologically they own. It can be physical (building codes as a defense strategy) or symbolic. It is called \"natural\" because it arises from the normal, routine use of the environment</td>\n<td>Low edge hedges next to the sidewalks of an apartment complex (they mark each dwelling and prevent shortcuts). Color, texture, surface variations, signage and wayfinding. Real barriers (fences, walls) and symbolic markers (warning signs, low hedges, low wooden picket fences)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural surveillance</td>\n<td>More visibility for occupants and casual observers means more detection of intruders or misconduct</td>\n<td>A tall wooden fence that hides a loading dock invites theft; a chain-link fence that allows seeing through discourages it. Windows, door viewers, mirrors. Open sidewalks and streets, designs without hiding places, adequate nighttime lighting, activity areas in front of the building (lobby–street visual connection)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural access control</td>\n<td>Real and symbolic barriers (doors, fences, shrubs) that define and limit access</td>\n<td>Dense, thorny shrubs under low windows, window locks or alarm. Hinges not accessible from the outside, secure access to roofs and to ladders, strikeplates anchored to the frame, closed windows, key control policies, neighborhood watch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Management and maintenance</td>\n<td>The space must look cared for. \"Broken windows\" theory (Wilson &amp; Kelling, 1982): unrepaired deterioration gives an impression of abandonment and of the absence of a capable guardian, and increases criminal opportunity</td>\n<td>Markers of deterioration: graffiti, trash, abandoned furniture, weeds. A car parked for a long time with a broken window will soon have more. Maintain lighting, paint, signage, fences, walkways and repair what is broken</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legitimate activity support</td>\n<td>For places that are hard to protect because of their location or geography. Drugs and crime thrive where residents and management do not \"claim\" the space with legitimate activities</td>\n<td>A hotspot can disappear if the police set up a substation or if maintenance staff occupy the space</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Key element of CPTED:</strong> designing or redesigning a place to reduce the opportunity and fear of crime, by natural, mechanical and organizational or procedural means.</li>\n<li>CPTED is a prevention theory based on <strong>environmental criminology</strong>.</li>\n<li>Premise: well-designed buildings, parks and parking lots deter opportunities, reduce fear and improve quality of life.</li>\n<li>Benefit to the organization: it improves its security posture and security awareness.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>10.1.1 Criminal Behaviors and Patterns</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Applying CPTED requires knowing the basic theory and practice of crime prevention.</li>\n<li>The <strong>American Crime Prevention Institute</strong> (University of Louisville, Kentucky) set operating assumptions. Two apply especially to CPTED:<ol>\n<li>Knowledge in prevention evolves and is interdisciplinary: successful practices and emerging technologies must be analyzed, and findings shared.</li>\n<li>Strategies must be flexible and creative: what works in one case may fail in a similar one with a different culture or environment.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>CPTED is a living discipline. It changes with design and construction, the use of space, social mentality and criminal behavior.</li>\n<li><strong>Flash mobs case:</strong> many young people burst into a store at the same time for mass theft and vandalism. They organize almost instantly through social networks. They are hard to stop because of the number of perpetrators.</li>\n<li>The physical characteristics of the site influence the offender's decisions, because they alter his chances of being detected.</li>\n<li><strong>Key rule:</strong> crime is more likely when the offender perceives a low probability of detection and identification and a high probability of escape.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Target Selection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Priority: first prevent the most serious crimes and protect the most victimized people and places.</li>\n<li>Steps against repeat victimization:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>How it is applied</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Quickly remove signs of victimization</td>\n<td>Repair visible damage as soon as possible; sometimes the victim must be helped. In apartments: cover or replace glass, repair door jambs, change locks and keys, repair broken or burned-out lights, trim shrubs that block the view and apply CPTED strategies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Improve physical security</td>\n<td>Improves natural surveillance, visibility, sightlines and access control. Reduces piggybacking (slipping in behind someone authorized), intrusion and other unauthorized access. Redesign exterior entrances to see everyone who enters or leaves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Block easy access to targets</td>\n<td>Doors, gates, screens and real or symbolic barriers. Relocate cash registers, vending machines, service vehicles, automated teller machines (ATM) and other valuable assets. In stores, expensive items in locked display cases. At gas stations, the employee behind impact- and bullet-resistant polycarbonate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Protect especially vulnerable targets</td>\n<td>If they cannot be moved: removable bollards, roll-down gates, fences and screens to deny access after hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Regulate access to high-risk assets or areas</td>\n<td>Require credentials, permits or fees for restrooms, parks, schools and parking lots</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Capable Guardian Concept</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It comes from the <strong>routine activity theory</strong>: the presence of capable guardians can deter crime.</li>\n<li>Offenders avoid victims they perceive as armed, able to resist or dangerous.</li>\n<li>They avoid areas heavily patrolled by police, guards or \"nosy neighbors\".</li>\n<li>They also avoid passive barriers: alarms, fences and locks.</li>\n<li>They look for the easiest, lowest-risk path. Natural surveillance and capable guardians lower the site's perceived vulnerability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Situational Crime Prevention</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Origin: Great Britain, late 1970s and early 1980s.</li>\n<li>It reduces opportunities in all behavioral contexts, not just in buildings and spaces.</li>\n<li>At first it attacked the opportunity for specific crimes. Recent versions also attack motives.</li>\n<li>Those social prevention strategies connect it with second generation CPTED.</li>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> to manage, design or manipulate the environment systematically and as permanently as possible in order to:<ol>\n<li>increase the effort required of the offender;</li>\n<li>increase his risk;</li>\n<li>reduce the reward he perceives.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Impact: it changed British crime control policy. It shifted from a focus on the offender (personality, behavior, background) to a focus on the environment that creates opportunities.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>10.1.2 Later Developments in CPTED</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Generation</th>\n<th>When</th>\n<th>Focus</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First generation</td>\n<td>Jeffery 1971 and Newman 1972</td>\n<td>Reduce physical opportunities: territoriality, surveillance, access control, maintenance and legitimate activity. \"Fortified city\" mentality. Local scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second generation</td>\n<td>Approx. 2003–2008</td>\n<td>In addition to removing physical opportunities, create a sense of neighborliness to reduce the motives for crime. Identifies the community's social resources. Reinforces legitimate activity with community cohesion. Seeks a socioeconomically balanced community and a well-maintained city for everyone. Local scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third generation</td>\n<td>Current (modern-day)</td>\n<td>Environmental sustainability and green technology. Reprograms urban space by digital means and green technologies. Retains surveillance and control (1st) and effective physical design and sociocultural diversity (2nd). A green and sustainable urbanity is perceived as safe. Treats security as a global matter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Sources of the table: UNICRI and MIT 2011 (United Nations Interregional Crime and Justice Research Institute and Massachusetts Institute of Technology); Fennelly and Perry 2018 (local vs. global scope).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Second generation CPTED: 4 strategies (Saville &amp; Cleveland, 2013):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cohesion</td>\n<td>Community groups, neighborhood associations and personal development programs: leadership, financial and organizational skills, conflict resolution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacity threshold</td>\n<td>Also called tipping point theory. It balances land uses and urban elements. Too many abandoned properties tip the area toward crime. A balance of legitimate commerce, recreation and diverse housing improves livability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Community culture</td>\n<td>Cultural, artistic, sports and recreational activities that unite neighbors around a common purpose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connectivity</td>\n<td>Links the neighborhood with neighboring neighborhoods and with funding and political support from businesses and higher levels of government</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>CPTED 3-D and Beyond</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It looks at space through three Ds: <strong>Designation</strong> (its purpose), <strong>Definition</strong> (management and identity) and <strong>Design</strong> (according to the desired function and behavior).</li>\n<li>Figure 10.1 evaluates the 3-D factors. It also includes the traditional <strong>\"4 Ds\"</strong>: deter, detect, delay, deny, and other considerations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 10.1 — Tool for Evaluating CPTED 3-D Factors (questions by factor):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>Key questions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Designation</td>\n<td>What is the designated purpose of the space? What was it originally intended for? How well does it support its current or intended use? Is there conflict? How and where?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition</td>\n<td>How is the space defined? Is its ownership clear? Where are its boundaries? Do social or cultural definitions influence its use? Are there clear legal and administrative rules in policy and effectively enforced? Do signs indicate the correct use or the limits of access? Is there conflict or confusion between purpose and definition?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design</td>\n<td>Does the physical design support the intended function? Does it support the desired or accepted behaviors? Does it clash with the productive use of the space or the intended human activity? Is there confusion about how the design aims to control or modify behaviors?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deterrence</td>\n<td>Does security personnel deter the illegitimate and promote the intended behavior? Does the design allow good surveillance and access control to and from the property? Does the intended behavior itself discourage the illegal or illegitimate?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detection</td>\n<td>Can entry be controlled? Is there a process to assess whether an intrusion is legitimate or not? Is detection achieved by physical design, mechanical technology or operational manpower? Is a person or agency notified to respond?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delay</td>\n<td>Are there active barriers? Are there passive barriers? Are there guards or other designated responders? How much delay is needed to detect and respond?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Response</td>\n<td>What are the roles and post orders of the responding guards? What equipment is needed for a complete response? What tactics allow a quick and clear response? What training do they receive?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reporting</td>\n<td>What communication network documents intrusions or calls for help? What is the written incident reporting protocol? How is the documentation organized and stored? Is it sufficiently detailed and clear?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discrimination</td>\n<td>Does the staff know how to distinguish legitimate users or threats from illegitimate ones? Is the equipment sensitive enough to distinguish false threats from real ones?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neutralization</td>\n<td>Was the threat sufficiently deterred? Was the system reset and tested to avoid complacency or false alarms? Were the attackers controlled, the police notified and the scene secured again? In the case of fire, smoke or flood: was it neutralized, was damage assessed and was the scene secured against contamination or pilferage?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>After answering, the space is rated by how well it supports natural access control, natural surveillance and territoriality.</li>\n<li>Goal of the questions: that there be no conflicts between the space, its activities and the expected behaviors.</li>\n<li><strong>Case:</strong> an access control that is hard to use or that fails frequently leads employees to prop doors open. It shows that the system was poorly chosen and users were not trained.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text announces the \"4 Ds\" (deter, detect, delay, deny), but Figure 10.1 has no \"Deny\" section. In its place it adds Response, Reporting, Discrimination and Neutralization.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-10-e1\">10.2 Tools of CPTED</h3><h4>10.2.1 Tools That Address the Three Elements of CPTED</h4>\n<p><strong>Territoriality</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>People take better care of a space when they feel ownership or responsibility for it, formal or informal.</li>\n<li>Effect on users (employees, residents, visitors): they are more observant, report what is suspicious, notice and correct risks, and take better care of the property.</li>\n<li><strong>Most basic tool:</strong> a good training and awareness program. People know what to look for and feel empowered to participate.</li>\n<li><strong>Simple tool:</strong> signage. The environment communicates its designated use and appropriate behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Graphic</td>\n<td>Symbol that conveys a message with an image</td>\n<td>Figure of a man on the men's restroom</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signage</td>\n<td>Message in letters or words</td>\n<td>\"No trespassing\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A business that has already suffered crimes is \"on notice\": the crime may be considered foreseeable.</li>\n<li>Signs put users on notice and try to shift part of the responsibility to them. To achieve this, they must set clear rules.</li>\n<li>Examples of rules from the book: \"Do not walk on the grass\" · \"Enter at own risk\" · \"Lock your valuables\" · \"No trespassing\" · \"Don't even think of committing a crime\".</li>\n<li>In a parking lot, signage indicates: entrances and exits, speed bumps, speed limits, direction of traffic, keeping valuables put away, direction of parking, use of video surveillance, fire exits and alarms, and panic buttons and intercoms.</li>\n<li><strong>Note:</strong> posting a sign does not relieve the building owner of legal liability.</li>\n<li>Signage directs people to destinations and areas, especially in multitenant buildings. It reduces wandering by strangers or people with bad intentions.</li>\n<li>At the same time it creates a welcoming environment and protects the security and privacy of the occupants. An information kiosk or a well-placed concierge/receptionist achieves the same.</li>\n<li>Consider the demographics of employees, visitors and neighbors: it may call for signs in several languages.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Considerations for security signage and graphics:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Architectural design considerations</td>\n<td>The architect or graphics consultant advises on letter size and type, reading distance, reflectivity, lighting, location and intended audience. Some typefaces are more readable from a distance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Systems considerations</td>\n<td>Consistent, uniform and well-distributed signs. Like the fire exits on all stairways, security signs must be in all critical areas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Procedural considerations</td>\n<td>They clarify procedures (Figure 10.2): wearing an ID badge, warning of a change in floor level in a restaurant, warning shoppers of anti-theft surveillance, indicating areas where sensitive information is not discussed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Signage figures (for a person with 20/20 vision):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reading distance</td>\n<td>50 ft (15 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum letter height</td>\n<td>6 in (15 cm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum height of graphics or symbols</td>\n<td>15 in (38 cm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum indoor illumination</td>\n<td>215 lumens per square meter (= 215 lux, about 20 fc; equivalence outside the book)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lamps</td>\n<td>Placed to avoid glare on the sign</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>If the user does not follow the posted rules, the burden of responsibility shifts to him and his intent can be questioned.</li>\n<li>Without notice, the behavior is left to personal interpretation and is hard to question.</li>\n<li>Early involvement of the architect is advisable in order to use signage well.</li>\n<li><strong>Warning (Figure 10.2):</strong> the sign that keeps vehicle exhaust from entering the building also tells a terrorist where to place a chemical or biological device. Signage can harm security.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Surveillance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Definition: the act of observing.</li>\n<li>It is not just cameras, monitors and digital recorders. Natural surveillance can be just as effective for certain objectives.</li>\n<li><strong>Figure 10.3 (Typical Elevator Lobby):</strong> the office has glass doors. The receptionist, well positioned, observes the elevator lobby and notices suspicious activity or risks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Access Control</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It can also be natural.</li>\n<li><strong>Classic example: the castle.</strong> Surveillance with high towers and clear zones. Access control with walls, moats, berms, drawbridges, terrain, water, rock/stone and few access points.</li>\n<li><strong>Figure 10.4 (Berm and Tree Line):</strong> an office complex uses a landscaped berm and a line of trees to control the perimeter and provide privacy.</li>\n<li>The berm has a single vehicular entrance/exit. It was chosen from the start instead of a standard fence.</li>\n<li>Result: perimeter security, an aesthetic environment for employees and easy approval from residential neighbors.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>10.2.2 Reducing Crime Through Architectural Design</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The security professional integrates CPTED into the design by working with community groups and professionals.</li>\n<li><strong>Integrating CPTED into initial planning is more cost-effective</strong> than changing once construction has begun.</li>\n<li>Designing without thinking about security can cause: lawsuits, injuries, expensive retrofitting and more security staff.</li>\n<li>Measures added later: distort building functions, raise personnel costs and end up exposed and unsightly.</li>\n<li>A building must: allow efficient work, meet the needs of users and protect them from hazards and crime.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Traditional building planning process (5 steps):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Programming</td>\n<td>The owner informs the architect of the purpose of the building and its occupants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Schematic design</td>\n<td>The architect makes bubble diagrams with circulation patterns and proximity relationships. They evolve into floor plan, site plan and elevations, the start of engineering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Design development</td>\n<td>The architect presents ideas and makes corrections. The drawings add structural, mechanical and electrical engineering, ventilation and site planning. Larger scale (in the U.S., typically 1/4 in = 1 ft)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Construction documents or working drawings</td>\n<td>Final drawings with all technical data and technical specifications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Bids for construction and selection of contractor</td>\n<td>Drawings and specifications go out to bid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Security must be addressed in the programming phase.</strong></li>\n<li>Main responsibility of the owner or client: to define threats to people, information and property; to set the required level of security and the budget. He may consult a security professional.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Three security challenges for architects:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Challenge</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Determining requirements</td>\n<td>Analyze the designated purpose of the space. Examine the cultural, legal and physical definitions of prescribed, desired and acceptable behaviors. Design to support them. Ask about existing policies and practices and integrate them into programming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Knowing the technology</td>\n<td>Technology advances quickly. Many projects add specialists and systems integrators. The architect needs a grounding in security principles to evaluate and work with specialists and manufacturers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Understanding architectural implications</td>\n<td>Integrate security and life safety goals, sometimes in conflict, with other requirements. Plan space, function and people to deter, detect, delay, deny and respond (deter, detect, delay, deny, response)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The architect converts the requirements into an <strong>architectural program</strong>. Like a restaurant menu: it defines what will be produced and how much it will cost.</li>\n<li>The architect decides circulation, access, materials, fenestration and other features that help or frustrate security.</li>\n<li>After that point, every security change requires modifying drawings and specifications, with more time and money.</li>\n<li>Some jurisdictions require a security review by the police to approve the building permit. Inspectors look for points for assaults, muggings, burglaries and crimes of opportunity.</li>\n<li>Many jurisdictions have security ordinances: lighting levels and security doors and windows. Some companies require similar reviews.</li>\n<li>If security is just one more design requirement, it carries no more weight than fire protection or landscaping.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CPTED vs. target hardening:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>What it uses</th>\n<th>Limitation or advantage</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Traditional target hardening</td>\n<td>Barriers: locks, alarms, fences, gates</td>\n<td>Often overlooks natural access control and surveillance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CPTED</td>\n<td>Natural and normal uses of the environment</td>\n<td>Sometimes achieves the same effects as mechanical hardening and surveillance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>The 3 basic CPTED strategies</strong> (natural access control, natural surveillance, natural territorial reinforcement) are applied through three methods:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Organized</td>\n<td>Staffing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mechanical</td>\n<td>Technological products</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural</td>\n<td>Site planning, design, landscaping and signage</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A checklist helps incorporate CPTED principles into proposed projects.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>10.2.3 Access Control, Surveillance, and Territorial Reinforcement</h4>\n<p><strong>Where to consider access control:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>All entrances and exits of the site and the building.</li>\n<li>Internal access points to restricted or controlled zones.</li>\n<li>Features of the environment and the building used to enter: approach routes, trees, ledges, skylights, balconies, windows, tunnels.</li>\n<li>Security screening devices: guard posts, surveillance, identification equipment, turnstiles, card readers.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Objective of access control:</strong> to deny access to the target (asset) and to create in the potential offender a perception of risk, detection, delay and response.</li>\n<li>Surveillance reinforces access control.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Access control</th>\n<th>Surveillance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Organized</td>\n<td>Security guards</td>\n<td>Police and guard patrols</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mechanical</td>\n<td>Hardening: locks, card systems, windows with protective glazing, special door and window hardware, reinforced walls, floors or doors</td>\n<td>Lighting and video</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural</td>\n<td>Spatial definition and circulation patterns, sometimes with security zoning</td>\n<td>Windows, low landscaping, elevated entrances</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Site Development and Security Zoning</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Security planning should begin, if possible, at <strong>site selection</strong>. Goal: a site that meets architectural requirements and provides security advantages.</li>\n<li>The analysis evaluates conditions inside and outside the site: topography, vegetation, neighboring land uses, circulation patterns, neighborhood crime patterns, police patrol patterns, sightlines, hiding zones, location of utilities and current and planned lighting.</li>\n<li>Also: access points and circulation of vehicles, employees, service personnel, visitors and external pedestrians.</li>\n<li>Questions at this stage:<ol>\n<li>What is the site physically like and how does it influence security?</li>\n<li>What land uses surround it?</li>\n<li>What crimes occur in the area and how often?</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>A high-risk site is not automatically ruled out. The owner may choose it and address the threats with design, technology, personnel and security management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The three levels of defense the architect designs:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>What it protects</th>\n<th>Key measures and data</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1st Site perimeter and grounds</td>\n<td>Site analysis is the first level of defense</td>\n<td>Walls, dense plantings, fences, berms, ditches, lighting and natural topographic separations. Against intruders, the most common: walls, chain-link and other barriers. Landscaping marks the property line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2nd Building perimeter/exterior</td>\n<td>A critical \"second line of defense\". Think of the area as having four sides, a roof and a floor</td>\n<td>Main entry points: windows, doors, skylights, storm sewers, roof, floor and fire escapes. Doors: frame, latches, locks, hinges, panic bars and the wall around them. Windows: type of glass, frame, hardware and size of the opening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3rd Internal space protection</td>\n<td>Sensitive zones inside the facility</td>\n<td>Security technology, personnel and restricted circulation. Zones with different levels of security</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Doors and windows are inherently poor at resisting penetration.</li>\n<li><strong>Key figure:</strong> most stud walls and metal deck roofs can be breached with hand tools in <strong>less than 2 minutes</strong>.</li>\n<li>Unreinforced concrete block walls are quickly broken with a sledgehammer or a car.</li>\n<li>Zoning lets each person reach his destination and keeps him from entering where he has no business.</li>\n<li>Controlling access by department filters out unwanted visitors, reduces congestion and helps employees identify and challenge unauthorized people.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Types of security zones:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Zone</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Unrestricted zones</td>\n<td>Open to everyone during hours of use. The design must invite people to do their business and leave without entering controlled or restricted zones</td>\n<td>Lobbies, reception areas, snack bars, certain personnel and administrative offices, public meeting rooms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Controlled zones</td>\n<td>A valid reason is needed to enter. Once inside, one moves between departments without severe restriction</td>\n<td>Administrative offices, staff dining rooms, security offices, office work areas, loading docks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Restricted zones</td>\n<td>Sensitive areas only for assigned personnel. Some sections require additional access control</td>\n<td>Vaults, sensitive records, chemicals and drugs, food preparation, mechanical areas, telephone equipment, electrical equipment, control rooms, laboratories, laundry, sterile supply, special equipment and sensitive work areas</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> administrative offices appear in two zones. \"Certain\" offices are unrestricted; the rest are controlled.</li>\n<li>Logical order: first resolve circulation with zoning; then consider mechanical solutions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Visibility: Privacy versus Security</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Balancing privacy and security is difficult.</li>\n<li>A hedge or a low fence defines, physically and psychologically, the public and the private.</li>\n<li>A picket fence marks the edge without blocking the view or limiting surveillance.</li>\n<li>Adding trees gives a sense of enclosure, but it allows seeing between the fence and the tree canopy.</li>\n<li>Block or brick walls secure, but they also hide thieves. Bare walls invite graffiti.</li>\n<li>Walls with thorny plants (bougainvillea, carissa, wild lime) protect more and form a better barrier. Certain plants deter even without a wall.</li>\n<li>Disadvantages of thorny plants: risk to small children and a maintenance challenge.</li>\n<li>Carissa sizes: emerald blanket (dwarf), boxwood blanket (up to 6 ft / 1.8 m) and carissa grandiflora (7–8 ft / 2.1–2.4 m).</li>\n<li>In housing: hedges, shrub beds or low ground covers discourage entry through windows. Avoid tall, large-leaved plants that hide the intruder.</li>\n<li>Pygmy date palms in front of windows let the breeze through, but the thorns at the base of their leaves stop anyone who climbs. Similar: Jerusalem thorn and cinnecord.</li>\n<li>Even if the thief enters through a door and leaves through a window, carrying large objects out through shrubs and hedges is much harder.</li>\n<li><strong>Earth berms:</strong> very effective for natural access control, but they can block the view.</li>\n<li><strong>Park case:</strong> some berms broke up the monotony of flat terrain, but kept the police from seeing play areas used by gangs. They were lowered to <strong>no more than 2.5 ft (0.7 m)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Landscaping rules for a secure design</strong> (in addition to climate):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Figure or detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Plants must not block large parts of a main path or recreation area</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consider growth rate and maintenance</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low plants set back from the edge of paths</td>\n<td>1 yd (1 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum height of low shrubs</td>\n<td>32 in (81 cm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thorny shrubs at possible hiding places, areas of illegitimate activity or walls with windows that must be kept away from people</td>\n<td>They can attract trash; a low fence may be needed against wind-blown debris</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hard landscaping that is vandal-resistant and without \"projectiles\"</td>\n<td>No pavers or loose gravel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Furniture and landscape elements must not serve for entry or for seeing over walls or hedges</td>\n<td>Furniture for brief use, not suitable for sleeping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tree canopies pruned from below</td>\n<td>Up to 8 ft (2.4 m) where appropriate: clear line of sight, fewer hiding places and ambushes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The type and location of trees greatly affect the coverage of exterior lighting. Security lighting should go from the tree canopy downward.</li>\n<li>The security professional should take part in the landscaping and lighting plans.</li>\n<li>Know whether trees are deciduous (lose their leaves) or evergreen like the pine.</li>\n<li><strong>Trees and video:</strong> on the plan the camera seems to have a clear view, but trees create blind spots. Consider height and foliage when locating cameras.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> here the shrub limit is 32 in (81 cm) and the berm limit 2.5 ft; do not confuse with other \"2 or 3 feet\" rules from other sources (outside the book).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-10-e2\">10.3 CPTED Applications in Various Settings</h3><ul>\n<li>Considerations, problems and challenges when applying CPTED in specific settings or sectors.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>10.3.1 Commercial Office Buildings</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>An office project (renovation, expansion or new construction) obliges security and owners to work with designers in new ways.</li>\n<li>The architect needs information for the architectural program. Their typical source is the <strong>security director</strong>.</li>\n<li>If the client has no in-house security professional, it can hire one to advise the company and the architect.</li>\n<li>The security professional identifies the vital assets. <strong>Three categories of assets: people, information, property.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Asset to Be Protected: People (the most valuable asset)</strong></p>\n<p>Six questions for the architect's needs assessment:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Who: who are the users?</td>\n<td>Visitors, staff, service crew, sales</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What: what can they do in the building?</td>\n<td>Tasks, recreation, work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Why: why are they there?</td>\n<td>Official business, visiting as guests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When: when do they arrive and leave?</td>\n<td>Shifts or other patterns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where: where can they go?</td>\n<td>Horizontal, vertical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How: how do they get there?</td>\n<td>Access methods, circulation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Figure 10.5 — Access and Hours (table the security professional gives the architect):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Who</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n<th>What</th>\n<th>When</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>How</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Vice President</td>\n<td>Company business</td>\n<td>Administration, management</td>\n<td>0800–1800, M–F</td>\n<td>All areas, total access</td>\n<td>Staff elevator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custodian/cleaning crew</td>\n<td>Clean offices, vacuum</td>\n<td>Clean, collect trash</td>\n<td>\"0111–0400\", M–F</td>\n<td>Lobby floors</td>\n<td>Officer lets in, has access to special service…</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the cleaning crew's time \"0111\" appears to be an erratum in the book. <strong>Pending:</strong> confirm the start time and the end of the \"How\" cell (cut off in the extracted text).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Custodian/cleaning crew case:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Security implications</th>\n<th>Design implications</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Control of after-hours access</td>\n<td>Sign-in desk for service trades</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Verify that the cleaning employee is who he says he is</td>\n<td>Access control system to control entry and record movements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security personnel who log and supervise entries and exits</td>\n<td>Location of trash containers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key control</td>\n<td>Location of the service elevator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Location of service doors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Alarms for offices and for critical wiring to and from the control room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Infrastructure and structure lines</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>It is only a sample of what the architect must resolve with the security professional's information.</li>\n<li>The answers to the six questions go into the architectural program or \"problem-seeking\" stage. Later they are used for the schematic, development and construction drawings.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Asset to Be Protected: Information</strong></p>\n<p>Critical needs assessment questions:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Who has access to the information? Who enters after hours?</td>\n<td>Staff, management, contractors/consultants, vendors, joint venture partners, mail room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What information is it?</td>\n<td>Data, trade secrets, personnel files, blueprints, computer programs, classified government information, third-party information, operational or business plans</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What format is it in?</td>\n<td>Personal knowledge, paper, electronic media, models/prototypes, equipment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How transportable and transferable is it?</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Why is it worth protecting?</td>\n<td>Competitive advantage, critical technology, strategic value, regulatory requirements, privacy protection, contractual or legal restrictions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When is it accessible or vulnerable, and for how long?</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where is it accessible or vulnerable?</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How can it be obtained or compromised, legitimately and illegitimately?</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>With the answers, the security professional prepares an <strong>information asset protection plan</strong> for the architect.</li>\n<li>With it, architectural, technological and organizational responses are designed for a comprehensive strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Architectural features to protect information:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Recommendation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Doors and windows</td>\n<td>Minimum of exterior openings, easy to observe. Controlled and monitored doors for accountability. Main entrance defined architecturally. Service entrance secured and supervised. Storerooms monitored and where a supervisor can see movement. Take care what information, activities and equipment can be seen through internal and external windows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reception desk</td>\n<td>Screens visitors, vendors and outsiders. It must see the entrance doors and the elevators. It marks the layering between public and private entrance. Screens, visitor logs and security data not visible to visitors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Controlled areas</td>\n<td>Distinguish and restrict access: VIP zones, computer/data center and sensitive operations areas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Computer/server room</td>\n<td>Strict access control, protected service lines, high security glass for easy supervision and central location</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Computer anchoring</td>\n<td>Secure laptops, tablets and computers with security cables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employee traffic patterns</td>\n<td>Control entry and exit. A controlled and supervised exit allows checking packages, briefcases and bags. Locker areas well lit and supervised against theft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevators</td>\n<td>They should open onto the supervised core. Special floors or VIP offices may require access control in the elevator or dedicated elevators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loading dock</td>\n<td>Its own path, away from employees and visitors. Ground loops and an intercom to alert security to a truck when no one is supervising. If possible, physically separate shipping and receiving</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mail room</td>\n<td>At the end of a clear, unobstructed path from the loading or mail delivery area. Secure room with a monitored door to control access and for accountability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vaults</td>\n<td>Locate vaults, fireproof safes and files according to the site and frequency of use. Supermarkets put the vault at the front so it can be seen; other stores hide it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conference or meeting facilities</td>\n<td>Outside the restricted zone, normally next to the lobby and before the access control point. In this way attendees need not be given a badge and are kept out of private or semiprivate areas</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Asset to Be Protected: Property</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The same process applies: ask the key questions and set security and architectural criteria.</li>\n<li>Market advantage: \"designing out\" shoplifting, pilferage, espionage, assaults and terrorism.</li>\n<li>A building can have environmental conflicts that give opportunities to the offender. CPTED and defensible space principles help prevent them.</li>\n<li>The owner does not control his whole surroundings: neighborhood streets; nearby stadiums, bars, parks or dumps; public services such as the police.</li>\n<li>Even so, if the security professional assesses the risks well and does a good needs assessment, the right solutions emerge.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CPTED Considerations for Offices and Office Buildings</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typical office risks: walk-in thefts, burglary, theft by deception and fraud, vandalism, loss of information and employee theft.</li>\n<li>After identifying risks, consider site, building and internal security requirements:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Recommendation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Site location</td>\n<td>Study zoning and building code restrictions of the site and surroundings. Review current and planned landscaping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External areas</td>\n<td>Design garages and parking lots with care. Arrange entrances, exits, paths and streets to avoid traffic conflicts. Identify and prioritize fences, gates and lighting. <strong>Design the video after laying out circulation.</strong> Address delivery and trash areas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access points</td>\n<td>For employees, visitors and service personnel. Limit the number of entrances; if needed, use exit-only doors. Consider fire exits and the life safety code early so as not to undermine security. Secure loading docks. Needs of basements and mechanical rooms. Exterior stairs, roof access, doors and windows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal locations</td>\n<td>Arrange with care: lobby entrances, secondary entrances, reception, cashier, computer areas, electrical or telephone services, executive areas, dining rooms, staff restrooms, security command center, vaults and special equipment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lobby entrance</td>\n<td>Make it clear to everyone that the lobby is the right place to enter. Higher quality materials to project success, stability and power</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reception desk</td>\n<td>A layer of building security. The receptionist identifies and approves visitors before letting them through. Good view of who enters; can block passage to nonpublic areas, elevators and stairs. Do not overload with tasks that distract from screening. Emergency call button. Can hide video equipment (angles and ventilation). Design that slows attacks, like the wide counters in banks that prevent reaching or jumping over</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access control and video surveillance</td>\n<td>Resolve them in the design and the architectural program. Review stairs, elevators and corridors. Choose a key system that allows for growth and change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pedestrian access</td>\n<td>A direct path from the street to the front of the building</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Orientation</td>\n<td>Orient the building to allow seeing into the site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Doors and windows</td>\n<td>Extra attention, especially on the ground floor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conduits</td>\n<td>Plan conduits for security, with future capacity, from the design stage; avoids expensive renovations. Consider shielding lines to protect communications</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>10.3.2 Industrial Buildings and Facilities</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>High risk of: employee theft, burglary, robbery, industrial espionage, vandalism and arson.</li>\n<li>Steps for the architect or the security professional:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Recommendation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Traffic</td>\n<td>Clearly define incoming and outgoing traffic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Perimeter</td>\n<td>Define the boundaries with landscaping, fences, walls, etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paths of travel</td>\n<td>Separate the paths of public, private and service vehicles, and of pedestrians. Pedestrian routes and accesses easy for others to see</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Building shell</td>\n<td>Minimum of openings. Clear lobbies and entrances, as a transition from the security perimeter to the plant area. <strong>Reinforce every opening larger than 96 sq in (619 cm²) and less than 18 ft (5.5 m) from the ground:</strong> polycarbonate, window sheets, meshes or others. If a perimeter door must be left open for ventilation, add a chain-link door (air and visibility with security)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitoring</td>\n<td>Exterior emergency doors with alarm, video and security monitoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service doors</td>\n<td>They should lead directly from outside to the service areas, to reduce interior traffic. Under in-person or video surveillance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shipping and receiving</td>\n<td>Separate both functions as much as possible to reduce collusion and pilferage. Dock inside the building to expose the material less. Waiting room with restrooms for drivers, so they do not cross the building</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trash</td>\n<td>Cleaning staff should reach compactors or incinerators without leaving the building</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Research and development (R&amp;D)</td>\n<td>R&amp;D and sensitive activities away from normal circulation routes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employee entrances</td>\n<td>Directly from the employee parking lot. Wide doors for flow and so that staff can supervise against pilferage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Punch clocks</td>\n<td>Near the employee entrance, separated by barriers for controlled entry and credential checks by security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personnel office</td>\n<td>At the outer edge of the building, so that applicants do not walk through it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevators</td>\n<td>Separate freight and personnel elevators to expose cargo less to theft and pilferage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warehouse</td>\n<td>Finished product away from operating areas. Access control at its doors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other storage</td>\n<td>Tool rooms and storerooms with ceiling enclosure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>10.3.3 Parking Facilities</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Parking lots and garages use CPTED with surveillance, access control and territorial reinforcement.</li>\n<li>It is achieved through the design interface, security patrols and technology.</li>\n<li>Common threats: vehicle theft, theft from vehicles, attacks on people (assault, robbery, rape) and vandalism.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vulnerability Assessment: the first step</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the U.S., the standard of care requires that it include:<ol>\n<li>crime history of the site;</li>\n<li>review of landscaping, lighting, stairs, elevators, surveillance capacity, access control equipment, signage and restrooms;</li>\n<li>inspection of supervision or payment facilities;</li>\n<li>examination of operating and personnel policies and procedures.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Who, why, what, when, where, how questions:<ol>\n<li>Whom does it serve: shoppers, commuters, students or employees?</li>\n<li>How many cars use it and how fast do the spaces turn over?</li>\n<li>Are the sightlines clear or blocked by walls, columns or ramps?</li>\n<li>What are its hours and how do they affect the user's environment?</li>\n<li>Is the light natural or artificial? Are the luminaires at ceiling height? If so, what color is the ceiling and how are they placed?</li>\n<li>Is there video surveillance? With what details?</li>\n<li>Is there street-level protection: gates, screens or other barriers?</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Additional questions: vehicle and pedestrian entrances, accessible routes for the disabled, condition of elevators, location and visibility of stairs, and whether little-used areas can be selectively closed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On the Ground</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Surface parking: where it can be seen from the street and from nearby occupied buildings (natural surveillance).</li>\n<li>Defining the perimeter and controlling access deters unwanted pedestrian passage: fences, changes of level, ground-floor protection and other barriers that channel toward designated accesses and avoid hiding places.</li>\n<li><strong>Ground floor with wire mesh; upper floors open</strong> with tensioned cables so cars do not go past the stall.</li>\n<li>Mesh instead of wall and open floors: allow natural surveillance and let calls for help be heard.</li>\n<li>The ground-floor mesh should not run from floor to ceiling: it would serve for climbing to upper floors.</li>\n<li>Low shrubs next to the perimeter wall discourage climbing or cutting the mesh.</li>\n<li>Exterior garage doors: <strong>egress only</strong>.</li>\n<li>Varied landscaping: better low hedges with tall-canopy trees than a solid hedge. Keep everything pruned for good visibility and without hiding places.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Vegetation rule in parking lots</th>\n<th>Figure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Plants over 3 ft (1 m) prohibited near entrances</td>\n<td>Not within 10–15 ft (3–4.5 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pruning of low branches of mature trees</td>\n<td>Up to 8 ft (2.5 m)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book converts 8 ft as 2.4 m in 10.2.3 and as 2.5 m here. The figure that counts is 8 ft.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vehicle and pedestrian access</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Traffic engineers prefer several accesses; but more entrances make it harder to control users and uses.</li>\n<li><strong>CPTED prefers a single entrance and exit for all vehicles.</strong></li>\n<li>If volume requires more, each access needs: booth with an attendant, gate arm, roll-down shutter to close after hours, video and good lighting.</li>\n<li>Pedestrian access is often forgotten or poorly designed. <strong>Primary rule: do not force the pedestrian to cross the path of the cars.</strong></li>\n<li>If unavoidable, create a safe path to a marked crossing that alerts drivers.</li>\n<li>Pedestrian routes can pass by the attendant's booth to make surveillance easier.</li>\n<li>Accessibility: reserved spaces and attention to ramps, railings, floors, crossings and pedestrian routes, stairs and elevator location and design.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Booths, restrooms and payment machines</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Booths: <strong>360-degree</strong> field of view, monitored by video, with security glass, duress alarms and drop safes with a sign that the attendant cannot take money out.</li>\n<li>Booth lighting sufficient for the video and dimmable, so that the attendant can see outward at night.</li>\n<li>Attendant's restroom: near the booth, in a surveillable area, locked and with a personal alarm inside.</li>\n<li><strong>Public restrooms: leave them out of the design.</strong> They are a meeting point for victims and predators, and hard to secure because of privacy.</li>\n<li>If unavoidable: doors visible from the attendant's post; open maze-type entrances to hear screams; panic and help buttons; motion-activated light.</li>\n<li>Automated payment machines: in view of users and staff, against vandalism, theft and attacks on customers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Structural Elements</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>New construction: <strong>round columns</strong>, because they give more visibility than rectangular or square ones.</li>\n<li>Ramp most consistent with CPTED: <strong>exterior loop</strong>. It leaves floors flat and sightlines clear.</li>\n<li>Where a solid wall is needed: portholes with mesh, windows or openings to give openness and allow casual observation.</li>\n<li>Stairs and elevators: central location and visible from the attendant's post. If not possible, video to watch entrances and exits.</li>\n<li>Panic alarms and door position switches alert the attendant that someone is in the stairwell.</li>\n<li>Stairs visible from street level and of transparent glass.</li>\n<li>The bottom of the stairs must not offer hiding places. Roof exits, if it is not a parking level, must be secured.</li>\n<li>Rooftop mechanical room doors: always locked.</li>\n<li>Basement and roof doors: position switches, intercoms, screech alarms and signal transmission to security or police.</li>\n<li>Elevators: all the glass and visibility the structure allows. Those with glass walls on the exterior of the building are seen from the street and the garage. With intercom and audible alarms.</li>\n<li>High-rise or underground mixed-use garages: elevators that lead into a <strong>lobby</strong>, not directly to office or residential floors. From the lobby another elevator or stair is used, with screening, access control and surveillance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Surveillance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cameras in areas of constant light, by day or with luminaires.</li>\n<li>Low-light cameras: possible, but more expensive and they tacitly admit that the lighting may be poor.</li>\n<li>Cameras with an unobstructed view and out of reach. On surface lots, good sightlines and maximum coverage.</li>\n<li>Protected in dark polycarbonate domes: they resist vandalism and hide where they are looking.</li>\n<li>Video monitored in real time and recorded digitally to replay and enhance images.</li>\n<li>Color cameras: better identify vehicles and people; useful as evidence.</li>\n<li>Panic buttons (call boxes) integrated with the video: when pressed, a camera is activated.</li>\n<li>Video integrated with access control: captures license plates on entry or exit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lighting</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Without good light, video surveillance and natural surveillance are weakened.</li>\n<li>The book says that \"at least 175 yards of lighting\" suffice to detect human movement. Exam note: the sentence is confusing (yards is not a unit of illumination); it appears to be an erratum.</li>\n<li>Reference: <em>Guideline for Security Lighting for People, Property, and Critical Infrastructure</em> (Illuminating Engineering Society of North America, IESNA, 2017).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Recommended level</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gathering areas: stairs, elevators, ramps</td>\n<td>5–6 fc (foot-candles) = 54–65 lux (lm/m²)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Walkways around garages</td>\n<td>5 fc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open parking lots (also in commercial zones, hotels, motels and apartment buildings)</td>\n<td>Minimum 3 fc (32 lux)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Entrances</td>\n<td>10 fc (108 lux) or twice the level of the surroundings, so that they stand out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Perimeter fences</td>\n<td>At least half of the average horizontal illumination on both sides of the fence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paths to garages</td>\n<td>3 fc (32 lux), to see a person at 30 ft (9 m) or more; average/minimum ratio no greater than 4:1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Luminaire height</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical poles of 30–45 ft (9–14 m)</td>\n<td>Wide band of light, but deep shadows between cars</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pedestrian-level light at 12–14 ft (3.6–4.2 m)</td>\n<td>Passes through car windows and reflects off them; reduces shadows and dark areas</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Ideal in an open parking lot: combine high and low light for more coverage and fewer shadows and hiding places.</li>\n<li>Garage interior painted in light colors to reflect more light.</li>\n<li>Luminaires with polycarbonate lenses against vandalism and breakage.</li>\n<li>Maintenance protocol: repair damaged lights and replace burned-out bulbs promptly; replace those that work according to their service life.</li>\n<li><strong>Fort Lauderdale (Florida) case:</strong> a garage painted white circles on the ceiling to reflect light. Its ceilings were higher than average, which improved the distribution.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Light source</th>\n<th>Service life</th>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Metal halide</td>\n<td>About 20,000 h</td>\n<td>Preferred by most CPTED practitioners; renders the color of cars, clothing and people well</td>\n<td>More expensive than high-pressure sodium and mercury vapor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low-pressure sodium vapor</td>\n<td>About 50,000 h</td>\n<td>The most energy-efficient</td>\n<td>Poor color rendition; not suitable for capturing crime scene details</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-pressure sodium vapor</td>\n<td>Shorter than metal halide</td>\n<td>Cheaper than metal halide</td>\n<td>Lasts less and renders color worse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mercury vapor</td>\n<td>Shorter than metal halide</td>\n<td>Cheaper than metal halide</td>\n<td>Lasts less and renders color worse</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>There is no single answer. CPTED allows for diversity of lighting according to the risk and threat assessment and the desired user experience.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Signage</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Well lit, with letters or symbols <strong>at least 8 in (20 cm)</strong> high.</li>\n<li>Wall signs for pedestrians and vehicles: graphic whenever possible, for universal comprehension and clear direction.</li>\n<li>Graffiti is illegitimate signage: it usually marks the territory of gangs or vandals. Remove it as soon as possible.</li>\n<li>Prevention: anti-graffiti epoxy paint on walls and more light in problem areas to increase natural surveillance.</li>\n<li>Trying to prevent graffiti communicates that the property is the territory of its legitimate owner.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mixed Uses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trend: integrating parking into mixed-use developments. More legitimate users and more \"eyes on the street\" reinforce territoriality.</li>\n<li>Many garages add shops: copy centers, fast food or car washes, compatible activities that attract legitimate users.</li>\n<li>By day, spaces reserved for businesses; at night, flat rate for clubs, restaurants and neighbors with a night permit.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>10.3.4 Schools</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Challenge: large population, many entrances and many windows on the ground floor.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditions that generate problems:</strong> (1) poorly defined campus boundaries; (2) informal gathering areas out of sight; (3) layout with isolated points (Figure 10.6); (4) bus loading that conflicts with cars; (5) student parking as far as possible from the building; (6) students parked on the street, in conflict with the neighborhood; (7) parking lots hidden by plants; (8) lockers that create confusion and help hide contraband; (9) excessive use of hallways with blind spots; (10) restrooms far from supervision.</li>\n<li><strong>Figure 10.6 (Layouts to Avoid):</strong> avoid \"crime generators\": remote, little-used or unobserved areas.</li>\n<li>CPTED contributes with natural access control, surveillance, territoriality, boundary definition, management and maintenance, <strong>without turning the school into a fortress</strong>. Technology complements.</li>\n<li><strong>Focus areas:</strong><ul>\n<li><em>Site:</em> landscaping, perimeter boundary, exterior pedestrian routes, vehicle routes and parking, recreation areas.</li>\n<li><em>Building design:</em> building organization, covered exterior walkways, entry points, enclosed exterior spaces, auxiliary buildings, walls, windows, doors, roofs, lighting.</li>\n<li><em>Interior spaces:</em> lobby and reception, hallways, restrooms, stairs, cafeterias, auditoriums, gymnasiums, libraries and media centers, classrooms, locker rooms, laboratories, workshops, music rooms, computer rooms, administrative areas.</li>\n<li><em>Systems and equipment:</em> alarms and surveillance, fire control, HVAC (heating, ventilation and air conditioning) and mechanical equipment, vending machines, drinking fountains, elevators, telephony, information systems.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The school communicates its relationship to the surroundings through its <strong>edges</strong>. Landscaping marks boundaries and can restrict access. Fences and gates must allow seeing through; plants must not create hiding places.</li>\n<li>Administrative offices with a clear view of play areas, gathering areas and parking: raises the risk of identification and intervention for the intruder. Allow seeing toward and from courtyards, classrooms and high-risk areas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Design recommendations for schools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Observation from classrooms:</strong> parking and circulation in view of the classrooms (many students, more casual observation).</li>\n<li><strong>Observation of vehicular traffic:</strong> offices with a line of sight to entry roads and parking lots. No one should enter without being detected.</li>\n<li><strong>Surveillance points:</strong> view of problem areas from spaces of public use, such as a common stairway.</li>\n<li><strong>Exterior circulation:</strong> wide paths that do not serve as an informal gathering place; bicycle racks in a highly visible area.</li>\n<li><strong>Traffic calming:</strong> parking lots with few or no long straight stretches; speed and stop signs; bus without conflict with other traffic.</li>\n<li><strong>Signage:</strong> announce intended and prohibited uses; clear, reasonably sized and easy to see.</li>\n<li><strong>Spatial/temporal issues:</strong> safe activities in unsafe places (hallways next to offices; adult education at night). Separate cafeteria entrance and exit. Different lunch times for older and younger students. Driving classes in the parking lot.</li>\n<li><strong>Lighting:</strong> dusk-to-dawn light on the grounds.</li>\n<li><strong>Covered circulation:</strong> minimize blind spots and entrapment points; eliminate \"door in the face\" incidents.</li>\n<li><strong>Main entry:</strong> obvious and with few entrances; planned access to other areas; little confusion. Weapons detectors can be integrated.</li>\n<li><strong>Recessed entries:</strong> avoid blind spots; chamfer corners to see around the turn and avoid ambushes.</li>\n<li><strong>Doors and doorways:</strong> poorly designed recessed doors create blind spots. Institutional-grade doors and frames. Faceplates over locks against jimmying.</li>\n<li><strong>Courtyards and gathering places:</strong> formal areas that are defined, lit and observed. If the courts attract problems at night, remove nets and hoops at the end of the day. If the gym is used after hours, be able to isolate the rest of the building.</li>\n<li><strong>Walls:</strong> no hiding places; graffiti- and vandal-resistant materials; landscaping can protect them. <strong>Perimeter wall: at least 7 ft (2.1 m) with barbed wire on top.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Windows:</strong> several small windows give the same as one large one with more security. High windows (clerestory) give light and air without easy access. Glass block wall with clerestory: few openings, security and light. All with self-engaging locks.</li>\n<li><strong>Video surveillance:</strong> continuous operation and archived recordings.</li>\n<li><strong>Duress alarms:</strong> in isolated areas such as restrooms and lockers; integrated and with top response priority.</li>\n<li><strong>Computers/Laptops:</strong> secure them one by one and check them regularly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>10.3.5 Automated Teller Machines, ATMs</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are \"where the easy money is\": they are often isolated and the user carries or deposits cash. CPTED reduces assault, robbery and homicide of customers.</li>\n<li><strong>1. Adequate lighting:</strong> the user sees suspects and witnesses (including the police) see the crime. Light at the building corners next to the ATM and in the nearby parking lot, without shadows or hiding places.<ul>\n<li><strong>The U.S. has no national standards for bank lighting</strong>; some cities and states do.</li>\n<li>Typical minimums, measured at 3 ft (1 m) from the ground: <strong>25 fc (269 lux) at the front of the ATM; 10 fc (107 lux) within 5 ft (1.5 m); 2 fc (22 lux) at 50–60 ft (15–18 m).</strong></li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> here 10 fc = 107 lux and in parking lots 108 lux. It is rounding; the figure is 10 fc.</li>\n<li>The minimums do not cover everything: shadows, type, color, direction and uniformity of the light, glare and obstacles. A lot of light at the front and little around prevents seeing dangers.</li>\n<li>Photosensors that turn on at dusk; monitor levels; long-life bulbs; automatic monitors that alert the operator; protected luminaires.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>2. Visible landscaping:</strong> prune routinely; <strong>the best is to have no shrubs</strong>; if there are any, slow-growing. Remove containers, benches or walls that block the view.</li>\n<li><strong>3. Mirrors:</strong> rearview mirrors at the ATM and at nearby corners.</li>\n<li><strong>4. Where natural surveillance abounds:</strong> in view of pedestrians, drivers, patrols and cameras. The opportunist avoids open sites, and help for the victim increases. Vestibules with large vision panels and duress alarms with real response. Protect the person servicing the machine (alarms, secure rooms). In stores: no plants or blinds, visible from the street. Against attacks on the machine: alarms, impact and seismic sensors, weight and resistance to pulling, heat detectors and locking mechanisms.</li>\n<li><strong>5. At police stations:</strong> if the extra traffic does not fit, only at night, when the risk of robbery is higher. Also at post offices or fire stations.</li>\n<li><strong>6. High-risk sites:</strong> review local crime rates. Not in drug trafficking areas, next to abandoned buildings or problem bars. If needed: move, close or limit hours.</li>\n<li><strong>7. Cameras:</strong> two purposes, deterring robbery and fraud and identifying the offender. Record the close-up of the user and what is behind. Visible ones deter more but suffer vandalism. <strong>Dummy cameras only if there are also real ones</strong>; otherwise, false security and a negligence lawsuit for the \"illusion of security\". One operator used off-camera heat sensors that trigger a voice message or silent alarm.</li>\n<li><strong>8. Calling for help during the robbery:</strong> panic button (risk of false alarms); telephone next to the ATM; monitored open microphone; alarm if the vestibule door is left open too long; Reverse PIN (PIN, personal identification number, entered backwards or with an extra digit triggers a silent alarm; expensive and hard under stress, which is why it is rarely installed).</li>\n<li><strong>9. Private guards:</strong> only at high-risk ATMs or patrolling several at random. It is expensive.</li>\n<li><strong>10. Prohibit loitering and aggressive panhandling:</strong> laws give the police authority to move opportunists away.</li>\n<li><strong>11. Locked vestibules:</strong> they can help, but the customer can be trapped with the offender; people hold the door for others; they attract homeless people.</li>\n<li><strong>12. Daily withdrawal limits:</strong> reduce the loss and discourage some; but most street robbers do not expect much money and take the risk anyway.</li>\n<li><strong>13. Training users:</strong> put the money away discreetly before leaving; check whether they are being watched; go accompanied at night; look inside the vestibule before entering; keep others from seeing their PIN over the shoulder; watch the parking lot (loiterers, containers, cars).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>10.3.6 U.S. Federal Buildings</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1995: bombing of the Alfred P. Murrah Federal Building (Oklahoma City).</strong> It generated security standards for all federal facilities; several states did the same.</li>\n<li>The standards of the <strong>GSA (General Services Administration)</strong> promote the defensible space/CPTED approach: edges that define circulation and filters between legitimate and illegitimate users.</li>\n<li>They cover: security glazing, blast-resistant design and construction, landscaping, site lighting and natural and mechanical surveillance.</li>\n<li>They vary by level: a <strong>Level 1</strong> may not require entry control; a <strong>Level 4</strong> may require electronic controls and video.</li>\n<li>Context (outside the book): the 1995 Department of Justice report defined five levels (I–V); today the federal standards are set by the ISC (Interagency Security Committee).</li>\n<li><strong>Four GSA categories:</strong><ul>\n<li><em>Perimeter and exterior security:</em> parking area and controls; video monitoring; lighting with emergency backup; physical barriers.</li>\n<li><em>Entry security:</em> intrusion detection system; upgrade to life safety standards; screening of mail, people and packages; entry control with video and electric door strikes; high security locks.</li>\n<li><em>Interior security:</em> employee credentials and visitor control; control of access to utilities; emergency power for critical systems; location of day care centers.</li>\n<li><em>Security planning:</em> locate tenants according to their security needs; blast standards.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>These criteria balance cost-effectiveness, acceptance of some risk and public access to the buildings.</li>\n<li><strong>Measures against bomb attacks:</strong><ul>\n<li>Secure perimeter as far out as possible: <strong>setback of 100 ft (30 m)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Concrete barriers disguised as planters or works of art, next to the sidewalk and far from the building, <strong>spaced less than 4 ft (1.2 m) apart</strong> to stop vehicles from passing.</li>\n<li>New buildings with a simple rectangular plan: less diffraction of the blast wave that bounces off U- or L-shaped buildings.</li>\n<li>Reduce ornaments that can come loose; lightweight exterior cladding.</li>\n<li>Eliminate hiding places; clear views and building in view of other occupied facilities.</li>\n<li>Eliminate vehicle approaches perpendicular to the building; minimize vehicle accesses.</li>\n<li>Eliminate or strictly control parking under the building; parking as far away as possible and in view of occupied rooms.</li>\n<li>Light the building exterior.</li>\n<li>Secure access to power or heating plants, gas pipes, water, and electric and telephone service.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-10-e3\">10.4 Integration of CPTED and Traditional Security</h3><ul>\n<li>Only in rare cases is CPTED enough by itself to manage risk.</li>\n<li>Within a comprehensive asset protection strategy it is an excellent, cost-effective complement to typical physical security. A single tool rarely solves everything.</li>\n<li><strong>How it can reduce costs:</strong> (1) reduce or reassign guards; (2) reduce the necessary reach of the video system; (3) natural access control that replaces some structural barriers; (4) lower insurance premiums.</li>\n<li>It also improves life safety: better evacuation routes and fewer incidental crimes against employees, residents and users.</li>\n<li>In a \"big picture\" program, protection objectives are better met, normally at lower cost or with the cost shared among departments or cost centers.</li>\n<li>Future threats: street crime and workplace violence will continue; sabotage and terrorism against critical infrastructure may be added.</li>\n<li>CPTED may not stop the most determined terrorist. But terrorism often begins with intrusion and unauthorized access to study vulnerabilities.</li>\n<li>If the target is not easy to access or has good security, the attacker may look for a more vulnerable one.</li>\n<li>Conclusion: CPTED is a legitimate strategy for reducing the opportunity for terrorism and ordinary crime.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-10-e4\">Key terms</h3><p>CPTED · Defensible Space · built environment · territoriality · natural surveillance · natural access control · natural territorial reinforcement · management and maintenance · legitimate activity support · broken windows theory · capable guardian · routine activity theory · situational crime prevention · repeat victimization · piggybacking · target hardening · mechanical / organizational / natural measures · first / second / third generation CPTED · cohesion · capacity threshold (tipping point) · community culture · connectivity · CPTED 3-D (designation, definition, design) · 4 Ds (deter, detect, delay, deny) · programming · schematic design · design development · construction documents · architectural program · security zoning · unrestricted / controlled / restricted zones · berm · clerestory windows · foot-candle · metal halide · low-pressure sodium · crime generators · duress alarm · illusion of security · reverse PIN · setback · GSA security levels</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"10.1 Principles of CPTED"},{"id":"e1","t":"10.2 Tools of CPTED"},{"id":"e2","t":"10.3 CPTED Applications in Various Settings"},{"id":"e3","t":"10.4 Integration of CPTED and Traditional Security"},{"id":"e4","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"10.1 Principles of CPTED","h":"the book writes \"Jeffrey\"; the criminologist's name is C. Ray Jeffery (outside the book)."},{"s":"10.1 Principles of CPTED","h":"Crowe places \"fencing\" among the mechanical measures, but \"gates\" and \"walls\" among the natural ones."},{"s":"10.1 Principles of CPTED","h":"the text announces the \"4 Ds\" (deter, detect, delay, deny), but Figure 10.1 has no \"Deny\" section. In its place it adds Response, Reporting, Discrimination and Neutralization."},{"s":"10.2 Tools of CPTED","h":"administrative offices appear in two zones. \"Certain\" offices are unrestricted; the rest are controlled."},{"s":"10.2 Tools of CPTED","h":"here the shrub limit is 32 in (81 cm) and the berm limit 2.5 ft; do not confuse with other \"2 or 3 feet\" rules from other sources (outside the book)."},{"s":"10.3 CPTED Applications in Various Settings","h":"the cleaning crew's time \"0111\" appears to be an erratum in the book. <strong>Pending:</strong> confirm the start time and the end of the \"How\" cell (cut off in the extracted text)."},{"s":"10.3 CPTED Applications in Various Settings","h":"the book converts 8 ft as 2.4 m in 10.2.3 and as 2.5 m here. The figure that counts is 8 ft."},{"s":"10.3 CPTED Applications in Various Settings","h":"here 10 fc = 107 lux and in parking lots 108 lux. It is rounding; the figure is 10 fc."}]},"physical-11":{"html":"<ul>\n<li>Sections: 11.1 Access Control Systems · 11.2 Contraband Detection · 11.3 Physical Intrusion Detection Systems · 11.4 Video Surveillance · 11.5 Communications and Annunciation Systems · 11.6 Trends and Issues in Electronic Systems Integration.</li>\n<li>The chapter has no introduction of its own: it opens directly with 11.1.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-11-e0\">11.1 Access Control Systems</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Electronic access control system:</strong> validates one or more credentials of three types:<ul>\n<li>something you know (<em>what you know</em>): PIN, password;</li>\n<li>something you are, inherent to you (<em>what you are</em>): biometrics;</li>\n<li>something you carry (<em>what you have</em>): card, key, credential.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Components of a complete system:</strong> <em>credential reader</em>; communication wiring; <em>distributed processor</em>; central database; software; interfaces with external systems; applications for <em>request-to-exit</em> (REX) devices at the doors that require them.</li>\n<li><strong>Personnel access control:</strong> the part of the system that authorizes entry and verifies the authorization of whoever asks to enter a controlled area. It checks whether the person:<ol>\n<li>carries a valid credential;</li>\n<li>knows a valid <em>personal identification number</em> (PIN);</li>\n<li>has the unique physical trait that matches the one recorded at <em>enrollment</em>: fingerprint, hand geometry.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Key difference between methods:</strong><ul>\n<li>Except for biometrics, the devices can be used without the authorized person (they are lent or stolen).</li>\n<li>Only the match of a physical trait verifies the person's identity.</li>\n<li>A credential or a number only proves that someone has a valid credential or knows a valid number.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Combining technologies:</strong> reduces <em>throughput</em>, but makes the system harder to defeat.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry authorization methods:</strong> PIN; credentials; <em>positive personnel identity verification</em> or biometrics.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Forms of identification required</th>\n<th>Name in the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Single factor authentication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Dual factor authentication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Three factor authorization</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book calls the third level <em>three factor authorization</em>. For consistency with the other two, it is <em>authentication</em> (three-factor authentication).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.1.1 Personnel Access Control</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure for deciding whom to let through</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Credentials or objects the person carries</td>\n<td>Metal key; proximity device; chip insertion card; magnetic stripe card; photo credential (basic access control tool)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private information the person knows</td>\n<td>Password; PIN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Biometric traits (give the highest level of security)</td>\n<td>Fingerprint; hand geometry; iris and retina patterns; signature; speech patterns</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The most secure systems combine several methods to authenticate and validate.</li>\n<li>A company with several sites, even if far apart, can control them all with a single electronic system.</li>\n<li>There are <em>remotely managed access control services</em> run by third parties. They are not recommended for high security.</li>\n<li>Sites with classified material, such as SCIFs (<em>Sensitive Compartmented Information Facilities</em>):<ul>\n<li>follow the requirements of the security agency that oversees them;</li>\n<li>use only approved products and services.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other sites may use electronic <em>pushbutton locks</em>: no central administration or connection, with minimal protection.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Means of Defeating Access Control Points</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attack</th>\n<th>What it consists of</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Deceit</em></td>\n<td>False pretexts to convince the guard or an employee to let someone through. <em>Social engineering</em> is a real threat. Training staff in the security plan helps, though it is not infallible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Direct physical attack</em></td>\n<td>Force and tools to get in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Technical attack</em></td>\n<td>Forging a credential; guessing or obtaining passcodes or PINs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Objectives of a <em>comprehensive access control system</em></h5>\n<ol>\n<li>Let in and out only authorized people and vehicles.</li>\n<li>Allow auditing of compliance with access control policies.</li>\n<li>Detect and prevent the entry of contraband.</li>\n<li>Detect and prevent unauthorized removal of assets.</li>\n<li>Provide information to assess and respond in time to exceptions and alarms.</li>\n</ol>\n<h5>Key design questions</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Will it be integrated with alarms, video surveillance, building automation and elevators?<ul>\n<li>Almost all products allow it: some out of the box, others with extra software modules.</li>\n<li>Review the product's limitations and licenses carefully.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>What <em>throughput</em> is wanted at each controlled point?</li>\n<li>Are entries and exits of people and vehicles recorded? If so, how are authorizations, rejections and exceptions coded for later investigation?</li>\n<li>Once integrated, does each component meet building and fire codes?</li>\n<li>Who maintains the database? Will it be integrated with the employee information system? This is a major concern for Human Resources.</li>\n<li>Balancing access control and <em>life safety</em> creates challenges. Codes and life safety almost always prevail.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Personal Identification Number (PIN)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>How it works:</strong> the user types a memorized number on a <em>keypad</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Variant with credential:</strong> the coded credential locates the reference file for that badge number in the database. Then the person types the PIN. If it matches the stored one, the person enters.</li>\n<li>The PIN is chosen by the person or assigned. Usual length: 4 to 6 digits.</li>\n<li><strong>Weaknesses:</strong><ol>\n<li>The PIN and the credential can be passed to someone unauthorized.</li>\n<li>An adversary can see it on the sly, looking over the shoulder (<em>shoulder surfing</em>).</li>\n<li>It can be obtained under duress.</li>\n<li>In addition, people tend to write it down.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Against observation:</strong> <em>scrambler-type PIN reader</em>.<ul>\n<li>The digits change places on each use.</li>\n<li>It can only be seen from a very narrow angle.</li>\n<li>It is usually paired with credential readers, such as proximity readers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Two criteria for a secure PIN:</strong><ol>\n<li>Enough digits so that it cannot be guessed. This is key if the PIN is the only entry criterion.</li>\n<li>That it have no special meaning for its holder.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Numerical example:</strong> for a few hundred people, 4 digits are enough. They give 10,000 combinations, many more than people; guessing is unlikely.</li>\n<li><strong>Numbers to avoid if the person chooses it:</strong> birth dates; parts of the social security number; telephone numbers; the year; easy sequences such as 1-1-1-1 or 1-2-3-4.</li>\n<li><strong>Duress code:</strong> several integrators offer a panic code:<ul>\n<li>swapping the last two digits of the PIN; or</li>\n<li>typing the PIN backwards.</li>\n<li>Requirement: the last two digits (when swapping) or all four (when reversing) must be different. Otherwise, the panic code would be the same as the normal PIN.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Maximum attempts:</strong> some systems, after several failed attempts, cancel the credential or send an alarm to the central station.</li>\n<li>Combining PIN with credential and biometrics raises security.</li>\n<li><strong>Scenarios the software allows, by time of day and day of the week:</strong><ul>\n<li>credential without PIN;</li>\n<li>PIN without credential;</li>\n<li>PIN and credential together.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Credentials</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of credential</th>\n<th>How it is checked</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Photo identification badge</em></td>\n<td>By hand, by a very attentive guard</td>\n<td>The most practical and accepted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Exchange badge</em></td>\n<td>By hand, by a very attentive guard</td>\n<td>The inside badge never leaves the area</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Stored-image badge</em></td>\n<td>By hand, by a very attentive guard</td>\n<td>Compares against a stored image</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Coded credential</em></td>\n<td>Automatically</td>\n<td>Basis of electronic systems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Photo identification badge</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Common, but not always effective:<ul>\n<li>it can be forged;</li>\n<li>someone can use makeup to look like the photo on a stolen badge;</li>\n<li>the check is manual and the guard gets distracted, especially when many people are entering.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Modern printers curb forgery with <em>overlays</em>, holograms and <em>dye sublimation</em> (the photo is printed directly on the card).</li>\n<li>Many access control packages include a photo module: enrollment, photo capture and issuance in a single step.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Printer function</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Bulk card printing</em></td>\n<td>After photographing many people, it prints unattended for later distribution. Options such as alphabetical order make distribution easier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Dye sublimation printing</em></td>\n<td>Prints directly on the badge. May require lamination against UV (ultraviolet) rays and scratches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Overlays</em></td>\n<td>Can be customized, with holograms or other elements that are hard to copy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>On-board encoding</em></td>\n<td>Some print bar codes and encode magnetic stripe and smart card</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The software must be synchronized: a single integrated credential database.</li>\n<li><strong>Control against the <em>insider threat</em>:</strong><ul>\n<li>Keep blank cards and those already encoded locked in a secure container.</li>\n<li>Whoever makes the card requests the blank material from a second person, preferably a supervisor.</li>\n<li>Reason: an intruder with a valid card is very hard to stop.</li>\n<li>Protect the encoding information in the same way. If it leaks, the adversary can clone a credential in use, with the same access.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Update photos every 5 to 10 years:</strong> reprinting costs money, but people change a lot in a decade.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exchange badge</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>At each control point a twin badge of each employee is kept.</li>\n<li>The guard compares the person with the photo on the twin. If they match, he exchanges badges and lets the person through.</li>\n<li>The twin can carry more information and be a different color.</li>\n<li>The employee's stays at the control point until he leaves; then they are swapped back.</li>\n<li><strong>Advantage:</strong> the badge used inside the secure area never leaves it. Reduces forgery, loss and theft.</li>\n<li><strong>Limit:</strong> it does not prevent someone from using makeup to look like the photo on a stolen badge.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Stored-image badge (stored image or <em>video comparator</em>)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The guard compares a securely stored image with the real-time image of whoever asks to enter.</li>\n<li><strong>Its two most important features:</strong><ul>\n<li><em>Enrollment capability</em>: maximum number of images the system stores.</li>\n<li><em>Access time</em>: from when the identification number is entered until the image appears.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The image is retrieved with a coded credential or keypad: by hand or by passing the credential through a reader at the guard post.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not <em>personnel identity verification</em></strong>: it does not measure a unique, measurable trait, like a fingerprint.</li>\n<li><strong>Advantage over the manual photo:</strong> the stored image is hard to alter. In that respect it equals the exchange badge.</li>\n<li><strong>Limit:</strong> it is still vulnerable to makeup.</li>\n<li>The function of displaying the stored image (<em>call-up</em>) comes in most software. If needed, confirm that the vendor offers it.</li>\n<li>More advanced systems show the photos on portable devices:<ul>\n<li>they read the card at a distance and request that the image be sent;</li>\n<li>they reinforce checkpoints and extend the perimeter.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Coded credential: functions the system can offer</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Record of entry authorization for each credential.</li>\n<li>Unique machine-readable identification codes.</li>\n<li>Deactivation of a person without having to recover his credential.</li>\n<li>Several levels of authorization: only certain access points or certain hours.</li>\n<li><em>Antipassback</em> on entrances and exits, such as parking lots or controlled laboratories.</li>\n<li><em>Time zone control and holiday management</em>: who goes where and when, according to time zones and local holidays.</li>\n<li>Integration with visitor management and issuance of temporary credentials.</li>\n<li>Photo software: capture, printing and link to employee data.</li>\n<li>Interface with Human Resources for payroll and other records.</li>\n<li>Contractor management and payment.</li>\n<li>Interface for <em>biometric template enrollment</em>.</li>\n<li>Alarm management: prioritization, response notes, passwords, maps and other graphical interfaces.</li>\n<li>Notification of doors held open beyond the access time or forced.</li>\n<li>Manual opening and closing of access points by authorized operators.</li>\n<li>Integration with video to assess alarms and analyze past events.</li>\n<li>Interface with <em>building management</em>.</li>\n<li>Administrator user levels to create, modify and delete records.</li>\n<li>Limits on which parts of the database and which functions each operator uses.</li>\n<li>Record of who modified or attempted to modify a file, field or database (system audit).</li>\n<li>Each use of the credential can be recorded: action, time, place and credential number, listable on request.</li>\n<li>Many coded credentials are a badge worn on the premises.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Badge Coding and Types</h5>\n<p>Most common techniques: magnetic stripe, Wiegand wire, bar code, proximity and smart card.</p>\n<p><strong>Magnetic stripe</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Widely used in credit and debit cards. A magnetic stripe on one edge stores the data; it is read when swiped or inserted in the reader.</li>\n<li><strong><em>Coercivity</em>:</strong> the material's resistance to having its data changed by a magnetic field. It is the field strength needed to change the information.</li>\n<li><strong>Unit:</strong> oersted (Oe).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Material</th>\n<th>Coercivity</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n<th>Risk of erasure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Low-coercivity</em></td>\n<td>300 Oe</td>\n<td>Credit cards</td>\n<td>Relatively easy to erase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>High-coercivity</em></td>\n<td>2,500–4,000 Oe</td>\n<td>Security credentials (the most used)</td>\n<td>Very unlikely by accident. Household magnets do not erase it; <em>rare-earth magnets</em> can alter it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Alphanumeric encoding allows including name and credential number. There is little space on a credit-card-size card: take care with the design.</li>\n<li><strong>Disadvantage:</strong> forging is relatively easy. With commercial equipment the data are decoded or duplicates are encoded.<ul>\n<li>Mitigation: proprietary, nonstandard encoding and reading.</li>\n<li>Cost: it limits connection with other equipment and the options for expansion or upgrade.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wiegand wire vs. OSDP</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wiegand:</strong> old technology that is now hardly used. Its signal format has been a de facto standard since the 1980s, and its data protocol is still widespread.</li>\n<li><strong>OSDP (<em>Open Supervised Device Protocol</em>):</strong> communication protocol for access control that improves interoperability between products.<ul>\n<li>It became an international IEC (International Electrotechnical Commission) standard and was adopted by the SIA (Security Industry Association) in 2020.</li>\n<li>It provides more security, functionality, interoperability and consistency than Wiegand.</li>\n<li><strong>Wiring:</strong> Wiegand requires 12 or more wires between door and panel (reader, electric strike, alarm contact and REX). OSDP needs only 2 wires for everything.</li>\n<li>It protects readers against hacking with AES-128 encryption (<em>Advanced Encryption Standard</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> in practice OSDP runs over RS-485 and usually uses 4 conductors (2 for data and 2 for power). The IEC standard is 60839-11-5 (2020). For the exam, stick with the book's figure: 2 wires versus 12 or more.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bar code</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Widely used in retail; sometimes on credentials. The width of the bars and spaces forms the code.</li>\n<li>An optical sensor scans it and sends the data to a decoder. It is printed on the credential and used like the magnetic stripe.</li>\n<li><strong>Disadvantage:</strong> easy to duplicate, unless it carries an opaque cover. That cover is increasingly common on security credentials.</li>\n<li><em>2-D bar codes</em> store more information than one-dimensional ones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Proximity badge</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is read without physically inserting it into the reader. It is a small transponder or radio frequency (RF) transmitter.</li>\n<li>It is classified by three criteria:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Types</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Power source</td>\n<td><strong>Active:</strong> long-life battery. Some use the battery only on entering the interrogation field; others always transmit and the antenna picks up the data as they approach. <strong>Passive:</strong> takes its energy from the reader's RF signal on entering the field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frequency range</td>\n<td><strong>Low frequency:</strong> 125 kHz range. <strong>High frequency:</strong> from 2.5 MHz to over 1 GHz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data capacity</td>\n<td><strong>Read-only:</strong> code set at the factory, does not change. <strong>Read/write:</strong> larger data field; programmed by the system administrator</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Advantages:</strong><ul>\n<li>widely used; somewhat hard to duplicate;</li>\n<li>readers with no moving parts, suitable for any climate;</li>\n<li>readers in vandal-resistant housings, protected from almost all vandalism;</li>\n<li>long-range readers exist, convenient for parking gates and vehicle entrances.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Risks and countermeasures:</strong><ul>\n<li>Long range also allows reading and copying poorly secured cards with an RFID (<em>Radio Frequency Identification</em>) tool. Countermeasure: sleeves, wallets or badge holders with RFID shielding.</li>\n<li>Readers with compromised firmware: the attacker gains access to what is stored in the reader. Countermeasure: dual factor enabled (proximity + PIN or proximity + biometrics).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><em>Facility code</em>:</strong> extra number encoded on the credential, unique to the company.<ul>\n<li>It keeps another tenant with the same credential number from entering your facility.</li>\n<li>It is read together with the credential number.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Smart card</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Credit card size, with an integrated circuit inside.</li>\n<li><strong>Contact:</strong> metal contacts on the surface that communicate with the reader.</li>\n<li><strong><em>Contactless</em>:</strong> communicates by RF; it has no metal contacts.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory-only card vs. true smart card:</strong><ul>\n<li>Memory-only: works like the magnetic stripe (number, name and other data).</li>\n<li>True: carries a microprocessor; that is what makes it \"smart\".</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Memory:</strong> from 8 KB to 64 KB, with a projection of 1 MB in the future.</li>\n<li><strong>Main advantages:</strong> lots of memory and strong resistance to forgery or compromise. Many encrypt the communication. They are valued relative to their cost.</li>\n<li><strong>When it makes sense:</strong><ul>\n<li><strong>Does not make sense</strong> (poor return on investment): large population and a security level that is not extreme.</li>\n<li><strong>Makes sense:</strong> small population in a very high security facility.</li>\n<li><strong>Makes sense:</strong> limiting access to certain areas of a large facility, such as precious metals or executive suites.</li>\n<li><strong>Can be cost-effective:</strong> if it combines security with administrative records (training, medical files or asset control).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hybrid cards</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They combine several technologies: smart card functions, high-coercivity magnetic stripe, holder photo and even bar code.</li>\n<li><strong>Limit:</strong> the space available for more encoding arrays.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mobile credentials: NFC and Bluetooth</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>NFC (<em>Near Field Communication</em>) and Bluetooth on smartphones are gaining a lot of traction as a credential.</li>\n<li>Banking apps and other trusted transactions (\"I am who I say I am\") are quickly raising the bar for this type of credential.</li>\n<li>Many smart card manufacturers are developing NFC credential and provisioning software.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.1.2 Locks</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They secure the movable parts of barriers.</li>\n<li>They should not be the only protection for important areas: with skill and time they are defeated. Always with complementary measures.</li>\n<li>They are the most widely used method for controlling physical access: homes, vehicles, offices, hotels, safes, desks, cabinets, file cabinets, briefcases, display cases and jewelry boxes.</li>\n<li>They are among the oldest security devices.</li>\n<li><strong>Two general classes:</strong><ol>\n<li>Purely mechanical.</li>\n<li>Those that combine electrical power with mechanical operation; they are associated with automated access control systems.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>They vary by physical type, application and mode of operation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Mechanical Locks</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Examples: door locks, cabinet locks and padlocks. An arrangement of physical parts prevents the bolt or latch from being opened.</li>\n<li><strong>Two main components:</strong><ul>\n<li><em>Coded mechanism</em>: key cylinder, or <em>wheel pack</em> in a combination lock.</li>\n<li><em>Fastening device</em>: latch or bolt.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th><em>Latch</em></th>\n<th><em>Bolt</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Behavior</td>\n<td>Retracts by itself when the door is closed</td>\n<td>Stays fixed until someone moves it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Convenience</td>\n<td>More convenient</td>\n<td>Less convenient</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security</td>\n<td>More vulnerable</td>\n<td>More secure, especially if it projects at least 1 in (2.5 cm) from the edge of the door</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Many locks that used to be \"mechanical\" now come with an integrated battery and credential reading. Some have wireless communication: they bridge the mechanical and the electrified.</li>\n<li><em>Disc tumbler</em> lock: born for the early automobile industry. Today it is used in doors, cabinets, padlocks and cylinders.</li>\n<li><strong>Four functional sets of a mechanical lock:</strong><ol>\n<li><em>Bolt or latch</em>: joins the movable part (door, window) to the fixed part (jamb, frame).</li>\n<li><em>Keeper or strike</em>: where the bolt enters. It is not part of the mechanism; it is its secure housing when closed.</li>\n<li><em>Tumbler array</em>: the barrier or maze that must be overcome to move the bolt.</li>\n<li>Key or unlocking device: designed to overcome the barrier and move the bolt.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Normally the bolt and the barrier are in the fixed hardware (<em>lockset</em>) and the key is separate.</li>\n<li><strong>Exception, physical logic devices:</strong> the whole lock is a single assembly. Examples: integrated digital keypad that mechanically releases the bolt; dial combination locks.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Origin</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Uses</th>\n<th>Security</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Warded lock</em></td>\n<td>The first developed and the oldest in use</td>\n<td>Open, visible keyhole; long barrel-type key</td>\n<td>Old houses, farm buildings, old inns</td>\n<td>Very simple. Weak against spring manipulation with any key the wards do not stop, and against corrosion. A modern program does not include it: replace or reinforce it in phases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Lever lock</em></td>\n<td>Lever principle perfected in the 18th century</td>\n<td>Flat metal levers on a common pivot, held by a spring. Each has a cut for the <em>fence</em>, a projection of the bolt. When aligned, the bolt retracts</td>\n<td>Desks, cabinets, lockers, bank safe-deposit boxes, U.S. mail boxes</td>\n<td>More secure than the warded lock. With 2 or more cuts per lever it admits a master key. Prone to <em>picking</em>, although there are anti-pick measures. Do not confuse with a lever handle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Pin tumbler</em></td>\n<td>19th century, Linus Yale (who also created the dial combination lock)</td>\n<td>Mechanism that moves the bolt + maze + keyway; it is distinguished in the maze. Conventional cylinder: equidistant pins in a row; 5, 6 or 7 pins</td>\n<td>Probably the most widely used in the U.S. on exterior and interior doors</td>\n<td>The most important development in the history of the mechanical lock. With recent improvements it achieves high security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Wafer tumbler</em></td>\n<td>Fairly late development</td>\n<td>Flat wafers of metal or other material lock the <em>plug</em> to the <em>shell</em></td>\n<td>Not indicated by the book. Admits <em>double-bitted keys</em></td>\n<td>Admits a master key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Dial combination lock</em></td>\n<td>Also by Linus Yale</td>\n<td>Keyless. Similar to the lever lock: aligns the <em>gates</em> of the wheels so that the fence enters. Circular, interdependent wheels: moving one moves the others</td>\n<td>Safes and cabinets</td>\n<td>Order matters: it is a true combination lock, not a permutation lock</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Pin tumbler details</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>With a master key, <em>split pins</em> are added. The number of <em>key changes</em> drops considerably.</li>\n<li><strong>Change keying system:</strong> the key that opens a single lock within a master keyed system.</li>\n<li><strong>Basic countermeasure against all manipulation:</strong> tight tolerances and more pins, discs or levers.</li>\n<li><strong>High-security cylinder:</strong><ul>\n<li>Pins and <em>drivers</em> are interlocked. Neither a pick nor a key without the exact code aligns them.</li>\n<li>The key is cut at precise angles, in addition to depths. It raises each pin to the <em>shear line</em> and at the same time rotates it.</li>\n<li>In this way the interlock passes through a special slot at the base of the plug, and the plug turns enough to move the bolt.</li>\n<li>The book calls this design \"paracentric\"; it seeks to make picking difficult.</li>\n<li><strong>Medeco variant:</strong> instead of the slot at the base of the plug, a <em>side bar</em> enters a recess in the shell or retracts into slots in the pins.</li>\n<li>In both types, keys with specific angles are very hard to copy without the manufacturer's special equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> in locksmithing, \"paracentric\" usually describes a zigzag-profile keyway, not rotating pins. Memorize the book's version.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Calculating combinations (dial lock)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Theoretical maximum = positions per wheel raised to the number of wheels. A good lock typically has 100 positions per wheel.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">combinations</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.7144em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Example from the book: 4 wheels of 100 positions give 100,000,000 possible combinations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Electronic combination locks</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They replace dial locks on safes and secure document cabinets.</li>\n<li>The user generates the power by turning the dial.</li>\n<li>The number is seen on an LCD (<em>liquid crystal display</em>), not on the dial markings.</li>\n<li>The display can only be seen from a limited angle. The number bears no relation to the dial position.</li>\n<li><strong>Functions:</strong><ul>\n<li>time limit of a certain number of seconds between each number dialed;</li>\n<li><em>two-person rule</em>: two numbers must be dialed to open;</li>\n<li>one number per user: audit trail of which combination opened it and when;</li>\n<li>memory of failed attempts.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Because of their electronic precision, they do not lose combinations to unusable adjacent numbers.</li>\n<li>They are claimed to be immune to the typical attacks on mechanical ones and to electrical and magnetic attacks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Master Keying</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Principle:</strong> a lock is opened by more than one key. Each key acts on different tumblers or on different aspects of the same tumblers.</li>\n<li><em>Reusable cores</em> provide security and an economical solution to the loss or theft of keys.</li>\n<li><strong>Access hierarchy:</strong> from a single lock, to increasingly larger groups, up to all the locks.</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> with hundreds or thousands of locks, it is not feasible for someone who needs broad access to carry one key per lock. Master groups are created where one key opens the whole group.</li>\n<li><strong>Three security difficulties</strong> (weigh them against convenience):<ol>\n<li><strong>Key control:</strong> requires very strict accountability. Losing, compromising or misusing a master key exposes the whole group. Restricted-access key cabinets and PC software help.</li>\n<li><strong>More weak points against manipulation:</strong> several shear lines or gate openings are created for opening on the sly.</li>\n<li><strong>More maintenance in cylinders:</strong> there are more parts. The master pieces, often fragile, get displaced or break; the lock jams and has to be disassembled.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Security Vulnerabilities of Mechanical Locks</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Some attacks require a great deal of skill; others are almost invited by the very look of the lock.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attack</th>\n<th>How it is done</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Attack by force</em></td>\n<td>Separating the door from the jamb; pulling the lock out of its housing and moving the bolt by hand; breaking the pins so the plug turns freely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Surreptitious attack</em></td>\n<td>Picking, raking, bumping or manipulation of design flaws with special locksmith tools. There are tutorial videos of these \"hacks\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Impression-making and try keys</em></td>\n<td><strong>Impression:</strong> a blank key of the correct profile, without cuts, is inserted and turned with pressure to leave slight marks. <strong>Try keys</strong> (or <em>jingle keys</em>): well-milled blanks for that keyway, with random cuts; they are inserted, turning and raking until the lock opens</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Rearranging Mechanical Locks</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>When it is needed:</strong> changes in authorized personnel; system compromise; loss or lack of control of keys; major changes in occupancy.</li>\n<li><strong>Ways of doing it:</strong><ol>\n<li><strong>Relocate the lock:</strong> rotate locks between doors or cabinets. Only works if nothing on the lock lets a former key holder recognize it.</li>\n<li><strong>Reorder the tumblers (most effective):</strong> new combination inside each lock.\n - Levers and wafers: disassemble and change the order; the same tumblers serve many times.\n - Pins: the same pins, in different positions.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Convertible or interchangeable core:</strong> option from some pin tumbler lock manufacturers.<ul>\n<li>Allows combinations to be redistributed very quickly.</li>\n<li>It is swapped on the spot for another core already prepared with the new scheme.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Electrified Locking Mechanisms</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>They are locked and unlocked from a remote device: electric button, motion sensor, card reader, digital keypad or biometric reader.</li>\n<li>They can be mechanical or electromagnetic.</li>\n<li><strong>Power:</strong> direct current (DC), alternating current (AC), batteries or PoE (<em>Power over Ethernet</em>). It must be compatible with the available power and with the other access control functions.</li>\n<li><strong>Boolean logic:</strong> organizes conditions into sets. Example: \"if door A and B are closed, C can be opened\". Useful in <em>mantraps</em> and other high security measures.</li>\n<li>When analyzing failures and ways of defeating a lock, also assess the vulnerability of the remote-control devices.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fail-safe, fail-secure and fail-soft</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Where it applies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fail-safe</td>\n<td>Unlocks on any failure. The typical failure is a power outage; failures of the mechanism and of the connected control also count</td>\n<td>Most (not all) of the locks on code-required exits: free exit if a blackout and a fire coincide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fail-secure</td>\n<td>Stays locked on loss of power or other failure</td>\n<td>Can be on the egress route if free exit exists whatever the state of the power or the control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fail-soft (earlier term)</td>\n<td>Reduced capacity of the system due to the failure of some element</td>\n<td>System as a whole</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Fail-safe and fail-secure usually refer to doors on the <em>path of egress</em>. They must open throughout occupancy or only on a detected fire.</li>\n<li>Fail-safe is not needed if the door already lets people out by turning the handle or pushing the bar from the secure side. Example: the electrified panic bar opens mechanically when pushed, with or without power.</li>\n<li><strong>Single action rule:</strong> codes require exit with a single action and without special knowledge.<ul>\n<li><strong>Valid:</strong> turning the handle; pushing an <em>exit device</em> or <em>panic bar</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Not valid:</strong> pressing a button, turning a key, using a reader or entering a code before turning the handle.</li>\n<li><strong>Exceptions:</strong> banks, jewelry stores and other high security environments.</li>\n<li>Fire codes vary by municipality: consult them when specifying any lock.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Primary types of electrified locks (1): deadbolt, latch, strike and mortise lock</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Advantage or use</th>\n<th>Limit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Electric deadbolt</em></td>\n<td>The oldest and simplest. A solenoid (electromagnet) moves a bolt, normally mounted in the frame, in or out of a strike plate in the door</td>\n<td>Can be fail-safe or fail-secure</td>\n<td>Not recommended on doors that must open with the fire alarm: the bolt binds if people push before power is cut. There are tapered bolts against binding; consult codes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Electric latch</em></td>\n<td>Solenoid in the frame and strike plate in the door, like the deadbolt, but with a beveled latch</td>\n<td>It does not have to be retracted to close: the spring pushes it as it passes over the strike plate</td>\n<td>The book does not indicate limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Electric strike</em></td>\n<td>Complement to any standard mechanical lock. A solenoid opens or closes the strike plate. It is not a lock: it works with one</td>\n<td>Classic use in apartment buildings, offices, stores and occupancies in general</td>\n<td>The book does not indicate limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Electric lockset</em></td>\n<td>Normal <em>mortise</em> lock, electrified to control whether the handle turns</td>\n<td>Increasingly popular in automated access control</td>\n<td>The door must be drilled and the cable run to the hinge side, through the hinge or around it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Electric strike in practice:</strong><ul>\n<li>Controls passage in one or both directions. The handle is fixed on the controlled side or sides.</li>\n<li>Entry is only remote: button or switch inside the secure area, or card reader or keypad.</li>\n<li>If the handle on the secure side turns, exit is free.</li>\n<li>If it is fixed on both sides, exit is made with the same devices as for entry.</li>\n<li>If the lock has a cylinder on one or both sides, it also opens with a key.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Electric lockset in practice:</strong><ul>\n<li><em>Storeroom function</em>: a reader controls the exterior handle, normally fixed. The interior one always turns for unobstructed exit.</li>\n<li>Some models come with a switch that activates when the interior handle is turned. It sends a request-to-exit (REX) signal.</li>\n<li>That signal shunts the magnetic contact or the local siren: no alarm sounds on a valid exit.</li>\n<li>It requires a 4-wire transfer hinge, instead of 2, for the power and the sensor.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Primary types of electrified locks (2): exit device and electromagnet</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exit device (panic bar or crash bar)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Common on egress doors of high-occupancy buildings.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mounting</th>\n<th>Features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Rim-mounted</em></td>\n<td>Requires little modification of the door</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Mortise-mounted</em></td>\n<td>The mechanism is mortised into the edge of the door, like a mortise lock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Vertical rod</em></td>\n<td>For double doors without a fixed frame or <em>mullion</em> to receive the latch. The rods enter recesses or strike plates in the header and floor. They are exposed or inside a hollow door</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Electrified, they allow controlled re-entry from a distance with a button or reader/keypad.</li>\n<li><strong><em>Delayed egress locking system</em>:</strong> compromise between life safety and asset protection.<ul>\n<li>For doors used only for fire evacuation.</li>\n<li>When the bar is pushed, a delay of 15 or 30 seconds begins; afterwards the door opens.</li>\n<li><strong>Requirements:</strong> special signage that warns of the delay; connection to the fire alarm system; no delay in a fire or other defined life safety emergency.</li>\n<li>It does not usually give time to intercept a thief. But a local alarm sounds and it is reported to a monitoring center.</li>\n<li>A camera can identify the perpetrator and what he is carrying, and record the incident.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Electromagnetic lock (or <em>maglock</em>)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Electromagnet + metal armature or strike plate. When energized, it attracts the armature and keeps the door closed.</li>\n<li><strong>Mounting:</strong> normally on the header. Also on the side: it reduces the clear width, but the door is much more secure.</li>\n<li><strong>Where it is useful:</strong> doors of architectural value and where mechanical locking must be coordinated with life safety codes. It is governed by the authority having jurisdiction (AHJ).</li>\n<li><strong>Rating:</strong> by the force to separate the armature from the electromagnet, from 500 to 2,000 lb (≈ 227–907 kg, outside the book).</li>\n<li>One per door is almost always enough; in high security several are installed. Consider the composition and materials of the door.</li>\n<li><strong>Advantages:</strong> no moving parts; less maintenance than mechanical or electromechanical ones. It works if the surfaces are clean and aligned and power is assured.</li>\n<li><strong>Sensors:</strong> the best models monitor the magnet–armature bond and the door position.<ul>\n<li>They prevent the trick of inserting a nonmetallic sheet between magnet and strike plate to weaken the bond.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Inherently fail-safe:</strong> without power, it releases the strike plate.</li>\n<li><strong>Backup power:</strong> the book recommends it in high security, provided they are not on designated egress doors or on accesses to emergency exits.</li>\n<li><strong>Combination with electric bolt or strike:</strong> area secure against unauthorized entry, with free exit in a defined emergency.</li>\n<li><strong>Functions added by some manufacturers:</strong><ul>\n<li>passive infrared (PIR) sensor that detects whoever approaches from the secure side and opens;</li>\n<li>miniature camera;</li>\n<li>piezoelectric \"embarrassment alarm\" sounder if the door is held or left open beyond the programmed time;</li>\n<li>finishes in metals and designer covers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>In delayed egress:</strong> a switch in the magnet detects the push and starts the countdown.</li>\n<li><strong>Legal requirements:</strong><ul>\n<li>strictly comply with occupancy codes;</li>\n<li>AHJ approval before activating them;</li>\n<li>fail-safe wiring and connection to the fire alarm to release.</li>\n<li>According to the book, in most jurisdictions it is a crime to put a backup battery on a maglock.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> contradiction in the book. First it recommends backup power in high security (outside egress doors). Then it says that a backup battery on maglocks is a crime almost everywhere. Safe answer: maglock on egress = fail-safe, no battery and tied to the fire alarm.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Designing Secure Locking Systems</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Despite cards and biometrics, the mechanical key lock remains the most widely used system for restricting access in businesses and institutions.</li>\n<li><strong>Why:</strong> low cost, simple operation and reliability.</li>\n<li><strong>Those advantages are lost without professional planning and management:</strong><ul>\n<li>Losing a master key can force the replacement of all the cylinders: thousands of dollars.</li>\n<li>Without control of the issue and return of keys, losses from theft or destruction can reach millions of dollars.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What a locking system controls</th>\n<th>Example from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Door or portal giving access to an area</td>\n<td>Building perimeter door</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A room</td>\n<td>Confidential records file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A smaller container</td>\n<td>File cabinet or safe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The use of equipment</td>\n<td>Cash register</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Individual assets</td>\n<td>Cable lock for portable computer equipment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Evaluate the whole area:</strong> the strength of all the barriers that give access. Door and frame, walls, windows, ceiling and floor.<ul>\n<li>Expensive lock on a hollow wooden door: it deters the amateur, but for the professional it only leaves evidence of forcing.</li>\n<li>Strong metal door: does not stop a determined intruder if the frame is weak, the wall is standard sheet rock or he can get through the drop ceiling.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Professional's initial questions:</strong><ol>\n<li>What assets must be protected (people, structure, equipment, negotiable instruments, operations, information) and how much are they worth to the organization?</li>\n<li>What threats do they face and how likely are they?</li>\n<li>Who needs access to them and how often?</li>\n<li>How much flexibility must the system have?</li>\n<li>What impact will the controls have on daily operations?</li>\n<li>How much weight do the organization's culture or image carry?</li>\n<li>Are there staff and skills to plan, design, implement, operate and maintain the system? Or will part or all of it have to be outsourced?</li>\n<li>What budget is there to implement? And, often more important, how much to operate and maintain?</li>\n<li>Is there a need to audit who went where and when for regulatory compliance?</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>The Design Plan</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>A locking system is a coordinated set of elements that support and complement each other.</li>\n<li><strong>The plan considers:</strong><ul>\n<li>different, simultaneous levels of control for enclosed spaces;</li>\n<li>that those levels will change over time;</li>\n<li>that some (or many) users will need common access in some spaces and exclusive access in others;</li>\n<li>that keys, cards or <em>tokens</em> will be lost, compromised, damaged or stolen;</li>\n<li>effective means of adapting to planned and unforeseen changes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>A locking system is designed, not just installed.</strong> Adding or removing locks \"as needed\" is not the same as a concept that withstands changes in size, occupancy or use.</li>\n<li><strong>Without planning:</strong><ul>\n<li>security degrades to mere privacy;</li>\n<li>unauthorized persons get into secured spaces;</li>\n<li>control of the devices issued is lost;</li>\n<li>the convenience of <em>status-level management</em> is sacrificed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If it is not clear who accesses what, the program <strong>is not secure</strong>. If there are no multiple levels of access, <strong>it is not convenient</strong>. In both cases it fails and is not economical: money is misapplied.</li>\n<li><strong>Convenience–security tension:</strong> more convenience usually means less security, and vice versa. It is balanced if the needs are well defined from the outset.</li>\n<li>Choose the mechanism suited to each application: conventional mechanical or electronic, with codes or physiological traits.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Lock System Planning Considerations</h5>\n<p>Study systematically before deciding on hardware or scheme:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Total number of locks.</li>\n<li>Major categories or sectors of the system.</li>\n<li>Security objectives.</li>\n<li>Size and turnover of the population.</li>\n<li>Related or supporting security subsystems.</li>\n<li>Intelligence or information needs.</li>\n<li>Criticality of asset exposure.</li>\n<li>Drawings or floor plans of the facility.</li>\n<li>Door numbering scheme.</li>\n<li>Door type (metal, wood, glass).</li>\n<li>Locked perimeter assets (utility switches, storerooms, gates, building hatches).</li>\n</ol>\n<h5>Locking Policy</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The program must be based on a written policy, especially in large organizations or those with many sites.</li>\n<li><strong>The policy must:</strong><ol>\n<li>Require a systematic approach to the use of locks for security purposes.</li>\n<li>Assign someone specific to develop the program: the security department if there is one; if not, any responsible manager.</li>\n<li>Hold accountable for compliance everyone who uses or has access to keys, locks, access devices or combinations.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>That responsibility is enforced through the chain of command. It is evaluated in the salary review, along with overall performance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Rapid Entry Systems</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>They allow emergency services to enter when no one is there to open.</li>\n<li><strong><em>Rapid entry key vault</em>:</strong> fixed, weatherproof box holding the facility's essential keys.</li>\n<li>The building alarm can monitor it to detect unauthorized opening or tampering.</li>\n<li>The key to the box is handed to the emergency services when it is installed.</li>\n<li>It usually goes at the main entrance. It is usually required by the AHJ when there are life safety requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Credential-Operated Locks</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>A card or other unique device is presented to a reader at the controlled point.</li>\n<li>The system checks the holder's identity and the time and place requested. It compares them with what is stored and opens or denies access.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Combination Locks</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Mechanical or electrical. An integrated alphanumeric keypad receives a predetermined sequence of numbers or letters.</li>\n<li>Sometimes combined with:<ul>\n<li>a key that only works after the correct sequence;</li>\n<li>a card reader;</li>\n<li>a biometric trait.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Biometric Locks</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>They verify a specific physical trait: fingerprint, hand geometry, face, iris or retina.</li>\n<li>Once the trait is verified, they open only if the point's parameters are met: authorized place and time.</li>\n<li><strong><em>Personnel identity verification</em>:</strong> corroborates the claimed identity with one or more unique biometric traits.</li>\n<li>There is commercial equipment for hand or finger geometry, handwriting, eye pattern, fingerprints, voice, face and other physical traits.</li>\n<li><strong>Every system of this type considers three factors:</strong><ol>\n<li>how unique the trait is (<em>uniqueness</em>);</li>\n<li>how much the trait varies (<em>variability</em>);</li>\n<li>how hard it is to implement the system that processes it.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>Who initiates</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Verification</em></td>\n<td>The person claims an identity</td>\n<td>Presents the trait and the equipment confirms that it matches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Recognition</em></td>\n<td>No one claims an identity</td>\n<td>The equipment tries to identify the person; if it matches the database, the person enters</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Error rates (performance indicator)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Error</th>\n<th>Other name</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Type I error</em></td>\n<td><em>False reject</em></td>\n<td>Rejecting a valid user</td>\n<td>Authorized people are denied passage; the security manager receives complaints, especially if it affects executives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Type II error</em></td>\n<td><em>False accept</em></td>\n<td>Accepting an unauthorized person</td>\n<td>Security breach; the adversary never reports it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Equal error rate (EER):</strong> crossover point where Type I and Type II errors are equal. It is obtained by combining both curves on a graph.<ul>\n<li>It is not necessarily the point at which the equipment should operate.</li>\n<li>It does not coincide with the point where both errors are lowest.</li>\n<li>It is useful for comparing biometric devices with each other.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says the curves are shown \"in a fault tree\". It is a graph of error curves, not a fault tree (outside the book).</li>\n<li><strong>Both errors cannot be minimized at the same time.</strong> The balance must be decided according to the security objectives.<ul>\n<li>Setting for few false rejects: all authorized people get in, but security is compromised.</li>\n<li>Setting for few false accepts: high security, but authorized people are sometimes denied passage.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes that \"a <em>low</em> false accept rate compromises security\". It is the other way around: a <em>high</em> false accept rate compromises it.</li>\n<li><strong>Environment:</strong> some biometric technologies do not adapt well to outdoors or harsh environments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Biometric technologies</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technology</th>\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>Key figures</th>\n<th>Security level according to the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Iris (<em>eye pattern</em>)</td>\n<td>Recognition (no PIN)</td>\n<td>Reads at 10–12 in (25–30 cm). Transaction of 4–5 s (with practice) to 15 s (novices). \\~2% cannot be enrolled</td>\n<td>No false acceptances in the lab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hand geometry (<em>hand geometry</em>)</td>\n<td>Verification (with credential or PIN)</td>\n<td>Type I and II errors &lt; 1% at Sandia</td>\n<td>Not rated; gives only the error figure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two-finger geometry (<em>two-finger geometry</em>)</td>\n<td>Same as hand geometry</td>\n<td>A single guide pin</td>\n<td>The book does not state it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Handwriting (<em>handwriting</em>)</td>\n<td>Signature verification</td>\n<td>No figures</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fingerprint (<em>fingerprint</em>)</td>\n<td>Verification</td>\n<td>Used for more than 100 years</td>\n<td>Among the most reliable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Voice (<em>voice</em>)</td>\n<td>Verification by phrase</td>\n<td>No figures</td>\n<td>Low today</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Face (<em>face</em>)</td>\n<td>Verification; potential to identify people in crowds</td>\n<td>No figures</td>\n<td>Inferior to mature technologies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Iris (<em>eye pattern</em>)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The iris is the colored part of the eye; it regulates the light that enters. A video camera captures its structure, which is unique.</li>\n<li>It operates in recognition mode: there is no need to type a PIN.</li>\n<li><strong>Advantages:</strong> no contact between the face and the reader; the eye is illuminated from outside with visible light, with no LED inside the eye.</li>\n<li><strong>Laboratory test of a prototype (Bouchier, Ahrens &amp; Wells 1996):</strong><ul>\n<li>glare from eyeglasses caused some false rejections (Type I);</li>\n<li>there were no false acceptances (Type II);</li>\n<li>later equipment added glare detection and compensation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cannot be enrolled (\\~2%):</strong> blindness or other damage to the iris, very dilated eyes, or very dark irises. They need another secure access method.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hand or finger geometry</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It characterizes the shape of the hand in three dimensions: width and length of the fingers and thickness of the hand.</li>\n<li><strong>Process:</strong><ol>\n<li>Presenting an encoded credential or typing a PIN starts the reading.</li>\n<li>The hand rests on a reflective plate (<em>platen</em>) with guide pins to align the fingers.</li>\n<li>A camera captures the hand, with a side view for thickness. With infrared light and a reflective plate, the hand appears as a silhouette.</li>\n<li>The system measures lengths and widths and creates a feature vector (<em>feature vector</em>).</li>\n<li>It compares it with the enrollment template (<em>template</em>). If they match within tolerance, the verification is positive.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The guide pins are designed for the right hand. The left hand is enrolled and read with the palm facing up.</li>\n<li><strong>Tests at Sandia National Laboratories:</strong> Type I and Type II errors below 1% (Holmes, Wright &amp; Maxwell 1991).</li>\n<li>Ruehle and Ahrens (1997) reported on its use in an operational environment.</li>\n<li><strong>Two-finger variant:</strong> measures the length and width of the index–middle pair. A single guide pin between the two: it works equally with the left or right hand.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Handwriting</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Banks have used signature verification for years, although signatures are easily forged.</li>\n<li>Automatic systems measure the dynamics of writing: displacement, speed, and acceleration.</li>\n<li>Statistics indicate that each person's signature is unique and fairly consistent.</li>\n<li>The sensors (<em>transducers</em>) are in the pen or in the tablet.</li>\n<li>Low security. Better where transactions are already signed. No significant advances for access control or credentials.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fingerprints</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A means of identifying people for more than 100 years; it remains among the most reliable.</li>\n<li>Automatic systems use image processing and pattern recognition.</li>\n<li>Most use minutia points (<em>minutia points</em>): endings and bifurcations of the ridges. Some compare the whole image.</li>\n<li>All require correct finger placement and precise analysis and comparison.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Capture method</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Advantage</th>\n<th>Drawback</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Optical</td>\n<td>Prism + solid-state camera</td>\n<td>It is the most widely used method</td>\n<td>Dry or worn fingerprints are difficult. The plate is coated to improve the optical coupling with the finger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ultrasound</td>\n<td>Scans beneath the superficial skin, where the fingerprint is not damaged</td>\n<td>Less sensitive to dry or worn fingerprints</td>\n<td>Slower: the transducer performs a raster scan (<em>raster scan</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Direct solid-state sensor</td>\n<td>Capacitive, electric-field, or thermal</td>\n<td>Cheap silicon chips: expected to appear on desktops for logging in</td>\n<td>Hardening delicate chips delayed their adoption</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Silicon sensors are gaining popularity. New materials and manufacturing methods overcome electrostatic discharge, grease, and sweat on the finger.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Voice</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A useful trait for verifying identity and suitable for automatic processing.</li>\n<li><strong>Measurements to distinguish the speaker:</strong> waveform envelope; voice pitch period; relative amplitude spectrum; resonance frequencies of the vocal tract.</li>\n<li>The system asks the person to say a predetermined word, or to repeat words or numbers that it chooses.</li>\n<li><strong>Low security today, but attractive:</strong><ul>\n<li>easy to deploy and well accepted by the public;</li>\n<li>it is enough to install it at one end of a telephone system, perhaps centralized: fewer units;</li>\n<li>almost everyone knows how to use a telephone: little training and little distrust.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Procedural problem:</strong> the voice changes with illness or stress. An alternative procedure or access must be provided.</li>\n<li>Little evidence of new developments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Face</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It uses distinctive features of the face. Almost all capture the image with a video camera; one system uses thermal imaging with an infrared camera.</li>\n<li>It extracts features and compares them with those stored. If they match within tolerance, the verification is positive.</li>\n<li>Studied for years, but commercial only recently. <strong>Two difficult problems:</strong><ol>\n<li>Variation in how the face is presented: tilt and turn of the head; with or without eyeglasses; changes in facial hair; changes in expression.</li>\n<li>Variation in lighting: day versus night; one place versus another.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Its performance is still not close to that of mature biometric technologies.</li>\n<li><strong>Attractions:</strong> no contact; potential to identify known or wanted criminals in a crowd (<em>face-in-the-crowd</em>). Useful in casinos, shopping malls, and crowded places.</li>\n<li>Test it thoroughly in the real environment before implementing it.</li>\n<li>It is improving thanks to megapixel cameras and greater computing speed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other techniques</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keystroke patterns (<em>keystroke technology</em>):</strong> developed and marketed for logging in to computers.</li>\n<li><strong>Studied but not yet in use:</strong> ear shape, gait (<em>gait</em>), fingernail bed (<em>fingernail bed</em>), and body odor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>People who cannot use biometrics</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Every technology leaves out some people at enrollment: procedures must be in place for them.<ul>\n<li>Examples: very dry or very damaged skin; signatures and voice patterns that are not repeatable.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Temporary injuries of authorized users: broken fingers or hands; eye injuries or surgery; other medical conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>HSPD-12 Implementation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HSPD-12 (<em>Homeland Security Presidential Directive 12</em>):</strong> U.S. presidential directive signed by George W. Bush in August 2004.</li>\n<li>It requires the entire federal government and its contractor agencies to use a single high-security credential.</li>\n<li><strong>Technical basis:</strong> the FIPS 201 standard (<em>Federal Information Processing Standard 201</em>). It uses a contact and contactless smart card.</li>\n<li>According to the book, its implementation is still under way.</li>\n<li><strong>Who is affected:</strong> mainly federal and contractor facilities. It may also affect private industry.<ul>\n<li>Example: someone who regularly enters those facilities with a vehicle may need a federal ID.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Oversight:</strong> GSA (<em>General Services Administration</em>) and NIST (<em>National Institute of Standards and Technology</em>). They oversee the development and testing of credentials, readers, access control systems, and issuance procedures.</li>\n<li>If a company must comply, the details are agreed case by case with the government.</li>\n<li>Be careful when searching the internet: many vendor sites come up. Some claim to comply with HSPD-12 without mentioning certification.</li>\n<li>(outside the book) The resulting credential is known as the PIV card (<em>Personal Identity Verification</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>UL Standards</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>UL (<em>Underwriters Laboratories</em>):</strong> prepares safety standards, mainly as a guide for manufacturers. It then certifies whether the equipment it receives meets them.</li>\n<li>The standards arise from broad feedback: the public, insurers, government, academia, inspection authorities, consumer organizations, and end users.</li>\n<li><strong>UL 294, 7th edition (2018):</strong> standard for access control system units.<ul>\n<li>It evaluates the construction, performance, and operation of the system.</li>\n<li>The listing evaluates its safety and that it works reliably without creating hazardous conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book presents UL as \"perhaps the best known of these\" without naming any other body beforehand. It is a leftover from an earlier edition.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Code Considerations and Governance</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Milestone</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2000</td>\n<td>The IBC (<em>International Building Code</em>), from the ICC (<em>International Code Council</em>), replaces the UBC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1997</td>\n<td>Last edition of the UBC; until then it was updated approximately every 3 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1990</td>\n<td>ADA (<em>Americans with Disabilities Act</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1927</td>\n<td>The ICBO publishes the UBC (<em>Uniform Building Code</em>): the same safe construction requirements from one city to another</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The ICC was born from the merger of three bodies:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Body</th>\n<th>Code it published</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ICBO (<em>International Council of Building Officials</em>, according to the book)</td>\n<td>Uniform Building Code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BOCA (<em>Building Officials and Code Administrators International</em>)</td>\n<td>BOCA National Building Code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SBCCI (<em>Southern Building Code Congress International</em>)</td>\n<td>Standard Building Code</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> the official name of the ICBO is <em>International Conference of Building Officials</em>. The book says \"Council.\"</li>\n<li>The IBC seeks uniform safe construction standards and the elimination of differences among the three earlier codes. It is used mainly in the U.S.</li>\n<li><strong>NFPA 101 Life Safety Code</strong> (<em>National Fire Protection Association</em>): used in the U.S. where the IBC was not adopted as the building code.</li>\n<li><strong>Canada:</strong> the NBC (<em>National Building Code</em>) and the NFC (<em>National Fire Code</em>) play a similar role. Note: here NFC is not <em>Near Field Communication</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>ADA of 1990:</strong> federal anti-discrimination law, based on the <em>Civil Rights Act</em> of 1964.<ul>\n<li>It is not a building or fire code, but it affects access control at entrance and exit doors.</li>\n<li><strong>Two ways it affects access control and life safety:</strong>\n1. If it requires the employer to accommodate an employee with a disability: all communication systems must be equally effective for that person, with auxiliary aids where needed.\n2. If the building falls under the public accommodation (<em>public accommodation</em>) requirements, the system must meet them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Greatest concern when installing access control:</strong> being able to leave the building, especially in emergencies such as a fire.</li>\n<li><strong>Means of egress (<em>means of egress</em>), three parts:</strong><ol>\n<li>Path of travel to an exit (<em>path of travel to an exit</em>).</li>\n<li>The exit itself (<em>exit</em>).</li>\n<li>Exit discharge (<em>exit discharge</em>): the path to a safe area outside.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The code also regulates:</strong><ul>\n<li>the number of exits according to the intended use, how many people can be present at one time, and where they are;</li>\n<li>special places where evacuation is difficult: hospitals, nursing homes, and prisons;</li>\n<li>specific hazards, such as industries with flammable or toxic chemicals.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Before installing:</strong> review the design for code compliance. Some jurisdictions require prior design review and later testing before the system is activated.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-11-e1\">11.2 Contraband Detection</h3><h4>11.2.1 Metal Detectors</h4>\n<p><strong>Magnetometer</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Passive</strong> device: it monitors the Earth's magnetic field and detects the changes caused by <strong>ferromagnetic</strong> materials (those attracted by a magnet).</li>\n<li>It does not detect copper, aluminum, or zinc.</li>\n<li>Many weapons are made of steel, but many others are made of composite materials: the magnetometer may not see them.</li>\n<li>“Magnetometer” is used as a synonym for any metal detector, but the traditional one differs greatly from modern active detectors.</li>\n<li><strong>It should not be used to detect contraband.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Typical use and operators</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common use: screening people who enter to prevent firearms and other weapons from getting in.</li>\n<li>For this reason the operators are usually <strong>armed</strong>: they will have to disarm anyone who tries to enter with a weapon.</li>\n<li>Normally <strong>two operators</strong>: one searches manually; the other observes and protects the searcher from an attack by the person being screened.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Active metal detectors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They generate a variable magnetic field for a short period.</li>\n<li>They detect one of two things: the change in the field when metal enters it, or the eddy currents that a pulsed field induces in the object.</li>\n<li>The magnitude of the response depends on:<ul>\n<li>Conductivity of the metal.</li>\n<li>Magnetic properties of the metal (relative permeability).</li>\n<li>Shape and size of the object.</li>\n<li>Orientation of the object within the magnetic field.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The two active methods</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Continuous wave</th>\n<th>Pulsed field</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Availability</td>\n<td>No longer sold commercially</td>\n<td>The one used today</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signal</td>\n<td>Steady-state, sinusoidal field, from 100 Hz to 25 kHz</td>\n<td>Fixed-frequency pulses, 400–500 pulses per second</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coils</td>\n<td>One transmitter on one side of the arch; balanced receivers on the opposite side; the person passes between them</td>\n<td>Several low-inductance transmitters; it does not need balanced receivers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Principle</td>\n<td>Balanced differential amplifier: with no metal there is no difference and no output; metal unbalances the receivers and generates a signal</td>\n<td>Field bursts of up to 50 microseconds, 200–400 times per second; receivers off during the pulse and on a few tens of milliseconds later; the metal retains an eddy current that is read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alarm</td>\n<td>Signal amplified and phase-verified; alarm if it exceeds the threshold</td>\n<td>Same: amplification, phase detection, and threshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extra</td>\n<td>Phase allows optimizing for ferromagnetic (high permeability) or nonferromagnetic (low) metals</td>\n<td>Same; in addition, the multiple coils reduce the effect of object orientation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In the pulsed field, the eddy current decays quickly according to the metal's resistivity, but lasts long enough to be read.</li>\n<li>Modern digital technology analyzes the signal better: it distinguishes types of metal and separates real threats from harmless objects.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book gives two different figures for the pulsed field: 400–500 pulses per second and, a few lines later, 200–400 times per second.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Very small quantities of metal (for example, gold)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Very hard to detect with a portal metal detector.</li>\n<li>With continuous wave, a higher-than-normal frequency improves detection.</li>\n<li>In all cases very high sensitivity is needed, which triggers nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>An area may be required to remove steel-toed footwear and other metal objects.</li>\n<li>Handheld detectors detect very small quantities: they are better for tiny objects.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Handheld detector: disadvantages</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It requires active participation by the guard.</li>\n<li>Time the search takes.</li>\n<li>It is intrusive because of its closeness to the body; even more so if the searcher and the person searched are of different sexes.</li>\n<li>Many sites, especially airports, provide same-sex operators.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Detector placement</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The field is not limited to the space between coils: it detects moving metal outside the arch.</li>\n<li>Nuisance alarms: moving metal objects in front or to the side, such as doors, forklifts, and carts.</li>\n<li>False alarms: electromagnetic transients, such as radio transmitters, power line fluctuations, and flickering fluorescent lights.</li>\n<li>They tolerate some stationary metal: reinforcing bar in floors and walls and other metal construction materials.</li>\n<li>Installing it right next to a steel beam is not recommended.</li>\n<li>Too much metal severely distorts the field. Two possible effects:<ul>\n<li>The detector does not operate and gives an error alarm.</li>\n<li>It keeps operating with zones of extremely low or high sensitivity: undetected threats or many nuisance alarms.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Nearby objects such as security equipment, metal trash cans, or chairs do not disable it, but they distort the field.</li>\n<li>Solution: a <strong>no-move rule</strong> for those objects near the detector after the installation tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.2.2 Package Search</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Two paths: <strong>manual</strong> search or <strong>active interrogation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Active interrogation uses a family of X-ray techniques:<ol>\n<li>Single-energy transmission X-ray.</li>\n<li>Multiple-energy X-ray.</li>\n<li>Computed tomography (CT).</li>\n<li>Backscatter X-ray.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>In general they are <strong>not safe for people</strong>. Exception: low-dose backscatter for people (see 11.2.3).</li>\n<li>Single energy: useful for finding <strong>metal objects</strong> (weapons, tools, metal components of bombs).</li>\n<li>The other techniques are useful for seeing <strong>low atomic number (low-Z)</strong> materials.</li>\n<li>Atomic number (Z): number of protons in the nucleus of the atom.</li>\n<li>Low-Z contraband: explosives, drugs, and some foods.</li>\n<li>Low-Z atoms: hydrogen, oxygen, carbon, and all elements up to aluminum.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says that aluminum has Z = 26. This is an erratum: aluminum is Z = 13 and Z = 26 is iron (outside the book).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Conventional single-energy X-rays</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They produce an image that an operator inspects.</li>\n<li>They are effective if the operator is well trained and attentive, and if the image is not cluttered.</li>\n<li>Metals attenuate X-rays heavily; low-density and low-Z materials attenuate them little.</li>\n<li>They do not penetrate the heavy materials of some shipping containers or vehicles.</li>\n<li>For large, dense containers and vehicles: high-energy or multiple-energy X-rays.</li>\n<li>Almost all low-Z development is aimed at explosives, but most of those technologies can be adjusted to look for drugs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.2.3 Explosives Detection</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>What it looks for</th>\n<th>Characteristics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bulk</strong></td>\n<td>Macroscopic, detonable quantities of explosive</td>\n<td>Targets specific threat quantities; usually uses ionizing radiation, not suitable for people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Trace</strong></td>\n<td>Particle and vapor residues left by the handling of explosives</td>\n<td>A finding leads to secondary screening</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The best protection against high-explosive bombs is to <strong>prevent them from being placed</strong>.</li>\n<li>References cited by the book: description of technologies (Yinon, 1999); survey of commercial equipment (Theisen et al., 2004); survey of standoff technologies (National Academy of Sciences, 2004).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Bulk Explosives Detection</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>It measures characteristics of bulk materials:<ul>\n<li>X-ray absorption coefficient.</li>\n<li>X-ray backscatter coefficient.</li>\n<li>Dielectric constant.</li>\n<li>Interaction with gamma rays or with neutrons.</li>\n<li>Microwave or infrared emissions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>From these are calculated: mass; density; nitrogen, carbon, and oxygen content; and effective atomic number (effective Z).</li>\n<li>None is exclusive to explosives, but they indicate a high probability that one is present.</li>\n<li>Few everyday objects share those characteristics.</li>\n<li>Automated detection is effective if the equipment is:<ul>\n<li><strong>Sensitive</strong> enough: the minimum detectable quantity is less than the threat mass.</li>\n<li><strong>Specific</strong> enough: low rate of nuisance alarms.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Advantages of automating: lower labor cost and less dependence on a human interpreting the image.</li>\n<li>X-rays range from portable package equipment to systems that scan a truck with its cargo.</li>\n<li>Dual purpose: the same scan looks for weapons or contraband and, at the same time, explosives.</li>\n<li>Single energy is not enough for explosives: more information must be extracted.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technology</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Data, advantages, and disadvantages</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dual-energy X-ray</td>\n<td>Measures the mass absorption coefficient and approximates the effective Z</td>\n<td>Colors the low-Z areas (such as explosives) to draw the operator's attention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backscatter X-ray</td>\n<td>Low-Z materials send a lot of energy back toward the source</td>\n<td>Low-Z areas look bright; at low dose it is suitable for people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Computed tomography (CT)</td>\n<td>Source and detectors mounted on a rotating gantry; the computer reconstructs the contents in 3D</td>\n<td>Automated; detects small threat masses; <strong>the only X-ray technique that calculates mass, density, and mass absorption coefficient</strong>. Drawbacks: much more expensive to buy and maintain (heavy gantry); nuisance alarms of up to 20% against traces, mainly from foods and some polymers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-energy X-rays (vehicles and containers)</td>\n<td>Highly penetrating illumination</td>\n<td>Gives a reasonable image through the engine or the full trailer of a truck; large, fixed equipment, sometimes with its own building; it does not matter how the high energy is produced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gamma rays with radioactive source</td>\n<td>Radioactive source instead of an X-ray tube</td>\n<td>Same use; backscatter can be combined with high energy to detect low-Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thermal neutron activation (TNA)</td>\n<td>A slow neutron is absorbed by nitrogen-14, which becomes excited nitrogen-15 and emits a gamma ray of specific wavelength</td>\n<td>Measures <strong>nitrogen</strong>; many explosives are rich in nitrogen; automatic detection; very penetrating neutrons and gamma rays, suitable for large, dense objects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pulsed fast neutron absorption (PFNA)</td>\n<td>“Fast” = high energy, several MeV</td>\n<td>Measures <strong>carbon and oxygen</strong>; together with TNA, also nitrogen; carbon + nitrogen + oxygen identify better and reject nuisance materials rich in nitrogen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quadrupole resonance (QR)</td>\n<td>Low-energy radio pulses</td>\n<td>Detects materials rich in nitrogen; very sensitive for some explosives; thin metal shielding blocks it, but it detects the shielding and gives notice; compact, about USD 100,000, no ionizing radiation; handheld versions under development for screening people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raman analysis</td>\n<td>Spectroscopy: laser interrogation and analysis of the spectrum of the scattered light</td>\n<td>Portable, lightweight equipment for hazardous materials, including explosives; the laser passes through some containers (glass) or goes directly to the surface; requires small but visible quantities; useful for bottles or plastic bags, <strong>not</strong> for packages</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>TNA and PFNA: disadvantages</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cost: for vehicles, from USD 500,000 (TNA is cheaper).</li>\n<li>Size.</li>\n<li>Throughput.</li>\n<li>Use of radioactive material in the source or in the neutron generator tube.</li>\n<li>Small TNA systems exist for packages: less than 100 lb (45 kg).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Food irradiation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>International law prohibits irradiating food with more than 10 MeV, because of the risk of making it radioactive.</li>\n<li>There is a risk if a system of more than 10 MeV screens food shipments.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes “milli-electron volts (MeV).” MeV means mega-electron volts, that is, millions of electron volts (outside the book).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Low-dose backscatter for people</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It gives an image of the body under the clothing to find hidden objects.</li>\n<li>The person is scanned <strong>twice</strong>: front and back, to ensure that no explosives are hidden.</li>\n<li>Dose per person: about <strong>10 microrem</strong>.</li>\n<li>It meets the limit of the NRC (Nuclear Regulatory Commission, the U.S. nuclear regulatory body): maximum <strong>100 millirem/year</strong> (10 CFR Part 20, Section 20.1301(a)(1), 1991).</li>\n<li>Exposure must always be as low as reasonably achievable (ALARA, as low as reasonably achievable) (10 CFR Part 20, Section 20.1301(d)(3), 1991).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Standoff detection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In high demand: it makes it possible to detect devices from a safe distance.</li>\n<li>A lot of research and few commercial products; it is a difficult challenge.</li>\n<li>Options:<ul>\n<li>Infrared cameras: images of people to see hidden objects that could be explosives.</li>\n<li>Passive and active millimeter-wave, of about 100 GHz (sometimes called terahertz, THz): like infrared, but in another part of the spectrum.</li>\n<li>Laser methods that look for characteristic fluorescence or atomic emission (under development).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Verify what the vendor promises against the defined threat and in the real environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Combining technologies</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All bulk technologies have strengths and weaknesses.</li>\n<li>A good system combines two or more: with enough information it is possible to truly determine whether an explosive is present.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Trace Explosives Detection</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>It became common at checkpoints in the last decade.</li>\n<li>It looks for vapors and microscopic particles associated with explosives and their handling.</li>\n<li>Technologies:<ol>\n<li>Ion mobility spectrometry (IMS).</li>\n<li>Colorimetric.</li>\n<li>Chemiluminescence.</li>\n<li>Mass spectrometry.</li>\n<li>Fluorescence.</li>\n<li>Canine olfaction.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Key metrics:<ul>\n<li><strong>Limit of detection:</strong> the smallest detectable quantity.</li>\n<li><strong>Selectivity:</strong> ability to distinguish one material from another.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Many detect <strong>less than a nanogram</strong>. Even so, verify what the vendor promises before buying.</li>\n<li>A real trace detection leads to a search for the materials in the threat definition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sampling</strong>: the residue must be collected and taken to the detector.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it is done</th>\n<th>Best for</th>\n<th>Invasiveness</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Swipe sampling</td>\n<td>A cloth swab is rubbed over the person, package, or vehicle; it is heated and the vapor passes to the detector</td>\n<td>Particles on hard surfaces; it is the most efficient method and collects the most mass</td>\n<td>More invasive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vapor sampling</td>\n<td>The air next to the object is drawn in, often by agitating it</td>\n<td>Inside of containers and soft surfaces</td>\n<td>Less invasive: it does not touch the object</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>The challenge of detecting vapor</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Several common high explosives have very low vapor concentrations: parts per billion (ppb) or per trillion (ppt).</li>\n<li>They are even lower if the explosive is in a gel or oily solvent (example: RDX in the plastic explosive C-4).</li>\n<li>The molecules stick (adsorb) to almost everything at room temperature and decompose with moderate heat or a lot of energy.</li>\n<li>Result: transporting and collecting the vapor involves a large loss of sample.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trace technologies</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technology</th>\n<th>Principle</th>\n<th>Figures</th>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>IMS (ion mobility spectrometer)</td>\n<td>Negative ionization with a radioactive nickel-63 (Ni-63) source and chloride dopant; a shutter lets ions into the drift cell; they advance through an electric gradient against an inert gas flow; light ones arrive first; they strike a Faraday plate that records voltage against drift time</td>\n<td>Shutter every \\~20 ms; \\~5 cm cell with 200 V/cm; drift times of 5 to 15 ms; nanogram sensitivity to dynamite, military TNT, and plastics; USD 40,000 bench-top and USD 25,000 handheld</td>\n<td>Sensitivity, easy to operate, robust, little maintenance; sufficient specificity; few nuisance alarms in the field, except for fragrances of lotions and perfumes</td>\n<td>Handhelds are cheaper, but require more maintenance and alternating current (AC) to operate for more than a few hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Colorimetric</td>\n<td>Kit with spray, reactive paper, or an ampoule that is consumed; a chemical reaction changes the color; often several solutions in sequence to find out which explosive it is</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Low cost and portability</td>\n<td>Many nuisance alarms, consumable chemicals must be disposed of, odor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chemiluminescence</td>\n<td>Photochemical detection: a fast gas chromatograph separates the sample; heat breaks nitrogen compounds down into nitric oxide (NO); NO + ozone gives excited nitrogen dioxide (NO2), which emits a photon read by a phototube</td>\n<td>Identifies several explosives in a sample in less than 1 minute; <strong>picogram</strong> sensitivity, even RDX and PETN (very low vapor pressure)</td>\n<td>Excellent sensitivity; without the chromatography one would only know that nitrogen is present, with it the nitrated explosive is identified</td>\n<td>The most expensive of the commercial ones, the longest analysis, and more maintenance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GC/ECD (gas chromatograph / electron capture detector)</td>\n<td>The ECD exploits the high electron affinity of nitro compounds; alone it does not identify the explosive; coupled to the GC, it does</td>\n<td>Used more in the laboratory than at checkpoints</td>\n<td>Low cost, good specificity, low detection limits</td>\n<td>Long analyses (typically minutes) and frequent maintenance of the GC column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mass spectrometry</td>\n<td>Magnetic and electric fields determine the mass/charge ratio of ions</td>\n<td>“Gold standard” of the analytical chemistry laboratory</td>\n<td>Specificity and low detection limits; easily reprogrammed for new substances, useful against changing threats</td>\n<td>High cost and maintenance and expert operators; newer equipment is better suited to checkpoints</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amplifying fluorescent polymers</td>\n<td>Their fluorescence is quenched by a molecule such as TNT; one molecule quenches thousands of monomers and amplifies the effect; polymers in capillary tubes next to a photomultiplier tube; alarm if the change exceeds a threshold</td>\n<td>From low picograms to <strong>femtograms</strong></td>\n<td>Small, cheap, and highly sensitive</td>\n<td>Not all explosives respond; coatings for more explosives are being researched</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Canine olfaction</td>\n<td>The handler detects by observing the dog's behavior</td>\n<td>Widely used by police and military for explosives and drugs</td>\n<td>Mobility: searching buildings and quick relocation; cheap to acquire</td>\n<td>Constant retraining for synthetic compounds; depends on the vigilance and skill of the handler and on the dog's health and willingness; frequent rest breaks, which may require several teams; the handler's labor is a recurring cost; for this reason it is less common at fixed checkpoints</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Mass spectrometry variants</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Quadrupole mass spectrometer:</strong> negative ionization by electrical discharge, acceleration in an electric field, and focusing on the detector with the quadrupole's magnetic field.<ul>\n<li>It monitors characteristic explosive masses separately or scans a continuous range.</li>\n<li>It determines the parent ion and the fragments or daughter ions.</li>\n<li>It alarms when the current exceeds a threshold for a mass or combination of masses.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Quadrupole ion trap time-of-flight:</strong> the ions orbit in the trap; it is emptied periodically and their time to the detector is measured.<ul>\n<li>Time of flight depends on the square root of the ion's mass.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>IMS versus time of flight:</strong> they are similar, but IMS operates at atmospheric pressure and time of flight in a vacuum.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9012em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">flight</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∝</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.04em;vertical-align:-0.1908em;\"></span><span class=\"mord sqrt\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8492em;\"><span class=\"svg-align\" style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"padding-left:0.833em;\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-2.8092em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"hide-tail\" style=\"min-width:0.853em;height:1.08em;\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"400em\" height=\"1.08em\" viewBox=\"0 0 400000 1080\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMinYMin slice\"><path d=\"M95,702\nc-2.7,0,-7.17,-2.7,-13.5,-8c-5.8,-5.3,-9.5,-10,-9.5,-14\nc0,-2,0.3,-3.3,1,-4c1.3,-2.7,23.83,-20.7,67.5,-54\nc44.2,-33.3,65.8,-50.3,66.5,-51c1.3,-1.3,3,-2,5,-2c4.7,0,8.7,3.3,12,10\ns173,378,173,378c0.7,0,35.3,-71,104,-213c68.7,-142,137.5,-285,206.5,-429\nc69,-144,104.5,-217.7,106.5,-221\nl0 -0\nc5.3,-9.3,12,-14,20,-14\nH400000v40H845.2724\ns-225.272,467,-225.272,467s-235,486,-235,486c-2.7,4.7,-9,7,-19,7\nc-6,0,-10,-1,-12,-3s-194,-422,-194,-422s-65,47,-65,47z\nM834 80h400000v40h-400000z\"/></svg></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1908em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>IMS: positive and negative ions</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Common high explosives form negative ions.</li>\n<li>Some emerging threats, such as TATP (triacetonetriperoxide), also form positive ions.</li>\n<li>IMS units with positive and negative ion analysis are already on sale.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trace explosives detection portals</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Developed in the last decade; deployed at many airports.</li>\n<li>Short bursts of air agitate the clothing and release residues; the air around the person is filtered.</li>\n<li>The filter collects vapors and particles for several seconds and is then heated to release (desorb) them into an IMS or a mass spectrometer.</li>\n<li>Screening time: <strong>10 to 25 seconds</strong>.</li>\n<li>Advantages: automated detection, high sensitivity (nanograms), and noninvasive screening of the whole person.</li>\n<li>Disadvantages: size, cost (about USD 150,000), and maintenance.</li>\n<li>Comparison: swabbing a person is possible, but would be considered invasive and would take more than a minute.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How to choose a commercial trace detector</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Select it according to the needs of each facility.</li>\n<li>Always verify what the vendor promises with tests in the real operating environment.</li>\n<li>Factors to consider:<ul>\n<li>Sensitivity.</li>\n<li>Resistance to nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>Response time.</li>\n<li>Operating and maintenance costs.</li>\n<li>List of explosives in the threat definition.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.2.4 Chemical and Biological Agent Detection</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is normally done with <strong>point sensors</strong> that look for signs of attack at the <strong>perimeter</strong> of the site.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Chemical agent</th>\n<th>Biological agent</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lethality</td>\n<td>Sudden attack with large, rapidly lethal concentrations</td>\n<td>Most are not immediately lethal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Response time</td>\n<td>Critical</td>\n<td>Less critical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System goal</td>\n<td>Early warning to interrupt and neutralize the adversary</td>\n<td>Identify the agent to treat exposed personnel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Method</td>\n<td>Air sampling at several points on the perimeter; some use optical methods to detect from a distance (standoff)</td>\n<td>Filter air for several hours and then analyze the filter for several more hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limitation</td>\n<td>May not be suitable for checkpoint screening</td>\n<td>Because of the delay, it is hard to detect in time to avoid exposure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Chemical detectors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Military and environmental trace chemical detectors exist, developed over the last century.</li>\n<li>They may need modifications if they must operate continuously for long periods.</li>\n<li>Take great care with the nuisance alarm rate: the response to a chemical attack must be quick and complete.</li>\n<li>Those who must respond tolerate nuisance alarms or drills poorly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Biological detectors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Because of their limitations, biological detection is today a very active area of research.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Outside the scope of contraband detection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is also possible to monitor what crosses the perimeter by water (rain, streams, piped drinking water) and air (pollen, pollutants).</li>\n<li>That is considered <strong>environmental monitoring</strong>, not contraband detection.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-11-e2\">11.3 Physical Intrusion Detection Systems</h3><p><strong>Role of the sensor</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sensors are the <strong>basic building blocks</strong> of an intrusion detection system (IDS).</li>\n<li>They start the detection function: they indicate an intrusion attempt or a tamper event.</li>\n<li>Everything that follows the initial alarm depends on the sensor technology: logical discrimination, transmission, processing, display, and recording.</li>\n<li>That technology can be optical, electronic, electromechanical, or mechanical.</li>\n<li>If the sensor is not appropriate for the environment or the threat, or is not properly installed, operated, maintained, and tested, the whole system is severely limited.</li>\n<li>In that case the system becomes more of a burden than a benefit.</li>\n<li>The Electronic Security Association (formerly the National Burglar and Fire Alarm Association) seeks to reduce nuisance alarms to <strong>one per year per system</strong>.</li>\n<li>In reality, it is very unlikely that an effective sensor has such a low rate: choosing the device well is paramount.</li>\n<li>Key: match the sensor to the threat and the environment, and integrate it into the physical protection system (PPS).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Components of an IDS (they work in combination)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exterior and interior intrusion sensors.</li>\n<li>Video alarm assessment.</li>\n<li>Access control.</li>\n<li>Alarm communication systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Definition and scope</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intrusion detection:</strong> the process of detecting a person or vehicle attempting to enter an area without authorization.</li>\n<li>When a sensor is integrated, it must satisfy three things: probability of detection, adequate maintenance, and false alarm frequency.</li>\n<li>The detection boundary is thought of as a <strong>sphere</strong> around the protected asset.</li>\n<li>This is how every intrusion is detected: by surface, by air, underwater, or underground.</li>\n<li>Exterior technology focuses on detecting on the ground or slightly above it; this section mainly addresses ground-level intrusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.3.1 Performance Characteristics</h4>\n<p>Three main characteristics, valid for exterior and interior sensors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Probability of detection (PD).</li>\n<li>Nuisance alarm rate (NAR).</li>\n<li>Vulnerability to defeat.</li>\n</ol>\n<h5>Probability of Detection</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>A perfect PD would be 1; in reality it is always less than 1.</li>\n<li>After thousands of tests, a sensor's PD only approaches 1.</li>\n<li>For a specific sensor and scenario, effectiveness is described with two values: <strong>PD and confidence level (CL)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Manufacturers usually give the PD without the CL. In that case they probably imply a CL of at least 90%.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes “a sensor's partial discharge (PD).” This is an erratum: here PD means probability of detection.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Factors the PD depends on</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Target to be detected (for example, an intruder who walks, runs, or crawls, or who digs a tunnel), plus the direction and speed with which they cross the sensor pattern.</li>\n<li>Design of the sensor hardware.</li>\n<li>Installation conditions.</li>\n<li>Sensitivity setting.</li>\n<li>Weather conditions.</li>\n<li>Condition of the equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>PD is conditional</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Those conditions vary: a fixed PD cannot be assigned to a component or piece of equipment.</li>\n<li>Any assigned PD is <strong>conditional</strong>: it is based on assumptions about the conditions.</li>\n<li>A sensor can have one PD against a low threat (a vandal) and another, lower one against a sophisticated threat.</li>\n<li>The <strong>design basis threat (DBT)</strong> guides the design of the system:<ul>\n<li>DBT of three criminals with a lot of knowledge and skill: use a higher-PD sensor.</li>\n<li>Less sophisticated threats: a lower PD is tolerated.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Sensor selection must fit the application and the environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Detection criteria</strong></p>\n<p>The designer must specify them. They must state:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What will be detected.</li>\n<li>What actions are expected.</li>\n<li>Other conditions, such as weight or speed of movement.</li>\n<li>What PD is required.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Measurable conditions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Criterion for a perimeter system</td>\n<td>Detect a person of <strong>35 kg or more</strong> who crosses the zone walking, crawling, jumping, running, or rolling, at <strong>0.15–5 m/s</strong>, or who climbs the fence at any point in the zone; PD of <strong>90% with 95% confidence</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UL 681 standard</td>\n<td>Detect after walking <strong>three steps</strong>, at one step per second, with steps of <strong>30 inches</strong>, in <strong>three of four</strong> walk test patterns, from any point in the coverage area</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Both are clear, measurable conditions, not vague phrases such as “it should detect most of the time.”</li>\n<li>If a high PD is required at all times and in any weather: use <strong>multiple sensors</strong>.</li>\n<li>Contingency plans and procedures with compensatory measures are needed if one or all of the sensors are lost.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Nuisance Alarm Rate (NAR)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nuisance alarm:</strong> any alarm <strong>not caused by an intrusion</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>NAR:</strong> number of nuisance alarms in a given period. Ideally it would be zero.</li>\n<li>Every sensor interacts with its environment and does not always distinguish an intrusion from other events.</li>\n<li>That is why <strong>alarm assessment</strong> is needed: it is not effective to send the security force to every alarm.</li>\n<li>Assessment determines the cause and decides whether a response is needed. <strong>Without assessment, detection is incomplete.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of alarm</th>\n<th>Origin</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nuisance from natural cause</td>\n<td>Natural environment</td>\n<td>Vegetation (trees, brush); fauna (animals, birds); weather (wind, rain, snow, fog, lightning)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nuisance from industrial cause</td>\n<td>Industrial environment</td>\n<td>Ground vibration; trash blown by the wind; electromagnetic interference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>False alarm</strong></td>\n<td>The equipment itself</td>\n<td>Poor design, inadequate maintenance, or failure of a component</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A false alarm is a <strong>type</strong> of nuisance alarm.</li>\n<li>Each type of sensor, interior or exterior, is more or less vulnerable to each source.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Specifying the false alarm rate (FAR)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Example: the FAR of the entire perimeter must not average more than <strong>one false alarm per week per zone</strong>, while maintaining a <strong>PD of 0.9</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is much more useful than saying “higher FAR and NAR are tolerated if they do not degrade the system,” which is hard to measure.</li>\n<li>With concrete values it is easier to decide when to report a sensor for maintenance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Vulnerability to Defeat</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The ideal sensor could not be defeated, but <strong>all existing sensors can be defeated</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each type and model has different vulnerabilities.</li>\n<li>Goal of the PPS designer: that the system be very hard to defeat.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Way to defeat it</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bypass</td>\n<td>Every detection zone is finite: its volume or pattern is gone around; some accessible sensors can be masked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adversary path exploitation</td>\n<td>The direction and speed of movement may not activate the sensor; example: advancing head-on toward a passive infrared (PIR) sensor, which detects cross-walk better</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spoof</td>\n<td>Any technique for passing through the normal detection zone without generating an alarm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Alarm Initiation Conditions</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Type of sensor</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Possible intrusion event</td>\n<td>Intrusion sensors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change in a monitored security or process condition, for example a rise in temperature or the presence of smoke</td>\n<td>State sensors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss of electrical power</td>\n<td>Fault event sensors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Opening, short-circuiting, or grounding of the circuit; tampering with the sensors; impedance changes in a cabinet or in distributed control panels (transponders)</td>\n<td>Tamper sensors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure of the sensor itself</td>\n<td>Another fault event that must be detected</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Operating Conditions</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Location</th>\n<th>Temperature range</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Interior</td>\n<td>32 to 120 °F (0 to 49 °C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exterior or unheated structures</td>\n<td>−30 to 150 °F (−34 to 66 °C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All equipment</td>\n<td>Must operate at 90 °F (32 °C) with 95% relative humidity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>11.3.2 Standards</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They help judge the quality and suitability of sensors from different manufacturers.</li>\n<li>Many standards, including those in the U.S., are often revised because of technological advances and best practices.</li>\n<li>In each design and installation project: review the most recent version of the standards for that geographic area.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>UL Standards (Underwriters Laboratories)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The best-known body; headquartered in Northbrook, Illinois.</li>\n<li>It prepares safety standards mainly as a guide for manufacturers, and certifies whether the equipment submitted to it meets them.</li>\n<li>The standards arise from broad feedback: the public, insurers, government, academia, inspection authorities, consumer organizations, and end users.</li>\n<li>The standards are public, but UL asks that its <strong>periodic directories</strong> be used as a guide: lists of equipment submitted, tested, and certified by UL for a specific standard.</li>\n<li>Directory of security equipment: <em>Automotive, Burglary Protection and Mechanical Equipment Directory</em>.</li>\n<li>The asset protection professional must know the standard in detail to understand the real scope of a UL approval.</li>\n<li>UL approval is often required in system specifications and in municipal building and fire codes. List of standards: www.ul.com.</li>\n<li>Many UL fire and security standards deal mainly with the engineering and manufacture of alarms and their controllers.</li>\n<li><strong>Key:</strong> they are <strong>safety standards (safety), not protection against adversaries (security)</strong>.</li>\n<li>They guide correct installation to reduce the risk of fire or other safety events.</li>\n<li>They <strong>do not</strong> address equipment vulnerabilities or its ability to detect intrusions by malicious adversaries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>UL security system standards</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>UL number</th>\n<th>Name</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>365</td>\n<td>Police Station Connected Burglar Alarm Units and Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>606</td>\n<td>Linings and Screens for Use with Burglar Alarm Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>609</td>\n<td>Local Burglar Alarm Units and Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>611</td>\n<td>Central-Station Burglar Alarm Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>634</td>\n<td>Connectors and Switches for Use with Burglar Alarm Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>636</td>\n<td>Hold-Up Alarm Units and Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>639</td>\n<td>Intrusion Detection Units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>681</td>\n<td>Installation and Classification of Burglar and Holdup Alarm Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>827</td>\n<td>Central-Station Alarm Services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>827A</td>\n<td>Central-Station Alarm Hosted Services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1037</td>\n<td>Anti-Theft Alarms and Devices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1076</td>\n<td>Proprietary Burglar Alarm Units and Systems USA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1610</td>\n<td>Central Station Burglar Alarm Units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1635</td>\n<td>Digital Burglar Alarm Communicator Units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1641</td>\n<td>Installation and Classification of Residential Burglar Alarm Systems USA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2050</td>\n<td>National Industrial Security Systems for the Protection of Classified Materials</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>All of these standards are also designated as a national standard by ANSI (American National Standards Institute).</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Pending:</strong> the book says the standards in bold are the most relevant, because they set how to install or operate in the U.S. In the PDF they all have the same format, so it is not possible to know which they are.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>UL-listed equipment versus UL-certificated system</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is an important difference between the UL standards for <strong>equipment</strong> and a <strong>UL-certificated burglar alarm system</strong>.</li>\n<li>The same applies to the alarm service company (ASC) of a certificated system, such as those under UL 611, 827, and 2050. It covers:<ul>\n<li>Response options of an agent sent to the site (runner response), with and without keys.</li>\n<li>Optional categories of UL response requirements.</li>\n<li>Annual maintenance duties of the ASC.</li>\n<li>Defects found: minor, major, and maintenance-related.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Insurers often require these systems for higher-risk commercial IDS.</li>\n<li>High-security classified systems: <strong>UL 2050</strong> is normally required.</li>\n<li>It is required by government security standards such as the NISPOM (National Industrial Security Program Operating Manual) or ICD-705 (Intelligence Community Directive) for IDS in classified spaces or SCIFs.</li>\n<li>SCIF: sensitive compartmented information facility, a facility for compartmented classified information (outside the book).</li>\n<li>UL 611 and UL 2050 require UL-listed equipment (such as UL 634 and 639) and <strong>UL 681 for the installation</strong> of the IDS.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>ASTM Standards</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>ASTM (American Society for Testing and Materials), headquartered in Philadelphia.</li>\n<li>It has <strong>Committee F-12, Security Systems and Equipment</strong>.</li>\n<li>It develops and standardizes for security systems and equipment: nomenclature, definitions, test methods (prepared in design or installation), specifications, classifications, and recommended practices.</li>\n<li>It promotes knowledge about property protection and life safety; it works with other committees, organizations, and individuals.</li>\n<li><strong>ASTM does not yet have standards for alarm systems or for sensors.</strong></li>\n<li>It published <em>Building Security</em> (Stroik, 1981): a reference base for evaluating the performance of building security systems, components, and equipment.</li>\n<li>Especially relevant section: “Design Considerations for High Security Interior Intrusion Detection Systems.”</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Other Standards and Specifications (mainly North America)</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Body</th>\n<th>Document</th>\n<th>Key facts</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>GSA (General Services Administration, U.S.)</td>\n<td>Specification for alarm system components</td>\n<td>First edition in <strong>1969</strong> as Interim Specification WA-0045A; revised twice; final version W-A-45OC/GEN (as it appears in the book)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sandia Laboratories (Albuquerque, New Mexico)</td>\n<td><em>Intrusion Detection System Handbook</em> (<strong>1976</strong>)</td>\n<td>Based on evaluations by government laboratories and information from commercial vendors; several revisions; it is <strong>not</strong> intended to be an equipment specification or standard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NFPA (National Fire Protection Association)</td>\n<td><strong>NFPA 72</strong>, National Fire Alarm Code</td>\n<td>In <strong>1993</strong> it consolidated NFPA 71, 72A, 72B, 72C, 72D, 72E, 72F, 72G, 72H, and 74; covers municipal, central station, proprietary, and local fire alarms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NFPA</td>\n<td>National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code Handbook</td>\n<td>Explanatory text for interpreting and applying the formal language of the code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NFPA</td>\n<td><strong>NFPA 731</strong>, Standard for Installation of Electronic Premises Security Systems</td>\n<td>Installation of electronic premises security systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NFPA</td>\n<td><strong>NFPA 70</strong>, National Electrical Code</td>\n<td>Two articles regulate all low- and high-voltage wiring of IDS and electronic systems such as access control</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>NFPA updates and publishes its standards regularly.</li>\n<li>Technicians must comply with NFPA 70 in addition to the manufacturer's documentation, especially in listed electronic systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Current volumes of the Sandia handbook</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Intrusion Detection System Concepts.</li>\n<li>Considerations for Sensor Selection and Subsystem Design.</li>\n<li>Exterior Intrusion Sensors.</li>\n<li>Interior Intrusion Sensors.</li>\n<li>Alarm Assessment Systems.</li>\n<li>Alarm Reporting Systems.</li>\n<li>Intrusion Detection System Integration.</li>\n</ol>\n<h5>European Alarm Standards</h5>\n<p>Four grades of intruder alarm system, according to the expected level of attack (intruder's knowledge and tools):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Grade</th>\n<th>Expected intruder</th>\n<th>Risk it is suited for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Little knowledge and limited tools</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Limited knowledge and some tools</td>\n<td>Low to medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Knowledge and a full range of tools</td>\n<td>Medium to high</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Sophisticated knowledge and tools</td>\n<td>High</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Each piece of equipment is marked with the grade it is suitable for.</li>\n<li>Equipment of different grades can be mixed: the official grade of the system is that of the <strong>lowest-grade equipment</strong>.</li>\n<li>There is little grade 4 equipment, so installers tend to use grade 3.</li>\n<li>Grade 4 direct-wire signaling does exist: it must be installed at every medium- to high-risk premises, such as jewelers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Who decides the grade (United Kingdom)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The ABI (Association of British Insurers) stopped deciding the grade as a group of insurers.</li>\n<li>Reason: the OFT (Office of Fair Trading) considered it anticompetitive.</li>\n<li>Now alarm companies decide the grade according to the risk; some recommend lower grades to win customers.</li>\n<li>Jewelers: insurers usually require a <strong>grade 3 system with grade 4 signaling</strong>.</li>\n<li>Whatever the grade of the system, grade 4 signaling is required.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Transition from British to European standards</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>On <strong>October 1, 2005</strong>, British standards in conflict with European ones were withdrawn.</li>\n<li>BS 4737, 6799, and 7042 were replaced by the European series <strong>BS EN 50131</strong>.</li>\n<li>The European standards are <strong>not retroactive</strong>: systems installed to a British standard continue to be maintained and updated under it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Grading of detection and control systems</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In addition to more event memory in the panel and more recommended detection, the key difference among grades 2, 3, and 4 is this:<ul>\n<li><strong>Grade 3:</strong> motion sensors must detect <strong>masking</strong>, that is, something placed over the lens.</li>\n<li><strong>Grade 4:</strong> they must detect <strong>range reduction</strong>, that is, something that blocks part of the field of view.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Grading of signaling systems</strong></p>\n<p>Each grade has options with acceptable combinations: local siren or link to an alarm receiving center (ARC). The likely ones in the United Kingdom:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Grade and option</th>\n<th>Configuration</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade 2 – Option X</td>\n<td>Siren on site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade 2 – Option B</td>\n<td>Siren + one link to the ARC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade 2 – Option C</td>\n<td>Two links to the ARC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade 2 – Option D</td>\n<td>One link to the ARC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade 3 – Option B</td>\n<td>Siren + one link to the ARC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade 3 – Option C</td>\n<td>Two links to the ARC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade 3 – Option D</td>\n<td>One link to the ARC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade 4 – Options B, C, and D</td>\n<td>Same as those for grade 3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Notice to the ARC of signaling failure (example with option B)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Grade</th>\n<th>Maximum time to report the failure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>25 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>5 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>3 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Grading of maintenance</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Grade and option</th>\n<th>Minimum maintenance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade 2 – Option X</td>\n<td>One site visit per year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grades 2 and 3 – Options B, C, and D</td>\n<td>Two visits per year, or one visit and one remote check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade 4 – Options B, C, and D</td>\n<td>Two site visits per year</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Confirmed alarms</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Systems that generate confirmed alarm conditions are also governed by the British Standards Institute document <strong>DD 243</strong>.</li>\n<li>These documents set minimums for alarms in commercial or domestic buildings.</li>\n<li>They do not cover everything insurers require for commercial premises: when buying a new system, additional requirements must be given to the installer.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says “these two documents” without naming the second. From context it appears to be the BS EN 50131 series.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h5>Canadian Standards (Canadian alarm and fire safety standards)</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Standard</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S524-06</td>\n<td>Installation of Fire Alarm Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S525-07</td>\n<td>Audible Signal Devices for Fire Alarm Systems, Including Accessories</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S526-07</td>\n<td>Visible Signal Devices for Fire Alarm Systems, Including Accessories</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S527-99</td>\n<td>Control Units for Fire Alarm Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S528-05</td>\n<td>Manual Stations for Fire Alarm Systems, Including Accessories</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S529-02</td>\n<td>Smoke Detectors for Fire Alarm Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S530-91</td>\n<td>Heat Detectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S531-02</td>\n<td>Smoke Alarms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S533-08</td>\n<td>Egress Door Securing &amp; Releasing Devices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S536-04</td>\n<td>Inspection &amp; Testing of Fire Alarm Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S537-04</td>\n<td>Verification of Fire Alarm Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S540-M86</td>\n<td>Installation of Residential Fire Warning Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S541-07</td>\n<td>Speakers for Fire Alarm Systems, Including Accessories</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ULC-S545-02</td>\n<td>Residential Fire Warning System Control Units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S548-08</td>\n<td>Devices &amp; Accessories for Water Type Extinguishing Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S552-02</td>\n<td>Inspection &amp; Testing of Smoke Alarms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S553-02</td>\n<td>Installation of Smoke Alarms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S559-04</td>\n<td>Equipment for Fire Signal Receiving Centers and Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S561-03</td>\n<td>Installation and Services for Fire Signal Receiving Centers and Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAN/ULC-S573</td>\n<td>Installation of Ancillary Devices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ULC-S571</td>\n<td>Flame Detectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ULC-S567</td>\n<td>Door Closers and Electromagnetic Door Holders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ULC-S575</td>\n<td>Commissioning of Life Safety &amp; Fire Protection Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ULC-S572</td>\n<td>Photoluminescent and Self-Luminous Exit Signs and Path Marking Systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ULC-S576</td>\n<td>Mass Notification System Equipment and Accessories</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>ULC: Underwriters Laboratories of Canada; CAN indicates a Canadian national standard (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.3.3 Exterior Sensors</h4>\n<h5>Classification</h5>\n<p>One way to classify exterior sensors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Passive or active.</li>\n<li>Covert or visible.</li>\n<li>Line-of-sight or terrain-following.</li>\n<li>Volumetric or line detection.</li>\n<li>Application.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Option A</th>\n<th>Option B</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Energy</td>\n<td><strong>Passive:</strong> only receives. It detects the energy the target emits (for example, the mechanical energy of someone walking on the ground or climbing a fence) or the change it causes in a natural field (for example, the local magnetic field altered by a metal). It can detect vibration, heat, sound, or capacitance</td>\n<td><strong>Active:</strong> transmits energy and detects changes in what it receives, caused by the presence or movement of the target. It usually has a transmitter and a receiver. Examples: microwave, infrared, and other radio frequency (RF)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Advantage</td>\n<td>Passive: does not emit, so it is hard to find; safer in environments with vapors or explosive materials</td>\n<td>Active: fewer nuisance alarms, because of its stronger signal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Visibility</td>\n<td><strong>Covert:</strong> hidden, for example underground; hard for the intruder to detect; does not alter the appearance of the surroundings</td>\n<td><strong>Visible:</strong> for example on a fence or structure; can deter; usually easier to install, repair, and maintain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Terrain</td>\n<td><strong>Line-of-sight (LOS):</strong> needs a clear line of sight between transmitter and receiver; on uneven terrain it requires a lot of site preparation</td>\n<td><strong>Terrain-following:</strong> detects equally on flat or uneven terrain; the transducers and the radiated field follow the terrain and give uniform detection across the whole zone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zone</td>\n<td><strong>Volumetric:</strong> alarms when the intruder enters the detection volume; it is hard to know that the space is being monitored (video motion, magnetic field, microwave)</td>\n<td><strong>Line:</strong> detects movement along a line, for example when the fence fabric where the sensor is mounted is moved; its zone is usually easy for the intruder to identify</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Groups by application</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Buried line.</li>\n<li>Fence-associated.</li>\n<li>Freestanding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References cited by the book</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reviews of sensor technologies: Barnard, 1988; Cumming, 1992; Fennelly, 1996 and 2013; Williams, 1988.</li>\n<li>More detail on the technologies: Garcia (2006 and 2008).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Technologies (most common exterior technologies)</h5>\n<p><strong>Classification summary of the technologies in this part</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technology</th>\n<th>Passive / active</th>\n<th>Covert / visible</th>\n<th>LOS / terrain-following</th>\n<th>Application</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ported coax</td>\n<td>Active</td>\n<td>Covert</td>\n<td>Terrain-following</td>\n<td>Buried</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fence disturbance sensors</td>\n<td>Passive</td>\n<td>Visible</td>\n<td>Terrain-following</td>\n<td>On fence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sensor fences (such as taut-wire)</td>\n<td>Passive</td>\n<td>Visible</td>\n<td>Terrain-following</td>\n<td>Form the fence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electric field / capacitance</td>\n<td>Active</td>\n<td>Visible</td>\n<td>Terrain-following</td>\n<td>On fence or on their own posts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Ported Coaxial Cables</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Also called <em>leaky coax</em> or <em>radiating cable sensors</em>.</li>\n<li>Active, covert, and terrain-following; they are <strong>buried</strong>.</li>\n<li>They respond to the movement of material with <strong>high dielectric constant or high conductivity</strong>: human bodies and metal vehicles.</li>\n<li>The outer conductor does not fully shield the center one: part of the signal leaks out through its ports.</li>\n<li>Detection volume: about <strong>1.5–3.0 ft (0.46–0.91 m) above the surface</strong> and about <strong>3–6 ft (0.91–1.83 m) wider</strong> than the separation between cables.</li>\n<li><strong>More sensitive in frozen soil than in thawed soil</strong>: conductive soil absorbs part of the energy, and frozen soil conducts less.</li>\n<li>Major sources of nuisance alarms: <strong>moving metal objects and moving water</strong>.</li>\n<li>To avoid distorting the field, exclude nearby metal objects and utility lines from the detection volume.</li>\n<li>Its PD depends on:<ul>\n<li>Processor settings.</li>\n<li>Orientation of the intruder.</li>\n<li>Soil characteristics.</li>\n<li>Presence of metal, including large amounts of salt or metals in the soil.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fence Disturbance Sensors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Passive, visible, and terrain-following; they are installed mainly on <strong>chain link fences</strong>.</li>\n<li>“Terrain-following” because the fabric itself follows the terrain.</li>\n<li>They detect movement or impact; they mainly look for the intruder who <strong>climbs or cuts</strong> the fabric.</li>\n<li>Transducers used:<ul>\n<li>Switches.</li>\n<li>Electromechanical transducers.</li>\n<li>Strain sensitive cable.</li>\n<li>Piezoelectric crystals.</li>\n<li>Geophones.</li>\n<li>Fiber optic cable.</li>\n<li>Electric cable.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Nuisance alarms: wind, wind-blown trash, rain with wind, hail, and seismic activity from nearby traffic and machinery.</li>\n<li>They are reduced with rigid posts and taut fabric:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Maximum allowed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Post</td>\n<td>Pull of 50 lb (22.7 kg) at 5 ft (1.5 m) above the ground</td>\n<td>Move 0.5 in (12.7 mm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fabric</td>\n<td>Pull of 30 lb (13.6 kg) at the center between posts</td>\n<td>Deflect 2.5 in (6.4 cm)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In a <strong>two-fence</strong> system, put the sensors on the <strong>inner</strong> one: the outer one stops trash and small animals.</li>\n<li>Do not hang movable objects on the fence (signs, loose ties): they generate nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>How they are defeated: <strong>tunneling under</strong> or <strong>crossing over without touching it</strong>.</li>\n<li>Against digging: concrete under the fence, and even the bottom edge of the fabric embedded in the concrete.</li>\n<li>PD depends on the installation (fabric tension, processor settings, fence rigidity, noise coupled to the fence) and on how the adversary attacks.</li>\n<li>If the adversary uses a ladder and never touches the fence, the PD is very low or zero.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sensor Fences</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Passive, visible, and terrain-following: the fence <strong>is made of the transducers themselves</strong>.</li>\n<li>Designed mainly to detect climbing or cutting of the fence; there are several configurations.</li>\n<li><strong>Taut-wire fence:</strong><ul>\n<li>Many parallel horizontal wires, of high tensile strength, connected under tension to transducers.</li>\n<li>It detects deflections of the wire: cutting it, climbing it, or spreading it apart to pass through.</li>\n<li>The wire is usually barbed; the transducers are mechanical switches, strain gauges, or piezoelectric elements.</li>\n<li>It is mounted on the posts of an existing fence or on its own row of posts.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Fewer nuisance alarms than disturbance sensors: they are not sensitive to vibration and require about <strong>25 lb (11.3 kg)</strong> of force on the wire to alarm.</li>\n<li>But they are defeated with the same methods as fence disturbance sensors.</li>\n<li>Nuisance alarms in taut-wire: mainly large animals hitting the fence, poor installation or maintenance, and ice storms.</li>\n<li>Its PD depends on: wire tension, wire friction, and spacing between wires.</li>\n<li>If the spacing lets a person through undetected, the PD drops sharply. Keep it at <strong>4 in (10 cm) or less</strong> (Greer, VTW-250 and VTW-300, 1990).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fiber Fences</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Light transmitted through a fiber optic cable attached to an existing fence.</li>\n<li>The light pulses are monitored continuously: a change in pattern or power indicates tampering or cutting, possible intrusion.</li>\n<li>They detect minimal changes in the received light.</li>\n<li>Advantages: immune to electromagnetic interference; safe in any environment because they do not conduct electricity; exceptional range.</li>\n<li>The cable uses microphonic technology to detect attempts to climb or cut.</li>\n<li>It resists environmental nuisance alarms; it is not affected by heat, ultraviolet (UV) light, light, or humidity.</li>\n<li>A flexible microphonic cable (stealth-flex) is attached with UV-resistant ties.</li>\n<li>The control analyzer goes in a cabinet with a NEMA (National Electrical Manufacturers Association) rating, on a pole or inside a metal security box.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Electric Field or Capacitance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Active, visible, and terrain-following.</li>\n<li>They detect a change in the capacitive coupling among several wires attached to a fence but electrically isolated from it.</li>\n<li>Sensitivity adjustable up to <strong>3.3 ft (1 m)</strong> beyond the wire or plane of wires; more sensitivity usually gives more nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>Nuisance alarms: lightning, rain, humidity, fence movement, and small animals.</li>\n<li>Ice storms can damage wires and insulators.</li>\n<li>Nuisance alarms are reduced by:<ul>\n<li>Good grounding of the sensors and of the metal objects in the field.</li>\n<li>Dual-technology sensors, which require two different sensors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Harder to defeat</strong> than other fence sensors by digging or bridging: their detection volume extends beyond the plane of the fence (Follis, 1990).</li>\n<li>They can be mounted on their own posts, with no fence:<ul>\n<li>Without the fabric, the detection volume is wider and there are fewer nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>In freestanding use, some processors add wires in the horizontal plane to reduce the effect of distant lightning and small animals.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Classification summary (second part of technologies)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technology</th>\n<th>Passive / active</th>\n<th>Covert / visible</th>\n<th>LOS / terrain-following / volumetric</th>\n<th>Application</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Multibeam infrared (IR)</td>\n<td>Active</td>\n<td>Visible</td>\n<td>Line-of-sight</td>\n<td>Freestanding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bistatic microwave</td>\n<td>Active</td>\n<td>Visible</td>\n<td>Line-of-sight</td>\n<td>Freestanding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground-based radar (GBR)</td>\n<td>Active (reflected RF energy)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Volumetric</td>\n<td>Large open areas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exterior video motion detector (VMD)</td>\n<td>Passive</td>\n<td>Covert</td>\n<td>Line-of-sight</td>\n<td>On top of the video camera</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Freestanding Infrared Sensors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Active, visible, line-of-sight, and freestanding.</li>\n<li>Photoelectric beam systems:<ul>\n<li>An infrared LED emits the beam through a collimating lens.</li>\n<li>At the other end, a collecting lens focuses the energy on a photodiode.</li>\n<li>If an opaque object blocks the beam, the sensor detects the drop in received energy.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Wavelength: about <strong>0.9 microns</strong>, invisible to the human eye.</li>\n<li>In high security, <strong>multiple-beam</strong> sensors are used: a single beam is too easy to defeat.<ul>\n<li>They usually have two vertical columns of transmitter and receiver modules; number and configuration depend on the manufacturer.</li>\n<li>They form a “fence” of infrared beams.</li>\n<li>They often include electronics that detect attempts to spoof the beams with another infrared source.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Nuisance alarms:<ul>\n<li>Atmospheric conditions that block the beam: fog, snow, dust storms.</li>\n<li>Grass, other vegetation, and animals; keep the area between posts clear, because even mowed grass moves in the wind.</li>\n<li>Ground heave, optical misalignment, and deep snow.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Section of the detection volume of a multibeam: about <strong>2 in (5 cm) wide and 6 ft (1.8 m) high</strong>. Like fence sensors, it is a narrow plane.</li>\n<li>The beam goes in a straight line: it needs <strong>flat ground</strong>.<ul>\n<li>Convex ground: blocks the beam.</li>\n<li>Concave ground: allows passing underneath without detection.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>One can dig under the lower beam, unless there is a concrete sill or paved surface.</li>\n<li>Very high PD in multibeam, but it is defeated by bridging, pole vaulting, or passing or sliding between beams.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bistatic Microwave Sensors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Active, visible, line-of-sight, and freestanding.</li>\n<li>Two identical antennas at opposite ends of the zone (Figure 11.1, Bistatic Microwave Sensors):<ul>\n<li>One connected to a transmitter of about <strong>10 GHz or 24 GHz</strong>.</li>\n<li>The other to a receiver that measures the received energy.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Received energy = vector sum of the direct beam and the signals reflected from the ground and other objects.</li>\n<li>They respond to changes in that vector sum caused by moving objects. The sum can rise or fall, depending on whether the reflection arrives in phase or out of phase.</li>\n<li>They are usually installed to detect someone who <strong>crawls or rolls</strong> with the body parallel to the beam (the smallest target). Consequences:<ul>\n<li><strong>Flat ground</strong> between transmitter and receiver, so that nothing is in the shadow of the beam: specification <strong>+0, −6 in (15.2 cm)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Even so, it may not detect someone crawling if the antennas are much farther apart than <strong>120 yd (109.7 m)</strong>.</li>\n<li>There is a <strong>zone with no detection</strong> in the first few meters in front of the antennas: the <strong>offset distance</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Design of a continuous microwave perimeter</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parameter</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Antenna arrangement</td>\n<td>Overlapped, not adjacent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offset distance assumed</td>\n<td>10 yd (9.1 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overlap between adjacent sectors</td>\n<td>Twice the offset: 20 yd (18.3 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Antenna height</td>\n<td>18–24 in (46–60 cm) above the sensor bed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum slope of the plane</td>\n<td>1 in (2.5 cm) of drop in 10 ft (3 m) from any point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sensor bed</td>\n<td>4 in (10.2 cm) of river gravel of maximum 1.5 in (3.8 cm) diameter, neutral in color for assessment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alternative</td>\n<td>Crushed stone that passes through a 1 in (2.5 cm) mesh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Largest detection section</td>\n<td>Halfway between antennas: about 4 yd (3.7 m) wide and 3 yd (2.7 m) high</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vegetation</td>\n<td>Maximum 1–2 in (2.5–5.0 cm); better none</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Main cause of nuisance alarms: <strong>standing water</strong>.</li>\n<li>Larger gravel: rain still causes nuisance alarms. Smaller stones: they fill with dirt and do not drain.</li>\n<li>The plane for detecting someone who crawls must have a <strong>cross slope</strong> to drain, and gravel to prevent puddles.</li>\n<li>The detection volume varies, but it is large compared with almost all other sensors.</li>\n<li>They tolerate many environmental conditions without nuisance alarms. Problems:<ul>\n<li>A parallel chain link fence nearby, with loose fabric that flexes in the wind.</li>\n<li>Heavy wind-blown snow: nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>Accumulated snow: lowers the PD, especially for someone who crawls.</li>\n<li>Antenna completely buried in snow: constant alarm.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Bridging or digging underneath is difficult because of the size of the detection volume.</li>\n<li>Sophisticated adversaries could use secondary transmitters to defeat it.</li>\n<li>It is advisable to fence the zone to reduce nuisance alarms and protect the prepared area.</li>\n<li>They are not practical where hills, trees, or other natural features obstruct the beam.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ground Based Radar (GBR)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Radar: Radio Detection and Ranging.</li>\n<li>It uses radio frequency energy like line-of-sight microwave, but is considered a <strong>volumetric</strong> sensor.</li>\n<li>Intended for large, unobstructed open exterior areas; a good option at night and where rain and fog are a concern.</li>\n<li>It determines <strong>distance, altitude, direction, and speed</strong> of objects by means of reflected energy.</li>\n<li>The area always has elements that reflect energy; in steady state that is the <strong>clutter</strong> (background echo).</li>\n<li>A reflection that stands out from the background in a sweep may be of interest; it is considered an intrusion if it appears in several consecutive sweeps.</li>\n<li>Hardest to detect: objects moving <strong>tangentially</strong> (at constant distance from the radar) or slow objects.</li>\n<li>Integrating analytics improves effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book calls the background echo first “clutter” and then “chatter.” The technical term is clutter (outside the book).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>GBR: deployment keys</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Avoid obstacles that create blind spots.</li>\n<li>Clear line of sight.</li>\n<li>Locate it to detect <strong>radial</strong> movement (toward or away from the radar).</li>\n<li>Reduce clutter.</li>\n<li>Avoid reflective objects, such as metal and glass.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>GBR data</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Horizontal field of view (azimuth)</td>\n<td>100–120°</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertical field of view (elevation)</td>\n<td>20–30°; more degrees give margin to detect downward from the unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Potential coverage</td>\n<td>Hundreds of acres</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Advertised range: vehicles</td>\n<td>Up to 13 km (the greatest)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Advertised range: people</td>\n<td>Up to 800 m</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Advertised range: drones</td>\n<td>Up to 500 m</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>LiDAR (Light Imaging, Detection, and Ranging)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It uses pulsed laser light to measure distances, in a way similar to radar.</li>\n<li>It measures how long the light takes to reach an object or surface, calculates the distance, and forms data points.</li>\n<li>Millions of points (<strong>point clouds</strong>) are processed in seconds as a 3D visualization, with no need for a video system.</li>\n<li>According to the book, unlike radar or sound, it does not dissipate when returning to its scanner.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>LiDAR</th>\n<th>Radar (GBR)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wavelength</td>\n<td>Very short: less than one millionth of a meter</td>\n<td>Up to about 5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weather</td>\n<td>Easily affected by fog, rain, or dust: droplets absorb the light</td>\n<td>Passes through fog, rain, or snow droplets or even sand</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Image</td>\n<td>Color gradients that indicate elevation and great 3D detail; useful for recognition</td>\n<td>Monochrome: objects as “blobs” of similar tones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assessment</td>\n<td>Very effective for detecting people in open areas or crossings, even where they are looking or moving</td>\n<td>Also requires a video camera to confirm the type of object</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Analytics and artificial intelligence (AI) are integrated to learn practically any object.</li>\n<li>They give 3D geolocation to identify and recognize targets faster.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book's text says “all without and VSS”; it appears to be an erratum for “without any VSS (video surveillance system).”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Exterior Video Motion Detectors (VMDs)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Passive, covert, and line-of-sight: they process the video signal from the video surveillance system.</li>\n<li>The same cameras can serve at once for detection, surveillance, and alarm assessment.</li>\n<li>To operate 24 hours, lighting is required.</li>\n<li>They detect a change in the level of the video signal in a defined part of the scene:<ul>\n<li>A large rectangle.</li>\n<li>A set of discrete points.</li>\n<li>A rectangular grid.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>They detect human movement well, except in fog, snow, heavy rain, or darkness. That limitation is overcome with thermal cameras.</li>\n<li>If the resolution does not let the operator quickly know what caused the alarm, the VMD performs poorly.</li>\n<li>Nuisance alarms outdoors:<ul>\n<li>Apparent movement of the scene due to unstable camera mounts.</li>\n<li>Lighting changes: cloud shadows, bright floodlights, vehicle headlights.</li>\n<li>Moving objects: birds, other animals, wind-blown trash, and precipitation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Tactics to defeat it: take advantage of poor visibility and blend in with the background (Ringler &amp; Hoover, 1994; Matter, 1990).</li>\n<li>It helps with assessment: it delivers an image to personnel and usually highlights the area with movement.</li>\n<li>One camera protects a large area, limited only by the lens's field of view and the resolution.</li>\n<li>Or it protects only selected regions by means of <strong>masking</strong>: the parts the VMD monitors are selected and the rest is ignored.</li>\n<li>Masking makes it possible to create several zones in a single camera view.</li>\n<li>Sensitivity can reach <strong>a single pixel</strong>, but more sensitivity gives more nuisance alarms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Analog versus digital VMD</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Analog (older)</th>\n<th>Digital</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Status</td>\n<td>Still manufactured; can be very effective depending on its sensitivity setting</td>\n<td>Much more common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost</td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n<td>Somewhat more expensive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analysis</td>\n<td>Little ability to analyze the image and discard false alarms (leaves on a tree, waves in a pool)</td>\n<td>A/D (analog to digital) converters sample the signal and convert it to digital values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resolution</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>The higher the resolution, the greater the precision: longer zones, fewer cameras, and slower and smaller targets at greater distance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjustments</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>It somewhat compensates for gradual light changes and some camera vibration; it distinguishes in a limited way wind, rain, snow, leaves, small animals, or birds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best use</td>\n<td>Low end: detecting any movement in the scene</td>\n<td>High end, well adjusted: good false alarm rejection, pixel-level detection, and estimation of the direction of movement; effective with low contrast, low light, and slow movement</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Adjustable VMD variables</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Masking: variable number of detection areas and their dimensions.</li>\n<li>Target size and sensitivity, according to the application.</li>\n<li>Tracking functions to help assess the video.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recommended use and video analytics</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The VMD works best <strong>together with other sensors</strong>; its algorithms are continually improving.</li>\n<li>Indoors it has always been effective; with digital ones it is gaining popularity outdoors.</li>\n<li><strong>Video analytics:</strong> rule-based algorithms on the digital image to provide situational awareness.</li>\n<li>Examples of preprogrammed alarms given by the book:<ul>\n<li>People gathering.</li>\n<li>People moving fast.</li>\n<li>Abandoned packages.</li>\n<li>Removal of packages.</li>\n<li>Loitering.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It makes it possible to search for exceptions in investigations, assess threats quickly, and detect volumetrically.</li>\n<li><strong>The VMD is more suitable for indoors</strong>: good detection and low NAR.</li>\n<li>Outdoors, development is lacking to reduce its excessive NAR before using it at high-security sites.</li>\n<li>If it is combined with other exterior sensors, its sensitivity can be lowered: fewer nuisance alarms and the operator retains some visual assessment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Other Exterior Sensor Concepts</h5>\n<p><strong>Technology Maturity</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Risk: unknowingly accepting a significant risk by deploying an immature technology too early.</li>\n<li>Solution: use a <strong>maturity model</strong> for security technologies.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Research</td>\n<td>Research</td>\n<td>Scientific basis established; the security application is not necessarily identified yet. Example: the ferroelectric properties of lithium niobate, a material used to sense infrared energy in IR detectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Concept feasibility</td>\n<td>Demonstrated in the laboratory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>II</td>\n<td>Research prototype</td>\n<td>Handmade in the laboratory; breaks often; does not withstand an operational environment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Engineering prototype</td>\n<td>Close to 90% of functionality; reliability improves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Field prototype</td>\n<td>Fully functional in an operational environment; reliable, repeatable results; driven and accepted by the user; ready for production at scale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V</td>\n<td>COTS (commercial off-the-shelf)</td>\n<td>Production units available, with spare parts infrastructure and technical support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VI</td>\n<td>Performance testing</td>\n<td>Sets metrics: PD, NAR, vulnerability to defeat, and performance. Outdoors they last <strong>about 12 months</strong> to observe all climates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VII</td>\n<td>Onsite testing</td>\n<td>Daily; real performance in the operational environment (foliage, weather, and terrain), including integration with the site's monitoring station</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VIII</td>\n<td>Nontechnical maturity factors</td>\n<td>The site's concept of operations: how the response force uses the information, how it should respond, and whether legal or policy issues prevent using the technology</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Novelty at the perimeter: <strong>thermal imaging radar</strong>.<ul>\n<li>Cameras that rotate continuously with high-definition thermal surveillance.</li>\n<li>Full 360° thermal images, with onboard geospatial and computer analysis.</li>\n<li>They send alerts through integrated Wi-Fi, cellular, or satellite modem.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Continuous Line of Detection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Perimeter: closed loop around the area being protected.</li>\n<li>Design goal: uniform detection along its entire length.</li>\n<li>The zone of each sector must <strong>overlap with those of the two adjacent sectors</strong>.</li>\n<li>Where the primary sensor cannot be deployed well, the gap is covered with another type of sensor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Protection-in-Depth</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Here, protection or security-in-depth = <strong>multiple lines of detection</strong>.</li>\n<li>In high security: <strong>at least two continuous lines</strong> of detection.</li>\n<li>Some systems have three lines and a few, four.</li>\n<li>Example of three lines: buried sensor that creates a magnetic field to detect vehicles + fence-associated sensor + freestanding sensor.</li>\n<li>Advantages: more detection, more reliability, and <strong>fail safe</strong>, because it still gives some protection.</li>\n<li>A sensor can fail without compromising the security of the facility.</li>\n<li>Eliminating single-point or component failures ensures balanced protection even in adverse conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Complementary Sensors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The perimeter is improved with multiple lines and also with <strong>complementary types</strong>, for example microwave and active infrared.</li>\n<li>It takes advantage of the fact that each technology has a different PD, NAR, and vulnerabilities. Results:<ul>\n<li>It detects a wider range of intruders.</li>\n<li>It keeps operating under different environmental disturbances.</li>\n<li>It is harder for the intruder to defeat the system.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>They are an alternative to dual-technology sensors: each sensor performs at its maximum, with no compromises from locating them together and filtering.</li>\n<li>They are expensive, but give more protection: that is why they are preferred in high security.</li>\n<li><strong>Detection patterns must overlap</strong> to be complementary.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Combination</th>\n<th>Complementary?</th>\n<th>Reason</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Microwave + infrared</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Patterns can be overlapped and the technologies differ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Microwave + ported coax</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Patterns can be overlapped and the technologies differ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ported coax + infrared</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Patterns can be overlapped and the technologies differ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Microwave + fence sensor</td>\n<td><strong>No</strong></td>\n<td>Their patterns cannot be overlapped without serious nuisance alarm problems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bistatic + monostatic microwave</td>\n<td><strong>No</strong></td>\n<td>Both are vulnerable to the same attack methods and the same sources of nuisance alarms</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Priority Schemes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Disadvantage of multiple lines: more nuisance alarms may appear.</li>\n<li>If the operator becomes saturated, the effectiveness of the system drops.</li>\n<li>PD drops as the time to assess alarms increases.</li>\n<li>The assessment subsystem must help the operator. One aid: a computer that orders the assessment of simultaneous alarms.</li>\n<li>Priority is based on the probability that the alarm is a real intrusion.</li>\n<li>Alarms are displayed in decreasing order of priority until all are assessed.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in this section the book inserts a sentence unrelated to the topic: that occasional access points are best for passive sonic and infrasonic sensors. It appears to be misplaced text.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Combination of Sensors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ideal: high PD for all expected intrusions and low NAR for all expected environmental conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>No current exterior sensor meets both perfectly</strong>: all have limited detection and high NAR under certain conditions.</li>\n<li>Two main techniques: <strong>OR</strong> combination and <strong>AND</strong> combination.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes “OR combinations and combinations”; the word AND is missing.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>OR combination (OR gate)</th>\n<th>AND combination (AND gate)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>When it alarms</td>\n<td>When <strong>any</strong> sensor is activated</td>\n<td>When two or more sensors are activated within a selected period</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When it is suitable</td>\n<td>The sensors compensate for each other's deficiencies: each detects different types of intrusion. Those that detect intrusion above ground, overhead, and by tunnel are combined by OR</td>\n<td>The nuisance alarms of the sensors are not correlated. Example: seismic + electric field, which respond to different things; if they alarm almost at the same time, it is probably an intrusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NAR</td>\n<td><strong>Sum</strong> of the NARs of each sensor: it rises, so it is useful mainly with sensors of low individual NAR</td>\n<td><strong>Lower</strong> than that of each sensor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PD</td>\n<td>The book does not quantify it</td>\n<td><strong>Lower</strong> than that of each sensor; with independent detection and redundant coverage, it is the <strong>product</strong> of the individual PDs; each sensor needs a high PD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weakness</td>\n<td>More nuisance alarms</td>\n<td>The intruder only has to defeat one sensor to avoid the alarm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In AND, a long interval helps detect slow intruders; if it is too long, the NAR does not drop enough.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0077em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">O</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.9291em;vertical-align:-1.2777em;\"></span><span class=\"mop op-limits\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.6514em;\"><span style=\"top:-1.8723em;margin-left:0em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.05em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span><span class=\"mrel mtight\">=</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.05em;\"></span><span><span class=\"mop op-symbol large-op\">∑</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-4.3em;margin-left:0em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.05em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.2777em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0077em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">A</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.9291em;vertical-align:-1.2777em;\"></span><span class=\"mop op-limits\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.6514em;\"><span style=\"top:-1.8723em;margin-left:0em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.05em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span><span class=\"mrel mtight\">=</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.05em;\"></span><span><span class=\"mop op-symbol large-op\">∏</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-4.3em;margin-left:0em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.05em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.2777em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Example (outside the book): two sensors with a PD of 0.9 in AND give 0.9 × 0.9 = 0.81.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clear Zone</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The perimeter performs best in an isolated clear zone (clear zone or isolation zone), usual in high security.</li>\n<li>Benefits:<ul>\n<li>It increases the PD.</li>\n<li>It reduces nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>It prevents the system from being defeated.</li>\n<li>It makes visual assessment of the causes of alarms easier.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Normally it is defined by <strong>two parallel fences</strong> along the entire perimeter.</li>\n<li>The fences keep people, animals, and vehicles out of the zone.</li>\n<li>Between them, aboveground structures, including overhead utility lines, and plants are removed.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not put sensors on the outer fence</strong>: it would have nuisance alarms from wind-blown trash and small animals.</li>\n<li>Assessing with video outside the fence is difficult: the camera cannot see through the fabric.</li>\n<li>Clear zones with several complementary sensors are reserved for high security: nuclear plants, prisons, military bases, and other government facilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sensor Configuration</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Overlapping the detection volumes of different sensors in each sector creates a larger total volume.</li>\n<li>Defeating the pair is less likely: one must go around a larger volume or defeat two technologies.</li>\n<li>A third sensor improves things more if it forms a <strong>separate line of detection</strong>, rather than overlapping with the first two.</li>\n<li>Physically separate lines help prioritize simultaneous alarms.</li>\n<li>The order of alarms in a sector or in adjacent sectors can follow the logical sequence of an intrusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Site-Specific System</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A program designed for one site <strong>cannot be transferred to another</strong>: each site combines a unique configuration and physical environment.</li>\n<li>The physical environment defines which types of sensors to use.</li>\n<li>The natural and industrial environments define the nuisance alarm rates.</li>\n<li>Topography defines the shape and size of the detection space: width of the clear zone and flat or uneven terrain.</li>\n<li>Recommendation: set up a <strong>demonstration sector</strong> at the site with the candidate sensors before committing to the full system.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tamper Protection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hardware and design must prevent the system from being defeated by tampering.</li>\n<li>The system must be <strong>tamper-resistant</strong> and <strong>tamper-indicating</strong>.</li>\n<li>Measures:<ul>\n<li>Tamper switches on the sensor electronics and in the mounting or wiring junction boxes.</li>\n<li>Power and signal cables aboveground inside metal conduit.</li>\n<li><strong>Line supervision</strong> on the alarm communication lines: it detects cuts, disconnections, short circuits, or bridging.</li>\n<li>A relatively vulnerable sensor is placed, if possible, where the intruder must be in or pass through the detection volume to get close to the receiver.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Self-Test</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Detection capability must be tested regularly.</li>\n<li>Manual testing is recommended, but it consumes a lot of personnel time.</li>\n<li>Remote test: the central control system sends trigger signals (closing or opening a switch) at random times.</li>\n<li>The system verifies that the alarm occurs within a given period and resets within another.</li>\n<li>If not, there may be a hardware failure or tampering, and an alarm must be generated.</li>\n<li>Computer self-test must keep a history of tests and results.</li>\n<li>Irregularities are checked physically and repaired or adjusted as soon as possible.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pattern Recognition</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cheap, powerful computers analyze the sensors' signal patterns looking for those typical of an intruder.</li>\n<li>With neural networks or artificial intelligence, they learn the patterns of intruders and thus avoid nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>It applies to any sensor or combination with a signal that is not just on/off; example: smart infrared and fence sensors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Effects of Physical and Environmental Conditions</strong></p>\n<p>Six conditions, different at each site:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n<th>Measures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Topography</td>\n<td>Ravines, slopes, lakes, rivers, and swamps must be studied during design</td>\n<td>Level hills and slopes; drainage to reduce water running through ravines and ditches and avoid seismic disturbances; avoid lakes, rivers, and swamps, because few commercial sensors work in water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vegetation</td>\n<td>Underground: wind moves trees and roots and triggers seismic sensors. Aboveground: large plants and trees give cover to the intruder and attract small animals</td>\n<td>Prune, remove, sterilize the soil, or pave</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fauna</td>\n<td>Large animals hit the equipment and damage it; burrowing ones gnaw the cable insulation; birds and insects cause alarms that are hard to assess</td>\n<td>Double mesh fence and chemical controls; poisons and repellents may be regulated locally</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Background noise</td>\n<td>Wind, traffic, electromagnetic interference, and seismic sources</td>\n<td>Identify them with a site survey and information from the utility companies and plant engineering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Climate</td>\n<td>Hail, thunderstorms, rain, and wind</td>\n<td>Obtain climate data: frequency, speed, accumulation, and duration of each phenomenon; average minimum and maximum temperatures; other environmental conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Soil and pavement</td>\n<td>Seismic and electrical conductivity of the medium</td>\n<td>See detail below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Detail of noise sources</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wind: passes energy to the ground through trees, light and power poles, and fences. Sensors on fences: alarms from strong winds and trash.</li>\n<li>Traffic from highways, railways, and airports: alarms in seismic sensors.<ul>\n<li>Keep roads smooth and speed limits low.</li>\n<li>Seismic sensors are not practical near heavy air or rail traffic, which causes disturbances even at great distances.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Electromagnetic interference: lightning, radio transmitters, welding, and electrical transients.<ul>\n<li>It is reduced by shielding the signal cable in conduit or shielding the sensors, with a proper common ground.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Soil and pavement</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In buried seismic sensors, what is decisive is the <strong>seismic conductivity of the medium</strong>: enough for them to work, but not so much that it causes nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>Wet soil conducts well: it can give alarms from distant seismic sources.</li>\n<li>Seismic-magnetic and seismic sensors can be embedded in, or under, concrete or asphalt pavement.<ul>\n<li>Embedded and well coupled to the medium: gains sensitivity.</li>\n<li>Poorly coupled: can be less sensitive than in soil or under the pavement.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In ported coax, very conductive soil <strong>reduces the detection volume</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lightning Protection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lightning can disable, damage, or destroy the electronics of exterior sensors.</li>\n<li>Three main precautions:<ol>\n<li><strong>Shield the signal cables</strong>, by their internal construction or with metal conduit.</li>\n<li><strong>Good grounding system</strong>: eliminate ground loops and ground at a single point.</li>\n<li><strong>Passive transient suppressors</strong> at the ends of the cables.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Fiber optic is not affected by lightning: it is the preferred method for transmitting data over long distances outside a building.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Integration with Video Assessment System</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The designs of the systems and subsystems must be compatible.</li>\n<li>Video linked to sensor activation greatly reduces the time to know what caused the alarm.</li>\n<li>That takes advantage of the remaining delay and increases the probability of interrupting the adversary.</li>\n<li>Assessment can be by video or in person.</li>\n<li>Advantage of video: remote assessment, without dispatching officers to every alarm, who might arrive too late to assess well.</li>\n<li>Locate the sensors so that, when they alarm, the camera for that zone sees <strong>the entire zone</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Design decision</th>\n<th>Value or criterion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Width of the clear zone</td>\n<td>Sensor engineers prefer it wide (fewer nuisance alarms); video engineers, narrow (better resolution). Compromise: <strong>10–15 yd (9.1–13.7 m)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Camera tower</td>\n<td>Must see the entire area; the sensors must be far enough away to avoid nuisance alarms and distortion of the detection volume</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical tower position</td>\n<td><strong>1–2 yd (0.9–1.8 m) inside the outer fence</strong> of the clear zone, so the adversary cannot use it to bridge</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Megapixel cameras and digital zoom are covered in section 11.4, Video Surveillance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Integration with Barrier Delay System</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A balanced PPS includes barriers or access denial systems.</li>\n<li>They provide delay time to assess the alarm by video and for the response force to arrive.</li>\n<li>The barrier must not degrade the performance of the sensors or block part of the camera's view.</li>\n<li>Perimeter barriers are usually in, or near, the <strong>inner fence</strong> of the clear zone.</li>\n<li>That way the intruder must first cross the detection zone before being able to tamper with or defeat the barrier.</li>\n<li>Result: the response begins <strong>before</strong> the delay begins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Procedures</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>An effective system integrates <strong>people, procedures, and equipment</strong>.</li>\n<li>Establish installation, maintenance, testing, and operating procedures <strong>before</strong> training the operators.</li>\n<li>Current and new personnel need training in them, to learn the basics and stay up to date.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Manufacturer's instructions</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are a starting point. Many manufacturers serve high- and low-security markets, so it is advisable to go beyond them to optimize.</li>\n<li>Example: several microwave manufacturers say they operate with up to <strong>300 yd (274 m)</strong> of separation; at that distance they do not detect someone crawling.</li>\n<li>Example: many fence sensor manufacturers suggest using them around a corner; that zone is not practical to assess.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Maintenance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All sensors and components need periodic maintenance:<ul>\n<li>Calibration.</li>\n<li>Sensitivity checks.</li>\n<li>Alignment.</li>\n<li>Visual inspection.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Poor maintenance affects the PD and NAR and can make the system more vulnerable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Operational tests</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Regular, day and night, to verify performance against the design basis threat (DBT).</li>\n<li>Test with adversaries at various speeds: walking or running (slow and fast) and crawling.</li>\n<li>Also test how slowly an object can move and still be detected.</li>\n<li>Ideally, standardized tests; each type of sensor needs its method:<ul>\n<li>Microwave: an <strong>aluminum sphere</strong> simulates a crawling intruder; it gives more accurate and repeatable results than actually crawling.</li>\n<li>Infrared: measure the <strong>attenuation factors</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contingency plans</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>For when a sensor or other equipment is lost.</li>\n<li>They must say when they apply; example: if two of the three perimeter sensors are lost.</li>\n<li>If they include compensatory measures (portable sensors, security officers), they must specify them.</li>\n<li>Procedures defined in advance and always at hand for the operators.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key documentation that site personnel must gather, keep, and maintain</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Required, recommended, and troubleshooting procedures.</li>\n<li>Maintenance logs for each sensor.</li>\n<li>Training records for all employees.</li>\n<li>Results of one-of-a-kind cases, such as the causes of any false alarm.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.3.4 Interior Sensors</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are very effective against the <strong>insider threat</strong> if combined with administrative procedures, access control, and material monitoring.</li>\n<li>Properly located, installed, maintained, and tested, they alarm on unauthorized acts or unauthorized presence by insiders and outsiders.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Performance criteria</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>As with exterior sensors, clear, measurable specifications are needed.</li>\n<li>Example specification: a volumetric sensor must detect a person moving at <strong>1 ft/s (0.3 m/s) or more</strong> throughout its field of view.</li>\n<li>Example from the <strong>UL 681</strong> standard: detect after <strong>3 steps</strong>, at <strong>1 step per second</strong>, with steps of <strong>30 in</strong>, in <strong>3 of 4</strong> walk test patterns, from any point in the covered area.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> later (volumetric detection) the book cites the same UL 681 with <strong>4 steps</strong> and detection 3 out of 4 times. It contradicts itself: 3 steps versus 4 steps.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Nuisance / false alarms</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nuisance alarm</td>\n<td>Alarm not caused by an intruder. Sources indoors: electromagnetic, acoustic, thermal, meteorological, seismic, optical, and fauna (birds, insects, and other animals).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>False alarm</td>\n<td>Type of nuisance alarm generated by the equipment itself: poor design, inadequate maintenance, or component failure.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Each type of sensor has a different sensitivity to these sources.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Vulnerability to insiders</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The interior system is vulnerable to attacks by outsiders and insiders.</li>\n<li>The insider has authorized access: many exterior perimeter sensors are not on their route. Interior ones can detect their theft or sabotage.</li>\n<li>During working hours the sensors are usually in <strong>access mode</strong>: their alarms are temporarily masked and do not appear at the monitoring station. This makes insider tampering easier.</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance</strong> personnel probably have the greatest opportunity and technical ability to compromise sensors or the entire system. Other employees may have them too.</li>\n<li>What an insider with technical knowledge can do:<ul>\n<li>lower the sensor's sensitivity;</li>\n<li>shift its coverage area;</li>\n<li>change the characteristics of a zone.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>They do not disable the sensor entirely, but open a <strong>gap in detection</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Classification</h5>\n<p>The book classifies interior sensors in four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>active or passive;</li>\n<li>covert or visible;</li>\n<li>volumetric or line detection;</li>\n<li>application.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Advantages and notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Active or passive</td>\n<td>Active</td>\n<td>A transmitter emits a signal and a receiver detects its changes or reflections. It generates an energy field.</td>\n<td>Bistatic: transmitter and receiver separate. Monostatic: together. A very sophisticated adversary can detect the field before entering the zone.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Active or passive</td>\n<td>Passive</td>\n<td>It does not emit; it only receives nearby energy: vibration (person walking, truck), infrared (human, hot object), sound of a destructive break-in, or mechanical change in the sensor (simple electromechanical ones).</td>\n<td>Harder to locate: puts the intruder at a disadvantage. Safer with vapors or explosive materials: it emits no energy that could detonate them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Covert or visible</td>\n<td>Covert</td>\n<td>Hidden: inside walls or under the floor.</td>\n<td>Harder to detect and locate, therefore more effective. It does not alter the aesthetics of the place.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Covert or visible</td>\n<td>Visible</td>\n<td>In plain view: on a door or other supporting structure.</td>\n<td>Can deter. Easier to install and repair.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Volumetric or line</td>\n<td>Volumetric</td>\n<td>Protects all or part of the volume of a room or building. Examples: ultrasonic, microwave, passive infrared.</td>\n<td>Detects the intruder in the zone regardless of how they entered, if the application and mounting are correct.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Volumetric or line</td>\n<td>Line</td>\n<td>Detects only at a point or very narrow strip: forced entry through doors, windows, or walls.</td>\n<td>Only detects if the intruder violates that specific entry point.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says that volumetric sensors have a “higher partial discharge.” It appears to be an erratum; from context it should say higher probability of detection.</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>Classes by application</h6>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>What it detects</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Boundary-penetration sensors</td>\n<td>The penetration of the boundary of an interior area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interior motion sensors</td>\n<td>The movement of the intruder within a confined interior area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Proximity sensors</td>\n<td>The intruder next to an object in an interior area, or when they touch it.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Some sensors can be applied in several ways.</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>Four types of interior motion detection according to UL 681</h6>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it covers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Trap detection</td>\n<td>Narrow or wide detection in high-traffic zones or the intruder's expected routes to get from A to B.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spot detection</td>\n<td>A high-value object or zone: safes, vaults, warehouses, money rooms.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Channel detection</td>\n<td>Narrow detection where the intruder is expected to cross.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Volumetric detection</td>\n<td>Wide detection in a defined area. It is adjusted to detect a person at 1 step per second, in 4 steps, with detection 3 out of 4 times.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Technologies</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The book groups the technologies by application.</li>\n<li>Sources it cites for more technical detail: Barnard (1988); Cumming (1992); Fennelly (2013); Garcia (2006 and 2008); Patterson (2013); Rodriguez, Dry, and Matter (1991); Zwirn (2014).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Boundary-Penetration Sensors</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The most common: <strong>vibration</strong> and <strong>electromechanical</strong>.</li>\n<li>They best protect roofs, floors, walls, and doors.</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>Vibration sensors</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>Passive and line; visible or covert.</li>\n<li>They detect the movement of the surface they are attached to.</li>\n<li>A blow makes the surface vibrate at a frequency of its own construction. The tool used also influences the frequency.</li>\n<li>They give <strong>early warning</strong> of a forced entry.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Typical frequencies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Jiggle switch</td>\n<td>The simplest; the book does not detail its operation.</td>\n<td>The book does not give it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inertial switch</td>\n<td>Metal ball on metal contacts. When the sensor body moves, inertia separates the ball for an instant and it alarms.</td>\n<td><strong>2–5 kHz</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Piezoelectric sensor</td>\n<td>The element moves relative to the sensor's mass. When it flexes it generates a voltage that is analyzed.</td>\n<td><strong>5–50 kHz</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Glass-break detectors</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Glass-mounted (shock)</td>\n<td>Vibration or shock sensor, piezoelectric or inertial, on the glass or its mounting surface. It alarms on the frequencies of broken glass.</td>\n<td>Must be suitable for the type of glazing: some do not generate the right frequency. Consider aftermarket additions: plastic anti-theft laminates (security film) or solar film.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Active glass-break</td>\n<td>It introduces a vibration into the glass; another transducer listens for it at another point. When the glass breaks, the signal changes and it alarms.</td>\n<td>More expensive, but with fewer nuisance alarms.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acoustic (audio glass break), simple or dual</td>\n<td>Microphone on a wall or ceiling that listens for the frequency of broken glass (Figure 11.2). It needs a general direct line of sight to the fixed or movable glass.</td>\n<td>Typical range <strong>25–40 ft</strong>, but it should never be so far from the protected glass.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dual-technology acoustic</td>\n<td>Requires two signals: an ultra-low frequency (like striking the glass) and an ultra-high one (like breaking it).</td>\n<td>It is the dual version of the acoustic.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Key points of the acoustic:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Choose only <strong>UL-listed</strong> equipment; there are unlisted products that look the same.</li>\n<li>Curtains, covers, and blinds dampen sound: the detector does not hear it.</li>\n<li>Furniture in front of the glass creates an acoustic barrier.</li>\n<li>Respect the manufacturer's glass types: double insulated, plate, laminated, tempered, or with security film.</li>\n<li>To reduce false alarms: <strong>discrimination algorithms</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fiber-optic intrusion sensors (vibration)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Passive and line; visible or covert.</li>\n<li>They detect <strong>micro-bending</strong> of the cable from surface vibration.</li>\n<li>The processing unit sends light through the cable and receives it at the other end. Micro-bending changes the received light.</li>\n<li>Typical settings: low- and high-frequency filter, amplitude filter, and pulse duration and count. They seek to reduce sensitivity to nuisance sources.</li>\n<li>Before choosing it, consider vibrations from nearby machinery, vehicles, trains, and air traffic.</li>\n<li>Filtering those frequencies is possible, but may reduce sensitivity to intrusions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>When to use vibration sensors</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation of the structure</th>\n<th>Decision</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Exposed to external vibrations</td>\n<td>Risk of nuisance alarms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Severe vibrations</td>\n<td>Do not use vibration sensors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Only occasional impacts</td>\n<td>Use with a <strong>pulse accumulator / count circuit</strong>: it tolerates a limited number of impacts before alarming</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h6>Electromechanical sensors</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>Passive, visible, and line.</li>\n<li>The most common: magnetic switch or contact on doors and windows.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sensor</th>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Strengths and weaknesses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple magnetic switch</td>\n<td>Switch unit with a <strong>reed switch</strong> on the fixed part. Magnet unit with a permanent magnet on the moving part. Closed: the field keeps the switch closed (secure). When it opens the field drops and it goes to open (alarm).</td>\n<td>Easily defeated by bringing a strong magnet near the switch, which forces it to the secure position.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>BMS</strong> (balanced magnetic switch)</td>\n<td>It has an adjustable <strong>bias magnet</strong> in the switch unit. It detects changes in the strength of the magnetic field.</td>\n<td>Prevents spoofing with an external magnet. Protects more than magnetic or mechanical contacts and tilt switches.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hall effect switch</strong></td>\n<td>Fully electronic, with no mechanical reeds; <strong>requires power</strong>. It measures and monitors the field strength of the magnet unit. It alarms if it detects enough field change.</td>\n<td>More security than the BMS. Harder to tamper with and defeat.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Anti-tamper alternatives to the BMS:<ul>\n<li>several reed switches and several magnets;</li>\n<li>fuses and voltage breakdown sensing devices;</li>\n<li>shielded case.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Some come with internal electromagnets for self-test. Their complexity increases that of the equipment and reduces its vulnerability.</li>\n<li>Hall effect: a current-carrying conductor exposed to an external magnetic field accumulates charges on one side. The amount and polarity of that separation are proportional to the strength and polarity of the field. It is measured between the sides of a metal strip.</li>\n<li>Scale of difficulty for the intruder: simple contact → BMS → Hall effect. Each step requires more knowledge.</li>\n<li>BMS and Hall protect better than the simple contact against internal tampering and spoofing.</li>\n<li>Limit: the protection is only as good as the penetration resistance of the door or window. They only alarm if it is opened. Cutting through the leaf avoids the BMS.</li>\n<li>Example design criterion for a BMS: alarm if the door separates <strong>1 in (2.5 cm) or more</strong> from the jamb. <strong>Do not</strong> alarm at <strong>0.5 in (1.3 cm) or less</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>UL 634, 9th edition (2007): high security switches</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>HSS1 (Level One high security switch)</td>\n<td>What used to be called a BMS</td>\n<td>May remain in an existing SCIF. If modified or upgraded, it must be changed to HSS2 to comply.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HSS2 (Level Two high security switch)</td>\n<td>Higher level, much harder to defeat and with additional requirements</td>\n<td>Mandatory in new high-security IDS (intrusion detection system) applications, such as a SCIF.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>SCIF = Sensitive Compartmented Information Facility, a facility for compartmented classified information (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Continuity / break wire sensor</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fine conductive wires and electronics: it alarms when the conductor breaks.</li>\n<li>It is attached to or embedded in walls, ceilings, or floors to detect penetration.</li>\n<li>The wires can form any pattern, even for irregular areas. They also exist on printed circuit.</li>\n<li>Break wire grids and screens for forced penetrations in: ventilation grilles, floors, walls, ceilings, closed cabinets, vaults, and skylights.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Weaknesses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Few nuisance alarms: the wire has to break</td>\n<td>Defeated with a jumper around the cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adapts to any shape</td>\n<td>Defeated by moving the wire aside to pass</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>With electrical supervision tampering is reduced (recommended)</td>\n<td>Only detects if there is a break or cut</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Fiber-optic version: same principle. The fiber must break or be damaged until the light is cut or greatly reduced.</li>\n<li>It is different from, and much simpler than, vibration fiber optic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Interior Motion Sensors</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>By far the most common: <strong>monostatic microwave</strong> and <strong>passive infrared (PIR)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>Microwave sensors</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>Active, visible, and volumetric.</li>\n<li>They create an energy field, normally on the order of <strong>10.5 GHz</strong>.</li>\n<li>Indoors they are almost always <strong>monostatic</strong>: a single antenna transmits and receives.</li>\n<li>They detect through the <strong>Doppler shift</strong> between the emitted and received signal, caused by a moving object.</li>\n<li>To alarm, the Doppler change needs sufficient amplitude and duration.</li>\n<li>If a higher or lower frequency comes back, the target is approaching or receding.</li>\n<li>According to the book, most operate in the <strong>K band</strong> and trigger radar detectors of that band.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> 10.5 GHz is X band; the K band is around 24 GHz (outside the book). For the exam, stay with what the book says.</li>\n<li>Optimal detection: target that <strong>approaches or recedes</strong> from the sensor, not one that crosses the zone. Orient it to force that movement.</li>\n<li>Mount it <strong>near the ceiling</strong> of the protected area.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Detection zone (Figure 11.3)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elongated balloon</strong> shape, defined by the antenna: microwave horn, flat printed-circuit, or phased array.</li>\n<li>The manufacturer's pattern may not be the real one. If the target falls in the concave part of the real pattern, it can be defeated.</li>\n<li>Some are defeated by crawling slowly and close to the floor. This happens if the pattern was raised so that foliage or rodents do not alarm.</li>\n<li>It passes through most glass, plaster, gypsum, plywood, and other common wall materials.<ul>\n<li>Advantage: it detects through partitions within the protected volume.</li>\n<li>Disadvantage: it detects movement outside the area or building, with nuisance alarms.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Metal objects (large bookcases, desks, monitors, fences): create shadow zones and incomplete coverage, or nuisance alarms from reflection.</li>\n<li>Because it reflects, metal can also improve detection in shadow zones. Take care that it does not reflect energy toward zones that must be avoided.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages (in addition to penetration and metals)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Invisible and inaudible pattern</td>\n<td>Requires fully rigid mounting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reliable and low maintenance</td>\n<td>Susceptible to pattern drift</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low cost per area covered</td>\n<td>Tends to reflect off metal objects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High probability of detection</td>\n<td>Requires special care in light construction (glass, gypsum, wood)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Immune to high air turbulence and to changes in temperature and humidity</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Variety of detection patterns</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Use and installation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>As a spot sensor: limited coverage of a point where other sensors cover poorly or are vulnerable to tampering.</li>\n<li>Typical use of the monostatic: automatic door openers in supermarkets and airports.</li>\n<li>Mounting: near the ceiling, aimed at the desired coverage and away from reflecting metals.</li>\n<li>Several in the same area: <strong>different frequencies</strong>, or they interfere and alarm nonstop.</li>\n<li>Sources of nuisance alarms:<ul>\n<li>movement of nonhuman objects inside and outside the zone;</li>\n<li>small animals or birds;</li>\n<li>vibration from poor installation or mounting;</li>\n<li><strong>fluorescent lamps</strong>: their ionized gas reflects microwaves at the rate of <strong>60 Hz</strong>. They must not be in the zone; some models filter that Doppler.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Vulnerabilities:<ul>\n<li>slow targets;</li>\n<li>absorption or reflection of the energy;</li>\n<li>blocking of the field of view (stacked boxes, moved furniture);</li>\n<li>movement along the circumference of the pattern.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>More sensitive to approaching and receding movement; less to crosswalk. Choose the mounting with that in mind.</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>Passive infrared sensors, PIR</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>Passive (they do not transmit), visible, and volumetric.</li>\n<li>They respond to changes in the energy an intruder emits in their field of view. It is roughly equivalent to the heat of a <strong>50 W bulb</strong>.</li>\n<li>Under certain conditions they detect changes in the background thermal energy if someone hides in it. Relying on that is not recommended: their optics and electronics limit detection to moving sources.</li>\n<li>They only detect what they see in their coverage pattern.</li>\n<li>Most effective with movement that <strong>crosses</strong> the pattern (crosswalk): the target must enter and leave several segments. Less sensitive to approaching and receding.</li>\n<li><strong>Frequent walk tests</strong> are recommended.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Four characteristics of infrared radiation:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>All objects emit it in proportion to their temperature.</li>\n<li>It is transmitted without physical contact between the emitting and receiving surface.</li>\n<li>It heats the receiving surface; any device sensitive to temperature changes detects it.</li>\n<li>It is invisible to the human eye.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>PIR response band according to the book: <strong>8 to 14 nanometers (nm)</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> erratum. The thermal infrared of the human body is at 8–14 <strong>micrometers (µm)</strong>, not nanometers (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How it works</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thermopile</strong> or <strong>pyroelectric</strong> detector: it receives the intruder's radiation and converts it into an electrical signal.</li>\n<li>The signal is amplified and passes through logic circuits that usually require the source to move in the field of view.</li>\n<li>If the signal is strong and the required movement is present, it alarms.</li>\n<li>It detects by the temperature difference between intruder and background: <strong>minimum resolvable temperature (MRT)</strong>. Some manufacturers indicate an MRT of only <strong>1 °C (1.8 °F)</strong>.</li>\n<li>A high ambient temperature affects detection, depending on the mounting and the intruder's speed and direction.</li>\n<li>Pyroelectric: certain dielectric materials of low crystal symmetry exhibit spontaneous dielectric polarization.</li>\n<li>The energy reaches the detector through <strong>segmented parabolic mirrors</strong> or <strong>Fresnel lenses</strong>.<ul>\n<li>A single long cone (single-segment): protects corridors.</li>\n<li>Several segments (multisegment): protects large open areas.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>As with microwave, the pattern does not have a perfect shape: take care when locating it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Nuisance alarms and installation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Birds and flying insects: they block the background energy; if their movement meets the alarm criterion, it alarms. Also an insect walking on the lens.</li>\n<li>Infrared does not pass through most materials, nor glass. What is outside the building does not usually cause alarms.</li>\n<li>But sun coming in through windows heats interior surfaces, which radiate in that band.</li>\n<li>Install it away from heat sources: radiators, heaters, hot pipes, or other heating elements. They create thermal gradients in front of the lens that alter the background.</li>\n<li>An unshaded incandescent bulb at <strong>3–5 yards (2.7–4.7 m)</strong> can alarm if it burns out or goes off in a power outage. Exam note: 5 yards is about 4.6 m, the book gives 4.7 m (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fully passive technology</td>\n<td>Moderate sensitivity to vibration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Well-defined detection zones</td>\n<td>Sensitivity varies with ambient temperature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No interaction among several units</td>\n<td>Operates by line of sight; field is easy to block</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low to moderate cost</td>\n<td>Possible nuisance alarms from rapid temperature changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relatively few nuisance alarms</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Microwave versus PIR</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Microwave</th>\n<th>PIR</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Classification</td>\n<td>Active, visible, volumetric</td>\n<td>Passive, visible, volumetric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Movement it detects best</td>\n<td>Approaching or receding from the sensor</td>\n<td>Crossing the pattern (crosswalk)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Passes through glass and light walls</td>\n<td>Yes (advantage and source of alarms)</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Several units in the same area</td>\n<td>Need different frequencies</td>\n<td>They do not interact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical alarm source</td>\n<td>Fluorescents, metals, moving objects</td>\n<td>Heat sources, sun, birds and insects</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h6>Photoelectric beam</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>Also called <strong>active infrared sensor</strong>.</li>\n<li>Active: it transmits infrared light to a photocell and alarms when the beam is interrupted.</li>\n<li>Depending on the application, it works as a perimeter or space sensor.</li>\n<li>Ideal in long, straight, defined areas, for example across corridors.</li>\n<li>Also as backup on several overhead doors or skylights.</li>\n<li>It exists in dual-beam so that birds do not cause nuisance alarms.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Dual-Technology Sensors</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Active and passive at once, visible, and volumetric.</li>\n<li>They seek <strong>absolute alarm confirmation</strong> without losing a high probability of detection (PD).</li>\n<li>Ideal: two technologies, each with a high PD and <strong>no shared susceptibilities</strong> to nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>The usual today: <strong>microwave + PIR</strong> combined in <strong>AND-gate</strong> logic. It only alarms if both alarm almost at the same time.</li>\n<li>Well applied, and if each has a low nuisance alarm rate (NAR), they usually have a lower NAR than a single-technology sensor.</li>\n<li>Drawback: with AND, the combined PD is <strong>lower</strong> than that of each sensor. It is the product of both.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9694em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span><span class=\"mpunct mtight\">,</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">mi</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">cr</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">o</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0269em;\">w</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">a</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">e</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9694em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span><span class=\"mpunct mtight\">,</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">I</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.95</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.95</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.90</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>The book rounds 0.9025 to <strong>0.90</strong>.</li>\n<li>In addition, microwave detects approach better and PIR detects crossing better. In a single housing the PD is lower than with two sensors mounted <strong>perpendicular</strong> to each other, with overlapping patterns.</li>\n<li>To optimize the PD: sensors <strong>mounted separately but combined logically</strong>.</li>\n<li>In high security: <strong>never</strong> a single dual sensor in place of two separate sensors.</li>\n<li>If duals are used, install several units that cover each other.</li>\n<li>Many government standards <strong>do not allow</strong> dual sensors in high security, such as a SCIF or other classified spaces.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Video Motion Detection</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>VMD (video motion detector)</strong>: <strong>passive</strong> sensor that processes the signal from a video surveillance camera.</li>\n<li>A single camera serves for detection, assessment, and surveillance.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Analog</td>\n<td>Monitors the signal and detects changes in brightness. The detection area (percentage of the camera's field) varies greatly. It generates an external alarm if brightness or other conditions (for example, time) are met. It highlights on the monitor the section where it detected.</td>\n<td>Cheaper than digital.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Digital</td>\n<td>Digitizes the signal. Divides the scene into zones, elements, or cells and processes each one. It monitors: change in brightness or contrast; logical movement between neighboring cells; speed between cells; size of objects in the cells; global changes in almost all the cells.</td>\n<td>More sophisticated and with fewer nuisance alarms. Better distinguishes intruder from nuisance source, which broadens its use.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Digital performs well indoors if the nuisance sources are analyzed and there is good lighting, good cameras, and good transmission equipment.</li>\n<li>Indoor nuisance sources: insects on or near the lens, flickering lights, pets, birds, and rats.</li>\n<li>The camera matters: low-contrast output reduces detection; a lot of noise causes nuisance alarms. It needs sufficient, uniform light.</li>\n<li>Any change in brightness can alarm. Flickering lights and camera movements raise the nuisance alarm rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Very slow</strong> movement defeats most VMDs.</li>\n<li>Indoors they work well because there is no snow, fog, traffic, or clouds.</li>\n<li>Tests before installing:<ul>\n<li><strong>low-profile</strong> target, such as a person crawling;</li>\n<li>fast high-profile targets, such as people walking or running;</li>\n<li>always in the <strong>lowest-contrast</strong> lighting condition expected.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Factors for selecting a VMD:<ul>\n<li>constant, controlled lighting (no flicker);</li>\n<li>camera vibration;</li>\n<li>objects that create blind zones;</li>\n<li>moving objects, such as fans, curtains, and small animals;</li>\n<li>changing sun or shadows coming in through windows or doors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Proximity Sensors</h5>\n<p>Two types: pressure mats and capacitance sensors.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sensor</th>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Status and use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Pressure mat</td>\n<td>Under the carpet next to doors, on steps, or at other strategic points. It alarmed with a weight of <strong>5–20 lb/ft² (24.4–97.6 kg/m²)</strong> on the surface.</td>\n<td>Practically obsolete; replaced mainly by PIR. New installations are rare; some remain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weight transducer (variant of the mat)</td>\n<td>With other detectors, it controls the number of people or the gross weight allowed in a portal. At a card or keypad access it prevents anyone exceeding a programmed weight from passing.</td>\n<td>Still in use. With distributed databases and a unique PIN or card, it stores the holder's actual weight ± a margin. When the PIN is entered or the card is swiped, the software compares the measured weight.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacitance sensor</td>\n<td>Large electrical capacitor that radiates energy and detects changes in the capacitive coupling between an antenna and ground. The wire is connected to the protected object (safe, file cabinet). Anyone who touches it absorbs energy, alters the circuit, and it alarms.</td>\n<td>Many replaced by PIR, which detect the intruder much earlier. Still the preferred option if detection must be limited to <strong>only the protected object</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Wireless Sensors</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The most common: <strong>radio frequency (RF)</strong>.</li>\n<li>In the U.S. they usually operate in the <strong>300 MHz or 900 MHz</strong> bands. Some use <strong>spread-spectrum</strong>.</li>\n<li>Components: sensor/transmitter units and a receiver.<ul>\n<li>Sensor/transmitter: sensor and transmitter in a single package, with a battery.</li>\n<li>Advertised battery life: <strong>2 to 5 years</strong>, depending on the number of alarms and transmissions.</li>\n<li>Each unit carries a <strong>unique identification code</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each system has its range and its maximum number of sensors per receiver.</li>\n<li>Receiver outputs: RS-232, logic levels, or relay contact.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>To save battery, the transmitter stays at rest (sleep mode) until an event requires transmitting.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Event</th>\n<th>When it is transmitted</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Alarm</td>\n<td>When it occurs</td>\n<td>Alarm notice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tamper</td>\n<td>When it occurs</td>\n<td>Tamper notice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State-of-health</td>\n<td>At fixed intervals</td>\n<td>Battery, alarm, and tamper status; confirms that the sensor is still present and operating</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>If the receiver does not get a state-of-health message on time, it indicates a <strong>fault</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Universal transmitter</strong>: in wireless and hybrid systems it allows connecting a wired device that does not require power, such as an alarm contact.</li>\n<li>Wiring between the universal transmitter and the wired circuit: <strong>maximum 3 ft</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>RF problem</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Collision</td>\n<td>Several signals arrive at once (for example, state-of-health) and the receiver reads none of them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signal fade</td>\n<td>Excessive distance or a path blocked by material that shields RF: large metal objects or metal cladding on the building.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interference</td>\n<td>Other RF sources in the same band exceed the sensor's signal.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Mitigation: spread-spectrum transmission and random dithering of the timing of state-of-health messages.</li>\n<li>Before installing: verify a good transmission path, away from sources of interference.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Other Interior Sensor Concepts</h5>\n<h6>Environmental Conditions</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>They generate noise in the same energy spectrum the sensor is looking for. They degrade its performance and can cause alarms with no intruder.</li>\n<li>They are often the cause of vulnerabilities: the technology must be chosen and operated well.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Sources</th>\n<th>Effect and mitigation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Electromagnetic energy</td>\n<td>Lightning; electrical distribution lines and equipment; radio transmissions; telephone lines and equipment; lighting; computer and data processing equipment; electric vehicles (forklifts, elevators); TV equipment; car ignition; electrical machinery; intercom and paging; aircraft.</td>\n<td>Wood and concrete do not shield: there may be a lot of external background energy. Mitigate with electromagnetic shielding of all components (including data links) and a common, proper electrical ground.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nuclear radiation</td>\n<td>Environments with radiation.</td>\n<td>Damages components such as semiconductors. Total invulnerability is not achieved; design, components, and shielding reduce the risk. Neutrons degrade semiconductors and integrated circuits according to the total dose.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acoustic energy</td>\n<td>Inside or outside the area: meteorological phenomena; HVAC (heating, ventilation, and air conditioning); air compressors; TV equipment; electronic telephone equipment; aircraft; vehicles; trains.</td>\n<td>Affects the performance of interior sensors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thermal environment</td>\n<td>Climate; HVAC; machinery that produces heat; interior lighting; chemical and radioactive reactions; sun variation through windows and skylights.</td>\n<td>Uneven temperature, air movement, and expansion and contraction of the building.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Optical phenomena</td>\n<td>Sunlight; interior lighting; highly reflective surfaces; infrared and ultraviolet energy from other equipment.</td>\n<td>Light energy that affects the sensor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seismic phenomena</td>\n<td>Earth tremors; machines or equipment; vehicular traffic; trains; thunder; strong winds.</td>\n<td>They produce vibrations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meteorological phenomena</td>\n<td>Lightning; thunder; rain; hail; temperature; wind; earth tremors; high relative humidity; sunlight.</td>\n<td>They adversely affect interior sensors.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h6>Sensor Selection</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>Before choosing, consider the interaction among <strong>equipment, environment, and potential intruders</strong>.</li>\n<li>Two key physical conditions:<ul>\n<li>the <strong>construction</strong> of the building or room;</li>\n<li>the <strong>equipment and objects</strong> that occupy the space.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Interiors are usually controlled, predictable, and measurable: there are almost always sensors that perform well there.</li>\n<li>Consider the sources of nuisance alarms present, especially if motion detectors are used.</li>\n<li>With the right combination of sensors and technologies, optimal performance is achieved.</li>\n<li>Useful summary of operational topics: <strong>Adams (1996)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>Procedures</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>They increase the effectiveness of the system:<ul>\n<li>two-person rule;</li>\n<li>sensor effectiveness tests;</li>\n<li>good maintenance and documentation practices.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>When buying: choose the sensors closest to the performance goals and protection requirements, and compatible with future systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Two-person rule</strong>: two knowledgeable people take part in an activity. It prevents a single insider from compromising security.<ul>\n<li>It applies to granting access within the site and to handling critical assets, information, or equipment.</li>\n<li>Each person must be able to detect the other's tampering.</li>\n<li>It fails if it is relaxed because of friendship or a long relationship between them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>Testing Mechanisms</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>It is advisable for the sensor to have an audible or visible alarm indicator, recognizable at <strong>10–35 ft (3–10 m)</strong>. It must be deactivated during normal operation.</li>\n<li><strong>Walk tests</strong>: daily at first; later they are spaced out if they go well.</li>\n<li>Test the performance of <strong>all</strong> sensors after any maintenance.</li>\n<li>Sensitivity analysis or effectiveness test: confirms performance, verifies coverage, and looks for blind zones from changes in the room layout.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-test</strong>, as separate equipment or integrated: it makes it possible to test the sensor and alarm communication often.</li>\n<li>The self-test must be activated <strong>randomly</strong> (Graham and Workhoven, 1987).</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>Installation and Maintenance</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>At a minimum, according to the manufacturer's specifications. It can be optimized beyond them.</li>\n<li>Periodic inspection: check the required configuration and specifications, and look for alterations or modifications.</li>\n<li>Acceptance tests, operational tests, and maintenance logs per piece of equipment indicate how many and which spares to keep.</li>\n<li>Spares: inspect them carefully before installing them and store them securely to deter tampering.</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>Inspection</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>Inspect the sensors after any maintenance.</li>\n<li>After maintenance of the data-collection control panel: walk test of all its sensors.</li>\n<li>Require <strong>prior approval by security personnel</strong> for plant modification plans, such as:<ul>\n<li>relocating the detectors;</li>\n<li>adding objects that cause nuisance alarms;</li>\n<li>moving large objects within the protected area.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>After a remodel, the sensitivity of the detectors may need to be readjusted.</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>Documentation</h6>\n<ul>\n<li>Have available:<ul>\n<li>theory of operation of the equipment;</li>\n<li>functional block diagrams;</li>\n<li>wiring diagrams;</li>\n<li>schematics;</li>\n<li>parts lists with the manufacturer's number and its commercial equivalent.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>With the maintenance logs, reliability is monitored and problem components or areas are detected.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>System Integration</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Definition: the process of combining <strong>technological elements, procedures, and personnel</strong> into a single facility security system.</li>\n<li>It requires balance among hardware, personnel, and operating procedures.</li>\n<li>Interior sensors, like exterior ones, are integrated with:<ul>\n<li>the display and control subsystem;</li>\n<li>the access control subsystem;</li>\n<li>the delay mechanisms.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Integration must include:<ul>\n<li>protection-in-depth;</li>\n<li>balance on all routes into the facility;</li>\n<li>backup systems and contingency plans.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Line Supervision</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Definition: a way to <strong>monitor the communication link</strong> between the sensor and the alarm control center.</li>\n<li>Supervised lines and continuous monitoring of tamper switches help against the insider threat.</li>\n<li>Better still if each tamper is on its own <strong>24-hour zone</strong>: it gives notice of tampering even if the system is disarmed.</li>\n<li>Supervision techniques the designer must know:<ul>\n<li>reverse polarity;</li>\n<li>sound monitoring;</li>\n<li>radio class C;</li>\n<li>steady direct current class B;</li>\n<li>tone;</li>\n<li>digital classes A and AB.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Off-site communication path</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Digital dialer</td>\n<td>The most common method in IDS monitored off site. It uses the existing telephone line. Cellular or radio backup is recommended: most intruders cut the line.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IP network transmitter</td>\n<td>Rapidly growing technology. It requires a network connection. It adds encryption and instantly transmits alarm and trouble signals.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Several sensors on a single alarm processor: line supervision is needed between the processor and <strong>each</strong> sensor.</li>\n<li>Recommended method: <strong>end-of-line (EOL)</strong> device, normally a resistor of the value the manufacturer indicates.</li>\n<li>The EOL goes in the circuit <strong>next to the sensor, in the field</strong>, <strong>not at the</strong> IDS control <strong>panel</strong>.</li>\n<li>Liability risk: on some panels, a short in the data or power bus, intentional or accidental, can shut down the entire system depending on where it occurs.</li>\n<li>After that short, the panel may stop communicating off site or stop working properly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Summary of 11.3 (closing of the section in the book)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Exterior and interior sensors were analyzed along four axes:<ul>\n<li>classification and application;</li>\n<li>probability of detection;</li>\n<li>nuisance alarm rate;</li>\n<li>vulnerability to defeat.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Subsystem</th>\n<th>What the designer must consider when integrating sensors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Exterior (perimeter)</td>\n<td>Specific design goals; effects of physical and environmental conditions; interaction with a balanced, integrated PPS (physical protection system). Desired features: clear zone; good sensor configuration in it; combination and alarm priority schemes; tamper protection; self-test capability. Site-specific design, well integrated with the video and barrier subsystems.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interior</td>\n<td>Skill level of the intruder; design goals; effects of environmental conditions; interaction with a balanced, integrated PPS.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Principles of a good IDS:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>protection-in-depth;</li>\n<li>complementary sensors;</li>\n<li>eliminate single-point failures;</li>\n<li>minimum consequence on failure of a component;</li>\n<li>balanced protection;</li>\n<li>integration of people, equipment, and procedures.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Follow the manufacturer's requirements and recommendations, and proven standards, listings, and codes such as <strong>UL</strong> and <strong>NFPA 70</strong>. That way the IDS performs well, is more reliable, and complies with regulations.</li>\n<li>NFPA 70 is the U.S. National Electrical Code (outside the book).</li>\n<li>Sensors and subsystems must offer:<ul>\n<li>high probability of detection;</li>\n<li>low nuisance alarm rate;</li>\n<li>low vulnerability to the defined threat.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other desirable features:<ul>\n<li>fast communication to send and assess alarms;</li>\n<li>good lighting and good assessment systems;</li>\n<li>balanced system with adequate protection on all routes to the target's perimeter.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-11-e3\">11.4 Video Surveillance</h3><ul>\n<li>According to the Partner Alliance for Safer Schools (PASS), video surveillance is an integral part of the physical security plan.</li>\n<li>It provides <strong>deterrence</strong>, <strong>detection</strong>, and, in advanced implementations, <strong>enhanced response</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is a <strong>dual-purpose</strong> solution:<ul>\n<li>Primary use: physical security.</li>\n<li>Secondary use: business applications (growing with hardware and software analytics).</li>\n<li>The primary security function <strong>always takes priority</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Uses of video surveillance</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Surveillance</td>\n<td>Monitoring video in real time, manually or automated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assessment</td>\n<td>Viewing recorded video to assess something that is happening or has just happened; verifying active threats.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Forensics</td>\n<td>Using recorded video as a record of what happened; it serves as evidence of illicit activity. It answers who, what, when, and where, after the fact.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk Mitigation</td>\n<td>Using video analytics to proactively warn that an event is occurring. Proactive detection and prevention system.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Main elements of a video surveillance system</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Field of view</td>\n<td>Area visible through the lens.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scene</td>\n<td>Place or activity to be observed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lens</td>\n<td>Determines the sharpness and the size of the field of view.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Camera</td>\n<td>Converts the optical image from the lens into an electronic signal for transmission. It requires a mounting support and, at times, a housing against vandalism or weather (rain, snow).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transmission medium</td>\n<td>Carries the signal to the display or recording equipment: coaxial cable, twisted pair, network cable, fiber optic, or radio frequency.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Workstation</td>\n<td>Client equipment with monitor, computer, and keyboard/mouse or other controls. Fixed or mobile. It displays one or several images; it allows live viewing, playback, alarm management, and control of capture and other devices.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recording equipment</td>\n<td>Recorders and equipment for choosing which images are recorded, at what speed, at what resolution, and with what compression format.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>From CCTV to VSS</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It used to be called <strong>CCTV</strong> (closed-circuit television).</li>\n<li>Today the correct term is <strong>VSS</strong> (video surveillance system).</li>\n<li>Reason: the circuit is no longer closed and a television is no longer used for viewing.</li>\n<li>The shift from analog to digital changed the basis of design.</li>\n<li>The application theory of video surveillance remains the same, even with more pixels, smaller components, and more reliability.</li>\n<li>The integrated VSS must be <strong>tested before being installed</strong>: verify that it picks up the alarm sensors fast enough to see the source of the intrusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key design points for the security manager</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The VSS is above all a <strong>visual tool</strong> for security and is applied as such.</li>\n<li>The primary security use governs the design. Business uses are allowed only if they do not interfere with it.</li>\n<li><strong>The application dictates the equipment, not the other way around.</strong></li>\n<li>Conduct periodic reviews and needs assessments to confirm the system still meets the site's needs.</li>\n<li>Always design with future growth or changes in the application in mind.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.4.1 Functional Requirements</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>First the purpose of the system is defined; for example, with the <strong>ASIS General Security Risk Assessment Guideline (2004)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Then a <strong>functional requirement</strong> is written for each component.</li>\n<li>A functional requirement is like a <strong>job description</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Questions to define the functional requirements</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>What is the purpose of the system?</li>\n<li>What, specifically, must each camera see?</li>\n<li>What is needed in proactive risk reduction, real-time monitoring, or recorded video?</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Camera Functional Requirements</h5>\n<p>Each function requires a different field of view. Three factors are considered:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Target</td>\n<td>People (individuals or groups); packages or objects; vehicles (individual); traffic; perimeters; terrain/property.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Activity</td>\n<td>Assault; vandalism; trespassing; robbery; abandoned package or vehicle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational requirement</td>\n<td>Identify a person, or show in which direction a suspect left a parking lot.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Example: two operational requirements, two designs</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Identify a person:</strong> defined focal view that includes the face. The best is a fixed camera.</li>\n<li><strong>Direction of exit from the parking lot:</strong> wider focal length that takes in the parking lot.</li>\n<li>The final size of the image is determined by the <strong>lens</strong>, the <strong>focal length</strong>, and the <strong>image sensor</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fixed camera vs. PTZ</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Fixed camera</th>\n<th>PTZ (pan/tilt/zoom) camera</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Movement</td>\n<td>Static; it does not move.</td>\n<td>Pan: rotates from side to side. Tilt: moves up and down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lens</td>\n<td>Fixed focal or varifocal.</td>\n<td>Motorized varifocal to open or close the field of view.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Advantage</td>\n<td>Always points at the desired field of view; makes assessment easier.</td>\n<td>Covers a much larger area; more flexibility.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disadvantage</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>If it looks elsewhere, it does not capture the event occurring outside its current view.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical use</td>\n<td>Identification at a defined point.</td>\n<td>Assessment or video patrol.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>11.4.2 Uses of Video Subsystems in Security</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Video used to be only a surveillance and assessment tool.</li>\n<li>With today's IP (Internet Protocol) cameras it is also a <strong>proactive</strong> tool: detect, deter, notify, respond automatically, and analyze events or threats.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Concepts</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Visual assessment</td>\n<td>Having identifying or descriptive visual information during an incident.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Surveillance forensics</td>\n<td>Having visual information stored in a format that allows the images to be studied or reviewed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Visual documentation</td>\n<td>Includes embedded authenticity points: time/date stamp or character generation.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>There are only four reasons to have cameras in security</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Get visual information about something that <strong>is happening</strong>.</li>\n<li>Get visual information about something that <strong>already happened</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Deter</strong> or discourage undesirable activities.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>video analytics</strong> tools.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>How to choose the camera (reasons 1 and 2)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They require the right combination of camera and lens.</li>\n<li>Order of criteria: <strong>1st sensitivity, 2nd resolution, 3rd features</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The camera is chosen before the lens.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sensitivity</td>\n<td>Minimum amount of visible light needed for a quality image; also invisible spectra such as infrared (IR).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resolution</td>\n<td>Measured in horizontal and vertical pixels; it defines the quality of the image in detail or reproduction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Features</td>\n<td>What gives one camera an advantage over another.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Lens + camera + image sensor determine how much image, and of what kind, appears on the monitor.</li>\n<li>The hardware is chosen for its ability to give the desired identification information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The three theoretical views of identification</strong></p>\n<h5>Subject Identification</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Ability to identify something or someone in the scene <strong>beyond a shadow of a doubt</strong>.</li>\n<li>Example of the $100 bill:<ul>\n<li>At <strong>3 ft (0.9 m)</strong>, someone with mediocre eyesight identifies it without doubt.</li>\n<li>At <strong>30 ft (9 m)</strong>, a person with normal eyesight can no longer identify it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Video does not allow touching, smelling, or tasting (and almost never hearing). The visual information must suffice on its own.</li>\n<li>The <strong>viewing angle</strong> matters: someone standing on a desk looking down at people's heads does not identify them well. With video, such a steep angle is even worse.</li>\n<li>It depends <strong>first on the size and detail of the image</strong> and <strong>second on the viewing angle</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>10% rule:</strong> the object must occupy at least <strong>10% of the width of the scene</strong>.<ul>\n<li>Average person: <strong>2 ft (0.6 m)</strong> wide.</li>\n<li>For full body and identification beyond doubt, the scene must be no more than <strong>20 ft (6 m)</strong> wide.</li>\n<li>Rule based on a minimum of <strong>325 horizontal lines</strong> of resolution (television quality).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Beware of digital storage or projection: often the on-screen resolution is much lower.<ul>\n<li>An image that is sharp in a 1–2 in (2.5–5.0 cm) frame on screen may look blurry printed at 5 × 7 in (12.7 × 17.8 cm).</li>\n<li>Match the recording resolution to the purpose of each camera.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Action Identification</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>It captures <strong>what happened</strong>.</li>\n<li>Example: someone pins a $100 bill to the wall and watches. Another person comes in and the first closes his eyes. The bill disappears. He saw the second person come in, but did not see him take it: there is no proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Lesson:</strong> the system must be <strong>automated by means of a trigger</strong>.</li>\n<li>Possible triggers: video motion detection, pressure on a floor mat, interruption of a photoelectric beam.</li>\n<li>Possible responses: raise the resolution, raise the recorded images per second, or bring the image to a guard's attention.</li>\n<li>That way the system records the important actions and captures useful evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Scene Identification</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Each scene must stand on its own.</li>\n<li>Example: an officer sees on video an employee who has collapsed and calls paramedics. If he does not read the on-screen character generation, he may send them to a place that looks similar but is not the right one.</li>\n<li>A form of identification that is <strong>crucial but often forgotten</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is critical to <strong>name and identify each camera and its view correctly</strong> in the recorder/VSS.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.4.3 Analog System Components</h4>\n<p><strong>Three main components</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Camera</td>\n<td>Transforms the image of reflected light into an electronic signal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transmission cable</td>\n<td>Carries the video signal from the camera to the monitor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitor</td>\n<td>Converts the video signal into an image on screen.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Other possible parts</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Pan or pan/tilt unit</td>\n<td>Pan: moves the camera from side to side. Pan/tilt: side to side and up/down. Today it is usually integrated into a single unit inside a dome. In most applications it is <strong>more cost-effective to use several fixed cameras</strong> than one pan/tilt. It used to reduce costs by saving cameras; today several fixed ones cost what a pan/tilt does. It is used mainly with zoom lenses, with an alarm interface, or with pre-positioning. It may require more staff time; do not add it without analyzing the application.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Controller</td>\n<td>Commands a function of the pan, the pan/tilt, or the automatic lens. The application chooses the equipment, not the other way around.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Switcher</td>\n<td>Shows several cameras on one or more monitors. It saves money: it allows more cameras than monitors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lens</td>\n<td>If it focuses light onto a chip or tube inside the camera, it is a lens. There are many sizes and fields of view (width and height of the scene). The right lens gives the proper identification within the scene.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video transmitter/receiver</td>\n<td>Transmits by cable, telephone line, radio waves, light waves, or other media. Mixing several in a system is common. <strong>Coaxial is the most common, but not always the best.</strong> Example: a long exterior stretch in fiber optic within a system that mostly uses twisted pair (two-wire).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amplifier</td>\n<td>Boosts the signal over long runs. <strong>Avoid it when you can</strong>: almost no installer has the tools to balance it. If the coaxial run is so long that it needs one, it is better to switch to fiber optic, microwave, or twisted pair.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video recorder</td>\n<td>Stores the video on CD, DVD, hard disk, digital or network recorder, or other medium. The need defines the form of recording. Modes: time-lapse, event-triggered, 24 hours, 72 hours, and more.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Types of switcher</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Passive four-position switcher</td>\n<td>The user presses a button and the image appears.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sequential switcher</td>\n<td>Changes automatically from camera to camera. <strong>Dwell time</strong> = time it stays on each camera before switching. It does not matter if the switcher has more inputs than cameras.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quad splitter</td>\n<td>Shows four images on one screen.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiplexing switcher</td>\n<td>Interacts with the recorder to store more information per camera. It plays back streams separately or in quad format. Recommended upgrade for anyone with a sequential or quad.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Matrix switcher</td>\n<td>Organizes large groups of video inputs and outputs and integrates them with alarms and display options.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>With the basics of each category, a simple or complex system that works in the field can be designed on paper.</li>\n<li><strong>Key ideas:</strong> (1) simplified, even the most complex system can be handled by almost anyone; (2) <strong>the application governs the choice of equipment</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.4.4 Network Video System Components</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Analog video has been left behind by network video solutions.</li>\n<li>In fully digital environments the term is <strong>digital VSS</strong> (digital video surveillance system).</li>\n<li>Sometimes also: <strong>DIS</strong> (digital imaging system) or <strong>VIS</strong> (visual imaging system).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Typical parts of a VSS</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Camera.</li>\n<li>Transmission media: coaxial cable, twisted pair, network cable, fiber optic, or radio frequency.</li>\n<li><strong>PoE</strong> (Power over Ethernet) network switch; sometimes without PoE, with an auxiliary power supply.</li>\n<li>Recording equipment: recording hardware on a server and viewing platforms (computers and mobile devices).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other parts of the digital system</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Switcher</td>\n<td>Three types: (1) high-speed analog-to-digital converter (<strong>encoder</strong>) that receives several analog signals and delivers a single multiplexed digital signal; (2) high-speed digital switcher, which is essentially a multiplexer; (3) <strong>DVR</strong> (digital video recorder) with built-in multiplexer. Once the camera is connected to a PC, the video is stored on hard disk, CD, DVD, or the Internet; not always multiplexed, it can go as sequences or image files.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lens</td>\n<td>One of the few elements that is <strong>not going digital</strong>. Many cameras come with lenses optimized or specific for the application.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video transmitter/receiver</td>\n<td><strong>Ethernet</strong> is the most common medium in digital, but not always the best. A long exterior stretch in fiber can be combined with twisted pair as the main medium. <strong>RG-59/U, RG-6/U, and RG-11/U</strong> coaxials can carry a digital signal with special converters.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amplifier</td>\n<td>In digital they are mainly <strong>repeaters</strong>. The signal is binary and is not amplified; what is maintained is the carrier. The signal is received and an exact copy is emitted with the carrier renewed. <strong>Normal maximum digital cable length: 328 ft (100 m)</strong>, except wireless, microwave, fiber, modified coaxial, or other long-distance medium.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video recorder</td>\n<td>DVRs arrived strongly at the start of this millennium, but in large applications stacking several DVRs was not a good solution. Fixed inputs: 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, or more cameras. Most are <strong>not true digital recorders</strong>: analog inputs and outputs. In large systems they are replaced by servers and massive digital storage. The <strong>NVR</strong> (network video recorder) ranges from equipment with a fixed number of channels (like a DVR) with a built-in switch to enterprise systems with a VMS (video management system) and servers.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>11.4.5 System Design</h4>\n<p><strong>Simple design rules</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>What it implies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep the system in perspective</td>\n<td>It can be as simple as cameras, cables, network equipment, server/recorder, and client/station.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Let the application choose the equipment</td>\n<td>Vendors may promise that their equipment covers everything without asking. First the needs are defined with a complete risk assessment and the system, security, and operational needs; then the equipment is chosen.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design generically</td>\n<td>The best design does not specify brands or models; it stays open according to the requirements of the site or scene.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design for the best option first</td>\n<td>Design before setting a budget. Then calculate the cost and, if needed, remove elements.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Don't feel the system must be built all at once</td>\n<td>Installation can be spread over several budget years. Use solid, proven equipment that will remain available for several years. Design with ample expansion capacity and easy upgrades.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Steps before putting the system out to bid</strong></p>\n<h5>Step 1: Write the Purpose of the Proposed System</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Monitoring the back hallway of a store requires little planning; covering several places in several complexes requires more.</li>\n<li>Over time, parts of the system may serve non-security purposes.</li>\n<li>Consider all possible uses. Example: some segments for security and others for traffic or product flow.</li>\n<li>That way the <strong>budget can be shared</strong> with other departments.</li>\n<li>Involve other stakeholders early: HR (human resources), risk management, operations, quality control, or marketing.</li>\n<li>The essential point: <strong>put the purpose in writing</strong>. It keeps the designer on course and allows logical changes during upgrades.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Step 2: Write the Purpose of Each Camera</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The security risk of each area viewed is weighed.</li>\n<li><strong>High-security area:</strong> it may be advisable to link the video to an alarm: door contact, microwave motion detector, photo beam, or video motion detector.</li>\n<li><strong>Low-security area:</strong> the alarm may not be needed; recording as reference backup may suffice.</li>\n<li><strong>Visible or covert?</strong><ul>\n<li>Visible: deters, to a limited extent, casual or opportunistic crime in shopping centers.</li>\n<li>Covert: favors security and leaves people comfortable in their surroundings.</li>\n<li>If a covert camera is used, the manager must <strong>respect privacy rights</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Step 3: Define the Areas to Be Viewed by Each Camera</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The actual view is defined by <strong>height</strong> and <strong>cardinal points</strong>.</li>\n<li>Example: camera No. 1 at 30 ft (9 m) height at the northeast corner of building 5, looking at the west door. Camera No. 2 above the west exit of the north hallway of building 6, looking east.</li>\n<li>Always include a <strong>drawing or plan</strong> with all the camera locations.</li>\n<li>Interior plans and exterior satellite images help confirm location and direction of coverage.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Step 4: Choose a Camera Style</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Based on sensitivity, resolution, features, and other design factors.</li>\n<li>Whenever possible, conduct a <strong>live, on-site demonstration</strong> of the proposed cameras for each location or type of location.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimum amount of light the camera needs to produce an image.</li>\n<li>The first thing is to analyze the lighting of the area:<ul>\n<li>Bright or dark? Constant or variable?</li>\n<li>Are there lights that brighten the scene at night or on cloudy days?</li>\n<li>Are there large windows? With heavy curtains? Are they closed during the day or opened according to the outside light?</li>\n<li>Will there be a bright background that leaves people backlit (silhouette)?</li>\n<li>What luminaires are there: sodium, metal halide, fluorescent, or LED?</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Indoors:</strong> sensitivity is not usually a concern, except for poorly lit areas at night.</li>\n<li><strong>Outdoors (several cameras):</strong> a <strong>lighting study</strong> is advisable. It requires some training and a good light meter.</li>\n<li>If there is not enough light at night: <strong>visible</strong> or <strong>infrared (IR)</strong> lighting.<ul>\n<li>IR is not seen by the human eye, but many cameras see it as visible light.</li>\n<li>Solution if the building's decor or the neighbors prevent putting up visible light.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sensitivity</th>\n<th>What for</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Full light</td>\n<td>Interiors with full, constant (or little varying) light.</td>\n<td>The cheapest.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lower light</td>\n<td>Light equivalent to dawn or dusk.</td>\n<td>Medium to high.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low light</td>\n<td>Little or no light.</td>\n<td>The most expensive.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A lens with a <strong>lower F-stop</strong> lets in more light.</li>\n<li>The supply of equipment for <strong>no-light</strong> conditions is growing.</li>\n<li>The conditions in which the system must operate must be assessed realistically.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Resolution</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Critical measure of image quality: number of <strong>horizontal scan lines</strong> (analog) or <strong>pixel arrays</strong> (digital).</li>\n<li>It must suffice to give enough detail to serve as valid visual evidence.</li>\n<li>Lens and camera are chosen for their ability to give the desired identification.</li>\n<li><strong>Analog:</strong> the level of identification is expressed in horizontal lines.</li>\n<li><strong>Digital:</strong> it is expressed in <strong>pixels per foot or per meter at the recording point</strong>.</li>\n<li>Thanks to HD (high definition) and megapixel, extreme identification is achieved, impossible with standard analog.</li>\n<li>Analog: 3 levels of identification (subject, action, scene). Digital: the book speaks of <strong>seven theoretical views of identification</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Digital identification views (pixels at the recording point)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>View</th>\n<th>What is achieved</th>\n<th>px/ft</th>\n<th>px/m</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>General</td>\n<td>Clothing and colors cannot be distinguished. No detail when enlarging person-sized targets.</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitor</td>\n<td>General flows of people or vehicles. No serious detail when enlarging.</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>23</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detect</td>\n<td>Person-sized targets are detected, but not identified. No significant detail when enlarging.</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>35</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Observe</td>\n<td>Clothing and colors begin to be distinguished. No good detail when enlarging people.</td>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>58</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recognize</td>\n<td>High accuracy to identify and separate known people. Good detail when enlarging in general.</td>\n<td>35</td>\n<td>118</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subject identification</td>\n<td>Establish identity beyond a shadow of a doubt. Excellent enlargement.</td>\n<td>46</td>\n<td>150</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>License plate identification</td>\n<td>Plates and similar-size objects with excellent detail when enlarging.</td>\n<td>70</td>\n<td>231</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Facial recognition</td>\n<td>Extreme detail. Excellent resolution for enlarging.</td>\n<td>88</td>\n<td>289</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In the analog view, the book says that in subject identification the subject equals <strong>20% of the width of the scene</strong> at the recording point (minimum 325 horizontal lines).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book announces “seven” views, but the list has <strong>eight</strong>. Memorize all eight with their figures.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in 11.4.2 the subject identification rule is <strong>10%</strong> of the width (2 ft in 20 ft). Here it says <strong>20%</strong>. Internal contradiction in the book.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> 35 px/ft is equivalent to about 115 px/m, not 118 (outside the book). Use the book's figures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Features (Camera features)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Digital Pan-Tilt-Zoom (digital PTZ)</td>\n<td>A fixed megapixel camera appears to be mounted on a mechanical pan/tilt. It moves across the imager along a predefined path without moving the camera. There are also digital cameras with mechanical pan/tilt inside a dome.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automatic gain control (AGC)</td>\n<td>Internal circuit that amplifies the video to keep the signal at a fixed level when the light drops. It was created so that the image would stay stable when panning through shaded areas. <strong>Every exterior camera must have AGC turned on.</strong> Many CCD (charge-coupled device) cameras do not allow turning it off. Disadvantage: it <strong>multiplies the noise by 10</strong> and greatly degrades the image in low light. Advantage: it lets you keep seeing areas somewhat below the minimum light, which would otherwise look black.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electronic shuttering</td>\n<td>Manual or automatic. It compensates for changes in light without automatic or manual iris lenses. Like glasses that darken in strong light. It allows working in a wide range of light without auto-iris or taking high-speed images of fast objects. With IP and digital hybrid cameras, <strong>each pixel</strong> is analyzed and filtered: it attenuates only the very bright points without lowering the overall sensitivity, with or without auto-iris. Even so, test the stated specifications in the field.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backlight compensation</td>\n<td>Challenge: seeing someone in front of a bright background; typical case, a person in front of a glass door to the outside. Methods in the following table.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Autofocus</td>\n<td>Camera and lens focus on their own when following an action or changing scenes. It fails if the target is among several closer objects. Dome covers can interfere. In general, digital hybrids and IP cameras with autofocus work well. The best equipment allows <strong>turning it off</strong> and focusing by hand in difficult cases.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Privacy blocking / image protection</td>\n<td>Hides a specific area of the image (for example, certain windows) with a control or digital effect. See detail below.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> AGC sensitivity is not the overall sensitivity. AGC gives a usable but low-quality image. It is sometimes cited to make the camera look, on paper, more sensitive than it is.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Backlight compensation methods</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Limit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto-iris lens</td>\n<td>The most common tool. Every analog auto-iris responds to the <strong>average amplitude</strong> of the raw video signal by means of a sampling circuit. More light on the CCD, more signal; less light, less signal. Properly installed, it keeps the image at an average of <strong>1 volt peak to peak (1 Vpp)</strong> with optimal light. It closes or opens in direct proportion.</td>\n<td>It works with normal light. Because it works with averages, when looking at bright areas it compensates for the brightness and leaves the rest dark or backlit. Many designers do not understand it and apply cameras beyond their capability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Masking</td>\n<td>Digital interface built into certain cameras and controllers. It divides the image into a grid, sections are programmed to be ignored, and the grid is turned off to view without obstruction.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electronic iris</td>\n<td><strong>First real digital enhancement method</strong> that eliminates the need for the auto-iris. It works with a true average of the signal: it <strong>de-amplifies the super-brights and amplifies the sub-blacks</strong>, giving an even 1 Vpp image. The person in front of the glass door is seen in detail, and so is the surroundings. Each manufacturer calls it something different.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wide dynamic range (WDR)</td>\n<td>Analog method that makes a <strong>double scan</strong> of the CCD: one overexposed image and one underexposed. The processor combines the best of each into a single image. In digital cameras it is called <strong>multi-scanning</strong>.</td>\n<td>It does not work in all situations.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Few methods are more effective than <strong>not pointing the camera toward direct light</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Privacy blocking: detail</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A single liability case is enough to remember that there are people behind the pan/tilts and the zooms.</li>\n<li>Some cameras stray from their use and become “invasive toys.” Even in public places, small invasions of privacy grow, especially if the images are published.</li>\n<li>The mask is <strong>mathematically anchored to the pixels</strong>: it keeps working when the camera pans, tilts, or zooms.</li>\n<li>It is affordable. Options:<ul>\n<li>Locked position, or pan/tilt/zoom keeping the mask.</li>\n<li>Flexible drawing: mask an area of any size or shape.</li>\n<li>Blackout or blur the area.</li>\n<li>Password protection against unauthorized reprogramming.</li>\n<li>Temporarily remove the mask or see through it during playback, for valid purposes and under control.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says that it “cannot be reversed” right after citing the option of removing the mask during playback. It seems contradictory; it is understood that a mask recorded into the image cannot be undone.</li>\n<li>Other methods:<ul>\n<li>Send the VMS <strong>two streams</strong> from the camera, one with the mask and one without, restricted by permission levels.</li>\n<li>Software that controls who accesses the original video.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other Design Factors</strong></p>\n<p><em>Environment</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Does the camera need protection from vandals, customers, or employees? Must the housing blend with the decor?</li>\n<li>Interior housings: ceiling mount (in place of a drop-ceiling tile), hanging bubbles, stainless steel or bronze corner housings.</li>\n<li>A lot of dirt or dust makes the camera heat up and shortens its performance or service life.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Exterior condition</th>\n<th>What is needed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Winter below <strong>35 °F (1.7 °C)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Heater</strong> in the housing. The CCD withstands the cold, but zoom and auto-iris lenses slow down or freeze because of the grease in the iris, focus, and zoom rings.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Summer above <strong>80 °F (26.7 °C)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fan</strong> or even air conditioning in the housing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Direct sun</td>\n<td><strong>Sun shield</strong> to avoid excessive heat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>East or west orientation</td>\n<td><strong>Sun visor</strong> against the glare of sunrise or sunset and overheating. Often the sun shield doubles as a visor.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Mounting</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If it goes up high, how is it reached for maintenance?</li>\n<li>Will the angle be so steep that only the tops of heads or the backs of necks are seen?</li>\n<li>Will it go under an eave or gutter next to a wall, or over obstacles such as air conditioning units?</li>\n<li>Is there room to open the housing from the top or is one with a bottom hinge preferable?</li>\n<li>Swing arm to access from a window or rooftop on high floors?</li>\n<li>In a drop-ceiling housing, is there enough space above the ceiling panel?</li>\n<li><strong>Objectives rule:</strong> do not ask a camera for more than <strong>two objectives (one major and one minor)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sweep rule:</strong> it must not auto-pan (physically or digitally) more than <strong>45° left or right</strong> of the center of its primary objective.</li>\n<li>Common mistake: expecting too much of a single camera or installing fewer than needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Analog:</strong> the higher the risk of the area, the fewer objects each camera should watch.</li>\n<li><strong>Digital megapixel:</strong> one camera covers what two or perhaps three analog cameras do with the same quality.</li>\n<li><strong>High security, 360°:</strong> at least <strong>four analog cameras</strong>; the budget may force a compromise.</li>\n<li>Today there are <strong>180° and 360°</strong> megapixel cameras with software for individual or panoramic views.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Power and enclosures</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The availability of power weighs heavily on the budget.</li>\n<li>The typical: power and video cables separated by conduit up to the camera.</li>\n<li>Some IP cameras receive power over the same cable as the video (PoE).</li>\n<li>Indoors: housings for physical protection or aesthetics. Outdoors: special housings for weather or extreme environments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Transport medium</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The security manager may find it hard to choose; he or she may prefer to leave it to the bidding contractor.</li>\n<li><strong>Coaxial:</strong> sufficient for analog cameras; <strong>it does not work for IP without a media converter</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Distances of 1,000 ft (304 m) or more</strong> between camera and control point: <strong>fiber optic</strong> is better, whatever the camera.</li>\n<li>Available methods (each with advantages, disadvantages, and costs): coaxial; fiber optic; twisted pair (two-wire); <strong>UTP</strong> (unshielded twisted pair, network cable); microwave and radio frequency; infrared transmission; existing telephone lines, Internet, or intranet.</li>\n<li>A system can use more than one method.</li>\n<li><strong>Encryption</strong> protects wired and wireless transmissions against hackers and unauthorized onlookers, but it can affect video speed.</li>\n<li>The VSS can be connected to the organization's main network.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Most common connection cabling</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cable</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Coaxial and BNC</td>\n<td>The first <strong>10BASE2</strong> Ethernet networks used <strong>RG-59</strong> coaxial, similar to that of analog CCTV but with a different diameter and resistance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Twisted pair and <strong>RJ-45</strong></td>\n<td>Common in Ethernet: <strong>eight wires in four twisted pairs</strong>. Normal maximum length: <strong>328 ft (100 m)</strong>. The categories (Cat) are defined in <strong>ISO/IEC 11801</strong>, <em>Information technology — Generic cabling for customer premises</em> (2010).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book expands BNC as “British Naval Connector.” The technical name is Bayonet Neill–Concelman (outside the book). On the exam, if it appears, accept the book's version.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Twisted pair categories (Cat)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Use according to the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cat-3</td>\n<td><strong>Should no longer be installed.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cat-5</td>\n<td>Still in use, even at new sites.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cat-5e</td>\n<td><strong>1000BASE-T</strong> gigabit networks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cat-6</td>\n<td>Subcategories according to distance and transfer frequency.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cat-7</td>\n<td>Up to <strong>600 MHz</strong>; meets the requirements of <strong>10GBASE-T</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Figure 11.4. Network types and required transmission medium</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Speed</th>\n<th>Medium</th>\n<th>Distance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>10BASE-T</td>\n<td>10 Mbit/s</td>\n<td>Twisted pair (TP) Cat. 3</td>\n<td>100 m</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>100BASE-TX</td>\n<td>100 Mbit/s</td>\n<td>Twisted pair (TP) Cat. 5</td>\n<td>100 m</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>100BASE-FX</td>\n<td>100 Mbit/s</td>\n<td>Multimode fiber (MMF)</td>\n<td>2,000 m</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1000BASE-SX</td>\n<td>1,000 Mbit/s</td>\n<td>Multimode fiber (MMF)</td>\n<td>500 m</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1000BASE-LX</td>\n<td>1,000 Mbit/s</td>\n<td>MMF or single-mode fiber (SMF)</td>\n<td>2 m to 5 km</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1000BASE-T</td>\n<td>1,000 Mbit/s</td>\n<td>Twisted pair (TP) Cat. 5e</td>\n<td>100 m</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10GBASE-LX4</td>\n<td>10,000 Mbit/s</td>\n<td>MMF or SMF fiber</td>\n<td>2 m to 10 km</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10GBASE-T</td>\n<td>10,000 Mbit/s</td>\n<td>Twisted pair (TP) Cat. 6a to 7</td>\n<td>100 m</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>MMF = multimode fiber. SMF = single-mode fiber.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Step 5: Choose the Proper Lens for Each Camera</h5>\n<p>It is determined by three factors:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Format size</td>\n<td>Size of the sensor area onto which the lens focuses the light; it is measured on the <strong>diagonal</strong>. The lens format must <strong>match or exceed</strong> that of the camera. If it is smaller, it does not fill the sensor and the image looks like <strong>tunnel vision</strong>. Example: a 1/4 in camera requires a 1/4 in lens or larger (1/2 in, 2/3 in…).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distance from camera to scene</td>\n<td>Defines the <strong>focal length</strong> needed. It is measured from the front of the camera to the main subject, accurately. It is calculated with the Pythagorean theorem (example below).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Field of view / focal length</td>\n<td>The height or width of the area viewed determines the focal length. It is calculated with simple math, a field-of-view calculator, a quick chart (cheat sheet), or a viewfinder. <strong>The more area viewed, the less detail.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Numerical example of distance (Pythagoras)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Camera on a building wall at <strong>40 ft</strong> above the ground.</li>\n<li>Center of the scene <strong>30 ft</strong> from the building.</li>\n<li>The relevant distance <strong>is not 30 ft but 50 ft</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9474em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">30</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">40</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">500</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:1em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇒</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:1em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.24em;vertical-align:-0.2811em;\"></span><span class=\"mord sqrt\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.9589em;\"><span class=\"svg-align\" style=\"top:-3.2em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"padding-left:1em;\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">500</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-2.9189em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.2em;\"></span><span class=\"hide-tail\" style=\"min-width:1.02em;height:1.28em;\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"400em\" height=\"1.28em\" viewBox=\"0 0 400000 1296\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMinYMin slice\"><path d=\"M263,681c0.7,0,18,39.7,52,119\nc34,79.3,68.167,158.7,102.5,238c34.3,79.3,51.8,119.3,52.5,120\nc340,-704.7,510.7,-1060.3,512,-1067\nl0 -0\nc4.7,-7.3,11,-11,19,-11\nH40000v40H1012.3\ns-271.3,567,-271.3,567c-38.7,80.7,-84,175,-136,283c-52,108,-89.167,185.3,-111.5,232\nc-22.3,46.7,-33.8,70.3,-34.5,71c-4.7,4.7,-12.3,7,-23,7s-12,-1,-12,-1\ns-109,-253,-109,-253c-72.7,-168,-109.3,-252,-110,-252c-10.7,8,-22,16.7,-34,26\nc-22,17.3,-33.3,26,-34,26s-26,-26,-26,-26s76,-59,76,-59s76,-60,76,-60z\nM1001 80h400000v40h-400000z\"/></svg></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2811em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">ft</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>A = horizontal distance from the building to the center of the scene; B = mounting height; d = camera-to-scene distance.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book prints “2,500, which equals 502.” It is an erratum from a lost superscript: it should read 50².</li>\n<li>Following the example: with a distance of 50 ft and a scene 20 ft wide, the lens focal length is calculated.</li>\n<li><strong>Megapixel:</strong> more area can be seen without losing the required detail, or the same area with <strong>two or three times more detail</strong>. What counts are the <strong>pixels per foot or per meter</strong>, not a fixed number of horizontal lines.</li>\n<li>Tools: <strong>rotating lens calculator</strong>, often a manufacturer giveaway; calculators on manufacturers' websites; comparison images and tutorials with mounting and interconnection suggestions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Three main types of lens</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Field of view</th>\n<th>Adjustment</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed lens</td>\n<td>Only one.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Varifocal lens</td>\n<td>Range of focal lengths and fields of view.</td>\n<td>Normally adjusted and <strong>refocused by hand</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zoom lens</td>\n<td>Range of focal lengths and fields of view.</td>\n<td><strong>No need to refocus.</strong> Often motorized and remotely controlled.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Step 6: Determine the Best Method for Transmitting the Video Signal</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>It may be hard for the security manager; he or she may prefer to leave it to the bidding contractor.</li>\n<li><strong>Coaxial:</strong> sufficient for analog; for IP it requires conversion electronics that transmit a digitally encoded signal over coaxial.</li>\n<li><strong>1,000 ft (305 m) or more</strong> between camera and control point: <strong>fiber optic</strong> is better, whatever the camera.</li>\n<li>Methods: coaxial; fiber optic; twisted pair (two-wire); Cat 5 or Cat 6 network cable; microwave; radio frequency or other wireless technology; infrared; telephone lines, Internet, or intranet.</li>\n<li>More than one method can be combined.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book gives 1,000 ft = 304 m in Step 4 and = 305 m here (1,000 ft = 304.8 m, outside the book). The key figure is <strong>1,000 ft</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Step 7: Lay Out the Control Area and Determine Needed Enhancements</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>With the purpose of each camera (Step 2), <strong>triggers and priorities</strong> are assigned and the functions of the control equipment are defined.</li>\n<li>Example:<ul>\n<li>Camera 1: digital video motion detection, extendable pole, and alarm interface.</li>\n<li>Camera 2: pan/tilt/zoom controller with pre-positioning.</li>\n<li>Camera 3: alarm activation by door contact.</li>\n<li>Manager's office in building 5: access to view or control all or part of the system, with a password.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Triggers and priorities <strong>automate</strong> the system. It is inefficient and ineffective to have people continuously watching dozens or hundreds of scenes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recording or storage system</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>VHS and SVHS</strong> tapes have been left behind by digital recording.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Digital option</th>\n<th>Compatibility</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple DVR</td>\n<td><strong>Analog cameras only.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NVR (network video recorder)</td>\n<td>Analog, IP, or hybrid cameras.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Server or network storage</td>\n<td>Separate space on the organization's own network.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External storage via Internet</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud storage</strong>, off site.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Keys to digital storage: formats compatible among cameras, compression factors, and space required per image.</li>\n<li>“At the beginning it is all math; at the end, it is all the quality of the recovered image.”</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.4.6 Equipment Selection</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The goal is not to choose a vendor, but to <strong>define the operating parameters</strong> that the application demands.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Camera: six main types</h5>\n<p>Knowing them avoids overspending on unnecessary functions.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard analog CCD</td>\n<td>Black and white or color. <strong>The most common</strong>; it works in every interior and in many exteriors. Analog, with or without digital effects. Resolution: <strong>220 horizontal lines (very low) to 580 (very high)</strong>. Sensitivity: <strong>0.00046 foot-candles (0.005 lux), very low, to 0.929 fc (10 lux), very high</strong>. Color ones are the most limited in low light. Hybrid solutions: IR sensitivity; color by day and black and white at night; or an <strong>intensifier</strong> between lens and CCD that amplifies the light tens of thousands of times.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IP cameras</td>\n<td>Digital, black and white or color. They need visible light. Three styles: <strong>standard definition, megapixel/high definition, and smart</strong>. They measure their resolution in multiples of <strong>CIF</strong> (Common Intermediate Format). CIF ≈ half the average analog resolution in horizontal lines; <strong>not recommended as a storage standard</strong>. Standard IP cameras range from <strong>1/4 CIF to 4 CIF</strong>. 4 CIF = twice the horizontal pixels and twice the vertical. Power: transformer or <strong>PoE</strong> from the network switch.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Megapixel cameras</td>\n<td>Sensor of <strong>more than one million pixels</strong>, normally minimum <strong>1280 × 1024</strong>. Great improvement over analog.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Infrared (IR) cameras</td>\n<td><strong>They need an IR light source.</strong> They are used where visible light is not an option.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thermal cameras</td>\n<td><strong>They need neither visible nor IR light.</strong> With special filters and lenses they measure the temperature of objects and represent it with colors: <strong>cold in shades of blue, hot in shades of red</strong>. Long-range surveillance, e.g., ships in a harbor at <strong>5 mi (8 km)</strong>. Popular with police and border patrols.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Day-night cameras</td>\n<td>All cameras give some day-night function, but these work outdoors and in low-light interiors. They almost always use a <strong>removable IR-cut filter</strong> (IR-cut or IR-blocking filter). They monitor sensitivity by themselves: by day, <strong>color</strong> image; at night, they remove the filter, let the IR through, and give a <strong>black and white</strong> image.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Smart cameras = edge devices</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They use powerful processors and software inside the camera.</li>\n<li>Functions: digital motion detection, facial recognition, privacy blocking, digital pan/tilt/zoom, and more.</li>\n<li>They are called <strong>edge devices</strong> because they bring the computing to the outer edge of the system, instead of sending the information to the controller to be processed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Lens</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>After the camera, choosing the lens is the <strong>second most important decision</strong> of the project.</li>\n<li>It depends mainly on the <strong>size of the scene</strong> and the <strong>degree of visual identification</strong> required.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Five types of lens</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Range</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wide-angle</td>\n<td>Very wide scene at short distance.</td>\n<td><strong>0 to 15 ft (4.5 m)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard</td>\n<td>Medium scene at medium distance. It reproduces what the human eye sees at the same distance, except that the eye has a peripheral vision <strong>about 2.5 times</strong> greater.</td>\n<td><strong>15 to 50 ft (4.5–15.25 m)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Telephoto</td>\n<td>Narrow area at long distance. Like a telescope: it brings the distant closer, but narrows the field of view.</td>\n<td><strong>More than 50 ft (15.25 m)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zoom</td>\n<td>Moving optics that give the wide-angle, standard, and telephoto views in a single unit. <strong>Manual:</strong> the installer sets the field and focus, and it stays fixed. <strong>Motorized:</strong> motors in the lens; the operator adjusts remotely with a controller. It has a <strong>tracking mechanism</strong>: a physical link between focus and zoom optics that readjusts the focus when going from tele to wide angle.</td>\n<td>Entire range</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Varifocal</td>\n<td>Smaller version of a manual zoom; the view is fine-tuned on site. The installer carries few lenses for many scene ranges.</td>\n<td>Short range around a standard lens (e.g., <strong>8 mm to 12.5 mm</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Varifocal vs. zoom: two differences</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The varifocal lens <strong>does not cover</strong> wide angle to telephoto; only a short range on either side of a standard fixed-focal-length lens.</li>\n<li>The varifocal lens <strong>has no tracking mechanism</strong>: it must be refocused every time the range changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lens–camera compatibility</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cameras are smaller and have changed: light transmission, detail, size, electronic controls (moved from the lens to the camera) and cost.</li>\n<li>Incompatibility causes <strong>physical damage</strong> and image problems. Over time, the industry is eliminating it.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>What it decides</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Is the light fixed, slightly variable or highly variable?</td>\n<td><strong>Fixed iris</strong> (fixed-iris / fixed-aperture): no light control. <strong>Manual iris</strong>: opened or closed at installation. <strong>Auto-iris</strong>: a motor opens or closes according to the video sampling circuit in the lens or the camera.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Format 1/4, 1/3, 1/2, 2/3, 1 in or megapixel?</td>\n<td>The lens format must <strong>match or exceed</strong> the camera's. The format <strong>does not define the final size</strong> of the image; the <strong>focal length</strong> does.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is it a color camera?</td>\n<td><strong>Color-corrected</strong> lens: different cut and a special coating so that all colors in the visible spectrum focus at the same point. Many old black-and-white cameras use uncorrected lenses: reusing them causes problems.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C or CS mount?</td>\n<td>See the mount table below.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AC/EC (video) or DC/LC lens?</td>\n<td>See the table below.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is it megapixel?</td>\n<td>See the explanation below.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is there IR illumination?</td>\n<td><strong>IR-corrected</strong> lens: optics and coatings that focus the longer IR waves on the CCD.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Auto-iris: cost and when not to use it</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>According to the book, auto-iris is now smaller, lighter and cheaper than fixed or manual iris; it costs “only a fraction.”</li>\n<li>In many applications it is prudent to use auto-iris on all cameras.</li>\n<li>Fixed or manual iris is preferable if the camera has a wide light range, automatic electronic shuttering or electronic iris.</li>\n<li>Risk: auto-iris can conflict with the camera electronics and cause <strong>extreme flutter</strong> (image flutter) or a <strong>total blackout</strong> (blackout).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in practice auto-iris usually costs more than a fixed lens (outside the book). For the exam, stick with what the book says.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C and CS mounts</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mount</th>\n<th>Sensor–lens distance</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>C (older)</td>\n<td><strong>17.526 mm</strong></td>\n<td>Many C cameras are still in use and C lenses are still sold.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CS (since the late 90s)</td>\n<td><strong>12.5 mm</strong></td>\n<td>The CCD moved closer to the mount; the CS lens sits less far into the camera.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C lens on a CS camera without an adapter ring: breaks the CCD.</strong> With an adapter ring, no problem.</li>\n<li><strong>CS lens on a C camera: focus problems with no solution.</strong></li>\n<li>Some cameras accept both. A <strong>5 mm spacer</strong> (C/CS adapter ring) converts a C lens for a CS mount.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says the CCD moved “15 mm” closer. With its own figures the difference is about <strong>5 mm</strong> (17.526 − 12.5), the same as the spacer. Probable erratum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>AC/EC (video) vs. DC/LC lenses</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Meaning according to the book</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Compatibility</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>AC/EC (video lens)</td>\n<td>AC = auto circuit; EC = electronic circuit (metric equivalent).</td>\n<td>Original, outdated auto-iris design. It contains a video sampling circuit that controls the iris according to the signal level.</td>\n<td>Older cameras accept only video lenses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DC/LC</td>\n<td>DC = direct circuit; LC = logic control (metric equivalent).</td>\n<td>No iris electronics; the camera supplies the positive or negative motor voltages. Cheaper and more efficient. <strong>It is the current standard.</strong></td>\n<td>Cameras from <strong>1995 to 2002</strong>: accept both. Since <strong>2002</strong>: only DC/LC.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Megapixel lens</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Main difference: the megapixel lens is of <strong>much higher quality</strong>.</li>\n<li>Every lens focuses the image pixel by pixel. A 4 CIF lens focuses on an area the size of a 4 CIF pixel.</li>\n<li>A 16 CIF camera (1.3 megapixels) has twice the horizontal and vertical pixels: <strong>four pixels</strong> where the 4 CIF has one.</li>\n<li>A 4 CIF lens on that camera focuses onto four pixels instead of one: the final image is <strong>equivalent to 4 CIF</strong>.</li>\n<li>Result: <strong>technology, money and effort are wasted</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says those pixels are “half” the area. With double the density on each axis, each pixel occupies <strong>one quarter</strong> of the area (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Specification sheets</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Compatibility is ensured by checking the <strong>specification sheets</strong>.</li>\n<li>The camera's sheet says which lenses it accepts. The lens's sheet says whether it meets what the camera requires.</li>\n<li>You do not need to understand every process, but you do need the basic principles and the nomenclature.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Camera Formats and Lenses</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The smaller the CCDs, the harder it is to match lens and camera.</li>\n<li>Example: a site with 1/4 in cameras and 6.5–65 mm zoom lenses wants to add a 1/3 in camera with the same view.</li>\n<li>Key: know the <strong>standard lens</strong> for each format and calculate from there. The standard lens recreates what the human eye sees at the same distance.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Format</th>\n<th>Standard lens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1 in</td>\n<td><strong>25 mm</strong> (reference)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1/2 in (half of 1 in)</td>\n<td><strong>12.5 mm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1/3 in (one third of 1 in)</td>\n<td>8.333 mm, rounded to <strong>8 mm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Format conversion example</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A 1/2 in camera with an 8 mm lens is changed to the 1/3 in format with the same image.</li>\n<li>Step 1: convert to the 1 in equivalent. The 1 in is double: 8 mm × 2 = <strong>16 mm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Step 2: convert from 1 in to 1/3 in: 16 mm ÷ 3 = <strong>5.33 mm</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0496em;vertical-align:-0.3552em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3448em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5198em;margin-left:-0.1076em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1/3</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3552em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.1256em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.4396em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.7452em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3448em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5198em;margin-left:-0.1076em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1/2</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3552em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">mm</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">16</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">mm</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5.33</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">mm</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Best option: a <strong>1/3 in varifocal</strong> whose range includes 5.33 mm.</li>\n<li>Alternative: a fixed-focal-length lens close to 5.33 mm, in 1/3 in format or larger.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Field of view and aspect ratio</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The field of view is the final size of the area viewed, in width and height.</li>\n<li><strong>Analog:</strong> images <strong>4 parts wide by 3 high</strong> (4:3). If the image is 12 ft wide, it is 9 ft high.</li>\n<li><strong>IP/digital:</strong> works the same way, but the image is no longer fixed at 4 × 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CIF and IP resolutions</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digital resolution is measured in <strong>CIF</strong> (Common Intermediate Format). All resolutions are multiples or divisions of CIF.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 11.5. Common IP resolutions</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ratio</th>\n<th>Label</th>\n<th>Horizontal × vertical</th>\n<th>Total</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>CIF</td>\n<td>CIF</td>\n<td>352 × 240</td>\n<td>84,480 pixels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One quarter CIF</td>\n<td>QCIF</td>\n<td>176 × 120</td>\n<td>21,120 pixels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 times CIF</td>\n<td>4CIF</td>\n<td>704 × 480</td>\n<td>337,920 pixels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16 times CIF</td>\n<td>16CIF</td>\n<td>1,280 × 1,024</td>\n<td>1.31 megapixels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25 times CIF</td>\n<td>25CIF</td>\n<td>1,700 × 1,200</td>\n<td>2.04 megapixels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>36 times CIF</td>\n<td>36CIF</td>\n<td>2,112 × 1,440</td>\n<td>3.04 megapixels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Film equivalents: <strong>4 megapixels = 400 ASA film</strong>; <strong>6 megapixels = 100 ASA film</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What determines the final resolution (in order)</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The camera.</li>\n<li>The transmission method.</li>\n<li>The weakest link in the video system interface.</li>\n<li>The playback capability of the storage system.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>The higher the resolution, the sharper the image.</li>\n<li>A high-resolution camera yields a low-resolution image if low-resolution monitors or high compression are used. <strong>The monitor is rarely the problem.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Resolution losses in analog</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Loss</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Analog video recorders</td>\n<td>Play back an average of <strong>325 horizontal lines</strong>: the image looks good live and bad on playback.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiplexers</td>\n<td>Digitize for functions and switching and convert back to analog: <strong>up to 25%</strong> of the resolution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coaxial cable</td>\n<td><strong>10% to 15%</strong> more from careless installation, poor connections and cheap cable.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Digital resolution and compression</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digital resolution is ultimately <strong>unlimited</strong> and very flexible.</li>\n<li>Compression reduces bandwidth and storage.</li>\n<li>Most effective and fastest-growing compression standard according to the book: <strong>H.265</strong>; good compression with little or no effective loss of detail.</li>\n<li><strong>Once compressed, the image almost never recovers its original quality or detail.</strong></li>\n<li>Resolution is not a big problem in most interiors and many exteriors.</li>\n<li>If you depend on recorded video: make sure the object is large enough, or that after compression there are <strong>enough pixels per foot (or meter)</strong> to recognize it for the original purpose.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Controlling Software</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Most modern systems connect to a <strong>DVR (analog), NVR or server</strong>.</li>\n<li>With a DVR or NVR, all the controls and functions come in the equipment used together.</li>\n<li>In large, complex applications, all the video is brought together on a network and controlled from a central point with software.</li>\n<li>Depending on size, a single PC may suffice or a great deal of computing power and software may be needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Network concepts</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Node:</strong> central point where equipment is gathered to enter a network path.</li>\n<li><strong>Network path:</strong> central connection between all the field nodes and the <strong>head-end</strong> (headend or control center).</li>\n<li>In digital, because of length and access limits, most cameras are wired <strong>to the nearest node</strong>, not “home run” (direct run) to the central point.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key design questions</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>What it defines</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How many cameras per node?</td>\n<td>Encoding, power and switching at each node, and the bandwidth from each node to the head-end. Depends on the size of the area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Will any camera be viewed at more than one monitoring point? Does each point have its own control?</td>\n<td>Cabling, bandwidth per data sent or received, and the requirements of the software and client stations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Will there be integrated alarm triggers?</td>\n<td>Door contacts, infrared motion detectors or photoelectric beams that bring a camera to the screen when an intruder or motion is detected. Advantage: the operator attends to other tasks until the alarm. Smart/edge cameras often have <strong>alarm inputs on the camera itself</strong>, which avoids running wire from the contact to the node or head-end.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Must the switcher activate other devices on alarm?</td>\n<td>Buzzers or lights; also sending an email, text or image to a mobile device.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Will there be future expansion?</td>\n<td>If a 16-input node has a small power supply in a wall box and six or seven cameras are added later, the supply, switching or box may need to be replaced. Planning can save <strong>hundreds or thousands of dollars</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What goes in a rack? A table or desk?</td>\n<td>Secondary decisions about the type of monitoring station.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The final layout of nodes, head-end, switching, encoding, network and software <strong>cannot be done until the camera views are fully defined</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Recording Systems</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>First decide whether the video will serve to <strong>verify</strong>, <strong>prove</strong> or <strong>support prosecution</strong> (prosecution).</li>\n<li>Video retention for prosecution <strong>varies between states and countries</strong>: consider this when choosing storage and data management.</li>\n<li>That decision defines the type of image, the quality or resolution and the images per second per camera.</li>\n<li>In court, <strong>admissibility</strong> may depend on quality, on how it was obtained and on <strong>proof of originality</strong>.</li>\n<li>Consult the <strong>legal team</strong> before installing.</li>\n<li>Modern storage comes down to: how many images, at what resolution, for how long.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Numerical storage example</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 camera of <strong>1.3 megapixels</strong>, <strong>H.264</strong> format, <strong>15 images per second</strong>, <strong>2.35 MB per image</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Storage per second</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.2586em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">15</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.9136em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">s</span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.4461em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">images</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.3534em;vertical-align:-0.4811em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.35</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8723em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">image</span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">MB</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.4811em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.2173em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">35.25</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8723em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">s</span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord text mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">MB</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>That is an <strong>unrealistic</strong> amount to store.</li>\n<li>To save space, reduce: the images per second; the resolution (normally with compression); the actual recording time per camera (with triggers such as video motion detection); and more.</li>\n<li>It can be useful to meet with the server or NVR manufacturer to calculate storage according to the final design.</li>\n<li><strong>The only reason to cut bandwidth and storage is cost.</strong></li>\n<li>Other factors that weigh heavily: lighting, scene complexity, percentage of motion over time and percentage of motion in the scene.</li>\n<li>There are compression technologies with noise reduction and dynamic adjustment that save bandwidth and storage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Basic recorder types</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>DVR, digital video recorder (analog)</td>\n<td>Captures an analog signal and converts it to digital. Stores on hard disk, CD, DVD or other media. Requires a lot of space, so it compresses with a <strong>codec</strong>: a compression engine or sequence that combines colors, lowers resolution or both. After compression, quality can be poor. <strong>Test before buying</strong>, especially playback and enlargement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NVR, network video recorder (digital)</td>\n<td>Accepts a digital or analog signal from the field. <strong>Much better than the DVR</strong>; controllable over the network; very sophisticated. Usually sufficient for systems of <strong>several hundred cameras</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Server/cloud applications (digital)</td>\n<td>For sophisticated control software, analytics or complex integrations, and for very large systems. Compresses the signal and stores it in complex tiers on servers. Advanced searches and multiple visual outputs for fixed and mobile equipment. From variable frame rate per camera to full digital zoom and pan/tilt control.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Managed video systems</td>\n<td>Storage and viewing <strong>“for hire”</strong> (for hire). Video stored and retrieved remotely from many mobile and desktop devices. Priced by number of cameras and clients (viewers), among other criteria already discussed.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Remote video management services (interactive services)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Video escort for workers after hours (escort surveillance).</li>\n<li>Monitoring of unattended deliveries.</li>\n<li>Alarm verification and streaming to view possible incidents.</li>\n<li>Also used in residential applications.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.4.7 Additional Design Considerations for Video Assessment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The video assessment system is designed as a <strong>component of the total intrusion detection system</strong> (IDS, intrusion detection system).</li>\n<li>Interactions among video, sensors and the display system to consider in conceptual design:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interaction</th>\n<th>What it covers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Site/sector layout</td>\n<td>Sensors arranged so that assessment is possible at reasonable cost.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video automation software</td>\n<td>Automates tracking between cameras.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video/sensor interference</td>\n<td>That the video does not cause nuisance alarms from the sensors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Command center</td>\n<td>Central place to view, record, retrieve or respond to video.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitor location</td>\n<td>Where the monitors go in the display system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction</td>\n<td>Common construction and installation requirements, techniques and locations.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Site/Sector Layout</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>A perimeter assessment system should show as much of the <strong>clear zone</strong> as possible, with both the inner and outer fence.</li>\n<li>Camera and lens must ensure detection and classification of any visible cause of a fence or sensor alarm in the clear zone, at all times.</li>\n<li>Criteria:<ul>\n<li>(a) Separation between inner and outer fence <strong>relatively uniform</strong>.</li>\n<li>(b) Consider <strong>minimum widths</strong> of the clear zone.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Grade the terrain</strong> or remove vegetation in the clear zone.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Adequate lighting</strong> of the area.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Departing from these criteria lowers efficiency and raises cost (more cameras and equipment to achieve the same effectiveness).</li>\n<li><strong>Exterior: one fixed camera per assessment zone.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Interior:</strong> evaluate the effect of using more than one camera per alarm.<ul>\n<li>Small facilities or lower threat: several cameras per alarm may work without duplicating viewing and recording equipment too much.</li>\n<li>Large systems: <strong>one camera per alarm</strong> is simpler. Switching decisions are simpler and the operator concentrates on little video.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Video Automation Software</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>“Stitches” camera to camera the logical path of an intruder or an activity of interest.</li>\n<li>The operator focuses on assessing, without having to choose which camera the event passes through.</li>\n<li>Requires prior programming, now much simpler.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Video/Sensor Interference</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Outdoors, camera towers are installed near the sensors.</li>\n<li>Tower height and location: so that <strong>pole vibration from wind</strong> does not generate seismic energy that sets off <strong>buried cables</strong>.</li>\n<li>Locate towers so that an adversary <strong>does not use them to cross</strong> the perimeter or the isolation zone.</li>\n<li>Power, video, sync and control cables: placed so that noise is not induced between video and sensor cables.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Command Center</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Central place where personnel view, record, retrieve or respond to video from one or more cameras.</li>\n<li>Range: from a closet with one camera watching a store's cash register, only for later investigation, to a facility with hundreds or thousands of cameras integrated with access control and intrusion detection.</li>\n<li>Paradigm shift: the command center can be <strong>virtual</strong>. Staff collaborate and manage everything from anywhere with a good network connection.</li>\n<li>The physical center may still exist, but network connectivity opens new scenarios of <strong>redundancy and emergency recovery</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Monitor Location</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>On the control console, where they allow <strong>effective and rapid</strong> assessment without interference from other controls and system outputs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Construction</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>It is common to run signal and power cables and to modify buildings.</li>\n<li><strong>Combining</strong> the sensor and assessment requirements (conduit, junction boxes) lowers costs and improves the design.</li>\n<li>Leave <strong>room for expansion</strong> in these elements.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Alarm Assessment by Response Force</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Security officers supplement video assessment with visual checks.</li>\n<li>They must be able to assess if the video is not working (maintenance, bad weather) or is not available (e.g., at some classified facilities).</li>\n<li>Whether by video or by officers, the alarm must be assessed <strong>shortly after it is reported</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Towers with officers:</strong> effective if number, design and location give full visual coverage of the perimeter.</li>\n<li><strong>Patrols or mobile officers:</strong> effective only if they respond in time (before the intruder or the cause of the false alarm disappears) and if <strong>enough delay</strong> remains in the system.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Integration with Safety Systems</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Today it is common to add cameras to detect critical safety events.</li>\n<li>It can lower labor costs, but also the <strong>effectiveness of the security system</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Large or complex facilities:</strong> separate functions with different views or roles in the VMS client. That way safety events do not distract and do not <strong>mask a malicious attack</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Simple, low-threat facilities:</strong> sharing functions may be acceptable, but it can still compromise effectiveness during an attack.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intelligent video</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Also called video intelligence, video content analysis or video analytics. Rapidly developing market.</li>\n<li>Benefits: proactively reduce or prevent threats; shorten response times; reduce the operators needed; extract information for other purposes, such as <strong>people counting</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sandia National Laboratories (US):</strong> an operator probably <strong>loses important information after only 20 minutes</strong> in front of a monitor.</li>\n<li>Main use: high-risk transportation, government and retail (<strong>Nilson, 2017</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Architecture</th>\n<th>Where the intelligence is</th>\n<th>Advantage</th>\n<th>Disadvantage</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Centralized</td>\n<td>On the recording server.</td>\n<td>Server almost always an open platform: adding functions is easy.</td>\n<td>The algorithms consume a lot of computing: one server handles only <strong>a few cameras</strong> at a time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distributed</td>\n<td>At the edge: network camera or video encoder.</td>\n<td>Local analysis; <strong>fully scalable</strong>; can reduce bandwidth because the camera decides whether to send video based on its content.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Legal Issues</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Privacy is a major consideration.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not place cameras</strong> in locker rooms, restrooms or other places with a <strong>reasonable expectation of privacy</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hidden or covert cameras: legal in many cases, but consult an attorney about justification or legal authority.</li>\n<li><strong>Dummy cameras = legal liability.</strong> They create an expectation of protection: someone who is attacked believes they are being watched and that help is coming.</li>\n<li>Accepted legal practice: <strong>signage</strong> for a video-surveilled area, often at the entrances, to minimize signs and to notify visitors and staff.</li>\n<li>For video to be <strong>admissible as evidence</strong>, depending on the jurisdiction:<ul>\n<li>Image quality, date/time stamp and percentage of the scene occupied by the subject.</li>\n<li>An <strong>eyewitness</strong> may be required.</li>\n<li>In many states, a <strong>unique scene identifier</strong> proving where it was recorded (distinguishing one office or hallway from another).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Digital watermarks:</strong> ensure integrity and prevent tampering, with varying legal acceptance.</li>\n<li>Consult an attorney or the law enforcement authority in the jurisdiction.</li>\n<li><strong>Audio:</strong> modern IP cameras capture it. Ask legal counsel whether it is legal and appropriate; it may vary by country.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Procedures</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Order</th>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1st</td>\n<td><strong>Sensitivity</strong></td>\n<td>Needed for a complete video signal under the lighting of the area to be assessed. It must match the lighting design objectives, whatever the sensor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2nd</td>\n<td>Sensor <strong>resolution</strong></td>\n<td>Defines how many cameras are needed along a straight perimeter. <strong>The higher the resolution, the greater the spacing between cameras.</strong> Object resolution is defined before choosing a camera, although it can be adjusted a little if there are few options.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3rd</td>\n<td>Camera <strong>format</strong></td>\n<td>Smaller formats = less sensitivity and less resolution. In exchange, lower price; but the camera is only part of the total cost. The format affects the field of view and the range of lenses available by focal length. Carefully evaluate special lenses with a nonstandard focal length.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Evaluate cameras under the <strong>actual lighting</strong> expected at the site.</li>\n<li>Experience from other installations helps narrow the options.</li>\n<li>Manufacturer literature <strong>should not be the only criterion</strong>: its specifications or test conditions may be unrealistic for the case.</li>\n<li>Whenever possible, <strong>demonstration in the environment of use</strong>.</li>\n<li>Other criteria: difficulty of maintenance; <strong>MTBF</strong> (mean time between failure); <strong>total cost of ownership</strong>, including power consumption; housing appropriate to the environment; manufacturer maintenance support; documentation.</li>\n<li>The documentation should include: operating, adjustment and maintenance procedures; theory of operation; block diagrams; schematics; manufacturer and commercial parts lists.</li>\n<li>Seriously consider <strong>rejecting any product without that documentation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Acceptance Testing</h5>\n<p><strong>Incoming inspection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Every camera purchased is inspected, whether for evaluation or for final installation, with different parameters.</li>\n<li>Evaluation cameras: compared with others purchased for the same purpose.</li>\n<li>Cameras for final installation: conformance to the manufacturer's specifications, compatibility with the design criteria and <strong>uniform performance across cameras</strong>.</li>\n<li>The final inspection at the factory is not consistent; performance can deviate widely and the equipment can be damaged in shipping.</li>\n<li><strong>Burn-in:</strong> operating the equipment for <strong>a few hundred hours</strong> continuously before installing it reduces maintenance problems at the perimeter site.</li>\n<li>Problems found are referred to the manufacturer <strong>while the warranty lasts</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Installation and resolution verification</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exterior cameras: install per the manufacturer and <strong>focus at night</strong> with the same lighting as normal operation.</li>\n<li>If possible, evaluate resolution before buying.</li>\n<li><strong>Target method:</strong><ul>\n<li>Shapes of <strong>1 ft (0.3 m)</strong>: circle, square and triangle.</li>\n<li>Painted <strong>black on one side and white on the other</strong>.</li>\n<li>Placed at the <strong>far end</strong> of the assessment zone; the operator must recognize each shape. This quickly shows whether the resolution is sufficient.</li>\n<li>Moved to light and dark areas: <strong>black side for dark areas, white side for bright areas</strong>.</li>\n<li>The 1 ft target simulates the <strong>cross-section of a person crawling</strong>.</li>\n<li>The size can vary with the threat; resolution charts also work in interior or exterior areas.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other aids: a large resolution chart in the area, or test targets such as the <strong>Rotakin</strong>.</li>\n<li>There are no accepted resolution standards for private integrators. The designer or the manager must <strong>define the required resolution and specify it</strong> in contracts and purchases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Test bench</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the laboratory it measures resolution, focus and sensitivity; it can be more cost-effective than performance tests.</li>\n<li>It is <strong>not enough</strong> to ensure performance in the protection system.</li>\n<li>Some cameras come prefocused, but in an environment different from the operating one.</li>\n<li>Afterward: indoor or outdoor tests and final adjustments of focus, sensitivity and field of view according to the actual light.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exterior lighting</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Survey with quality light meters and a <strong>grid</strong>: e.g., every <strong>3 ft (0.9 m)</strong>, at <strong>1 ft (0.3 m)</strong> above the ground.</li>\n<li>Do it when installing the lighting and <strong>repeat it every year</strong>.</li>\n<li>Prepare a <strong>preventive maintenance</strong> program for lamps: replace all at once or as they fail, depending on size and budget.</li>\n<li>Exterior replacement usually requires a <strong>bucket truck</strong>. If the site owns one, the schedule is more flexible than if it must be rented. It is also useful for exterior cameras.</li>\n<li>Over time, the data make it possible to set a routine replacement cycle.</li>\n<li><strong>Turning the lighting on:</strong> one photocell for all the lights; one cell per light, per side or per sector; or manual switching.</li>\n<li>The type of light affects the <strong>CRI</strong> (color rendition index). A poor CRI alters colors and harms evidence or prosecution.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the usual name is color rendering index (outside the book); the book says “color rendition index.” Same acronym, CRI.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Interior lighting</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Evaluate it continuously, with less effort than outdoors.</li>\n<li>There are lighting specifications for tasks such as reading, inspecting or office work.</li>\n<li>Almost always the work lighting is enough for video, but it must be <strong>verified</strong>.</li>\n<li>Watch for furniture or objects that move and create <strong>shadows or blind spots</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Subsystem speed and vendor acceptance tests</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Test that alarm detection and video capture are fast enough to capture the actual intrusion.</li>\n<li>Performance tests: alarms captured and reported <strong>within one second</strong>, camera switching times and recording times.</li>\n<li>It is strongly recommended to require <strong>acceptance tests</strong> from every vendor or integrator. They should cover:<ul>\n<li>Adequate resolution in the actual operating environment.</li>\n<li>Recording speed.</li>\n<li>Number of alarms that are acquired and stored for review in one second.</li>\n<li>Related details, such as the light-to-dark ratio.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Include the specifications and the acceptance test statement in the <strong>terms and conditions of the contract</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Withholding final payment until the acceptance test is passed</strong> ensures that the project gets finished.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Maintenance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>With incoming inspection and burn-in, problems are minimized in the short term.</li>\n<li><strong>Camera adjustment</strong> consumes most of the maintenance time.</li>\n<li><strong>Optical focus</strong> has always been a major, slow factor in installation. It is caused mainly by day-night lighting changes and changes in the energy spectrum.</li>\n<li>Focusing <strong>at night</strong>, with the scene lighting and from the final location, is more reliable.</li>\n<li>Sealed environmental housings greatly limit this procedure.</li>\n<li>Many new cameras are refocused remotely through the web interface (GUI, graphical user interface) or the VMS.</li>\n<li>Best person responsible: <strong>competent on-site staff</strong> who understand the concepts of the original design and know how to diagnose electronic systems.</li>\n<li>Periodic maintenance requirements come from the manufacturer, in writing.</li>\n<li>A <strong>nuisance alarm rate specification</strong> is advisable:<ul>\n<li>It allows some nuisance alarms without penalty.</li>\n<li>Value of occasional nuisance alarms: <strong>they maintain confidence that the system works</strong>.</li>\n<li>Example: <strong>one nuisance alarm per zone per day</strong>.</li>\n<li>Low enough to operate under varying conditions, but not so high that it creates a vulnerability: officers being tempted to ignore them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Investigate every recurring nuisance or false alarm to improve the system.</li>\n<li>After maintenance done by outside personnel, verify that everything works and <strong>has not been modified</strong>.</li>\n<li>Keep <strong>equipment logs</strong> of replacements and repairs.</li>\n<li>Spares: <strong>10% to 20%</strong>, especially of cameras, depending on budget and site size.</li>\n<li>If cameras are replaced with new or different models, test compatibility and performance and note it in the log.</li>\n<li><strong>Contingency plans</strong> for loss of video (for different periods or in one or several places): assign a guard to the location until repaired, or deploy portable systems.</li>\n<li>Keep the manufacturer's documentation <strong>on site and in a central file</strong>. The same goes for modifications made on site.</li>\n<li>Log of repairs and adjustments for each camera = history per piece of equipment. It shows trends, recurring failures and underperforming equipment, and greatly reduces repair time.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.4.8 Evaluation of Video Assessment Systems</h4>\n<p><strong>Parameters that determine the effectiveness of the video assessment subsystem</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Minimum time</strong> between the sensor alarm and the display of the video.</li>\n<li><strong>Complete coverage</strong> of the sensor's detection zone (called the <strong>assessment zone</strong> when sensors and video are integrated).</li>\n<li>Ability to <strong>classify a 1 ft (0.3 m) target</strong> at the far edge of the assessment zone.</li>\n<li><strong>Vertical field of view</strong> at the far edge of the exterior zone that spans the height of a standard fence (if there is one) and a person climbing over it.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuous 24/7 operation.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Minimum sensitivity to environmental conditions</strong> in all cameras.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimum obstruction</strong> of the assessment zone: trees, fences or junction boxes outdoors; furniture blocking the view indoors.</li>\n<li>Integration of the field of view and the recording system that <strong>shows the operator the source of the alarm</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>The further the subsystem departs from these requirements, the worse the image quality and the more performance degrades.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Classification vs. identification</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Classification</td>\n<td>Accurately distinguishing an object in the image by category.</td>\n<td>Human, animal, wind-blown trash or other.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identification</td>\n<td>Distinguishing between people. Some protection systems require it.</td>\n<td>“It's John, not Jim.”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Both depend on <strong>image quality</strong>, which is measured by <strong>video resolution</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What a VSS evaluation normally verifies</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>That cameras and pan/tilt/zoom controllers work.</li>\n<li>That date/time stamps and other on-screen text are accurate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Test targets</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple geometric shapes: <strong>circle, square and triangle of 1 ft (0.3 m)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Their size is based on a horizontal field of view with a required resolution of <strong>six horizontal television lines per foot</strong> (6 HTVL/ft, horizontal television lines).</li>\n<li>That resolution is enough to <strong>classify a crawling intruder</strong> with adequate lighting.</li>\n<li>If the expected threat always presents a larger profile, a lower horizontal resolution is accepted. The targets work in both cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Black on one side and white on the other</strong>: check resolution in dark and bright areas, respectively.</li>\n<li>A simple way to see whether they can be distinguished at the lighting extremes of the area.</li>\n<li>They work for <strong>black-and-white and color</strong> cameras.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How they are used</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are placed at several points in the assessment zone (or across the field of view, in a surveillance system). The operator must distinguish them.</li>\n<li><strong>The more targets clearly distinguished, the more confidence</strong> in the image quality.</li>\n<li>Locations are chosen that <strong>do not make</strong> identification easier: dark or bright areas, obstructed view or uneven surface.</li>\n<li>Valid for exterior and interior cameras.</li>\n<li>They test the black and white extremes of the subsystem against the background color of the area.</li>\n<li>Other quality aspects to consider: lighting, camera mounting, transmission system and integration of switchers and controllers to assess alarms.</li>\n<li><strong>Far-field resolution:</strong> the far field is the most distant point and has <strong>fewer lines per foot</strong>. Verifying it confirms that the horizontal resolution holds throughout the zone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recorded video</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Live quality is only one aspect.</li>\n<li>Not all alarms can be assessed live: multiple alarms, a distracted operator or an adversary running across the zone.</li>\n<li>That is why a <strong>recording and storage</strong> system is needed.</li>\n<li>Recording and playback must be fast and detailed enough to determine the cause of the alarm.</li>\n<li>If the response will be an after-the-fact review, speed matters less, as long as quality is sufficient.</li>\n<li>The test targets also verify the quality of recorded video.</li>\n<li>Depending on the medium and the configuration, the recorded image <strong>may have less resolution than the live image</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.4.9 Maintenance</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>If the VSS (cameras, recorders, monitors) does not work well, the organization is left:<ul>\n<li><strong>Vulnerable.</strong></li>\n<li>With <strong>delayed incident response</strong>.</li>\n<li>Exposed to <strong>legal liability</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Camera maintenance is considered <strong>before implementing</strong> the system.</li>\n<li>Recommended:<ul>\n<li>Sufficient spares.</li>\n<li>Trained staff <strong>or</strong> a service contract with a vendor or integrator.</li>\n<li>Periodic testing and operation of all video equipment (the book expands on this in later chapters).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.4.10 Future of Video Surveillance Systems</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Trend according to the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cameras</td>\n<td>Improving in light sensitivity and flexibility, lens quality, resolution (especially megapixel sensors) and image processing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video compression</td>\n<td>Will improve and greatly speed up data transfer between video devices and network equipment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Networked audio and video</td>\n<td>Rapid improvement with great impact on security.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wired and wireless networks</td>\n<td>More performance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Servers and storage</td>\n<td>Will hold more for less; the cloud will be even more affordable.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analytics, video management (VMS) and intelligent video</td>\n<td>Easier for the user to configure; new integrated functions and software scalability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Controls and services</td>\n<td>Remote and local controls and hosted services will continue to evolve.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"physical-11-e4\">11.5 Communications and Annunciation Systems</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>AC&amp;D, Alarm Communication and Display:</strong> the part of the electronic security system that carries alarm and assessment information to a central point. There it is presented to a human operator.</li>\n<li>Challenge: presenting the data effectively and reacting in time.</li>\n<li><strong>Two critical elements of the AC&amp;D:</strong><ol>\n<li>Transport or communication of the data.</li>\n<li>Presentation (display) of that data to the operator in an understandable way.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.5.1 AC&amp;D Attributes</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attribute</th>\n<th>What it requires</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Robustness</td>\n<td>A measure of performance in all likely environments. E.g., outdoor equipment that withstands large temperature changes without failing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reliability</td>\n<td>Durable components with a long MTBF (mean time between failures). Less maintenance and more operator confidence. Reliable communication and display, with no loss of information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Redundancy</td>\n<td>Backup for critical components, because every electronic component eventually fails.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timeliness</td>\n<td>The speed of the AC&amp;D must be a small fraction of the total assessment and response time. It must be a negligible factor in those calculations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security</td>\n<td>The AC&amp;D is a main component of the PPS (physical protection system). It must be protected against adversaries.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ease of use</td>\n<td>Show only the essentials, without overloading the operator. Efficient interaction and fast operations. Reduces training and retraining.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reliability:</strong> no system guarantees 100% delivery. Modern equipment approaches it with:<ul>\n<li>Data checking and verification techniques.</li>\n<li>Local and remote display of events.</li>\n<li>Storage of the history of events and responses.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Maximizing robustness + reliability + redundancy = minimum downtime (down).</li>\n<li><strong>AC&amp;D security in practice:</strong><ul>\n<li>Procedures that limit who accesses the displays and the configuration.</li>\n<li>Only authorized persons access information, components and wiring.</li>\n<li>Protect the alarm communication subsystem against attackers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Most important measure of effectiveness:</strong> how quickly and clearly it communicates sensor alarm data to the operator.</li>\n<li><strong>On an alarm, the AC&amp;D must tell the operator:</strong><ul>\n<li>Where it occurred.</li>\n<li>What or who caused it.</li>\n<li>When it occurred.</li>\n<li>What response or action is required.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The operator must know how to respond: through training and through system prompts.</li>\n<li><strong>Problem in measuring effectiveness (dual nature):</strong><ul>\n<li>Electronic communications are quantifiable: network topologies are modeled and times are measured.</li>\n<li>The response of the human operator is very hard to measure.</li>\n<li>For the human side, \"soft\" sciences are needed: ergonomics, human factors engineering and physiology studies.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>AC&amp;D subsystems (6):</strong><ol>\n<li>Communications.</li>\n<li>Line supervision and security.</li>\n<li>Information handling.</li>\n<li>Control and display.</li>\n<li>Assessment.</li>\n<li>Off-line.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book announces that it covers all six in detail. In fact it develops communication and line supervision (11.5.2–11.5.3), control and display (11.5.4) and video assessment (end of 11.5.4). Information handling and off-line are not developed.</p>\n<h4>11.5.2 Alarm Communication Subsystem</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Function:</strong> transfer data from one physical location to another. It goes from the point of acquisition (sensors) to the central repository (display).</li>\n<li>If the central repository has several computers or displays, it also moves data within that network.</li>\n<li><strong>Characteristics that guide the design:</strong><ul>\n<li>Amount of alarm data.</li>\n<li>High reliability required.</li>\n<li>Speed of data delivery (throughput).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Assured message delivery:</strong> if a sensor activates, the AC&amp;D computers must receive accurate data about that activation. It requires a reliable communication system.</li>\n<li>The alarm reporting speed is determined by: human factors + interaction between the AC&amp;D and the assessment system.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operating model</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Proprietary, in-house</td>\n<td>Ownership, operation, management and response with internal resources.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remote central station</td>\n<td>Alarms reach an outside operator, who has a list of agreed responses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hybrid</td>\n<td>Internal response supplemented by an outside central station under contract.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reporting times:</strong><ul>\n<li>For human factors: no perceptible delay for the operator = a few tenths of a second.</li>\n<li>AC&amp;D–assessment interaction: only milliseconds, a small fraction of the total assessment time.</li>\n<li>Communication is only one part of the total reporting time. That is why automation software is used to manage, prioritize and respond to incoming alarms.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Other design factors:</strong><ul>\n<li>Bandwidth of the physical medium sufficient to operate at full capacity.</li>\n<li><strong>Protocols:</strong> communication rules that the endpoints of a connection use when exchanging signals.</li>\n<li>System speed dictates the type of protocol. Protocol overhead must match the type of data.</li>\n<li>Balance channel bandwidth and protocol overhead to achieve the required speed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Ideal vs. reality:</strong> the ideal would be instantaneous and 100% reliable communication. It is impossible, and high speed + high reliability is expensive.</li>\n<li>Good design = a balance between cost and performance. Protocols are combined to balance speed, reliability and cost.</li>\n<li>Advances: alarm broadcasting by e-mail. Alarms not attended to within a predefined time are escalated to other people for response and notification.</li>\n<li><strong>Maximum-security or very complex systems:</strong><ul>\n<li>Redundant hardware against failures.</li>\n<li>Automatic routing of messages through that redundant hardware.</li>\n<li>Protocols that detect and correct errors and duplicate messages.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.5.3 Security Communications</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Task of the assets protection professional: address the relevant concerns when choosing a communications application. The growing variety of technologies complicates the task.</li>\n<li><strong>Strategies to mitigate damage to communications:</strong><ul>\n<li>Divergent routing.</li>\n<li>Two physically separate paths for the cables entering the facility.</li>\n<li>Hot/cold sites.</li>\n<li>Replacement agreements.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Media of choice:</strong> telephone, cellular, optical fiber, satellite and Internet/network connectivity.<ul>\n<li>Fiber: the cable preferred by terrestrial carriers.</li>\n<li>Satellite: versatile, useful in developing areas, with explosive growth.</li>\n<li>Network: is displacing the other methods.</li>\n<li>All modes must be evaluated to achieve maximum reliability.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The professional's 4 concerns in any communication:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concern</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrity of the communications medium</td>\n<td>Availability of the message path.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrity of the message</td>\n<td>Complete, error-free transmission.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timeliness</td>\n<td>The data arrive within an adequate time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Message security</td>\n<td>Only authorized persons have access.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Wire and Cable Communications</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alarms:</strong> pair of direct current (DC) conductors, shielded or unshielded.</li>\n<li>The sensor manufacturer usually specifies connectivity and cable, both power and signal.</li>\n<li><strong>Cable gauge and resistance:</strong><ul>\n<li>Resistance varies directly with the length of the line.</li>\n<li>It varies inversely with the diameter of the cable.</li>\n<li>Resistance limits the effective length of the line.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Audio:</strong> alternating current (AC) shielded twisted pairs. In telephony they are called voice-grade lines.</li>\n<li>Alarm and audio can travel together on the same shielded twisted pair.</li>\n<li><strong>Over the power line:</strong><ul>\n<li>Little used commercially: it is hard to pass the signal through transformers and quality is poor.</li>\n<li>US: power at 60 Hz (cycles per second). Other countries use other frequencies.</li>\n<li>A device couples the higher-frequency signals onto the AC cable.</li>\n<li>At the receiver the frequencies above 60 Hz are separated out and displayed or recorded. The normal 60 Hz current is not altered.</li>\n<li>Risks: a transformer can block the path, and interference can degrade or distort the signal.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> strictly, resistance depends on the cross-sectional area, that is, on the square of the diameter. The book simplifies it as \"inverse to the diameter.\"</p>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0463em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ρ</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3603em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<h5>Optical Fiber</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> strand of high-purity spun glass, about the thickness of a human hair.</li>\n<li>Transmits enormous volumes at the speed of light. Its capacity far exceeds that of a similar amount of copper.</li>\n<li><strong>Numerical example:</strong> a system at 565 Mbit/s (565,000,000 bits per second) supports 8,064 telephone conversations over fiber.</li>\n<li>Carries video, data and audio, one at a time or simultaneously, in one or two directions, over a single fiber.</li>\n<li><strong>How it works:</strong><ol>\n<li>A light source (laser or LED) introduces a modulated beam into the fiber.</li>\n<li>The beam reaches the other end with practically no attenuation or change.</li>\n<li>There it is decoded and the original information is recovered.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>LED = preferred light source:</strong> lasts longer without maintenance and is less sensitive to fluctuations in power, temperature and humidity.</li>\n<li>Carries voice-grade, video and digital or data signals.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Differences of fiber compared with metallic cable</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>It is not affected by EMI (electromagnetic interference) or RFI (radio frequency interference).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It does not conduct current or radiate signals. Very valuable where there are electrical storms and ground strikes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It carries many more multiplexed messages.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It is much smaller and lighter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It is flexible and can follow irregular paths.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It is not vulnerable to interception by acoustic or inductive coupling.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Video Transmission</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>There are products for sending video over DC lines, but they are not suitable for commercial use.</li>\n<li>Media: coaxial, optical fiber, standard telephone line and balanced twisted pairs. Wireless and network are growing.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Medium</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Distance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Coaxial</td>\n<td>No extra processing between camera and monitor if the distance is short.</td>\n<td>Typically 1,000 ft (305 m). Farther if amplified along the way.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Normal telephone line</td>\n<td>Video → digital → audio. At the receiver the audio goes back to video. The operator amplifies continuously and it can pass over cable, microwave or satellite.</td>\n<td>No theoretical limit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Optical fiber</td>\n<td>Conversion of video to an optical signal at the transmitter and reconversion at the receiver.</td>\n<td>1 mile (1.6 km) or more without amplification.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dedicated twisted pair</td>\n<td>No bridging, taps or other connections along the path. The equipment must do conversions, impedance matching and frequency compensation.</td>\n<td>Good performance up to 4,000 ft (1.2 km).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Fiber + digital telephone signal = real-time transmission, because fiber carries more data and faster than the copper pair.</li>\n<li>Fiber supports color or monochrome video.</li>\n<li>Over the same fiber as the video travel the control data: pan/tilt, zoom and auxiliaries (housing heaters and wipers).</li>\n<li>Three functions can travel simultaneously, in two directions, over a single fiber.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Status and Alarm Transmission</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>4 types of installation:</strong> loop, point-to-point, multiplex and wireless.</li>\n<li>They can use proprietary wired networks, leased lines and internal Wi-Fi networks.</li>\n<li>In any configuration, whoever gets to the alarm panel can disable the whole system. That is why the physical security of the panel or console is critical.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Loop</td>\n<td>Devices on a pair of wires that runs through an area, building or facility and reaches a control center. A signal indicates an anomaly in the loop.</td>\n<td>May be enough for a small space or a single facility.</td>\n<td>A short or a cut interrupts all signals beyond the cut. Cutting the loop at the right point can disable all the detectors in an area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Point-to-point</td>\n<td>Each sensor has its own pair of wires direct to the control center (hardwired).</td>\n<td>A line failure affects only one detector. The attacker would have to identify and disable the cable of each sensor.</td>\n<td>More expensive than the loop because it requires more cable.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiplexing</td>\n<td>Several signals at once over the same medium: they are encoded into a composite signal on a single circuit. At the receiver they are separated and go to a control unit for recording and display. Medium: cable, RF (radio frequency), microwave or fiber.</td>\n<td>May be more cost-effective over long distances. Reduces leased-line charges and gives more security. Large savings in wiring labor.</td>\n<td>Expensive equipment: do not assume it comes out cheaper. If the link is cut, all its signals go down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wireless</td>\n<td>See \"Wireless Communications\" and \"Wireless Alarm Signals\" below.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loop, recommended improvement:</strong> encode the signal of each detector to know its origin at the central point.</li>\n<li><strong>McCulloh circuit:</strong> partly corrects the loop problem.<ul>\n<li>Sends current from the control unit down both sides of the circuit to the point of the break.</li>\n<li>With one break (open), all the devices on both sides keep working.</li>\n<li>With two breaks, the devices between them are lost: there is no path to them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Multiplexing, when it applies:</strong> as the number of remote sources or sensors grows, the loop or one line per sensor stops being practical.<ul>\n<li>Always do a cost analysis for each system.</li>\n<li>Because of the savings in wiring labor, it usually has an advantage over hardwiring.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Redundant path for multiplexing</strong> (for large installations or high protected value):<ul>\n<li>Alternate multiplexed trunk.</li>\n<li>Digital communicator over telephone circuits between remote transponders and console.</li>\n<li>RF link between transponders and console.</li>\n<li>Its cost must enter the cost-benefit analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Figures:</strong> 11.6 Multiplexing on a Pair of Wires; 11.7 Elements of Multiplexing (process sequence); 11.8 Simplified Time Division Multiplex System. <strong>Pending:</strong> the graphic content of the three figures is not in the extracted text.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two methods to separate signals that share a path:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Principle</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>TDM (time division multiplexing)</td>\n<td>Each sensor has a time slot and transmits only in it. Same path, but not simultaneous.</td>\n<td>Channels are sampled in regular sequence and then it restarts. No channel is monitored continuously. Sampling must be so fast that the amplitude barely changes between samples. The small time differences are transparent to personnel.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FDM (frequency division multiplexing)</td>\n<td>Each sensor occupies a different portion of the spectrum on the common line.</td>\n<td>They are transmitted at the same time, but their frequency makes them identifiable at the receiver.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>FDM example from the book:</strong> three transducer outputs modulate three subcarrier frequencies.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Channel</th>\n<th>Center</th>\n<th>Peak deviation</th>\n<th>Occupied range (own calculation from the book's data)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>400 Hz</td>\n<td>±30 Hz</td>\n<td>370–430 Hz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>560 Hz</td>\n<td>±42 Hz</td>\n<td>518–602 Hz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>730 Hz</td>\n<td>±55 Hz</td>\n<td>675–785 Hz</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3175em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1076em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mop mtight\"><span class=\"mtight\">m</span><span class=\"mtight\">i</span><span class=\"mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">400</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">370</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Hz</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1076em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mop mtight\"><span class=\"mtight\">m</span><span class=\"mtight\">a</span><span class=\"mtight\">x</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">730</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">55</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">785</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Hz</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Total common band: 370 to 785 Hz. The channels do not overlap.</li>\n<li>Unused guard bands between channels ensure separation.</li>\n<li><strong>FDM receiver:</strong> bandpass filters separate the channels. Each goes to its discriminator, which demodulates and recovers the signal. The output ends at a display in the control center.</li>\n<li><strong>In both types:</strong> the demultiplexer must operate at exactly the same frequency as the multiplexer.</li>\n<li><strong>TDM:</strong> depends on precise timing. Multiplexers and demultiplexers must be exactly synchronized.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Wireless Communications</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Requirements (4):</strong><ol>\n<li>Transmitter that supplies radio frequency energy.</li>\n<li>Antenna that radiates that energy into the atmosphere.</li>\n<li>Receiver.</li>\n<li>Power for transmitter and receiver.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The transmitter modulates (varies) a carrier wave to represent the characteristics of the source signal.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Modulation</th>\n<th>What varies in the carrier</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>AM (amplitude modulation)</td>\n<td>The amplitude or range of the signal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FM (frequency modulation)</td>\n<td>The frequency.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The receiver tunes the signals, amplifies them and demodulates them. The original signal is reproduced on a printer or speaker.</li>\n<li><strong>Rule:</strong> all wireless communication that is unscrambled or unencrypted must be considered interceptable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Voice Radio</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The transmitting base station is usually in the control center, where the other alarm signals terminate.</li>\n<li>Security personnel use mobile or portable units.</li>\n<li>Units that talk directly to each other must be on the same frequency.</li>\n<li>A base with several frequencies can relay between units that do not share a frequency.</li>\n<li>Trend: communication between different products and protocols, radio to radio. Also integration of digital and cellular into a unified communications package.</li>\n<li><strong>What determines output power and cost:</strong> transmission distances, physical barriers and interference in the area.</li>\n<li>Repeater stations or remote transmitters may be needed. They receive the signal, amplify it and retransmit it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Wireless Alarm Signals</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>At the protected site:</strong> alarm detection array + RF interface module.</li>\n<li><strong>The provider supplies:</strong> network of RF receiver sites (base stations), linked by redundant leased lines to its central station.</li>\n<li>Some networks have thousands of base stations and cover almost the whole country.</li>\n<li><strong>Sequence:</strong><ol>\n<li>The sensor activates.</li>\n<li>An omnidirectional antenna sends a coded alarm signal to all base stations in range.</li>\n<li>The station that receives the strongest signal passes it over a leased line to the central station.</li>\n<li>The central station sends an acknowledgment to the site panel over the same channel.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>RF or digital cable can be chosen. <strong>Dual mode:</strong> the alarm travels over both paths at once.</li>\n<li>The central station can poll the system at regular intervals to verify that it works.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Cordless Telephones</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Normally not used in security operations. But a security call to an executive's residence might be answered on one.</li>\n<li>Components: base and handset, both transceivers, linked by radio frequency.</li>\n<li><strong>Nominal range:</strong> 700–1,000 ft (213–305 m). May be greater depending on the equipment or atmospheric conditions.</li>\n<li>US law prohibits intercepting them, but listening to the neighbor's calls is not unusual.</li>\n<li>Historically, a hook switch bypass turned a telephone into a hidden microphone, whether in use or not.</li>\n<li><strong>More security:</strong> certain frequency ranges, security codes and multiple frequency settings.</li>\n<li><strong>Basic rule:</strong> do not discuss sensitive information on any cordless telephone call.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Cellular Telephones</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>In countries without a developed fixed network, several generations were skipped: digital cellular is widespread.</li>\n<li><strong>Principle:</strong> the mobile user has a radio link, via a computer-controlled switching center, to the fixed network or another mobile.</li>\n<li><strong>Cellular concept:</strong><ul>\n<li>The service area is divided into cells grouped in clusters. Cells can have any shape and are not uniform.</li>\n<li>Each cluster receives a group of frequencies. Adjacent cells use different frequencies.</li>\n<li>A frequency is not repeated within the same cluster. The same pattern is repeated in the neighboring cluster.</li>\n<li>The power level keeps the signal from reaching cells of other clusters with the same frequency.</li>\n<li>Each cell has at least one cell site: low-power transmitters, receivers, control system and antennas.</li>\n<li>Dedicated voice and data trunks link the cells to a computer. It controls the cells, connects to the fixed network, captures billing and controls the activity of the units.</li>\n<li><strong>Handoff:</strong> when changing cells, the call passes to the new cell on another frequency. This lets many users use the frequencies at once.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Service</th>\n<th>US</th>\n<th>Other countries</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cellular in general</td>\n<td>800 MHz or 1,900 MHz</td>\n<td>900 MHz or 1,800 MHz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analog: AMPS (advanced mobile phone service)</td>\n<td>800 MHz</td>\n<td>Often incompatible with local technology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Digital: DCS (digital cellular service), also called PCS (personal communication service)</td>\n<td>1,900 MHz</td>\n<td>900 MHz or 1,800 MHz</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The professional should not assume that service in one area is compatible with that of another. Multifrequency phones solve many of those problems.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interception</th>\n<th>Analog (AMPS)</th>\n<th>Digital (DCS/PCS)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What the interceptor gets</td>\n<td>Clear signal, usable immediately.</td>\n<td>A bit stream that cannot be used immediately.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Residual risk</td>\n<td>Total.</td>\n<td>It can be recorded and converted to analog to reveal the content.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Signal compression:</strong> two digital ways to make better use of the spectrum in cellular and PCS. They are incompatible with each other.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>TDMA (time division multiple access)</td>\n<td>Divides calls into pieces of data. The receiver identifies them by the assigned time slot.</td>\n<td>One cellular channel handles several calls at once.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CDMA (code division multiple access)</td>\n<td>Spreads call segments over a wide range of frequencies. Each segment carries a code from the originating phone, which the receiver uses to reconstruct the signal.</td>\n<td>Capacity 10 to 20 times greater than analog cellular.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uses of cellular in security (5):</strong><ul>\n<li>Backup for the wired network in transmitting alarms.</li>\n<li>Disasters that interrupt the wired network.</li>\n<li>Hostage situations.</li>\n<li>Backup for routine security communications.</li>\n<li>Mobile applications that control AC&amp;D products and software suites.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Scrambling in cellular:</strong><ul>\n<li>Normally a scrambler is needed at the origin and another at the destination.</li>\n<li>With a telephone privacy service, the customer needs only one.</li>\n<li><strong>Procedure:</strong> 1) from its phone with a privacy device, it calls a toll-free number of the provider, which has similar equipment; 2) the privacy network gives it another dial tone; 3) it dials the destination number.</li>\n<li>The device at the origin scrambles the voice and the one at the privacy network restores it.</li>\n<li>The voices of both parties travel securely over the cellular network to the privacy service's switch.</li>\n<li>Two cell phones with the device can call each other directly and securely.</li>\n<li>Only calls that need privacy go through the service. The others are made normally.</li>\n<li>Supports secure domestic and international calls. The provider can assign a private toll-free number to receive secure calls.</li>\n<li>There is a version for an office or home landline, compatible with fax.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> with the privacy service, the book guarantees only the leg up to the provider's switch.</p>\n<h5>Private Fixed Wireless Systems</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wireless PABX:</strong> low-power transmitter that communicates with portable phones within a limited range.<ul>\n<li>Main advantage: two-way communication with employees who move around the facility.</li>\n<li>Risk: as with digital cellular, it is intercepted with a receiver tuned to that frequency.</li>\n<li>Transmission compression, such as TDMA, adds some security.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book develops PABX as \"private automatic board exchange.\" The usual term is \"private automatic branch exchange\" (outside the book).</li>\n<li><strong>Local digital fixed wireless:</strong><ul>\n<li>The US FCC (Federal Communications Commission) allowed providers to compete in local service.</li>\n<li>The competitor is expected to lease facilities from the local provider that already has cables.</li>\n<li>A large provider created a system where low-power transmitters serve several properties in an area. Each property has a device that sends and receives voice and data.</li>\n<li>It eliminates installing or leasing wiring. Its security is yet to be evaluated.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Satellite Communications</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>GEO (geostationary earth orbit) satellites are at high altitude and have inherent signal delay. It is a problem for some applications.</li>\n<li>Delay-sensitive communications benefit from LEO (low earth orbit) systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Footprint:</strong> wide reception area. Any properly tuned receiver within it receives the signal.</li>\n<li><strong>Rule:</strong> all satellite communication must be considered susceptible to interception.</li>\n<li><strong>Portable satellite phones:</strong> today they cost about US$500 with the antenna in the cover. In 1993 they cost US$20,000.</li>\n<li>They can be compatible with cellular networks and switch depending on whether there is cellular coverage.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>System</th>\n<th>Altitude</th>\n<th>Main use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>GEO + global network of earth stations</td>\n<td>22,300 mi (36,000 km), fixed position</td>\n<td>TV, digital voice, fax, data and e-mail.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MEO (medium earth orbit)</td>\n<td>6,500 mi (10,300 km)</td>\n<td>Dual-mode portable phones (satellite + cellular), phones on ships and aircraft, fixed phones in developing areas.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>28 LEO satellites</td>\n<td>480 mi (770 km)</td>\n<td>Data messages for people and industries: monitoring of industrial installations and tracking of truck trailers and barges.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>66 LEO satellites</td>\n<td>421 mi (675 km)</td>\n<td>Dual-mode portable phones, paging, low-speed data and fax.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>48 LEO satellites</td>\n<td>763 mi (1,220 km)</td>\n<td>Worldwide mobile and fixed telephony.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>840 LEO satellites</td>\n<td>440 mi (700 km)</td>\n<td>Characteristics of terrestrial fiber and coaxial networks. No delay: suited to delay-sensitive applications.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>840-LEO system:</strong> transmits at high frequency, 30 GHz uplink and 20 GHz downlink. That allows small, low-power terminals and antennas.</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> 6,500 mi is equivalent to about 10,460 km, not 10,300 km. For the exam, use the book's figures.</li>\n<li><strong>Transportation:</strong> companies in the US and in developing areas equip trucks with two-way satellite messaging.<ul>\n<li>The message for the vehicle must be typed.</li>\n<li>Advantages: the driver concentrates on driving and the message arrives even if the vehicle is unoccupied.</li>\n<li>With GPS (global positioning system), the base interrogates a transponder and knows the vehicle's location at any time.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Wireless Interference</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Good reception depends on the equipment distinguishing the desired signals from the undesired ones and from noise.</li>\n<li><strong>Interference:</strong> unwanted signals. It can appear in any radio system.</li>\n<li><strong>Most common causes:</strong> signals from other transmitters, industrial noise and atmospheric noise.</li>\n<li>There are standards on frequency deviation tolerance and maximum authorized power. Interference can still occur because:<ul>\n<li>Another transmitter let its frequency drift.</li>\n<li>Atmospheric conditions bring in a station of the same frequency from elsewhere in the world.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of noise</th>\n<th>Sources and traits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Man-made</td>\n<td>Electrical transients radiated by circuits with electric arcs: switches, motors, ignition systems, industrial precipitators, high-frequency heating and other equipment. It affects AM more than FM: using FM is one way to avoid it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural</td>\n<td>Shows up as static. Example: an electrical storm.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Noise in video:</strong> momentary interruptions of the image, lines or waves on the screen, or \"snow.\"</li>\n<li><strong>Effect of construction:</strong> heavy steel and concrete limit the radio and video reception distance. Dead spaces are common.</li>\n<li><strong>Usual solution:</strong> antennas connected to the receiver, or antennas that radiate from a loop or run laid throughout the building.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Microwave Transmissions</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Operates at \"super high\" frequencies, between 30 and 300 billion Hz (30–300 GHz).</li>\n<li><strong>Components (4):</strong> microwave generator, power amplifier, means of modulating the carrier and antenna to transmit into the atmosphere.</li>\n<li>Supports one-way or two-way communication. The transmitting antennas can also receive.</li>\n<li>Because of the frequencies and powers used, it often requires an FCC license.</li>\n<li>Passes through rain, fog and snow. It is not affected by man-made noise.</li>\n<li><strong>Uses:</strong> TV transmission, multiplexed telephone signals, multiplexed alarm circuits and high-speed data transfer.</li>\n<li>Travels in a straight line (line of sight). If it hits an object it is reflected, with possible loss of energy depending on the object's shape, size and reflectivity.</li>\n<li><strong>How to get around obstacles:</strong><ul>\n<li><strong>Passive reflector:</strong> surface where the microwave bounces like a billiard carom. Any flat surface works, such as the side of a building, or a metal plate designed for it.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeater:</strong> another microwave station at a third point with a view of both ends. It also serves to extend the range. It uses the same hardware as a terminal.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Short distances:</strong> it can be cost-effective in protection systems.<ul>\n<li>The transmitter is connected to an outdoor antenna with a low-voltage power cable and a signal cable. It requires no conduit or elaborate installation.</li>\n<li>At the receiver: metal reflector or antenna, connected to the receiver with a power cable and a signal cable.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> in the usual classification, 30–300 GHz is EHF (extremely high frequency). SHF (super high frequency) is 3–30 GHz. For the exam, use the book's figure.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> erratum in the book, it writes \"line of site\" for \"line of sight.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Laser Communication</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>LASER = light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation.</strong></li>\n<li>Laser light is coherent (highly focused in one direction). It can be modulated to carry signals, like radio waves.</li>\n<li><strong>How it works:</strong><ol>\n<li>The beam passes through a crystal. An electric field applied to the crystal modulates the polarization of the monochromatic light.</li>\n<li>The audio, video or data signal enters the transmitter circuit and changes that electric field.</li>\n<li>The crystal rotates the axis of the beam and varies the transmitted light. The beam flickers at a very high rate that corresponds to the information.</li>\n<li>At the receiver, a photodetector follows the changes in intensity and recovers the changes in the electric field.</li>\n<li>An electronic circuit converts the electrical signal into conventional audio or video.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>It is practically impossible to intercept the beam without being detected.</li>\n<li>Requires line of sight. Range of up to 4 mi (6.4 km) without repeaters.</li>\n<li>Without line of sight, the beam is reflected off one or several mirrors. Each reflection reduces resolution quality.</li>\n<li>Snow, fog and rain interfere. This is compensated for by expanding the beam.</li>\n<li>Does not require FCC approval.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Comparison</th>\n<th>Microwave</th>\n<th>Laser</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Line of sight</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weather</td>\n<td>Passes through rain, fog and snow.</td>\n<td>Snow, fog and rain interfere; compensated for by expanding the beam.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FCC license</td>\n<td>Often yes</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Obstacles</td>\n<td>Passive reflector or repeater.</td>\n<td>Mirrors, with loss of resolution at each reflection.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interception</td>\n<td>The book does not say.</td>\n<td>Almost impossible without being detected.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Range</td>\n<td>The book gives no figure.</td>\n<td>Up to 4 mi (6.4 km) without repeaters.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Interconnection</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FCC rule (US):</strong> private systems may connect to the public telephone network if the equipment is safely compatible with it.</li>\n<li>The equipment must comply with <strong>Part 68</strong> of the FCC rules.</li>\n<li>When connecting, the telephone company must be notified of the FCC registration number and the ringer equivalency rating of the equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>Bundling:</strong> one provider, such as a cable company, delivers TV, network and voice over a single path. It is very common.<ul>\n<li>For conventional digital alarm communicators to work, the panel must be compatible with the carrier's system.</li>\n<li>The cable company must provide a modem interface, called VoIP (voice over Internet Protocol).</li>\n<li>VoIP converts the analog alarm signal to digital. It is converted back to analog on arrival at the monitoring company.</li>\n<li>This configuration has a higher probability of failure: consider alternate communication paths.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Copper:</strong> traditional telephony uses copper pairs because they support DC and AC voltage.<ul>\n<li>Much of that infrastructure fails, is expensive to maintain and does not support new network technology.</li>\n<li>That is why digital alarm communicators/dialers are quickly becoming obsolete.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Communications Security: Line Protection</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The switched telephone network, dedicated telephone lines or proprietary circuits can be used.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical measures:</strong><ul>\n<li>Exterior wiring: underground.</li>\n<li>Interior wiring: in conduits.</li>\n<li>Ask the telecommunications provider for an underground service entrance.</li>\n<li>In very vulnerable situations, take the service drop from a farther pole, not the nearest one, to conceal the cable path.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If a conventional telephone path is used, such as a digital dialer, also use an alternate path: cellular or radio.</li>\n<li>Local and long-distance service is open to competition. The large networks are very reliable, but there have been several large-scale outages.<ul>\n<li>Thoroughly evaluate the reliability of the proposed provider.</li>\n<li>Splitting the needs between two providers gives some assurance of continuity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Line Supervision</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Every wired system must have line supervision: it checks the circuits automatically and immediately signals line faults.</li>\n<li><strong>Simplest form:</strong> end-of-line resistor (EOL). It introduces a constant, measurable current.<ul>\n<li>A variation beyond a set threshold generates an alarm.</li>\n<li>Normally detects three faults: open circuit (broken connection), ground and wire-to-wire short.</li>\n<li>An attempt to tap or defeat the circuit usually causes a variation and is detected.</li>\n<li><strong>Limit:</strong> a tampering that barely changes the circuit value is not detected by basic supervision.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>High-value assets or information, other methods (4):</strong><ol>\n<li>Reduce the variation allowed in the circuit value.</li>\n<li>Quasi-random pulses that the control equipment must recognize.</li>\n<li>Changes in the transmission frequency.</li>\n<li>IP network communicators with encryption. They are becoming the preferred method for communicating and monitoring alarms off-premises.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Communication</th>\n<th>What someone who accesses the line gets</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Analog</td>\n<td>Mere audio reproduction of the source signal (voice, instrument or other tone). It sounds intelligible. That is why devices that alter intelligibility were created.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Digital</td>\n<td>The voice is converted to a binary stream (zeros and ones). Only bits are obtained, not immediately intelligible. It could be recorded and converted to analog, but the content may still be protected by encryption.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Scramblers</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>What travels over copper on the telephone network can be intercepted at any point in the circuit.</li>\n<li><strong>Scrambler:</strong> tool that disguises information so that it is unintelligible to a listener.</li>\n<li><strong>Selection criterion:</strong> the importance of the information and the skill of whoever might intercept it.</li>\n<li>Voice has two modifiable traits: frequency (pitch) and amplitude (loudness). Scramblers always distort the frequency.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Weaknesses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Frequency inverter, Figure 11.9</td>\n<td>The simplest: inverts the frequency content of the voice.</td>\n<td>Low cost. Tolerates poor channel conditions.</td>\n<td>A trained listener understands the voice. It is a single-code device: anyone with a similar scrambler can break it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Band splitter, Figure 11.10</td>\n<td>Extension of the inverter: divides the voice band into smaller bands, which are inverted and swapped before transmission.</td>\n<td>With 5 bands there are in theory 3,080 combinations.</td>\n<td>90% of those combinations are no more secure than an inverter. A trained listener obtains a lot of information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rolling band splitter</td>\n<td>Continuously changes how the bands are swapped, following a predetermined pattern.</td>\n<td>Normally cannot be broken by trial and error of combinations.</td>\n<td>A trained listener still obtains some information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frequency or phase modulator</td>\n<td>Frequency: inverts the spectrum and shifts it continuously following a pattern. Phase: the same, but changes the phase of the wave.</td>\n<td>Similar to the rolling band splitter, but usually more secure: the pattern changes more often.</td>\n<td>A trained listener can still extract some intelligence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Masking</td>\n<td>Modifies the amplitude by adding another signal in the voice band. It is removed at the receiver. The mask can be a single tone, switched tones or RF noise.</td>\n<td>Very effective at destroying or interfering with part of the syllabic content.</td>\n<td>Alone, it is not very secure: the voice is understood fairly easily through the mask.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Key combination:</strong> masking + band splitter, or masking + frequency or phase modulator = high degree of security.</li>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> the graphic content of Figures 11.9 (Frequency Inverter) and 11.10 (Band Splitter) is not in the extracted text.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rolling codes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rolling band splitters, frequency and phase modulators and many scramblers with masking change their format periodically and in a predetermined way.</li>\n<li><strong>Usual rate of change:</strong> between 100 times per second and once every 10 seconds.</li>\n<li><strong>Keystream:</strong> the electronic control signals that change the format.<ul>\n<li>Ideally it would be totally random, so that no one can duplicate it.</li>\n<li>But it must be predetermined, because the receiving scrambler has to reproduce an identical one.</li>\n<li>It has a fixed pattern length and then repeats. Typical length: between 15 bits and several billion bits.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudo-random generator:</strong> electronic shift register with well-connected feedback. It makes it cheap and easy to generate long keystreams.</li>\n<li>Keystream length, with N = number of register stages:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9747em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8913em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Book example:</strong> 23-stage register → keystream of 8,388,607 bits. Changing 10 times per second, it repeats every 9.7 days.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9474em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">23</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">388</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">607</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> bits</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\"> </span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">s</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.7401em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.989em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">−</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">388</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">607</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">838</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">861</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> s</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">9.7</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> days</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Second trait of the generator:</strong> many connection configurations, each with a completely different maximum-length keystream. The 23-stage register has 364,722 configurations or codes.</li>\n<li>A scrambler cannot decode what is transmitted by another programmed with different codes.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the extracted text of the book shows \"2N-1\". It must be read as 2 to the power N, minus 1: that is what matches 8,388,607.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost of scramblers:</strong> all of them degrade voice quality.<ul>\n<li>It is worse when the link is already degraded.</li>\n<li>Those with the highest security demand more link performance than those with lower security.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Digitized communication:</strong> can be encrypted with any data encryption method. With a fast computer, the conversation is in real time and the parties do not notice the countermeasures.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.5.4 Alarm Control and Display</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Control and display subsystem:</strong> gives information to the security operator and lets the operator enter commands that affect the operation of the AC&amp;D.</li>\n<li><strong>Goal:</strong> support rapid assessment of alarms.</li>\n<li>The alarm display equipment is the operator's console. It receives the information from the sensors.</li>\n<li><strong>Concerns in console design (4):</strong><ol>\n<li>What information is shown to the operator.</li>\n<li>How it is presented.</li>\n<li>How the operator communicates with the system.</li>\n<li>How the equipment is laid out at the operator's station.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Effective subsystem:</strong><ul>\n<li>Presents the information quickly and clearly.</li>\n<li>Responds quickly to commands.</li>\n<li>Does not overwhelm with detail: only what is necessary.</li>\n<li>Offers only the controls pertinent to the context of the current display.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Information that helps zone security (10):</strong><ul>\n<li>Zone status: access, secure, alarm or tamper (access/secure/alarm/tamper).</li>\n<li>Location of the zone.</li>\n<li>Time of the alarm.</li>\n<li>Hazards or special materials associated with the zone.</li>\n<li>Instructions for special actions.</li>\n<li>Phone numbers of the people to call.</li>\n<li>Maps of the zone.</li>\n<li>Video, where available.</li>\n<li>Retrieval of video before and after the alarm (pre- and post-alarm video call up).</li>\n<li>Status-change icons with color and animation, to make use easier.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It is necessary to analyze how the operator is alerted that action is needed: type of display equipment, format and visual features of the information, and design of the input equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Ergonomics: Human Factors</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The subsystem must be designed to serve the human operator.</li>\n<li>Normal temperature, humidity, noise and comfort promote effectiveness and reduce frustration and fatigue.</li>\n<li>Adjustable lighting to see the video monitors well.</li>\n<li>The console must facilitate the system–operator exchange: alarm reports, status indications and commands.</li>\n<li>The data must show their interrelationships. Human–machine input techniques must limit errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Workspace design considers (3):</strong><ul>\n<li>What the operator must see: people, equipment, displays and controls.</li>\n<li>What the operator must hear: other operators, communication equipment and warning indicators.</li>\n<li>What the operator must reach and manipulate: hand or foot controls and communication equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Around the operator there are zones of differing accessibility and visibility. All displays must be nearly perpendicular to the line of sight and visible from the normal position.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area (Figure 11.11)</th>\n<th>What is placed there</th>\n<th>Movement required</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary (primary interface area)</td>\n<td>The most important indications and inputs, already prioritized.</td>\n<td>Little eye or head movement: within a 30-degree viewing cone.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secondary</td>\n<td>Frequently used operating displays.</td>\n<td>Move the eyes, not the head.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Beyond secondary</td>\n<td>Rarely used support displays, such as backup systems and power indicators.</td>\n<td>The book does not specify.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> the graphic content of Figure 11.11 (Placement of Operator Controls in an AC&amp;D Console) is not in the extracted text.</li>\n<li><strong>Audible signals:</strong> the operator does not always look at the panel, so they announce significant status changes.<ul>\n<li>Different tones and volumes distinguish classes of alarm: security, safety or maintenance.</li>\n<li>Computerized voice can also be used.</li>\n<li>Few types of audible signal, and clearly distinguishable in the complex sound environment of the control room.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Console layout:</strong> displays in the center, with easy-to-identify controls above, below or around them.<ul>\n<li>Clarity: orderly labeling, color coding, well-spaced grouping and shape coding.</li>\n<li>Each control near its display: less searching and less eye movement.</li>\n<li>Touch panels put the controls on the display and eliminate many other devices.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Visual signals:</strong> flashing lights or blinking messages for significant information.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Traffic-light color</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Red</td>\n<td>Alarm / action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yellow</td>\n<td>Caution / abnormal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Green</td>\n<td>Proceed / normal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Support equipment:</strong> located according to its importance and frequency of use.<ul>\n<li>Communication equipment needs adequate space on the console: microphones, telephones, additional video monitors and controls.</li>\n<li>What is not needed for display and control goes outside the operator's immediate space.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Separate room</strong> for computers and automatic control circuits: video switching and communications electronics, except microphones and controls. Advantages:<ul>\n<li>More space for maintenance personnel.</li>\n<li>Maintenance does not disturb operators.</li>\n<li>Less distracting noise, such as from fans.</li>\n<li>Better protection of the equipment against tampering.</li>\n<li>Environmental conditions suited to the equipment. E.g., cooling and humidity that would be uncomfortable for operators.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Several operators at once:</strong> consider the operator–equipment interrelationships.<ul>\n<li>Duplicate the essential equipment for each operator.</li>\n<li>Share the secondary or infrequently used equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Ergonomics: Graphical Displays</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Well-designed GUIs (graphical user interfaces) improve alarm display. Poorly designed ones quickly overwhelm the operator.</li>\n<li><strong>Good graphical annunciator:</strong> few functions. It limits the ways of showing information and restricts the operations allowed.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Rules from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Windows</td>\n<td>Maximum 3 at a time. One full-screen with the overview of system status. Another smaller one with subordinate information, no larger than half the screen. A third with menus or other controls. The operator should not have to resize or move windows.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Menus</td>\n<td>At the top of the window; can be nested. Maximum 9 items and 3 levels. Common commands do not go in menus, but as buttons.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Buttons</td>\n<td>Work as push buttons. Only commands valid in the current context. Sensor or map icons can act as buttons. Grouped in bars. Maximum 9 visible, with a text label indicating their function.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maps</td>\n<td>Scanned from paper or electronic graphics. The best are stylized drawings without excessive detail. Small scale of approximately 1:5,000. They must be interactive: show the sensors and allow operating them from the graphic.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sensor icons</td>\n<td>Consistent graphics, sizes and colors. Group sensors into a single icon when possible. Maximum 50 sensor or group icons per map. The group icon shows the status of the worst sensor: if one is in alarm, the group shows alarm.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text</td>\n<td>Dedicated areas with sensor descriptions. Only what is vital; the details go in subordinate windows. Quick help must be available.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Color</td>\n<td>Sparingly. Do not rely on color: 10% of the population has some degree of color blindness. Maximum 7 colors. Menus, buttons and backgrounds in consistent tones, often gray. Maps in black and white or with low-saturation colors. Red, yellow and green reserved for sensor status.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Why short menus:</strong> in deeply nested menus the user gets lost. A short menu reduces search time. Complicated ones intimidate novices and annoy experts.</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> in cartography, 1:5,000 is considered large scale (a lot of detail). The book calls it \"small-scale.\" For the exam, remember the figure 1:5,000.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Design philosophy: \"operator first.\"</strong> Rules (7):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Minimize the actions per command. One important command = one click or a single key.</li>\n<li>Only operations valid in context. E.g., not being able to grant access to a sensor that is already in access.</li>\n<li>Guide the operator through complex operations. Context-based command selection directs without taking control away.</li>\n<li>Do not interrupt operations in progress. If the operator is assessing an alarm, the new alarm does not replace the screen: a nonintrusive notification arrives and the operator decides whether to abort.</li>\n<li>Do not be annoying: avoid loud, continuous alarms or bright, flashing displays.</li>\n<li>Every command must be doable in several ways: menu, button and key. The user chooses.</li>\n<li>Screens customized per user: nothing is omitted, only the presentation is adapted to each one.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book recommends flashing lights and messages for what is significant, and then asks to avoid bright, flashing displays. It is reconciled this way: flashing to highlight, never continuous or annoying.</li>\n<li><strong>Central message:</strong> the AC&amp;D exists to improve the security of the site. If it fails at that, it fails as a system.<ul>\n<li>Elaborate graphics do not save an ineffective system.</li>\n<li>An easy-to-use system is more likely to succeed than an unnecessarily complex one.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Video Alarm Assessment</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Assessment</td>\n<td>Associates the immediate capture of an image with a sensor alarm to decide the response.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Surveillance</td>\n<td>Collects video without associated sensors.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Video alarm assessment system:</strong> cameras in the assessment areas, monitors at the local end, and transmission, switching and recording systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Main component (7)</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Camera and lens</td>\n<td>Convert the optical image of the scene into an electrical signal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lighting</td>\n<td>Illuminates the alarm location evenly and with sufficient intensity for camera and lens.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transmission</td>\n<td>Connects the remote cameras with the local monitors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video switching</td>\n<td>Connects the signals of several cameras to monitors and recorders.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video recording</td>\n<td>Produces a record of the event.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitors</td>\n<td>Convert the electrical signal into a visual scene.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video controller</td>\n<td>Interface between the alarm sensor system and the assessment system.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The required resolution depends on (4):</strong> the expected threat, the expected tactics, the asset to be protected and the use that will be made of the video.</li>\n<li>Assessment performance must support the objectives of the protection system.</li>\n<li>The assessment subsystem is designed as a component of the intrusion detection system (IDS).</li>\n<li>The interactions among video, intrusion sensors and display must be considered.</li>\n<li><strong>Book summary on the AC&amp;D:</strong> it is key to a successful and timely response.<ul>\n<li>It controls the flow of information from sensor to operator and shows it quickly and clearly.</li>\n<li>It collects alarm data, presents the information and lets the operator give commands.</li>\n<li>Goal of the display: rapid assessment of alarms.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the close says that operator loading was covered. In these pages it appears only implicitly: do not overload the operator with data.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-11-e5\">11.6 Trends and Issues in Electronic Systems Integration</h3><p>The book lists 12 trends:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Trend</th>\n<th>Key idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Big data</td>\n<td>One of the strongest technology trends: it affects everything from the economy to the climate and the forecasting of flu outbreaks. In security, it helps process large volumes of video and of alarm inputs and outputs. Analyzing large data sets will be the basis of competition, productivity and innovation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data storage technologies</td>\n<td>Exponential growth. From early magnetic storage to micro-drives, SD cards, Blu-ray discs, xD cards, WMV-HD, HD-TV and cloud backup.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analytics</td>\n<td>Ability to distinguish patterns in the data. Requires a lot of computing and depends on algorithms and software. \"Perfect storm\": big data + storage + optimization and analysis software.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standards</td>\n<td>Pressure on manufacturers to meet communication standards and database architecture standards, and thus integrate into a global security control system. Video and building management systems lead, with standards already consolidated. Leading devices will perform better and meet standards.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data aggregation</td>\n<td>Managing diverse databases from a single interface, thanks to standards. Makes it possible to take advantage of legacy control systems. Proprietary protocols will cease to thrive. Compatibility with different operating systems is crucial for reliability and performance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NFC (near field communication)</td>\n<td>Lets smartphones and other devices communicate by radio. The standard covers protocols and is based on existing RFID (radio frequency identification) standards.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smart cards</td>\n<td>More storage allows data to be kept on the credential itself. Miniaturization will bring new applications and perhaps more attacks on the card and its electronics, in hardware and software. The industry will have to adopt broad standards of functionality and security.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smart cards + biometrics</td>\n<td>The combination usually yields a trusted credential. Source cited: Smart Card Alliance Physical Access Council (www.smartcardalliance.org). It will probably change the identification landscape.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Managed services</td>\n<td>Cloud to store and retrieve data and applications, and remote third-party services for a recurring fee. Some organizations are not attracted to it; others value reducing staff or not buying software.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Product hybridization</td>\n<td>Formerly independent products merge. E.g., smoke detectors with mini-cameras, microphones and inert gas monitoring. Better communications + standardization = more hybrid products.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Robotics</td>\n<td>First, mechanical arms for repetitive tasks in manufacturing. Later, \"humanization\" for tasks with human attributes. Advances in health care and services for the elderly will probably lead to robotic security functions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E-learning training tools</td>\n<td>New suite of training software under continuous improvement, for users of legacy and new systems, with case-specific training.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NFC, details:</strong><ul>\n<li>Driven by the proliferation of smartphones and mobile devices.</li>\n<li>Not all mobile networks are ready to commercialize it, but new phones already come with the hardware.</li>\n<li>Platforms are emerging to track all kinds of goods.</li>\n<li><strong>Uses:</strong> payments, marketing and promotion, transportation and ticketing, education, health care, municipal services, remote work and connectivity, and identification (IDs).</li>\n<li>There are already smart card integration kits for contactless cards, and more adaptations are coming.</li>\n<li>Goal for businesses: secure access from mobile phones.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Managed services, available applications:</strong><ul>\n<li>Access control.</li>\n<li>ID and credentialing.</li>\n<li>Video viewing.</li>\n<li>Virtual guard tours.</li>\n<li>Unattended delivery.</li>\n<li>Video escort.</li>\n<li>Remote opening of perimeter checkpoints.</li>\n<li>In some cases: backup after evacuating the site, or coverage of posts after hours without on-site staff.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>E-learning, benefits:</strong><ul>\n<li>Create, publish and manage repeatable enterprise-wide training and site-level awareness programs.</li>\n<li>Reduces the costs of training operators and administrators.</li>\n<li>Improves compliance training in regulated industries.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> WMV-HD and HD-TV are video formats, not storage media. The book mixes them into the list.</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> the Smart Card Alliance changed its name to Secure Technology Alliance in 2017. For the exam, use the book's name.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-11-e6\">Key Terms</h3><p>electronic access control system · what you have / what you know / what you are · single / dual factor authentication · credential reader · request-to-exit (REX) · enrollment · throughput · deceit · social engineering · shoulder surfing · duress code · photo identification badge · exchange badge · stored-image badge (video comparator) · coded credential · antipassback · magnetic stripe · coercivity · oersted · Wiegand · OSDP · proximity badge · facility code · smart card · hybrid card · NFC · fail-safe · fail-secure · Boolean logic · mantrap · electric strike · delayed egress · electromagnetic lock (maglock) · AHJ · verification vs. recognition · Type I error (false reject) · Type II error (false accept) · equal error rate · minutia points · HSPD-12 · FIPS 201 · UL 294 · NFPA 101 Life Safety Code · means of egress · magnetometer · continuous wave · pulsed field · eddy currents · low-Z · dual-energy X-ray · computed tomography (CT) · backscatter X-ray · thermal neutron activation (TNA) · pulsed fast neutron absorption (PFNA) · quadrupole resonance (QR) · bulk vs. trace detection · ion mobility spectrometry (IMS) · chemiluminescence · canine olfaction · trace portal · ALARA · probability of detection (PD) · confidence level (CL) · nuisance alarm rate (NAR) · false alarm rate (FAR) · bypass · spoof · UL 681 · UL 2050 · NISPOM · ICD-705 · BS EN 50131 · Grades 1–4 · ported coax · fence disturbance sensor · taut-wire sensor · bistatic microwave · ground based radar (GBR) · LiDAR · video motion detector (VMD) · technology maturity model · complementary sensors · OR / AND combination · clear zone · balanced magnetic switch (BMS) · Hall effect switch · HSS1 / HSS2 (UL 634) · passive infrared (PIR) · minimum resolvable temperature (MRT) · dual-technology sensor · trap / spot / channel / volumetric detection · two-person rule · walk test · line supervision · end-of-line (EOL) resistor · 24-hour zone · video surveillance system (VSS) · subject / action / scene identification · PTZ · pixels per foot · AGC · WDR · privacy blocking · varifocal lens · C-mount / CS-mount · CIF · H.265 · NVR · VMS · dummy cameras · burn-in · Rotakin · CRI · alarm communication and display (AC&amp;D) · MTBF · McCulloh circuit · point-to-point · multiplexing (TDM / FDM) · TDMA / CDMA · PABX · GEO / MEO / LEO · FCC Part 68 · scrambler · keystream · \"operator first\" · assessment vs. surveillance · managed services · product hybridization</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"11.1 Access Control Systems"},{"id":"e1","t":"11.2 Contraband Detection"},{"id":"e2","t":"11.3 Physical Intrusion Detection Systems"},{"id":"e3","t":"11.4 Video Surveillance"},{"id":"e4","t":"11.5 Communications and Annunciation Systems"},{"id":"e5","t":"11.6 Trends and Issues in Electronic Systems Integration"},{"id":"e6","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"11.1 Access Control Systems","h":"the book calls the third level <em>three factor authorization</em>. For consistency with the other two, it is <em>authentication</em> (three-factor authentication)."},{"s":"11.1 Access Control Systems","h":"contradiction in the book. First it recommends backup power in high security (outside egress doors). Then it says that a backup battery on maglocks is a crime almost everywhere. Safe answer: maglock on egress = fail-safe, no battery and tied to the fire alarm."},{"s":"11.1 Access Control Systems","h":"the book says the curves are shown \"in a fault tree\". It is a graph of error curves, not a fault tree (outside the book)."},{"s":"11.1 Access Control Systems","h":"the book writes that \"a <em>low</em> false accept rate compromises security\". It is the other way around: a <em>high</em> false accept rate compromises it."},{"s":"11.1 Access Control Systems","h":"the book presents UL as \"perhaps the best known of these\" without naming any other body beforehand. It is a leftover from an earlier edition."},{"s":"11.3 Physical Intrusion Detection Systems","h":"later (volumetric detection) the book cites the same UL 681 with <strong>4 steps</strong> and detection 3 out of 4 times. It contradicts itself: 3 steps versus 4 steps."},{"s":"11.3 Physical Intrusion Detection Systems","h":"the book says that volumetric sensors have a “higher partial discharge.” It appears to be an erratum; from context it should say higher probability of detection."},{"s":"11.3 Physical Intrusion Detection Systems","h":"10.5 GHz is X band; the K band is around 24 GHz (outside the book). For the exam, stay with what the book says."},{"s":"11.3 Physical Intrusion Detection Systems","h":"erratum. The thermal infrared of the human body is at 8–14 <strong>micrometers (µm)</strong>, not nanometers (outside the book)."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"the book announces “seven” views, but the list has <strong>eight</strong>. Memorize all eight with their figures."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"in 11.4.2 the subject identification rule is <strong>10%</strong> of the width (2 ft in 20 ft). Here it says <strong>20%</strong>. Internal contradiction in the book."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"35 px/ft is equivalent to about 115 px/m, not 118 (outside the book). Use the book's figures."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"AGC sensitivity is not the overall sensitivity. AGC gives a usable but low-quality image. It is sometimes cited to make the camera look, on paper, more sensitive than it is."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"the book says that it “cannot be reversed” right after citing the option of removing the mask during playback. It seems contradictory; it is understood that a mask recorded into the image cannot be undone."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"the book expands BNC as “British Naval Connector.” The technical name is Bayonet Neill–Concelman (outside the book). On the exam, if it appears, accept the book's version."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"the book prints “2,500, which equals 502.” It is an erratum from a lost superscript: it should read 50²."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"the book gives 1,000 ft = 304 m in Step 4 and = 305 m here (1,000 ft = 304.8 m, outside the book). The key figure is <strong>1,000 ft</strong>."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"in practice auto-iris usually costs more than a fixed lens (outside the book). For the exam, stick with what the book says."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"the book says the CCD moved “15 mm” closer. With its own figures the difference is about <strong>5 mm</strong> (17.526 − 12.5), the same as the spacer. Probable erratum."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"the book says those pixels are “half” the area. With double the density on each axis, each pixel occupies <strong>one quarter</strong> of the area (outside the book)."},{"s":"11.4 Video Surveillance","h":"the usual name is color rendering index (outside the book); the book says “color rendition index.” Same acronym, CRI."},{"s":"11.5 Communications and Annunciation Systems","h":"the book announces that it covers all six in detail. In fact it develops communication and line supervision (11.5.2–11.5.3), control and display (11.5.4) and video assessment (end of 11.5.4). Information handling and off-line are not developed."},{"s":"11.5 Communications and Annunciation Systems","h":"with the privacy service, the book guarantees only the leg up to the provider's switch."},{"s":"11.5 Communications and Annunciation Systems","h":"the book develops PABX as \"private automatic board exchange.\" The usual term is \"private automatic branch exchange\" (outside the book)."},{"s":"11.5 Communications and Annunciation Systems","h":"erratum in the book, it writes \"line of site\" for \"line of sight.\""},{"s":"11.5 Communications and Annunciation Systems","h":"the extracted text of the book shows \"2N-1\". It must be read as 2 to the power N, minus 1: that is what matches 8,388,607."},{"s":"11.5 Communications and Annunciation Systems","h":"the book recommends flashing lights and messages for what is significant, and then asks to avoid bright, flashing displays. It is reconciled this way: flashing to highlight, never continuous or annoying."},{"s":"11.5 Communications and Annunciation Systems","h":"the close says that operator loading was covered. In these pages it appears only implicitly: do not overload the operator with data."}]},"physical-12":{"html":"<ul>\n<li>Evolution of the name: <em>watchmen</em> → <em>guards</em> → today <strong>protection officer</strong> or <strong>security officer</strong> (preferred term).</li>\n<li>The name change reflects the greater responsibility, training and professionalism demanded.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e0\">12.1 Security Officer Utilization Growth</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Data (US)</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2002</td>\n<td>After Sept. 11, 2001 the security workforce grew <strong>13%</strong>; more than <strong>1 million</strong> jobs</td>\n<td>U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS), 2007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2008</td>\n<td><strong>1.1 million</strong>; \\~<strong>55%</strong> in <em>contract agencies</em>; \\~<strong>6 of every 7</strong> full-time</td>\n<td>BLS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2019</td>\n<td>Fairly stable figures</td>\n<td>BLS, 2019</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Many of those who were not full-time were <em>moonlighting law enforcement officers</em>.</li>\n<li>The growth of private police and private security is similar worldwide.</li>\n<li><strong>New settings</strong> (sometimes replacing police or soldiers):<ul>\n<li>patrolling urban centers or military installations;</li>\n<li>guarding heavily trafficked areas: stadiums, shopping centers, large apartment complexes;</li>\n<li>transporting prisoners or detainees.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Growth in regions with civil unrest and war: energy exploration companies face armed adversaries and use heavily armed forces.</li>\n<li><strong>Legal cannabis</strong> (medical and adult-use): expanding global market. Requires officers at:<ul>\n<li>cultivation centers;</li>\n<li>manufacturing and production plants;</li>\n<li>dispensaries;</li>\n<li>secure transport.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In cannabis, regulation often <strong>requires contract officers</strong>.</li>\n<li>More diverse services: communications, customer service, transportation and other nontraditional functions.</li>\n<li>Consequence: more responsibilities and a need for higher-quality personnel.</li>\n<li>With technology, fewer officers cover more tasks.</li>\n<li>Trend: better-trained and better-educated officers, who give a higher return on investment (ROI).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e1\">12.2 Contemporary Challenges</h3><ul>\n<li><em>Security in 2025</em> (Fennelly, Beaudry and Perry, 2017) points to three growing challenges closely tied to officer services:<ul>\n<li>climate and population insecurity on a global scale;</li>\n<li>advances in <em>machine learning</em> and other artificial intelligence (AI) technologies;</li>\n<li>privacy concerns.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4><strong>Growing Regulation</strong></h4>\n<ul>\n<li>States and local jurisdictions increasingly regulate officers and those who employ them.</li>\n<li>A reflection of this: the <strong>IASIR</strong> (International Association of Security and Investigative Regulators).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>IASIR</th>\n<th>Data</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Founding</td>\n<td><strong>1993</strong>, with <strong>15</strong> regulators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Today</td>\n<td><strong>32</strong> regulatory agencies or boards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td><strong>20</strong> US states, <strong>7</strong> Canadian provinces and <strong>France</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Associates</td>\n<td><strong>38</strong> associate memberships <strong>without a vote</strong> (they represent the regulated industries)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Challenge: keeping up with legal changes on licensing, registration, reporting, training and uniforms.</li>\n<li>To comply: monitor the regulator's communications. Good practice: subscribe to its email alerts, if offered.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4><strong>Technological Change</strong></h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The officer must handle programs on desktop computers and mobile devices.</li>\n<li>Must understand how to operate electronic systems for:<ul>\n<li>barriers;</li>\n<li>intrusion detection;</li>\n<li>alarm assessment;</li>\n<li>identification (ID);</li>\n<li>communication;</li>\n<li>visitor management;</li>\n<li>video surveillance;</li>\n<li>access control.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Evolution of guard tours: <strong>Detex Watchclocks (1902)</strong> → electronic versions with Bluetooth, GPS and WiFi.</li>\n<li>Evolution of reports: paper → cloud reporting software and computer-aided dispatching (CAD).</li>\n<li>Ability and willingness to learn and adapt are required.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4><strong>Education and Certifications</strong></h4>\n<ul>\n<li>University security programs and the prestige of certifications are growing.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Organization</th>\n<th>Certification</th>\n<th>Full name</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS International</td>\n<td>CPP</td>\n<td>Certified Protection Professional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS International</td>\n<td>PSP</td>\n<td>Physical Security Professional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS International</td>\n<td>PCI</td>\n<td>Professional Certified Investigator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS International</td>\n<td>APP</td>\n<td>Associate Protection Professional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IFPO (International Foundation for Protection Officers)</td>\n<td>CPO</td>\n<td>Certified Protection Officer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IFPO</td>\n<td>CSSM</td>\n<td>Certified in Security Supervision and Management</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The IFPO's are highly relevant for front-line officers and supervisors.</li>\n<li>The <strong>CPO</strong> is entry level. It serves to acquire knowledge, improve promotion options and professionalize the industry.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4><strong>Expectations and Cost</strong></h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Services outside security's own scope are requested as <strong>“value add”</strong> to justify the expense.</li>\n<li>Reason: it is hard to demonstrate what return the money invested in officers yields.</li>\n<li>Caution: those extra services must not weaken overall security.</li>\n<li>More expectations require more resources for training. This applies to those who have proprietary officers and to governments or companies that contract.</li>\n<li>Persistent challenge in contract security: overvaluing <strong>low price</strong> when buying. Typical result: poor, compromised quality.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e2\">12.3 Determining the Need for a Security Force</h3><ul>\n<li>Every security plan or integrated asset protection plan starts from the risks it must control.</li>\n<li>Balanced approach: <strong>defense-in-depth</strong> with layered controls.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Name in the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Behavior modification</td>\n<td>Policies, procedures, training, awareness programs</td>\n<td><em>Security management</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electromechanical</td>\n<td>Security systems, locks, lighting</td>\n<td><em>Security technology</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personnel</td>\n<td>Personnel in general, including security officers</td>\n<td><em>Security operations</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The officer is <strong>not an isolated resource</strong>: the officer is part of a comprehensive protection plan.</li>\n<li>Personnel are usually the <strong>most expensive component</strong> of the program.</li>\n<li>In exchange, they provide capabilities that technology or other controls cannot viably provide.</li>\n<li>Periodically reevaluate the use of officers: does it meet the objectives? does it make the most of the budget?</li>\n<li>Goal: a balance among <strong>people, process and technology</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>“Necessary human being” concept</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Key question: do the characteristics and reactions the position demands require human capabilities?</li>\n<li>Every position combines three types of skill:<ul>\n<li><strong>cognitive</strong> (knowledge);</li>\n<li><strong>psychomotor</strong> (physical);</li>\n<li><strong>affective</strong> (attitude).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A human is needed if the position demands any of these five capabilities:<ol>\n<li>Distinguishing among events, people, circumstances or objects using complex and variable criteria.</li>\n<li>Conversing rationally face to face and deciding the next step based on that conversation.</li>\n<li>Using physical force, or the threat of using it, to subdue one or more people.</li>\n<li>Using judgment to choose a logical course of mental or physical action in randomly changing circumstances.</li>\n<li>Reporting one's activities continuously.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>If a human is not needed, the position may not require personnel.</li>\n<li>Examples of substitution:<ul>\n<li>person-trap with interlocking or revolving doors for entries and exits;</li>\n<li>fully automated access with video, audio transceivers and card readers; the human responds only to a problem.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If personal recognition is required and the queue is <em>low-density queuing</em>, <strong>a remote operator can control 3 or 4 entrances</strong>.</li>\n<li>Using officers only where human capabilities are needed maximizes spending and effectiveness.</li>\n<li>The <strong>size of the force</strong> is decided within the risk analysis. That analysis considers:<ul>\n<li>the organization's mission;</li>\n<li>core values;</li>\n<li>operating environment;</li>\n<li>security objectives;</li>\n<li>threats;</li>\n<li>vulnerabilities;</li>\n<li>risk acceptance.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The book leaves this list open (“and other matters”).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e3\">12.4 Security Force Models</h3><ul>\n<li>If it is decided that officers mitigate a risk, there are three models:<ul>\n<li><strong>proprietary</strong> (or <em>in-house</em>): direct employees of the organization;</li>\n<li><strong>contract</strong>: employees of a security services company;</li>\n<li><strong>hybrid</strong>: both at once.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Factors in choosing:<ul>\n<li>type of organization to be protected;</li>\n<li>program goals, based on a needs assessment;</li>\n<li>location or locations;</li>\n<li>regulatory requirements;</li>\n<li>financial and management capabilities;</li>\n<li>performance expectations;</li>\n<li>cultural factors (the book leaves the list open with “and others”).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Weigh the advantages and disadvantages of each model and build <strong>budget models</strong> for each alternative.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Proprietary</td>\n<td>More direct control of selection, screening, training and supervision. Employee loyalty to the organization: lower turnover, more job knowledge, better performance</td>\n<td>Can be <strong>more expensive</strong>. The organization takes on what the contractor would do: recruiting, application processing, screening, training, benefits administration, performance management, discipline and open post coverage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contract</td>\n<td>Shifts part of the burden of hiring, training and supervising to the security company. A legal review will say whether the contract shifts part of the legal liability. More flexibility in workforce size</td>\n<td>Less control. Loss of the officer's knowledge of the organization's specific needs and other elements of the program</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hybrid</td>\n<td>More control of operations, less administrative burden and maximum savings. Combines benefits of the other two</td>\n<td><strong>Co-employment</strong> rules must be reviewed before implementing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Variants of the hybrid model</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proprietary managers administer a program staffed by contract officers.</li>\n<li>Contract officers work side by side with proprietary officers.</li>\n<li>Contract officers only for specific tasks or as supplemental on-call coverage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Buying contract security</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do not decide <strong>on the lowest bid alone</strong>.</li>\n<li>Companies cut costs in order to sell. The largest cost component is the <strong>officer's wage</strong>.</li>\n<li>Lowering the price usually lowers wages and, with them, the quality of service.</li>\n<li>Client's responsibility: write detailed <strong>bid specifications</strong>.</li>\n<li>They must set requirements, wages, benefits and performance expectations.</li>\n<li>That way the contractor can be held to the quality standard after award.</li>\n<li>The trend toward cutting staff and outsourcing favored the contract model.</li>\n<li>Case: <strong>General Motors</strong> converted its large proprietary force into a contract operation. A common pattern when the budget tightens.</li>\n<li>If the security company subcontracts selection or training, the subcontractor must meet the company's criteria.</li>\n<li>Those criteria must be consistent with legal, regulatory and contractual obligations (ASIS PSO, 2019).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e4\">12.5 Basic Security Officer Functions</h3><ul>\n<li>Whatever the model, the specific tasks the force will perform must be defined.</li>\n<li>The <strong>12 basic functions</strong> according to the book:<ol>\n<li>Control of access and of pedestrian and vehicle movement.</li>\n<li>Patrol of buildings and perimeters.</li>\n<li>Inspection of security and fire exposures.</li>\n<li>Monitoring of assets from a control center.</li>\n<li>Safety and emergency response.</li>\n<li>Traffic control.</li>\n<li>Dealing with disturbed people.</li>\n<li>Escort of material and of people.</li>\n<li>Special assignments.</li>\n<li>Recordkeeping.</li>\n<li>Reporting of incidents and damage.</li>\n<li>Enforcing regulations.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The specific application of these functions at each post is set in the <strong>post orders</strong>.</li>\n<li>Good practices with <em>post orders</em>: easy access, central topic of training and consultation while performing the tasks.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the list has 12 functions, but the book develops only 10 sections (12.5.1–12.5.10). “Reporting of incidents and damage” and “enforcing regulations” have no section of their own; the latter reappears as a role in 12.6.3.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.5.1 Access Control</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> regulating movement into, out of and within a building or designated area.</li>\n<li>People, means of transport and assets are controlled.</li>\n<li>Typical tasks:<ul>\n<li>verifying identification;</li>\n<li>ensuring that employees wear their badge at all times inside the facility;</li>\n<li>regulating access and issuing passes to visitors;</li>\n<li>identifying and reporting anyone without identification or authorization; some people may be <strong>temporarily detained</strong>;</li>\n<li>reporting anyone who tries to bring in alcohol or other contraband, or appears to be under the influence of alcohol or controlled substances;</li>\n<li>identifying and questioning anyone removing goods, to verify that they have a <strong>property removal pass</strong> or other authorization;</li>\n<li>monitoring materials entering and leaving;</li>\n<li>monitoring vehicles entering and leaving.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the sentence that introduces this list is cut off in the book (editing error); the list itself is complete.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.5.2 Patrol</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Foot patrol</td>\n<td>The officer on foot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mobile patrol</td>\n<td>Cars, trucks, bicycles, golf carts, mopeds, Segways and horses</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>With any method: cover the area <strong>systematically</strong>, retracing steps often and using unexpected routes.</li>\n<li>The officer must always be alert and know the area and its surroundings well:<ul>\n<li>shortcuts and dead ends;</li>\n<li>ongoing construction;</li>\n<li>building hours and operations;</li>\n<li>hazards;</li>\n<li>legitimate and illegitimate activities;</li>\n<li>the institution's policies and procedures.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>That knowledge makes it possible to detect what is unusual, suspicious or implies a threat or risk of loss.</li>\n<li>Typical response: report to the appropriate personnel (may include the police) and document according to procedure.</li>\n<li>The vast majority of private officers <strong>are not police</strong>: their key task is to <strong>observe and report</strong>.</li>\n<li>Conditions sought on patrol:<ul>\n<li>unsecured doors, windows or other openings;</li>\n<li>suspicious people, vehicles, packages or circumstances;</li>\n<li>disorderly or unusual activity, for example people under the influence of alcohol or other substances;</li>\n<li>dangerous or unsafe conditions: liquid leaks, fire hazards, failing equipment;</li>\n<li>equipment running at odd hours;</li>\n<li>problems with fire equipment;</li>\n<li>violations of the company's or client's security or <em>safety</em> policies;</li>\n<li>unauthorized entries or trespassers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.5.3 Inspection</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Can be done along with other tasks, such as patrol.</li>\n<li>Special inspections of:<ul>\n<li>fire hazards;</li>\n<li>unlocked doors, safes and windows;</li>\n<li>other security and <em>safety</em> matters, including packages, bags and personal belongings.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Because of their 24-hour presence, at many sites they are expected to watch mechanical equipment, including fire detection and suppression equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>Documenting the findings is paramount.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.5.4 Monitoring</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>More and more officers watch from centralized <strong>security control centers</strong>.</li>\n<li>Typical systems in a control center:<ul>\n<li>security video;</li>\n<li>access control;</li>\n<li>fire alarm controls;</li>\n<li>elevators;</li>\n<li>building lighting;</li>\n<li>duress alarms;</li>\n<li>emergency call stations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Scope: a single site or sites across a country or around the world.</li>\n<li>Required profile: very well trained and comfortable with electronic systems, computers and high-stress environments.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.5.5 Emergency Response</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The officer must be prepared for the emergencies possible at the site.</li>\n<li>Often <strong>the first to arrive</strong>: must direct, coordinate and carry out the responses set by security management.</li>\n<li>Possible responses:<ul>\n<li>first aid;</li>\n<li>full or partial evacuation;</li>\n<li>lockdown;</li>\n<li>shelter-in-place.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Example of partial evacuation: for a bomb threat on a specific floor, concentrate on <strong>the floors immediately above and below</strong>.</li>\n<li>Emergencies the officer may face:<ul>\n<li>active shooter;</li>\n<li>fire alarms, real and nuisance;</li>\n<li>hazardous materials (HAZMAT);</li>\n<li>medical emergencies;</li>\n<li>natural disasters, including extreme weather;</li>\n<li>power outages;</li>\n<li>demonstrators and civil disturbances;</li>\n<li>suspicious packages;</li>\n<li>terrorist acts, including bomb threats and bombings.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.5.6 Traffic Control</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Tasks:<ul>\n<li>regulating vehicle flow;</li>\n<li>protecting pedestrians;</li>\n<li>assisting emergency vehicles;</li>\n<li>directing those who ask for information.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Essential equipment (Holm, 2010):<ul>\n<li>clothing suited to changes in weather;</li>\n<li>reflective vests and armbands;</li>\n<li>flashlights;</li>\n<li>whistles;</li>\n<li>radios;</li>\n<li>flags;</li>\n<li>stop and go signs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.5.7 Dealing with Persons with Altered Mental Status</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Possible causes the officer will see:<ul>\n<li>mental illness;</li>\n<li>effects of alcohol or illegal drugs;</li>\n<li>temporary loss of control from physical or emotional trauma;</li>\n<li>diabetic emergency;</li>\n<li>traumatic brain injury;</li>\n<li>any other irrational behavior that may harm the person or others.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The officer is <strong>not qualified to determine the cause</strong>. Only a physician can do so.</li>\n<li>The officer's task: secure the person and the scene, call emergency medical services (EMS) and keep the person in custody until handing them over.</li>\n<li>Key rule: it is a <strong>medical emergency, not a police problem</strong>.</li>\n<li>The safety of the responding officer is <strong>paramount</strong>.</li>\n<li>Requires great sensitivity and training.</li>\n<li>Objective: avoid harm and control the person without injury, discomfort or humiliation.</li>\n<li>Once the danger has passed, witnesses will judge the treatment. Poor treatment exposes the officer's conduct to scrutiny.</li>\n<li>Goal in any crisis: safety of the person and of those nearby.</li>\n<li>Other considerations:<ul>\n<li>legal liability of the force and the organization if the disturbed person is injured;</li>\n<li>legal liability if the person is <strong>not</strong> contained and later injures others;</li>\n<li>impact of the response on relations with employees, the community, the public and the media.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Recommendation: periodic training and certification in <strong>crisis intervention</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.5.8 Escort</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Escorting someone carrying large sums of money or information or special goods.</li>\n<li>Escort on request, for employee safety:<ul>\n<li>accompanying an employee to the parking lot at night;</li>\n<li>helping sick or physically disabled people;</li>\n<li>supporting human resources during a termination.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Rule: the <strong>fixed-post</strong> officer should not escort visitors or customers, because the officer abandons the protection task.</li>\n<li>Alternatives: have whoever invited the visitor escort them, or a <strong>non-static post</strong> officer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.5.9 Special Assignments</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Because the force is on duty 24 hours, it is sometimes the only personnel available.</li>\n<li>Examples: messenger, communications coordinator, driver.</li>\n<li>Risk: diverting officers to miscellaneous services <strong>erodes the protection program</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.5.10 Recordkeeping</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The officer documents activities and events in a <strong>post log</strong> or an incident log.</li>\n<li>Some incidents require a more complete <strong>formal report</strong>.</li>\n<li>The organization must teach report writing and define what goes into a formal report and what goes into the log.</li>\n<li>Form and format depend on the site's mission and security objectives; increasingly supported by technology.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Log</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Post log</td>\n<td><strong>Chronological and local</strong> record of events at <strong>a single post</strong></td>\n<td>The hourly count of people or vehicles required by the <em>post orders</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main / control log</td>\n<td><strong>Cumulative and consecutive</strong> history of significant events at <strong>all posts</strong></td>\n<td>Proof that that count was reported to the control center or supervisor and received</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Data carried by each log entry</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Entry number</td>\n<td>Makes it easy to locate or cite</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Day of the week</td>\n<td>Normally abbreviated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time of day</td>\n<td>In <strong>military time</strong> (24-hour format)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Category tag</td>\n<td>One or two words on the nature of the entry (e.g., “Weather”); allows quick scanning of the page</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Description of the incident or event</td>\n<td>Brief; often with symbols or codes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference</td>\n<td>Name, date, number or other identification of another document with more detail (e.g., a detailed complaint)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Electronic and paper logs</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Technology platforms allow custom data fields and required fields in each entry.</li>\n<li>With a sufficient sample of records, most allow reports and trend analysis.</li>\n<li>On paper: write <strong>in ink</strong> and <strong>in consecutive order</strong>, so that nothing is altered or removed.</li>\n<li>Any correction is made <strong>on a separate line, as a new entry</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>“The eyes and ears of management”</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Officers are said to be the eyes and ears of management, but their information often does not reach it.</li>\n<li>Typical case: daily logs of “Arrived 0700. All quiet. Relieved 1500.”</li>\n<li>Several days like that suggest one of three things: the post is unnecessary, the officer is not performing, or was not trained to report.</li>\n<li>The <em>post orders</em> should require recording concrete facts: key sign-outs, escorts, assistance to visitors or contractors.</li>\n<li>Every event relevant to security or to the continuity of operations deserves an entry.</li>\n<li>Logs and reports have been used in <strong>court proceedings</strong> as an authoritative source to prove:<ul>\n<li>the exact time of an event;</li>\n<li>the presence of certain people in the facility;</li>\n<li>the receipt of a telephone call;</li>\n<li>any action by security personnel.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>That is why the officer must understand the importance of the data being recorded.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e5\">12.6 Security Officer Roles</h3><ul>\n<li>In addition to the basic functions, the officer can fill <strong>five support roles</strong>:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Key idea</th>\n<th>Training required</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>12.6.1 Public relations / management representative</td>\n<td>Agent of management to visitors, customers, tenants and vendors</td>\n<td>Corporate expectations and culture; dealing with the media</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12.6.2 Information collection</td>\n<td>Detects and reports criminal or adversary activity and loss conditions</td>\n<td>Fundamentals of investigation, interviewing and investigative reporting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12.6.3 Enforcement / compliance agent</td>\n<td>Enforces management's rules</td>\n<td>Rules, enforcement procedure, conflict handling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12.6.4 Legal consultant</td>\n<td>Understands legal and quasi-legal matters of the work environment</td>\n<td>Civil liability, negligence, contracts, criminal, labor and regulatory law</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12.6.5 Physical security or crime prevention specialist</td>\n<td>Advises on physical protection and prevention</td>\n<td>Technical aspects of equipment; lifelong learning</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>12.6.1 Public Relations/Management Representative</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>To fill it, the officer must master the company's expectations and culture.</li>\n<li>With that knowledge the officer becomes an <strong>adjunct member of the management team</strong>.</li>\n<li>Is often the <strong>first contact</strong> for visitors, customers, vendors or employees: sets the first impression of the organization.</li>\n<li>Helps create good relations between the security department (proprietary or contract) and the rest.</li>\n<li>If the officer takes part in the security awareness program, he or she encourages employees to report or reduce risks.</li>\n<li>Dealing with people hostile to those who exercise authority: courtesy, restraint and good judgment usually overcome resistance.</li>\n<li>Most training programs <strong>do not cover</strong> this role well.</li>\n<li>Exceptions that do recognize it:<ul>\n<li><em>Private Security Officer Selection and Training Guideline</em> (ASIS, 2019): added <strong>media relations</strong> as a recommended topic;</li>\n<li><em>The Professional Protection Officer: Practical Security Strategies and Emerging Trends</em>, 8th ed. (Holm, 2010), text of the CPO program.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>New chapters of that CPO text:<ul>\n<li>“Human Relations in a Global Environment”;</li>\n<li>“Community Relations: Making the Strategy Come Alive”;</li>\n<li>“Networking and the Liaison Function”.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Media:</strong> the officer is often on the front line of media incidents. The organization must give clear guidance.</li>\n<li>Usual guideline: do not make statements on behalf of the organization; put the media in contact with the public relations spokesperson.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.6.2 Information Collection</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Gathering asset protection information, especially on <strong>criminal or adversary</strong> activity: a role of great value.</li>\n<li>Includes discovering and reporting violations of rules by employees or tenants that may cause losses.</li>\n<li>Patrols look mainly for conditions or situations that would cause losses; they also assess fire and <em>safety</em> risks.</li>\n<li>The role grows when there is <strong>organized crime</strong>:<ul>\n<li>narcotics sales;</li>\n<li>retail theft rings;</li>\n<li>prostitution;</li>\n<li>illegal gambling;</li>\n<li>gang activity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Most critical: when the target is suspected <strong>terrorist activity</strong>.</li>\n<li>Training needed: fundamentals of investigation, interviewing techniques and writing investigative reports.</li>\n<li>Must know what to look for, whom to report to, and assess whether to correct on the spot or only report.</li>\n<li>Must be alert to <strong>criminal indicators</strong> and <strong>terrorism indicators</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.6.3 Enforcement/Compliance Agent</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Ensures that management's rules are followed.</li>\n<li>Resembles the police function, but within the legal and cultural framework of the protected environment.</li>\n<li>Sometimes enforces laws that are also internal rules, especially if the officer has <strong>commissioned police officer</strong>.</li>\n<li>Needs to know the rules and the procedural side of their enforcement.</li>\n<li>Must achieve compliance efficiently and in line with what management expects.</li>\n<li>Must reduce and resolve conflicts: the <strong>interpersonal side is vital</strong>.</li>\n<li>Has an investigative side: documents and <strong>testifies</strong> about violations. There the intelligence role combines with the compliance role.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.6.4 Legal Consultant</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Must understand the legal and quasi-legal (disciplinary) matters of the environment.</li>\n<li>Must know: torts, negligence and contracts.</li>\n<li>Practical knowledge of criminal procedure: it may not bind the private officer, but it does bind the police. Knowing the basics improves the police–security relationship.</li>\n<li>Administrative or regulatory laws: great weight in many protected environments.</li>\n<li>Industry standards: they are not law, but their principles and the need to comply are similar.</li>\n<li>Example: understanding and complying with <strong>audits</strong> by government, insurers and professional organizations.</li>\n<li>Labor law: privacy rights, disciplinary processes and union contracts.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.6.5 Physical Security Specialist</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Also called a <strong>crime prevention specialist</strong>.</li>\n<li>Physical security is the core of the discipline. The role grows because of technological complexity.</li>\n<li>Learning the technical side of new equipment can be a lifelong task.</li>\n<li>Reference publication: <em>Journal of Physical Security</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Author (year)</th>\n<th>Idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Anderson (2001)</td>\n<td>The officer who knows how to advise the client on prevention quickly gains respect and cooperation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maggio (2009)</td>\n<td>Security = protection against danger, risk, threats and problems in general; acts as a lubricant that touches the whole organization and makes it work better</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Palacios and Hertig (2010)</td>\n<td>Central function of the officer: to <strong>improve</strong> the policies, procedures and functions of the post, not just follow them; provides perspective to management</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Conclusion: the role varies greatly by organization, but it is always demanding and dynamic.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e6\">12.7 Uniforms and Equipment</h3><ul>\n<li>Many private security regulators set uniform and equipment requirements.</li>\n<li>Before buying, review state and local laws and regulations.</li>\n<li><strong>Purpose of that regulation:</strong> so that the public can clearly tell the private officer from the police.</li>\n<li>Case: the <strong>Arizona Department of Public Safety</strong> (state licensing authority) details requirements for badges, shoulder and chest patches, shirts, jackets and vehicle lights.</li>\n<li>It also requires <strong>prior approval</strong> of each garment before issuing the agency's license.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 12.1 — Uniforms and Equipment (what to consider)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Considerations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Uniforms</td>\n<td>Meet the state or local standard, suit the environment and project a professional image appropriate to the facility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communication devices</td>\n<td>Complete training on two-way radios and smartphones, if used. Test them regularly to avoid failures in a crisis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Safety</em> equipment</td>\n<td>Each post may require different equipment (flashlight, safety vest; the book leaves the list open)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ID badge</td>\n<td>Identifies the officer and gives access to the areas patrolled or entered in an emergency. Those with proximity or memory chips require care so they are not damaged or deactivated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keys</td>\n<td>Basic tool of almost every post; mechanical or electronic. Training in <strong>key control</strong> policies and procedures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weapons and restraints</td>\n<td>Complete training in use, care and storage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vehicles</td>\n<td>Safe driving, regular inspections and adherence to procedures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Laptops or tablets</td>\n<td>Comply with IT and device policies; consider security and encryption; protect them in dusty or dirty environments</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Issue and uniform policy</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most organizations <strong>issue</strong> uniforms and equipment: that way the appearance is standard and the size is right.</li>\n<li>Complete uniforms are usually issued by season, including bad-weather clothing.</li>\n<li>Quantity to issue: according to the type of service and the frequency of laundering or dry cleaning.</li>\n<li>To be recognizable <strong>without appearing authoritarian</strong>: casual or civilian clothing with shoulder or pocket patches.</li>\n<li>The policy should say whether the uniform may be worn off duty, including the commute to work.</li>\n<li>Allowing it only on duty avoids bad publicity if the officer is involved in an incident in his or her free time.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.7.1 Weapons</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Factors in deciding whether to arm officers:<ul>\n<li>state and local regulation;</li>\n<li>greater legal liability;</li>\n<li>higher costs of training, insurance and regulatory compliance.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Whoever employs or provides officers must have a <strong>use-of-force policy</strong> and integrate it into training.</li>\n<li>Objective of that policy: reduce injuries to officers and third parties, and mitigate liability for inappropriate or excessive force.</li>\n<li>Many jurisdictions require the armed officer to be licensed or registered and to meet minimum qualification and training standards.</li>\n<li>Some require the agency to have a <strong>gun custodian</strong> and may prohibit officers' personal weapons.</li>\n<li>Because of the cost and time of training, many organizations opt for <strong>unarmed</strong> officers.</li>\n<li>In <strong>Canada and Latin America</strong>, according to the book, armed officers are rare: only cash-in-transit or custody of precious metals.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> answer with the book's information. In practice, in several Latin American countries the armed guard is common (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>When to arm (firearms)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Issuing lethal weapons is one of an organization's <strong>greatest legal responsibilities</strong>.</li>\n<li>If the regulations allow it, arm only if one or both conditions exist:<ol>\n<li>Without the weapon there is <strong>greater danger to life</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is reasonable to expect that the officer will have to use <strong>lethal force</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Case of a lot of cash and risk of robbery: the armed officer deters more and lowers the overall risk to life. Against: a shootout puts third parties at risk.</li>\n<li>Strategic value: the aggressor may desist if he knows there will be armed resistance.</li>\n<li>But the <strong>quality and quantity</strong> of defensive weapons must be a real obstacle.</li>\n<li>Example: one or two officers with pistols may not deter if the target is very attractive and there are no other countermeasures.</li>\n<li>Stronger deterrence: combine armed officers with vehicle control, close-in and boundary security, and hardened locations.</li>\n<li>Shoulder weapons in hardened or defensible positions along the attack route deter more than pistols.</li>\n<li>It can also be justified with a <strong>lone officer</strong> at a post or escort where <em>felonious attacks</em> have occurred or are expected.</li>\n<li>Examples: remote access booths and high-crime areas with exterior patrol.</li>\n<li>Alternative: not using officers and replacing them with remote surveillance or intrusion alarms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management's responsibilities if it arms its officers</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>adequate training and maintenance of qualification;</li>\n<li>selection of appropriate weapons and ammunition;</li>\n<li>maintenance of the weapons by a <strong>qualified gunsmith</strong>;</li>\n<li>records of all of the above;</li>\n<li>sufficient liability insurance.</li>\n<li>The officer must master the weapon <strong>before</strong> carrying it.</li>\n<li>Training: handling and storage, live-fire in the field to qualify, and periodic refreshers.</li>\n<li>If officers' personal weapons are permitted, set and enforce standards for type of weapon and ammunition. Alternative: prohibit them.</li>\n<li>Company weapons and ammunition: issued at the start of the shift and collected at the end.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Policy Considerations</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Besides the officer's competence, the lethal weapons policy must cover:<ol>\n<li>conditions under which weapons are issued;</li>\n<li>specifically who receives a weapon;</li>\n<li>type of weapon and ammunition;</li>\n<li>quality and reliability of the weapon when issued;</li>\n<li>repair and maintenance by a qualified armorer;</li>\n<li>accountability for each weapon and its ammunition;</li>\n<li>specific training with that weapon;</li>\n<li>safety precautions when issuing and carrying;</li>\n<li>return of the weapon when not needed for authorized use;</li>\n<li>secure storage when not in use: unloaded, separate from ammunition and locked in an approved container;</li>\n<li>accurate records of all of the above.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Extra controls for weapons in large quantities, in areas prone to theft by terrorists or other adversaries, or highly dangerous (automatic):<ul>\n<li>storage in a hardened building;</li>\n<li>intrusion detection;</li>\n<li>armed patrols;</li>\n<li>dual access.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The book leaves that list open; everything is part of defense in depth.</li>\n<li>In these cases, government standards and the insurer's requirements must be <strong>exceeded</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Less Than Lethal Weapons</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They demand the <strong>same</strong> thorough reflection as firearms.</li>\n<li>Do a full analysis: do the benefits outweigh the risks?</li>\n<li>Depending on the makeup of the organization, convene a <strong>multidisciplinary team</strong> to evaluate risks and legal liability.</li>\n<li>They can kill: in 2017, <strong>Reuters</strong> documented <strong>more than 1,000 deaths</strong> in US police incidents involving the use of a TASER.</li>\n<li>In most of those deaths other force was also used, such as batons or pepper spray (Reuters, 2017).</li>\n<li>Properly used, they put the officer in a better position to protect self and others from serious injury or death.</li>\n<li>In private security they are <strong>self-defense tools only</strong>, never offensive weapons.</li>\n<li>They are not used to show force, intimidate or apprehend.</li>\n<li>Every tool of the officer has a single purpose: to defend his or her life and that of another person.</li>\n<li>They are never deployed without two elements:<ol>\n<li>a clear and concise use-of-force policy;</li>\n<li>sufficient initial and recurrent training.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Risks and rules</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Electroshock: <strong>TASER</strong> (registered trademark)</td>\n<td>Compressed gas fires <strong>two barbed darts</strong>. Affects the neurological system; the subject usually loses muscle control</td>\n<td>Temporary effect; falls can cause secondary injuries</td>\n<td>Carli, 2018</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electroshock: <strong>stun gun</strong></td>\n<td>Requires <strong>direct contact</strong> with the subject</td>\n<td>May cause pain without incapacitating</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electroshock: <strong>stun baton</strong></td>\n<td>Direct contact, but it is longer: the officer does not get as close</td>\n<td>May cause pain without incapacitating</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Batons</strong></td>\n<td>Perhaps the most widely used less than lethal weapon in security. The <strong>expandable / collapsible</strong> one predominates; also the straight-handled nightstick and the side-handled one. Of wood, metal or plastic resin</td>\n<td><strong>Impact</strong> weapon: almost always causes some injury. Only on large muscle masses (thigh, shoulder). <strong>Never</strong> on joints or the head. Misuse: serious injury or death</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Aerosol irritants</strong> (“tear gas,” “pepper spray”)</td>\n<td>CS, CN and OC compounds mixed with propellant in a compact container. Irritate mucous membranes and eyes: tearing, coughing, difficulty breathing, skin irritation</td>\n<td>Today they last less and leave fewer symptoms. They require <strong>aftercare and custody</strong>. Greater residual risk for third parties: irresponsible in places such as a crowded emergency room</td>\n<td>Mesloh, 2008</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Handcuffs</strong></td>\n<td>Technically not weapons; they help to subdue</td>\n<td>Can cause discomfort and injury: require training</td>\n<td>Stowell, 2016</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Electroshock weapons are also called <strong>CEW</strong> (conducted electrical weapons) or <strong>CED</strong> (conducted energy devices).</li>\n<li>Their effects are temporary and can stop aggression; in general they leave no permanent effects.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book swaps the chemical names. It gives CS = 2-chloroacetophenone and CN = “0-chlorobenzylidene.” The correct ones are CN = 2-chloroacetophenone and CS = o-chlorobenzylidene malononitrile (outside the book).</li>\n<li>OC = oleoresin capsicum, the base of pepper spray.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e7\">12.8 Security Officer Selection</h3><ul>\n<li>The quality and competence of the force directly determine the success of operations.</li>\n<li>That is why <strong>minimum standards</strong> for hiring and training must be set.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.8.1 ASIS Guideline PSO–2019</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In <strong>2001</strong> ASIS International recognized the need to raise the standards of private officers.</li>\n<li>It developed the <em>Private Security Officer Selection and Training Guideline</em> (ASIS GDL PSO–2010), <strong>revised in 2019</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Section 5.3, <em>Selection of Personnel</em>,</strong> of the PSO 2019. The organization must:<ol>\n<li>set minimum criteria of knowledge, skills, experience and competence for each position;</li>\n<li>make them consistent with legal, regulatory and contractual obligations;</li>\n<li>evaluate each person's competence against those criteria;</li>\n<li>screen out anyone who does not meet them, <strong>or</strong> give them the training needed to meet them.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book mixes dates (need in 2001, cited version of 2010, revision of 2019). The first edition of the guideline was published in 2004 (outside the book).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.8.2 Personal Attributes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The essential attributes must be <strong>aligned with the core values</strong> of the organization.</li>\n<li>The six common attributes and how the book develops them:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attribute in the list</th>\n<th>Section of the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Good character</td>\n<td><em>Character</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good customer service skills</td>\n<td><em>Behavior</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neat appearance</td>\n<td><em>Appearance</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Knowledge of the job</td>\n<td><em>Knowledge of the Job</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Education</td>\n<td><em>Education</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ethical conduct</td>\n<td><em>Ethics</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Character:</strong> honest, courageous, alert, disciplined and loyal.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Trait</th>\n<th>What it implies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Honesty</td>\n<td>Is custodian of company and employee property. The regulations must require scrupulous honesty <strong>in writing</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Courage</td>\n<td>There is always danger. Courage in physical incidents and emergencies, and also to report every violation completely and faithfully</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alertness</td>\n<td>Can make the difference between life and death. Difficult when routine makes the work monotonous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discipline</td>\n<td>Obey promptly and carry out orders well. <strong>Never leave the post</strong> until relieved or ordered. Personal opinions must not influence performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loyalty</td>\n<td>To the post and the company; decide according to what is best for the organization. Discretion with confidential information</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Causes for discipline, including dismissal:<ul>\n<li>failing to prevent damage or theft of property;</li>\n<li>accepting bribes or gratuities;</li>\n<li>allowing violation of company rules.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Because the officer often works <strong>alone and without direct supervision</strong>, strength of character is key.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Behavior:</strong> the officer is often the first contact and shapes the perception of the company. Three factors:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Courtesy</td>\n<td>Expressing consideration for others. Eliminates friction and achieves cooperation. Applies when informing, directing or enforcing rules and traffic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Restraint</td>\n<td>Must be emphasized in training. Act without haste or excessive emotion; avoid abusive language, arguments and, if possible, force. Calm, dignified conduct; tolerance for other opinions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interest</td>\n<td>Without interest in the job, the officer projects a bad attitude and an unprofessional image</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Appearance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Good appearance = good impression of the organization.</li>\n<li>Ripple effect: employees respect officers and the security department more, and the department's morale rises.</li>\n<li>A single officer influences, for better or worse, the opinion of many people.</li>\n<li>In uniform or in civilian clothes: always neat and well groomed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Knowledge of the Job</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Continuous learning: the <em>post orders</em> and policies change often.</li>\n<li>Must know the facility thoroughly, also for the public relations side (giving information to employees and outsiders).</li>\n<li>Must know:<ul>\n<li>applicable federal, state and local laws;</li>\n<li>company rules and regulations;</li>\n<li>criminal procedures;</li>\n<li><em>safety</em>;</li>\n<li>fire protection;</li>\n<li>emergency plans;</li>\n<li>first aid.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Education</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The educational level has improved greatly since reports that criticized the low education and competence of private officers.</li>\n<li>Academia and professional organizations created degree programs and certificates in response to demand.</li>\n<li>Available in the US, Canada and other countries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ethics: IFPO code of ethics.</strong> The protection officer must:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Respond to the professional needs of the employer.</li>\n<li>Maintain exemplary conduct.</li>\n<li>Protect confidential information.</li>\n<li>Maintain a safe and secure workplace.</li>\n<li>Dress in a way that projects professionalism.</li>\n<li>Enforce all lawful rules and regulations.</li>\n<li>Encourage liaison with public officers.</li>\n<li>Cultivate good relations within the profession.</li>\n<li>Strive to attain professional standards.</li>\n<li>Promote high standards of ethics among officers.</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"physical-12-e8\">12.9 Security Officer Training</h3><ul>\n<li>After hiring, the organization must train its officers well.</li>\n<li>The content is aligned with the officer's responsibilities and with the officer's role in the organization's mission.</li>\n<li>Reference guideline: ASIS <em>Private Security Officer Selection and Training Guideline</em> (GDL PSO-2010, revised in 2019), section 5.4 <em>Training and Competencies</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Factors for setting the training criteria for each position (PSO 5.4):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Jurisdictional laws/statutes.</li>\n<li>Policies and procedures of the organization or the client.</li>\n<li>Contractual provisions.</li>\n<li>Site-specific job responsibilities.</li>\n<li>Security-related risks associated with duty assignment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example methods and formats (PSO):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Orientation programs.</li>\n<li>Online training.</li>\n<li>Instructor-provided classroom training.</li>\n<li>Scenario-based approaches.</li>\n<li>Lectures and/or professional development courses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other PSO points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Training must cover the position's minimum criteria and the legal, regulatory and contractual obligations.</li>\n<li>Duration and content are set according to each officer's assignment.</li>\n<li><em>Proficiency</em> and <em>competency</em> level must be measured.</li>\n<li>Training monitoring is continuous, to detect improvements.</li>\n<li>Ultimate goal: build, promote and sustain a professional culture.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.9.1 Benefits of Training</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Training costs money; costs and benefits must be evaluated to achieve the highest return on investment (ROI).</li>\n<li>Five main benefits:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n<th>What it provides</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Improved job performance</td>\n<td>Gives mastery of the tasks. Without training, competence cannot be expected.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ease of supervision</td>\n<td>Well-trained personnel are easier to supervise. The manager spends time on other matters, not on teaching the basics.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Staff motivation</td>\n<td>Raises morale, self-esteem and job satisfaction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reduced turnover</td>\n<td>Reduces stress in the face of new challenges and shows management's interest. Knowing the environment, expectations and problem solving lowers anxiety.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal protection</td>\n<td>Failing to train can lead to union grievances or negligence lawsuits.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>How to document training (for legal protection):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Attendance list and list of activities for each class.</li>\n<li>Tests signed by the instructor and by the person tested.</li>\n<li>Centralized database of all officer training.</li>\n<li>Checklists for on-the-job training.</li>\n<li>Course evaluations completed by employees: they show the instructor's effectiveness and what remains to be clarified or improved.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.9.2 Identifying Training Requirements</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Before giving any instruction, the requirements must be identified.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sources:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>First step: legal requirements.</strong> Review state statute; administrative rules, rulings and forms; contracts with clients; union contracts (if applicable). Insurers may also impose requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>Then: standards, guidelines and best practices.</strong> ASIS International, the National Association of Security Companies (NASCO) and the International Association of Chiefs of Police (IACP) have published officer training guidelines.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Community standards:</strong> see what training similar facilities in the area provide (hotels, universities). Their schedules reveal your own deficiencies, innovations and useful resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance deficiencies:</strong> they show up in after-action reports and in performance evaluations. Training prepares for routine and emergency functions.</li>\n<li><strong>The officers themselves:</strong> they can identify and prioritize topics. It is a form of job task analysis.<ul>\n<li>Uncovers previously unknown needs.</li>\n<li>Validates their self-esteem and encourages them to participate later.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other Organizations Promoting Training Standards and Guidelines:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Organization</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1988</td>\n<td>International Foundation for Protection Officers (IFPO)</td>\n<td>Officer education and certification. Develops and maintains training standards to improve performance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1989</td>\n<td>Confederation of European Security Services (CoESS)</td>\n<td>Umbrella organization of national security services companies in Europe. Represents common interests; gathers and disseminates information on the sector.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1991</td>\n<td>British Security Industry Association (BSIA)</td>\n<td>Training materials and standards. Lobbying and work with governments so that regulation reflects the sector and its clients.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1991</td>\n<td>Security Industry Training Organization (SITO), created by the BSIA as an independent body</td>\n<td>Training, conferences, consulting, publications and apprenticeship program.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1993</td>\n<td>National (later International) Association of Security and Investigative Regulators (IASIR)</td>\n<td>Founded by 15 licensing regulators from several US states. Model laws and regulations, reciprocity between states and expedited background investigations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Service Employees International Union (SEIU), labor union</td>\n<td>Survey of state requirements with a rating for each state. Position papers and testimony before governing bodies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Loss Prevention Foundation</td>\n<td>Loss Prevention Qualified (LPQ) for loss prevention personnel in retail. Loss Prevention Certified (LPC): management certification.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>International Association of Healthcare Security and Safety (IAHSS) and IFPO</td>\n<td>Have similar designations.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Certifications and career path:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Certifications matter, among other reasons, because they create a visible career path.</li>\n<li><strong>Health care:</strong> IAHSS programs basic → advanced → supervisor. Later: Certified Healthcare Protection Administrator (acronym CHPA: outside the book).</li>\n<li><strong>Any setting (example ladder):</strong><ol>\n<li>Certified Protection Officer (CPO), from the IFPO.</li>\n<li>Security Supervision and Management Program → Certified in Security Supervision and Management (CSSM).</li>\n<li>Associate Protection Professional (APP).</li>\n<li>Years later: Certified Protection Professional (CPP).</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>APP and CPP are from ASIS International (outside the book; the text does not say so here).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.9.3 Methods of Training</h4>\n<p><strong>Training as a process:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Series of structured sessions that support each other. Each one builds on the previous one.</li>\n<li>Ideal: lifelong learning, constant throughout the entire employment relationship.</li>\n<li>To ensure this, it is divided into segments. Each topic has an introduction, a body and a close (summary + test).</li>\n<li>Some redundancy and a sequence are needed. The manager checks that each topic has all three segments.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Example from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Introduction</td>\n<td>At orientation the topics are presented: patrol functions, fixed posts, executive protection, vehicle safety and fire prevention.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Body</td>\n<td>On-the-job training (OJT), study of the Certified Protection Officer (CPO) program, online programs or in-house courses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Close</td>\n<td>Supervisor sign-off on an OJT form; commercial test (CPO, Critical Information Network — CiNet, American Safety Training); or the in-house course's own test.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>What conditions the choice of method:</strong> schedules, available technologies, the instructor's comfort and the number of people to be trained.</p>\n<p><strong>Six methods to consider</strong> (more detail in chapter 2 of <em>POA: Personnel</em>):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>What it is and when it works</th>\n<th>Limits and tips</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lectures</td>\n<td>Traditional method. Gives a group a common body of knowledge. Works to introduce a topic and set the direction; then continue with role-playing, video or physical skills.</td>\n<td>Not suited to individual instruction. Ineffective unless short and dynamic. After a meal: drink water before sitting, a bit more light and get them to move. Use visual aids: whiteboard, slides, videos.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Case studies</td>\n<td>Created at Harvard University in the 1880s; used to teach law. Useful where discretionary judgment is required: crisis management, use of force and ethics. Complements the lecture: the facts are given and then discussed; a video shortens the presentation of facts.</td>\n<td>Works best in groups with active discussion. Could be used in individual tutoring; new communication technologies make it viable one on one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Job aids</td>\n<td>Practical reminders of how to do a task. Detailed (video surveillance system operator's manual) or simple (signs for operating detection equipment). They ensure that what was taught is followed.</td>\n<td>They supplement, not replace, training.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distance learning</td>\n<td>Learner and teacher in different places, but in communication. From the correspondence course to the online course. Used by certifications (individual study + test) and service providers, to deliver uniform training at many sites. More popular after COVID-19 and with remote work.</td>\n<td>Some trainers reject it. Whoever does not use all the techniques, including this one, almost certainly will not train everyone well. It is accepted and necessary.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On-the-job training (OJT)</td>\n<td>Delivered at the work site. Very useful for ensuring the transfer of learning.</td>\n<td>Works if structured, with procedures and a competent coach. Written part: simple checklist of key topics with space for comments. Without structure it can be ineffective.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mentoring</td>\n<td>See the next table.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Mentoring and field training officer (FTO):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many officers today are representatives of management. They must know its philosophy and the corporate culture: they must be thoroughly socialized.</li>\n<li>Mentoring and FTO programs reinforce that socialization.</li>\n<li>In both, the new officer completes pre-assignment training and then works alongside a guide.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Mentoring</th>\n<th>FTO program</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Role of the guide</td>\n<td>Teaches, but does not evaluate.</td>\n<td>Is also a supervisor and evaluates performance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Final decision</td>\n<td>Does not decide.</td>\n<td>At some point recommends retaining or dismissing the employee.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complexity</td>\n<td>Lower. Helps socialize and bridges the gap between the classroom and the reality of the post.</td>\n<td>Complex role; requires planning and training of the FTO.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Book's verdict</td>\n<td>Works best in most security settings.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Recognize good mentors: a raise or a distinct title (\"lead protection officer\", \"senior security officer\").</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.9.4 The Training Process</h4>\n<p><strong>Learning:</strong> positive change in a person's knowledge, skill, ability or perspective.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each person learns a little differently: there are visual learners, readers and active learners (who need to be involved).</li>\n<li>It also changes with life and career stage:<ul>\n<li>Veteran or supervisor versus novice.</li>\n<li>Older versus younger.</li>\n<li>With a graduate degree: better view of management theory. With work experience only: adapts ideas to the practical better.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Instruction cannot be designed for each individual style. Several media are combined, systematically and continuously throughout the employment relationship.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Three domains of learning:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>What it covers</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cognitive (knowledge-based)</td>\n<td>The intellectual: how much material is learned and its theory. Key to solving problems: grasping the theory and applying it.</td>\n<td>Thibodeau (2008): do not shorten it with brief, generic videos without an instructor. It requires full explanation. Video is supported with questions to the viewer, a pre-test, a challenging and valid post-test, and an instructor or supervisor guide.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Affective (attitudes and perception)</td>\n<td>Changes how the person sees a situation. Examples: understanding other cultures, dealing with people with disabilities, complying with workplace <em>safety</em> rules and knowing how adversaries obtain information. Also: understanding loss and its effect, understanding the adversary and the nature of aggressive behavior.</td>\n<td>One must grasp the criminal mindset and the speed of an attack; whoever does not understand it becomes a victim. Essential in the face of terrorism, espionage, workplace safety and relations with the various publics: customers, employees, vendors, police, emergency medical personnel and hazardous materials (HAZMAT) teams. It is integrated with the cognitive and the psychomotor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychomotor (physical skills)</td>\n<td>The physical or practical: from operating equipment to defensive driving and emergency response.</td>\n<td>Requires repetition, practice and refresher courses. Examples: defensive tactics and firearms regularly, fire extinguishers every year, first aid recertification. Research the recognized refresher frequency and include it in the schedule.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Retention and transfer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Retention:</strong> how much learning remains over time. Requires repetition.</li>\n<li>The highest retention is achieved when the learner does the task while listening to the instructor.</li>\n<li>Continuous learning helps retention and transfer.</li>\n<li><strong>Transfer:</strong> relevance of what is learned to the job; that what was seen in the classroom is used at the post.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Socialization:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Process by which the employee learns and adopts the values of the workplace.</li>\n<li><strong>Formal:</strong> training (occupational or professional socialization) and supervision. Management must send a single coherent message.</li>\n<li><strong>Informal:</strong> usually influences more than the formal. Beware of negative socialization through peer group influence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Education, Training, and Development:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Education</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Training</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Guided Experience</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Development</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Supervisors and managers guide the experience so that it is formative. That is why it is key that they know how to teach, counsel and coach.</li>\n<li>They are three distinct concepts, related and mutually reinforcing. All produce learning: a positive change in job behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Traits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Education</td>\n<td>Acquiring knowledge and information. Broadens perspective and appreciation (affective learning).</td>\n<td>Concerned with the why, not the how; may not apply directly to the post. The more education, the easier it is to learn and accept change. They read faster, write better and solve problems better, the officer's greatest challenge.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training</td>\n<td>Acquiring knowledge, skills and abilities directly tied to performance. It is the formal process that facilitates learning.</td>\n<td>Defined purpose: e.g., operating detection equipment, conducting interviews or monitoring alarms. Uses practice and repetition; changes skills and behavior. Must be continuous and repetitive.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Development</td>\n<td>Culmination of education, training and experience, plus a strong dose of professional commitment.</td>\n<td>\"Experience is the best teacher\" is not necessarily true. Experience must be guided; if it is not understood, wrong lessons are learned and repeated.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>What development achieves (7 effects):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Adds new skills or competencies.</li>\n<li>Sharpens or improves existing competencies.</li>\n<li>Encourages applying knowledge or skills to the post.</li>\n<li>Increases performance: the employee is more efficient and works smarter.</li>\n<li>Prepares the employee for a future promotion.</li>\n<li>Prepares the subordinate for new challenges.</li>\n<li>Helps the officer or the investigator solve problems.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>12.9.5 Training Strategies</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Objective: make training more cost-effective.</li>\n<li><strong>12 strategies used successfully:</strong><ol>\n<li>Off-duty training.</li>\n<li>Tuition reimbursement.</li>\n<li>Recognition programs.</li>\n<li>Integrated training.</li>\n<li>Videos.</li>\n<li>Selling security services.</li>\n<li>Supervisory training.</li>\n<li>Internships.</li>\n<li>Previous experience.</li>\n<li>Officer selection.</li>\n<li>Job rotation.</li>\n<li>Professional memberships.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The book develops only 7 (below). Videos, previous experience, selection, rotation and memberships are only mentioned.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Benefits and cautions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Off-duty training</td>\n<td>Voluntary, outside working hours. E.g., Saturday seminars on executive protection, counterterrorism and defense against multiple attackers. Provide food and a certificate.</td>\n<td>Motivates more than mandatory training (e.g., recertifying first aid or cardiopulmonary resuscitation, <em>CPR</em>), which can seem repetitive. The most motivated stand out and positive peer pressure is generated. Does not generate overtime.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tuition reimbursement</td>\n<td>Pay for emergency medical technician, state firearms qualification or the CPO program from that budget.</td>\n<td>Does not come out of the department's training budget. Low cost compared with the large growth. These milestones can be a criterion for promotion to supervisor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recognition programs</td>\n<td>Rewards that reinforce the officer's role (details below).</td>\n<td>Must be designed to project the desired image: customer service, enforcement or emergency response.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrated training</td>\n<td>Including officers in the parent company's or client's training: customer service, communication, writing and other corporate courses.</td>\n<td>Gives cheap access to computer-based instruction from corporations and government. Socializes and broadens perspectives; keeps the focus on service. Builds trust and a high profile for the department. Employees trust officers they know: it is community policing that helps investigate and obtain intelligence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Selling security services</td>\n<td>Equipment is expensive: costs are shared. Departments sell their training to other entities and fund their internal training. Some contractor providers create corporate institutes for clients and their own staff.</td>\n<td>Watch liability in certain types of training. The contractor must not end up training its competition.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervisory training</td>\n<td>Teaching supervision topics to officers.</td>\n<td>Reinforces their role as representatives of management. They are not supervisors, but they lead in routine and emergencies: they are trained as adjunct members of the management team.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internships</td>\n<td>Students see line and management functions. They research to create instruction and help recruit their peers.</td>\n<td>Recruiting tool: a college-student officer already trained and who knows the environment. They also attract older people changing careers. Postsecondary level: management focus. They are mentoring and education of novices.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Recognition programs, examples:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uniform patch for completing advanced first aid.</li>\n<li>CPO pin for whoever earns the designation.</li>\n<li>Plaques with photo and achievements (CPO, IAHSS Basic, CSSM) in visible areas; also online certificates or plaques.</li>\n<li>Company newsletter and dinners or lunches for training milestones.</li>\n<li>Lateral promotions or \"promotion-in-place\": same level, but with a raise, change of rank or title, or change of duties.<ul>\n<li>Extra benefit: creates specialties. E.g., workplace <em>safety</em> specialists, investigators, crime prevention coordinators and FTOs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Bonuses: one-time payment that controls salary cost and reinforces behaviors. E.g., advanced first aid, CPO, certification to carry weapons, the IFPO's Security Supervision and Management Program or the IAHSS Supervisor Program (health care).<ul>\n<li>They create another level of professional expertise within the force.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Off-duty, how to lower instructor costs:</strong> partner with US high school protective services programs. In exchange: a guest speaker from the company, job shadowing for students or visits to the facility.</p>\n<p><strong>Internships versus externships:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Internships</th>\n<th>Externships</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Origin</td>\n<td>Postsecondary level (university).</td>\n<td>Protective services programs of secondary schools.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duration</td>\n<td>Longer.</td>\n<td>Shorter: the student accompanies a professional for a day, more or less.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use</td>\n<td>Management focus; recruiting.</td>\n<td>Recruiting entry-level officers.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>12.9.6 Obstacles to Providing Training</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The obstacles must be known before launching the program and how to face them planned.</li>\n<li>Launching it blindly usually derails it, and the officers are left without the training they need.</li>\n<li><strong>The 5 most common obstacles:</strong> budget limits, scheduling, lack of management expertise in training, stereotypes and ego.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>1. Budgetary limitations:</strong> the great enemy of training.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Realistically identify all the costs:<ul>\n<li>Salary of those being trained.</li>\n<li>Overtime of replacement officers.</li>\n<li>Instructor fees.</li>\n<li>Training aids and equipment.</li>\n<li>Purchase of materials.</li>\n<li>Administrative development.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>With a firm figure, decide how to proceed.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Way to stretch the budget</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Buying off-the-shelf programs</td>\n<td>Much cheaper than custom development. They must be relevant, not perfect, especially if the alternative is not to train or to take a long time. They can be supplemented with in-house material.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subcontracting to a community college or technical institute</td>\n<td>Fixed courses at an outside institution. Can be a condition of employment or promotion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seeking grant money</td>\n<td>State and federal programs may fund job training.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Joining professional organizations</td>\n<td>ASIS International, IFPO and the International Law Enforcement Educators &amp; Trainers Association (ILEETA) give discounts to members. Access to instructors and materials that are hard and expensive to replicate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sharing instructional materials</td>\n<td>With other branches or neighboring facilities with similar needs; also training alliances.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Using distance learning</td>\n<td>Reduces classroom and instructor time and costs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Using free training from government agencies</td>\n<td>What is offered in the community.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>2. Scheduling:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In 24/7 operations it is hard to find time; classes must be repeated many times to cover everyone.</li>\n<li>Most useful: some type of distance learning or self-study.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option (5 in the book)</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Online learning programs</td>\n<td>Synchronous: instructor and learner at the same time, in real time. Asynchronous: feedback is not immediate. There are packages by type and level of officer and webinars with a test and documentation. More stimulating and interactive than media that are simply passed from hand to hand.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>↳ within option 1: in-house e-learning</td>\n<td>For organizations with a lot of personnel, especially providers with a centralized training department. In proprietary settings there is economy of scale if it serves more people: managers, emergency squads or all employees. A learning needs assessment of the organization says whether it is viable.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Written procedures</td>\n<td>Handed out and then the content is tested. Cheap, specific to the facility or organization and easy to administer. There must be some human interaction to ensure they are interpreted correctly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Supplemental media</td>\n<td>Procedures, instructions or articles that pass from one recipient to another: by mail, website or a document with a routing slip. Pass-along logs are cheap, keep learning alive and remind of what is important.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Distance learning</td>\n<td>Online programs from ASIS International or universities. Used mainly for supervisors and managers; they also serve investigators.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Certifications</td>\n<td>Physical Security Professional (PSP), from ASIS International. Certified Protection Officer (CPO), from the IFPO. Certified Institutional Protection Specialist (CIPS), from the International Foundation for Cultural Property Protection. Specialties: IAHSS and the American Hotel and Lodging Association. Usually distance or at home.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Benefit of certifications for the employer: recognized educational standard, benchmark and promotional tool for the security area. They also bring international reach in a global economy.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book lists \"online learning programs\" and \"distance learning\" as separate options. The second refers to programs for supervisors and managers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Lack of management expertise in training:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Almost every manager can teach some topics. Without training in design and delivery, it will be hard to do so.</li>\n<li>Often outside the manager's expertise: diagnosing needs, writing instructional objectives, creating materials and designing tests.</li>\n<li>Two ways out: learn the whole process or hire someone expert.</li>\n<li>Creating materials takes time; even knowing how, the manager may not have the time. It is crucial that the manager assess strengths and limits.</li>\n<li>Main instructor certification in the sector: <strong>Certified Protection Officer Instructor (CPOI)</strong>, from the IFPO. Those with teaching experience who are already CPOs may apply.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4. Stereotypes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Society's view of the officer is usually not very positive.</li>\n<li>2020 survey of UK residents: the security officer ranked 9th out of 10 in a list of essential services.</li>\n<li>In popular culture the officer is portrayed as older or uneducated, sitting in a booth or making rounds.</li>\n<li>Managers may also believe it. The slightest hint of prejudice in instruction can derail the program.</li>\n<li>The manager must examine his or her own feelings toward subordinates. A willing and creative one always finds a way to train.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>5. Ego:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Some managers believe they have the expertise and the time to write every lesson plan and deliver every course.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Frog Syndrome\" (Hertig, 1993):</strong> the manager \"jumps in\" to train everyone in person and \"jumps out\" on seeing the load. Result: training stops and organizational development stagnates.</li>\n<li><strong>McGlaughlin case (2003):</strong> a security director saw that he needed help. He redesigned a sergeant-level vacancy so that the new hire would evolve into training operations manager. It shows that this is a rational way out that adds to organizational development.</li>\n<li>A position dedicated to training lets the department train other areas:<ul>\n<li>Hospital: certified training for nursing in nonviolent crisis intervention or management of aggressive behavior.</li>\n<li>Hotel: Training for Intervention Procedures by Servers of Alcohol (TIPS) certification for restaurant staff.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e9\">12.10 Managing the Security Officer Force</h3><ul>\n<li>Despite the efficiency technology provides, security officers remain:<ul>\n<li>The largest segment of employees in the security sector.</li>\n<li>The largest cost item in most security operating budgets.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>For that reason, their deployment is guided by rational, objective business criteria, with a complete assessment of needs.</li>\n<li>Once the need is established, the next step is to schedule and assign them cost-effectively.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.10.1 Personnel Requirements</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The manager determines how many posts and officers are needed to protect the facility's assets.</li>\n<li>Post orders are based on standard operating procedures (SOP), which must be site-specific.</li>\n<li><strong>SOP:</strong> a compendium of the procedures the security group follows day to day. It may include emergency response procedures.</li>\n<li>By setting what the organization expects, SOPs help determine the requirements of each post.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Definition of a security officer post:</strong> any location or combination of activities that requires a trained human being.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Post type</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stationary or fixed post</td>\n<td>Stationary location.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Patrol post</td>\n<td>Officer on a roving patrol.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The location may have desirable or undesirable activities that require the officer's presence.</li>\n<li>Or it may be a predetermined point: access control for employees, visitors, or contractors, or a central control facility.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.10.2 General, Post, and Special Orders</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Scope</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>General orders</td>\n<td>Canons or body of principles for the officer. They have been used by military police, municipal police, state police, and contract security firms.</td>\n<td>Apply to the entire force.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post orders</td>\n<td>The most important written instructions of the security force.</td>\n<td>Specific to each post and site.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special orders</td>\n<td>Address temporary events.</td>\n<td>Only during a defined period.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Common general orders (9):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Familiarity, fraternization, and unnecessary conversation are prohibited while on duty.</li>\n<li>Maintain professional or military bearing.</li>\n<li>Smoking and eating are prohibited while on duty.</li>\n<li>Any statement to the media is made by the designated spokesperson or the public information officer (PIO).</li>\n<li>Enforce the rules with diplomacy.</li>\n<li>Do not use the employer's uniform or equipment without authorization.</li>\n<li>Immediately inform the employer of any criminal conviction.</li>\n<li>Do not leave the post without proper relief; never leave a post open for any reason.</li>\n<li>When leaving, fully brief the relief and pass on every order issued.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Functions of post orders (4):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They express the policies of the protected company.</li>\n<li>They summarize the tasks the officer must perform.</li>\n<li>They avoid the problems of word-of-mouth instructions.</li>\n<li>They are the basis of site-specific training.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why they matter:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In serious incidents, the officer's integrity, competence, or ability can be questioned. Clear orders carry weight there.</li>\n<li>Not everything can be foreseen; innovation, flexibility, and improvisation are important qualities in an officer.</li>\n<li>But if an officer decides poorly in a foreseeable incident without clear instructions, it is a sign of poor management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Criteria for writing post orders (7):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. One subject per order</td>\n<td>It is found quickly when consulted. It makes it easier to revise or cancel if circumstances change.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. As brief as possible</td>\n<td>It is an action document: what to do and when. The reasons are explained elsewhere in the training.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Simple, clear language</td>\n<td>No jargon, nuances, or ambiguity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Detailed index</td>\n<td>A complete, cross-referenced index; each subject listed in several ways. E.g., \"Snow removal, emergency procedures\" and \"Emergency procedures, snow removal\".</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Available at every post</td>\n<td>Up to date and accessible: they are the link between what the client requires and the officer's ability to deliver it. Review them periodically so they are current and complete.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6. Coordinated effort</td>\n<td>There must be no contradictory orders. This happens with complex systems; electronic reporting or many posts make it worse. Always maintain a single coherent set.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7. Special orders for temporary events</td>\n<td>E.g., temporary closure of another gate that affects a post, or a special event. They must clearly state the period in which they are in effect.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Where to keep post orders:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Post type</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed</td>\n<td>In a designated place at the post.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vehicle patrol</td>\n<td>Inside the vehicle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Foot patrol</td>\n<td>Electronic devices (smartphone or other mobile device) that give access to the orders and allow reporting and sharing information. On paper: a photographically reduced, pocket-size version, only for that post.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> typo in the book on special orders: it says \"clearly specific the time period\"; it should read <em>specify</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.10.3 Scheduling</h4>\n<p><strong>Scheduling software:</strong> now common in private security. It is usually modular.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Module</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Basic</td>\n<td>Assign personnel to posts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Additional</td>\n<td>Time and attendance recording; payroll administration.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>For contract companies</td>\n<td>Client billing; job cost control.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>They generate reports, charts, and graphs; the data can usually be exported to other programs.</li>\n<li>Security: access authorization levels, from read-only to permission to change data.</li>\n<li>To build the best schedule, they cross-match work requirements against the pool of available personnel.</li>\n<li>Some automatically report the officer's arrival and departure. The officer logs into the system with a site number, officer number, and personal password.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Work site requirements:</strong> detailed analysis of posts and schedules, whether manual or with software. It includes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Each post by type (fixed, vehicle, or foot) and by post number.</li>\n<li>Start and end times of each shift, by day.</li>\n<li>Number of officers per shift and per day.</li>\n<li>Special staffing requirements per shift, if applicable.</li>\n<li>Names of personnel who are not assigned to the post.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Personnel database:</strong> a profile of each employee with:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Name, address, and phone numbers.</li>\n<li>Employee number.</li>\n<li>Shift and overtime preferences.</li>\n<li>Maximum hours the employee can work.</li>\n<li>Availability (key for part-time officers).</li>\n<li>Special training, certifications, and qualifications (skills inventory).</li>\n<li>Vacation days entitled to and days requested.</li>\n<li>Scheduled training days.</li>\n<li>Posts the employee is excluded from.</li>\n<li>Other considerations, such as physical limitations.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Schedules:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>By combining site requirements and personal data, the program optimizes the use of personnel and avoids overtime.</li>\n<li>If an additional officer is needed, it considers: travel time from the officer's home to the site, scheduled hours, and the overtime policy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Proprietary force versus contract force:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Proprietary force</th>\n<th>Contract force</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Basic schedule</td>\n<td>Besides post hours and shifts, other factors must be included, such as each officer's paid holidays.</td>\n<td>Client and agency agree on the basic schedule: total officer-hours per week. The agency places the officers at the posts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Holidays</td>\n<td>Some collective bargaining agreements give the option to refuse to work a holiday: review their wording.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Illness</td>\n<td>Average sick days of the force over 2 years as a basis for estimating future years. Look at their distribution: absences are higher in winter.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other absences</td>\n<td>Jury duty, illness of family members, or emergencies.</td>\n<td>Covered by the agency.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How they are covered</td>\n<td>With overtime. Expensive in small forces. Other costs: fatigue, damaged morale, and reduced alertness. They can cancel out the apparent savings of a tight basic schedule.</td>\n<td>In theory, the agency has a broad base and brings in officers from other clients. In practice, the replacement may never have been at the site.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>The post is not learned in 1 or 2 hours; an inexperienced officer can do more harm than good. The problem shifts from the client to the agency, but it is no easier.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solution</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>If relief must come from the site's regular staff: overtime. In large accounts: occasionally assign personnel from other clients for relief, so they gain experience at the site.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Maximizing the straight time schedule:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Planned overlapping of shifts makes it possible to have more personnel at peak hours without paying overtime.</li>\n<li>A 30-minute or 1-hour meal break within an 8-hour shift extends the officer's presence by that same amount of time.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Shift</th>\n<th>Scheduled hours</th>\n<th>With 1-hour meal break</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First shift (day)</td>\n<td>0700–1500</td>\n<td>0700–1600</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second shift (afternoon)</td>\n<td>1500–2300</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overlap</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>1500–1600: both shifts fully on duty</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Useful for peaks: plant shift changes or office breaks.</li>\n<li>But during the meal break the officer leaves his or her duties. It may be unpaid, depending on the policy or the collective bargaining agreement.</li>\n<li>Difficult with small forces or forces with only fixed posts. Solutions:<ul>\n<li>Creative use of patrol officers.</li>\n<li>Temporary closure of fixed posts.</li>\n<li>A supervisor covers posts during meals.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>With a collective bargaining agreement, or if a contract prohibits it, the supervisor cannot do the officer's work on a regular basis. It can cause a formal grievance.</li>\n<li>Officers must log their breaks like any activity. In the event of an incident, the log makes it possible to verify whether the officer was on break or should have been at the post.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.10.4 Supervision</h4>\n<p><strong>Organization models:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>Rank structure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Military or police (historical origin)</td>\n<td>Security chief (highest rank) → captain → lieutenant → sergeant → officer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business (current trend)</td>\n<td>Entry level: security officer → supervisor → conventional managerial titles, e.g., assistant manager, manager, and director.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The trend is to move away from the military or paramilitary model and organize with business supervision lines.</li>\n<li>The number and levels of supervision depend on factors such as facility size, schedules, and geographic location of the posts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Special training for everyone who supervises:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Management and leadership.</li>\n<li>Human relations and interpersonal communication skills.</li>\n<li>Labor and criminal law.</li>\n<li>Emergency response.</li>\n<li>Other topics.</li>\n<li>Ideal: complete it before promotion to supervisor and maintain it as a continuing requirement in the position.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Role of the shift supervisor:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is on the front line: is a mentor, provides on-the-job training (OJT), and fosters employee satisfaction.</li>\n<li>Must understand the site's requirements, management's needs, and his or her own role.</li>\n<li><strong>The quality of asset protection is a direct function of local supervision.</strong></li>\n<li>The experienced supervisor is a local trainer:<ul>\n<li>Observes performance and gives feedback when something was not done correctly or the officer hesitated.</li>\n<li>Introduces new material to the officer or to the whole shift.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Formation at shift change:</strong> at the start or end of the shift. In those minutes a topic is explained, e.g., the essentials of a new post order. Then the officers are asked to read it during the shift.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Orientation of new officers to the site:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If there is an opportunity and budget, pair the new officer with the supervisor or with an experienced patrol officer.</li>\n<li>Keep log sheets and training records for all officers.</li>\n<li>Training log sheets break each responsibility of the site down into a series of tasks.</li>\n<li>Trainer and trainee initial each task to verify it was completed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Verifying that a new order was read (during the post visit):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ask one or more specific questions about the order.</li>\n<li>Observe the officer in a real situation where it applies.</li>\n<li>Pose a hypothetical situation that requires showing practical knowledge of the order.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>It takes a few minutes and should be routine on every supervisory visit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Post visits:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The supervisor must visit every post. If he or she stays at the command post or another fixed post, performance at the others drops and problems requiring his or her intervention increase.</li>\n<li>Radios, tablets, public address systems, and smartphones eliminate the need to stay at a fixed post.</li>\n<li>Regular but unscheduled visits to all posts, with immediate communication with the entire force.</li>\n<li>Observes the post, officer, surroundings, and level of activity. By observing the route between posts, he or she performs a supplementary patrol.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Evaluations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Constant problem: rating officers' performance.</li>\n<li>Without an incident that shows very good or very bad performance, the shift may go unevaluated. That causes problems in the salary review.</li>\n<li>Partial solution: the supervisor evaluates and records performance after each post visit.</li>\n<li>The evaluation must include, at a minimum (5):<ol>\n<li>Personal appearance and condition of the officer.</li>\n<li>Physical condition of the post.</li>\n<li>Availability and condition of the required personnel and equipment, including post orders.</li>\n<li>Quality of the response to questions or training situations.</li>\n<li>Quality of the response to real situations that occurred during the visit.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Record everything and pass it to a security force management program. It serves to detect trends and training needs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.10.5 Effectiveness Assessment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Applies both to those who provide contract security and to those with in-house officers.</li>\n<li>They must create methods to ensure the service meets the security and organizational objectives.</li>\n<li><strong>Objective:</strong> to test, inspect, or identify problems, normally at the service or contract site, in order to:<ul>\n<li>Correct them.</li>\n<li>Maintain and improve the service.</li>\n<li>Prevent problems before they occur.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It is essential for maintaining quality and continuously improving performance and operations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.10.6 Inspections</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>On-site supervisors are essential for the force to perform as expected.</li>\n<li>Some organizations, especially contract security companies, add inspectors outside the force's direct chain of command.</li>\n<li>They conduct quality control inspections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Advantages of \"independent\" inspectors (3):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Advantage</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. They are not known</td>\n<td>The inspector can observe or interact without the officer knowing he or she is being evaluated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. More objectivity</td>\n<td>The immediate supervisor may be influenced by personal biases.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Better reaction tests</td>\n<td>When testing the officer's reaction, e.g., bringing a suspicious package into the facility, it is best done by someone the officer does not know.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Objectives of inspections:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identify poor performance.</li>\n<li>Identify topics requiring supplementary training.</li>\n<li>Identify procedures that need clarification.</li>\n<li>Identify and reward exceptional performance.</li>\n<li>Communicate the quality of performance to clients or to management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Procedures that must be established:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Frequency of inspections.</li>\n<li>Method for capturing and recording the data.</li>\n<li>How and to whom the data are communicated.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>An effective quality control plan is the basis of continuous improvement of the contract. It is fundamental to the success of security operations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.10.7 Management Use of Data</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Management must regularly review and analyze sources that show the force's strengths and weaknesses.</li>\n<li><strong>Sources cited by the book (6):</strong><ol>\n<li>Post logs.</li>\n<li>Main or control logs.</li>\n<li>Supervisor reports.</li>\n<li>Independent inspector reports.</li>\n<li>Disciplinary actions against employees.</li>\n<li>Complaints or commendations.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Automated web-based data management systems make capture more efficient and generate reports quickly.<ul>\n<li>They provide a bird's eye view or isolate one element for in-depth analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Making use of all the data is a continuing challenge.</li>\n<li>Even if collected because of legal or contractual requirements, they are useful for detecting trends.</li>\n<li><strong>Regular trend analysis:</strong><ul>\n<li>Basis of continuous improvement and fine-tuning of operations.</li>\n<li>It can justify the existence of the operation, by detailing incidents and officer responses.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reading the reports (source of officer dissatisfaction):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The officer is bothered when management appears not to read reports or logs.</li>\n<li>The manager must read them promptly, recognize what is important, and order correction if needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> an officer reports a burned-out emergency exit light and it stays that way for weeks. He thinks no one cares and stops reporting that problem and others.</li>\n<li>Lesson: be proactive when the data show something needs correcting.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> typo in the book: \"when a data sources clearly reveals\"; it should read <em>a data source</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>12.10.8 Enhancing Job Performance</h4>\n<p><strong>Why the job is demanding:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tasks range from routine to highly complex.</li>\n<li>The officer often works alone with little direct supervision.</li>\n<li>He or she is expected to take personal risks that other employees avoid and to deal with the full spectrum of human behavior.</li>\n<li>Makes decisions that affect the well-being of others and millions of dollars in physical assets.</li>\n<li>Because of the importance of first impressions, an inefficient or indifferent officer affects visitors, other security employees, and the entire workforce.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Implications:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>From both the fiscal and the human standpoint, efficient officer performance is critical to the asset protection program.</li>\n<li>The officer is an expensive and valuable resource; management must ensure optimal performance.</li>\n<li>The consequences of poor personnel development are quickly noticed when a serious incident occurs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Factors of performance excellence:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Factors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Traditional</td>\n<td>Pay, supervision, and deployment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Additional</td>\n<td>Job analysis, training techniques, work environment (including corporate and departmental cultures), and ergonomics.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Managers and supervisors must detect deficiencies and decide on and apply the appropriate corrective actions.</li>\n<li>Attending to these factors has a direct impact on job satisfaction, the turnover rate, and ultimately the success of security operations.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e10\">12.11 Leveraging the Human Element</h3><ul>\n<li>The human element of security goes beyond contract, proprietary, and hybrid security officers. It also includes non-security personnel.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Examples of non-security personnel with security functions:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Person</th>\n<th>Main focus</th>\n<th>Security functions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Office building concierge</td>\n<td>Customer service.</td>\n<td>Access control, observation, and reporting.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Manager, often in human resources</td>\n<td>The book does not detail it.</td>\n<td>Workplace violence and preemployment screening. May be a liaison with law enforcement and with community support resources.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Who receives security tasks (officially or unofficially) depends on:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The organization's needs assessment.</li>\n<li>Resources.</li>\n<li>Location and schedules.</li>\n<li>Each person's capabilities, interests, and current workload.</li>\n<li>Caution: these tasks often require specialized knowledge. Seek training and education from organizations such as ASIS International.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Security awareness for the whole organization:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>According to Chapter 2, a comprehensive strategy includes awareness training for personnel in all departments.</li>\n<li>It applies with proprietary, contract, hybrid security, or none at all.</li>\n<li>According to section 12.6 (<em>Security Officer Roles</em>), officers perform tasks beyond the traditional; at times they are a liaison with management, clients, and outside agencies.</li>\n<li>Their visibility and daily contact with people put them in a unique position to expand awareness.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost-effective and practical ideas:</strong><ul>\n<li>Include it in new employee orientation.</li>\n<li>Special lunches.</li>\n<li>Regular work meetings.</li>\n<li>Free online courses: Nationwide SAR (Suspicious Activity Reporting) Initiative; courses from the U.S. Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) in the Independent Study Program (ISP) of the Emergency Management Institute.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Underlying principle: awareness is a powerful security strategy, especially with broad training that reaches beyond security personnel.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Business Watch programs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They expand awareness and reduce burglary, robberies, shoplifting, fraud, and vandalism.</li>\n<li>Similar to the very successful Neighborhood Watch programs. They take advantage of the interest of businesses and community groups united by a common cause.</li>\n<li>Strategy: link designated police officers with business and community leaders. Together they help local owners, operators, and employees report and prevent crime.</li>\n<li>They usually include security, safety, and awareness training, and programs for marking valuables, such as Operation Identification.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Case: Prince William County (Virginia) Police Crime Prevention Unit (Prince William County Police, 2020):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Its Business Watch allows a participating organization to become a Certified Crime Prevention Business.</li>\n<li><strong>Requirements for certification (4):</strong><ol>\n<li>Have written policies and procedures that address crime.</li>\n<li>Train its employees in crime prevention.</li>\n<li>Accept an on-site security assessment by Crime Prevention Unit personnel who know CPTED (Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design; Chapter 10).</li>\n<li>Designate a point of contact as liaison with the police department.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>What it teaches: involving the community recognizes the enormous power of non-security personnel in crime prevention.</li>\n<li>It is another important aspect of the human element in an integrated physical security strategy.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-12-e11\">Key terms</h3><p>security officer / protection officer · necessary human being · defense-in-depth · proprietary security force · contract security force · hybrid model · co-employment · bid specifications · post orders · general orders · special orders · property removal pass · foot patrol · mobile patrol · security control center · duress alarm · lockdown · shelter-in-place · altered mental status · emergency medical services (EMS) · crisis intervention · post log · main/control log · military time · management representative · enforcement/compliance agent · torts · use-of-force policy · gun custodian · qualified armorer · less than lethal weapons · conducted electrical weapon (CEW) / conducted energy device (CED) · TASER · stun gun · oleoresin capsicum (OC) · Private Security Officer Selection and Training Guideline (PSO) · job task analysis · after-action reports · IASIR · IFPO code of ethics · CoESS · BSIA · SITO · CPO · CSSM · CPOI · APP · CPP · PSP · CIPS · LPQ / LPC · computer-aided dispatching (CAD) · lectures · case study · job aids · distance learning · synchronous / asynchronous delivery · on-the-job training (OJT) · mentoring · field training officer (FTO) · cognitive / affective / psychomotor learning · retention · transfer · socialization · Education + Training + Guided Experience = Development · tuition reimbursement · promotion-in-place · internships / externships · off-the-shelf programs · pass-along logs · Frog Syndrome · TIPS · SOP · fixed post / patrol post · PIO · personnel database / skills inventory · straight time schedule · training log sheets · effectiveness assessment · independent inspectors · quality control inspections · trend analysis · Business Watch · Operation Identification · Certified Crime Prevention Business · Nationwide SAR Initiative</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"12.1 Security Officer Utilization Growth"},{"id":"e1","t":"12.2 Contemporary Challenges"},{"id":"e2","t":"12.3 Determining the Need for a Security Force"},{"id":"e3","t":"12.4 Security Force Models"},{"id":"e4","t":"12.5 Basic Security Officer Functions"},{"id":"e5","t":"12.6 Security Officer Roles"},{"id":"e6","t":"12.7 Uniforms and Equipment"},{"id":"e7","t":"12.8 Security Officer Selection"},{"id":"e8","t":"12.9 Security Officer Training"},{"id":"e9","t":"12.10 Managing the Security Officer Force"},{"id":"e10","t":"12.11 Leveraging the Human Element"},{"id":"e11","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"12.5 Basic Security Officer Functions","h":"the list has 12 functions, but the book develops only 10 sections (12.5.1–12.5.10). “Reporting of incidents and damage” and “enforcing regulations” have no section of their own; the latter reappears as a role in 12.6.3."},{"s":"12.5 Basic Security Officer Functions","h":"the sentence that introduces this list is cut off in the book (editing error); the list itself is complete."},{"s":"12.7 Uniforms and Equipment","h":"answer with the book's information. In practice, in several Latin American countries the armed guard is common (outside the book)."},{"s":"12.7 Uniforms and Equipment","h":"the book swaps the chemical names. It gives CS = 2-chloroacetophenone and CN = “0-chlorobenzylidene.” The correct ones are CN = 2-chloroacetophenone and CS = o-chlorobenzylidene malononitrile (outside the book)."},{"s":"12.8 Security Officer Selection","h":"the book mixes dates (need in 2001, cited version of 2010, revision of 2019). The first edition of the guideline was published in 2004 (outside the book)."},{"s":"12.9 Security Officer Training","h":"the book lists \"online learning programs\" and \"distance learning\" as separate options. The second refers to programs for supervisors and managers."},{"s":"12.10 Managing the Security Officer Force","h":"typo in the book on special orders: it says \"clearly specific the time period\"; it should read <em>specify</em>."},{"s":"12.10 Managing the Security Officer Force","h":"typo in the book: \"when a data sources clearly reveals\"; it should read <em>a data source</em>."}]},"physical-13":{"html":"<h3 id=\"physical-13-e0\">Project management and projects</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Project management:</strong> planning, organizing, and controlling a project's resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Project vs. operations:</strong> a project is temporary, with a defined start and end; operations are ongoing.</li>\n<li>A project pursues specific goals and objectives that benefit the organization.</li>\n<li>Two types of security project:<ul>\n<li>Inherent to the security mission. Example: improving visitor security.</li>\n<li>The security part of a larger project. Example: the security (capital and operating) of a new building on a campus.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Limits: start and end date, and an allocated budget.</li>\n<li>Exceeding them has consequences for the organization, stakeholders, the team, and above all the project manager.</li>\n<li>Challenge: achieving all the goals within time and budget, with minimal budget and operational impact.</li>\n<li>Well managed, it leaves lasting benefits; poorly managed, it leaves lasting negative effects.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-13-e1\">Goals and objectives of security projects</h3><ul>\n<li>Improve the experience of employees and others in how security is managed.</li>\n<li>Ensure the effectiveness of the security organization so that:<ul>\n<li>Only authorized persons enter semipublic, controlled, or restricted spaces.</li>\n<li>Responders are reliably alerted to any unauthorized entry.</li>\n<li>Incidents receive an effective response.</li>\n<li>Usable evidence is gathered from incidents.</li>\n<li>Usable metrics are gathered on everything security-related and used to manage better and support the mission.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Examples of projects:<ul>\n<li>Design and installation of <strong>physical protection systems (PPS)</strong> in a new or renovated building.</li>\n<li>Helping the architect plan the space with <strong>CPTED (crime prevention through environmental design)</strong> or <strong>security landscaping</strong>.</li>\n<li>Human resources planning to optimize facility security.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-13-e2\">Project constraints</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Constraint</th>\n<th>What it limits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Project scope</td>\n<td>What the project includes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project schedule</td>\n<td>Dates and deadlines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project budget</td>\n<td>Money allocated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project quality</td>\n<td>Level of quality required</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A well-conceived project → the project manager can balance all four and not exceed them.</li>\n<li>Exceeding them is usually due to poor conception or poor management.</li>\n<li>A poorly conceived project will surely fail to meet its goals.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-13-e3\">Project management process elements (Project Management Institute, 2013)</h3><p>In addition to scope, schedule, and budget, the process includes these areas and their subprocesses:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Subprocesses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Project integration management</td>\n<td>Project plan development · project plan execution · integrated change control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project quality management</td>\n<td>Quality planning · quality assurance · quality control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project human resource management</td>\n<td>Organizational planning · staff acquisition · team development</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project communications management</td>\n<td>Communications planning · information distribution · performance reporting · administrative closure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project risk management</td>\n<td>Risk management planning · risk identification · qualitative risk analysis · quantitative risk analysis · risk response planning · risk monitoring and control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project procurement management</td>\n<td>Procurement planning · solicitation planning · solicitation · source selection · contract administration · contract closeout</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book cites PMI 2013, but these subprocess names are those of old editions of the PMBOK (Project Management Body of Knowledge). Recent editions have other areas, such as stakeholder management (outside the book). For the exam, use the book's list.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-13-e4\">Roles of the security project manager</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Who or what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Creator of the design concept</td>\n<td>Creates the concept to gain senior management support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Principal decision maker</td>\n<td>For example, CEO or CFO; influences scope, budget, and schedule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Funding controller</td>\n<td>Manages or influences the budget</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project influencer</td>\n<td>Security manager, chief legal counsel, or consultant; influences scope and budget</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project stakeholder</td>\n<td>Security manager when others control scope and funds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project contractor</td>\n<td>Manages labor and materials according to a schedule</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"physical-13-e5\">Project phases</h3><ol>\n<li>Project conception.</li>\n<li>Project planning.</li>\n<li>Project design management.</li>\n<li>Project bid process management, or participation.</li>\n<li>Project construction, or construction review.</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"physical-13-e6\">Integrating security elements</h3><p>The project integrates physical, electronic, and operational elements into a cohesive solution for five functions:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Deter</td>\n<td>Signage and fences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detect (detect and assess)</td>\n<td>Perimeter detection and video cameras</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delay</td>\n<td>Barriers and doors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Respond</td>\n<td>PPS, console personnel, communications equipment, patrol vehicles, and response officers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recover</td>\n<td>No specific examples in the book</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The system must also accumulate evidence for discipline or criminal prosecution.</li>\n<li>That evidence also serves procedures, training, and program improvement.</li>\n<li>Design and integration bring the technological and the physical into the overall asset protection program.</li>\n<li>Result: an integrated program that combines the architectural, the technological, and the operational in a flexible, responsive system.</li>\n<li>Key design factors: facility environment, unique needs, threats, risks, vulnerabilities, and constraints.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-13-e7\">Security risk assessment</h3><ul>\n<li>Every security project must be based on a <strong>security risk assessment</strong>.</li>\n<li>Without it, the scope is decided without knowing threats, likelihoods, vulnerabilities, or consequences.</li>\n<li>The organization is left unprepared for unknown risks; decisions are made only on assumptions.</li>\n<li>The risk assessment: identifies assets to protect, determines the impact of losses and the perils to the assets.</li>\n<li>Typical phrase after a catastrophe: “I had no idea that could happen”.</li>\n<li>Knowing the risk in advance can prevent worst-case events, often at minimal or no cost.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says that a project without a risk assessment “will not necessarily be flawed”. From context, it means the opposite: it will necessarily be flawed. Probable typo.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-13-e8\">Basic tasks of PPS implementation</h3><ol>\n<li>Planning and assessment: determine the security requirements.</li>\n<li>Develop conceptual solutions that address the vulnerabilities.</li>\n<li>Prepare the PPS design and construction documentation.</li>\n<li>Solicit bids, set prices, and negotiate with vendors.</li>\n<li>Install, test, and commission the PPS.</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"physical-13-e9\">Project manager’s tools</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Training:</strong> project management is not a natural skill; it is learned.</li>\n<li><strong>Project management software:</strong> scheduling software and office software.</li>\n<li><strong>Determination:</strong> firmly fight the necessary battles to control scope, schedule, and budget.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-13-e10\">Project management process for an integrated PPS project</h3><ol>\n<li><strong>Project concept:</strong>\n   - Understand who the decision makers, influencers, and stakeholders are.\n   - Define the scope: architectural aspects; security needs; monitoring, response, and management requirements; performance (functions and quality); capacity and flexibility; compliance with codes and regulation; operational considerations; visual aesthetics; ease of operation; cultural aspects.\n   - Define the preliminary budget.\n   - Define the schedule.\n   - Do a reality check of scope, schedule, and budget.</li>\n<li><strong>Designing the project:</strong>\n   - Gather the stakeholders’ operational needs.\n   - Apply design concepts to the vulnerabilities and operational needs.\n   - Coordinate with other disciplines: electrical, doors, elevators, architecture, landscaping, and lighting (the book leaves the list open).\n   - Adjust the design to applicable codes and regulations.\n   - Adjust it to the organization’s standards: product, aesthetic, signage, and operational (open list in the book).\n   - Create drawings, specifications, and perhaps a bill of quantities.\n   - Revise the preliminary budget.</li>\n<li><strong>Managing the bid process:</strong>\n   - Assemble the bid package: manufacturer contractual info, drawings, specifications, hardware schedules, instructions and requirements for bidders, licenses, experience and qualifications, terms and conditions, and sensitive information requirements.\n   - Look for qualified bidders: a questionnaire to find credible and competent vendors, or publish the package as a <strong>request for proposal (RFP)</strong>.\n   - Issue the bid package.\n   - Control sensitive information.\n   - If applicable, schedule a bidder’s conference or site visit <strong>one week after</strong> issuing the RFP.\n   - Receive the bids.\n   - Evaluate them: conformance with the requirements and financial evaluation.\n   - Select the bidder.</li>\n<li><strong>Managing the implementation process:</strong>\n   - Contract signing and initial kick-off meeting: sets the course for scope, schedule, and budget.\n   - Manage progress: weekly reports on status, challenges and solutions, and alerts about anything that may affect schedule or scope.\n   - Substantial completion.\n   - Initial testing: system programming, acceptance testing, and management of corrective work or the punch list.\n   - System monitoring training takes place <strong>30 days before</strong> acceptance testing.</li>\n<li><strong>Acceptance of the work.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Warranty:</strong> start and manage the <strong>12-month</strong> warranty period within scope and schedule; evaluate whether the warranty work conforms.</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"physical-13-e11\">PMP and PMBOK</h3><ul>\n<li>The <strong>Project Management Institute (PMI)</strong> offers the <strong>Project Management Professional (PMP)</strong> certification.</li>\n<li>The PMP is based on the PMI’s <strong>Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK)</strong>.</li>\n<li>The book’s recommendation: anyone serious about project management should pursue the PMP.</li>\n<li>Minimum expected: know the PMBOK very well.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-13-e12\">Key terms</h3><p>project management · project vs. operations · project scope · project schedule · project budget · project quality · project integration management · integrated change control · quality assurance · quality control · project communications management · administrative closure · project risk management · qualitative risk analysis · quantitative risk analysis · project procurement management · solicitation · source selection · contract administration · contract closeout · decision maker · funding controller · project influencer · stakeholder · physical protection system (PPS) · CPTED · security landscaping · deter, detect, delay, respond, recover · security risk assessment · commissioning · bill of quantities · bid package · request for proposal (RFP) · bidder’s conference · site visit · kick-off meeting · substantial completion · acceptance testing · punch list · warranty period · Project Management Institute (PMI) · Project Management Professional (PMP) · PMBOK</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"Project management and projects"},{"id":"e1","t":"Goals and objectives of security projects"},{"id":"e2","t":"Project constraints"},{"id":"e3","t":"Project management process elements"},{"id":"e4","t":"Roles of the security project manager"},{"id":"e5","t":"Project phases"},{"id":"e6","t":"Integrating security elements"},{"id":"e7","t":"Security risk assessment"},{"id":"e8","t":"Basic tasks of PPS implementation"},{"id":"e9","t":"Project manager’s tools"},{"id":"e10","t":"Project management process for an integrated PPS project"},{"id":"e11","t":"PMP and PMBOK"},{"id":"e12","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"Project management process elements","h":"the book cites PMI 2013, but these subprocess names are those of old editions of the PMBOK (Project Management Body of Knowledge). Recent editions have other areas, such as stakeholder management (outside the book). For the exam, use the book's list."},{"s":"Security risk assessment","h":"the book says that a project without a risk assessment “will not necessarily be flawed”. From context, it means the opposite: it will necessarily be flawed. Probable typo."}]},"physical-14":{"html":"<h3 id=\"physical-14-e0\">14.1 System Design Principles</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>System (in security):</strong> a combination of equipment, personnel, and procedures, coordinated and designed to optimally achieve its objectives.</li>\n<li>A system is more than hardware.</li>\n<li>It is evaluated by performance and cost-effectiveness in countering threats, reducing vulnerabilities, and lowering risk exposure.</li>\n<li>The professional examines all activities and considers assets, exposure, and losses.</li>\n<li>The process applies to technology and also to other security elements (design, purchase, and deployment).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Planning and assessment, first phase:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Task 1: identify critical assets, potential threats, vulnerabilities, probable risks, and functional requirements.\n   - Example: against burglary, harden the target (target hardening) to reduce opportunity.\n   - Information on needs, objectives, and constraints is gathered to control risk <strong>before</strong> the event.\n   - <strong>Proactivity vs. reactivity:</strong> a key aspect of effective risk management.</li>\n<li>Task 2: analyze the requirements and formulate countermeasure solutions or concepts that reduce or eliminate vulnerabilities and mitigate risks.</li>\n<li>Once the concepts are validated (operationally and budget-wise), design begins.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Output of the design phase:</strong> identification and location of hardware and software, integration, and performance documentation.</li>\n<li>That documentation must be so clear and complete that vendors and installers interpret it the same way.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Design is a serial process:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each phase and task is completed before the next; the output of one is the input of the other.</li>\n<li>An important concept to explain to senior management.</li>\n<li>It is only shortened a little with a <strong>design/build</strong> relationship with a contractor, as opposed to design, procurement and construction with an architect or the owner.</li>\n<li>Increasing staff or budget generally does <strong>not</strong> shorten it substantially.</li>\n<li>If the PPS is part of a larger construction project, it follows the schedules and documentation of the architectural design.</li>\n<li>That project is normally overseen by a certified architect (project overseer).</li>\n<li>Those responsible for security design work with the architect and put the requirements into the contract documents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Typical tasks of a technical security project:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Risk analysis.</li>\n<li>Conceptual, or schematic.</li>\n<li>Design development.</li>\n<li>Construction documents.</li>\n<li>Bidding.</li>\n<li>Construction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Figure 14.1. System Design Process:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Elements in the figure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Study and report</td>\n<td>Asset definition · threat · vulnerability and risk · requirements assessment · site survey</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Output of the study</td>\n<td>Basis of design · design concept</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preliminary and final design</td>\n<td>Hardware and software engineering · personnel requirements · operational requirements · support requirements · installation requirements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bid and negotiate</td>\n<td>No elements of its own in the figure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction</td>\n<td>Prepare facilities · acquire hardware/software · construction administration · establish support system · install system · select and train personnel · test system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operation</td>\n<td>System operation and support · establish self-sufficiency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Note:</strong> the text of the figure comes in an encoded font. I reconstructed it by position; verify the grouping against the original figure.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-14-e1\">14.2 Initial Phases</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Planning and assessment</strong> is the first phase of every security design project.</li>\n<li>It consists of gathering all the pre-design information about the assets and analyzing it against requirements and constraints.</li>\n<li>It always culminates in a <strong>security basis of design</strong>: the first and most important output of the process.</li>\n<li>The basis of design focuses on specific requirements and on a conceptual solution based on them.</li>\n<li>These phases: define threats (identify, gather, and organize their information), identify assets, analyze vulnerabilities, and assess risk.</li>\n<li>Security planning, assessments, and operational audits are formal processes to identify and analyze asset protection problems.</li>\n<li>They also develop requirements, objectives, criteria, concepts, and methods that the detailed design will use.</li>\n<li><strong>A survey is effective if</strong> its recommendations are acted upon and the results are measured against acceptable standards.</li>\n<li>It requires teamwork among operations, facilities, engineering, and architecture, to present the solution to management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Three key ingredients for planning success:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>A multidisciplinary, committed approach, by one person or a team.</li>\n<li>Devoting the necessary time and effort: more accurate design, fewer risks, lower cost of losses, and more durable and effective systems.</li>\n<li>Deciding with solid, relevant information on risk and on the assets’ environment.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Outputs of the planning phase:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A set of security requirements or objectives: the basis of the design, also called the <strong>design basis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Assessment:</strong> inspecting and analyzing assets and protection, normally with an initial site survey and vulnerability assessment.</li>\n<li>A <strong>conceptual solution</strong> that ranks vulnerabilities by criticality and chooses the preferred and most cost-effective scheme.</li>\n<li>In designing the solution, prevention, control, and recovery are considered.</li>\n<li>The initial solution depends entirely on how the designer interprets the functional requirements.</li>\n<li>Without requirements there is no meaningful design, and capital can be wasted on expensive construction.</li>\n<li><strong>Business case</strong> for the new or improved system: business impact, investments, quantifiable savings, and other metrics for deciding.</li>\n<li>Systems are evaluated for quality and reliability, but also for cost.</li>\n<li>Main feature of the business case: economic metrics such as return on investment (ROI), payback, and net present value (NPV) of the cash flow.</li>\n<li>A formal presentation is often mandatory before spending capital: needs, business case, costs and benefits, alternatives, and operational impact.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Equivalent in architecture and engineering:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The planning phase is called the <strong>programming and schematic design phase</strong>.</li>\n<li>It leads to the design basis (requirements analysis) and the conceptual design.</li>\n<li>The <strong>requirements analysis</strong> relies on the analysis of threats, assets, and risk.</li>\n<li>Reference in the book: <em>A Guide to the Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK Guide)</em>, Project Management Institute, 2017.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Defining design requirements:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This means developing functional design guidance that leads to the security strategies.</li>\n<li>First, the mission or general objectives of the PPS are formulated.</li>\n<li>They must support the corporate mission, or management will not fund or support them.</li>\n<li>They are validated as each planning task is completed; the mission may be revised.</li>\n<li>In the end, the requirements must accurately reflect the protection objectives.</li>\n<li><strong>Level-of-confidence factor:</strong> add a level of confidence to each requirement; that is why it is better to say <strong>detect</strong> and <strong>delay</strong>, not <strong>prevent</strong>.</li>\n<li>A PPS with absolute objectives (deny all entry, 100% confidence) is impossible or impractically expensive.</li>\n<li>The real danger is not unauthorized entry, but its <strong>consequences</strong>.</li>\n<li>The requirements should focus on preventing, delaying, or modifying those consequences.</li>\n<li>One solution can serve several vulnerabilities. Example: detecting and delaying access also helps against theft of trade secrets.</li>\n<li><strong>Group of assets:</strong> its protection must cover the group’s <strong>most critical</strong> asset.</li>\n<li>Alternative: separate the critical asset and protect it apart, instead of raising protection for the whole group.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Requirements analysis and definition serve to:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ensure that the solutions mitigate real, specific vulnerabilities.</li>\n<li>Justify each solution with cost/benefit.</li>\n<li>Identify all the elements (technology, personnel, and procedures) and resources of each solution.</li>\n<li>Provide a basis for a precise and complete specification for purchase and implementation.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in this part the book writes “PSS” once; it is a typo for PPS.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 14.2. Requirements Analysis (example of a requirements analysis):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Case: ground-floor building entrance, with a real estate office and a bank on either side.</li>\n<li>There are two entrances (north and south) and one exit door to the east.</li>\n<li>The real estate office and the bank receive staff and visitors: facilities must be demarcated upon entering.</li>\n<li>Security is essential at that level: the elevator lobby and the day care are vulnerable to outside visitors.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attribute</th>\n<th>Door 1 / First Floor</th>\n<th>Door 2 / First Floor</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Use</td>\n<td>Access to the terrace from the office</td>\n<td>Secondary door to the day care</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assets</td>\n<td>Personnel files of the human resources (HR) office</td>\n<td>Day care equipment · children’s records · the children</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Criticality</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Threats</td>\n<td>Break-in · arson · theft of records · theft of equipment · door left open</td>\n<td>Break-in · theft of records · theft of equipment · door left open · child abduction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vulnerabilities</td>\n<td>Fire enveloping the entire floor · compromised HR information</td>\n<td>Day care security and care are compromised</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk</td>\n<td>Injury to staff · loss of records · interruption of work processes · psychological harm</td>\n<td>Injury to staff and children · loss of records · work interruption · psychological harm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Note:</strong> table reconstructed from an encoded font and by position; verify against the figure. Some of the book’s “threats” (door left open) are strictly vulnerabilities.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-14-e2\">14.3 Design Phases</h3><h4>14.3.1 Development of Design Criteria</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Design criteria:</strong> basic rules and guidelines for the design; additional requirements that the design considers alongside the risks.</li>\n<li>All stakeholders give operational review in this phase: security manager, procurement, IT, and facilities.</li>\n<li>This brings in the human factor: decision making, common sense, and awareness.</li>\n<li>Categories: expected performance; operational and financial considerations; style, design, codes, and standards.</li>\n<li>Not all measures are possible or practical; other criteria set constraints on design, implementation, and operation.</li>\n<li>The detail goes in the design specifications and in the construction or contract documents.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Key idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Codes and Standards</td>\n<td>Comply with building, fire, and life safety codes, laws, and internal standards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quality</td>\n<td>Balance quality and cost, with uniform quality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacity</td>\n<td>Estimate capacity early and leave 10–15% in reserve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Performance</td>\n<td>Overall performance parameters in the basis of design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Features</td>\n<td>Prefer catalog (off the shelf) features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost</td>\n<td>Design fees and construction cost; budget as an initial criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operations</td>\n<td>Minimum impact on operations and natural use of the system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Culture and Image</td>\n<td>Reflect corporate culture and image</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitoring and Response</td>\n<td>Security operations center and response personnel</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Codes and Standards:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>National and local building, fire, and life safety codes must be followed. They are identified and applied from the initial design.</li>\n<li>Laws may apply, such as those on licensing and training of security officers.</li>\n<li>The organization’s own standards: work rules, insurance coverage, acceptable color schemes, and competitive bidding rules.</li>\n<li>Some organizations have security standards or guidelines for design and construction.</li>\n<li>Certain life safety codes greatly affect the selection, configuration, and operation of door control components.</li>\n<li>Failing to comply can lead to rejection of the design in the construction phase and to costly changes.</li>\n<li>Typical case: controls at points along the building’s established egress route that violate the code and must be eliminated.</li>\n<li>Another case: locks such as electric strike, electromagnetic, and vertical pin locks, their use on certain door types, and the required hardware configuration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Quality:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Know the quality and performance differences between components.</li>\n<li>Good design = a balance between component quality and total cost.</li>\n<li>Quality must be <strong>uniform</strong>. Typical errors:<ul>\n<li>A high-quality lock on a hollow wooden door.</li>\n<li>A metal door surrounded by a plain drywall wall.</li>\n<li>An alarm with sensors and a control unit, but no alarm communication and display system.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The alarm must have <strong>tamper protection</strong>: an alarm signal if the system is compromised.</li>\n<li>The designer shows options so that management understands the cost drivers and relative performance.</li>\n<li>The trade-offs between cost and quality must be documented.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Capacity:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Capacity, size, and space largely determine the solution.</li>\n<li>Examples: number of cardholders, monitored alarm zones, and doors with access control.</li>\n<li>It can change during design, but an early estimate reduces iterations.</li>\n<li>Nothing complicates a design more than revising capacity midway through the process.</li>\n<li>Expansion is planned from the start: typically <strong>10 to 15% reserve capacity</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Performance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The performance of each component goes in the performance or project specification.</li>\n<li>Overall parameters also go as criteria in the basis of design, especially if interacting with existing systems.</li>\n<li>Examples from the book:<ul>\n<li>Entry control must connect to the existing local area network.</li>\n<li>It must handle verification errors and personnel flow at shift changes.</li>\n<li>Turnstiles with reader: minimum <strong>500 cardholders per hour</strong> and building evacuation in <strong>10 minutes</strong>.</li>\n<li>Reliability and maintainability: turnstile with a <strong>mean time between failures (MTBF) of 2,000 hours</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Features:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are defined in summary in the basis of design and in detail in the performance specification.</li>\n<li>Example: optical turnstiles in the lobby of a tower, based on flow and evacuation.</li>\n<li>Throughput dictates the number of lanes, and lobbies allow few lanes.</li>\n<li>Uncommon features: less competition in the purchase and rising costs.</li>\n<li>Custom features: complicate interfaces, lengthen purchase and implementation, and make maintenance difficult.</li>\n<li>Know what is available off the shelf and do a reality check with several manufacturers before closing the list.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cost:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two major cost drivers: <strong>design fees</strong> and <strong>projected construction cost</strong>.</li>\n<li>Some owners ask installer/integrators to design, to save fees.</li>\n<li>Others hire a knowledgeable consulting engineer.</li>\n<li>Strategically, it is best for an expert person to lead the integrated design; it reduces construction, personnel, and procedure costs.</li>\n<li>Reasonable security, especially integrated (entry control, intrusion detection, and video), usually surprises with its cost.</li>\n<li>A well-documented, <strong>quantitative</strong> risk analysis makes it easier to justify more funds.</li>\n<li>The budget is often a mandatory design goal: it goes among the initial criteria.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Operations:</strong> two main criteria.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Minimum negative impact</strong> on productivity and operations.\n   - Restricted access in high-traffic production areas can affect operations.\n   - Consult operations managers early to find alternatives, such as a new production layout.</li>\n<li><strong>Natural use</strong> of the systems by security operations.\n   - The system must adapt to shift changes and normal patrol operations.\n   - It must allow scheduling of activations and concentrate alarms, video surveillance, assessment, and communications in one central place.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Culture and Image:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Corporate culture distinguishes each organization and determines how security is defined and implemented.</li>\n<li>Procedures and training must maximize acceptance of change.</li>\n<li><strong>Image:</strong> the perception of the organization from outside.</li>\n<li>It is shaped by customer service, promotional activities, and exterior and interior design.</li>\n<li>To support corporate goals, the program must reflect the corporate image.</li>\n<li>Whether high or low profile, it takes care of the aesthetics of what is visible: uniforms and security equipment.</li>\n<li>What is not covered by corporate standards is listed here.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Monitoring and Response:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Essential component: a central <strong>security operations center (SOC)</strong> and personnel assigned to monitor and respond.</li>\n<li>It gains importance with <strong>business continuity</strong>: after an event, the center assesses the damage, the next step in continuity.</li>\n<li>That helps management set critical functions and complete a <strong>business impact analysis</strong> (human, financial, and image costs).</li>\n<li>With global integrated systems, effective monitoring is even more important.</li>\n<li>If the budget limits the SOC or personnel: minimize monitoring or staffing requirements, or provide for outside monitoring.</li>\n<li>In enterprise systems, third-party monitoring calls the enterprise investment itself into question.</li>\n<li>Remote or single sites: alarms to a <strong>commercial central alarm station</strong> or to a proprietary on-site system.</li>\n<li>Enterprise video is monitored remotely, assesses local alarms, and retrieves archived scenes over the network.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of organization</th>\n<th>Preferred monitoring and response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Rudimentary systems, small or medium facilities</td>\n<td>Central station</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complex systems, large organizations</td>\n<td>Proprietary on-site personnel, in a well-designed and equipped SOC (preferred method)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mixed</td>\n<td>On-site personnel during working hours and remote monitoring after hours</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>On site, personnel training must match the complexity of the systems.</li>\n<li><strong>High risk:</strong> use a <strong>secondary</strong> alarm communication route and method, in case the primary one fails or is compromised.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.3.2 Basis of Design</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Once the requirements definition is complete, the designer prepares the basis of design and hands it to the project team.</li>\n<li><strong>Contents of the basis of design:</strong><ol>\n<li>Initial designation of critical assets.</li>\n<li>General objectives of the asset protection program.</li>\n<li>Results of the risk analysis.</li>\n<li>Functional requirements the design must meet.</li>\n<li>Narrative description of the operation of the proposed systems, personnel, and procedures that make up the PPS or program.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>It serves to achieve team <strong>consensus</strong> on goals, what the project includes, and how it will protect the assets.</li>\n<li>Every member of the design team must contribute.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not the time for:</strong> engineering details, budgets, or debating specific countermeasures.</li>\n<li><strong>It is the time for:</strong> conceiving the project, deriving requirements from a rigorous risk assessment, and functionally describing the subsystems.</li>\n<li>The initial site surveys can also be done: existing conditions and measures, and improvement needs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.3.3 Conceptual Design</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Also called <strong>design concept</strong> or <strong>schematic phase</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is the <strong>last task</strong> of planning and assessment; it is born as a product of the risk assessment.</li>\n<li>The designer formulates a complete solution: protection strategies grouped into a new program or reinforcing an existing one.</li>\n<li>It includes security systems complemented with procedures and personnel.</li>\n<li>At this stage it is expressed in narrative and general terms, with an <strong>initial budget estimate</strong> for design and construction.</li>\n<li>It incorporates the basis of design and documents findings, conclusions, and recommendations from the initial surveys.</li>\n<li>It is the first opportunity to document the design. In architecture: initial conceptual design or schematic phase.</li>\n<li>The designer collaborates with the owner on an integrated, holistic approach.</li>\n<li><strong>Concentric rings</strong> around the assets: it becomes harder and harder to reach the target and escape undetected.</li>\n<li><strong>Protection-in-depth</strong> or redundant schemes place barriers and delays on the intruder’s route so there is time to respond.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Level of detail of the concept:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is debate; normally it carries the elements above plus some initial detail.</li>\n<li>Never more than a high-level description of PPS elements, subsystems, and support systems.</li>\n<li>Subsystems and the interaction between them: described in narrative form.</li>\n<li>Representative details, for example: an entry door with access control or an emergency exit.</li>\n<li>General block diagrams: systems, subsystems, and connectivity at the element level, with construction cost estimates.</li>\n<li>For architecture: mark floor plans with devices, control points, and connectivity, to size interfaces (power).</li>\n<li>Architects demand a great deal of detail early.</li>\n<li><strong>Approval:</strong> the conceptual level is the ideal time to ask for management approval. There is already consensus on scope and detail for an initial budget.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Selection of countermeasures:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are chosen by the asset owners, guided by security professionals, according to their <strong>cost-effectiveness</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cost-effectiveness criteria from the book:<ul>\n<li>Operational constraints.</li>\n<li>Susceptibility to nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>Installation cost.</li>\n<li>Maintenance costs.</li>\n<li>Probability of detection.</li>\n<li>MTBF.</li>\n<li>Contribution to reducing manpower.</li>\n<li>Contribution to the asset’s vulnerability.</li>\n<li>Risk reduction, normally expressed as the monetary consequence of loss or destruction.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>Main strategies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Security designers (4)</td>\n<td>Prevention · detection · control · intervention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Homeland security (5)</td>\n<td>Preparation · prevention · detection · response · recovery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Figure 14.3. Countermeasures Development Table (floor: basement):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Door</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Assets</th>\n<th>Criticality</th>\n<th>Countermeasures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Loading dock</td>\n<td>Incoming materials</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Surveillance · access control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Shipping/receiving/storage</td>\n<td>Stock and products</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Access control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3 &amp; 4</td>\n<td>Door to the corridor</td>\n<td>General equipment</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Intercom · access control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5 &amp; 6</td>\n<td>Shipping/receiving office</td>\n<td>General equipment · stock</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Duress · door release · monitor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Note:</strong> the figure spells “Survillance” (typo for surveillance).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.3.4 Design and Documentation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In the building design industry it can be divided into two phases: <strong>design development (DD)</strong> and <strong>construction documents (CD)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Or a single phase called CDs, and the end of design development is called 30, 35, or 50%.</li>\n<li>The percentages indicate the level of progress of the final construction documents.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Percent complete</th>\n<th>What it includes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Conceptual or schematic</td>\n<td>No percentage in the book</td>\n<td>Precedes design development</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design development: preliminary design</td>\n<td>30–35%</td>\n<td>Follows the conceptual design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design development: close</td>\n<td>50–60%</td>\n<td>Ends the DD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction documents: start</td>\n<td>60%</td>\n<td>CDs begin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction documents: interim submission</td>\n<td>90%</td>\n<td>90% submission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Construction documents: final set</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n<td>Complete set of CDs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>With an architect:</strong> the submissions coincide with those in the architect–owner contract.</li>\n<li><strong>Without an architect:</strong> they are adapted to the project; there is almost always a conceptual, preliminary, and construction submission, with a design review at each.</li>\n<li><strong>Objective of the phase:</strong> to finish the design and document it to the level of detail that the chosen purchasing method requires.</li>\n<li>Deliverables: specifications, evaluation criteria, and implementation schedule.</li>\n<li>More detail → better responses from bidders and lower project cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contract (or bid) documents: three sections</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Contractual details.</li>\n<li>Construction specifications.</li>\n<li>Construction drawings.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>In the purchase of <strong>services</strong> (for example, security officers) the third section is not needed.</li>\n<li>In <strong>construction</strong> purchases (access control, video), specifications and drawings are called <strong>construction documents</strong>.</li>\n<li>In small projects it is common to put all the written specifications on the drawings themselves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Design development (DD):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It begins when the schematic design is approved.</li>\n<li>It carries the principles into drawings with the location and type of each device on site, in buildings, and on floors.</li>\n<li>It goes with an <strong>outline specification</strong>: from an index of sections, parts, and headings, to common template text.</li>\n<li>The detail of the outline specification is completed in the CD phase.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical DD drawings:</strong> title sheet; list of schedules (spreadsheets) per system (power, heat load, cable types); floor plans with device types and locations; list of physical details and single-line (block) diagrams for the CD package.</li>\n<li><strong>Title sheet:</strong> list of drawings, list of symbols, list of abbreviations, general notes, and other schedules common to all the drawings.</li>\n<li>At DD a <strong>realistic budget</strong> is already possible, because exact quantities and types of devices are fixed.</li>\n<li>DD drawings, outline specifications, and DD budgets go to the project team for approval.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Construction documents (CD):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>After the DD package is approved; the result is practically ready to bid or build.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical CD package:</strong><ul>\n<li>CD drawings.</li>\n<li>Specifications.</li>\n<li>Bill of quantities.</li>\n<li>Refined system budgets.</li>\n<li>Contract terms and conditions (normally from the owner’s purchasing department).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>CD vs. DD:</strong> the level of detail changes.</li>\n<li>A good CD package gives the contractor everything needed to quote accurately and answers every installation question.</li>\n<li>It includes coordination with other trades. Example: who supplies locks and their power supplies (security or hardware), how they are mounted, and how they are integrated into access control.</li>\n<li>\n<p>The same with each trade that touches security: elevators, electrical hardware, lighting, landscaping, signage, and IT (the book leaves the list open).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book calls the end of DD “30, 35, or 50%” and then says DD concludes at 50–60%. And 60% is both the close of DD and the start of the CDs. Memorize the sequence 30–35 → 50–60 → 60 → 90 → 100%.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.3.5 Specifications</h4>\n<p>Specifications reflect and complement the design with enough detail to achieve two things:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>That the final implementation reflects what was intended.</strong>\n   - The specification contains the instructions and performance criteria for building the systems.\n   - It must include functional tests and ongoing scheduled periodic tests, to maintain integrity over time.\n   - Without specifications, drawings and sketches are nearly useless and open to interpretation.\n   - <strong>Drawings = WHAT is built; specification = the owner’s intent and HOW it is built.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>That all bidders understand the same thing.</strong>\n   - Incomplete or erroneous specifications produce very different offers that cannot be compared.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Characteristics and format:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They tend to be lengthy and highly technical; they require a lot of experience in PPS design, procurement, construction, and operation.</li>\n<li>Standard format in <strong>three parts: general, products, and execution</strong>. Each part is divided into subsections and sub-subsections.</li>\n<li>With poor specifications, vendors choose quality and performance for the owner, without the owner knowing until operation.</li>\n<li>There are <strong>boilerplate specifications</strong> as a starting point; experienced designers have their <strong>master specifications</strong>.</li>\n<li>They must incorporate lessons learned. Example: a contractor misread a phrase and reduced functions or raised costs.</li>\n<li>Sections are numbered by trade so they can be issued separately.</li>\n<li>Sources of standard specifications: <strong>American Institute of Architects (AIA)</strong> and <strong>Construction Specifications Institute (CSI)</strong>.</li>\n<li>According to the book, CSI publishes the <strong>MasterFormat</strong> and <strong>MasterSpec</strong> standards; their divisions document an entire project.</li>\n<li><strong>Electronic Safety and Security = Division 28</strong> of the Facility Services Subgroup.</li>\n<li><strong>Note (outside the book):</strong> MasterSpec is actually published by AIA (via Deltek); CSI publishes MasterFormat. For the exam, remember Division 28.</li>\n<li>With integration and purchase from a single contractor, it is common to put all systems into <strong>a single custom section</strong>.</li>\n<li>Most architects and project managers prefer all security in a single specification.</li>\n<li>Not all titles apply; the designer adapts the format.</li>\n<li>The standard format ensures: (1) specifications that are complete in every detail; (2) that contractors easily find every detail when quoting or implementing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>A PPS specification must include, at a minimum:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Instructions to bidders, with the list of all contract documents.</li>\n<li>List of project references.</li>\n<li>Functional description of the entire system: intended operation in a concept of operations, maintenance and warranty requirements, quality assurance, and installation schedule.</li>\n<li>List of design drawings.</li>\n<li>List and description of the contract’s products and services.</li>\n<li>Products and services included in other contracts. Example: electrical hardware supplied under the hardware contract but connected to the PPS by the security contractor.</li>\n<li>List of applicable codes and standards.</li>\n<li>Support services: submittals of drawings, samples, and documentation, commissioning, testing, training, warranty, maintenance, and spare parts.</li>\n<li>Technical description of subsystems and components: capacity, functional capability, expandability, performance and operating parameters, environmental parameters, installation and integration details, appearance and finish, and acceptable makes and models.</li>\n<li>General site conditions, installation standards, exact particulars, materials, dimensions, and quality control standards.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>14.3.6 Drawings</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Together with the specifications, the drawings are the basis of every implementation.</li>\n<li>A drawing of the design intent is less likely to be misinterpreted.</li>\n<li><strong>Key rule:</strong> in case of discrepancies, <strong>the specifications take precedence over the drawings</strong>.</li>\n<li>Most are made with <strong>computer-aided design drafting (CADD)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Advantages of CADD vs. manual drafting:</strong> clearer files; changes are faster and cheaper; they are easier to share.</li>\n<li>If the designer draws directly in CADD: there are no transcription errors and no need to train another person in the requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>Types of security drawings:</strong> plans, elevations, details, risers, and hardware schedules.</li>\n<li>Each drawing is a site plan or a floor plan with the devices by type and location (Figure 14.4, Typical Floor Plan, Tim Sutton/Guidepost Solutions).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it shows</th>\n<th>Key data</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Plans</td>\n<td>Top view, map-like, of a site, a floor, or part of one</td>\n<td>Several sheets to cover all areas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevations</td>\n<td>Vertical surfaces: heights and locations of wall devices (cameras, readers, motion sensors)</td>\n<td>Background from the architect or the organization’s files (Figure 14.6, Security Door Elevation)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Details</td>\n<td>Elements in greater detail: special mounts, dimensions of custom parts, cable terminations</td>\n<td>Plans and elevations are at small scale, e.g., 1/8 in = 1 ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risers</td>\n<td>Complete subsystems (video, access control) with devices and interconnecting cables</td>\n<td>Master drawing; contractors count devices here (Figure 14.7)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Plans:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Background: fence lines, walls, partitions, doors, furniture, door and room numbers (called <strong>targets or tags</strong>), room names, floor materials, stairs, and fixed equipment.</li>\n<li>The architect usually provides the backgrounds in new construction; in an upgrade, the company may have them.</li>\n<li>Manual drafting: backgrounds on transparent sheets (<strong>reproducible</strong>) such as <strong>paper vellum</strong> or <strong>Mylar</strong>, on which symbols and connections are drawn.</li>\n<li>The background must be sufficient without being cluttered, so the security team does not get lost.</li>\n<li>In CADD the background has <strong>layers</strong>: walls, doors, furniture, lighting; they are turned on or off.</li>\n<li>Security symbols go on their own layer, as predefined blocks.</li>\n<li>If the architect changes the background, the old security layer is overlaid. It is only changed if it affects security (e.g., new or relocated alarmed doors).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Security symbols:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many players have their own sets: companies, magazines, architects, engineers, consultants, and standards bodies.</li>\n<li>The most common for manual drafting: <strong>ASTM International, <em>Standard Practice for Security Engineering Symbols</em></strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>1995:</strong> the International Association of Professional Security Consultants (IAPSC) and the Security Industry Association (SIA) created <strong><em>Architectural Graphics Standard–CAD Symbols for Security System Layout</em></strong>.</li>\n<li>Those symbols were incorporated into the ASTM standard.</li>\n<li>The specification must require the <strong>same set of symbols</strong> on the drawings the contractor delivers.</li>\n<li>Figure 14.5 (Typical Drawing Device Symbology): device symbology and numbering scheme to reference them in the schedules.</li>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> the symbols in Figure 14.5 are not legible in the extracted text.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Details and risers, what to know:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Details are usually made for each project, but can be reused from previous projects.</li>\n<li>Risers: on a campus, each building is a block; in a tower, each floor in vertical elevation-style format.</li>\n<li>In small projects, all risers fit on a single sheet.</li>\n<li>Since contractors quote from the riser counts, it must be <strong>exact and complete</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Note:</strong> the book numbers a figure as “Figure 14-5” (with a hyphen), a minor typo.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.3.7 Hardware Schedules</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hardware schedules:</strong> tables of related security devices.</li>\n<li>They give detailed information that is hard to show on drawings or in the specification text.</li>\n<li>They are used for repetitive devices: door hardware, control devices, intrusion sensors, cameras, monitors, and termination panels.</li>\n<li>They are usually on the security drawings, but can be attached to the specification.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 14.8. Sample Door Schedule (example, all in the basement):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Room/Location</th>\n<th>Door</th>\n<th>Access</th>\n<th>Electric lock</th>\n<th>Other hardware</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>B01</td>\n<td>Loading dock</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Intercom</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B02</td>\n<td>Shipping/receiving/storage</td>\n<td>DBL (double)</td>\n<td>Card reader</td>\n<td>Strike</td>\n<td>2 door contacts (recessed) · request to exit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B03</td>\n<td>Shipping/receiving office</td>\n<td>SGL (single)</td>\n<td>Card reader</td>\n<td>Strike</td>\n<td>2 door contacts (recessed) · request to exit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B04</td>\n<td>Shipping/receiving office</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Intercom</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B05</td>\n<td>Shipping/receiving office</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Monitor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B06</td>\n<td>Shipping/receiving office</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Telephone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B07</td>\n<td>Shipping/receiving office</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Duress</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Figure 14.9. Sample Camera Schedule:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Camera</th>\n<th>Location/View</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Lens</th>\n<th>Housing</th>\n<th>Mount</th>\n<th>Height</th>\n<th>Alarm call-up</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>B1</td>\n<td>Loading dock and area toward shipping/receiving</td>\n<td>PTZ (pan/tilt/zoom)</td>\n<td>10:01</td>\n<td>EnviroDOME</td>\n<td>Wall</td>\n<td>12 ft</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C01</td>\n<td>Ground-floor lobby; includes the corridor to the east exit</td>\n<td>PTZ</td>\n<td>3.2 mm–<strong>Pending</strong> mm varifocal</td>\n<td>DOME</td>\n<td>Ceiling</td>\n<td>Ceiling</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C02</td>\n<td>Third-floor lobby</td>\n<td>Fixed dome</td>\n<td>3.2 mm–<strong>Pending</strong> mm varifocal</td>\n<td>DOME</td>\n<td>Ceiling</td>\n<td>Ceiling</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Note:</strong> Figures 14.8 and 14.9 come in an encoded font; I reconstructed them by position. The upper limit of the varifocal lens is not legible.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.3.8 Design Coordination</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Many disciplines affect the security design.</li>\n<li>Coordination prevents <strong>missing elements</strong> or <strong>buying twice</strong>.</li>\n<li>Part of the system is described and purchased in sections of other disciplines.</li>\n<li>Example: in new construction, all electrical power (including security power) goes in a single specification and is installed by an electrical contractor.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Discipline</th>\n<th>What is coordinated</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Architect</td>\n<td>Space (console, locker rooms, riser closets, equipment rooms); the earlier, the more likely to obtain it. Specifies the door hardware, looks after appearance and finishes, and ensures door cutouts for hardware are made at the factory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electrical engineer</td>\n<td>Power at every point with security equipment; amperage of dedicated circuits; electrical backup (generator or UPS, uninterruptible power supply). Back boxes, junction boxes, and conduit are usually in its section and installed by the electrical contractor. Fire alarm interface to cut power to <strong>fail-safe</strong> locks, as the code requires</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mechanical engineer</td>\n<td>HVAC (heating, ventilation and air conditioning) of security spaces: heat loads and duration of occupancy (e.g., control room 24 h). If there is conduit above the ceiling not designed by the electrical engineer, coordinate it with the ducts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertical transportation designer</td>\n<td>Equipment in elevators, inside or outside the car. Location and mounting of cameras in the car, critical and dependent on car design. Traveling cable, power on the car roof, interfaces in the machine room. Also escalators</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The vertical transportation designer may be on the staff of the architect, of the mechanical or electrical engineer, or a specialist consultant.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-14-e3\">14.4 Contracting</h3><ul>\n<li>Section of the contract documents that describes the form of contract when selecting a vendor.</li>\n<li><strong>Covers:</strong> insurance and bonding requirements; site rules; labor rules (union or non-union, wage rates); delivery and payment; how to measure progress for partial payments; owner’s remedies in case of default; termination conditions; unit prices for additions and deletions; instructions to bidders (the book leaves the list open).</li>\n<li><strong>Large project:</strong> drafted by the architect or the owner’s construction manager, for all trades.</li>\n<li><strong>Small project:</strong> may be drafted by the company’s purchasing department.</li>\n<li>It is included in the contract documents and adjusted as the project advances.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.4.1 Initial Budget</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Another product of the planning phase: a <strong>capital</strong> budget and a <strong>recurring costs</strong> budget.</li>\n<li>Without detailed design or firm quantities, it is at most a <strong>conceptual order-of-magnitude</strong> estimate.</li>\n<li>Experienced designers can come out <strong>within 10%</strong> of the final bid price.</li>\n<li>Most need vendors, manufacturers, or contractors to obtain the <strong>MSRP (manufacturer’s suggested retail price)</strong> of equipment, installation, software, and support.</li>\n<li>An independent security consultant may be needed.</li>\n<li>Danger of a bad initial budget: repeating a long, difficult approval process.</li>\n<li>If one vendor will supply almost everything, its experience helps the initial estimate.</li>\n<li>Even if conceptual, its accuracy matters: in general, <strong>within 15 to 20%</strong> of the final bid price.</li>\n<li><strong>Too low:</strong> when the real cost becomes known, the project may be canceled or run out of funds for a complete PPS.</li>\n<li><strong>Too high:</strong> the budget may not be justified and may not be approved.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.4.2 Estimation Considerations</h4>\n<p><strong>Capital projects:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All equipment and support systems and their installation, primary and backup systems: software, components, mounting hardware, sensors, termination panels, control panels, back boxes, junction boxes, conduit, cable, backup batteries, UPS, and main power circuits.</li>\n<li>Freight and taxes (the book leaves the list open).</li>\n<li>Project management and supervision labor.</li>\n<li>Shop drawing submittals.</li>\n<li>Testing.</li>\n<li>Commissioning.</li>\n<li>Training of operators and users.</li>\n<li><strong>As-built</strong> (record) drawings at project close.</li>\n<li>Warranty.</li>\n<li>Design fees.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Service projects and recurring costs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Payroll of security personnel: supervision, benefits, holidays, vacations, and sick leave.</li>\n<li>Uniforms and equipment.</li>\n<li>Training.</li>\n<li>Maintenance, repair, and replacement of equipment.</li>\n<li>Consumables: paper, ink cartridges and toner, backup media.</li>\n<li>Replacement of access cards and badges, and review of procedures.</li>\n<li>Central alarm station monitoring and response.</li>\n<li>Early phases: a single lump sum per subsystem and in total, often narrative.</li>\n<li>Later estimates: more detailed (Figure 14.10).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.4.3 Types of Cost Estimates</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>When</th>\n<th>Contingency</th>\n<th>Prepared with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Budgetary estimate</td>\n<td>Initial planning; getting the system into the budget cycle, sometimes <strong>up to 5 years</strong> ahead</td>\n<td><strong>±10 to 20%</strong></td>\n<td>Costs from companies that installed recently, vendor estimates, <strong>RSMeans</strong> data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preliminary design estimate</td>\n<td>If part of a larger construction project; at the <strong>50%</strong> design review</td>\n<td><strong>±10%</strong></td>\n<td>Draft specifications, drawings, and schedules; vendor estimates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Final design estimate</td>\n<td>At <strong>100%</strong> design</td>\n<td>Minimal, <strong>±5%</strong></td>\n<td>Finished documents, drawings, and schedules</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The budgetary estimate is difficult without doing a good part of the design.</li>\n<li><strong>RSMeans:</strong> a company that provides construction cost information.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> do not confuse the 15–20% accuracy of the initial budget (14.4.1) with the ±10–20% contingency of the budgetary estimate (14.4.3). And 10% is what experienced designers achieve.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 14.10. Sample Cost Estimate Format (“Facility Security System Cost Estimate”):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Columns:</strong> manufacturer · model · unit cost · quantity · base price · installation time (hours) · labor · labor cost · installed cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Head end:</strong> PC workstations (computer/monitor/keyboard) · application software–network · alarm/access printer · network hub.</li>\n<li><strong>Access control:</strong> access control panel · alarm input/output board · signal multiplexer · enclosure · panel power supply · lock power supply · proximity cards–thin · card reader–proximity (surface mount) · network connection · modem/telephone connection · terminal server.</li>\n<li><strong>Photo ID equipment:</strong> photo identification system PC, software, camera, frame grabber, and printer/laminator.</li>\n<li><strong>Closing lines:</strong> hourly labor rate · total parts and labor · estimated sales tax · estimated freight · estimated permits · total estimated cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Pending:</strong> the numerical values and makes in the example come in an encoded font and are not legible.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.4.4 Life-Cycle Cost</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The real cost of a security system is its <strong>life-cycle cost</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>What it includes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Engineering and design costs</td>\n<td>Planning and design: choosing products for the specified functions; location drawings, subsystem connections, and wiring details</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hardware</td>\n<td>Original equipment plus startup spare parts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Software</td>\n<td>Operating system and application software to run the system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Installation costs</td>\n<td>Installation labor; inspection, testing, and commissioning; equipment rental; permits; bonds; supervision; overhead. <strong>It is the largest percentage of the system</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operating costs</td>\n<td>Personnel, energy consumption, and consumables (paper, cartridges)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maintenance costs</td>\n<td>Labor and parts for preventive and corrective maintenance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other costs</td>\n<td>State and local taxes · <strong>profit 10%</strong> · <strong>performance bonding 3–5%</strong> · <strong>contingency 5–10%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjustments</td>\n<td>RSMeans data are national averages; adjust for each locality</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>14.4.5 Sample Estimate</h4>\n<p><strong>Sample system (book text):</strong> 2 perimeter revolving doors · 10 single-leaf interior doors · 12 fixed cameras · 1 pan/tilt/zoom camera in the ceiling · 2 video monitors · 1 digital video recorder (DVR) · 1 computer monitor for access control.</p>\n<p><strong>Figure 14.11. Sample Estimate:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Qty</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n<th>Unit cost</th>\n<th>Extension</th>\n<th>Labor</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Revolving door</td>\n<td>$30,000</td>\n<td>$30,000</td>\n<td>$15,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>8 card reader control panel</td>\n<td>$1,000</td>\n<td>$2,000</td>\n<td>$1,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Access control software</td>\n<td>$1,000</td>\n<td>$1,000</td>\n<td>$500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Proximity card reader</td>\n<td>$100</td>\n<td>$1,100</td>\n<td>$1,100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>12V batteries</td>\n<td>$20</td>\n<td>$60</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Request to exit motion detectors</td>\n<td>$75</td>\n<td>$750</td>\n<td>$1,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>24V power supply</td>\n<td>$87</td>\n<td>$87</td>\n<td>$100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>24 VDC electric strikes</td>\n<td>$150</td>\n<td>$1,500</td>\n<td>$2,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Operator training</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>$500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>PTZ CCTV camera, lens, and enclosure</td>\n<td>$2,500</td>\n<td>$2,500</td>\n<td>$2,500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Fixed CCTV camera, varifocal lens, and enclosure</td>\n<td>$700</td>\n<td>$8,400</td>\n<td>$8,400</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>Subtotals</strong></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>$47,397</strong></td>\n<td><strong>$32,100</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Summary of costs</th>\n<th>Amount</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Components</td>\n<td>$47,397</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tax &amp; shipping (12%)</td>\n<td>$5,688</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Installation labor ($100 per hour)</td>\n<td>$32,100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subtotal</td>\n<td>$85,185</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Profit (10%)</td>\n<td>$8,518</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subtotal</td>\n<td>$93,703</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Performance bond (4%)</td>\n<td>$3,748</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total cost estimate</td>\n<td>$97,451</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\"><span class=\"mopen delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.12</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mclose delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\">]</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.04</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Total</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\"><span class=\"mopen delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\">[</span><span class=\"mord\">47</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">397</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">32</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mclose delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\">]</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.04</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">97</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">451</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li>C = cost of components; L = installation labor. The 12% tax and shipping applies only to components.</li>\n<li>Profit is calculated on the subtotal; the bond, on the subtotal with profit.</li>\n<li>$32,100 at $100 per hour equals 321 installation hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Note:</strong> the total comes in an encoded font; $97,451 is partially legible and matches the sum.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text says 2 revolving doors, but the figure prices 1. It also does not price the 2 monitors, the DVR, or the access control monitor.</li>\n<li><strong>Note:</strong> the example uses a 4% bond and 10% profit, within the ranges in 14.4.4, but does not include contingency.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-14-e4\">14.5 The Role of Consultants in Systems Design</h3><ul>\n<li>Key: knowing <strong>whether and when</strong> it makes sense to use a qualified security consultant.</li>\n<li>Many projects are done solely between owner and contractor, as <strong>turnkey projects</strong>.</li>\n<li>Some owners believe the consultant is a waste of time and money.</li>\n<li>There is good evidence that a good consultant saves a lot of money over the life of the system.</li>\n<li>The consultant builds the long-term strategic needs into the concept: capacity growth and flexibility in the face of operational changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Contractor</th>\n<th>Consultant</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Role</td>\n<td>Deliver products or services on time and cost-efficiently, with what is in its distribution chain</td>\n<td>Advise in the client’s best interest, with no other influence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Focus</td>\n<td>The current project and the needs the owner describes</td>\n<td>The long-term vision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Final product</td>\n<td>System installed and commissioned according to what the client described</td>\n<td>Biddable documents, the basis of the contractor’s work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who understands the environment</td>\n<td>The organization itself must describe it; with the contractor it is addressed only superficially</td>\n<td>Designs for the unique environment: security, operational, and physical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic planning</td>\n<td>Rarely participates; strategic documentation is scarce or nonexistent</td>\n<td>Considers it from the concept</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk analysis</td>\n<td>Very rarely is the turnkey project based on one done with the contractor; it is extremely rare for its staff to be able to do it</td>\n<td>Usually has training and certifications in risk analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independence</td>\n<td>Tied to its products</td>\n<td>No interests, influences, or biases toward manufacturers, contractors, or vendors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>What a consultant’s design covers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insists that every design (and the entire security program) be based on an <strong>updated risk analysis</strong>.</li>\n<li>Considers: cultural aspects; security, physical, operational, IT, and architectural environment; and budget constraints.</li>\n<li>The construction documents show only what bidders need to quote and build.</li>\n<li>But they are assembled within the much broader picture that the consultant handles.</li>\n<li>A good consultant optimizes capital and operations; can save <strong>many times their fees</strong>, sometimes already during construction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Case (Fortune 500 company):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The contractor proposed <strong>85 doors with readers</strong> in four corridors next to the main lobby, so visitors would not enter employee areas on their own.</li>\n<li>The building hardly received visitors; the traffic was mostly employees.</li>\n<li>The consultant changed the scope: automatic, aesthetic electronic doors in each corridor, where previously there were no doors.</li>\n<li>Each door with its readers, which recorded each person’s card on entering or leaving.</li>\n<li><strong>Total savings: more than $100,000.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lesson:</strong> the contractor solved within the existing conditions; the consultant <strong>modified the environment</strong> to solve the problem.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-14-e5\">14.6 Video Systems</h3><ul>\n<li>Security video is living through <strong>two evolutions</strong>: from analog to digital and from standard resolution to megapixel.</li>\n<li>Both continue to affect legacy or hybrid installations, and how video is recorded, stored, and used.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.6.1 The First Evolution: Analog to Digital</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It began in the early 2000s and is still ongoing.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Analog</th>\n<th>Digital</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost</td>\n<td>Cheap cameras and infrastructure</td>\n<td>Cameras and recorders can cost more</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Implementation</td>\n<td>Simple; requires no networking knowledge</td>\n<td>Requires a network</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hacking</td>\n<td>Difficult to hack</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Architecture</td>\n<td>Rigid</td>\n<td>Very flexible; covers almost any configuration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Recording options that digital allows:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Local, on the camera itself.</li>\n<li>Local per group of cameras (e.g., all those on the third floor).</li>\n<li>Centralized.</li>\n<li>A combination of centralized and local.</li>\n<li>Local or remote fail-over (backup): the camera temporarily stores data if the network goes down.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Phased migration (when requirements grow: backup, multi-building campus):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Remove the multiplexer and the VCR (video cassette recorder) and put an NVR (network video recorder) on a digital network.</li>\n<li>Convert the analog signal to digital with an encoder (or codec) connected to the network.</li>\n<li>Gradually replace analog cameras with digital ones.</li>\n<li>Reuse the coaxial cable with <strong>IP over coax</strong> converters (IP = Internet Protocol): the coax works as a network cable.</li>\n<li>In new installations, run network cable.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Advantage: the transition follows the organization’s own calendar, <strong>without losing the original capital investment</strong>.</li>\n<li>It can be done at the pace at which components fail, as a natural renewal of obsolete equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the analog image was poor quality:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tube cameras: 100% analog circuits.</li>\n<li>With solid-state imagers, the image was born digital and the camera converted it to analog output.</li>\n<li>Chain with multiplexers: digital (sensor) → analog (camera) → analog coax → digital (multiplexer) → analog tape → analog monitor.</li>\n<li>So many conversions degraded the image. The natural evolution was a fully digital camera, processing, and display.</li>\n<li>Analog protocols according to each country’s television: <strong>PAL, SECAM, or NTSC</strong>. Quality almost equal among them and low compared with today’s digital.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Four changes needed to go from analog to digital:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Convert the analog video technology to digital.</li>\n<li>Transmit over a TCP/IP (Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol) network instead of coax.</li>\n<li>Compress the files so they fit in the network bandwidth.</li>\n<li>Compress the files so they fit in the limited storage available.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Compression:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The first digital cameras used the same sensors as analog ones: low-quality images.</li>\n<li>Without compression, each frame would be a huge raw file.</li>\n<li>That would saturate the networks of the time, often <strong>100 Mbit/s for the entire network</strong>, and storage budgets.</li>\n<li>The industry adopted two algorithms: <strong>MPEG-4 and H.264</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Codec</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Advantage</th>\n<th>Disadvantage or use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>MPEG-4</td>\n<td>Based on the JPEG standard. Stores a complete JPEG frame and, for a fixed number of frames, only the differences. When there are enough changes, a new complete frame</td>\n<td>Subsequent frames are very small</td>\n<td>A rendering engine (software or hardware) must reassemble the images to view them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>H.264</td>\n<td>Same approach, even more efficient</td>\n<td>More efficient in transmission and storage</td>\n<td>Less efficient when rendering: requires more processing power</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>H.265</td>\n<td>Successor used when moving to megapixel</td>\n<td>Absorbs the greater data flow and bandwidth</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says the industry adopted \"two\" algorithms (MPEG-4 and H.264), but then adds H.265 for megapixel. Remember all three.</li>\n<li>(outside the book) The complete frame is called an I-frame; the difference frames, P-frames or B-frames.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.6.2 The Second Evolution: Standard Resolution to Megapixel</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A natural step after digital video: from consumer standard resolution (sub-megapixel) to megapixel or HD (high definition).</li>\n<li>Megapixel cameras give much more resolution than standard ones.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>New megapixel capabilities</th>\n<th>Infrastructure requirements (network and storage)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Digital zoom to see detail that does not exist in a standard image</td>\n<td>Megapixel-quality lenses: a standard lens on a megapixel camera is useless</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Show several portions of the same image on different monitors</td>\n<td>Greater network capacity: <strong>1 Gbps or more</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PTZ (pan/tilt/zoom) on recorded video, <strong>\"PTZ into history\"</strong>: see detail from minutes, hours, days, weeks, or months ago</td>\n<td>Much more storage capacity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>They are usually <strong>only a small part</strong> of the camera system.</li>\n<li>They are installed where their special capabilities are most needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.6.3 Processed Video, Video Analytics, and Intelligent Video</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Functions that digital video makes possible and that were hardly feasible in analog.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Processed video</strong></td>\n<td>Any technology that processes the image to improve its clarity or to assess it in some way</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Video analytics</strong></td>\n<td>Software or hardware technology that evaluates the video stream to detect undesired, inappropriate, or concerning behavior</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Image improvement processes (video image enhancement):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anti-glare.</li>\n<li>Anti-backlight.</li>\n<li>Contrast modification, of the whole image or a part.</li>\n<li>Anti-mist or anti-fog.</li>\n<li>Other clarity improvements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two types of video analytics:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Examples or features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Preprogrammed</strong></td>\n<td>Defined algorithm that looks for very specific behaviors</td>\n<td>Walking the wrong way (entering through the exit of an airport checkpoint, crossing onto the subway tracks). Leaving a package. Loitering on a platform. Floating face down in a pool. Increasingly cheap and capable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Learning algorithm</strong></td>\n<td>Observes the scene for a while and learns what is normal (<strong>baseline</strong>). Then alerts on what is not normal</td>\n<td>Very powerful: looks for anything out of the ordinary, not a predetermined behavior</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>What the baseline can include:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Traffic patterns.</li>\n<li>Weather patterns.</li>\n<li>Day and night patterns.</li>\n<li>Normal types of behavior.</li>\n<li>Normal pedestrian speed.</li>\n<li>Direction of traffic.</li>\n<li>Normal movement of trees, water, fog, and the like.</li>\n<li>Normally occupied areas (pedestrians who reach the railing of a bridge, but do not climb it).</li>\n<li>Normal image patterns (briefcases yes, AK-47 rifles no; luggage on the belt, children no).</li>\n<li>Example: someone jumps a fence with a backpack, leaves it next to a trash can, and flees → <strong>alarm</strong>.</li>\n<li>A pedestrian who enters through a vehicle exit → might generate only an <strong>alert</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the title includes \"intelligent video\", but the book does not define it separately.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Origin of the VMS (video management system):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Some consultants expected alarm and access control manufacturers to add video management.</li>\n<li>It was the logical extension of their PLC (programmable logic controller) architecture. It did not happen: they did not see the potential or the market.</li>\n<li>Video manufacturers did see it. They created software with a GUI (graphical user interface) that took advantage of the CPU of their equipment.</li>\n<li>That wave of GUIs came to be called VMS.</li>\n<li>Later, VMSs added partial or full integration with large alarm and access control systems, and then with security intercoms.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.6.4 Video Systems Integration</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Integrating video with other systems increases productivity and monitoring effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>ONVIF</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>(outside the book) ONVIF = Open Network Video Interface Forum.</li>\n<li>Founded in <strong>late 2008</strong> by <strong>Axis Communications, Bosch Security Systems, and Sony Corporation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Goal: open, standardized interfaces for the interoperability of IP-based physical security products.</li>\n<li>More than <strong>18,000 products</strong> conformant with profiles. This outnumbers proprietary equipment, which works only with other equipment from the same manufacturer.</li>\n<li>To be conformant, a product must meet <strong>at least one profile</strong>:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Profile</th>\n<th>Covers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>Broad access control configuration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>Physical access control and event management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>G</td>\n<td>Video recording and storage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q</td>\n<td>Quick installation and \"out of the box\" operation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>Basic video streaming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>Advanced video streaming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D (draft)</td>\n<td>Access control peripherals: locks, credential readers and biometrics, PIN keypads, license plate recognition cameras, door intercoms, sensors, and displays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M (draft)</td>\n<td>Metadata and analytics events for intelligent applications. Makes it easier to integrate IP cameras or analytics with VMS, and with IoT (Internet of Things)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>When the book was written, D and M were candidates under review. For the exam, answer them as drafts.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>PSIM (Physical Security Information Management)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Software platform that integrates applications and systems.</li>\n<li>Collects and correlates events from separate devices and systems.</li>\n<li>Goal: for personnel to identify and resolve situations proactively.</li>\n<li>Typical in a GSOC (global security operations center).</li>\n<li>Provides situational awareness, monitoring, and reporting from almost any input.</li>\n<li>Integrates security and enterprise-wide subsystems: doors, intercoms, cameras, building management, lighting control, telephones, network devices, HVAC (heating, ventilation, and air conditioning), tracking, fire, intrusion, mass notification, social media and deep web monitoring, and license plate and facial recognition databases.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Stages of video integration</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Basic</strong></td>\n<td>Integrates by make and model, or by ONVIF conformance. Typical: show one or several cameras upon an alarm event</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Intermediate</strong> = <strong>unified solution</strong></td>\n<td>The VMS is the single GUI to view and control related systems: access control (open doors and gates), intercom (receive and initiate calls), lighting, and delaying devices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Advanced</strong></td>\n<td>VMS and PSIM do all of the above, plus advanced image processing: image enhancement and disruptive risk detection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>How the integration is wired</h5>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Integration</th>\n<th>Direction of signal</th>\n<th>Steps</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alarms → video</strong></td>\n<td>From the alarm system to the video system</td>\n<td>The video switcher (analog switcher or digital software) is connected to the alarm activation signal. Simplest: a relay from the alarm system to a video alarm input. In matrix switchers there are alarm input modules. In digital: input modules or serial interface, if both have it. It is always necessary to program which cameras appear for each alarm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Access control portals</strong></td>\n<td>The reverse: from video to access control</td>\n<td>An icon of the portal is created in the video GUI. A video output relay module is connected to an access control alarm input. That input is programmed as a request-to-exit sensor that unlocks the door or opens the gate. The icon triggers the function when selected</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Advanced integration varies by device type, make, and model.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Advanced video functions</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>A conventional VMS or PSIM performs much better in surveillance and incident response if the way it is programmed is changed.</li>\n<li>Two methods to make it more useful day to day:</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>1. Geographically related video layouts (camera screens grouped by zone)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Instead of showing a single camera per alarm, each camera is part of a layout of neighboring cameras.</li>\n<li>Example: 5th-floor elevator lobby = 6 cameras on the floor + 3 in the cars.</li>\n<li>A duress alarm at the 5th-floor reception shows them all at once.</li>\n<li>An icon per layout on the GUI map enables the <strong>video guard tour</strong>, floor by floor and area by area.</li>\n<li>Each layout is a \"stop\" on the tour; the operator covers the entire campus quickly.</li>\n<li>They are the step prior to video pursuit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Video pursuit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A VMS or PSIM function for following a subject as if the operator were walking behind him or her.</li>\n<li>It combines the geographic layouts with a flat database of all the cameras and a function that relates them.</li>\n<li>How it works:<ol>\n<li>An alarm opens a tour stop: the primary camera in the center and the egress route cameras around it. It requires one layout per camera callable from an alarm.</li>\n<li>The subject passes into the field of an egress camera. The operator puts the mouse over it and presses the middle button.</li>\n<li>The system looks that camera up in the database and opens its layout, with it in the center and its exits around it.</li>\n<li>By repeating the click, the operator follows the subject from camera to camera.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Each layout is a database record; the focus camera is its primary field.</li>\n<li>A queued intercom can be linked to each focus camera for push-to-talk.</li>\n<li>Middle button over the focus camera = talk; over another camera = next camera in the pursuit.</li>\n<li>Effect: a voice that follows the subject through the building tends to disconcert him or her.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-14-e6\">14.7 Merging Legacy Systems</h3><ul>\n<li>Reviews the history of PPS (physical protection system) technologies and how to integrate them into modern digital systems.</li>\n<li>Two types of project: <strong>new construction</strong> or <strong>existing building</strong> (its use changes or the security program is expanded).</li>\n<li>In existing buildings always, and in new construction often, there are prior systems to extend.</li>\n<li><strong>Legacy systems:</strong> old or obsolete systems. They have all the data points of modern ones, but rarely easy interfaces for integration.</li>\n<li>The PPS designer must integrate them so the overall system works well.</li>\n<li>Typical scenario: several access control, video, and communications systems. They report to a chaotic monitoring center, or each building operates in isolation. Some are up to date and others have been obsolete for years.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Principles of integration:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Improves the accuracy of reporting and incident management.</li>\n<li>Reduces the time the console operator takes to <strong>detect, assess, and coordinate</strong> the response.</li>\n<li>It does so by automating the tasks with which the operator cross-references incoming information.</li>\n<li>Creates an evidence trail for criminal proceedings, employment discipline, or training.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Non-integrated system</th>\n<th>Integrated system</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Announces the alarm \"in a vacuum\"</td>\n<td>Instantly shows the right cameras, and even the <strong>pre-alarm video</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The operator must find out which cameras cover the area and select them</td>\n<td>The operator assesses on the spot: real or nuisance alarm, severity, and type of response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>While the video arrives, the aggressor may have left</td>\n<td>The best queue the nearby intercom: just press a button to talk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Automates functions: lighting the route from the parking lot to the office of an executive who arrives after hours; turning on heating or air conditioning when he or she uses a card</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>These integrations are usually feasible with modern equipment, <strong>not with legacy systems</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.7.1 Access Control Systems</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Alarm and access control systems are classified into <strong>five generations</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>1st, 4th, and 5th</strong> are still in use.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Generation</th>\n<th>Alarms</th>\n<th>Access control</th>\n<th>Key features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1st</strong></td>\n<td>Separate from access, with their own hardware. <strong>20 mA current loop</strong> monitored with an ammeter: open circuit = alarm. Up to <strong>300 ammeters</strong> in a row. At first without sound: guards scanned the meters. Later, interposing relays sounded a warning</td>\n<td>Stand-alone at a single door</td>\n<td>Its descendants are still on hotel doors all over the world</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2nd</strong></td>\n<td>Separate from access. Colored lamps: <strong>green</strong> = circuit active, <strong>red</strong> = alarm, <strong>yellow</strong> = bypassed</td>\n<td>Several card technologies (e.g., barium ferrite), today almost all obsolete. It brought a few doors (typically <strong>8 or 16</strong>) to a central control</td>\n<td>Very cryptic to use; the manual had to be at hand</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3rd</strong></td>\n<td>Alarm and access <strong>in a single system</strong>. They reported to a minicomputer the size of a four-drawer filing cabinet and to a line printer</td>\n<td>More doors (typically <strong>no more than 64</strong>), controlled from a dedicated terminal. <strong>All access decisions, in the central minicomputer</strong></td>\n<td>Also cryptic; a lot of training</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4th</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Since the <strong>late 1970s</strong>, with 8-bit microprocessors: Motorola 6800, Intel 8080, MOS Technology 6502, and Zilog Z80. <strong>Distributed</strong>, compact panels. Programming in <strong>EPROM</strong> (erasable programmable read-only memory) firmware</td>\n<td>Architecture still present in many products. Limited flexibility: it only does what was factory-programmed; almost no field programming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>5th</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Since the <strong>early 2000s</strong>. Almost no EPROM: each door’s programming is downloaded to the panel as a toolkit. <strong>Every entry and exit point is programmable</strong></td>\n<td>Acts like a PLC, the concept on which all access control is based. Very powerful and highly integrable. Still has little impact on the industry</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Key points of the 4th versus the 5th:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The 4th uses <strong>four hardware points per reader</strong>: reader, door position switch, request-to-exit sensor, and lock.</li>\n<li>It may need extra hardware for unique functions, such as a local door alarm.</li>\n<li>On doors with a reader in and a reader out, the leftover points are wasted.</li>\n<li>In the 5th, those leftover points are available as inputs and outputs.</li>\n<li>The 5th only performs at its best if the qualified installer has access to all of its programming.</li>\n<li>The industry still depends mostly on expensive <strong>PSIMs</strong> for functions that a 5th generation would make easy and cheap.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Access Cards</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Many old technologies have disappeared along with their market.</li>\n<li>Cards are the <strong>core of systems integration</strong>.</li>\n<li>Single pass authorization cards give entry to specific areas.</li>\n<li>They are usually <strong>the first thing integrated</strong>: a single technology is chosen and all systems are upgraded to that single standard.</li>\n<li>Modern cards combine several technologies in one: magnetic stripe, invisible barcode, and contact or contactless proximity. That way they work with all of the organization’s systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Card technologies still common:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>ABA magnetic stripe.</li>\n<li>Visible or invisible barcode.</li>\n<li>Complex barcode.</li>\n<li><strong>125 kHz</strong> proximity card.</li>\n<li><strong>13.56 MHz</strong> smart card, contact or contactless.</li>\n<li><strong>FIPS 201</strong>-compliant card (outside the book: Federal Information Processing Standard 201, the U.S. federal credential).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book expands ABA as \"American Banking Association\". The real name is American Bankers Association (outside the book).</li>\n<li>The effectiveness of each technology must be weighed: some can be defeated or lack encryption.</li>\n<li>Besides cards: keypad code or biometric credential (fingerprint, hand geometry, handwriting, iris, voice).</li>\n<li>In high security it is common to require <strong>two or more</strong>, e.g., card + fingerprint.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Access Control Systems from Different Manufacturers</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Even if a single card works for several brands, the organization may want to monitor everything from <strong>a single center</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fully integrating alarms and access databases may require replacing entire systems and panels.</li>\n<li>With a limited budget, the work can be done <strong>in phases</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Alarm Monitoring</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Usual first step: bring alarm monitoring to <strong>a single platform</strong> at the center, whatever the brand in the field.</li>\n<li>Reason: timely assessment of the data is critical.</li>\n<li>Method on a campus with several brands: one panel from each system at the monitoring center, one per system.</li>\n<li>Example: 2 systems of brand A + 1 of B, 1 of C, and 1 of D = <strong>5 panels</strong> at the center, one per building.</li>\n<li>The panels communicate with their systems by modem or network.</li>\n<li>Each panel is programmed to report any alarm to an output device, software or hardware.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Hardware Alarm Reporting Interface</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The output is a dry contact relay.</li>\n<li>The center’s panel handles relay cards: <strong>one relay for each alarm</strong> of the remote system.</li>\n<li>Each relay is wired to an alarm input of the center’s system. That way, each remote alarm is represented at the center.</li>\n<li><strong>Advantage:</strong> it is immune to software version updates of either system, which are known to put them out of sync.</li>\n<li>It may be mandatory on obsolete systems with proprietary databases, especially if panels are no longer available.</li>\n<li>If there is no spare hardware left, <strong>it is advisable to replace the system</strong>: a failure would take it out of service and create an operational emergency.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Software Alarm Reporting</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>There are at least <strong>three common methods</strong>:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>What it requires</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1. Database link</strong></td>\n<td>The databases of the monitoring system and the monitored system are linked. The monitor watches and reports the other’s alarms</td>\n<td>Qualified SQL (Structured Query Language) programmer and license agreements on both systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2. Alarm printer RS-232 port</strong></td>\n<td>The monitored system sends all its alarms to the RS-232 port of its panel at the center. An RS-232 port on the monitor captures them and an SQL database converts each line into an understandable alarm message</td>\n<td>SQL programmer to write the code. Configure the RS-232 interfaces of both systems. Configure the monitor to receive the messages from the SQL database</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3. PSIM</strong></td>\n<td>Detects data from several systems and translates them into a common alarm reporting regime. Combines hardware and software creatively</td>\n<td>Almost always integrates well with legacy systems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Variant of method 2: if the monitor does not adapt well, an <strong>interpreter SQL computer</strong> is installed. It receives the RS-232 messages and harmonizes its database with the monitor’s. It may require virtual device licenses.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says the SQL database converts the message into one \"understood by the monitored system\". Logically it should be the <strong>monitoring</strong> system; it appears to be a typo.</li>\n<li>If the monitored system <strong>does not use an SQL database</strong>, the <strong>hardware</strong> method is preferred.</li>\n<li>On campuses or companies with a mix of systems, a <strong>combination</strong> of hardware and software interfaces is often needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Access Control System Interfacing</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Integrating alarm reporting is not enough to manage access in common. For that there are two methods:<ol>\n<li><strong>Convert all systems to a common one.</strong> Almost always requires the same software version too.</li>\n<li><strong>Use a PSIM</strong> that integrates several manufacturers. Its success depends on the manufacturer: some do not cooperate with external interfaces.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Both give a common interface for programming cards and managing everything centrally.</li>\n<li>Benefit: security management applies standardized practices uniformly across the whole organization.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.7.2 Legacy Video Systems</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In the 1970s, video was high technology: few cameras, each with its own monitor.</li>\n<li>As the number of cameras grew, rooms filled with monitors: too much heat and too many images to watch.</li>\n<li>The industry responded with this sequence of technologies:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technology</th>\n<th>Key features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sequential switchers</strong></td>\n<td>First solution. <strong>8–16 inputs</strong> for cameras and almost always <strong>2 monitor outputs</strong>: one cycles through the cameras in sequence and the other shows a fixed camera</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Quad display units</strong></td>\n<td><strong>4 cameras</strong> on a single monitor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Early recording</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Sony U-Matic</strong>: the size of a suitcase; records <strong>1 camera for 2 hours</strong> on a cassette the size of a large book. Then <strong>VHS</strong>: 1 analog camera up to <strong>6 hours</strong> at the slowest speed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Multiplexers + time-lapse VCRs</strong></td>\n<td>Normal analog video shows <strong>30 frames per second</strong>. The multiplexer takes one frame from each of <strong>16 cameras</strong>, digitizes it, and records it on a slow VHS. Result: about 4 frames/s per camera and <strong>24 hours of 16 cameras on a single tape</strong>. Shows 1–16 cameras live or recorded at <strong>2–4 frames/s</strong>, on 2 monitors like the sequential switcher</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Networked video switchers</strong></td>\n<td>Larger switchers, with many inputs and outputs and a keyboard with PTZ controls (pan/tilt and motorized zoom already existed). Functions separated into boxes: camera input bays, monitor output bays, central processing unit, and <strong>cross-point switcher</strong>. Joined by ribbon cables forming a <strong>video buss</strong>. The boxes could be in different buildings: a single system for the whole campus. The bus between bays and matrix runs over <strong>fiber optic</strong>, because of the losses of long coax</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DVR</strong> (digital video recorder)</td>\n<td>Joins the multiplexer (which digitizes) with a real <strong>hard disk</strong> recorder. Eliminates analog recording. As disks grew and fell in cost, it completely replaced multiplexers and time-lapse. It is a drop-in replacement for multiplexer + VCR. Historically for analog cameras; it increasingly accepts digital cameras</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NVR</strong> (network video recorder)</td>\n<td>For digital cameras no analog inputs are needed. It connects to the same network as the cameras and is located wherever convenient: near the cameras, the monitoring center, or anywhere else</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> 30 frames/s divided among 16 cameras gives ≈ 1.9 frames/s per camera. The book says \"about 4\" and then \"2–4\". Remember the book’s figures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two versions of NVR:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it is like</th>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages / when to use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hardware-based NVR</strong></td>\n<td>Recording circuitry and software + internal or external disk array. Records video and sometimes audio</td>\n<td>Plug-and-play, almost no configuring. Easier to install and perhaps cheaper</td>\n<td>Small campus networks. Historically, poor handling of backup and fail-over, although that is changing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Software-based NVR</strong></td>\n<td>Conventional servers with VMS acting as NVR</td>\n<td>Well suited to large systems, campuses, or multiple campuses. Especially with centralized recording and, even more, if remote backup or fail-over is required</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Storage of a software-based NVR:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Internal.</li>\n<li>DAS (direct-attached storage): disks connected directly to the server.</li>\n<li>NAS (network attached storage): disks on the same network as the server.</li>\n<li><strong>SAN (storage area network)</strong>: dedicated high-performance device on its own separate network.<ul>\n<li>Made up of one or more video servers, a <strong>SAN switch</strong> (like a normal switch, but with much more throughput), and one or more recording units with disk arrays.</li>\n<li>Scales from a few terabytes to systems that record many times the digital volume of the U.S. Library of Congress.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.7.3 Legacy Intercom Systems</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>There are many types; the oldest are hard to integrate.</li>\n<li>Difficult example: hardwired intercom, with field stations that report to a dedicated master station.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Solution when there is a single master station at the console:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Replace the master with a single intercom station.</li>\n<li>Use the output relays of the alarm/access system to switch it to each station, with icons on the GUI.</li>\n<li>It can queue the intercom when an alarm is activated: video and intercom respond at the same time.</li>\n<li>The operator just presses push-to-talk to speak with the alarm’s field station.</li>\n<li>It requires a <strong>release call button</strong>: it prevents jumping from an active call to the alarm’s call in mid-conversation.</li>\n<li>A <strong>time-delay relay</strong> cuts calls on its own in case someone forgets to release them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>DRDP (direct ring-down phones):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Include elevator intercoms and emergency call stations.</li>\n<li>Phones programmed to dial <strong>a single number</strong> when picked up or when a button is pressed.</li>\n<li>They integrate with <strong>PABX</strong> (private automatic branch exchange) telephone switches, which receive and route the calls.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Easy to integrate:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intercoms with an <strong>intercom matrix switch</strong> at their core.</li>\n<li><strong>All</strong> <strong>VOIP</strong> (voice over Internet protocol) intercoms.</li>\n<li>They have digital interfaces for development with an <strong>SDK</strong> (software development kit) or <strong>API</strong> (application program interface).</li>\n<li>Almost always, the large access control, VMS, and PSIM manufacturers already have those interfaces written.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Requirements of an integrated intercom:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is operated from the security system’s GUI.</li>\n<li>Intercoms linked to an alarm are queued automatically.</li>\n<li>An alarm <strong>does not cut off</strong> a call in progress; the operator is notified to choose which call to take.</li>\n<li>Going forward, the easiest approach is to use VOIP intercoms with interfaces already written in the security GUI or the PSIM.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.7.4 Security Networks and Legacy Integration</h4>\n<p><strong>Capabilities that the move from proprietary wiring to network wiring gave:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Real enterprise systems, crossing campus, community, state, and country boundaries.</li>\n<li>Greatly expanding one part of the system without running many new cables to the monitoring center.</li>\n<li>Deep automation with the right software. Especially the PSIM: integration, automation, and intelligent incident response.</li>\n<li>Much higher video quality than analog, especially with cameras far from the center and with megapixel cameras.</li>\n<li>Growing from a small system to practically any size.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class of network</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Local</strong> (LAN, local area network)</td>\n<td>Normally a single building</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Campus</strong></td>\n<td>Several buildings on a single network</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Enterprise</strong></td>\n<td>Several campuses and buildings</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Network issues when integrating security systems:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Issue</th>\n<th>What the book says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dedicated vs. shared network</strong> with the business</td>\n<td>Dedicated: much easier, because there is no concern about the effect of security data on business data (or vice versa). But more expensive: it does not share the capital cost with other services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Segmentation</strong> (security data private and separate)</td>\n<td>Best ways: <strong>VLAN</strong> (virtual local area network) and <strong>VPN</strong> (virtual private network, encrypts all its data). On a shared network, encrypt everything <strong>in transit and at rest</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bandwidth for video</strong></td>\n<td>Follow the manufacturer’s recommendation based on each camera’s resolution and frame rate, summed per branch. Use <strong>no more than 45%</strong> of the bandwidth on any port, switch, or segment. That way there are no lost frames, artifacts, or impact on other data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Multicast</strong></td>\n<td>Some systems and configurations require the multicast protocol for video and audio</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Multigigabit architecture</strong></td>\n<td>Before, a gigabit network seemed excessive; today multigigabit is normal. A competent network architect ensures capacity and data security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tree vs. ring</strong></td>\n<td>Small networks: tree. Large networks: ring, or ring and tree</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Programming equipment to work together</strong></td>\n<td>Three ways: (1) wiring with relay contacts and input points; (2) software integration with API and SDK; (3) integration via PSIM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security of data at rest</strong></td>\n<td>Encrypt all security data on disk with a complex algorithm and key</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>With old systems, much of what seems impossible is achieved <strong>with relays</strong>.</li>\n<li>Case: a consultant created an intercom and video matrix switch for a campus. It queued calls and video on alarms, using only wired relays.</li>\n<li>He did it <strong>almost 10 years</strong> before intercom or video matrix switches existed. Lesson: relay wiring remains a valid way to integrate legacy systems.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-14-e7\">14.8 Procurement</h3><h4>14.8.1 Procurement Forms</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Three main forms: <strong>sole source</strong>, <strong>RFP</strong> (request for proposal), and <strong>IFB</strong> (invitation for bid).</li>\n<li>They vary depending on whether the buyer is a government agency or a commercial company.</li>\n<li>The form is chosen <strong>before or at the start of the design phase</strong>: it determines the level of detail of the construction documents.</li>\n<li>Large organizations that install, commission, test, and maintain their own systems: extensive design, but simple purchasing.</li>\n<li>In that case it is enough to issue hardware purchase orders, at prenegotiated prices, to prequalified vendors.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Sole source</th>\n<th>RFP</th>\n<th>IFB</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>When to use it</strong></td>\n<td>A vendor has already been chosen. Small projects</td>\n<td>The vendor is chosen by competition on several factors: cost, schedule, technical capability</td>\n<td>Government and organizations that require bidding and award to the lowest qualified, responsive bidder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Design detail</strong></td>\n<td>Simple documents; they complement what the vendor already knows</td>\n<td>Detailed design documents based on functional requirements. Generic and performance specifications; makes and models with the phrase <strong>\"or approved equal\"</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Extremely explicit</strong>, for a firm, fixed price for materials and installation. Choosing makes and models, and the accuracy of the design, rests <strong>solely with the design team</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>What the vendor delivers</strong></td>\n<td>Negotiates equipment, installation, and service cost with the owner, and co-designs the system</td>\n<td>Transmittal letter, <strong>cost proposal</strong>, and <strong>technical proposal</strong>. <strong>Alternates</strong> (alternative solutions) are allowed</td>\n<td>Only the cost offer (bid), with unit prices and the requested breakdown. Perhaps its qualifications. <strong>No</strong> technical proposal or alternatives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Award criterion</strong></td>\n<td>Direct negotiation</td>\n<td><strong>Best value</strong>, not necessarily the cheapest</td>\n<td><strong>Lowest price</strong>, normally without negotiation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Timing</strong></td>\n<td>Saves time</td>\n<td>Preparing the proposal: typical minimum of <strong>3–4 weeks</strong> on medium and large projects</td>\n<td>More design time and cost, but only <strong>1–2 weeks</strong> of purchasing process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>What the owner gets</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>A security <strong>business partner</strong>, not a one-time installer</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h5>Sole Source Procurement</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The organization prequalifies a reputable contractor, designs the system with it, and negotiates equipment, installation, and service.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple construction documents</td>\n<td>Tends to focus only on hardware and technology; leaves to others the equally important subsystems of <strong>personnel, procedures, and facilities</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lower design cost for the owner</td>\n<td>The owner may skip security planning and rely on a contractor with an interest in selling equipment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Saves time</td>\n<td>Without competitive bidding, there is no way to compare prices</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Recommended only</strong> if the owner can do its own security needs analysis and knows systems and prices well.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Request for Proposal (RFP)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the <strong>most common</strong> method.</li>\n<li>If specific models are required for compatibility or uniformity with existing equipment, compliance with those items (responsiveness) may be the most important thing.</li>\n<li>It can be open to any contractor or limited to a prequalified list.</li>\n<li>To compare costs meaningfully: everyone responds first to the specified design; afterward, if they wish, they offer alternates.</li>\n<li>It is common to require that an alternate improve performance in a measurable way and cost <strong>the same or less</strong> than the base bid.</li>\n<li>The owner takes advantage of the contractor’s experience without losing control of the design.</li>\n<li>It can choose one or several proposers for the final negotiation.</li>\n<li>It does not obligate acceptance of the lowest offer: it seeks the <strong>best value</strong>.</li>\n<li>The organization defines value, but it must include <strong>price, quality, experience, and schedule</strong>.</li>\n<li>If price will not be the deciding factor, the RFP must say so.</li>\n<li>Preparing the response takes longer than in other forms, because there is a technical and a cost proposal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Invitation for Bid (IFB)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Since the IFB is decided on price, the design must leave no <strong>loopholes</strong>. If it does, bidders substitute inferior or inadequate equipment to win.</li>\n<li>It is awarded, normally without negotiation, to the lowest qualified bidder that followed the instructions.</li>\n<li>Typical procedure:<ol>\n<li><strong>Sealed</strong> bids, delivered at a specific time and place.</li>\n<li>Bid opening, often <strong>public</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>apparent winner</strong> is announced.</li>\n<li>The contract is signed after checking that its proposal is complete, accurate, and meets the qualifications.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>14.8.2 Procurement Process</h4>\n<h5>Pre-bid conference</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>One representative from each contractor is invited.</li>\n<li>The owner or its consulting engineer thoroughly reviews the documents and leads a walk-through of the affected buildings and areas.</li>\n<li>If applicable, it is held at the installation site so they can see field conditions.</li>\n<li>Timing: <strong>approximately one week after</strong> issuing the documents for bidding. It gives time to review them and to incorporate information into the proposal.</li>\n<li>A representative of the design team records all questions and answers in the minutes.</li>\n<li>After the conference, questions are asked <strong>in writing</strong> and the answers are sent to <strong>all</strong> contractors.</li>\n<li>It is advisable to close questions <strong>one week before</strong> the proposal due date.</li>\n<li><strong>A single point of contact</strong> must be named for all questions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Evaluation of proposals</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Check that each proposal is complete and accurate.</li>\n<li>The contract may say that any inaccurate or incomplete proposal is rejected. But most commercial companies reject only on signs of incompetence or serious incompleteness.</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluation matrix:</strong> columns = contractors; rows = main functions and components of the system. It verifies that each technical proposal covers everything requested.</li>\n<li>Another similar matrix compares prices and alternates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Life-cycle cost:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>First, identify the system’s objectives and goals and its expected life.</li>\n<li>Simple form: <strong>capital cost + maintenance cost over the useful life</strong>.</li>\n<li>Typically, maintenance and warranty = <strong>11% of the total capital construction cost</strong> of the system.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book does not clarify whether that 11% is annual or total.</li>\n<li>This calculation reveals whether the cheapest bidder cut its margin in order to recover it with high maintenance charges.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>If one proposal is much lower than the others, check:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Math errors.</li>\n<li>Quality of the proposed equipment.</li>\n<li>The contractor’s experience on projects of that size and complexity.</li>\n<li>The contractor’s understanding of the project.</li>\n<li>The contractor’s financial stability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Before awarding:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Verify the references of <strong>all</strong> contractors.</li>\n<li>Interview the front-runners, with their project manager and their site supervisor present (they may be the same person).</li>\n<li>In the interview, the designer should find out:<ul>\n<li>Is there good chemistry with the contractor’s representatives?</li>\n<li>Do they have the experience and personality to work well with the other trades on the job?</li>\n<li>How did they resolve problems on other projects? It helps to know what other clients think of those solutions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Negotiation and closing:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If procurement rules allow it, the final price is negotiated with the short list, <strong>based on value</strong>.</li>\n<li>If the contractor’s margin is too low, quality and responsiveness suffer.</li>\n<li>A good contractor with a realistic profit makes the extra effort to resolve implementation problems.</li>\n<li>Success requires a well-defined and well-executed procurement contract.</li>\n<li>The implementation cycle of a typical system lasts <strong>18–24 months</strong>.</li>\n<li>Final goal: a <strong>business partnership</strong> between owner and contractor, not a one-time sale.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-14-e8\">Key terms</h3><p>system · planning and assessment · serial process · design/build · risk analysis · conceptual (schematic) design · design development (DD) · construction documents (CD) · basis of design (design basis) · requirements analysis · business case · return on investment (ROI) · payback · net present value (NPV) · design criteria · spare capacity · performance specification · mean time between failures (MTBF) · throughput · protection-in-depth · countermeasures · cost-effectiveness · outline specification · bill of quantities · contract (bid) documents · general / products / execution · MasterFormat · Division 28 Electronic Safety and Security · CADD · plans · elevations · details · risers · hardware schedules · ASTM Standard Practice for Security Engineering Symbols · order-of-magnitude estimate · MSRP · budgetary estimate · preliminary design estimate · final design estimate · contingency · RSMeans · life-cycle cost · performance bond · shop drawings · as-built (record) drawings · turnkey project · consultant vs. contractor · encoder/codec · MPEG-4 · H.264 · H.265 · megapixel · PTZ into history · video analytics · learning algorithm · baseline · VMS · ONVIF profiles A/C/G/Q/S/T/D/M · PSIM · GSOC · unified solution · video guard tour · video pursuit · legacy systems · 20 mA current loop · EPROM · PLC · FIPS 201 · dry contact · RS-232 · SQL · multiplexer · DVR · NVR · DAS / NAS / SAN · DRDP · PABX · VOIP · SDK · API · VLAN · VPN · multicast · sole source · RFP · IFB · or approved equal · alternates · base bid · best value · lowest qualified responsive bidder · pre-bid conference · evaluation matrix</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"14.1 System Design Principles"},{"id":"e1","t":"14.2 Initial Phases"},{"id":"e2","t":"14.3 Design Phases"},{"id":"e3","t":"14.4 Contracting"},{"id":"e4","t":"14.5 The Role of Consultants in Systems Design"},{"id":"e5","t":"14.6 Video Systems"},{"id":"e6","t":"14.7 Merging Legacy Systems"},{"id":"e7","t":"14.8 Procurement"},{"id":"e8","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"14.2 Initial Phases","h":"in this part the book writes “PSS” once; it is a typo for PPS."},{"s":"14.4 Contracting","h":"do not confuse the 15–20% accuracy of the initial budget (14.4.1) with the ±10–20% contingency of the budgetary estimate (14.4.3). And 10% is what experienced designers achieve."},{"s":"14.4 Contracting","h":"the text says 2 revolving doors, but the figure prices 1. It also does not price the 2 monitors, the DVR, or the access control monitor."},{"s":"14.6 Video Systems","h":"the book says the industry adopted \"two\" algorithms (MPEG-4 and H.264), but then adds H.265 for megapixel. Remember all three."},{"s":"14.6 Video Systems","h":"the title includes \"intelligent video\", but the book does not define it separately."},{"s":"14.7 Merging Legacy Systems","h":"the book expands ABA as \"American Banking Association\". The real name is American Bankers Association (outside the book)."},{"s":"14.7 Merging Legacy Systems","h":"the book says the SQL database converts the message into one \"understood by the monitored system\". Logically it should be the <strong>monitoring</strong> system; it appears to be a typo."},{"s":"14.7 Merging Legacy Systems","h":"30 frames/s divided among 16 cameras gives ≈ 1.9 frames/s per camera. The book says \"about 4\" and then \"2–4\". Remember the book’s figures."},{"s":"14.8 Procurement","h":"the book does not clarify whether that 11% is annual or total."}]},"physical-15":{"html":"<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Step (as named in the book)</th>\n<th>Step (plain wording)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Site preparation</td>\n<td>Site preparation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Contractor coordination</td>\n<td>Contractor coordination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Installation</td>\n<td>Installation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Testing and warranty issues</td>\n<td>Testing and warranty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Training</td>\n<td>Training</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Each step requires the attention of three actors: the contractor, the security system designer, and the owner’s representative.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-15-e0\">15.1 Site Preparation</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>It is the most important step</strong> when installing PPS elements: site preparation and planning.</li>\n<li>Everything must already be in the design package and located on drawings: door hardware, card readers, sensors, panels, cameras, monitors, console equipment, gates, signage, and lighting.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Who</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Verify locations</td>\n<td>Installation contractor</td>\n<td>Reviews the locations on drawings and notes the necessary changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Resolve differences</td>\n<td>Project manager + contractor</td>\n<td>Examine the installation requirements and resolve every difference before moving forward</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Site visit</td>\n<td>Contractor</td>\n<td>Verifies that actual conditions match the design package</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Written report of changes</td>\n<td>Contractor</td>\n<td>Documents every change to the site or conditions that affects system performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Inventory of what exists</td>\n<td>Contractor</td>\n<td>Inspects, tests, and documents the existing protection equipment and signal lines that will be integrated into the new system</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Rules for the contractor in this phase:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Takes no corrective action without written permission from the client.</li>\n<li>Items that do not work: delivers specification sheets or written functional requirements that support the finding, plus the estimated cost of correcting them.</li>\n<li>In the report notes the scheduled date of connection to existing equipment.</li>\n<li>Does not disconnect signal lines or equipment, or cause downtime, without prior written approval from the client.</li>\n<li>If a device or signal or control line fails after the contractor began working on it, the contractor diagnoses and corrects it.</li>\n<li>Pays for repairs caused by negligence or abuse of the client’s equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book calls site preparation \"the most important step\" of the installation.</p><h3 id=\"physical-15-e1\">15.2 Contractor Coordination</h3><ul>\n<li>The contractor continuously coordinates three things: <strong>resources</strong> (people and material), <strong>environment</strong> (that the site is ready for installation), and the <strong>project schedule</strong>.</li>\n<li>The quality of coordination with other building disciplines can decide the success or failure of the entire installation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Security interfaces with other building systems (20 in the book):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>System</th>\n<th>Plain wording</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Elevators</td>\n<td>Elevators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Escalators</td>\n<td>Escalators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Doors and door hardware</td>\n<td>Doors and hardware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Windows</td>\n<td>Windows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Electrical</td>\n<td>Electrical system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Lighting</td>\n<td>Lighting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Life safety systems (fire alarm)</td>\n<td>Life safety (fire alarm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Egress locking systems release</td>\n<td>Release of locks at exits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>HVAC or building management system</td>\n<td>Climate control (HVAC, Heating, Ventilation and Air Conditioning, outside the book) or building management system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Timekeeping</td>\n<td>Time and attendance control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Parking</td>\n<td>Parking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Landscaping</td>\n<td>Gardening and landscaping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Fences and gates</td>\n<td>Fences and gates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Enterprise resource planning (ERP)</td>\n<td>Enterprise resource planning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Human resource management</td>\n<td>Human resource management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Signage</td>\n<td>Signage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Concrete/asphalt driveways</td>\n<td>Concrete or asphalt vehicle access ways</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>Information technology systems</td>\n<td>Information technology (IT) systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19</td>\n<td>Camera sightlines</td>\n<td>Camera sightlines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>Directory services (Active Directory, LDAP)</td>\n<td>Directory services (LDAP, Lightweight Directory Access Protocol, outside the book)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Typical problem: the security contractor is usually the last to arrive on the project.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>What the security contractor must do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The final date is fixed, but the precursor work by others is not ready on time</td>\n<td>Immediately notify the owner’s representative. Push for the precursor work to be finished now. If necessary, request a schedule extension or a waiver of late-delay penalties</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The environment is not suitable for the specified equipment</td>\n<td>Notify the owner’s representative. Choose another location or a specialized enclosure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Example of an unsuitable environment: sensitive alarm or access control electronics in a boiler room. High temperature and humidity cause failures and shorten the panel’s life.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-15-e2\">15.3 Installation</h3><h4>15.3.1 Installation and Operation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The project manager instructs the contractor to install all components, including customer-furnished equipment.</li>\n<li>In many cases an integrator can also do the installation.</li>\n<li>The contractor or integrator installs each subsystem according to the manufacturer’s instructions and applicable installation standards.</li>\n<li>The contractor provides all connectors, terminators, interconnections, services, and adjustments for a complete and operable system.</li>\n<li>After installing, the project manager tunes the system to the specific operation of the facility.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>15.3.2 Component Installation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Reference standard: <strong>NFPA 731</strong>, <em>Standard for the Installation of Electronic Premises Security Systems</em> (2020), of the National Fire Protection Association (NFPA).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Card readers</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mounting as required: surface, semi-flush, pedestal, or weatherproof.</li>\n<li>Installed according to local codes, the authority having jurisdiction (AHJ), and applicable local, state, or federal standards.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Electric locks: comparison of requirements</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Electric door strike or bolt</th>\n<th>Electromagnetic lock</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>On power failure</td>\n<td>Releases (fail safe) or stays locked (fail secure), depending on the application</td>\n<td>Always releases automatically</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Principle</td>\n<td>Actuating solenoid energized with direct current (DC)</td>\n<td>No moving parts; electromagnetism only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Holding force</td>\n<td>The book does not state it</td>\n<td>At least 1,200 lb (544 kg)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Voltage</td>\n<td>12 or 24 V DC</td>\n<td>12 or 24 V DC (actuating armature)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum dissipation</td>\n<td>12 W</td>\n<td>12 W</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inrush current</td>\n<td>Maximum 1 A</td>\n<td>The book does not state it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Holding current</td>\n<td>Maximum 500 mA</td>\n<td>Maximum 500 mA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State change time</td>\n<td>Maximum 500 ms, from fully secure to fully open</td>\n<td>Maximum 300 ms, in both directions (secure to open and open to secure)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Line supervision</td>\n<td>End-of-line resistor</td>\n<td>End-of-line resistor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tamper resistance</td>\n<td>Mechanism inside hardened guard barriers against forced entry</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mounting method</td>\n<td>Single and double doors; mortise or rim hardware; right or left hand</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Size and weight</td>\n<td>Compatible with standard door frame preparations</td>\n<td>The book does not state it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signaling to the system</td>\n<td>Signal switches: notify if the bolt is not engaged or the strike is unlocked. They report forced entry</td>\n<td>Integrated bond sensor or integrated request to exit (RTE), on some models</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Internal circuit that eliminates residual magnetism and inductive kickback. Connects to local processors without altering them externally, internally, or functionally</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Integrated bond sensor:</strong> monitors like a door position switch (or \"door contact\").</li>\n<li><strong>Integrated RTE:</strong> allows unrestricted egress and gives the access control more information. That way it reduces nuisance alarms from door activity.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> strike = maximum 500 ms; electromagnet = maximum 300 ms. The 1 A inrush current is given only for the strike.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intrusion devices: where and how to install them</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Device</th>\n<th>Location and installation rules</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bell or alarm box</td>\n<td>On the front of the building or in plain view of neighbors and passersby: it deters many burglars. High enough to be out of easy reach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control panels</td>\n<td>Ideally inside, in a secure place. Unreachable without a ladder. Near the main electrical power. Never over combustible material. With a lock and 24/7 alarm supervision via tamper switches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Passive infrared (PIR) motion detector</td>\n<td>Do not expose it to infrared light. Avoid windows, fire, filament lamps, and heat sources such as radiators and heaters: they cause nuisance alarms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dual-technology detector (PIR + microwave)</td>\n<td>Locate it where both technologies are used, not just one. Microwaves detect through thin walls: do not use it where that happens. Also respect the PIR limits. Used to reduce nuisance alarms from a single sensor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Door and window contacts</td>\n<td>Normally on exterior doors and windows; also on any that are vulnerable. They detect opening. Use the correct type: on a steel door and frame, an industrial metal surface-mount or recessed contact, not the aluminum or wood type. High-security and fire-rated types exist. Surface-mount types are prone to damage and tampering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shock sensors</td>\n<td>In areas susceptible to forced entry, such as door or window frames. Contacts detect opening, but not necessarily breakage. If the intruder could kick in a panel or break a glass, supplement with shock sensors or PIR</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Interconnection of console video equipment</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Case</th>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Digital video over TCP/IP network</td>\n<td>No individual connection longer than 328 ft (100 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>From the patch panel to the PoE (Power over Ethernet) switch</td>\n<td>Less than 10 ft (3.048 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video cabling for interior and exterior cameras, and between PoE switches and patch panels</td>\n<td>Augmented Category 6 (CAT 6A) with unshielded twisted pair (UTP) and RJ-45 connectors. Test each cable against the manufacturer’s performance standards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analog video, path of 25 ft (7.6 m) or less</td>\n<td>RG-59/U coaxial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analog video, path longer than 25 ft</td>\n<td>RG-11/U coaxial or fiber optic</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Cables as short as possible, without straining the connectors.</li>\n<li>Rack-mounted equipment with sliding rails: cable long enough to fully extend the rails.</li>\n<li>NFPA 731 expands on how to connect equipment with \"category\" network cable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Surveillance cameras</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The camera needs a lens with the correct focal length to view the protected area.</li>\n<li>Chapter 7 of NFPA 731 details how to choose camera locations and lenses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Contractor steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Connect power and signal. With PoE, power travels over the same digital connection.</li>\n<li>Fixed iris lens: adjust the f-stop for the best image.</li>\n<li>Aim the camera to cover the protected area of interest.</li>\n<li>Exterior fixed camera facing the rising or setting sun: aim it far enough below the horizon so it does not see the sun directly.</li>\n<li>Focus the lens for a sharp image across the entire field of view.</li>\n<li>Synchronize all cameras so the image does not \"roll\" when switching cameras. Not required on digital cameras.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Exterior mounts (install according to the manufacturer, and also):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Exterior fixed mount</th>\n<th>Exterior pan/tilt mount</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Hardware sized to hold the mount, camera, and housing under the site’s maximum wind and ice load</td>\n<td>Install the pan/tilt, the receiver/driver, and their appurtenances according to the manufacturer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Foundation for each camera pole, per specification</td>\n<td>Hardware sized to hold the pan/tilt, camera, and housing under the maximum wind and ice load</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ground rod per pole, connected to the pole</td>\n<td>Install the pan/tilt control wiring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electrical and signal transmission wiring to the mount</td>\n<td>Connect the pan/tilt to the control wiring and to alternating current (AC)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connect signal lines and alternating current (AC) to the mount interfaces</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Connect a pole wiring harness to the camera</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Video recording and switching equipment</strong> — according to the manufacturer, and also:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Connect all subassemblies.</li>\n<li>Connect video signal inputs and outputs.</li>\n<li>Terminate the video inputs as required.</li>\n<li>Connect alarm signal inputs and outputs.</li>\n<li>Connect control signal inputs and outputs for auxiliary equipment or secondary control or monitoring sites.</li>\n<li>Load all site software: databases, operating parameters, and system, command, and application programs.</li>\n<li>Program the video annotation of each camera.</li>\n<li>Program screen layouts that group geographically nearby cameras on one screen.</li>\n<li>Configure a video guard tour so the console officer can tour the facility with those screens.</li>\n<li>Configure graphical user interface (GUI) icons on maps and plans: on clicking, they show the cameras in that area.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>15.3.3 Other Features and Considerations</h4>\n<p><strong>Conduit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>All interior wiring goes in rigid galvanized steel conduit. This includes low-voltage wiring outside the monitoring console, racks, cabinets, boxes, and similar enclosures.</li>\n<li>It must comply with applicable standards: Underwriters Laboratories (UL) in the U.S. or the Canadian Electrical Code in Canada.</li>\n<li>Exception: wiring between components in the same rack or cabinet does not need conduit.</li>\n<li>Minimum conduit size: <strong>1/2 in (1.3 cm)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tight-tapered and threaded joints. Threadless fittings or couplings are prohibited.</li>\n<li>Conduit boxes: cast metal or malleable iron with threaded hubs or bodies.</li>\n<li>Normally not permitted, unless required and approved by the client: electric metallic tubing (EMT), armored cable, nonmetallic-sheathed cable, and flexible conduit.</li>\n<li>Data transmission media do not share conduit, raceway, compartment, outlet box, or junction box with non-security building wiring.</li>\n<li>Do not power components with flexible cords or cord connections, unless required and approved by the client.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Grounding</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Per <strong>NFPA 70, National Electrical Code</strong> (2011), articles <strong>250 and 800</strong> (U.S.), or another applicable national standard. Plus the manufacturer’s requirements.</li>\n<li>All other circuits must test free of grounds.</li>\n<li>Objective: prevent ground loops, noise, and surges from affecting operation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Enclosure penetrations</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Always from below, unless the design requires another direction.</li>\n<li>Seal with an approved waterproof sealant: penetrations of interior enclosures with transition of conduit from inside to outside, and all penetrations of exterior enclosures.</li>\n<li>The conduit riser ends at a hot-dipped galvanized metal cable terminator.</li>\n<li>The terminator is filled with the sealant recommended by the cable manufacturer, without damaging the cable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cold galvanizing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Any field weld or brazing on factory-galvanized boxes, enclosures, and conduit is covered with cold galvanizing paint.</li>\n<li>The paint must have <strong>at least 95% zinc by weight</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>System startup: do not energize until these 5 points are met</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Requirement before energizing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>All equipment configured according to the manufacturer’s instructions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Visual inspection: no defective equipment installed and no loose connections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Wiring tested and verified as properly connected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Grounding and transient protection verified as properly installed and connected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Power supplies verified for voltage, phasing, and frequency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Data deliverables before testing</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Deliverable</th>\n<th>What it includes</th>\n<th>Deadline</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Configuration data</td>\n<td>The contractor loads into the database everything needed to operate. Delivers it to the client in suitable formats, with its field surveys and other pertinent information</td>\n<td>Completed forms to the client, for review and approval, <strong>at least 30 days before the database test</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Graphics</td>\n<td>When required, the contractor creates and installs them with contract and field data. Enough detail for the operator to assess the alarm. Color printed samples of each type of graphic, of <strong>at least 8 x 10 in (20 x 25 cm)</strong></td>\n<td>Samples to the client, for review and approval, <strong>at least 30 days before acceptance tests</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Signal line and data transmission system (DTS) supervision</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The system supervises all signal lines and DTS: it watches for changes or disturbances in the signal.</li>\n<li>It also monitors the conditions of <strong>UL 1076</strong>, <em>Standard for Proprietary Burglar Alarm Units and Systems</em> (1995), for line security equipment.</li>\n<li>It generates an alarm on a <strong>current change of 10% or more</strong>.</li>\n<li>It generates an alarm on opening, closing, short circuit, or grounding of signal lines and DTS.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Housing by location</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Enclosure requirement</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Indoor sensors</td>\n<td>Protection against dust, falling dirt, and dripping noncorrosive liquids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outdoor sensors</td>\n<td>Protection against windblown dust, rain and splashing, hose-directed water, and ice formation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Indoor electronics</td>\n<td><strong>NEMA 250-2014</strong>, <em>Enclosures for Electrical Equipment (1000 Volts Maximum)</em>, <strong>Type 12</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outdoor electronics</td>\n<td>NEMA 250-2014, <strong>Type 4X</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corrosive environment (per NEMA 250-2014)</td>\n<td><strong>Metal</strong> enclosure, NEMA 250-2014, <strong>Type 4X</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hazardous environment</td>\n<td>Enclosure that meets the manufacturer’s requirements for that specific hazardous environment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>NEMA = National Electrical Manufacturers Association.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Nameplates</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Consider laminated plastic nameplates or asset tags on the main components.</li>\n<li>Each plate identifies the device and its location in the system. It is attached <strong>inside</strong> the component’s enclosure.</li>\n<li>In addition, each main component carries a corrosion-resistant plate with: manufacturer’s name and address, type or style, model or serial number, and catalog number.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tamper switches</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are fitted to enclosures, cabinets, housings, boxes, and accessories with a hinged door or removable cover that contain circuits, connections, or power supplies.</li>\n<li>They are cover-operated, resist corrosion, and trigger an alarm when the door or cover is moved.</li>\n<li>Enclosure and switch work together: there must be no direct line of sight to the internal components before the switch activates.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>The tamper switch must…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Be inaccessible until it is activated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Have concealed hardware, so its location cannot be seen from outside</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Be connected to supervised circuits in a 24-hour zone, annunciated and supervised separately, regardless of protection mode</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Be spring-loaded and held closed by the door or cover</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Open the circuit when the door or cover is disturbed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Maintenance locks</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Locks on the system enclosures, for maintenance.</li>\n<li>Type: UL-listed round key with three double pins, one mushroom, or three plain. Alternative: conventional five-pin lock with five-point, three-position side bar.</li>\n<li>Keys stamped \"DO NOT DUPLICATE\". The key can be removed only in the locked position.</li>\n<li>Maintenance locks are keyed alike.</li>\n<li>Risk: manufacturers often deliver panels with the same key for all customers. It is convenient for integrators, but other installations receive the same key.</li>\n<li>Solution: change all locks to ones keyed alike specifically for the building or facility. It gives more control and security.</li>\n<li>Keys are managed with a key control plan: it is the best defense of locking devices because it accounts for all keys on a regular basis.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> \"keyed alike\" is recommended, but site-specific, never the factory one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wire and cable</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The contractor provides all cable that is not client equipment.</li>\n<li>Complies with national standards (NFPA 70 in the U.S.), unless the end user has stricter standards.</li>\n<li>Installation per security standards and each piece of equipment’s manual:<ul>\n<li><strong>NFPA 70</strong> — National Electrical Code.</li>\n<li><strong>UL 681</strong> — installation and classification of burglar and holdup alarm systems.</li>\n<li><strong>UL 1037</strong> — antitheft alarms and devices.</li>\n<li><strong>UL 1076</strong> — proprietary burglar alarm units and systems.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Service points that allow locating a fault in <strong>less than 20 minutes</strong>.</li>\n<li>Minimum conduit: 1/2 in (1.3 cm).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Local area network (LAN) cabling</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>According to the Telecommunications Industry Association (TIA): <strong>TIA-568 A or B, category 5</strong> (2012), or the country’s national standard.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> here the book asks for category 5 for LAN, but for video it requires augmented CAT 6 (15.3.2). They are not the same.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Quality assurance</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Codes the work must comply with in the U.S. (7)</th>\n<th>Organizations whose standards the materials must meet in the U.S. (3)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Current National Electrical Code (NEC)</td>\n<td>NEMA (National Electrical Manufacturers Association)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Applicable federal, state, and local codes</td>\n<td>American National Standards Institute (ANSI)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Current uniform building code</td>\n<td>Underwriters Laboratories, Inc. (UL)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local electrical code, if applicable</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Occupational Safety and Health Act (OSHA) standards</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Any other code in force at the job site</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>General rule: all work complies with regional and national codes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>15.3.4 Tuning the Alarm System</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>After installation, the system <strong>must</strong> be tuned to the facility’s operation. Otherwise it generates too many nuisance alarms and confuses personnel instead of helping them.</li>\n<li>Tuning it requires knowing how the facility operates, which employees come and go, and what activities take place.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Time periods for alarms</td>\n<td>The security manager runs periodic reports and reviews the alarm history: nuisance alarms, location, frequency, and time. If patterns appear because of daily operations, he or she adjusts the alarm operating hours so as not to distract personnel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Authorized personnel</td>\n<td>If they are trusted and can enter at any time, their alarms are shunted so they are not generated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nuisance alarms</td>\n<td>Causes: employee errors (opening the wrong door, leaving doors open, forgetting to disarm subsystems) and door hardware that fails. Remedies: signs such as \"keep doors closed\"; adjust patrol schedules to surprise whoever leaves doors open; have patrols check that all doors are closed; review the maintenance program, with frequent door inspections and prompt replacement of defective parts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Improper application</td>\n<td>Poorly chosen security or fire alarm components. Examples: standard motion detector in a harsh environment; microwave sensor in a room with a hallway outside. Those devices must be changed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reducing alarms</td>\n<td>See the three cases below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Cases for reducing alarms:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loading dock door</strong> that arrives nonstop at the monitoring center during working hours: move the monitoring of that point to the area’s personnel.</li>\n<li><strong>Mechanical or utility rooms</strong> with maintenance access: procedure to notify the monitoring center which area and for how long. That way the operator temporarily ignores those points.</li>\n<li><strong>Secured doors with controlled movement of material:</strong> mandatory procedure, such as a call to the central station. An officer may be needed to control access.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-15-e3\">15.4 Testing and Warranty Issues</h3><ul>\n<li>Implementation team tests can cover equipment, personnel, procedures, or any combination.</li>\n<li><strong>Ideal acceptance test:</strong> takes the system to the established limits of the site-specific threats. It simulates real threat conditions and gives conclusive evidence of effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of test</th>\n<th>What it determines</th>\n<th>Precautions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Equipment performance testing</td>\n<td>Whether the equipment works, has adequate sensitivity, and meets design and performance objectives</td>\n<td>Always coordinate with facility personnel. May require special test equipment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personnel performance tests</td>\n<td>Whether the procedures work, whether personnel know and follow them, and whether personnel and equipment interact well</td>\n<td>Some are done without personnel knowing: coordinate them well and take care with safety</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> it is not enough for a component to meet the manufacturer’s standard if it proves ineffective in the test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Four types of test described in the section:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Predelivery or factory acceptance tests (FAT, outside the book).</li>\n<li>Site acceptance tests (SAT, outside the book).</li>\n<li>Reliability or availability tests.</li>\n<li>Post-implementation tests.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Seven factors for deciding which tests to do:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Prioritize the site-specific threats.</li>\n<li>Identify the worst-case scenarios: lowest probability of detection, least delay, and the different routes into the facility.</li>\n<li>Identify the system functions most critical to protecting the assets: detection, assessment, or delay.</li>\n<li>Determine the assumed probability of detection of each subsystem and its vulnerability to being defeated.</li>\n<li>Determine the incident assessment time: immediate or deferred.</li>\n<li>Identify the last points where the adversary must be detected so the protection force can respond in time.</li>\n<li>Compare the vulnerabilities with the findings and resolution of past inspections and incidents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>General rules for tests:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>At the close of each phase (factory, site, reliability), the originals of all data go to the client before the test is approved.</li>\n<li>The client gives the vendor or integrator documentation with the tests required during installation and commissioning.</li>\n<li><strong>Commissioning:</strong> testing every alarm point and every automatic function of the new system.</li>\n<li>That documentation details the personnel, equipment, instruments, and supplies for the acceptance test. It also states who witnesses the performance verification and the reliability verification.</li>\n<li>The contractor needs written permission from the client to move to the next phase.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>15.4.1 Predelivery or Factory Acceptance Testing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Depending on size and complexity, the client may ask the contractor to assemble a test system with some or all of the components.</li>\n<li>Objective: demonstrate that performance meets what was specified, with approved factory test procedures.</li>\n<li>The client designs the tests, or the contractor does if the client requires it.</li>\n<li>It is scheduled <strong>before any installation</strong>. The client attends and observes.</li>\n<li>The model numbers tested must be identical to those to be delivered on site.</li>\n<li>At the end, the originals of all data and results go to the client to approve the test.</li>\n<li>The report correlates commands, stimuli, and responses to allow logical interpretation.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factory test setup (7 items)</th>\n<th>Equipment for testing video (9 items)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>All the monitoring equipment of the security control center</td>\n<td>At least 4 cameras and each specified lens type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At least one link of each type of data transmission, with its equipment, to represent an integrated system</td>\n<td>3 video monitors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>As many local processors (field panels) as the site design calls for</td>\n<td>Video recorder, if the system requires it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At least one sensor of each type used</td>\n<td>Video switcher with input and output modules and control and application software, if required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enough sensor simulators (manual or software) to match the number of sensors in the design</td>\n<td>Alarm input panel, if required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At least one terminal device of each type used</td>\n<td>Pan/tilt mount and controller, if there are pan/tilt cameras</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>At least one access configuration (portal) of each type, with all its interface devices</td>\n<td>Auxiliary camera circuit equipment: equalizing amplifiers, video loss or presence detectors, terminators, ground loop correctors, surge suppressors, and other in-line devices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Cabling for all components</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The client requires a report with all tests and results.</li>\n<li>Every deficiency is resolved to the client’s satisfaction <strong>before</strong> installation and the acceptance test.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>15.4.2 Site Acceptance Testing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The client requires the contractor, integrator, or installer to provide a plan to: calibrate and test all components, verify the data transmission system, and install, commission, and test the system.</li>\n<li><strong>Before</strong> the on-site test, the contractor delivers a report with the results of functional tests, diagnostics, and calibrations.</li>\n<li>That report includes written certification that the entire system is calibrated, tested, and ready. It arrives <strong>at least 2 weeks before</strong> starting, with a copy of the approved procedures.</li>\n<li>With those procedures, the contractor demonstrates that the system meets all the physical and functional requirements of the contract.</li>\n<li>With performance tests it demonstrates reliability and operability at the specified throughput of each access point.</li>\n<li>It also demonstrates the specified <strong>Type I and Type II</strong> error rates. Outside the book: Type I = false rejection of an authorized person; Type II = false acceptance of an unauthorized person.</li>\n<li>It calculates the nuisance and false alarm rates: they must be below the specified maximums, at the specified probability of detection of each subsystem.</li>\n<li>It starts only with the client’s written approval. The client can end it as soon as the system fails.</li>\n<li>If approved, the contractor delivers reports and documentation before starting further tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Acceptance test checklist (14 items):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Data and video transmission system, and signal or control wiring: installed, tested, and approved.</li>\n<li>Remote control or monitoring stations, or remote switching panels: work, communicate with the monitoring center, and perform their functions.</li>\n<li>Video switcher fully functional and its software programmed for the site.</li>\n<li>All system software functions work well.</li>\n<li>Operate all electrical and mechanical controls and verify that they do what was designed.</li>\n<li>All video sources and outputs give a full-bandwidth signal.</li>\n<li>All input signals properly terminated.</li>\n<li>All cameras properly aimed and focused.</li>\n<li>Cameras facing the rising or setting sun aimed below the horizon, not seeing the sun directly.</li>\n<li>If vehicles travel near the assessment areas: verify nighttime assessment. Check whether headlights cause blooming (loss of detail from a spot that is too bright) or degrade the image.</li>\n<li>Cameras synchronized: the image does not roll when switching.</li>\n<li>Interface with the intrusion detection subsystem functional, with automatic camera call-up at all designated alarm points.</li>\n<li>With pan/tilt: limit stops properly adjusted and pan/tilt or zoom controls operational.</li>\n<li>With pre-position controls: home positions properly set and tested, including the auto home function.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>At the end, the contractor delivers another report of functional tests, diagnostics, and calibrations. It includes written certification that the system is ready for the <strong>reliability test</strong>, with a copy of the approved acceptance procedures.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>15.4.3 Reliability or Availability Testing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is advisable to do it in alternating phases of testing and evaluation, to validate the tests and the corrections.</li>\n<li>It does not begin until the client notifies in writing of three things: acceptance completed, training finished (if specified), and outstanding deficiencies corrected.</li>\n<li>The contractor provides <strong>a representative available 24 hours</strong>, including weekends and holidays if needed.</li>\n<li>The client ends the test when the system does not perform as specified.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n<th>Key rules</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase I Testing</td>\n<td><strong>24 hours a day, 15 consecutive calendar days</strong>, holidays included. The system must operate as specified</td>\n<td>No repairs except with the client’s written authorization. No failures: goes straight to Phase II with written permission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase I Assessment</td>\n<td>The contractor identifies, explains, and repairs all failures. Delivers a written report: nature of each failure, corrective action, results, and when to resume</td>\n<td>About <strong>7 days</strong> later, the client convenes a review meeting on site. The contractor demonstrates the corrections with parts of the acceptance test. The client sets a restart date or requires Phase I to be repeated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase II Testing</td>\n<td><strong>24 hours a day, 15 consecutive calendar days</strong>, holidays included</td>\n<td>No repairs except with the client’s written authorization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phase II Assessment</td>\n<td>Same failure report as in Phase I</td>\n<td>Meeting about 7 days later. Parts of the site acceptance test are repeated. The client sets a restart or requires Phase II to be repeated. Retesting does not occur without written notice from the client. After each retest, the Phase II assessment is repeated</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>If the Phase I retest comes out with no failures, it moves to Phase II with written permission. If not, the cycle of testing and assessment continues until completed.</li>\n<li>Minimum duration: 30 days of continuous testing (15 + 15).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says \"about a week\" for the review meeting; it is not an exact deadline.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>15.4.4 Post-Implementation Testing</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Example or detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational tests</td>\n<td>Periodic; they test that the system operates well. They do <strong>not</strong> verify equipment specifications</td>\n<td>Yes: alarm when a protected door opens, sensor that activates when walking, tamper or duress button that work. No: the sensor’s detection pattern or the exact opening distance before alarming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Performance tests</td>\n<td>Verify that the equipment meets its specifications; they thus demonstrate effectiveness</td>\n<td>Measure parameters such as the probability of detection. May require meters, calibrated instruments, or special methods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-maintenance tests</td>\n<td>Operational tests after preventive or corrective maintenance</td>\n<td>Confirm that the system works according to specifications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subsystem tests</td>\n<td>Confirm that large parts of the system work together as designed</td>\n<td>Detection with normal response; detection with delays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limited scope tests</td>\n<td>Divide a complex system into subsystems or segments that are tested separately</td>\n<td>Useful when testing everything at once is difficult and slow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation tests</td>\n<td>Periodic and <strong>independent</strong>; they validate the vulnerability analysis and that overall effectiveness is maintained</td>\n<td><strong>At least once a year</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the operational test proves that it \"works\"; the performance test measures \"how well it meets the specification\". The post-maintenance test is an operational test.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>15.4.5 Warranty Issues</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Coverage</td>\n<td>Repair, correct, or replace any defect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duration</td>\n<td><strong>12 months</strong> from issuance of the certificate of practical completion; that is where the owner’s acceptance begins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Usual arrival time</td>\n<td>On site within <strong>4 hours</strong> of the problem report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spare parts</td>\n<td>Enough inventory to repair or replace quickly. Waiting for the manufacturer is <strong>not acceptable</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maintenance</td>\n<td>The warranty includes full maintenance according to the manufacturer’s recommendations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contact</td>\n<td>The contractor gives the client the details for reporting problems and defects</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Service visit log:</strong> the contractor records them in a database and gives the client report forms with 5 fields:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Date and time of the failure report.</li>\n<li>Nature of the reported failure.</li>\n<li>Date and time of the visit.</li>\n<li>Actual failure found.</li>\n<li>Corrective work performed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Three questions about the warranty:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the service provided by the system vendor or by a third party?</li>\n<li>Are its service levels consistent with those of system maintenance?</li>\n<li>If something fails under warranty, what happens to the maintenance services provided by other parties?</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"physical-15-e4\">15.5 Training</h3><ul>\n<li>No technological or procedural precaution works if it is not executed well.</li>\n<li>With good training, personnel prevent incidents, respond well to those that occur, and help more in recovery.</li>\n<li>Without training, personnel tend to create security risks unintentionally.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>15.5.1 General Training Requirements</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The client asks the contractor or integrator for a proposal of operation and maintenance courses for designated personnel.</li>\n<li>It must cover all installed systems, system administration, incident response, and the system overview.</li>\n<li>If there is video along with other systems, its training takes place at the same time and within that of the others.</li>\n<li>Manuals and aids for each participant, plus several extra copies for filing at the project site.</li>\n<li>Each manual includes: agenda, objectives defined per lesson, and a detailed description of the content of each lesson.</li>\n<li>The contractor provides audiovisual equipment, materials, and supplies. Delivers a copy of the audiovisual material in the same medium used in class.</li>\n<li>The contractor recommends how many course days and how many hours per day.</li>\n<li>The client approves content and schedule <strong>at least 30 days before</strong> the training.</li>\n<li>Instructors: certified by the manufacturer of the applicable hardware and software, with experience at other installations and approved by the client.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>15.5.2 Training Topics</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Course</th>\n<th>Who it is for / focus</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n<th>When</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>System Administration</td>\n<td>Administrators. Define and apply operating parameters and make adjustments</td>\n<td>All functions: design and issuance of ID badges, enrollment of cardholders and access levels, door programming, alarm configuration, data and reports, database backups. With video: architecture and configuration, hardware specifications, diagnosis and correction of faults</td>\n<td>Class 1 completed about <strong>30 days before</strong> the factory test (if there is one) or the site test. Class 2, <strong>1 week before</strong> the acceptance test. They take part in the acceptance and reliability tests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System Monitoring</td>\n<td>Operators. Daily operation</td>\n<td>Monitor alarm events, personnel access, and door status; assess, respond to, and clear alarms and messages; run routine reports. Topics: see list of 7 below</td>\n<td><strong>Before</strong> the acceptance test. They take part in the acceptance and reliability tests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alarm Assessment and Dispatch</td>\n<td>Operators. Assess the cause of each alarm condition and handle it properly</td>\n<td>Based on the types of alarm the subsystems can give</td>\n<td>Prerequisite: client and contractor already have alarm assessment and response procedures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incident Response</td>\n<td>Security response force</td>\n<td>Based on the scenarios it may encounter when responding to an alarm</td>\n<td>Prerequisite: incident response procedures already developed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System Troubleshooting and Maintenance</td>\n<td>Usually given by the vendor or integrator; the client can also create or request it. Internal workings so trainees can repair most problems</td>\n<td>Communications and network diagnostics; device configuration and programming; controller installation, wiring, and diagnostics; software fault resolution. Topics: see list of 5 below</td>\n<td>On site, about <strong>2 weeks before</strong> the reliability test. The trainees take part in it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IT Functions</td>\n<td>IT personnel. How security works on the local or wide area network (LAN/WAN)</td>\n<td>Topologies and communications of each subsystem; bandwidth impact of functions such as digital video storage; maintaining data security</td>\n<td>The book does not state it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System Overview</td>\n<td>The book does not state the audience</td>\n<td>How the system supports security goals; how it was adapted to operational requirements; how to communicate security awareness to all employees</td>\n<td>The book does not state it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>System Monitoring: 7 topics</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>General configuration of the system hardware.</li>\n<li>Functional operation of the system.</li>\n<li>Operator commands.</li>\n<li>Database entry.</li>\n<li>Report generation.</li>\n<li>Alarm assessment.</li>\n<li>Simple diagnostics.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>The course includes the specific configuration of the installed subsystems and how to operate them.</li>\n<li>Outcome 1: with documentation, the operator performs elementary operations with guidance and describes the <strong>general</strong> hardware architecture.</li>\n<li>Outcome 2: the trainee starts up the system, operates it, recovers it after a failure, and describes the <strong>specific</strong> hardware architecture.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book gives two different outcomes for the same course (general with guidance vs. specific with recovery after failure).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>System Troubleshooting and Maintenance: 5 topics</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Physical layout of each piece of equipment.</li>\n<li>Diagnostic and fault resolution procedures.</li>\n<li>Repair instructions.</li>\n<li>Preventive maintenance procedures and schedules.</li>\n<li>Calibration procedures.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Timeline of training vs. tests</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Moment</th>\n<th>Milestone</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>≥ 30 days before the course</td>\n<td>Client approves content and schedule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\\~30 days before the factory or site test</td>\n<td>Administration class 1 ends</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Before the acceptance test</td>\n<td>Monitoring course</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 week before the acceptance test</td>\n<td>Administration class 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\\~2 weeks before the reliability test</td>\n<td>On-site maintenance course</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Before the reliability test</td>\n<td>Specified training completed (requirement of 15.4.3)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"physical-15-e5\">Key terms</h3><p>site preparation · design package · owner's representative · precursor work · customer-furnished equipment · integrator · NFPA 731 · authority having jurisdiction (AHJ) · fail safe · fail secure · electric strike · electromagnetic lock · end-of-line resistor · inrush current · holding current · inductive kickback · integrated bond sensor · request to exit (RTE) · passive infrared (PIR) · dual-technology detector · shock sensor · Power over Ethernet (PoE) · Augmented Category 6 · RG-59/U · RG-11/U · video guard tour · rigid galvanized steel conduit · electric metallic tubing (EMT) · NFPA 70 · cold galvanizing · system startup · line supervision · UL 1076 · NEMA 250 Type 12 · NEMA 250 Type 4X · tamper switch · keyed alike · key control plan · TIA-568 · nuisance alarm · shunt · equipment performance test · personnel performance test · commissioning · factory acceptance test · site acceptance test · Type I / Type II error · throughput · blooming · reliability or availability test · operational test · performance test · post-maintenance test · subsystem test · limited scope test · evaluation test · certificate of practical completion · system administration · system monitoring · alarm assessment and dispatch · incident response</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"15.1 Site Preparation"},{"id":"e1","t":"15.2 Contractor Coordination"},{"id":"e2","t":"15.3 Installation"},{"id":"e3","t":"15.4 Testing and Warranty Issues"},{"id":"e4","t":"15.5 Training"},{"id":"e5","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"15.1 Site Preparation","h":"the book calls site preparation \"the most important step\" of the installation."},{"s":"15.3 Installation","h":"strike = maximum 500 ms; electromagnet = maximum 300 ms. The 1 A inrush current is given only for the strike."},{"s":"15.3 Installation","h":"\"keyed alike\" is recommended, but site-specific, never the factory one."},{"s":"15.3 Installation","h":"here the book asks for category 5 for LAN, but for video it requires augmented CAT 6 (15.3.2). They are not the same."},{"s":"15.4 Testing and Warranty Issues","h":"it is not enough for a component to meet the manufacturer’s standard if it proves ineffective in the test."},{"s":"15.4 Testing and Warranty Issues","h":"the book says \"about a week\" for the review meeting; it is not an exact deadline."},{"s":"15.4 Testing and Warranty Issues","h":"the operational test proves that it \"works\"; the performance test measures \"how well it meets the specification\". The post-maintenance test is an operational test."},{"s":"15.5 Training","h":"the book gives two different outcomes for the same course (general with guidance vs. specific with recovery after failure)."}]},"physical-16":{"html":"<p><strong>Starting points (chapter introduction)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typical case: sophisticated systems (critical infrastructure, religious destinations, shopping centers, or government centers) that degrade without maintenance after being installed.</li>\n<li>Cause: the installer came from far away and has already left; local resources are insufficient or are not even used.</li>\n<li>It also happens in developed countries: maintenance is ignored at purchase and afterward.</li>\n<li>Relying on on-call repairs leads to the premature death of the system through deferred maintenance.</li>\n<li>Every system needs regular maintenance; planning for it is an essential part of design.</li>\n<li>Support priority: the equipment whose failure has the greatest impact on the overall system.</li>\n<li>Choose systems according to the support the local market can provide after installation.</li>\n<li>If no local vendor can: allocate a maintenance budget, include spare parts in the original program, and hire a dedicated maintenance technician.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-16-e0\">16.1 Maintenance</h3><ul>\n<li>Greater dependence on PPS (physical protection systems), with more scale and complexity, requires careful maintenance planning.</li>\n<li>Software is never free of errors. Hardware is not immune to electrical or mechanical failures.</li>\n<li>The security investment must include maintenance services and a plan to reduce the probability and impact of failures.</li>\n<li>Effective program: the facility's own technicians (facility technicians), supported by contractor representatives, perform all testing, maintenance, calibration, and repairs.</li>\n<li>Signs of a poor maintenance program:<ul>\n<li>frequent system failures</li>\n<li>superficial testing procedures (cursory testing)</li>\n<li>an excessive number of components awaiting repair</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>When negotiating maintenance contracts, seek legal advice when needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two types of PPS maintenance</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>What it includes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Remedial maintenance or service contracts</td>\n<td>Corrects failures and returns the system to operation when a hardware or software component fails</td>\n<td>1) A maintenance function that receives and logs user requests when there is a problem; 2) investigate the problem; 3) resolve it directly or arrange for the fix if a third party is required; 4) restore the system or return its use to the customer; 5) update the documentation on the problem and its solution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preventive maintenance</td>\n<td>Scheduled maintenance to keep hardware and software in good condition</td>\n<td>1) Keep electromechanical equipment in good condition (fans, filters, backup batteries, door hardware; the book leaves the list open); 2) replace hardware components to maintain current specifications (e.g., engineering changes); 3) update system and application software (bug fixes, new releases); 4) test and analyze system reports (error logs, self-tests, parameters, performance measures); 5) maintain the system documentation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A system maintenance agreement normally includes <strong>both</strong> types.</li>\n<li>All components must work together. The failure of one may not matter or may bring down the whole system.</li>\n<li>Therefore the agreement must address noncritical problems as well as those that cause major business disruption.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Separate contracts vs. a single contractor</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Former practice: contract separately for hardware, software, networks, and services</td>\n<td>With complex, integrated systems it is harder to identify the failure. There is finger-pointing and time is lost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Often the best solution: a single contractor/integrator as the single point of contact</td>\n<td>Diagnoses the cause and manages the solution. May involve third parties: component vendors, telecommunications, or application software providers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>16.1.1 Remedial Maintenance</h4>\n<p><strong>Maintenance Plan</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It can be carried out by: the manufacturer, a systems integrator, a maintenance contractor, the users, or any combination.</li>\n<li>Who is responsible for each task must be set in writing:<ol>\n<li>fault identification</li>\n<li>diagnose and verify the problem</li>\n<li>correct the fault</li>\n<li>test the repair</li>\n<li>repair logging</li>\n<li>coordinate and follow up on maintenance</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Coordination is the most critical item: each technology may require different skills depending on where the failure occurs.</li>\n<li>It is advisable to train staff in preventive maintenance: this way they understand and operate the system better.</li>\n<li>Ideally, the vendor provides this training, the factory acceptance tests, and the operating procedures during purchase and installation.</li>\n<li>Give technicians time to update their skills by exchanging knowledge with other technicians during installation.</li>\n<li>The plan must provide for periodic tuning of the system to each installation. Objective: eliminate nuisance alarms and false alarms that affect those who monitor and respond.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>When contracting for maintenance, the client and contractor must:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Agree on the basis of the contract document.</li>\n<li>Document in detail the system components that will be maintained.</li>\n<li>Establish the service level agreement (SLA): response time.</li>\n<li>Set the service levels for each component or subsystem.</li>\n<li>Define the roles and responsibilities of the parties.</li>\n<li>Agree on prices and payments.</li>\n<li>Define how the agreement will be managed and administered.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Service Levels</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each failure has a different impact. Example: a camera failing matters less than the communications server for the entire network failing.</li>\n<li>But a workstation may support an essential service and require high priority.</li>\n<li>Client and contractor jointly develop the support plan, service level, and response time for each component.</li>\n<li>Rule: the greater the impact of the failure, the higher the level of support.</li>\n<li>Extreme case: an outstanding engineer on site with all the spare parts at hand.</li>\n<li>Most likely: an immediate callback from a technician and arrival on site within <strong>2 to 4 hours</strong>.</li>\n<li>Levels must be realistic, measurable, and consistent with the business, especially if they involve travel.</li>\n<li>Guaranteeing a response in <strong>under 4 hours</strong> drives costs up quickly (staff hours, travel, and equipment).</li>\n<li>Components can be left out of the full plan. Example: PCs already covered by the computer vendor's contract. Excluding or lowering the level requires careful thought.</li>\n<li>Levels and costs depend on:<ul>\n<li>the distance between the system and the vendor (one in another city greatly lengthens the response)</li>\n<li>the ability to diagnose and repair remotely</li>\n<li>whether support outside working hours is required</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Roles and Responsibilities</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Main goal of the agreement: for the system to operate at optimum capacity with minimum downtime.</li>\n<li>Another goal: minimize the number of parties managing the program.</li>\n<li>The roles of all parties are clearly defined, documented, and agreed with the maintenance provider.</li>\n<li>Sometimes the maintenance provider is the same as the hardware and software vendor, or its agent. The usual arrangement: the integrator assumes maintenance as the prime contractor.</li>\n<li>The plan must: set the responsibilities of everyone, establish the organization's central point of contact, and facilitate agreements among the parties.</li>\n<li>Parties that may be involved:<ol>\n<li>manufacturer or hardware vendor</li>\n<li>systems integrator</li>\n<li>provider of system tools and utilities (if not supplied by the hardware vendor)</li>\n<li>application software vendor</li>\n<li>building owner (power, water, and telephone/data circuits)</li>\n<li>air conditioning provider (for the monitoring room equipment)</li>\n<li>agent for the UPS (uninterruptible power supply) or emergency generator</li>\n<li>LAN and WAN (local area network and wide area network) equipment and service provider</li>\n<li>telecommunications provider (telephones, leased data circuits, access to private and public networks)</li>\n<li>PC vendor</li>\n<li>cabling provider</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Ideally, the main vendor or the integrator manages all of them when resolving failures or performing scheduled maintenance.</li>\n<li>In practice this is not always appropriate or cost-effective. Each client includes or excludes third-party responsibilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Prices and Payments</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Factors that set the contractor's rate:<ol>\n<li>complexity of the system</li>\n<li>cost of spare parts</li>\n<li>estimated number of failures per year</li>\n<li>frequency of product use</li>\n<li>number of system users</li>\n<li>age of the system</li>\n<li>geographic location and the possibility of online diagnosis and support</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Standard or high-volume systems: the rate may be a fixed percentage of the purchase price.</li>\n<li>Alternative: bill travel and lodging separately.</li>\n<li>Economies of scale: more units or customers in an area allow savings in travel, spare parts inventory, staff, training, and setup costs.</li>\n<li>A possible payment cycle: a fixed fee for an advance period (month, quarter, or year) plus an allowance or formula for:<ol>\n<li>discounts for longer-term contracts</li>\n<li>credits if target response times are not met</li>\n<li>extra costs for travel, lodging, or work not covered</li>\n<li>service calls outside the agreed hours (call-outs)</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Over time, most factors increase (inflation, product aging). Some decrease.</li>\n<li>The usual practice: price review milestones in the contract, with a prior understanding of the size of the increases.</li>\n<li>Typically not covered: misuse, vandalism, lack of training due to staff turnover, and acts of God. The book leaves the list open.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Administration</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The environment changes during the contract. The agreement must be monitored and updated.</li>\n<li>The security manager reviews the agreement regularly, measures the vendor, and reviews the scope. The review covers:<ol>\n<li>vendor performance against the service levels, and system performance, in the prior period</li>\n<li>call logging and account management</li>\n<li>changes to services or levels requested by the client or recommended by the vendor</li>\n<li>changes to the system's equipment or software list</li>\n<li>the client's future plans (staffing, new developments, upgrades, special events, changes in priorities)</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Documentation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The manufacturer or integrator delivers complete configuration documentation: switch settings, wiring diagrams, spare parts lists, and installation steps.</li>\n<li>All subsystems must have advanced diagnostics that identify defective components so they can be replaced in the field.</li>\n<li>In large, decentralized systems, remote diagnostics help a great deal.</li>\n<li>Subscribing to a hardware and software upgrade service guarantees incorporating the latest engineering change orders and field change orders.</li>\n<li>Those orders can affect the budget and fall outside the scope. The project manager reviews them and approves them if they extend the life of the system.</li>\n<li>All documentation is protected and controlled so as not to disclose the countermeasures installed: the SOW (statement of work), contracts, work orders, diagrams, shop drawings showing sensors, control equipment, power supplies and junction boxes, and cable routes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Records</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep accurate records, especially of maintenance and operators. They serve to:<ul>\n<li>know which parts fail or cause problems for operators (weak points and deficiencies)</li>\n<li>keep track of costs that justify replacing unreliable systems</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Records receive the same security controls as the system documentation.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Record</th>\n<th>Content and use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Maintenance records</td>\n<td>For all components, cross-referenced with subsystems, to see repair patterns. They indicate what to inspect closely during preventive maintenance. Whoever maintains the system records each task and builds cumulative chronological records by major component and for the complete system. A running log per device with calibration, repair, and programming data. Available for on-site inspection, as proof of planned and systematic adjustments and repairs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System operator records</td>\n<td>Operators' problems with certain subsystems or components. They are analyzed periodically to detect problem subsystems and update the operating procedures</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Spare Parts</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Buy spare parts and repair equipment in advance (perhaps with the original purchase) to reduce downtime.</li>\n<li>This is critical in countries with customs delays.</li>\n<li>The right quantity depends on: the time needed to obtain them, the cost of holding inventory, and the probability of replacement.</li>\n<li>Rule of thumb: <strong>about 5% of the equipment capital cost of a site, every year</strong>, to buy spare parts.</li>\n<li>Inventory must follow the vendor's recommendations.</li>\n<li>Standardizing devices reduces inventory and training. Ways: a sole-source relationship with a vendor, if requirements and the market are known, or strict purchasing specifications.</li>\n<li>A centralized budget for unexpected device replacements is recommended.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Maintenance Manuals</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The contractor delivers a maintenance manual for all the equipment covering: inspection, periodic preventive maintenance, fault diagnosis, and repair or replacement of defective components.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>16.1.2 Preventive Maintenance</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Develop checklists that incorporate the manufacturers' guidelines.</li>\n<li>Applies to almost all of the infrastructure. Examples: replacing batteries and bulbs, cleaning camera lenses and housings.</li>\n<li>The budget and resources must consider security technicians <strong>and</strong> IT (information technology) support.</li>\n<li>To save trips: perform preventive maintenance together with remedial maintenance whenever possible.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Typical preventive maintenance tasks</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Check cabinets: equipment (e.g., power supplies) must have voltage warning labels.</li>\n<li>Verify that the security system's warning labels, if any, are in place.</li>\n<li>Check enclosures: damage, unauthorized openings, and corrosion of metal parts. Repair and paint.</li>\n<li>Check the air passages and remove obstructions.</li>\n<li>Check, investigate, and resolve unusual odors.</li>\n<li>Check the lock devices. Repair.</li>\n<li>When operating and testing the equipment: listen for, investigate, and resolve unusual noises.</li>\n<li>Check that the equipment mounting is correct.</li>\n<li>Look for loose cables and components.</li>\n<li>Check electrical connections for discoloration or corrosion. Repair.</li>\n<li>Check the electrical insulation for discoloration and degradation. Repair.</li>\n<li>Check the grounding (conductors and connections). Repair.</li>\n<li>Clean the equipment: remove debris, dust, and other deposits from non-encapsulated components (e.g., ventilated control panels).</li>\n<li>Tighten the electrical connections.</li>\n<li>Give all electrical connections the design torque.</li>\n<li>Perform periodic operational tests to prove the subsystem works (not necessarily to verify specifications).</li>\n<li>Open protected doors.</li>\n<li>Enter protected rooms.</li>\n<li>Test metal detectors by passing metal through the detection zone.</li>\n<li>Test fence disturbance sensors by shaking the fence.</li>\n<li>Visually and operationally check the video system: switchers, peripherals, interface panels, recorders, monitors, electrical and mechanical controls, and the image quality of each camera.</li>\n<li>Test duress buttons and tamper switches.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Adjustments</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Periodic adjustments may be needed for the system to operate effectively.</li>\n<li>Example: detection patterns of motion sensors, according to test results.</li>\n<li>Example: camera varifocal lenses, to view the correct scene.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Backup Equipment</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subsystems require power: there must be an auxiliary source of batteries or generators.</li>\n<li>Switchover must be immediate and automatic if the main source fails.</li>\n<li>With a generator as the only backup, there is almost never an immediate switchover. Batteries are required; the generator takes the load when it reaches full power.</li>\n<li>Regular testing and maintenance program: periodic tests of equipment and circuits (including the backup) and a thorough inspection by qualified personnel.</li>\n<li>A record of each test: date, name of the person who performed it, and results.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the generator does not provide instant switchover; batteries cover the gap until the generator delivers full power.</p><h3 id=\"physical-16-e1\">16.2 Evaluation</h3><ul>\n<li>When the system's useful life ends, the replacement process begins.</li>\n<li>Factors to justify the cost of replacement:<ol>\n<li>maintenance cost</li>\n<li>lack of spare parts</li>\n<li>hardware and software obsolescence</li>\n<li>operating costs</li>\n<li>poor reliability</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Also justifying it: new technologies with better security, staff reductions, or other benefits. This often occurs long after the equipment has been fully amortized.</li>\n<li>The security manager forms a team of stakeholders, including IT, to choose a system that meets everyone's needs.</li>\n<li>Address the deficiencies of the old system. Examples: it does not read several card technologies; slow response time.</li>\n<li>Incorporate future uses of the system and of the credentials. Example: debit card function in the cafeteria.</li>\n<li>Provide ample capacity for growth: more sites, panels, and cards.</li>\n<li>Gather information from reputable companies backed by a national network of integrators.</li>\n<li>The software must pass the screening of IT (which will operate it) and Human Resources (a smooth interface between its employee database and the security software).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-16-e2\">16.3 Replacement</h3><ul>\n<li>Optimal: replacement is in the budget, just like that of PCs and laptops. Security equipment also has a useful life.</li>\n<li>Technology advances quickly: equipment only a few years old becomes obsolete at an accelerated rate.</li>\n<li>Getting the most from the investment requires anticipating the trajectory of technology and the needs of the organization.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trends cited by the book (2021)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>From</th>\n<th>Toward</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Video</td>\n<td>Analog cameras and systems (disappearing)</td>\n<td>Digital: moves from the early adopters phase to generally accepted use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alarms / access control</td>\n<td>Super-proprietary hardware</td>\n<td>IT-centered design with open standards; multiple credentials on a single card (multi-credentialing); more biometrics; easier integration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitoring (the deepest change)</td>\n<td>Proprietary software with visibility into a single system (alarm/access or video)</td>\n<td>PSIM (physical security information management)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>PSIM</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It connects to many types of sensors, not just security ones.</li>\n<li>It gives the console operator and the incident decision maker real understanding of the situation.</li>\n<li>Situational awareness PSIM: brings together alarms, video, two-way audio, maps, and response resources into a coherent picture of the incident.</li>\n<li>With growing or simultaneous incidents, it helps determine whether they are related (e.g., a moving shooter) or separate (e.g., a small fire and a heart attack in different areas).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Small systems</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digital video usually gives better quality than analog, especially when recorded.</li>\n<li>They may not need to be integrated, but when choosing technology the ability to integrate them as the organization grows must not be ruled out.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-16-e3\">Key terms</h3><p>remedial maintenance · preventive maintenance · deferred maintenance · on-call repairs · system maintenance agreement · service contract · single point of contact · systems integrator · prime contractor · finger-pointing · service level agreement (SLA) · response time · downtime · factory acceptance test · tuning · nuisance alarm · false alarm · call-out · economies of scale · acts of God · call logging · remote diagnostics · upgrade service · engineering change order · field change order · statement of work (SOW) · shop drawings · maintenance records · system operator records · spare parts · sole-source · maintenance manual · checklist · fence disturbance sensor · duress button · tamper switch · varifocal lens · backup power · switchover · uninterruptible power supply (UPS) · stakeholders · obsolescence · open standards · multi-credentialing · biometrics · physical security information management (PSIM) · situational awareness</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"16.1 Maintenance"},{"id":"e1","t":"16.2 Evaluation"},{"id":"e2","t":"16.3 Replacement"},{"id":"e3","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"16.1 Maintenance","h":"the generator does not provide instant switchover; batteries cover the gap until the generator delivers full power."}]},"physical-A":{"html":"<p>The book warns that many terms mean different things to different people. These are the definitions as the book uses them and, in general, as the professional security community uses them. There are 60 terms, in the order of the book.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>access control</td>\n<td>Control of people, vehicles, and materials through security measures applied to a protected area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>alarm system</td>\n<td>A set of sensors, controls, and annunciators arranged to detect and report an intrusion or other emergency. Annunciator: a device that signals with sound, light, or other means.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>asset</td>\n<td>Anything tangible or intangible that has value to a person or organization: people, property, and information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>barrier</td>\n<td>A natural or man-made obstacle to the movement or direction of people, animals, vehicles, or materials.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bollard</td>\n<td>Vehicle barrier: a vertical cylinder (almost always concrete-filled steel) anchored in a deep foundation. It stops vehicles and lets pedestrians and bicycles pass. It can take ornamental caps or aluminum, iron, or bronze sleeves over the impact tube (crash tube).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>building envelope</td>\n<td>The separation between the interior and exterior of the building. It is the shell that protects the interior environment and facilitates its climate control. Its design is an architecture and engineering specialty based on building science.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>camera</td>\n<td>A device that captures still or moving images. In security, it is part of a video surveillance system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CCT rating</td>\n<td>CCT (Corrected Color Temperature): measures how warm or cool a light is, in kelvin. The book says: sunlight = 5,000 K; above that, warmer (yellowish); below that, cooler (bluish). <strong>Exam note:</strong> this is backwards; the higher the kelvin, the cooler or bluer the light, and the lower, the warmer (outside the book). The usual name is Correlated Color Temperature (outside the book).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>closed-circuit television (CCTV)</td>\n<td>See video surveillance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>contract security service</td>\n<td>A company that provides security services to another entity in exchange for payment; typically, security officers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED)</td>\n<td>Pronounced “sep-ted.” Reduces crime and the fear of crime through the good design and use of the built environment. Uses organizational, mechanical, and natural methods to control access, improve natural surveillance, and foster territoriality, supporting legitimate activity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>crime</td>\n<td>An act or omission that violates a law that prohibits or orders it, and that may be punished with penalties.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>defense-in-depth (layered security)</td>\n<td>A strategy of forming layers of protection for an asset.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>denial</td>\n<td>Frustrating an adversary's attempt to carry out conduct that would be a security incident.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>design basis threat (DBT)</td>\n<td>The threat against which the asset is protected and on which the protection system is designed. It is the baseline type and size of threat that the building must withstand. It includes the aggressors' tactics and their tools, weapons, and explosives.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>detection</td>\n<td>Discovering an attempt, successful or not, to breach a physical or logical secure perimeter. Physical examples: climbing a fence, opening a locked window, entering an area without authorization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>duress alarm</td>\n<td>A device that lets a person under coercion call for help without raising suspicion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>event</td>\n<td>A notable occurrence; typically a security incident, an alarm, a medical emergency, or something similar.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>facility</td>\n<td>One or more buildings or structures and parking areas, related by function and location, that form an operating entity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>intrusion detection system</td>\n<td>A system with one or more sensors that detects an imminent or actual breach (physical or logical). It initiates an alarm, a notification, or a response.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lamp</td>\n<td>A manufactured light source: filament or arc tube, its glass envelope, and its electrical connectors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>light level (intensity, illuminance)</td>\n<td>The concentration of light on an area. Measured in lux (lumens per square meter) or foot-candles (lumens per square foot).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lighting</td>\n<td>The degree of illumination. Also, the interior or exterior equipment to increase it (measured in lux or foot-candles).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lock</td>\n<td>Equipment that prevents an opening from being opened without authorization (gate, window, building door, vault door; open list) and lets authorized users open it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>luminaire (fixture)</td>\n<td>The complete lighting unit: lamp, lampholder, and the reflectors and diffusers that distribute and focus the light.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>metrics</td>\n<td>Measures with a reference and at least two points (e.g., quantity over time) that give insight into performance, operation, or quality.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>perimeter protection</td>\n<td>Safeguarding a boundary or border, physical or logical.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>physical security</td>\n<td>The part of security that uses physical measures to safeguard, protect, or prevent unauthorized access to assets. Assets: facilities, equipment, people, intangibles, data, and other property.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>physical security assessment</td>\n<td>See security survey.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>physical security information management (PSIM)</td>\n<td>A software platform that integrates unconnected security applications and devices (video, access control, sensors, analytics, networks, building systems; open list) and controls them from a single comprehensive interface. It collects and correlates events from disparate systems to identify and resolve situations proactively.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>physical security measure</td>\n<td>A tangible device, system, or practice to protect people and prevent harm, loss, or unauthorized access to assets.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>policy</td>\n<td>A general statement of a principle according to which the organization carries out its business functions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>private security</td>\n<td>Nongovernmental, private-sector practice: protecting people, property, and information, investigating, and safeguarding assets. It is performed by an internal department (proprietary security) or an external provider (contract security).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>private security officer</td>\n<td>A person, uniformed or plainclothes, employed by an organization to protect assets. Formerly often called a “guard.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>procedure</td>\n<td>Detailed instructions for applying policies (security, organizational, or other) or a business practice of a specific function.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>progressive collapse</td>\n<td>The failure of a main structural element causes adjacent ones to fail, and those cause others to fail. The damage advances until a partial or total collapse of the building.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>proprietary security organization</td>\n<td>Typically, a department of the company that provides security services to the company itself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>risk</td>\n<td>The probability or potential that a threat exploits vulnerabilities and causes loss or harm to an asset.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>risk assessment</td>\n<td>A systematic process: identify and value assets, list credible threats, document vulnerabilities, describe the impacts of a loss event, and analyze the risks qualitatively or quantitatively. They are reported by priority or severity, with a risk level.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>risk management</td>\n<td>A business discipline that identifies, analyzes, and communicates risk. It then accepts, avoids, transfers, or controls it to an acceptable level, weighing costs and benefits.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>risk profile</td>\n<td>A description or representation of the risks of an asset, system, geographic area, or other entity, considering situational and influencing factors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>security incident</td>\n<td>An unwanted occurrence or action likely to affect assets.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>security measure</td>\n<td>A practice or device to protect people and prevent harm, loss, or unauthorized access to equipment, facilities, material, and information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>security officer</td>\n<td>A person, uniformed or plainclothes, employed to protect assets.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>security survey (physical security assessment)</td>\n<td>A critical on-site examination of a building or facility. It seeks to learn its security status, find deficiencies or excesses, set the necessary protection, and recommend improvements. Data: interviews, observation, and document review.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sensor</td>\n<td>An electronic device that measures a physical quantity (temperature, pressure, or sound intensity) and converts it into an electronic signal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>site hardening</td>\n<td>Applying reinforcement measures so that a site is harder to penetrate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>standoff distance (setback)</td>\n<td>The distance between the asset and the threat, typically against an explosive threat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>surveillance</td>\n<td>Observation of a place, an activity, or a person.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tailgating (piggybacking)</td>\n<td>Closely following a person or vehicle. In access control: attempting to enter a controlled area right behind someone authorized.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>threat</td>\n<td>Any entity or action that can cause a loss event, adverse impact, or other harm. It includes threat actors (e.g., a terrorist) and threat actions (e.g., a bombing).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>threat vector</td>\n<td>Any path for carrying out a threat action: physical route, sequence of events, or logical path (through cyber infrastructure).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>throughput</td>\n<td>The average rate of flow of people, vehicles, objects, or data through an access point, system, or process.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>token</td>\n<td>An electronically encoded device (smart card, key fob, USB device; open list) with data to authenticate the user's identity. Used in access control to facilities or to IT equipment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>uninterruptible power supply (UPS)</td>\n<td>A system that provides continuous power to an alternating current (AC) line within tolerances. It protects against overvoltage, loss of main power, and intermittent dips (brownouts). Often used with an emergency generator.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vault</td>\n<td>A specially constructed room or area that limits access and protects assets (e.g., against forced entry or fire).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>video analytics</td>\n<td>Computer processing of surveillance video for automatic tasks: detecting events instantly, recognizing activities, searching for items (faces, license plates, objects), or extracting data from recorded video.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>video management system (VMS)</td>\n<td>Software that manages video surveillance system functions: network storage, streaming, camera control, alerts, and IP (Internet protocol) interface.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>video surveillance</td>\n<td>A system that captures images and transmits them, typically to monitors, recorders, and control equipment. It includes CCTV and network video systems.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vulnerability</td>\n<td>A weakness, condition, or organizational practice that makes it easier or possible for a threat (intentional, natural, or inadvertent) to cause harm, or that enlarges a loss event.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book places CPTED before “crime,” out of alphabetical order. “Private security officer” and “security officer” are almost the same definition.</p>","toc":[],"ojo":[{"s":"Key Terms and Definitions","h":"the book places CPTED before “crime,” out of alphabetical order. “Private security officer” and “security officer” are almost the same definition."}]},"physical-B":{"html":"<h3 id=\"physical-B-e0\">What is a high-rise structure?</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> a structure taller than the maximum reach of the available fire-fighting equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>Absolute figure:</strong> varies between <strong>75 ft (23 m) and 100 ft (30 m)</strong>, about <strong>7 to 10 floors</strong>.<ul>\n<li>It depends on the slab-to-slab height between floors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The exact height is set by <strong>local building and fire codes</strong> (Craighead, 2003).</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> above that height, firefighters fight the fire <strong>from inside</strong> the building, not from outside with hoses and ladders.</li>\n<li><strong>Origin:</strong> the first high-rises in the <strong>United States, in the late 19th century</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumption of the appendix:</strong> an <strong>office</strong> building, urban or metropolitan, with direct access from one or more public streets.<ul>\n<li><strong>Mixed occupancy:</strong> public assembly areas, retail, and conventional offices.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> NFPA 101 (Life Safety Code) and IBC (International Building Code) use <strong>75 ft (23 m)</strong>. It is measured from the lowest level of fire department vehicle access to the highest occupiable floor.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-B-e1\">Life Safety Considerations</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Risk:</strong> the possibility of loss from a threat, security incident, or event (ASIS, 2004, <em>General Security Risk Assessment Guideline</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Depends on:</strong> the size of the facility, the number of occupants and, for intentional threats, the value of the target.</li>\n<li><strong>Why risk is greater in a high-rise:</strong><ul>\n<li>Very large occupied floors.</li>\n<li>Difficulty responding to and containing threats.</li>\n<li>The whole building cannot be evacuated immediately: stairs and elevators have limited capacity.</li>\n<li>In fires, the elevators on the affected floors are usually not a safe escape route.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Mitigation depends on</strong> structural design and technology to:<ul>\n<li>Deter and detect the threat.</li>\n<li>Communicate its nature and location.</li>\n<li>Initiate automatic or organizational responses.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Life safety is planned like a security program: address the assets at risk and follow <strong>deter, detect, delay, respond</strong>.</li>\n<li>Life safety is mandated mainly by building and fire <strong>codes</strong>.<ul>\n<li>They are <strong>minimum</strong> protection standards.</li>\n<li>Interpreting them usually requires a code expert and clarification from the local authority that enforces them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Key conflict:</strong> life safety requires unimpeded egress. Security does not want a thief with stolen goods to escape freely.<ul>\n<li>Solution: creative access control measures that comply with the codes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Most critical threats to life</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fire, explosion, and contamination of life support systems</strong> (air and drinking water).</li>\n<li>They can be accidental or intentional.</li>\n<li>They spread quickly and can reach catastrophic levels.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Most significant high-rise life safety factors (4)</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Early detection and precise location of incipient hazards.</li>\n<li>Reliable communications throughout the building and with outside agencies.</li>\n<li>Guaranteed safe escape routes.</li>\n<li>Rapid application of control measures:\n   - Fire extinguishment.\n   - Containment or replacement of contaminated air.\n   - Shutoff or filtering of drinking water.\n   - Containment and removal of explosives.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Why fire receives special attention</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Buildings contain a lot of combustible material.</li>\n<li>Constant risk that fire will rise, especially <strong>without an automatic sprinkler system</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>In a fire, the first 3 to 4 minutes are critical</strong> (Craighead, 2003).</li>\n<li>Difference from other emergencies (winter storm, civil disturbance): there the initial reaction does not necessarily determine the impact. In a fire, it does.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Considering Building Vulnerabilities</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Fire or explosion threaten <strong>structural integrity</strong> and essential services: lighting, communications, elevators, and escalators.</li>\n<li>Threats can spread upward, downward, or horizontally.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fire direction</th>\n<th>Cause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Upward</td>\n<td>The natural tendency of hot gases to rise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Downward</td>\n<td>Combustibles that follow a downward path (wall and floor coverings)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Horizontal</td>\n<td>Within the floor, especially without floor-to-ceiling walls or partitions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All three at once</td>\n<td>Large conflagrations without containment or early suppression</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Explosions or sustained conflagrations: partial or total collapse, with multiple injuries and deaths.</li>\n<li><strong>Airborne contaminants</strong> (smoke, chemical or biological agents):<ul>\n<li>They travel with the air from the point of entry, according to its distribution pattern.</li>\n<li>With return and recirculation they are redistributed again and again, unless they are filtered or the system is shut off.</li>\n<li>If an air-handling unit serves several floors, it contaminates all those floors.</li>\n<li>Depending on the system, they rise, descend, or move horizontally on each floor.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Water contaminants:</strong><ul>\n<li>They move with the water from the point of introduction.</li>\n<li>Attacking is difficult: any opening in a charged pipe causes a leak.</li>\n<li>But if a valve is closed, the contaminant is introduced downstream and travels when it is reopened.</li>\n<li>If that point is near the start of the network, a large part of the system is contaminated.</li>\n<li>Dissolved gases and solids reach sinks, drinking fountains, and toilets.</li>\n<li>Heavy contaminants accumulate in traps and pipes, but can be released at the outlets.</li>\n<li>A small quantity of a highly lethal agent has a wide effect: the water continues to be used before it is detected.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Communications:</strong><ul>\n<li>They rise through common vertical risers and are distributed on each floor from service ports.</li>\n<li>A complete cut of the cable stops service downstream and can affect upstream by interrupting the direct path.</li>\n<li>Their interruption mainly affects emergency communications.</li>\n<li><strong>Lesson:</strong> distribute alarms and communications so that local points operate on their own if the central panel or processor is lost.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Threat of Bombing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>An occupant, visitor, or delivery person can bring in explosives.</li>\n<li><strong>Screening:</strong> explosives detectors, X-ray machines, and metal detectors.</li>\n<li><strong>Strict vehicle control:</strong> reduces explosives in garages under the building or underground, and in loading docks.</li>\n<li><strong>Limit:</strong> in a dense urban area it is difficult to prevent a car bomb parked next to the building.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Building</th>\n<th>Measures</th>\n<th>Limit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td>Design to withstand a defined explosive load without progressive failure; maximize the setback distance; window design</td>\n<td>The cost must be justified by the probability of the event</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Existing</td>\n<td>Gain distance with heavy planters, bollards, other barriers, and parking restrictions; security film on windows against shards</td>\n<td>Structural solutions may be prohibitively costly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>4 questions to assess bomb risk:</strong><ol>\n<li>Is the building a likely target (e.g., a landmark that attracts attention)?</li>\n<li>Is any tenant a particular target of domestic or international terrorists?</li>\n<li>Is it an easy or soft target because of poor security?</li>\n<li>Is any nearby building a likely target (risk of collateral damage)?</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Although the probability is usually minimal, emergency plans and evacuation preparedness must be <strong>up to date and rehearsed</strong>.</li>\n<li>This weighs more in a high-rise than in a low building:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Single-story building</th>\n<th>High floor of a high-rise</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Quick exit via several horizontal routes</td>\n<td>The same horizontal routes <strong>plus</strong> vertical descent by elevators (if available and safe) and stairs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No vertical congestion</td>\n<td>Congested stairs in a full evacuation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low physical effort</td>\n<td>Going down many flights tires even people with full mobility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple access for people with disabilities</td>\n<td>Almost impossible without help from others or mechanical devices (evacuation chairs)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Dealing with the Life Safety Problem</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Many life safety countermeasures also protect assets. Anything that helps respond well adds to total protection.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk:</strong> an apparent emergency may be a <strong>diversion</strong> to attack assets.</li>\n<li><strong>Illustrative case:</strong><ul>\n<li>Stairwells locked from the inside (no reentry to the floor) that unlock with the fire alarm.</li>\n<li>An intruder activates a manual fire alarm station and enters the chosen floor by the stairwell.</li>\n<li>The occupants attend to the emergency, not the assets.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lessons:</strong><ul>\n<li>The system <strong>must</strong> unlock the doors in an emergency.</li>\n<li><strong>Life safety takes priority</strong>, unless it is clearly known to be a hoax.</li>\n<li>Do not rely <strong>only</strong> on locked stairwell doors if they have automatic unlocking; add protection (see Building Stairwells).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Local Code Compliance</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First step</strong> for life safety: comply with local building and fire codes.</li>\n<li>An old building that complied when built may not reflect the best approach today. It may require changes to comply with current requirements.</li>\n<li>Meeting or exceeding the code can offset its cost:<ul>\n<li>Lower commercial insurance premiums.</li>\n<li>Lower amounts in reserves for retained risks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Fire Detection</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It must be the first priority.</strong></li>\n<li>More precisely: detection of <strong>products of combustion</strong> or of the <strong>combustion process</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is the first step of every response: confinement → extinguishment → evacuation and escape.</li>\n<li>It must indicate precisely <strong>where</strong> the fire is and <strong>what stage</strong> it has reached.</li>\n<li>Codes require manual alarm stations (pull stations) on every floor, on the normal exit route. <strong>This is not enough.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> fire on the 4th floor; the person goes down and activates the lobby station. The system says \"Fire in Lobby\" and provokes a wrong response.</li>\n<li>The manual station lets people give warning while escaping, but does not locate the emergency area well.</li>\n<li><strong>Solution:</strong> do not eliminate or relocate the manual stations; <strong>complement them</strong> with automatic detection distributed throughout the building.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Smoke Detectors</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Automatic devices that detect the presence of smoke.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical locations</strong> (Craighead, 2003):<ul>\n<li>Open areas.</li>\n<li>Spaces above suspended ceilings.</li>\n<li>Spaces under raised floors, especially in computer rooms and data centers.</li>\n<li>Cafeterias.</li>\n<li>Air ducts.</li>\n<li>Lobbies of passenger and service/freight elevators.</li>\n<li>Elevator shafts.</li>\n<li>Elevator machine rooms.</li>\n<li>Enclosed stairwells.</li>\n<li>Dumbwaiter shafts.</li>\n<li>Chutes or vertical ducts.</li>\n<li>Electrical and mechanical equipment rooms.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Location and spacing: according to local laws, codes, and standards, and engineering issues.</li>\n<li>Even an incipient fire, controlled before the structure burns, can cost a lot in interiors.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Heat Detectors</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>They detect a given temperature or a <strong>rapid change in temperature</strong>.</li>\n<li>Roberts (2003): very reliable; <strong>the lowest false alarm rate</strong> of all automatic detectors.</li>\n<li><strong>Best use:</strong><ul>\n<li>Small, confined spaces where high-heat, rapidly growing fires are expected.</li>\n<li>Areas whose environmental conditions prevent the use of other detectors.</li>\n<li>Where very early warning is not required.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Sprinkler and Standpipe Alarms</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Wherever there are standpipes and sprinklers: <strong>water-flow</strong> and <strong>valve-position</strong> alarms.</li>\n<li><strong>Standpipe system:</strong> carries water vertically to upper floors to fight fire by hand with hoses.</li>\n<li><strong>Sprinkler system</strong> according to Puchovsky (2003):<ul>\n<li>Underground and overhead piping connected to an automatic water supply, installed throughout the building.</li>\n<li>Piping that is sized or hydraulically designed; inside the building it usually runs overhead.</li>\n<li>Sprinklers in a systematic pattern.</li>\n<li>A valve controls each riser, on the riser itself or on the supply pipe.</li>\n<li>It is normally activated by the heat of the fire and discharges water over the area.</li>\n<li>A device on the riser triggers an alarm when the system operates.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Nature of Fire Alarm Systems</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Computer-based; architecture similar to security alarms.</li>\n<li>Field devices (smoke detectors, air-handling controls) → field panels → main computer.</li>\n<li>They can be in a long series and be <strong>addressable</strong>: each one reports its status under its own name.</li>\n<li><strong>Some codes prohibit:</strong><ul>\n<li>The fire alarm performing functions unrelated to fire life safety.</li>\n<li>Using components not approved for fire life safety.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Effect:</strong> depending on the local authority, the fire alarm may not monitor or control security devices, nor vice versa.</li>\n<li>So, releasing electric locks in a fire requires coordinating the design of both systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Typical solution:</strong> the fire alarm sends no direct signal to the security system. It controls an <strong>approved relay</strong> connected to an <strong>approved power supply</strong> of the lock.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Fire Extinguishment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Automatic sprinklers</strong> are essential in a high-rise.</li>\n<li><em>Fire Protection Handbook</em> (Cote, 2003): with sprinklers, the probability of dying and the fire loss drop by <strong>one-third to two-thirds</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>3 reasons why sprinklers fail:</strong><ol>\n<li>Partial, outdated, poorly maintained, or inadequate systems.</li>\n<li>Explosions or flash fires that overwhelm the system before it reacts.</li>\n<li>Fires very close to people, who die before the system reacts.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Other Fixed Systems</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Standpipe and hose systems</strong> (Shapiro, 2003): typical in tall and large-area buildings.<ul>\n<li>Small hose connections, perhaps with cabinets, for internal use.</li>\n<li>Larger connections for the fire department.</li>\n<li>They avoid long hose runs from the truck to the fire.</li>\n<li>Even with sprinklers, they are <strong>backup and complement</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Water:</strong> the main agent in most high-rises. Other agents:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Agent</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dry chemical and wet chemical</td>\n<td>Hoods, ducts, and cooking appliances in kitchens and cafeterias</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Halon and halon substitutes</td>\n<td>Switchgear rooms and computer and data processing centers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carbon dioxide (CO2), in certain cases</td>\n<td>The same spaces as halon</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cost decision</strong> in a building with sprinklers: compare special system + probable damage when activated <strong>against</strong> sprinklers + probable water damage.</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> the Montreal Protocol eliminated halon production; today clean agents are used.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Smoke Control</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Smoke harms people, the building, and its contents.</li>\n<li>Obvious measure: limit flammable synthetic materials in furnishings; allow only fire-resistant ones.</li>\n<li>The proliferation of personal computers makes it hard to limit combustibles.</li>\n<li>The design and construction of the building influence smoke control.</li>\n<li>Milke (2003): smoke behaves differently depending on height.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Low buildings</th>\n<th>Tall buildings</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Moved by the effects of the fire: heat, convective movement, and fire pressures</td>\n<td>Predominant factors: <strong>stack effect</strong>, <strong>external wind</strong>, and <strong>forced air movement</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stack effect:</strong> natural vertical movement of air in the building due to temperature and density differences between interior and exterior air.</li>\n<li><strong>Forced air movement:</strong> caused by air-handling equipment.<ul>\n<li>HVAC (heating, ventilating, and air-conditioning).</li>\n<li>ACV (air-conditioning and ventilating).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Webb (2003): except for self-contained units, these systems use <strong>ducts</strong>, which can spread fire, gases, and smoke.</li>\n<li><strong>Keys to containing fire</strong> (Webb, 2003):<ul>\n<li>Location of equipment and of <strong>fresh-air intakes</strong>.</li>\n<li>Type of air filters and cleaners.</li>\n<li>Duct and plenum system.</li>\n<li>Use of <strong>fire and smoke dampers</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Systems vary by manufacturer, building, and the code in force when installed.</li>\n<li><strong>Two basic approaches</strong> (Webb, 2003):<ol>\n<li>Shut down the HVAC and isolate or compartmentalize the fire area.</li>\n<li>Keep the fans running and use the ducts for emergency smoke control.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>In addition, in many high-rises the alarm activates <strong>automatic stairwell pressurization</strong> with fans (Craighead, 2003).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Fire Department or Emergency Services Notification</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In the event of a fire life safety system incident, notify the fire department or emergency services <strong>immediately</strong>.</li>\n<li>In addition to notification by building staff: monitoring by an <strong>off-site central station</strong> or by the fire department itself.</li>\n<li>It may be required by the local code or be an additional measure by the owner or manager.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Occupant Notification</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Notify all affected occupants and responders promptly (Schifiliti, 2003).</li>\n<li>The alarm indicates to evacuate or relocate to a safe area. It can also notify emergency forces.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of notification</th>\n<th>Information conveyed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Audible signal (bells, sirens, whoopers) + strobe lights</td>\n<td><strong>A single bit:</strong> \"fire alarm\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Voice announcements or text or graphic annunciators</td>\n<td><strong>Several bits:</strong> alarm, location, and how and where to evacuate or relocate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>With detailed information, people evacuate <strong>faster and better</strong>.</li>\n<li>Audible and visible devices also indicate system <strong>trouble</strong>.</li>\n<li>Or they serve as a <strong>supervisory signal</strong> on the status of other systems, e.g., sprinklers.</li>\n<li><strong>Public address (PA) system:</strong> a <strong>one-way</strong> voice system from the fire control panel to the occupants.</li>\n<li>That panel is usually in the <strong>fire command center</strong> or <strong>fire control room</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Emergency Planning</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Essential: a comprehensive <strong>emergency management plan</strong>.</li>\n<li>It describes actions to protect employees, the public, and other assets.</li>\n<li>It covers <strong>2 categories of threats: natural and man-made</strong>.</li>\n<li>Threats are anticipated and initial reactions are designed. This way, in the emergency, the most important things are attended to.</li>\n<li><strong>Content for a high-rise (6 elements):</strong><ol>\n<li>Evacuation signage that clearly marks the exits.</li>\n<li>Types of emergency systems and equipment in the building and how they operate.</li>\n<li>Organization of emergency personnel, who act until outside agencies arrive:\n - Management and the building fire safety director.\n - Engineering, security, cleaning, and parking staff.\n - Floor staff: floor wardens or fire wardens.\n - Those responsible for assisting people with disabilities, and others as needed.</li>\n<li>Contact information for the necessary people and agencies.</li>\n<li>Procedures for each anticipated emergency, including evacuation and relocation methods.</li>\n<li>Training of occupants, wardens, and staff, including the frequency and type of fire drills.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Review the plan with the local fire marshal</strong> or equivalent.<ul>\n<li>Strategy varies according to size, materials, and the fire department equipment available.</li>\n<li>Depending on circumstances, evacuation can <strong>expose occupants to more risk</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Resource:</strong> the NFPA (National Fire Protection Association) offers <em>Guidelines for Developing Emergency Management Plans for All-Hazard Emergencies in High Rise Office Buildings</em> free of charge.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-B-e2\">Security Considerations</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Security differences between a high-rise and a low building</strong> (Craighead, 2009):<ol>\n<li><strong>Several occupied floors stacked</strong> → more occupants and more property to damage or steal.\n - The concentration of property attracts the criminal.\n - More people = more anonymity for the thief, especially if he dresses and acts like the users.\n - Kitteringham (2006): many are in central business districts. Close to mass transit and easily accessible, they attract <strong>professional thieves</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>More people gathered</strong> → greater chance that one will commit a crime against another.\n - Problem: crime pattern analyses are lacking for high-rises.\n - Crime incidence depends on the neighborhood, the building design, its use and type of tenants, and the security program.</li>\n<li><strong>All have stairs and elevators</strong>; a single-story building does not.\n - Stairwell: a little-used area except in evacuations → possible site for assault (including sexual assault) or theft.\n - Elevator: possible scene of vandalism and crimes against persons.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Entry and exit are usually channeled through the <strong>core</strong>, where the elevators and escalators are.</li>\n<li>Authenticating and controlling ingress and egress requires special consideration.</li>\n<li>Effective security measures minimize the impact of most threats.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The Problem of Exposed Assets</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Assets are vulnerable to natural hazards, theft, and malicious damage.</li>\n<li>In buildings open to the public, the flow of people in hallways, elevators, stairs, and public areas increases vulnerability.</li>\n<li><strong>Principle:</strong> the need for protection arises from the <strong>nature of the assets</strong>, not from who owns them.<ul>\n<li>It does not matter that the risk is shared by several organizations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A highly exposed tenant that does not protect itself threatens others: its assets attract attackers to the building.</li>\n<li>The final scheme must be a <strong>cooperative effort</strong> among owner, manager, and tenants.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The Life Safety and Security Dilemma</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The factors that complicate life safety also create security problems.</li>\n<li>The code requires free exit whenever there are occupants → egress with little or no surveillance.</li>\n<li>In a real emergency, security may not be ready to handle the emergency and heightened security at the same time.</li>\n<li><strong>Two-person attack:</strong><ul>\n<li>One simulates a fire with a manual station on a floor.</li>\n<li>The accomplice, many floors away, takes advantage of the doors unlocked by the emergency.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Countermeasures:</strong> intrusion alarms and video surveillance with alarm-activated camera deployment and recording.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The High-Rise Physical Environment (the model building)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A notional building that illustrates Figure B-1 (main lobby at street level):</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Height</td>\n<td>40 floors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Occupants</td>\n<td>About 6,000; about 150 per floor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Passenger elevators</td>\n<td>3 banks of 8 cars; each bank serves about one-third of the building</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service or freight elevators</td>\n<td>2 cars; they serve all floors, from B-2 to 40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Basements B-1 and B-2</td>\n<td>Mechanical and storage areas; served by the 2 service elevators and the low bank (C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vehicle access (H)</td>\n<td>Ramp to B-1: rolling metal shutter at the street and an open-grille rolling shutter at the bottom</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loading dock</td>\n<td>4 trucks; served by the service elevators</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Figure B-1 legend:</strong><ul>\n<li>A = high-rise elevator lobby (8 cars).</li>\n<li>B = mid-rise elevator lobby (8 cars).</li>\n<li>C = low-rise elevator lobby (8 cars).</li>\n<li>D = fire stair shafts and doors.</li>\n<li>E = service elevators (2 cars, B2 to 40).</li>\n<li>F = rear doors: one set of double swinging doors and two revolving doors.</li>\n<li>G = main doors: three revolving doors and two single swinging doors.</li>\n<li>H = rolling door and ramp down for trucks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Building Operating Modes</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Open</strong></td>\n<td>Free access at the entrance. Everyone goes directly to their floor; the occupant of the space sets its own control. There may be a concierge or security desk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Closed</strong></td>\n<td>Access to the elevator banks with credential verification. Visitors authenticated before the elevators. Sometimes, a separate mail center</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hybrid</strong></td>\n<td>A major tenant occupies a large part of the building. One or more banks operate closed; the multitenant banks, open</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Open building desk:</strong> gives directions, deters undesirable visitors (if they are identifiable), and applies procedures to couriers, deliverers, and contractors.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book announces the concept \"open or closed,\" but describes <strong>three</strong> modes (including hybrid).</li>\n<li><strong>Modes by schedule</strong> (according to traffic, occupancy, time, and day):<ul>\n<li><strong>Regular business hours:</strong> most tenants and operations are active.</li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate hours:</strong> 1 or 2 hours before and after regular hours; a transition period.</li>\n<li><strong>Off-hours or after-hours:</strong> nights, weekends, and holidays, with few or no tenants.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Access Control Recommendations (3 classes of interior spaces)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Includes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Public access or common areas</td>\n<td>Street-level entrance lobbies, main elevator lobbies, access to shops and restaurants, promenades, mezzanines and, increasingly, atriums</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rented or assigned occupancies, tenant spaces</td>\n<td>Spaces rented or occupied by the owner. Open to the public during hours or restricted to authorized persons, depending on the occupant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maintenance spaces</td>\n<td>Mechanical rooms and floors, communications and utility points, elevator machine rooms, janitorial rooms, and very limited-access spaces</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Access control measures vary for each class.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Access Control of Public Areas</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Classify those who enter the lobby according to their <strong>purpose</strong>:<ul>\n<li><strong>Regular tenants:</strong> credential (e.g., access card) to quickly verify their right to enter.</li>\n<li><strong>Visitors</strong> (contractors, deliverers, maintenance providers): additional screening. Authenticate them, check them, more scrutiny and escort.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Possible scheme:</strong> stairwells locked against entry from the lobby, with free exit to the lobby, and control points at each passenger elevator bank.<ul>\n<li>To be effective, everyone would show identification, allow inspection of the property they bring in, or both.</li>\n<li>Acceptable in periods of heightened security.</li>\n<li>Under normal conditions, some tenants resist it, especially those that receive walk-in clients.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Bank:</strong> usually on the ground floor and perhaps in part of the basement (vault and safe deposit boxes). Its lobby is usually open access.</li>\n<li><strong>Retail only on the ground floor:</strong> give it separate access from the street.<ul>\n<li>It makes use outside the building's hours easier.</li>\n<li>It reduces outside traffic in the lobby.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Owner who occupies some floors and rents others:</strong> plan the assignment or stacking of tenants to control access with minimum cost and inconvenience.</li>\n<li>The building operates as a hybrid. <strong>4 planning points:</strong><ol>\n<li><strong>Identify the owner's sensitive occupancies:</strong> executive offices, data processing, cash or valuables handling, large volumes of sensitive data, and any space the owner declares critical.</li>\n<li><strong>Isolate them:</strong> group them on floors of <strong>a single sector</strong> (low-rise, mid-rise, or high-rise).</li>\n<li><strong>Impose controls in the sector that contains them.</strong> Example with the model building:\n - The owner occupies the floors of bank A (high-rise) and half of bank B (mid-rise); rents the rest.\n - The sensitive occupancies are stacked on the floors of the high-rise bank.\n - The mid-rise lobby is divided: half of the cars serve floors 13 to 19; the other half, 20 to 27.\n - The sensitive half (20 to 27) is controlled the same way and with the same staff as the high-rise bank: card readers and queue or flow devices.\n - If the occupancy proportion changes, those controls are removed without affecting the high-rise sector.</li>\n<li><strong>Ensure the controls cannot be bypassed:</strong>\n - A common or crossover floor between banks must not give free access to a sensitive area.\n - Example: if floor 27 is served by the high-rise bank and the mid-rise bank, put a staffed control point on 27, or readers in the cars of both banks.\n - Without a crossover floor, to change banks you have to go down to the main lobby.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Elevator Control</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Occupancy changes over the years and with it the access control required.</li>\n<li><strong>Great advantage:</strong> design the passenger elevators (or retrofit them, if cost-effective) to control access to <strong>all</strong> floors.<ul>\n<li>This way each car is programmed: how it responds to calls from the car and from the lobbies.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Good practice:</strong> card readers in <strong>all</strong> cars, even if they are not always active.</li>\n<li><strong>Turnstiles:</strong> screen people before they enter the elevator.</li>\n<li><strong>Going from an open to a closed building:</strong> program the elevators with card access 24/7.<ul>\n<li>Give cards only to authorized persons, for the floors and hours set by each tenant's management.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Service or freight elevators:</strong> a special problem, because they usually serve all levels.<ul>\n<li>If they are self-service, they compromise the entire security program, unless:\n1. They are programmed not to reach sensitive floors without special arrangement.\n2. They are locked at the hoistway doors of the sensitive floors.\n3. They arrive at floors with the vestibule or lobby doors closed, where applicable.</li>\n<li><strong>Additional risk:</strong> unauthorized movement of property from or between accessible floors.</li>\n<li><strong>Mitigation:</strong> assign an operator or provide them only on request to security.</li>\n<li>Readers in the cars and cameras in cars or lobbies also help.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid</strong> using them for general passenger movement.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Building Stairwells</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Emergency exit stairs (fire stairs) are common areas.</li>\n<li>They must be accessible for escape, especially if the elevators are not available or not safe (e.g., in a fire).</li>\n<li><strong>Two factors define how to secure them:</strong><ol>\n<li><strong>Local code:</strong> with occupants, they are <strong>never</strong> locked on the egress route. Restricting <strong>reentry</strong> to the floor may be permitted.\n - Some jurisdictions: stairwell-to-floor doors locked, provided that <strong>every fourth floor</strong> they are open to exit the shaft.\n - Others: locked if they unlock by themselves (<strong>fail safe</strong>) when the fire life safety system is activated.</li>\n<li><strong>Is movement between floors by the stairs permitted?</strong>\n - In favor (multifloor tenants): saves time, reduces demand on elevators and energy, and is more convenient.\n - Against: reduces security of the stairs themselves.\n - <strong>Better, though costly:</strong> the multifloor tenant's own internal stair.\n - <strong>Ideal:</strong> access to the stairs from the common area of the floor (elevator lobby and corridors), not from inside the tenant space.\n - If the jurisdiction allows: doors with approved access control (readers) and two-way <strong>intercoms</strong> with security.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Choosing the stairwell locks is critical to comply with the code. Always coordinate with local authorities.</li>\n<li>Craighead (2003): the <strong>stairwell exit door on the ground floor</strong> is where security conflicts most with fire life safety.<ul>\n<li>Immediate, unimpeded exit lets the criminal flee quickly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Delayed-egress locking device:</strong> some authorities allow, outside an emergency, a <strong>15 to 30 second</strong> delay at the exit door to the ground floor.<ul>\n<li>Useful together with:\n1. An alarm that alerts security of the exit attempt.\n2. An intercom to speak with the person.\n3. A camera that views and records the event.</li>\n<li>The delay may be enough for security to intercept.</li>\n<li>They must unlock by themselves if the lock's <strong>power fails</strong> or the fire life safety system <strong>is activated</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Exam note (outside the book):</strong> NFPA 101 sets 15 s, extendable to 30 s if approved by the authority having jurisdiction (AHJ).</li>\n<li><strong>Intrusion alarms on stairwell doors:</strong> indicate entry into the shaft and allow a response.<ul>\n<li><strong>Doors locked against reentry:</strong> an officer goes to the ground-floor door of that stairwell. Another enters the shaft <strong>above</strong> the alarm point. This way the intruder is intercepted inside.</li>\n<li><strong>Doors generally open:</strong> alarms on sensitive floors indicate use of that door.</li>\n<li>Every intrusion alarm must be <strong>zoned</strong> to indicate exactly which door or area was breached.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Access Control of Interior Floors and Spaces</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It can be the only control or be integrated with the general lobby control.</li>\n<li>Programmable elevators give some control by floor.</li>\n<li>Variant, if there is no full control by elevator: readers in the cars for certain floors.</li>\n<li><strong>Visitors without a card:</strong> special handling by security staff.<ul>\n<li>Floor with few visitors: <strong>escort</strong>.</li>\n<li>Floor with heavy traffic: free access by elevator and a <strong>reception point on the floor</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Piggybacking or tailgating:</strong> an unauthorized person enters a secure area accompanying a cardholder.<ul>\n<li>Authorized users must help deter it.</li>\n<li>It is also controlled with turnstiles next to the elevators.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Reception in the elevator lobby itself</strong> (building of Figure B-1):<ul>\n<li>One end of the lobby, closed toward the floor (perhaps with remote release or card).</li>\n<li>The reception, at the opposite end.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Larger integrated system:</strong> the central console remotely controls access to floors or areas.</li>\n<li><strong>Small spaces within the floor:</strong> conventional keys, readers, remote latch release, communication or surveillance, or a receptionist.</li>\n<li><strong>Doors of elevator lobbies on upper floors:</strong> special care with the lock.<ul>\n<li><strong>Phase I:</strong> a smoke detector in the elevator lobby, shaft, or machine room is activated. The cars of that bank return by themselves to a designated level.</li>\n<li>If the alarm was in the lobby of the designated level, they return to an alternate floor.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase II:</strong> once recalled, they remain for fire department service with a <strong>special key</strong> (Donoghue, 2003).</li>\n<li>The key is <strong>for firefighters only</strong>, not for engineering or security. Its use is strictly controlled.</li>\n<li>Anyone in the lobby when the elevators are recalled must be able to exit to a fire stair.</li>\n<li>Consult the local jurisdiction on which locks are permitted on those doors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Access Control of Building Maintenance Spaces</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Critical areas:</strong><ul>\n<li>Mechanical rooms and floors.</li>\n<li>Air-conditioning rooms.</li>\n<li>Fire pump and fire control rooms.</li>\n<li>Telecommunications and utility installations, including electrical transformer vaults.</li>\n<li>Elevator pits.</li>\n<li>Elevator machine rooms.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Control with selective admittance devices and monitoring with intrusion alarms.</li>\n<li>If a door is opened without an authorized key, automatic device, or remote release → <strong>distinctive</strong> intrusion alarm to the monitoring center.</li>\n<li>Feasible even in large buildings: those who need access are few and easy to identify.</li>\n<li><strong>Fully automated system:</strong> cards and readers, or remote admission with latch control, video, and two-way communication.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimum low-cost control</strong> (small building, no large integrated system):<ol>\n<li>Conventional lock on the door.</li>\n<li>Controlled issuance of keys.</li>\n<li>Intrusion alarm zoned distinctively.</li>\n<li>Contact by telephone or radio from the key holder just before or after entering.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The contact is eliminated with a <strong>shunt switch</strong> operated with the authorized key.</li>\n<li><strong>Disadvantage of keys:</strong> they get lost and compromised.</li>\n<li><strong>Utility personnel</strong> (e.g., telephone installers in distribution rooms and terminal panels):<ul>\n<li>Regularly assigned: key or automatic entry device.</li>\n<li>Temporary: after verifying their identity, a <strong>one-day</strong> device. Ensure its return before they leave.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Access Control of Fresh Air Intakes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>HVAC intakes are usually at or near ground level, covered with louvered grilles.</li>\n<li>Protect them against the introduction of airborne chemical or biological agents.</li>\n<li><strong>Figure B-2 (Fresh Air Intake Protection Scheme):</strong> grille or baffle at <strong>90 degrees</strong> to the intake, with an <strong>intrusion alarm</strong> on the grille.<ul>\n<li>Limit: the suction could still draw in vapors from a source placed on the grille.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Best solution:</strong> locate the intakes high enough above the ground that access is impossible.</li>\n<li><strong>Figure B-2 legend:</strong><ul>\n<li>A = exterior building wall.</li>\n<li>B = grade level.</li>\n<li>C = open space and intake area.</li>\n<li>D = louvered intake shutter.</li>\n<li>E = grille, protected with an intrusion alarm.</li>\n<li>F = undisturbed earth.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Access Control of Telecommunication Services</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They leave the building in one or more trunk cables toward ducts and tunnels in the street.</li>\n<li>Telephone, computing, and alarms use that route: they are vulnerable along the cable.</li>\n<li><strong>High-security building:</strong> <strong>two physically separate routes</strong> to <strong>different telephone central offices</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Most critical points:</strong> where all services have already joined into one or two trunks that are still inside the building.<ul>\n<li>Old buildings: trunks exposed in basements or service areas.</li>\n<li>New buildings: usually more concealed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Review the route from the risers to the service entrance. Protect every access point.<ul>\n<li>Cable in an enclosed space: intrusion detection on hatch covers and doors.</li>\n<li>Exposed cable: harden the route with resistant construction.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-B-e3\">Additional Security Measures</h3><ul>\n<li>They are part of an integrated physical security strategy for high-rises (8):<ol>\n<li>Lighting.</li>\n<li>Selective site hardening.</li>\n<li>Locks and locking devices.</li>\n<li>Alarm sensors.</li>\n<li>Duress alarms.</li>\n<li>Integrated video and security systems.</li>\n<li>Security personnel.</li>\n<li>Policies and procedures.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Lighting</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It creates a <strong>psychological deterrent</strong> against crime, including theft and physical assault.</li>\n<li><strong>Uses:</strong> perimeter accesses (streets and walkways), perimeter barriers, landscaping, facades, open parking lots, and parking structures.<ul>\n<li>Also all interior areas, including loading docks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Relatively inexpensive. Well used, it can reduce the need for security personnel.</li>\n<li>It supports the effective use of video systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Selective Site Hardening</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A person or company that is already a high-risk target (including for terrorism) is more exposed in a high-rise.</li>\n<li>In addition to access control, hardening of the structure at or within target floors may be required.</li>\n<li><strong>Ideally from the initial construction:</strong> in a remodel there are fewer options and more cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Construction Features</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Main reason:</strong> resistance to <strong>impact and firearms</strong>.</li>\n<li>Bombings add the need to evaluate <strong>blast-resistant</strong> design in new high-profile construction.</li>\n<li><strong>Resistant materials (9):</strong><ol>\n<li>Standard masonry.</li>\n<li>Blast-resistant reinforced concrete.</li>\n<li>Sheet steel.</li>\n<li>Polycarbonates.</li>\n<li>Acrylic.</li>\n<li>Compressed fiberglass.</li>\n<li>Ballistic fabrics.</li>\n<li>Bullet-resistive glass.</li>\n<li>Security film for windows: holds broken glass together and keeps shards from becoming lethal projectiles.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Example (Figure B-1), key personnel floor:</strong><ul>\n<li>Close both ends of the elevator corridor with resistant material and equivalent controlled doors.</li>\n<li>Partition of masonry and fiberglass, or of steel between drywall panels.</li>\n<li>Resistant glass or polycarbonate door.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cost principle:</strong> place barriers and hardened materials as close as possible to the normal points of entry to the floor.<ul>\n<li>Hardening the elevator lobby and all stairwell doors is cheaper than hardening individual offices.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Suspended ceilings and raised floors</strong> with plenums or ducts can be used for intrusion. When hardening a floor:<ul>\n<li>Carry the resistant partition up to the slab above or below, or</li>\n<li>Close the space with heavy-gauge wire mesh, which lets air through.</li>\n<li>Complement with intrusion devices.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Key Reception Area</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>If entry control for a target floor is on the floor itself, the reception area must resist attacks.</li>\n<li>Executives often reject barriers in their reception: they say they send a bad message or make a bad impression.</li>\n<li><strong>Solutions:</strong><ol>\n<li><strong>Reception on another floor</strong> (above or below the protected one).\n - There the visitor is identified and a controller on the sensitive floor is notified.\n - Then the visitor goes up to the sensitive floor, where access is specifically controlled.</li>\n<li><strong>Two control points on the protected floor:</strong>\n - Staffed exterior reception, separated from the interior by protective barriers with observation capability.\n - Someone inside, behind the barriers, unlocks to admit the authorized visitor. They can do so before the visitor reaches the door.\n - Visitors do not notice that the interior doors are locked.\n - With no opening mechanism at reception, no one enters unless someone inside admits them or they have a key.\n - <strong>The receptionist must not have that key or device.</strong>\n - For her safety: a discreet <strong>hold-up button</strong> under the desk.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Physical Barriers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They protect against car bombs and discourage penetration by <strong>accident, force, and stealth</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Types:</strong> standoff distances, perimeter fences, walls, sidewalks, pathways, landscaping, fountains, ponds, sculptures, benches, planters, barricades, and bollards.</li>\n<li><strong>Functions (3):</strong> <strong>delay</strong> the intruder, <strong>define</strong> an area, and <strong>direct</strong> behavior.</li>\n<li><strong>Difficulty:</strong> achieving sufficient separation between the barrier and the building. In cities space is scarce and the buildings already exist.</li>\n<li>Even so, well-designed perimeter barriers prevent a vehicle with explosives from reaching the building before detonating.</li>\n<li><strong>Parking lots and loading docks</strong> (especially under the building): parking controllers and barriers.<ul>\n<li>Gate arms.</li>\n<li>In high security: <strong>crash-rated hydraulic impact barriers</strong>.</li>\n<li>Manual or automatic activation, to admit only authorized vehicles.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Building Locks and Locking Devices</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The type depends on the door or barrier and on the building or life safety code in force.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Location</th>\n<th>Recommended device</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Main entry and exit doors, and legal exits of the perimeter</td>\n<td>Electromagnetic locks with timer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interior side (stair side) of stairwell doors</td>\n<td>Hightower-function mortise lock or stair tower lock: a special hybrid electric lock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exterior side of stairwell doors that exit the building</td>\n<td>Key-operated pin tumbler locks and electromagnetic locks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevator lobby or vestibule doors</td>\n<td>Electromagnetic hold-open devices</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Electromagnetic locks at main entrances</strong> (Craighead, 2003):<ul>\n<li>The timer locks and unlocks them according to the building's schedule.</li>\n<li>Locked: access from outside with a card reader.</li>\n<li>Normal exit with panic hardware or automatic unlocking (e.g., motion detectors).</li>\n<li>Codes require that the power of any lock that restricts egress be <strong>supervised by the fire life safety system</strong>.</li>\n<li>Power failure or fire alarm → power is cut and they open (<strong>fail-safe</strong>). Occupants get out and firefighters get in.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Hightower-function mortise lock</strong> (Geringer, 1991):<ul>\n<li>Allows locking the door on the stair side for security.</li>\n<li>Always energized and locked. Access with a mechanical key, digital keypad, or reader.</li>\n<li>Its power is controlled by the life safety system. In an emergency it unlocks instantly, <strong>but remains closed and latched</strong>: it protects the stairwell from smoke and fire.</li>\n<li>Some jurisdictions allow delayed egress of 15 to 30 s outside an emergency, with automatic unlocking on power failure or system activation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Exterior of stairwell exits</strong> (Craighead, 2003):<ul>\n<li>They can be locked on the outside if they operate freely from the inside.</li>\n<li>When the exit bar (fire exit hardware) is pushed, they open instantly, except for delayed-egress ones.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Electromagnetic hold-open devices in elevator lobbies:</strong><ul>\n<li>When the life safety system is activated, their power is cut and they release the door.</li>\n<li>The door closes by itself and protects the lobby from smoke and fire.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Alarm Sensors</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Intrusion detection for perimeter areas, stairwells, maintenance, and tenants.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Duress Alarms</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Where:</strong> parking booths, other cash-handling areas, and reception points, including executive reception.</li>\n<li>Train those who activate them and those who respond, with solid policies and procedures.</li>\n<li><strong>Test them regularly.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Video Surveillance</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Very useful in a high-rise. Its effectiveness depends on <strong>camera location</strong> and on <strong>monitoring and response strategies</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Location</th>\n<th>What it includes or what it is for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Critical pedestrian entrances and exits</td>\n<td>Stairwell exits to the ground floor, perimeter, critical floor receptions, mechanical floors, and crossover floors. Monitor and identify who exits by the stairwell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remotely operated pedestrian accesses</td>\n<td>Remotely controlled doors: so that only authorized people enter, with no tailgating or piggybacking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accesses with automated control (readers)</td>\n<td>So that only the person who uses the card enters and there is no tailgating</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inside passenger and service cars</td>\n<td>Camera in a <strong>visible</strong> place: deters threats to people and property, and elevator vandalism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sensitive interior spaces</td>\n<td>Where remote surveillance gives a great advantage: mechanical areas, HVAC air intakes, vaults, storerooms, and elevator lobbies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vehicle entrances and exits</td>\n<td>Monitor vehicles and decide on remote access. In garages under the building: record all vehicles, <strong>license plates</strong>, and drivers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Covert surveillance</td>\n<td>Hidden cameras in sensitive places. <strong>Check local laws</strong> first. Very useful in investigations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>With remote access control, <strong>two-way voice</strong> is also needed between the entrance and the control point.</li>\n<li>Video without critical analysis or a response resource = <strong>operationally inadequate and economically wasted</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring requirements (all):</strong><ol>\n<li>Each camera's image always visible, unless alarm switched when needed.</li>\n<li>Monitors arranged for rapid visual analysis by a trained observer.</li>\n<li>A means to bring any camera of interest to a monitor in the operator's direct field of view.</li>\n<li>Permanent recording of one or more cameras, identified by <strong>camera location and time</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Systems of 2 or 3 cameras:</strong> one monitor per camera plus a recorder.</li>\n<li><strong>Systems of 10 or more cameras:</strong> require a more sophisticated design.</li>\n<li><strong>Design advice:</strong> \"design the application first and fit the equipment to it.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Security Operations Center</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A central place in the building to monitor and control security systems and equipment.</li>\n<li>It must be used on a <strong>dedicated</strong> basis.</li>\n<li>Because of cost and staffing, it is sometimes installed in the main lobby or next to heavily used entrances.<ul>\n<li>The operator attends to the system and, at the same time, to the people passing by.</li>\n<li>The theoretical savings of adding other fixed posts to the operator can cause inattention to loss events of much greater magnitude.</li>\n<li>It also exposes control of the system to compromise or attack.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>With a well-designed system, the monitoring and control center should be <strong>the most secure place in the building</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Security Personnel</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Main function:</strong> implement the building's life safety and security program.</li>\n<li><strong>The number depends on:</strong><ul>\n<li>Type of occupancy and its activities.</li>\n<li>Objectives of the security and life safety program.</li>\n<li>Role of security in that program.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Offices:</strong> more staff during regular hours than outside hours (Craighead, 2003).<ul>\n<li>During hours there are employees, visitors, vendors, tradespeople, building management, contractors, messengers, deliverers, promoters, and others.</li>\n<li>Outside hours, pedestrian and vehicle traffic drops sharply, except for special activities.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Residential:</strong> it can be just the opposite.</li>\n<li>The staffing level is evaluated, assigned, and adjusted according to the circumstances.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-B-e4\">Summary</h3><ul>\n<li>High-rise: taller than the maximum reach of fire department equipment; it has unique life safety and security needs.</li>\n<li>It concentrates people and property. Threats: fires, natural disasters, and crimes (theft of assets, workplace violence, and terrorism).</li>\n<li>In fires and explosions it is necessary to evacuate immediately. But stairs and elevators do not allow everyone to get out at once.</li>\n<li>Therefore: updated, complete, and well-thought-out emergency plans, and occupants trained to react.</li>\n<li>Measures must comply with strict building and life safety codes, and address security needs.<ul>\n<li>Those needs vary according to the type of tenants and the pattern of use.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>When they clash, comply with life safety <strong>without compromising</strong> the security objectives.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"physical-B-e5\">Key terms</h3><p>high-rise structure · slab-to-slab height · life safety · fire life safety system · deter, detect, delay, respond · unimpeded egress · life-support systems · incipient hazard · air-handling unit · charged pipe · vertical risers · progressive failure · setback · security film · soft target · collateral damage · diversion · hoax · retained risks · products of combustion · manual fire alarm (pull) station · smoke detector · heat detector · standpipe system · sprinkler system · water-flow alarm · valve-position alarm · addressable device · approved relay · approved lock power supply · flash fire · standpipe and hose system · dry chemical / wet chemical · halon · carbon dioxide · stack effect · forced air movement · HVAC / ACV · plenum · fire and smoke dampers · stairwell pressurization · off-site central station · notification appliances · trouble signal · supervisory signal · public address (PA) system · fire command center / fire control room · emergency management plan · building fire safety director · floor warden / fire warden · fire drill · fire marshal · exposed assets · notional building · open building · closed building · hybrid building · regular / intermediate / after-hours · public access or common areas · tenant spaces · maintenance spaces · stacking · crossover floor · turnstile · hoistway door · fire stairs · re-entry · fail safe · delayed-egress locking device · intrusion alarm zoning · piggybacking / tailgating · remote latch release · Phase I / Phase II operation · selective admittance devices · shunt switch · fresh air intake · grille / baffle · louvered intake shutter · trunk cable · service entrance · hatch cover · selective site hardening · blast-resistant design · bullet-resistive glass · key reception area · hold-up button · standoff distance · crash-rated hydraulic impact barrier · gate arm · electromagnetic lock · panic hardware · hightower-function mortise lock (stair tower lock) · key-operated pin tumbler lock · fire exit hardware · electromagnetic hold-open device · duress alarm · covert surveillance · alarm switched display · security operations center</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"What is a high-rise structure?"},{"id":"e1","t":"Life Safety Considerations"},{"id":"e2","t":"Security Considerations"},{"id":"e3","t":"Additional Security Measures"},{"id":"e4","t":"Summary"},{"id":"e5","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"Security Considerations","h":"the book announces the concept \"open or closed,\" but describes <strong>three</strong> modes (including hybrid)."}]},"physical-C":{"html":"<p><strong>What they are and how they are made</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thanks to national and international standards, parts, processes, and systems work regardless of who creates them, who uses them, or where.</li>\n<li>They are created to meet a market need or a gap in existing standards.</li>\n<li>They are coordinated by accredited standards developing organizations (SDOs). They bring together public and private stakeholders to identify best practices.</li>\n<li>They are the basis of innovation and competitiveness. They provide a common language for national and international trade.</li>\n<li>Four development principles: consensus, openness, due process, and transparency.</li>\n<li>In security they help nations, communities, societies, organizations, and individuals protect assets and improve resilience to natural and human threats.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Types of standards</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Key feature</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Voluntary standards</td>\n<td>They are not mandatory, but are widely adopted. Some are integrated into regulatory frameworks of several countries</td>\n<td>ISO and national institutes; in the US, associations such as ASIS International</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Statutory or regulatory standards</td>\n<td>Mandatory by law; enforced by formal authorities</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mixed standards</td>\n<td>The boundary blurs when a voluntary standard is incorporated into laws or regulations (fire, construction, human safety). Or it remains voluntary in theory but mandatory in practice</td>\n<td>Insurers that set the availability and cost of casualty insurance according to approved materials or standardized practices; contracts that require standards</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The adoption of standards is increasingly client driven.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Standards and organizations cited in the appendix</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Organization</th>\n<th>Code</th>\n<th>Title or topic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO (International Organization for Standardization)</td>\n<td>— (in general)</td>\n<td>Voluntary but widely adopted. Asset protection topics: facilities management; health, environment, and safety; risk management; security and resilience; IT and information security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO</td>\n<td>ISO 9001</td>\n<td>Quality management systems. One of the two most prominent management system standards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO</td>\n<td>ISO 14001</td>\n<td>Environmental management systems. The other most prominent management system standard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS International</td>\n<td>ASIS ORM.1-2017</td>\n<td>Security and Resilience in Organizations and their Supply Chains – Requirements with Guidance. Basis of Figure C.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS International</td>\n<td>— (library)</td>\n<td>Security management standards and guidelines. More information at asisonline.org, in the standards and guidelines section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NFPA (National Fire Protection Association)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Not all are voluntary: some are incorporated into regulations, such as building codes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UL (Underwriters Laboratories)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Several standards on security equipment: locks, alarms, and access control systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Illuminating Engineering Society (IES)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Lighting standards and practices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electronic Industries Association (EIA)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Electronic components and products</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the appendix gives a code only for ISO 9001, ISO 14001, and ASIS ORM.1-2017. The ASIS standard is published as ANSI/ASIS ORM.1-2017 (outside the book). The EIA has been called the Electronic Industries Alliance since 1997 (outside the book).</p>\n<p><strong>Benefits of Standards (benefits for the security professional)</strong> — a standard can do one or all of these things:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Codify best practices and processes, and share lessons learned.</li>\n<li>Provide tools to assess threats, risks, vulnerabilities, criticalities, and impacts.</li>\n<li>Define measurement methods.</li>\n<li>Document equipment performance requirements to ensure effectiveness and safety.</li>\n<li>Set design requirements so that devices, systems, and infrastructure withstand threats.</li>\n<li>Define effective methods to identify people.</li>\n<li>Increase knowledge and competence.</li>\n<li>Improve information sharing between jurisdictions and interoperability.</li>\n<li>Provide consistency in services.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Management System Standards</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They help improve how the organization delivers services and executes processes. Widely accepted in many fields.</li>\n<li>The most prominent: ISO 9001 (quality) and ISO 14001 (environmental).</li>\n<li>Approach: analyze the requirements of the organization and of stakeholders, and define processes that lead to success.</li>\n<li>They provide the framework for continuous improvement. They raise the probability of achieving strategic, operational, tactical, and reputational objectives, and strengthen resilience.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Plan-Do-Check-Act (PDCA) cycle</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the operating principle of management system standards: structured problem solving focused on continuous improvement.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>What is done (appendix text)</th>\n<th>What Figure C.1 says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Plan</td>\n<td>Identify and analyze the organization's problems and assets and rank them by importance</td>\n<td>Define and analyze a problem and its context</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Do</td>\n<td>Prioritize the next steps, make a detailed action plan, and apply it</td>\n<td>Devise a solution, develop a detailed action plan, and apply it systematically</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Check</td>\n<td>Examine whether the solutions give results consistent with the plan</td>\n<td>Confirm results against the plan; identify deviations and problems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Act</td>\n<td>If the solutions do solve the problems: standardize them across the organization, review the list of problems, and define new ones. There the cycle restarts</td>\n<td>Standardize the solution; review and define the next problems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Figure C.1 — Plan-Do-Check-Act Model (from ASIS ORM.1-2017)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Input: stakeholders and interested parties with their requirements and expectations.</li>\n<li>Center: organizational resilience management systems, turning in the PDCA cycle.</li>\n<li>Output: stakeholders and interested parties with managed risk.</li>\n<li>Note: the figure text comes in an encoded font in the PDF. I reconstructed it by deciphering its characters; it should be checked against the image.</li>\n</ul>","toc":[],"ojo":[{"s":"Standards in Security","h":"the appendix gives a code only for ISO 9001, ISO 14001, and ASIS ORM.1-2017. The ASIS standard is published as ANSI/ASIS ORM.1-2017 (outside the book). The EIA has been called the Electronic Industries Alliance since 1997 (outside the book)."}]}};
